{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.972683+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_000dcfcfe2b773b132a238e3b19d1560d4a6d4cf5e1cf0e539fcb1a2c71aa1f0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:defBmu of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For every $\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*$, we define 
    \begin{equs}
        \text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau)\eqdef\Big\{(\tau_1,\tau_2)\in\mathcal{T}^2 :\exists\,\underline{\tau}_i\in\tau_i\in\mathcal{T}\,,\;\exists\,n\in N(\underline{\tau}_1)\,,\;\underline{\tau}_2\graft_n\underline{\tau}_1\in\tau\Big\}
    \end{equs}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.436961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00123405b6363774d813fa54dc73e5a8c2955a65669943b6db5f3499a92f1a34","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2606.15178, 'The distribution of the de Moivre experiment'] Let $ X $ be a discrete random variable with the bi$^{s}$nomial distribution with parameters $ n $ and $ p $, $ X\sim \mathcal{B}^{(s)}(n,p) $. Then, since $X$ is a sum of $n$ i.i.d.\ $s$-Bernoulli variables, $G_X=(G_{X_1})^n$; explicitly,
	\begin{equation}
		G_X(t) =\left(\frac{q^{s+1}-(pt)^{s+1} }{q-p t}\right)^n=\sum_{k=0}^{sn}{n\choose k}_s (pt)^kq^{sn-k},\quad \text{ for } p\neq q,
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
		G_X(t) =\frac{1}{\left( s+1\right)^n}\left( \frac{1-t^{s+1}}{1-t}\right)^n=\frac{1}{\left( s+1\right)^n}\sum_{k=0}^{sn}{n\choose k}_s t^k, \quad \text{ for } p=q.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.746507+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0012cfcaeba746404eb54ca74a2e99f61b548ed6243eff4742cbc2e695a3afe9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:vector-q-mass-identity-paper of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] For every \(q>0\) and every \(n\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\lvert u \rvert=n}L_u^q\right]=\rho(q)^n,
\end{equation}
where the identity is understood in the extended sense, and where
\(
\rho(q)^0=1
\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.749510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00134c863a800ef3f1cb361cb8d1eb07233b3f1d5e7423ef729ed0c4c187e4e8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:fokker_superpo of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and let ${\boldsymbol\mu}:[0,\infty)\to \mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ (with ${\boldsymbol\mu}_0 = \nu$) be such that
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto {\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big],  \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto {\boldsymbol\sigma} {\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) \times \mathbb{R}^d , \overline{\boldsymbol\mu}),
\end{equation}
with $\overline{\boldsymbol\mu}$ as in Notation (not:measure_dt_dx of the paper).

Then:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] If $X$ is a solution to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$, with $[X_t]={\boldsymbol\mu}_t $ for $t\geq0$, then ${\boldsymbol\mu}$ is a weak solution to NL-FP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$.
\item[(ii)] [Superposition] Under the additional assumption that 
\begin{equation}
\Big[ t \mapsto \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \Big( |{\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)| + \| {\boldsymbol\sigma}{\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t) \| \Big) {\boldsymbol\mu}_t (d x)\Big], \  \big[ t \mapsto {\boldsymbol\mu}_{t} (\mathbb{R}^d) \big]  \in L^{\infty}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) ,
\end{equation}

if ${\boldsymbol\mu}$ is a (weak) solution to NL-FP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$, 
 then there exists a solution $X$ to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$ such that $ [X_t] = {\boldsymbol\mu}_t $.

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.649058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00152b42f7a290b779d3bd2c718792f3c4b04f8e34b6e3e40ed935b852c66f97","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem LastOne of arXiv:2411.13926, 'Random walks on random walks:\\ non-perturbative results in high dimensions'] Let $Y_k^{(1)} \in I^{(1)}$ be the random lift of $y_k \in I^{(k)}$. Then 
\begin{align}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(Y_k^{(1)}=x) = P^{RW}(X \in x \;|\;  D_k), \quad x \in  I^{(1)}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.319332+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0016af85956542def1ce965efbae40a654d95e7b632c2b85068cd8deacb5a35a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:extra:2-ST of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] Suppose that \((T,R_{+},R_{-},\gamma)\) is a non-longitudinal 2-ST. Then up to orbit equivalence, there is only one semiflow \(\varphi\) satisfying the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(\varphi\) is tight.
\item \(\varphi\) contains exactly one bi-infinite flowline \(\lambda\) and \(\lambda\) is a pseudo-hyperbolic closed orbit.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.953713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0018e2f96282192913266094c67e494edafdb5a342b8e8d6e29e4771dda32a5f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 26 of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] Fix $t,q\in (0,\infty)$. There exist constants $N_0(t, q) \in \bbN$ and $C(t, q) \in (0, \infty)$ depending on $(t,q)$ such that for all $N\geq N_0(t, q)$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \bbE\Big[\bbW_1^2(P_t^1(\bfJ),\gamma_q)\Big]\geq \frac{C(t,q)}{N}.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:17:34.002543+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_001c7f36886f71dbbc46dd09f3c4302a461617ff529426800088b961546405aa","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 64 of arXiv:2609.03403, 'Hyperpositive functions, sector bounded functions and a trace formula'] Let $0\le T\le I_m$, and let $F$ be a $\mathbb C^{m\times m}$-valued function analytic in $\mathbb C_r$. The kernel
\[
  K_{T,1}(z,w)=\frac{F(z)+F(w)^*-F(z)TF(w)^*}{z+\overline{w}}
\]
is positive definite in $\mathbb C_r$ if and only if $\det(F(z)+I_m)\not=0$, $z\in\mathbb C_r$ and
\begin{equation}

(F(z)+I_m)^{-1}\begin{pmatrix} F(z)\sqrt{I_m+T}&I_m\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
is a $\mathbb C^{m\times 2m}$-valued bounded function.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.640000+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_001ce081dc4b4c3104d2b1d30094d7f00f7af1373371f97f40152667c87e3f24","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2609.00690, 'Boundary behavior and optimal pointwise H\"older exponent of the total local time of $(1+\'] Let $V^\lambda$ be the bounded nonnegative distributional solution to \eqref{V lambda} which vanishes at infinity. Then we have
	\begin{equation}
		V^\lambda(x)=\bigg[\frac{\beta}{\sqrt{\beta+2}}|x|+\bigg(\frac{2}{\lambda\sqrt{\beta+2}}\bigg)^{\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}\bigg]^{-\frac{2}{\beta}},\quad x\in\mathbb R,\ \lambda>0.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.578871+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0022a926c4304dacb5149ae7b865212c443887973d2ae2cf8f9008f89b5ba249","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 54 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d\geq 2$. For every $\beta\geq 0$, and every $x,y\in \mathbb Z^d$,
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf P_{\beta}^{\emptyset,\emptyset}[x,y\in \mathbf{C}_{\n_1+\n_2}(0)]\leq \langle \sigma_0\sigma_x\rangle_{\beta}\langle \sigma_x\sigma_y\rangle_{\beta}\langle \sigma_y\sigma_0\rangle_{\beta}+(x\Leftrightarrow y),
\end{equation}
where $(x\Leftrightarrow y)$ denotes the same term with $x$ and $y$ inverted. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.415411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0022ae0b4017db496d521c8e4572fcfe2a52a462eade116f08c3fa04fdf185ac","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:comp-seq of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] There exists a sequence $\{(f_n, g_n)\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of pairs of bounded Lipschitz functions satisfying:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $f_n \le 0 \le g_n$ and $(f_n,g_n)\in\mathcal{F}$ for all $n$;
		\item $f_n$ and $g_n$ vanish on a neighborhood of $0$;
		\item $g_n(x) \nearrow \frac12 |x|^2$ and $f_n(x) \searrow \chi_\infty(x)$ pointwise as $n \to \infty$.
	\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.322076+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_002488fc7b24e4082e5a4d94030dba356bf73730007620f7c0e94b975d3bb01d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma keylemma of arXiv:2411.06245, 'On the longest/shortest negative excursion of a Lévy risk process and related quantities'] For $q,r>0$ and

for $x\in \mathbb{R}$,
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[ \mathrm{e}^{-q\tau _{0}^{-}}\mathbb{P}_{X_{\tau _{0}^{-}}}\left(U_{\mathrm{e}_q}<r,\tau _{0}^{+}>\mathrm{e}_q\right) \right]  =\left(1-\mathrm{e}^{-qr}\right)\left(Z_q (x) -\dfrac{q}{\Phi _{q}} W_q (x) \right)  \\
&&\qquad-\left(q\int_{0}^{r}\mathrm{e}^{-qs}\Lambda \left( x,s\right) ds- W_q (x)\mathrm{e}^{-qr}\int_{0}^{r}\int_{0}^{\infty }\mathrm{e}^{\Phi _{q}z}\frac{z}{r}
\mathbb{P}\left( X_{r}\in \mathrm{d}z\right) \mathrm{d}s\right).
\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.754251+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0024a41e4cd1d2784b7c8ade47609a23af03f731fad5df1377483b88df436266","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:intro-lbm-spectrum of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For every $0<\gamma<2$, almost surely, $A_h$ has compact resolvent, and its
positive eigenvalues, listed with multiplicity, satisfy
\begin{equation}
 0<\Lambda_1^h<\Lambda_2^h<\cdots\uparrow\infty.

\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $N\ge1$ and $1\le n_1<\cdots<n_N$,
\begin{equation}
 (\Lambda_{n_1}^h,\ldots,\Lambda_{n_N}^h)_\#\mathbb P
 \ll\mathcal L^N.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.077134+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0024d019c45b6764211e2f3661c358ec920ae342b7ecafd4253883fc15f77b90","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:B.2 of arXiv:2411.12192, 'Sample path properties and small ball probabilities for stochastic fractional diffusion eq'] For any $\varphi\in L^{1/{H_0}}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with $H_0\in(1/2,1)$,
  \begin{equation*}
