{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.290944+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00c6db1815bf4c8d6db84f22a173fafbf4e63b7cf538f0bbdf1da64bfaab80ee","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem unicty of arXiv:2411.05204, '\Large\bf Sample Path Properties of the Fractional Wiener--Weierstrass Bridge'] Let $ Y $ be a fractional Wiener--Weierstrass  bridge with parameters $\alpha$, $b$, and $H$, and $K=\min\{1,({-\log_b\alpha})\}$.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]

\item If $H<K$, then $\omega(u)=u^H\sqrt{\log (1/u)}$ is an exact uniform modulus of continuity for $Y$.
\item If $H=K$, then $\omega(u)=u^H\log (1/u)$ is an exact uniform modulus of continuity for $Y$.
\item If $H>K$, then there exists a non-constant and unbounded random variable $Z$ such that 
\begin{align}
    {\mathbb P}\Bigg(\lim_{h\to0}\sup_{\substack{t,s\in[0,1]\\ |t-s|<h}}\frac{|Y(t)-Y(s)|}{|t-s|^K}=Z\Bigg)=1.
\end{align}
In particular, $Y$ does not have an exact uniform modulus of continuity.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

