\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:29:27.984221+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_010b6169397e65cdeefff7693570631e9967d19d3f186677220228542eb6e375","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 (Strong-$A_\infty$ local geometry) of arXiv:2608.24863, 'Uniform Logarithmic Sobolev Inequalities for the 2D Coulomb Gas at the Diffusive Temperatu'] The measure $w\D z$ is doubling, with doubling constant depending only on $Q$.  It supports a weak $(1,2)$ Poincar\'e inequality, with constants depending only on $Q$.\footnote{Here, ``weak'' means that the gradient term may be averaged over a fixed dilate $\lambda B$ of $B$.  This differs from the semigroup-theoretic weak Poincar\'e inequality involving a scale-dependent remainder term; see \cite[p.~176]{Bjorn2001} for the former; for the latter, see \cite[\S7.5]{BakryGentilLedoux2014} and \cite{RocknerWang2001}.}  There are $\kappa_Q>1$, $\Lambda_Q\ge1$, and $C_Q<\infty$ such that, for every Euclidean ball $B=B(x,r)$,
\begin{equation}
 \left(\fint_B|f-f_{B,w}|^{2\kappa_Q}w\right)^{1/(2\kappa_Q)}
 \le C_Qr\left(\fint_{\Lambda_QB}|\nabla f|^2w\right)^{1/2}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.414273+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_010c7e7fc4e66a7649c2cffd5012c3a2ae1efac3113e9820c8ca021938cdf0df","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Expected length of arXiv:2601.07808, 'Supercritical long-range percolation on graphs of polynomial growth: the truncated one-arm'] \label[lemma]{lem:finite_exp_leng}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 1$, and let $J : V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ be a transitive kernel with $J(x,y) = O(d_G(x,y)^{-d \alpha})$ with $\alpha > 1$.

    Then for any $x \in G$ we have $\mathbf{E}_{\beta}\left[\mathrm{length}(x)\right] < \infty$ if and only if $\alpha > 1 + 1/d$, in which case there exists $c = c(G,J) > 0$ such that $\mathbf{E}_{\beta}\left[\mathrm{length}(x)\right] \leq c \beta$.","status":"unverified"}

