{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.362939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03082ee94dda4e06885d67c69067d943e8e72b9affb9034ac1741bd4f192c999","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Metzler largest eigenvalue of arXiv:2507.09421, 'Stability of randomly switching stochastic reaction networks with asymptotically linear tr'] Suppose that $M$ is a $d\times d$ Metzler matrix. Let $r$ denote the largest eigenvalue of $M$. Let $r'$ denote the maximum over $\varnothing\ne I\subsetneq[d]$ of the largest eigenvalue of the (Metzler) matrix $M^I$. Then $r'\le r$.
\begin{proof}
    This lemma, but for specifically non-negative matrices rather than general Metzler matrices, is Proposition 4 in [Gantmacher_1959]. To extend to Metzler matrices $M$, let $N:=M+\alpha I_d$ for some $\alpha\in\mathbb R_{>0}$ no smaller in absolute value than all diagonal entries of $M$, so that $N$ is non-negative. Then the largest eigenvalue of $N$ is $r+\alpha$. Moreover, for each $\varnothing\ne I\subseteq[d]$ the largest eigenvalue of $N^I$ is $\alpha$ plus the largest eigenvalue of $M^I$. It follows that the maximum over $I$ of the largest eigenvalue of $N^I$ is $r'+\alpha$. Since the lemma holds for non-negative matrices we have $r'+\alpha\le r+\alpha$; the lemma in full generality follows.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.731862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_030bab8da8a439fe8bd30e3168f95ad203bdcfe41a9b290dd5b0ad9444a8464d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 84 (First Picard multiplier products) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Put $\alpha_V=1/2-\betasum-\kappa$ and let $V\in C_T\cC^{\alpha_V}$ be spatially localized.  If $G\in C_TH^\lambda\cap L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma}$ with $\lambda\ge0$, $0<\sigma<\alpha_V$, and
\[
  r<\min\{\alpha_V,\lambda\},
\]
then
\begin{align}
  \norm{VG}_{C_TH^r}
  +\norm{VG}_{L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma}}
  \lesssim
  \norm{V}_{C_T\cC^{\alpha_V}}
  \paren{\norm{G}_{C_TH^\lambda}+\norm{G}_{L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma}}}.
\end{align}
The same conclusion holds when $G$ is another localized $\cC^{\alpha_V}$ function, with any $r<\alpha_V$.  Consequently every term in $\mathscr U_{\W}^\rho \mathscr U_{\K}^\rho$ containing at least one first Picard factor belongs to
