{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.293326+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_033d6dc43a30f5bbbef4c47f61c8a783343ea6e36d5abdcc4ba84299bcf3a233","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition type of arXiv:2411.05235, 'A comparative study of three mathematical approaches applied to the reversal of AMR'] \textbf{Equilibrium Point of a Stochastic Differential Equation.}\\
Consider the stochastic differential equation:
\begin{equation}
  dX = b(X) \, dt + \sigma(X) \, dB(t), 
\end{equation}
\noindent where $b$ and $\sigma$ are continuous functions on an interval $I \subset \mathbb{R}$, $B$ is a Brownian motion on a probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$, and let $\xi \in I$. We say that $\xi$ is an equilibrium point of ((SDE of the paper)), or that $\xi$ is a trivial solution of ((SDE of the paper)), if and only if $b(\xi) = \sigma(\xi) = 0$.","status":"unverified"}

