{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.463268+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04152f8d116691129fc5be09e963312f8aa3d59c6ca822e12c03acf4eb181afa","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 of arXiv:2606.28793, 'Randomized second order Riesz projections on the Hamming cube'] Let $1<p<\infty$, let $X$ be a Banach space, and let $F:\Omega_n\times\Omega_n\to X$.  Set $F_j(\eps)=\E_\delta[\delta_jF(\eps,\delta)],
 \qquad 1\leq j\leq n.$ Then
\begin{equation}

 \left(\E_\eps
 \left\|
 \sum_{j=1}^n\Delta^{-1}D_jF_j(\eps)
 \right\|_X^p\right)^{1/p}
 \leq C_{p'}\sqrt n\,
 \left(\E_{\eps,\delta}\|F(\eps,\delta)\|_X^p\right)^{1/p},
\end{equation}
where $p'=p/(p-1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.119808+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_041592d9ef1cce47a4832d310a02431534410b3539b758cdf0e261e4e5713ee1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition lemma:UpperBound of arXiv:2608.17031, 'A shape theorem for periodic branching diffusion'] Fix $e\in S^{d-1}$ and let $\bar{\mathbf{v}}$ be the effective drift introduced in (equation eq:Intro:EffectiveDrift of the paper). 
    Let $\lambda_e>0$ be such that $c^*(e)=\frac{\gamma(\lambda_e e)}{\lambda_e}$ as in 
     (equation eq:prelimCriticalSpeed of the paper), $\Tilde{c}(e)>0$ is the speed relative to $\bar{\mathbf{v}}$ defined in (equation eq:Intro:SpeedRelativeTo of the paper), and $\zeta_e:=\lambda_e e \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Let $\psi(\cdot ;\zeta_e)$ be the principal eigenfunction of the eigenvalue problem (equation eq:Intro:EigenvalueProblem of the paper),  normalized so that $\int_{\mathbb{T}^d} \psi(x;\zeta_e) dx=1$. Then for every $t>0$ and $f:[0,\infty)\to \mathbb{R}$,

    \begin{equation}
        \sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}^{d}}\proba{x}{}{\exists v\in \mathcal{N}_t : (X_t(v)-X_{0}(v)-t\bar{\mathbf{v}})\cdot e> \Tilde{c}(e)t + f(t)} \leq \frac{\max_{y\in \mathbb{R}^d}\psi(y;\zeta_{e}) }{ \min_{y\in \mathbb{R}^d}\psi(y;\zeta_{e}) } e^{-\lambda_e f(t)}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

