{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.024496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04300ac6e455a1db307daaf6beada6f486430ba6a5facb46f767e3eaa6d835bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (\citeauthor{RP81}, \citeyear{RP81}) of arXiv:2606.06739, 'The relationship between the transition functions of the labeled and unlabeled versions of'] Let $E$ and $E_0$ be complete separable metric spaces, and let $\Phi:E\mapsto E_0$ be Borel measurable and surjective.  Let $\lambda$ be a one-step transition function from $E_0$ to $E$ $($i.e., $y\in E_0\mapsto\lambda(y,\cdot)\in\mathscr{P}(E)$ is Borel measurable$)$ satisfying $\lambda(y,\Phi^{-1}(y))=1$ for all $y\in E_0$.  Let $P(t,x,\cdot)$ be the transition function for a Markov process $X$ in $E$ with initial distribution $\mu:=\int_{E_0}\lambda(y,\cdot)\,\mu_0(dy)$ for some $\mu_0\in\mathscr{P}(E_0)$.  If the condition
\begin{equation}
\int_E P(t,x,\cdot)\,\lambda(y,dx)=\int_E\bigg[\int_{E_0}\lambda(z,\cdot)\,P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(dz))\bigg]\lambda(y,dx),
\end{equation}
holds for every $t>0$ and $y\in E_0$, then $Y:=\Phi\circ X$ is a Markov process in $E_0$ with initial distribution $\mu_0$ and transition function
\begin{equation}
Q(t,y,\cdot):=\int_E P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(\cdot))\,\lambda(y,dx).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.016061+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0434e1c522587eaf4f22a9cbec709b0073261ebd9023ab28be4dd372a1be607b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma DtYts of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Under Assumptions (ass:LR of the paper), (ass:diff2 of the paper), and~(ass:SC of the paper), for $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$, $r \in [0,T]$, and every $v \in H$, the Malliavin derivative of the $H$-valued random variable $Y(t,s)v$ is given fibrewise by
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{D}_r [Y(t,s)v] \;=\; \mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)\,v,
\end{equation}
both sides being elements of $L^2(\Omega; L_2(U,H))$ for a.e.\ $r$, and directionally
\[
  \mathcal{D}^a_r[Y(t,s)v] \;=\; \mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v \quad \text{in } L^2(\Omega; H), \qquad a \in U .
\]
Here $\mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)v$ is the unique doubly-fibrewise solution, in the sense of Theorem~(thm:Z-wellposed of the paper), of the linearised equation (equation eq:second-var of the paper) obtained by applying both sides to $v$ and to the direction $a$, with initial condition~(equation eq:Z-initial-cond of the paper) (reproduced from Definition~(def:second-var of the paper)):
\[
  \mathcal{Z}(\max(s,r),s;r)\,v \;=\;
  \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } r < s,\\
    \mathcal{B}'_u(r,X(r))(Y(r,s)v) & \text{if } s < r \leq t,\\
    \mathcal{B}'_u(s,X(s))\,v & \text{if } r = s \text{ in Regime A},\\
    0 & \text{if } r = s \text{ in Regime B}.
  \end{cases}
\]
For $r > t$, $\mathcal{D}_r [Y(t,s)v] = 0$ (by causality). The relation~(equation eq:DtYts-explicit of the paper) is to be read fibrewise in $v$ and, in the noise slot, as an identity in $L_2(U,H)$. For each fixed $v \in H$, both sides are well-defined elements of $L^2(\Omega; L_2(U,H))$ (the left-hand side as the Malliavin derivative of the $H$-valued random variable $Y(t,s)v$, the right-hand side by Theorem~(thm:Z-wellposed of the paper)). The shorthand notation ``$\mathcal{D}_r Y(t,s) = \mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)$'' refers to this fibrewise identity, not to an operator-norm equality in $L(H)$ which would require the strictly stronger operator-valued moment bounds discussed after Proposition~(prop:Y-moments of the paper).

In the particular case $s = 0$, the explicit representation is, fibrewise,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{D}_r [Y(t,0)v] \;=\; \mathcal{Z}(t,0;r)\,v \qquad \text{for every } v \in H,
\end{equation}
an identity in $L_2(U, H)$ whose directional form reads $\mathcal{D}^a_r[Y(t,0)v] = \mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,0;r)v$ in $H$ for every $a \in U$.
This is the infinite-dimensional analogue of the ``second variation'' $D_t Y_T$ appearing in Lemma 5.4 of [mirafzali2025malliavin]; the present formulation differs from the finite-dimensional setting in being fibrewise rather than operator-valued, since the operator-norm interpretation requires structural input not provided by the abstract framework (cf.\ the discussion after Proposition~(prop:Y-moments of the paper) and Theorem~(thm:Z-wellposed of the paper)).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.438676+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0435a99bc134a6dfaab9fd253514186fd2cda281e49988a7f2d16d40c09014b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem integral of arXiv:2509.26254, '$K$-Branching Random Walk with Noisy Selection: \\ \Large Large Population Limits and Phas'] Let $f_N: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}\cup\{-\infty\}$ be a sequence of functions and let $f:\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}\cup\{-\infty\}$ be a c\`all\`al function with discontinuity set $D_f$ such that for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$,
\begin{align}
\lim_{\delta \to 0}\lim_{N \to \infty}\sup_{z \in B(x,\delta)}f_N(z) &=\lim_{\delta \to 0} \sup_{z \in B(x, \delta)}f(z), \\
\lim_{\delta \to 0}\lim_{N \to \infty}\inf_{z \in B(x, \delta)}f_N(z) &=\lim_{\delta \to 0} \inf_{z \in B(x, \delta)}f(z).
\end{align}
Let $(M_N)_{N \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of (random) point measures on $\mathbb{R}$ which has a limiting log-profile $g \in \mathcal{D}$ with discontinuity set $D_g$. If $D_f \cap D_g = \emptyset$, then for all $a,b \in \overline{\mathbb{R}} ~\setminus~(D_f~\cup~D_g)$, as $N \to \infty$, 
\[ \frac{\log\left(\int_{(a,b]}N^{f_N(x)} dM_N(x)\right)}{\log N} \xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}} \sup_{x \in (a,b]}\left(f(x) + g(x)\right)\in \mathbb{R} \cup \{-\infty\}.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.283151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0437d07c9b15dff3d6928e02549f7cbe194888929bf50866e52247274b7b3b10","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition c:1 of arXiv:2605.20587, 'Persistence and entropic repulsion of stationary Gaussian fields with spectral singularity'] [Simultaneous truncation and mollification]

