{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.467075+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_044ce47014672a69c0295bf56171c11d37cb5cd3df038e97b16a8e29842734e4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Oriented flattenings) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] For $S\subset[m]$, the \emph{oriented flattening} of $K$ across $S$ is
\begin{equation*}
  \Flat_S(K):=\Theta_{D_S,R_S}(\Pi_SK)
  \in\Sch_2(D_S,R_S).
\end{equation*}
Equivalently,
\[
  \Flat_S(K):\conj{\cA_S}\otimes\cC
  \longrightarrow \cA_{S^c}\otimes\cE.
\]
In coordinates,
\begin{align}

&\Flat_S(K)
  \left(
    \bigotimes_{\nu\in S}\conj{a^{(\nu)}_{i_\nu}}
    \otimes c_\alpha
  \right) \notag\\
&\qquad:=
  \sum_{(i_\mu)_{\mu\in S^c},\beta}
  K_{i_1\cdots i_m\alpha\beta}
  \left(\bigotimes_{\mu\in S^c}a^{(\mu)}_{i_\mu}\right)
  \otimes e_\beta.
\end{align}
For $2\le r\le\infty$, the \emph{simultaneous Schatten flattening
profile} is
