{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.852403+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04712be9bfdcc427e9870d21e597f7bc44349bd02f00081d535df6ac527f358a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2609.03873, 'Minimal proximal definable flows over the $p$-adics'] Let $G$ be a group and $X$ be a $G$-flow.
\begin{enumerate}
\item A ($G$-)subflow $Y$ of $X$ is a closed subset which is closed under the $G$-action. $X$ is minimal if there is no nonempty proper subflow.
\item Let $x,y\in X$. We say that $x,y$ are proximal if there is a net $(g_i)_i$ of $G$, such that $\lim_i g_i x=\lim_i g_i y$. $X$ is called proximal if any $x,y\in X$ are proximal.
\item $X$ is strongly proximal if for every $\mu\in \Prob(X)$, the weak-* closure of the orbit $G \mu$ contains a Dirac measure.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.523970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0475d6ee435d31fd77fdd2b0d436f889f4c3bab03a66c8078433d1e52f84d598","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem le:paraproduct of arXiv:2411.03482, 'Ergodicity of 2D singular stochastic Navier--Stokes equations'] For $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbf{R}$ and $p, p_1, p_2, q, q_1, q_2 \in [1, \infty]$ such that 
	\begin{equ}
		\frac{1}{p} := \frac{1}{p_1} + \frac{1}{p_2}\;, \qquad \frac{1}{q} := \frac{1}{q_1} + \frac{1}{q_2}\;,
	\end{equ}
	the following bounds hold uniformly in $\varphi, \psi$.
	\begin{equs}

		\| \varphi \olessthan \psi \|_{\Besov{\alpha}{p, q}} & \lesssim \| \varphi
    \|_{L^{p_1}} \| \psi \|_{\Besov{\alpha}{p_2, q}} \;, & &  \\ 

    \| \varphi \olessthan \psi \|_{\Besov{\alpha+ \beta}{p, q}} &\lesssim \| \varphi
    \|_{\Besov{\alpha}{p_1, q_1}} \| \psi \|_{\Besov{\beta}{p_2, q_2}} \;, \ \ & &  \text{if} \ \ \alpha  <
    0 \;, \\

    \| \varphi \odot \psi \|_{\Besov{\alpha+ \beta}{p, q}} &\lesssim \| \varphi
    \|_{\Besov{\alpha}{p_1, q_1}} \| \psi \|_{\Besov{\beta}{p_2, q_2}}\;, \ \ & & \text{if} \ \ \alpha
    {+} \beta > 0 \;.
	\end{equs}
	We will call the first two inequalities high-low paraproduct estimate, and the third inequality resonance estimate. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.632770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04762d9df0c55c0af9c3ab3e1d24e2b46a94f9562aee26a1af9d5b8e43c148f1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma EHI:l:NoA3example3 of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $S$ be a subordinator with Laplace exponent $\phi$, drift $\gamma=0$, and L\'{e}vy measure $\mu$. Let $X=(X_t)_{t \geq 0} = (B_{S_t})_{t \geq 0}$ be a subordinate Brownian motion with subordinator $S$, and let $j(r)$ be the jump kernel of $X$.
