{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.335340+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04bef854202b082bfe321af790115caabbe508d03af1f8507f60c625639f6ab1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm-busemann of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Let ${\operatorname{XDP}} \in \{\operatorname{LDP} , \operatorname{SDP}\}$. 
Let $\{x_k\}_{k\in\mathbbm Z}$ be the boundary vertices of $M_{0,\infty}$, as above.
There exists a unique function 
\[\mathcal X :  \mathbbm Z \to \mathbbm Z,\] 
called the \textbf{Busemann function}, such that $\mathcal X(0) = 0$ and the following is true. Let $k,k' \in \mathbbm Z$ and let\footnote{Our convention is that $\mathbbm N = \{1,2,\dots\}$ and $\mathbbm N_0 = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$.}
$\{w_j\}_{j\in\mathbbm N}$ be a sequence\footnote{The existence of at least one such sequence is ensured by Lemma~(lem-uibot-bdy of the paper): the rightmost directed paths in $M_{0,\infty}$ started from $x_k$ and $x_{k'}$ coalesce into each other, so we can take $\{w_j\}$ to be a sequence of points hit by both of these rightmost directed paths.} of distinct vertices of $M_{0,\infty}$ such that, for each $j\in\mathbbm N$, each of $x_k$ and $x_{k'}$ can be joined to $w_j$ by a directed path in $M_{0,\infty}$. 
Then
\begin{equation} 
\mathcal X(k') -\mathcal X(k) = {\operatorname{XDP}}_{M_{0,\infty}}(x_{k'} , w_j) - {\operatorname{XDP}}_{M_{0,\infty}}(x_{k } , w_j) ,\quad \text{for each sufficiently large $j\in\mathbbm N$.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:53.203969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04c11a876aabc0167af9a093e54ebbf8a2687448177a2503f4dd5ef56978dd3b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 21 (Convex Decomposition) of arXiv:2606.12670, 'The censored stochastic six-vertex model and parabolic Kazhdan--Lusztig $R$-polynomials'] For $k\in\mathbb Z$ we have
