{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:47:07.706930+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_05080372581359aec8cb3fbda065c55bca81d910db5f00534d8d112d20ce36fa","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (One telescoping for the family) of arXiv:2608.06232, 'The mean absolute deviation of the classical discrete distributions:\\ collapse identities'] Define
	\begin{equation}
		g(k)=
		\begin{cases}
			kq, & X\sim\Bin(N,p),\\[2pt]
			k, & X\sim\Poi(\lambda),\\[2pt]
			k/p, & X\sim\NB(r,p),\\[4pt]
			\dfrac{k\,(k+N-K-n)}{N}, & X\sim\Hyp(N,K,n).
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	Then in each case
	\begin{equation}
		(k-\mu)\,P(k)=g(k)P(k)-g(k+1)P(k+1)\qquad(k\ge0),
	\end{equation}
	and consequently, for \emph{every} integer $\nu\ge0$,
	\begin{equation}
		\sum_{k\ge\nu}(k-\mu)\,P(k)=g(\nu)\,P(\nu) .
	\end{equation}
	In particular, with $\nu=\lceil\mu\rceil$,
	\begin{equation}
		\E\,|X-\mu|=2\,g(\nu)\,P(\nu).
	\end{equation}
	When $\mu\in\Z$, the two choices $\nu=\mu$ and $\nu=\mu+1$ in \eqref{eq:collapse} give the
	same value.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:27.033079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_050c46806fa91c22b90eafe3685fba67924e3841bd5810f458e5e1c556593271","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:muckenhoupt_majorant of arXiv:2505.14012, 'Ergodicity for stochastic neural field equations'] If $J_0\in L^1(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is a radially decreasing majorant of $J\in L^1(\mathbb{R}^d)$,
then for all $u\in L^1_{\operatorname{loc}}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, we have the following pointwise estimate
\begin{align*}
(u\ast J)(x)\leq\|J_0\|_{L^1(\mathbb{R}^d)}\mathfrak{M}(u)(x),\quad x\in\mathbb{R}^d,
\end{align*}
where $u\ast J:=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}J(\cdot-y) u(y)\,dy$ denotes \emph{convolution}, and ${\mathfrak M}$ is as in (equation eq:Max of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

