{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:21:28.732236+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_056799fa8bfe00c8ee76afe0e6aa1df6d81a82458111996315b7e755c5365ba2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:gaussian-poincare of arXiv:2605.03718, 'Potential Hessian Ascent III:\\ Sampling the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick Model at $\beta < 1/'] Suppose $\mu$ is $\alpha$-log-concave, that is, $\mu(x)\propto e^{-U(x)}$ where $U$ is $\alpha$-strongly convex. Then $\mu$ satisfies a log-Sobolev and hence Poincaré inequality with constant $\alpha$, that is,
    \begin{align*}
    \forall f&:\mathbb R^n\to \mathbb R_{\geqslant0},& \mathop{{}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ent}}}_\mu(f)&\leqslant \frac{1}{2\alpha} \mathscr E(f,\log f)=\frac{1}{2\alpha} \int_{\mathbb R^n} \fc{\left\Vert {\nabla f}\right\Vert^2}{f} \,d\mu\\
        \forall f&:\mathbb R^n\to \mathbb R,& \mathop{{}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Var}}}_\mu(f)&\leqslant \frac{1}{\alpha} \mathscr E(f,f) =\frac{1}{\alpha} \int_{\mathbb R^n} \left\Vert {\nabla f}\right\Vert^2 \,d\mu.
    \end{align*}
    In particular, $\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)$ satisfies a log-Sobolev and hence Poincaré inequality with constant $\frac{1}{\sigma^2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:09.907798+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0567bbe01a0b4c8a9091ff0c783ba6caa6d4a4d952297380391eec9d103d20f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 4 (\rm Asymptotic behavior of the return probabilities for random walks  in cone) of arXiv:2603.26228, 'A local limit theorem for nonlattice multidimensional random walks in cones'] Assume  hypotheses  {\bf C},  {\bf M},  {\bf N} and {\bf A}.  Let ${\bf B}_1\subset \mathbb R^s$ be a  bounded Borel  set  whose boundary is negligible with respect to the Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb R^s$   and $ {\bf y}_2\in G_2$ such that ${\bf B}:= {\bf B}_1\times \{{\bf y}_2\}\subset \mathcal C$.  Then,  there exists  a  constant $\mathrm{C}$ such that,    for any $n \geq 1$ and $  {\bf x} \in \cC$,
\begin{equation}
\bP( \tau({\bf x})>n, {\bf x}  +S(n) \in  {\bf B})\leq   
\mathrm{C} \  { 1+\vert {\bf x}\vert^p \over n^{p+d/2}} \  \int_{{\bf B}_1}\widetilde{V}({\bf y}_1, {\bf y}_2) {\rm d} {\bf y}_1.
\end{equation}
Furthermore,   as $n \to +\infty$,  
\begin{equation}
\bP( \tau({\bf x})>n,  {\bf x}  +S(n) \in  {\bf B})\sim  
\mathfrak \kappa_1  { V({\bf x})\over n^{p+d/2}} \int_{{\bf B}_1}\widetilde{V}({\bf y}_1, {\bf y}_2) {\rm d} {\bf y}_1,
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde V$ is the harmonic function  for the random walk $(-S(n))_{n \geq 0}$   killed at   time $\tilde{\tau}$ of leaving $\mathcal C$ and $\kappa_1= \kappa_0^2H_0^2  \int_{\mathcal C} u^2({\bf y}) e^{-\vert {\bf y}\vert^2/2}{\rm d}{\bf y}.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.318592+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_05690381d854cf008f21f54627f2ede97f233e55a3761522eebcd5e45acb9999","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2607.27786, 'Gaussian free field in annulus: \\BPZ equations and crossing probabilities for level lines'] Fix $r>0$ and an even number $n=2N$. For $\bs{\alpha}, \bs{\beta}\in\LX_n$, we write $\bs{x}=\ee^{\ii\bs{\alpha}}$ and $\bs{y}=\ee^{\ii\bs{\beta}-r}$.
Let $\varphiann{n}$ be the bounded harmonic function in annulus $\A_r$ with alternating boundary data~\eqref{eqn::boundarydata}. Then
\begin{align} 
	\begin{split}
		\| \varphiann{n} \|_{\nabla(\A_r),\mathrm{reg}}^2= & \frac{\pi}{2r}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}(-1)^j (\alpha_j-\beta_j) \right)^2-6n\pi \sum_{m=1}^{\infty} \log \left(  1-\ee^{-2mr} \right) - \frac{n}{2}\pi r \\
		& - 2\pi \sum_{1\le i<j \le n} (-1)^{j-i} \left( \log \left| \Theta_1(r; \beta_{j}-\beta_i) \right| +\log \left| \Theta_1(r; \alpha_{j}-\alpha_i) \right| \right) \\
		& +2 \pi \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} (-1)^{j-i} \log \left| \Theta_3(r; \beta_{j}-\alpha_i) \right| .
	\end{split}
\end{align}
Moreover, its regularized Dirichlet energy is related to the partition function $\LZann{n}$ in~\eqref{eqn::LZann_def}: 
