{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.358828+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_063388acf59d0abd118b27a225c8405d6e0c2131082da0a9cd0c0d5b3b981d90","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:v cdot x^0>C of arXiv:2507.09421, 'Stability of randomly switching stochastic reaction networks with asymptotically linear tr'] In the setting of Theorem (thm:fast-switching-transience of the paper), for any constant $C\in\mathbb R$, any $i\in[n]$, and any $x\in\mathbb Z^d_{\ge0}$ such that $\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(x)\ne\varnothing$, there exists an $x^0\in\mathbb Z^d_{\ge0}$ such that $(x^0,i)$ is reachable from $(x,i)$ and $v\cdot x^0>C$.
\begin{proof}
Let $\Xi=\{y'-y:\exists i, y\to y'\in\mathcal R_i\text{ and }\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(y'-y)\ne\varnothing\}$ be the set of reactions in any environment which affect at least one species seen by $v$. By Lemma (lem:v not orthogonal to reactions of the paper), we may assume without loss of generality that $v$ sees all these reactions; that is, that $v\cdot(y'-y)\ne0$ for all $y'-y\in\Xi$.

For $m\in[d]$ let $z^m$ be as in the statement of Lemma (lem:generator positive of the paper); similarly, let $h_\kappa$ and $\mathcal L_\kappa$ be as in the statement of Lemma (lem:generator positive of the paper). As in the proof of Lemma (lem:generator positive of the paper), let $\varphi^i=(\varphi^i_1,\cdots,\varphi^i_d)$ be the vector defined by $\varphi^i_m:=v_m +\kappa^{-1} z^m_i$.

The proof will consist of three claims.

\textit{Claim 1:} As long as $\kappa$ is large enough, for any $i$ and any reaction $y\to y'\in\mathcal R_i$, the numbers $\varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)$ and $v\cdot(y'-y)$ have the same sign (positive, negative, or zero).

\vspace{.2cm}

Notice that for any reaction $y\to y'$, 
\begin{align*}
    \varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)
    &=\sum_{m=1}^d(v_m+\kappa^{-1}z^m_i)(y'_m-y_m)\\
    &=v\cdot(y'-y)+\kappa^{-1}\sum_{m=1}^d(z^m_i)(y'_m-y_m)
\end{align*}
In particular, $\varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)\to v\cdot(y'-y)$ as $\kappa\to\infty$, and hence $\varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)$ will have the same sign as $v\cdot(y'-y)$ whenever $\kappa$ is large enough, provided $v\cdot(y'-y)\ne0$. By our application of Lemma (lem:v not orthogonal to reactions of the paper) above, this includes all reactions $y\to y'$ with $\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(y'-y)\ne\varnothing$. Since there are only finitely many possible reactions, as long as $\kappa$ is large enough it follows that $\varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)$ has the same sign as $v\cdot(y'-y)$ whenever $\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(y'-y)\ne\varnothing$.

Now recall that $z_i^m=0$ whenever $v_m=0$. Therefore, $\operatorname{supp}(\varphi^i)=\operatorname{supp}(v)$ and hence if $y\to y'$ is a reaction such that $\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(y'-y)=\varnothing$ then $\operatorname{supp}(\varphi^i)\cap\operatorname{supp}(y'-y)=\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap(y'-y)=\varnothing$ and in particular $\varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)=0=v\cdot(y'-y)$. Therefore, $\varphi^i\cdot(y'-y)$ and $v\cdot(y'-y)$ still have the same sign in the case where $\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(y'-y)=\varnothing$, completing the proof of the claim.

\vspace{.2cm}

\textit{Claim 2:} If $x$ is such that $\operatorname{supp}(v)\cap\operatorname{supp}(x)\ne\varnothing$, then there exists at least one environment $i$ and at least one reaction $y\to y'\in\mathcal R_i$ such that $\lambda_{i,y\to y'}(x)>0$ and $v\cdot(y'-y)>0$.

\vspace{.2cm}

