{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:52.203519+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_065cb89d8df1b53ca68eadeaeabbf14f6ef62ce8bd851cef33307e72eb800e71","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2512.08745, 'Variance strikes back: sub-game--perfect Nash equilibria in time‑inconsistent $N$‑player g'] Let $\hat\balpha \in \cA_1^2$, and $\varepsilon >0$. We define
\begin{align*}
\ell_\varepsilon \coloneqq \inf \Big\{ \ell >0 : \exists (i,t,\alpha) \in \{1,2\} \times [0,T] \times \cA_1, \;
\P \big[ \omega \in \Omega: \big(J(t,\omega,\hat\balpha) - J\big(t,\omega,(\alpha\otimes_{t+\ell}\hat\alpha^{i}) \otimes_i \hat\alpha^{\smallertext{-}i}\big) \big) \cI^i < - \varepsilon \ell \big] >0 \Big\},
\end{align*}
where $\cI^i \coloneqq -\mathbf{1}_{\{i=1\}}+\mathbf{1}_{\{i=2\}}$. We say that $\hat\balpha$ is a {\rm zero-sum sub-game--perfect Nash equilibrium} if for any $\varepsilon >0$, it holds that $\ell_\varepsilon >0$. The set containing all {\rm zero-sum sub-game--perfect Nash equilibria} is denoted by $\mathcal{N}\!\mathcal{A}_{s,0}$.","status":"unverified"}

