{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.061319+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0720f5c1f2f347904241fe1963e641966c820e9ed0da909007c52469d10baa26","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:mild-skorohod of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] An $\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\ge 0}$-adapted random field $u=\{u(t,x), t\ge0, x\in {\mathbb R}^d\}$ is called a mild Skorohod solution to (equation eqn:DO process of the paper) if $\mathbf{E}[u^2(t,x)]<\infty$ for all $(t,x)\in {\mathbb R}_+\times {\mathbb R}^d$ and if it satisfies the following integral equation
\begin{equation}
u(t,x)=w(t,x)+\int_0^t \int_{{\mathbb R}^d} p_h (t-s,x-y) u(s,y) W(ds,dy). 
\end{equation}
where $w(t,x)$ is given in (equation e:h of the paper) for the heat equation. The stochastic integral on the right-hand side is a Skorohod integral as in (equation e:skorohod-int of the paper). In particular, it is implicitly assumed that for each $t\ge 0, x\in {\mathbb R}$, the process $v_{t,x}(s,y)=p_h(t-s,x-y) u(s,y) \mathbbm{1}_{[0,t]}(s)$ lies in Dom $\delta$. See equation~(equation e:duality of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

