{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.014058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_079f270bfeff39b3d99a5cfae29742314b125bcd60c916cfdb53876359450fbe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi Main2 of arXiv:2605.13666, 'When Does the Dice Sum Become Prime?'] Let $P_N(s)$ be the probability that 
the sum of the numbers rolled, starting at~$s$,
survives beyond~$N$, i.e., that the sum never hits an element of~$A$ until it is greater than~$N$. It can be calculated exactly by backward induction:

\begin{equation} 
P_N(s)  = 
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } s > N, \\
0, & \text{if } s \leq N \text{ and } s \in A,  \\
