Let $\langle \cdot \rangle_{\Lambda, \beta, N, B}$ denote expectation with respect to the measure $\mu_{\Lambda, \beta, N, B}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.923598+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07b8a8c7a77ab8576a6d11d0a8c6700c98a535a98f85e14be3c3efff9f0ba6d3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:multinomial_measures of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Let $G = (V,E)$ be a bipartite graph with 
 vertex multiplicities ${\mathbf N} = (N_v)_{v\in V}$. Given edge weights $(w_e)_{e\in E}$, the corresponding \textit{multinomial dimer measure} is the probability measure on $\Omega_{{\mathbf N}}$ where
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}(M) = \frac{\left|\pi^{-1}(M)\right|}{Z} \prod_{e\in M} w_e^{M_e}
\end{align*}
where $Z=Z_{G,{\mathbf N}}$ is the same partition function, given below. In other words, the multinomial dimer measure is the measure on $\Omega_{{\mathbf N}}$ which is the projection of the Boltzmann measure on dimer covers of $G_{{\mathbf N}}$ defined in (equation eq:lifted_measures of the paper). We also refer to the measure in (equation eq:lifted_measures of the paper) as the \textit{lifted} version of the multinomial dimer measure.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.538727+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07b915a24f945af7a72bd61a19efab657364ccc1bb1a00a71e9c45ab564ee845","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn The cut-off kernel of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] Given a kernel $J$ as above and a parameter $r>0$, we write $J_r$ for the \textbf{cut-off kernel}
\[
J_r(x,y) = \mathbbm{1}(x\neq y, \|x-y\|\leq r) \int_{\|x-y\|}^r |J'(s)| \dif s.
\]
and write $\mathbb P_{\beta,r},\mathbb E_{\beta,r}$ for probabilities and expectations taken with respect to the law of long-range percolation on $\mathbb Z^d$ with kernel $J_r$ at parameter $\beta$. We will always write $\beta_c$ for the critical parameter associated to the original kernel $J$, so that the cut-off measure $\mathbb P_{\beta_c,r}$ is subcritical at $\beta_c$ when $r<\infty$. (Indeed, for every $r<\infty$ there exists $\varepsilon=\varepsilon(r)>0$ such that $J_r(x,y)\leq (1-\varepsilon)J(x,y)$ for every $x\neq y$, so that $\mathbb P_{\beta_c,r}$ is stochastically dominated by the subcritical measure $\mathbb P_{(1-\varepsilon)\beta_c}$.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.758767+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07bc097b682ea604f109b456325f1b3ad6c5ec768070bf992c9de4e2d577e3cf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:moment-bound:H^m:d=3 of arXiv:2411.02459, 'Existence and higher regularity of statistically steady states for the stochastic Coleman-'] Let $d\le 3$ and assume the hypotheses of Theorem (thm:well-posed of the paper). Suppose further that \nameref{cond:phi:d=3}, \nameref{cond:Q:higher_regularity} hold for $m\ge 2$. Then for all $k=2,\dots,m$ and $n\ge 1$, there exist constants $c_{k,n}$, $C_{k,n}$ and integers $q_{k,n}$, independent of $U_0$, $t$ and $\mu\in\mathcal{M}_\delta$, such that
\begin{equation}  
\emph{d} \Psi_k(U(t))^n\le -c_{k,n} \Psi_k(U(t))^n\emph{d} t+C_{k,n}\emph{d} t+C_{k,n}\Psi_{k-1}(U(t))^{q_{k,n}}\emph{d} t+\emph{d} M_{k,n}(t),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} 
\mathbb{E}\Psi_k(U(t))^n \le C_{k,n}e^{-c_{k,n}t}\Psi_k(U_0)^{q_{k,n}}+C_{k,n},
\end{equation}
where $\Psi_{k}$ {is given as in} (equation form:Psi_m of the paper) and $M_{k,n}(t)$ is the semi-martingale given by
\begin{equation} 
M_{k,n}(t)=\int_0^t n\Psi_k(U(r))^{n-1}\langle A^{k/2}u(t),A^{k/2}Q\emph{d} w(r)\rangle_H.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, it holds that
\begin{equation} 
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbb{E} \Psi_m(U(t))^n+c_{m,n}\int_0^t \mathbb{E} \Psi_m(U(r))^{n-1}\big(\|A^{\frac{m+1}{2}}u(r)\|^2_H+\|\eta(r)\|^2_{M^{m}_\mu}\big)\emph{d} r\\
& \le C_{m,n}\Psi_m(U_0)^{q_{m,n}}+C_{m,n}t,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
and that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E} \exp\Big\{\beta_{m,0}\Psi_m(U(t))^{\gamma_{m,0}}\Big\}\le C_{m,0}\exp\Big\{C_{m,0}e^{-c_{m,0}t} \big(1+\Psi_m(U_0)^{q_{m,0}}\big)\Big\},
\end{equation}
for some positive constants $\gamma_{m,0}\in (0,1)$, $\beta_{m,0}$, $q_{m,0}$, $c_{m,0}$, $C_{m,0}$
also independent of $U_0$, $t$ and $\mu\in\mathcal{M}_\delta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

