{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.585324+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_080b2a3ce23bddbba7e4c829935ff093cee4c35470b453547be5f46fa2066ba2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 6 of arXiv:2608.16144, 'Central limit theorem in R\'enyi divergence for lattice random variables'] [Fourier transform on $a+h\mathbb{Z}$]
For $f\in \ell^1(a+h\mathbb{Z})$, its Fourier transform is defined by
\begin{equation*} 
\widehat f(t)=\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}} f(a+kh)e^{it(a+kh)},\quad t\in\left[-\frac{\pi}{h},\frac{\pi}{h}\right).
\end{equation*}
The Fourier inversion formula is given by
\begin{equation} 
f(a+kh)=
\frac{h}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi/h}^{\pi/h}\widehat f(t)e^{-it(a+kh)}\,dt.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

