{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.351230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_082bef5ca5c761c0457001b363386b23704b4d5dc4cfd0dc1ddcb0b2dbed3183","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Pro mark_bruit_gaussien of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Consider two intervals $T \subset \mathbb{R}$, $J \subset \mathbb{R}_+^*$, a Brownian motion $(B_u)_{u \geq 0}$ on a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ and a family $(k_t)_{t \in T}$ of elements of $L^2(J)$ such that $\int_{J} k_t(u)^2du = 1$  for every $t \in T$. Set $X_t := \int_J k_t(u) dB_u$ for every $t \in T.$
		The, the law $P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^T)$ of $(X_t)_{t \in T}$ is a Gaussian measure with covariance function $K : (s,t) \in T \times T \mapsto \int_J k_s(u)k_t(u) du$. Moreover, set $C_{s,t}(h^*) := \ens{(v,h) \in [s,t[ \times ]0,h^*]}{v+h \in [s,t]}$ for every $(s,t,h*) \in T \times T \times \mathbb{R}_+^*$ and assume:
		\vspace{0.2cm}
		\begin{description}
			\leftskip=0.3in
			\item[$(H_1)$ ]  $\exists f : T \times J \to \mathbb{R}, \forall (s,t,u) \in T  \times T \times J$,  
			\begin{equation}
				\sup_{(v,h) \in C_{s,t}(h^*)} |h^{-1}(k_v(u)- k_{v+h}(u)) - f(v,u)| \xrightarrow[ h^* \to 0^+]{} 0 \ ;
			\end{equation} 
			\item[$(H_2)$ ]   $\exists g \in L^1(J), \exists h_0 \in \mathbb{R}_+^*, \forall (h,t,u) \in ]0,h_0] \times T \times J,\left| h^{-1}(k_{t}(u)-k_{t+h}(u)) - f(t,u) \right| \leq g(u)$;
			\item[$(H_3)$ ]  $\alpha : t \in T \mapsto \int_J k_t(u)f(t,u)du$ is continuous.
		\end{description}
		\leftskip=0in
		Then $P_\alpha$ is the strong Markov transform of $P.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.615274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_082ca20756c4ee5d2ee09e5d0730faf9700782bd386d19a745606d4e8aced527","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:boot of arXiv:2412.05491, 'Near-critical and finite-size scaling for \\ high-dimensional lattice trees and animals'] Let $d > 8$.
