{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:21:56.739854+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0844cbb9b5f83b0105c547efdc82618d903400876859d21e76c59ca5fff694e5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm-parekh of arXiv:2507.19222, 'Convergence of the KMP model to the KPZ equation'] {parekh2}}]
 	The sequence  $(\mathfrak{h}^{N})_{N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant 0}}$ defined in (equation field-general of the paper) is tight with respect to the topology $C\left([0,T],\mathcal{S}^{'}(\mathbb{R})\right)$. Furthermore, any limit point as $N\to \infty$ lies on $C\left((0,T],C(\mathbb{R})\right)$ and coincides with the law of the It\=o solution to the multiplicative noise stochastic heat equation given by  
 	\begin{equation}
 		\begin{cases}
 			\partial_{t}\mathcal{Z}(t,x)=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{xx}\mathcal{Z}(t,x)+\gamma \;\mathcal{Z}(t,x)\Xi(t,x)\\
 			\mathcal{Z}(0,x)=\delta_{0}(x)
 		\end{cases}\,.
 	\end{equation}
 	Here, $\Xi$ is a standard white noise and the noise variance is given by 
