whenever $\|U_0^n-U_0\|_{\mathcal{H}^0_\mu}\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.959333+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a1875fcc31a8110f4f6db3e0cb56a3ac429f521aace3fc14a7b78e1459a349","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 25 of arXiv:2512.02286, 'Pfaffian Transition Probability and Correlation Kernel of TASEP in Half-space'] Let $N$ be a fixed even integer. Let $L:\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}\times\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}\to\mathbb{C}^{2\times 2}$ be defined as follows:
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
		L(\virst_i,\virst_j)&\,=\,\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			1 & 0 \\
			0 & 0
		\end{array}\right]\mathbbm{1}_{i+1=j}
		+\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			-1 & 0 \\
			0 & 0
		\end{array}\right]\mathbbm{1}_{j+1=i},\\
		L(\virst_i,(j,x_2))&\,=\,\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			0 & 0 \\
			\phi_{i}(\virst_i,x_2) & 0
		\end{array}\right]\mathbbm{1}_{i=j},\\
		L((i,x_1),\virst_j)&\,=\,-L(\virst_j,(i,x_1))^T,\\
		L((i,x_1),(j,x_2))&\,=\,\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			0 & 0 \\
			\phi_{i}(x_1,x_2) & 0
		\end{array}\right]\mathbbm{1}_{i+1=j<N}+\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			0 & \phi_{j}(x_2,x_1) \\
			0 & 0
		\end{array}\right]\mathbbm{1}_{j+1=i<N} +\left[\begin{array}{cc}
			0 & 0 \\
			0 & \Psi(x_1,x_2)
		\end{array}\right]\mathbbm{1}_{i=j=N},\hspace{-40pt}
	\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	for $1\leq i,j\leq N$ and $x_1,x_2\in\mathbb{N}$.
        Then for $A\subset \mathcal{X}$ we have
	\[\Pf\left[L\big|_{\mathcal{V}\cup A}\right]=\begin{dcases*}
		\mathcal{W}_{N,0}(A|\emptyset) & if $A\in\Lambda_N$,\\
		0 & otherwise.
	\end{dcases*}\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.776477+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a3eb62524cd053b6bb9def60c4ca663ed57cb125214a3f4db18e9f5bc640ec","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop shuffling-general-m of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] Let $(\mathbb{X},\mathcal X)$ be a measurable space.
	Let $\psi$ be a measurable $\mathbb{X}$-shuffling rule, and let $({\mathfrak{m}}_T)_{T\in\trees}$ be a collection  of finite measures on $\mathbb{X}^T$ respectively.
	If $\psi$ is $\mathfrak{S}$-equivariant  and $({\mathfrak{m}}_T)_{T\in\trees}$ is $\mathfrak{S}$-consistent, then the measure ${\mathfrak{m}}$ on $\trees{[\mathbb{X}]}$ given by
	\begin{align}
	{\mathfrak{m}}(\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}T,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\boldsymbol{x}})=\sum_{T_0\in\trees}\delta_{T_0}(\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}T)\,{\mathfrak{m}}_{T_0}(\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\boldsymbol{x}})
	\end{align}
	is invariant
	\footnote{
		We say that a measure $m$ is invariant under $f$ if we have $f_\#\,m=m$.
	}
	under the mapping $F^\psi\colon(T,{\boldsymbol{x}})\mapsto \bigl(({\boldsymbol{\sigma}}^\psi_{T,{\boldsymbol{x}}})\cdotT,({\boldsymbol{\sigma}}^\psi_{T,{\boldsymbol{x}}})_*\,{\boldsymbol{x}}\bigr)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.363310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a5ef8f74bb6a7cef3bd4ba3546d9a4f51a2403012a62b961504c7cda779404","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop lem:distancebetweencrossings of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] Consider percolation on some annulus $A(x;n^{-1}R^{\kappa-f},n^{-1}R^{\kappa-f+1})\subseteq \Lambda_n$. Let $\phi,\{v_i\}_i$ be the loop with defects corresponding to the annulus $A(x;2n^{-1}R^{\kappa-f},4n^{-1}R^{\kappa-f}) \subseteq \Lambda_n$, as defined in Lemma~(lem:decompositioncrossings of the paper). Furthermore, consider the event $F=F(A(x;n^{-1}R^{\kappa-f},n^{-1}R^{\kappa-f+1}))$ defined as above with parameters $M_1,a_1,b_1,c_1,M_2,a_2,b_2,c_2,\xi$. Let $0\leq s\leq 1$. Define $G=G(x,s,f)$ to be the event that $F$ happens and that for every $v_i,v_j$ that are connected inside $\intt\phi$, the (graph resp.\ effective resistance) distance between them in $\operatorname{int} \phi$ is at most $s\mathbf{q}_n$.

Let $\alpha$ be the exponent of the a priori estimate. Then for all $\zeta>0$, there exists a constant $C=C(\alpha,\zeta,R,M_1,a_1,b_1,c_1,M_2,a_2,b_2,c_2,\xi)$ such that for all $R$ sufficiently large depending on $\zeta$, and $p$ small enough depending on $R$,
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{P}(G^{c}\mid F)\leq Cs^{-(\frac{4}{3}+\zeta)\alpha} R^{-\alpha f},
\end{equation} for all levels $f$ such that $\xi\frac{c_1c_2}{8}\cdot R^{\kappa-f-1}\geq R$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.680281+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a60cda537ebc5baedea1761e1a435b7ac0812db8bf75691415271566482cd3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2607.25986, 'Asymptotics of a two-species particle system associated to the doubly parabolic Keller-Seg'] Fix $\alpha>0$ and $\gamma\in (\frac 32,2)$. Assume that $\chi>0$ and $\theta>0$ are such that
$C_1(\alpha,\gamma)>0$ and $C_1(\alpha,\gamma)>\chi C_2(\theta,\alpha,\gamma)$.
For all $T>0$, all $\sigma\in (0,\frac 12]$ and all $\zeta>0$, there exists a constant $A_{\sigma,\zeta,T}$
(also depending on $\chi,\theta,c_0,\gamma,\alpha$) such that if $(N,M,\e)\in \Aa_\sigma$, then for
all $u \in [0,T]$,
\begin{align*}
&\E\Big[ \int_0^u \int_0^t \int_{\R^2}\frac{1}{(t-s + \alpha |R^{1,2}_{t,s}-\sqrt\e y|^2)^{\gamma}} \phi(y)\dd y\dd s\dd t \Big]\\
\leq& \frac{(1+\zeta)\alpha^{1-\gamma}}{C_1(\alpha,\gamma) -\chi C_2(\theta,\alpha,\gamma)} 
\E\Big[ \int_0^u \int_{\R^2} \frac{1}{|R_{s,s}^{1,2}-\sqrt\e y|^{2(\gamma-1)}}\phi(y)\dd y \dd s\Big] + A_{\sigma,\zeta,T}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:52:51.769680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a714645a0f63e3308793746deef440978a8a996ca6f4369a1cec42a9b8cdd5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm vertex of arXiv:2605.03151, 'Giants through Higher-Order paths in Random Simplicial Complexes'] Let $X_n \sim \LM_d(n, \lambda/n)$. If $\lambda < 1/d$, then
