{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:55.644794+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a970bb18ca63e4c8ddcd14c3fd7684fe50a1bdaef60d0d707dcc3260e01177","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 25 of arXiv:2605.24445, 'Matrix concentration inequalities for time-inhomogeneous Markov chains'] \label[definition]{def:sigma}
Let \(t\ge 1\). Since \(\mu_t=\mu_{t-1}P_t\), the Markov operator $P_t$
maps \(L^2(\mu_t)\) to \(L^2(\mu_{t-1})\). Define
the \emph{one-step (second) singular value}
\[
\sigma_t
:=\|P_t\|_{L^2_0(\mu_t)\to L^2_0(\mu_{t-1})}
=\sup\Big\{\|P_t f\|_{2,\mu_{t-1}}:\ \|f\|_{2,\mu_t}=1,\ \mu_t(f)=0\Big\}.
\]
Equivalently, for all \(f\in L^2(\mu_t)\) with \(\mu_t(f)=0\),
\begin{equation}
\|P_t f\|_{2,\mu_{t-1}}\le \sigma_t\,\|f\|_{2,\mu_t}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.236396+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08ac11c52ee09628727698b8ab32fec0e33a0c9e2d6e278af28ff4c3a5d07802","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 93 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] Fix a grounded enumeration $g_0,g_1,\dots$ of $\G$, let $F_n := \{g_0,g_1,\dots,g_n\}$ for each $n$, and assume that $F_{n+1}$ is an enlargement of $F_n$ in direction $s_n$.  Given $\phi \in \S^\circ_k(\G)$, let $C_n$ be the Verblunsky coefficient of $\phi$ from $F_n$ to $F_{n+1}$.  Then the map
\begin{equation}
	\S^\circ_k(\G) \to \prod_{n\ge 0}\Xi^\circ(k,k|F_n\setminus s_nF_n|):\phi\mapsto (C_0,C_1,\dots)
	\end{equation}
	is a bijection.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.231170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08aee436c120cab6cba3e5666a5720d2b855740ab73cf6531ed99c52ad596a0a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 25 ({\cite[Theorem 1.7 and Remark 1.10) of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] {DV:LongestSub}}]
On the full-space $\R^{4}_{\uparrow}$, we have convergence of the LPP landscape $\LLeps$  to the directed landscape $\mathcal{L}$  (Definition \ref{def:DirectedLandscape}), i.e., as $\varepsilon \rightarrow 0$ 
\begin{equation}
\big(\LLeps(x,s\tilde{\omega}^{-1};y,t\tilde{\omega}^{-1})\big)_{(x,s;y,t) \in \R^{4}_{\uparrow}} \rightarrow \big(\mathcal{L}(x,s;y, t)\big)_{(x,s;y,t) \in \R^{4}_{\uparrow}}
