{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.368728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08f2722690b2c48c9992786daa8c93d8803f2685af30599b178d2df8614e130e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition best strategy with feedback of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] Let $\mathbf{G}=\{g_j\}_{j=1}^n$ be the following process.

    i.) $g_1=1$ a.s.

    ii.) $g_{j+1}=r_j +1$, $\tau>j$. If $r_j=n-2$, then $g_{j+1}=n-1$ or $n$.

    iii.) If $\tau=\tau^{n-1}$, then $g_{\tau+1}=n$ a.s. and $g_{\tau+m+1}=K_{\tau}^m$ a.s. Otherwise, if $\tau=\tau^{n}$, then $g_{\tau+m}=K_{\tau}^m$ a.s.
    Then, $\mathbf{G}$ maximizes $F^G$ for every $n$. 
    \begin{proof}
        For the first card, we resort to the no-feedback case. This proves i.)
        By Proposition $(properties of r of the paper)$ i.), we have 
        \begin{equation}
           P(r_{j+1}=r_j+1\,|\,r_j,\,\tau>j)=\frac{1}{2}
        \end{equation}
        for every $1\leq j\leq n-2$. Applying a pigeonhole argument as in Proposition $(first card of the paper)$, we deduce ii.). Part iii.) follows from Proposition $(properties of r of the paper)$ iii.), as the probabilities are all $1$ and thus must be the unique maxima.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

