{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:53:54.887964+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08fa2fbcdaa47df4e44ea5c1d89256aaf52b51490edc087fd115c7fdebc5c129","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2607.17087, 'Transportation-cost inequalities for invariant measures of stochastic reaction-diffusion e'] We say that a measure $\mu$ on $(X,d)$ satisfies the $L^p$-transportation-cost inequality $T_p(C)$ if there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for all probability measures $\nu$ on $(X,d)$,
\begin{equation}
 W_p(\nu, \mu)\leq \sqrt{2C H(\nu| \mu)}.
 \end{equation}
The case $p=2$ is referred to as the quadratic transportation-cost inequality, also called the Talagrand inequality.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.595661+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08fe43059a9ccb62305bdc6b4870950ca4a0994af4c1bb481f6008fa97d8bfda","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem intro:thm:asympt of arXiv:2606.06187, 'How to Study Reflected Brownian Motion in a Quadrant via Kernel Functional Equations?\\ \m'] Let $e_\alpha=( \cos \alpha, \sin \alpha)$ with $\alpha_0 \in (0, \pi/2)$. 
