For $d=1$ the last condition is vacuous. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.793038+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093c3010fe6c075c03fa08de2ae6db1e66a7e9f7bf3e4ef2f3fc08d9534ed549","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.mu of arXiv:2605.11785, 'Regularization of a mean-field SDE by an additive common noise: the conditional expectatio'] There exists a Borel measurable map $\mu:[0,T]\times {\mathcal C}\to \mathcal P_2(\mathbb{R}^d)$ such that, for ${\mathcal W}^{\overline\mu_0}-$a.e. ${\bm{x}}\in {\mathcal C}$, $\mu^{{\bm{x}}} \in C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$ is the unique solution in the sense of distributions to 
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \mu^{{\bm{x}}}_t - \frac{1}{2} \Delta \mu^{{\bm{x}}}_t +{\rm div}\Bigl(\mu^{{\bm{x}}}_t \Bigl( b(y+{\bm{x}}_t, \bm{x}_t)-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} b(z+{\bm{x}}_t,\bm{x}_t)\mu^{\bm{x}}_t(dz)\Bigr)\Bigr) =0
\end{equation}
with initial condition $\mu^{\bm{x}}_0(dy)= (Id- \overline\mu_0)\sharp \mu_0$.  In addition, the map $\bm{x} \to \mu^{\bm{x}}$ is nonanticipating: 
$$
\mbox{\rm for ${\mathcal W}^{\overline\mu_0}-$a.e. $\bm{x}, \bm{x}' \in \mathcal C$, $\bm{x}=\bm{x}'$ on $[0,t]$ implies $\mu^{\bm{x}}_t=\mu^{\bm{x}'}_t$ on $[0,t]$.}
$$
 Moreover this solution is stable with respect to $b$: namely, if $(b^n)$ is a sequence of Lipschitz maps from $\mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbb{R}^d$ into $\mathbb{R}^d$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup_n \|b^n\|_\infty < \infty,\qquad b^n(x,y)\to b(x,y) \;\mbox{\rm for $dx\otimes dy$ a.e. $(x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbb{R}^d$},
\end{equation}
then for ${\mathcal W}^{\overline\mu_0}-$a.e. ${\bm{x}}\in {\mathcal C}$, the solution  $\mu^{n,\bm{x}}\in C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$ in the sense of distributions to 
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \mu^{n,{\bm{x}}}_t - \frac{1}{2} \Delta \mu^{n,{\bm{x}}}_t +{\rm div}\Bigl(\mu^{n,{\bm{x}}}_t \Bigl( b^n(y+{\bm{x}}_t, \bm{x}_t)-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} b^n(z+{\bm{x}}_t, \bm{x}_t)\mu^{n,\bm{x}}_t(dz)\Bigr)\Bigr) =0
\end{equation}
with initial condition $\mu^{n,\bm{x}}_0( dy)= (Id- \overline\mu_0)\sharp \mu_0$, is unique,  has a density which is  locally bounded and continuous on $(0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^d$, and converges to $\mu^{\bm{x}}$ in $C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$, and weakly in $L^p([\epsilon,T]\times B_R)$ for any $\epsilon,R>0$ and any $p\in (1, \infty)$. Finally, for any $n$, the map $\bm{x} \to \mu^{n,\bm{x}}$ is continuous from $\mathcal C$ to $C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.764666+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093c868d2389040455a4757e2e0d280d37132c60b46f0b1acd9bdb20b451942d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary c2 of arXiv:2411.19433, 'Singular mean-field backward stochastic Volterra integral equations in infinite dimensiona'] Let condition $(A_{2})$ hold. Then, for any \( \Psi(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}_b}(\delta, b; H) \), singular MF-BSVIE (equation 3.7 of the paper) admits a unique adapted M-solution

\[
(X(\cdot), \aleph(\cdot, \cdot)) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(\delta, b; H) \times L^2(\delta, b; L^2_{\mathcal{F}}(\delta, b; L_0^2)).
\]

Moreover, if \( \bar{\eta} \) also satisfies $(A_{2})$, \( \bar{\Psi}(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}_T}(\delta, b; H) \), and \( (X(\cdot), \aleph(\cdot, \cdot)) \) is the unique adapted M-solution of (equation 3.7 of the paper) with \( (\eta, \Psi) \) replaced by \( (\bar{\eta}, \bar{\Psi}) \), then

\begin{align}
&\quad \mathbb{E} \left[ \sup_{t \in [\delta, b]} \vert X(t) - \bar{X}(t)\vert_H^2 + \int_{\delta}^b \vert \aleph(t, s) - \bar{\aleph}(t, s)\vert_{L_0^2}^2 \, ds \right] \nonumber\\
&\leq C \mathbb{E} \left[ \vert \Psi(t) - \bar{\Psi}(t) \vert_H^2 + \int_t^b \vert \eta(t, s, \aleph(t, s),\mathbb{E}\aleph(t, s)) - \bar{\eta}(t, s, \aleph(t, s),\mathbb{E}\aleph(t, s)) \vert_H^2 \, ds \right],
\end{align}
for \( t \in [\delta, b] \), where \( C \) is a constant depending on the problem's parameters. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.481002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093dda0a9a837d3545bebdbe0835db29c98c3d9a2de882d5526a2b419c100048","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] A measurable space is a set $X$, given with a set of subsets $M\subset P(X)$, called measurable sets, which form an algebra, in the sense that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\emptyset,X\in M$.

\item $E\in M\implies E^c\in M$.

\item $M$ is stable under countable unions and intersections.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.121259+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093e39213404e9f842fe689cd37911d304b58368cf93cfe52426a26245ab0f30","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:point_measure_restricted_to_hit_B_but_not_the_closure_of_A of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Given a point measure $\mathcal{X} = \sum_{i \in I} \delta_{(x,H)_i} \in \mathbf{Z}$, some $R \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$, a finite connected set $A \subseteq V(G)$ and a subset of its exterior boundary $B \subseteq \partial_{\mathrm{ext}} A$ let us introduce the following restriction of the point measure:
	\begin{equation}

		\check{ \mathcal{X} }_{A,B}^R :=
		\mathcal{X} \, \mathds{1} \left[
		Z^R(B) \setminus Z^R \left( \overline{A} \setminus B \right)
		\right].
	\end{equation}
	That is, we consider those animals only that have volume at most $R$, hit $B$, but do not intersect $\overline{A}$ otherwise. Let us also introduce the notation 
	\begin{equation}

		\check{\mu}_{A,B}^R (x) := \mu^{ \check{ \mathcal{X} }_{A,B}^R }(x)
		\qquad\text{and}\qquad 
		\check{ \Sigma }_{A,B}^R := \Sigma^{ \check{ \mathcal{X} }_{A,B}^R } = \sum_{x \in V(G)} \check{\mu}_{A,B}^R (x)
	\end{equation}






	for the (total) occupation measure of the restricted point measure.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.752075+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093e5a857f7d0c1da84fa7ecb879a529700223bb851f625af4748c4018ebaa5e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 26 of arXiv:2511.02725, 'Mixing of general biased adjacent transposition chains'] Let $Y^{(k)}$ be drawn from the $k$-particle $q$-ASEP stationary distribution with $q> \rc 2$ as in~\eqref{eq:q-eps-relation} ($\fc{q}{1-q}=1+\ep$). Then for every $k\le n$, for $r \ge C_q$,        \begin{align*}
            \nu^{(k)}_{[n]}( \max \{v: Y^{(k)}(v) =1\} \ge k + r) 
