{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.719990+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0964760519239d682466ee7d3584f613e26832a17b46b66a9f55d3180a5eee63","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2609.01190, 'Rough differential equations on manifolds via natural bundles'] Let $n\in\Nsf$, $p\in [1,3)$, and let $\omega$ be a control function on $[S,T]$. An \emph{$n$-dimensional branched rough path on $[S,T]$ with regularity $p$ and control $\omega$} is a tuple $X=(X^\tau)$ consisting of functions $X^\tau: \Delta[S,T]\to\Rsf$ (for $\tau\in\Fcal_n^2$) such that the equalities
	\begin{align}
		X^{\ta}(s,t) &= X^{\ta}(s,u) + X^{\ta}(u,t),

		\\
		X^{\tab}(s,t) & = X^{\ta}(s,t)X^{\tb}(s,t)

		\\
		X^{\tc}(s,t) & = X^{\tc}(s,u) + X^{\tc}(u,t) + X^{\ta}(s,u)X^{\tb}(u,t),

	\end{align}
hold for for every $\alpha,\beta\in\llbracket 1,n \rrbracket$ and every $(s,u,t)\in [S,T]^3$ satisfying $s\leq u\leq t$ and such that there exists a constant $C\in (0,\infty)$ such that the inequalities
	\begin{equation}

		\left|X^{\ta}(s,t)\right| \leq C \omega(s,t)^\frac{1}{p},
		\quad 
		\left|X^{\tab}(s,t)\right| \leq C \omega(s,t)^\frac{2}{p}, 
		\quad\mbox{and}\quad 
		\left|X^{\tc}(s,t)\right| \leq C \omega(s,t)^\frac{2}{p}
	\end{equation}
hold for for all $\alpha,\beta\in\llbracket 1,n \rrbracket$ and $(s,t)\in\Delta[S,T]$. The least such $C$ is denoted by the symbol $\|X\|$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.326349+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0965964ffd4911d0d7208c862081756a084e965544b36cc0021597fcbec246aa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma cofibrant-G-gl-are-flat of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Every orthogonal $G$-spectrum that is cofibrant in the $G$-global level model structure is flat in the sense of Definition~(def:flat of the paper).
    \begin{proof}
        Consider the class $\mathscr F$ of \emph{flat cofibrations}, i.e.\ maps $f\colon X\to Y$ such that the induced map $X({\mathbb{R}}^n)\amalg_{L_nX}L_nY\to Y({\mathbb{R}}^n)$ is an $({\textup{O}}(n)\times G)$-equivariant cofibration for all $n\ge0$, where $L_nX, L_nY$ denote the nth latching objects of $X$ and $Y$ as defined in Definition~(def:flat of the paper). We will show that $\mathscr F$ contains all $G$-global cofibrations, which will then in particular imply the lemma.        

        For this we observe that $\mathscr F$ is closed under pushout, transfinite composition, and retracts: for example, using that both evaluation at ${\mathbb{R}}^n$ and the restriction functor $i^*$ from the previous construction also have \emph{right} adjoints, one can identify $\mathscr F$ with the class of maps having the right lifting property against a certain class of maps. It therefore suffices to show that the generating cofibrations are contained in $\mathscr F$.

        As $\textbf{\textup{O}}_{\le n-1}\hookrightarrow\textbf{\textup{O}}_{\le n}$ is fully faithful, the counit $\varepsilon\colon{\textup{sk}}^{n-1}X\to X$ is an isomorphism if $X$ is left Kan extended from $\textbf{\textup{O}}_{\le n-1}$ and in particular for $X=\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp_\varphi G_+\smashp K_+$ with $\dim(V)<n$. Thus, also the latching map is an isomorphism; we conclude that for the generating cofibration \[X=\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp_\varphi G_+\smashp\partial D^m\hookrightarrow \textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp_\varphi G_+\smashp D^m=Y,\] the map $X({\mathbb{R}}^n)\amalg_{L_nX} L_nY\to Y({\mathbb{R}}^n)$ is an isomorphism, and hence in particular an equivariant cofibration, whenever $n>\dim(V)$. If $n\le\dim(V)$ on the other hand, then ${\textup{sk}}^{n-1}\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)=0$, and hence $L_n\big(\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,-)\smashp_HG_+\smashp K\big)=0$. For $X\to Y$ the generating cofibration from before, we can then identify $X({\mathbb{R}}^n)\amalg_{L_nX}L_nY\to Y({\mathbb{R}}^n)$ with $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,{\mathbb{R}}^n)\smashp_\varphi G_+\smashp(\partial D^m\hookrightarrow D^m)$. If $n<\dim(V)$, both sides are zero, so this is even an isomorphism; on the other hand, for $n=\dim(V)$, this can be further identified via Lemma~(lemma:O-mapping-spaces of the paper) with $({\textup{O}}(n)\times G)_+/\Gamma_{H,\varphi}\smashp(\partial D^m\hookrightarrow D^m)_+$, which is one of the generating cofibrations.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

