{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:48:00.297123+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0a673a74831d36857f9d39b3edc8fa0fdb9404fcbbc0adc47b33afd468d79bcc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem graph_averaged_labels_equal_virtual_McShane_Whitney of arXiv:2609.03129, 'A Closed-Form Formula for Consistent Lipschitz Regression on Metric Spaces with Sparse Neu'] Let $(X,\rho)$ be a metric space, let
$N_0,N_1\in\mathbb{N}_+$, and set $N\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}} N_0+N_1$.  Let $
    \mathbb{D}_N
\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    ((X_n,Y_n))_{n=1}^{N}
    \in
    (X\times\mathbb{R})^N$, fix $\beta>0$, $q\in[N_1]_+$, and $L>0$.  For each
$i\in[N_1]_+$ and $k\in[N_0]_+$, define
\[
\begin{aligned}
    H_{ik}^{(\beta)}
    & \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{
        \rho(X_{N_0+i},X_k)\le\beta
    \}},
\,\,
    S_k^{(\beta)}
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \sum_{i=1}^{N_1}H_{ik}^{(\beta)}
,\,\,
    R_{ik}^{(\beta)}
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \sum_{\ell=1}^{i}H_{\ell k}^{(\beta)},
\\
    A_{ik}^{(\beta,q)}
    & \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{S_k^{(\beta)}\ge q\}}
    H_{ik}^{(\beta)}
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{R_{ik}^{(\beta)}\le q\}}
,\,\,
    G^{(\beta,q)}
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \frac{1}{q}A^{(\beta,q)}
\\a^{\operatorname{av}}
    & \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    (Y_{N_0+1},\ldots,Y_{N_0+N_1})
,\,\,
\mbox{ and }
    V^{(\beta,q)}
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    a^{\operatorname{av}}G^{(\beta,q)}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Let
$
    ((X_k,\overline Y_k))_{k=1}^{N_0}
$
be the output of
Algorithm~(alg:LocalAveragingRobustification of the paper) with inputs
$(\mathbb{D}_N,\beta,q)$.  Then
\begin{equation}

    \overline Y_k
    =
    V_k^{(\beta,q)}
    =
    \left(
        a^{\operatorname{av}}
        G^{(\beta,q)}
    \right)_k
\end{equation}
for every $k\in[N_0]_+$.  Consequently, if
$
    \theta^{(\beta,q,L)}
\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \left(
        \left(
            V_k^{(\beta,q)},X_k,L
        \right)
    \right)_{k=1}^{N_0}
$, then
\begin{equation}

    \hat{f}_{\mathbb{D}_N}^{(\beta,q,L)}(x)
    =
    \hat{f}_{\theta^{(\beta,q,L)}}(x)
\end{equation}
for each $x\in \mathcal{X}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.661424+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0a686e3ff06a08653a66b5dcbc78a5d338dff36649e3a4b00a45d562179ef33f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop eq:switching of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] For any set of vertices $A\subset D_\delta\backslash \partial D_{\delta}$,
