{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.645133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0ad0f3e9ba5e4df534a4e5f6fb65f63ace8cf191ad307044e1bdcff377621181","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm ES-XOR of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] {spin-perc-height}}, {[DRC-1]}]
    Let $D$ be a Jordan domain with discrete domain approximation $D_\delta\subset \delta \mathbb Z^2$. The XOR-Ising model $\tau_\delta$ with free/free boundary conditions in $D_\delta$ can be written as 
    \begin{align*}
        \tau_\delta = \sum_{k=1}^{N} \xi_{k}^\delta \mu_{k}^\delta,
    \end{align*}
    where $(\mu_k^\delta)_{k=1}^N$ is a collection of measures with pairwise disjoint supports $(C_k^\delta)_{k=1}^N$, and $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^N$ are i.i.d. symmetric signs. 

    \noindent The same statement holds under $+/+$ boundary conditions by adding a symmetry-breaking (positive) measure $\mu_0^\delta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.986224+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0ad16c85dad0de47bab8570d52264dc7b6ff4cc208cf928d7ef02a150ad7c345","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 19 (Packetwise spatial primitive--Fock realization) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] Let $F$ be a finite packet in
$\bigoplus_{q\le Q}\cW_q(E)$ and let $\cS(F)$ be its minimal primitive
support.  Apply
Theorem~\ref{primitive:thm:separable-realization} to $\cS(F)$.  Then:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
 \item for every $q\le Q$, labeled primitive evaluation extends to a
 unitary
 \begin{equation}
  \mathbb U_q^{\mathrm{sp}}:
  \cW_q(\cS(F))\xrightarrow{\ \simeq\ }\cA_q^{\mathrm{sp}},

 \end{equation}
 where $\cA_q^{\mathrm{sp}}$ is the closed graded subsystem generated by
 the replica fibres;
 \item the primitive sector of this minimal subsystem is exactly
 \begin{equation}
  \cK_s(\cA^{\mathrm{sp}})=J_s\cS_s(F);

