{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:50:36.757256+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0b7635e8f8fb332b380fd36d3ebb5df178a8ec4aa14641cdd06c7c51d86d60ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 40 of arXiv:2609.04153, '\vspace*{-3cm}Smooth autonomous fast dynamo action on the three-torus'] Consider the class of operators of the form
\begin{equation}

\mathcal A_{\lambda,\eps}=\partial_t-\eps (a^{0,0}\partial_t^2+2a^{0,j}\partial_t \partial_j +a^{i,j}\partial_i \partial_j+H^0\partial_t+H^j \partial_j +H),
\end{equation}
where $\partial_j , j =1,2$ denotes differentiation in $x \in \TT^2$, and the coefficients satisfy the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The coefficients $a^{i,j}$, $i,j=0,1,2$ are real, symmetric, scalar functions, which depend smoothly on $x,t \in \TT^2 \times [0,T]$, and are independent of $\eps, \lambda$.
    \item There exists a constant $\Lambda_0>0$ independent of $x,t, \eps, \lambda$ so that the hermitian matrix $A=(a^{i,j})_{i,j}$ is bounded below by $\Lambda_0$.
    \item The coefficients $H^j$, $j=0,1,2$, $H$ are matrix valued, and depend smoothly on $x,t, \lambda \in \TT^2 \times [0,T] \times \CC$.
\end{enumerate}
Then, for any compact $K \subset \CC$, and any $T>m$, there exists $C_m>0$ independent of $\eps,\lambda \in K$ so that if $\mathcal A_{\lambda,\eps} B=0$, satisfying the boundary conditions $B(x,y,0)=B_0(x,y)$, $B(x,y,T)=B_T(x,y)$, with $B_0, B_T \in C^\infty(\TT^2)$, there holds 
\begin{align}
&\|B(x,y,m)\|_{\mathcal X} \leq (1+C_m\eps^{\frac{1-2r-\delta}{2}})\|B_0\|_{\mathcal X}+C_m\|B_0\|_{\mathcal Y}+C\e^{-1/C\eps^{-1}}\|B_T\|_{L^2},\\
& \|B(x,y,m)\|_{\mathcal Y} \leq C_m\|B_0\|_{\mathcal Y}+C_m\e^{-1/C\eps^{-1}}\|B_T\|_{L^2},\\
&\|B(x,y,m)-B_0(x,y)\|_{\mathcal Y} \leq C_m(\eps^{\frac{1-2r}{2}}+\eps^{\frac{\delta}{2}})\|B_0\|_{\mathcal X}+C_m\e^{-1/C\eps^{-1}}\|B_T\|_{L^2}.
\end{align}
Furthermore, by density, these bounds may be extended to $B_0 \in \mathcal{X}$ $B_0 \in \mathcal{Y}$, and $B_T \in L^2(\TT^2)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

