{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.331143+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0ba087e1a381072d3ece3db6a3b6cca421ae3f18461f4d95bf96f1f8a091541e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorema them:mimicking_intro of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ denote a Gaussian process with  continuous covariance function $K$ and positive variance function. Assume $\alpha^K$ (recall (equation eq:IDR of the paper))  is well defined and continuous. 		
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item \emph{Existence:} There exists a Gaussian process $Y =(Y_t)_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ with covariance function $K'$ satisfying:
			\begin{itemize}
				\leftskip=0.3in
				\item[(1)] For every $t \in \mathbb{R}$, $\text{Law}(X_t) = \text{Law}(Y_t)$;
				\item[(2)] The process $Y$ has the same instantaneous decorrelation rate as $X$, \textnormal{i.e.} , \  \[\lim_{h \to 0^+} \frac{1}{h}\left(1 - \frac{K'(t,t+h)}{\sqrt{K'(t,t)}\sqrt{K'(t+h,t+h)}} \right)= \alpha^{K}(t);\] 
				\item[(3)] $(Y_t)_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ is a Markov process.
			\end{itemize}

			In the following of the article, if a Gaussian process $Y$ satisfies $(1),(2)$ and $(3)$, we allow ourselves to say that $Y$ is a \emph{mimicking process} of $X.$
			\item \emph{Uniqueness in law:} Every mimicking process of $X$ with covariance function $K' :\mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ has the same mean function as $(X_t)_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ and, for every $s<t \in \mathbb{R}^2$,
			\begin{equation*}
				K'(s,t) = K(s,s)^{1/2}K(t,t)^{1/2} \exp\left(-\int_s^t \alpha^K(u) du\right).
			\end{equation*}

			\item \emph{The mimicking process is a Markov transform (under the following reinforced hypothesis):} Assume 
			\begin{equation}
				\sup_{v \in [s,t]} \left| \alpha^K(v) - \frac{1}{h}\left(1 - \frac{K(v,v+h)}{\sqrt{K(v,v)}\sqrt{K(v+h,v+h)}} \right) \right| \xrightarrow[h \to 0^+]{} 0
			\end{equation} for every $s<t \in \mathbb{R}^2$. Let  $(R_n)_{n \geq 1} \in \mathcal{A}$ be an admissible sequence and $Y$ be the mimicking process of $X$ (see Point $1$ and $2$). For every $n \geq 1$, we denote by $X^{R_n}$ the transformation of $X$ made Markov at times $R_n$. Then $(X^{R_n})_{n\geq 1}$ and $Y$ almost surely have continuous paths and $X^{R_n}$ converges weakly to $Y$ on compact sets.
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

