{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.275408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bdb0c82ca5790470827ddf1b3924f4b1d6dd4af2afb3a2acdfc216237d51356","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2604.07579, 'Topology of Percolation Clusters: Central Limit Theorems beyond the Lattice'] Let $G$ be the Cayley graph of an amenable group $\Gamma$
    admitting a finite-index left-orderable subgroup $H$, with
    $n=[\Gamma:H]$. Consider Bernoulli bond percolation on $E(G)$ with parameter
    $p\in(0,1)$ and an exhaustive F{\o}lner sequence $(A_{r})$. Fix a local
    graph simplicial complex rule $\Delta$, and let $\beta_{n}^{r}$ denote the
    $n$-th Betti number of the simplicial complex $\Delta(G(\omega;A_{r}))$.

    Let $\preccurlyeq$ and $(\mathcal{F}_{e})_{e\in E(G)}$ be as in Theorem~\ref{TLC_func}.
    If $\beta_{n}^{r}>0$ a.s.\ for some $r$, then there exists a finite $H$-fundamental
    set of edges $\tilde{E}\subset E(G)$ such that:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item $\displaystyle \lim_{r\to\infty}\frac{1}{|A_{r}|}\Var_{p}(\beta
        _{n}^{r})=\sigma^{2}$;

      \item $\displaystyle |A_{r}|^{-1/2}\Bigl(\beta_{n}^{r}-\E_{p}[\beta_{n}
        ^{r}]\Bigr)\ \overset{d}{\Longrightarrow}\ \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}
        )$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Moreover, the asymptotic variance is given by
    \[
      \sigma^{2}=\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{e\in\tilde{E}}\E_{p}\!\left[\E_{p}\!\left
      ( D_{e}^{\infty}\,\middle|\, \mathcal{F}_{e}\right)^{2}\right ],
    \]
    where $D_{e}^{\infty}$ is the limit in probability, as $r\to\infty$, of
    the difference in $\beta_{n}^{r}$ caused by perturbing the state of $e$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.752398+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bdea1a12a6f1fcec1b06070dcae2fe31897ce352bfad1a3f478cb0aaf6116b6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2604.11682, 'Semilocalization for inhomogeneous random graphs'] We define the strict order relation $\prec$ on the set of vertices $[N]$ as
  follows. For any two vertices $x, y \in [N]$,
  \begin{equation*}
    x \prec y \text{ if and only if } \Bigl( (D_{x} < D_{y}) \text{ or } (D_{x} = D_{y} \text{ and } x > y) \Bigr)\,.
  \end{equation*}

  We define the (random) permutation $\pi \in \Sym_{N}$ to be the
  unique permutation such that
  \begin{equation*}
    \pi(N) \prec \pi(N-1) \prec \cdots \prec \pi(2) \prec \pi(1)\,.
  \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:00:15.221159+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bdf2840ad136a49a98f51768d4134d67be16edaec19537c7c636a9e204d0de3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 30 of arXiv:2607.18201, 'Finding Adam in noisy trees'] Let $X=\sum_{i=1}^\infty \mathrm{Ber}(p_i)$ where the summands are independent, $\mu:=\Exp{X}=\sum_{i=1}^\infty ip_i<\infty$, $k \geq 1$ and $x>\mu+k$. Then 
