{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.673449+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0be97f5b1e3402000934771d247354f93fa49faa66d129ddb33f78d745239ef7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Centered Gaussian relative entropy) of arXiv:2607.10769, '\fontsize{14}{17}\selectfont\bfseries Sharp Poincar\'e interpolation along Wasserstein geo'] Let $\mu_0$ and $\mu_1$ be $1$-strongly log-concave, and let
$(\mu_t)_{0\leq t\leq1}$ be their quadratic Wasserstein geodesic.  Then
$0\leq W_{\rm c}^2\leq2n$, and
$\mathcal D_t=\mathcal D(\mu_t)$ satisfies the following distortion
inequality along the whole geodesic:
\begin{equation}
 e^{-\mathcal D_t/n}
 \geq
 \frac{\sin((1-t)\omega)}{\sin\omega}
 e^{-\mathcal D_0/n}
 +\frac{\sin(t\omega)}{\sin\omega}
 e^{-\mathcal D_1/n},\qquad \omega=\sqrt{\frac{2}{n}}\,W_{\rm c}.
\end{equation}
The sine quotients are understood by continuity when $\omega=0$, in
which case they equal $1-t$ and $t$, respectively.
Moreover, equality in \eqref{eq:intro-entropy-distortion} at some
$t\in(0,1)$ holds if and only if $\mu_1$ is a translate of $\mu_0$.
In this case, equality holds for every $t\in[0,1]$. 

In particular, if the endpoints have the same barycenter, then the same statements
hold with $\Ent_\gamma(\mu_t)$ in place of $\mathcal D_t$ and with
$W_2(\mu_0,\mu_1)$ in place of $W_{\rm c}$. In this case, equality at
an interior time holds if and only if $\mu_0=\mu_1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.752111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bea5acc1415760d42b4797a2c82e30e770bba1c5fe27b504f4d06f18751e677","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:sol_lin_fp of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $s\in [0,T)$ and $\nu\in \mathcal{M}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. A measurable flow ${\boldsymbol\mu}:[s,T)\to \mathcal{M}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is a (weak) solution to FP$(b,\sigma;s,\nu)$ if
\begin{equation}
\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \varphi(y) {\boldsymbol\mu}_t (d y)    = \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \varphi(y) \nu (d y) + \int_{s}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}  \Big[\big\langle b(r , y) , \nabla \varphi(y)\big\rangle + \frac{1}{2} \text{tr}\big( \sigma\sigma^\top(r , y ) \nabla^2 \varphi(y) \big)   \Big]  {\boldsymbol\mu}_r (d y) d r, \qquad t\in [s,T),

\end{equation}

for any $\varphi\in C^{\infty}_0(\mathbb{R}^d)$. 

Furthermore, if ${\boldsymbol\mu}_t$ is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure, namely
\begin{equation}
{\boldsymbol\mu}_t (d x) = v_t(x) d x, \qquad t\in [s,T), 
\end{equation}
then the density flow $v:[s,T)\to L^{1}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ will be called a density solution to FP$(b,\sigma;\nu)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.902578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0beaef976bdb48cbf5c7ed0a6689bc1332ac2cb435410c5851e12946d2dff8a0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2606.20908, 'On It\^o-Stratonovich formula for rough sheets'] Recall from Section~\ref{incr} that $\cac_{1}$ is the space of functions on $[0,1]$ and $\cac_{2}$ is the space of $1$-increments. Let $f\in \cac_1$, $g \in \cac_2$, $\kappa > 0$, and $p \geq 1$. Define
\begin{equation}
U^1_{\kappa,p}(g) :=
\left(
\int \int_{[0,1]^2}
\frac{|g_{vw}|^{2p}}{|w-v|^{2\kappa p + 2}}
\, dv \, dw
\right)^{\frac{1}{2p}}.
\end{equation}
Then the following holds true:
   \begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\emph{(\roman*)}}]
       \item If $U^1_{\kappa,p}(\delta f) < \infty$, then $f \in \cac_1^{\kappa}$ (in the sense of \eqref{def:hnorm-c1}); more precisely,
       there exists a universal constant $c_0 > 0$ such that
\begin{align}
\|\delta f\|_{\kappa} \leq c_0  \, U^1_{\kappa,p}(\delta f).
\end{align}

       \item If $\delta g \in \cac_{3}^{\kappa}$ and $U^1_{\kappa,p}(g) < \infty$, then $g \in \cac_2^{\kappa}$ (in the sense of \eqref{eq:def-norm-C2}); specifically, 
              there exists a universal constant $c_1 > 0$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|g\|_{\kappa} \leq c_1 \big( U^1_{\kappa,p}(g) + \|\delta g\|_{\kappa} \big).
\end{equation}
   \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:46.463642+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bed00b6f046a80c33ba92c1367344056de532efa4a4b62e0b7c7e762704d502","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 33 (Projection tensorfields and hypersurface tangency) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] \hfill
\begin{enumerate}
\item We define the type $\binom{1}{1}$
$\Sigma_t$-projection tensorfield $\Sigmatproject$ and the type $\binom{1}{1}$ $\ell_{t,u}$-projection tensorfield 
$\smoothtorusproject$ as follows, where $\updelta_{\beta}^{\alpha}$ denotes the Kronecker delta: 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
\Sigmatproject_{\beta}^{\ \alpha}
& \eqdef 
	\updelta_{\beta}^{\alpha}
	+ 
	\Transport^{\alpha} 
	\Transport_\beta,  

	\\
\smoothtorusproject^{\ \alpha}_\beta 
& 
\eqdef 
\updelta_{\beta}^{\alpha}
+  
\Transport^{\alpha} \Transport_\beta 
- 
X^{\alpha} X_\beta 
= 
\updelta_{\beta}^{\alpha}
- 
\Lunit^{\alpha} \updelta^0_\beta 
+
X^{\alpha} \Lunit_{\beta}.

\end{align} 
\end{subequations}\item Given any type $\binom{m}{n}$ spacetime tensorfield $\upxi$, 
we respectively define its $\gfour$-orthogonal projection onto $\Sigma_t$, denoted by $\Sigmatproject \upxi$, its $\gfour$-orthogonal projection onto $\ell_{t,u}$, denoted by $\smoothtorusproject \upxi$, as follows:
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
(\Sigmatproject \upxi)_{\beta_1 \cdots \beta_n}^{\alpha_1 \cdots \alpha_m}
& 
\eqdef 
\Sigmatproject_{\widetilde{\alpha}_1}^{\ \alpha_1}
\cdots 
\Sigmatproject_{\widetilde{\alpha}_m}^{\ \alpha_m}
\Sigmatproject_{\beta_1}^{\ \widetilde{\beta}_1}
\cdots 
\Sigmatproject_{ \beta_n}^{\ \widetilde{\beta}_n}
\upxi_{\widetilde{\beta}_1 \cdots \widetilde{\beta}_n}^{\widetilde{\alpha}_1 \cdots \widetilde{\alpha}_m}, 

	\\
(\smoothtorusproject \upxi)_{\beta_1\cdots\beta_n}^{\alpha_1\cdots\alpha_m} 
& \eqdef 
\smoothtorusproject_{\widetilde{\alpha}_1}^{\ \alpha_1} 
\cdots 
\smoothtorusproject_{\widetilde{\alpha}_m}^{\ \alpha_m} 
\smoothtorusproject_{ \beta_1}^{\ \widetilde{\beta}_1}
\cdots 
\smoothtorusproject_{ \beta_n}^{\ \widetilde{\beta}_n}
\upxi_{\widetilde{\beta}_1 \cdots \widetilde{\beta}_n}^{\widetilde{\alpha}_1 \cdots \widetilde{\alpha}_m}.

\end{align}
\end{subequations}\item
We say that a spacetime tensorfield $\upxi$ is $\Sigma_t$-tangent if $\Sigmatproject \upxi = \upxi$. 
We say that a spacetime tensorfield $\upxi$ is $\ell_{t,u}$-tangent if $\smoothtorusproject \upxi = \upxi$.  
\item If $\upxi$ is a symmetric type $\binom{0}{2}$-spacetime tensor and $V$ is a vectorfield, 
then we define $\slashed{\upxi}_V \eqdef \smoothtorusproject(\upxi \cdot V)$, 
where $\upxi \cdot V$ is the one-form with components
$(\upxi \cdot V)_{\alpha} \eqdef \upxi_{\alpha \beta} V^{\beta}$.
\item If $\upxi$ is a spacetime tensor, then we define $\slashed{\upxi} = \smoothtorusproject \upxi$.
From 3 above, it follows that $\xi$ is $\ell_{t,u}$-tangent if and only if $\slashed{\upxi} = \xi$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.225554+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bed0961779f88206c2d64d5a672d96e86e93162aa2f6070dde788680cf60dd2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:L1contraction of arXiv:2512.18598, 'Long-time reverse transportation inequalities for non-globally-dissipative Langevin dynami'] Under Assumption (ass:farfield of the paper),  Let $f(\cdot)$ be a Lyapunov function as defined in (equation eq:Lyapunov of the paper) with $R_f = R$ and $C_f = \frac{1}{4}R(L+m)$. 
    Choose
    \begin{equation}
        \eta_t =\frac{aa'}{e^{a(T-t)}-1},\quad t\in [0,T)
    \end{equation}
    with any $a$, $a'>0$.
    Then for any $t\in [0,T]$ and $x \neq x'$,
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}|X_t-X_t''| \leq C_1\mathbb{E}f(|X_t-X_t''|) \leq C_1\exp\left(-2\nu t - C_1^{-1}\big(\sqrt{|x-x'|}\vee\sqrt{R+1}\big)^{-1}\int_0^t \eta_s ds \right)|x-x'|.
    \end{equation}







    where
    \begin{equation}
        \nu := \frac{m}{2}\exp\bigl(-\frac{1}{4}R^2(L+m)\bigr),\quad C_1 := \exp\bigl(\frac{1}{4}R^2(L+m)\bigr),
    \end{equation}
    In particular,
    \begin{equation}
