{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.996768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bf9e0ff42a0b4b6feee03e837b945a48406182bcef7a1a083d3918491212983","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 46 of arXiv:2604.01685, 'Probability with Measure\footnote{Lecture notes. University of Ljubljana.}'] Let $(\Omega,\FF,\mu)$ be a measure space and $f\in \FF/\mathcal{B}_{[-\infty,\infty]}$. 
	\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
		\item If $f$ is $\FF$-simple, we put $\int f \dd\mu:=\sum_{a\in \mathrm{range}(f)}a\mu(f^{-1}(\{a\}))$ \big($=\sum_{a\in \mathrm{range}(f)}a\mu(f=a)$\big).\footnote{It may happen that $\mathrm{range}(f)=\emptyset$; of course this occurs iff $\Omega=\emptyset$. We always put $\sum_\emptyset:=0$. (And $\prod_\emptyset:=1$.)} 
		\item If $f\geq 0$, but $f$ is not $\FF$-simple, then we put 
		\begin{equation*}
		\int f \dd \mu:=\sup\left\{\int g\dd\mu: g\text{ is $\FF$-simple and }g\leq f\right\}.
		\end{equation*}
		\item If $f$ is not nonnegative, then we put $\int f\dd \mu:=\int f^+\dd\mu-\int f^-\dd \mu$.

	\end{enumerate}

$\int f\dd \mu$ is called the (Lebesgue\footnote{We shall tend to omit this qualification.}) integral (also, expectation if $\mu$ is a probability measure) of $f$ under $\mu$. We write variously $\mu[f]:=\mu^x[f(x)]:=\int f(x)\mu(\dd x):=\int f\dd\mu$, whichever is the more convenient. If further $A\in \FF$, we put $\mu[f;A]:=\mu^x[f(x);A]:=\mu^x[f(x);x\in A]:=\int_Af(x)\mu(\dd x):=\int_{x\in A}f(x)\mu(\dd x):=\int_A f\dd \mu:=\int f\mathbbm{1}_A\dd \mu$.

The integral of $f$ against $\mu$ is well-defined \deff  $\int f^+\dd \mu\land \int f^-\dd \mu<\infty$.

$f$ is $\mu$-integrable \deff $\int f^+ \dd\mu\lor \int f^-\dd\mu<\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.787769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bfa3d8eb9a365c78971f6665492dca8cd965edb86b70621ffb8470575e87a59","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop last_val_perm of arXiv:2509.07729, 'Permuton and local limits for the Luce model'] Let $\mu$ be the Luce permuton corresponding to a positive, finite, measurable function $f$ on $[0,1]$, as defined in (thm:permuton_conv of the paper). Set $\pi_n=\Perm(\mu,n)$. Then
    \begin{multline*}
        \mathbb P \mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\pi_n(a_1)=1,\dots, \pi_n(a_k)=k\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)\\
        =\mathbb E\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[\int_{x_1>x_2>\dots>x_k>0}\prod_{j=1}^k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(f(U^{a_j})\mathrm{e}^{-f(U^{a_j})x_j}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)\prod_{i\in[n]\setminus\{a_1,\dots,a_k\}}\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(1-\mathrm{e}^{-f(U^{i}) x_k}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright) \, \text{d} x_1\dots\text{d} x_k\aftergroup\egroup\originalright],
    \end{multline*}
    where $(U^1,\dots,U^n)$ are the order statistics of $n$ i.i.d.\ uniform random variables in $[0,1]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:59:24.054207+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bfba31b39bf7c6567c3ec473a4ab97b9f1daf93750bc50239e664c1d3c407d1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:canonical-second-variation of arXiv:2608.30904, 'Entire monotone solutions of the anisotropic Allen--Cahn equation in dimension 5'] Let $u$ be a weak solution satisfying
$\mathcal L^4(\{Du=0\})=0$. Set
\[
A_u=D^2H(Du)
\qquad\text{a.e. on }\{Du\neq0\},
\]
and define it arbitrarily on $\{Du=0\}$ according to
$\lambda I\le A_u\le\Lambda I$. Then, for every
$\varphi\in C_c^1(\mathbb R^4)$,
\begin{equation}
\left.
\frac{\,\mathrm d^2}{\,\mathrm d t^2}
\right|_{t=0}
E_\varepsilon(u+t\varphi;\Omega)
=
Q_{\varepsilon,u}(\varphi),
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{supp}\varphi\subseteq \Omega$ and
\begin{equation}
Q_{\varepsilon,u}(\varphi)
:=
\varepsilon\int_{\mathbb R^4}
A_uD\varphi\cdot D\varphi\,\,\mathrm d x
+
\frac1\varepsilon\int_{\mathbb R^4}
W''(u)\varphi^2\,\,\mathrm d x.
\end{equation}
The value of $Q_{\varepsilon,u}$ is independent of the extension of $A_u$
to $\{Du=0\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.122986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0c0365f2546f27aead66a4ee7046d8f3fbf010974c78335d9037857b41f995ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2605.27713, 'Lower path regularity in all dimensions'] Suppose that $X=(X_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ satisfies Assumption \ref{a1} and that $\alpha$ is as in \eqref{E:parameters}. 
