{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.488183+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0c32e82e2eef64270b1963121ac997707070fe60da058b7578d69de60bed374e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem mild-strong of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Fix $r \in [2,\infty), \theta \in \mathbb{R}$, $F: B \to \mathcal{B}_{\theta, r}$, $G: B \to \gamma(\mathcal{U},\mathcal{B}_{1/2+\theta, r})$, a stopping time $\tau$, and assume $x \in L^p(\Omega;C([0,T];B))$.
    If $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ of (equation eq:gefx of the paper) up to time $\tau$ and there is some $\epsilon > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        \|x_0\|_{\theta + \epsilon,r} + \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^{T \wedge \tau} \|F(x_t)\|_{\theta + \epsilon,r} + \|G(x_t)\|_{\theta + 1/2+\epsilon, r}^2dt\Big] < \infty \,,
    \end{equation}
    then $x_t$ is a strong solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ up to time $\tau$. Conversely, if $x_t$ is a strong solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ up to time $\tau$ and (equation eq:mild-vs-strong of the paper) holds, then $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta+1,r}$ up to time $\tau$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.043319+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0c3345acc895c1c41d9e9df5b14afbf6021d89f2d08e586fb5906676ef31bee6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2606.31936, 'Coupling and Maximal Inequalities for Graph-Dependent Empirical Processes'] Suppose $(N_{n},V_{n})$ has exponential (shell) growth with constant $a$. Then, for any $n \in \mathbb{N}$, any $a,b >0$, any $p \in [1,\infty]$, and any $d \geq 1$, there exists a  partition \(\mathcal P_n(q_n)=\{B_1,\ldots,B_{M_n}\}\) satisfying $\max_{m\in[M_n]}|B_m|\lesssim q_n $ with $q_{n} \in [n] $ such that
	\begin{align}
		\left\|
		\sup_{f\in\mathcal F}\mathbb G_n(f)
		\right\|_{L^1(\mathbb P)}
		\lesssim\;&
		q_n e^{a r_n/2}
		\max_{m\in[M_n]}\Gamma_p(B_m)
		\gamma_{2,a}
		\left(
		\mathcal F,
		\max_{i\in N_n}\|\cdot\|_{L^{2p}(P_{Z_i})}
		\right)
		\\
		&+
		\left(
		\frac{q_n^3 e^{a r_n}}{\sqrt n}
		+
		\sqrt n 	\bar{\tau}_{\operatorname{cone} \mathcal F,G_n}
		\left( 	\mathcal P_{n}(q_{n}), 	\mathcal C_{r_{n}}[\mathcal P_{n}(q_{n}) ] \right) 
		\right)
		\gamma_{1,b}
		\left(
		\mathcal F,\|\cdot\|_\infty
		\right).
	\end{align}
	In particular, if \(q_n\asymp 1\) and
	\(	\bar{\tau}_{\operatorname{cone} \mathcal F,G_n}
	\left( 	\mathcal P_{n}(1), 	\mathcal C_{r_{n}}[\mathcal P_{n}(1) ] \right)    = : 
	\bar{\tau}_{\operatorname{cone}\mathcal F,G_n}(r_n)\), then
	\begin{align}
		\left\|
		\sup_{f\in\mathcal F}\mathbb G_n(f)
		\right\|_{L^1(\mathbb P)}
		\lesssim\;&
		e^{a r_n/2}
		\gamma_{2,a}
		\left(
		\mathcal F,
		\max_{i\in N_n}\|\cdot\|_{L^{2p}(P_{Z_i})}
		\right)
		\\
		&+
		\left(
		\frac{e^{a r_n}}{\sqrt n}
		+
		\sqrt n\,
		\bar{\tau}_{\operatorname{cone}\mathcal F,G_n}(r_n)
		\right)
		\gamma_{1,b}
		\left(
		\mathcal F,\|\cdot\|_\infty
		\right).
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.537647+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0c3461287b8680328a68f31e300267eed747df8266fb6a610076ca1e2f59928a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition th:GR of arXiv:2411.16913, 'Entropies of the Poisson distribution as functions of intensity: ``normal'' and ``anomalou'] \begin{enumerate}
