{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.908003+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0cf408d0a96c8d398b5cf41433a3a854961897387d5ae4b1b39e9da929042c67","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem mainresult:lowrank of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let \\ \allowbreak $\mathbf{X}_\star \in  \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2}$  be a matrix of rank $r$.
Assume that the measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2} \longrightarrow \R^m $ satisfies the NSP (eq:NSP:definition of the paper) of order $r$ with constant $\eta_r <3/5$. Let $\left(  \mathbf{X}^{(k)}\right)_{k\geq 0}$ and $\left(\varepsilon_{k} \right)_{k \geq 0}$ be the \texttt{MatrixIRLS} iterates obtained from (alg:algo1 of the paper) with measurements $\mathbf{y} = \mathcal{A}(\mathbf{X}_\star)$, rank estimate $\widetilde{r} = r$ and initializing weight operator $W^{(0)}$, and assume that the weight operators $\W{\mathbf{X}^{(k)}}{\varepsilon_{k}}$ are admissible in the sense of (def:admissible:weightoperators of the paper), with associated constant $c_q$ of (eq:c_q:def of the paper).

Then it holds for all iterations $k \in \mathbb{N}_0$ carried out by (alg:algo1 of the paper) that
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon_{k}} \left( \mathbf{X}^{(k)} \right)  -  \nucnorm{ \mathbf{X}_\star } 
\le 
\left(  1-  \frac{C_{\eta_r}}{c_q\eta_1 d}   \right)^k 
\left(    \mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon_{0}} \left( \mathbf{X}^{(0)} \right) -  \nucnorm{\mathbf{X}_\star} \right)
\end{equation}
as well as
\begin{equation}
\nucnorm{ \mathbf{X}^{(k)} - \mathbf{X}_\star} \le 
A_{\eta_r} \left(  1-  \frac{C_{\eta_r}}{c_q\eta_1 d}   \right)^k  \nucnorm{ \mathbf{X}^{(0)}-\mathbf{X}_\star}
\end{equation}
where $A_{\eta_r}$ and $C_{\eta_r}$ are as in (eq:A_eta_r:def of the paper) and $ 0 < \eta_1 \leq \eta_r$ denotes the NSP constant of $\mathcal{A}$ of order~$1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.986565+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0cf41d9c58b0146233580dbb0e672c037e13cd6afc2d49b42aff085161e09165","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 (Failure of marginal Torelli reconstruction) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] Let $G_1,G_2$ be independent standard Gaussian variables and set
\begin{equation}
 X=G_1(G_1^2+G_2^2),
 \qquad
 Y=G_1^3-3G_1G_2^2.

\end{equation}
Then $X$ and $Y$ have the same law, but their defining polynomials are not
related by an orthogonal change of variables.  More precisely, with the
probabilists' Hermite polynomials,
\begin{equation}
 X=H_3(G_1)+G_1H_2(G_2)+4G_1,
 \qquad
 Y=H_3(G_1)-3G_1H_2(G_2),

\end{equation}
so, writing $(P_t)_{t\ge0}$ for the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck semigroup, their
correlation profiles are respectively
\begin{equation}
 \ip{X}{P_tX}_{L^2}=16e^{-t}+8e^{-3t},
 \qquad
 \ip{Y}{P_tY}_{L^2}=24e^{-3t}.

\end{equation}
Thus a marginal law does not determine the terminal chaos grading or its
minimal graded support.","status":"unverified"}

