{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.971535+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d27a52397c84bf23ff5fa8bb43ae3f214bb59633719951f8cdf1511c1621477","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin R\'enyi divergence of arXiv:2506.23062, 'Shifted Composition IV\@: \\ Toward Ballistic Acceleration for Log-Concave Sampling'] The R\'enyi divergence of order $q > 1$ between two probability measures $\mu$, $\nu$ is defined as
    \begin{align*}
        \mathsf{R}_q(\mu \mathbin{\|} \nu) \coloneqq \frac{1}{q-1} \log \int \Bigl(\frac{\mathrm{d} \mu}{\mathrm{d} \nu}\Bigr)^q \, \mathrm{d} \nu\,,
    \end{align*}
    if $\mu \ll \nu$, and $+ \infty$ otherwise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.431358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d27c742625ed0d4608872b98ac27333698cb6a5c9d7df809319eb395f21fc6d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm app_thm_alphaIsAlwaysOpti of arXiv:2411.16464, 'Generating social networks with static and dynamic utility-maximization approaches'] For any individuals set $\mathscr{I} \in (\mathcal{E}, \innerproduct{.}{.}) \times \mathcal{U}_N$ , and weight list $\alpha$ of dimension $n_N = \binom{N}{2}$ satisfying:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\forall a \in \alpha, a \in [0 ,1)$
\item $\nexists i \in \Iintv*{1, N }, \forall j \in \Iintv*{1, N-1 }, \alpha^i_j = 0 $
\item $\forall M \leq I, \left(\sum_{i\leq M} H_i\right)\left( C, \alpha \right)$ is bijective in its first component.
\end{itemize}

There exists:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a unique compatibility list $\bar C$ such that $\alpha$ is the unique unconstrained solution of the Social Optimization Problem for the individuals graph $\mathcal{G}(\mathscr{I} , \alpha, \bar C)$
\item a set of compatibility lists $\underbar C$ such that $\forall C \in \underbar C$, $\alpha$ is the unique solution of the Social Optimization Problem for the individuals graph $\mathcal{G}(\mathscr{I} , \alpha, C)$. 
$$\underbar C = \left\{ C \in (\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+})^{n_I}, \quad c_{ij} \in \begin{cases} \{\bar c_{ij}\} & \text{if } \alpha_{ij} > 0 \\  (0 , \bar c_{ij}]& \text{if } \alpha_{ij} = 0 \end{cases}\right\}$$
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.240806+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d2995cdc5a31c2c9835156caafab023a4d29481afe9ffcab4945a56f7d6354a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 (Riemannian shape derivative) of arXiv:2609.00571, '\textbf{Shape Optimization and Bernoulli Free Boundary Problems\\ for the Riemannian $p$-L'] Let $\Omega\in\Og$ have sufficiently regular boundary and let
$V\in C_c^1(M,TM)$ vanish in a neighbourhood of $K$. Then
