{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.520812+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0dad62ab5dc099727094c89c7c47e0d4f495b91feb2641eb7ad50bbb374c68ca","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Loop equation operator of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] We define an operator $M$ on the space of functions $f : \Omega_B \ra \R$ as follows. Let $f : \Omega_B \ra \R$. Let $(s, K) \in \Omega_B$ be such that $s$ is non-null, and let $\mbf{e} = \mbf{e}_s$ be the first edge of $s$. Define
\begin{equs}
(M f)(s, K) := \mp \sum_{s' \in \mbb{S}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e})} f(s', K) \mp \frac{1}{N^2} \sum_{s' \in \mbb{M}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e})} f(s', K) \mp \frac{\beta}{N} \sum_{(s', K') \in \mbb{D}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e}, K)} f(s', K').
\end{equs}
For null loops, we define
\begin{equs}
M f(\varnothing_x,K):= f(\varnothing_x ,K), ~~ x \in \Lambda.
\end{equs}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.973625+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0dae5582fc734330a7b83c14d67250b01f34dd1cd51a117b70a5aec04af1c85b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm erasable loops have one plaquette assignment of arXiv:2508.13827, 'Surface sums in two-dimensional large-$N$ lattice Yang--Mills: Cancellations and explicit '] Let $\upbeta\in \mathbb{R}$. For any non-trivial loop $\ell$, \begin{equation}
        \phi(\ell) = \sum_{K\in \mathcal{K}_{\ell}}c(\ell, K)\cdot\upbeta^{\area(K)},
    \end{equation}
    where we recall that:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathcal{K}_{\ell}$ is the canonical collection $\mathcal{K}_{\ell}$ of plaquette assignments associated with $\ell$ introduced in (defn:collection-plaquette-ass of the paper);
        \item $c(\ell, K) = \sum_{M\in \mathcal{N}\mathcal{P}\mathcal{M}(\ell,K)}w_{\infty}(M)$ are the coefficients (independent of $\upbeta$) introduced in (equation eq:coefficents of the paper);
        \item $\area(K)= \sum_{p\in \mathcal{P}}K(p)$.
    \end{itemize}
    \noindent More specifically, we have that 
    \[\phi^K(\ell)=0, \quad\text{for all } K\notin \mathcal{K}_{\ell}.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.064989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0db244238193122e6c7a94dd7d4251c4103bbf5f36fe3d313f1117d6414a3699","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.rep_grad_psi of arXiv:2411.14105, 'Simultaneous replica-symmetry breaking \\ for vector spin glasses'] Under~(i.setting of the paper), let $X_0 = \sqrt{2L_q(0)}\eta$ for a standard $\mathbb{R}^{D}$-valued Gaussian vector $\eta$ independent of everything else. Then, for every smooth increasing path $L':[0,T]\to S^{D}_+$, we have\begin{align*}
    L'(0)\cdot \alpha(0)\mathbb{E}_\eta [R(0)] + \int_0^T L'(t)\cdot \mathbb{E}_\eta[R(t)]\mathrm{d} \alpha(t) = \int_0^T L'(t)\cdot L_p(t)\mathrm{d} \alpha(t),
\end{align*}
where $\mathbb{E}_\eta$ averages over the randomness of $\eta$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:55:37.872531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0db31818bbdb61118c7b60c23c8ff930411620536f04699f8d1c2704e8e3c221","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition cond_traccia_gamma of arXiv:2605.07832, 'A Bismut -Elworthy formula for BSDEs with degenerate noise'] \begin{enumerate}[\rm(i)]
