{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.375222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0dc5f5d0576658d2032fc580a8c86cb0b9a36334e2681b1d8fa8eec5b59237b0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] $M$ has an eigenvalue $1$ with multiplicity $1$. $M$ has an eigenvalue $0$ with multiplicity $n/2$ if $n$ is even, and multiplicity $(n-1)/2$ if $n$ is odd.
    \begin{proof}
        By the Perron-Frobenius theorem, $M$ has an eigenvalue $1$ with multiplicity $1$. We also note that by Corollary $(stationary distribution of the paper)$, one can take $\vec{v}=[1,1,...,1]^T$ as the corresponding eigenvector. Note that $M_{ij}=M_{i,n-j+1}$ and $M_{ij}=0$ if and only if $i+1\leq j\leq n-i$, so the rank of $M$ is $n/2$ if $n$ is even and $(n-1)/2$ if $n$ is odd. This shows that $0$ is an eigenvalue with the desired multiplicity.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

