{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.295555+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f39b8a3f216348a7ddb7b3a98159552c935a30e7bbba06704b333c61f84626a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defWellPoseHadmard of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Consider the equation

\begin{equation}
F(u) = v, \qquad u\in U,\; v \in V.
\end{equation}

The equation (equation defWellPoseHadmard-eq1 of the paper) is called well-posed in the sense of Hadamard if the following three conditions are satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
    \item For any $ v \in V $, there exists a solution $ u \in U$ such that $ F(u) = v $.
    \item This solution is unique.
    \item The solution $ v = F^{-1}(u) $ depends continuously on $ u $ in $U$.
\end{enumerate}
If at least one of these conditions is failed, the problem is called ill-posed.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.522826+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f3d348223081fd1cbb7c2f13597dc1c532bfb8a5813bfabf4f6ecf63709e62f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 47 ({\cite[Lemma 2.3) of arXiv:2608.30602, '2608.30602'] {JN01}}]
	Let $\mathbb{A}$ be an operator matrix on the orthogonal direct sum of Hilbert spaces $\mathcal{H}_1\oplus\mathcal{H}_2$,
	\[
	\mathbb{A}=
	\begin{pmatrix}
		a_{11} & a_{12}\\[2pt]
		a_{21} & a_{22}
	\end{pmatrix},
	\qquad a_{ij}\colon \mathcal{H}_j\rightarrow\mathcal{H}_i,
	\]
	where $a_{11},a_{22}$ are closed and $a_{12},a_{21}$ are bounded. Assume that $a_{11}$ has a bounded inverse. Then $\mathbb{A}$ has a bounded inverse if and only if the operator $d:= a_{22}-a_{21}a_{11}^{-1}a_{12}$ has a bounded inverse, in which case
	\begin{equation*}
		(\mathbb{A})^{-1}=
		\begin{pmatrix}
			a_{11}^{-1}a_{12}d^{-1}a_{21}a_{11}^{-1}+a_{11}^{-1} & -a_{11}^{-1}a_{12}d^{-1}\\[4pt]
			-d^{-1}a_{21}a_{11}^{-1} & d^{-1}
		\end{pmatrix}.
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:01:56.349845+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f401693fe9001ce52b5d8e6329bea42cf10228f9ffba56629b52c527e159590","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:technical_D_lemma of arXiv:2609.02491, 'Generalised Random Parking for Trapeziums on a Strip'] Let $a>0$ and $b\in [0,a]$, and set $c\coloneqq (a+b)/2\le a$. Suppose $\mathfrak{D}:(0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies, for all $\varpi>a$,
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{D}(\varpi)\le \frac{a}{\varpi-a}\Big(\mathfrak{D}(\varpi-a)+\mathfrak{D}(\varpi-c)\Big).
\end{equation}
For $\varpi>a$ define $k_\varpi\coloneqq \inf\{n\in\mathbb{N}: \varpi-an\le a \}$. Then
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{D}(\varpi)\le \frac{a^{k_\varpi}}{(\varpi-a)(\varpi-2a)\cdots(\varpi-k_\varpi a)} \sum_{r=0}^{k_\varpi}\binom{k_\varpi}{r} \mathfrak{D}\big(\varpi-ra-(k_\varpi-r)c\big).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

