W^n \to W \ \ \text{in probability in } C([0,T];\mathscr{U}) \ \ \text{and} \quad
G^n \to G \ \ \text{in probability in } C([0,T];L_2(\mathscr{U},X)),
\end{equation*}
it holds that 
$\displaystyle \int_0^t G^n dW^n \to \int_0^t G dW  \ \ \text{in probability in } C([0,T];X).$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.976147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f9625d54f0a20095c38082506c497a070416ac3ee88755a92d998fa5c2498df","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 11 of arXiv:2606.11271, '\LARGE The $K$-th nearest neighbor random walk on a Poisson point process gets trapped'] For any given $\varepsilon >0$, $R\in \N^*$ and $u \in \Z^d$, the block $\Lambda_R(u)$ is said $\varepsilon$-good if both following statements are satisfied.
