{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.288883+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1069024104248e6f122ab04f572c7ac99f0c53d5e5534ef1a3a75448bb17948e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 12 of arXiv:2604.25725, 'What is The Probability That A Random Graph With A Given Degree Sequence is Connected?'] Fix $0<\eps<10^{-6}$. Let $\cD$ be such that $m$ is large enough and $n_1,n_2 \le 10^{-6}m$. Let $H$ be a possible subgraph of $G(\cD)$ and let $h$ be a half-edge out of $v\in V(H)$ and not matched in $E(H)$. Suppose $H$ is such that the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\sum_{w\in V(H)}d(w)\le \epsilon m$, and
    \item Either $D^*\leq\eps m$, or $d(w)<\sqrt{m}(\log{m})^{-2}$ for all $w \in V(H)$.
\end{enumerate}
Let $\cF$ denote the event that $h$ is matched onto $V(H)$ or onto a vertex of degree at most $2$. Let $\cA$ denote the event that no neighbour of $v$ outside of $V(H)$ has degree $10d(v)$ or more. Then $\Cprob{\cF\cap\cA}{H\subseteq G(\cD)}<0.001$. If $D^*\leq\eps m$, then $\Cprob{\cF}{H\subseteq G(\cD)}<0.001$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.285979+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1069389c0e4190138ffe6dfabd4da645f689d30b0daa978c53798a278f5a53c3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn mild solution of arXiv:2411.01847, 'Well-Posedness of Stochastic Chemotaxis System'] We say that $(u,v)$ is a mild solution of system ((main of the paper)) if $(u,v)$ is a progressively measurable stochastic process with values in $C(\bar{\mathcal{O}})\times W^{1,\infty}({\mathcal{O}})$ that satisfies:
	\begin{align*}
		&u(t) = e^{-tA}u_0 - \int_0^t e^{-(t-s)A}\left( \nabla \cdot \left( \chi u(s) \nabla v(s) \right) - g(u(s)) \right)\, ds + \sum_{i=1}^\infty\int_0^t e^{-(t-s)A} \sigma_i(u(s)) \,  \, dW_s^i, \\
        &v(t)=G*u(t),
	\end{align*}
for all $t\geq 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.792375+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1069c59b3f318f94e45263d7ed8706c02487cfb34a2e21fc3cb730d623296546","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:alterprojmain of arXiv:2411.08295, 'Improving the convergence of Markov chains via permutations and projections'] Let $m,n \in \mathbb{N}$. Let $P \in \mathcal{L}(\pi)$ and $Q_i \in \mathcal{I}(\pi) \cap \mathcal{L}$ for $i \in \llbracket 0,m-1 \rrbracket$ be a sequence of isometric involution transition matrices. Define $R_n$ as in (equation eq:Rndef of the paper). The following limit exists (pointwise or in total variation):
