{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.773766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_109d4e97a818393bec4933949f62e0d54bbe5cc29594ae8da3f442df8ed50d16","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Newton-inequalities of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] Let $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$ be a non-negative and non-zero sequence with finite length $m\geq1$, and let $(e_k)_{0\leq k\leq m}$ be the sequence of the elementary symmetric polynomials in the $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$, or equivalently let $e_k=e_k(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m,0,0,\dots)$ for $0\leq k\leq m$.
	Then the sequence $(e_k)_{0\leq k\leq m}$ satisfies the \emph{ultra-log-concavity} inequality, that is
	\begin{align}
	\frac{e_k^2}{\binom{m}{k}^2}\geq 
		\frac{e_{k-1}}{\binom{m}{k-1}}\cdot
		\frac{e_{k+1}}{\binom{m}{k+1}},
		\qquad k\in\{1,\dots,m-1\}.
	\end{align}
	In particular, ${\mathbf{e}}(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m,0,0,\dots)$ is log-concave.
		\footnote{
		This is deduced from the inequality $\binom{m}{k}^2\geq\binom{m}{k-1}\binom{m}{k+1}$ for $0<k<m$, which holds by log-concavity of the binomial coefficients.
		Note that in this paper we require log-concave sequences to have no internal zeros, but we already justified that this is the case for sequences having the form ${\mathbf{e}}({\boldsymbol{\uptheta}})$ with ${\boldsymbol{\uptheta}}$ a summable weight sequence.
		} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.858011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a5d81861634706283d77e8de44525859b7891f4338d6100b8396a736cfc6ee","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem lem:gen_ibp of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] For all $l \in H_0^1~\cap~H^2$ and $F \in C_b^1(L)$ the following two equations hold
    \begin{align}
        \langle \!\langle F(\beta), \bar{l}_a\delta_0(\beta_a)\rangle \!\rangle &= \int_{L} F \langle l,\ufd{\sigma^{(a)}}\rangle \,d\var{\sigma^{(a)}},\\
        \Big\langle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\langle F(\beta), \int_{[0,1]} \bar{l}_x\delta_0(\beta_x) dx \Big\rangle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\rangle &= \int_{L} F \langle l,n_{\nu}\rangle \,d\| \nu \|.
    \end{align}
	Here, the LHSs are interpreted as discussed in Theorem (thm:ibpf of the paper). Moreover, for all elements $l\in H_0^1~\cap~H^2$ and $G \in (C_b^1)_{H_0^1\cap H^2}$, the two equations
    \begin{align}
        \int_{L} D^*G \rho^a \, d\mu^{\beta} &= \int_{L} \langle G,\ufd{\sigma^{(a)}}\rangle \,d\var{\sigma^{(a)}},\\
        \int_{L} D^*G \rho \, d\mu^{\beta} &= \int_{L} \langle G,n_{\nu}\rangle \,d\| \nu \|
    \end{align}
    hold. Using these results, [RZZ12], Theorem 3.1(iii), directly implies that $\rho^a,\, \rho \in \mathrm{BV}(L, H_0^1)$, $V(\rho^a)=\var{\sigma^{(a)}}(L)$ and $V(\rho)=\| \nu \|(L)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.888936+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a645f18be63c1d85445a77d68812028397f35ff7959cb20732693f55cbe73a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definizione 13 of arXiv:2506.11937, 'Random rotational invariance of integration by parts formulas within a Bismut-type approac'] Given two (trivial) principal bundles $ M \times G$ and $  M' \times  G $, an isomorphism between principal bundles is a diffeomorphism $ F:  M \times G \rightarrow M' \times G$ that preserves the principal bundle structure of $ M \times G$ and $ M' \times G$. In other words, there exists a diffeomorphism $\Phi: M \rightarrow M'$ such that 
\begin{equation} 
 \pi_{M'} \circ F = \Phi \circ \pi_{M}, \ \ \ \ \ \  \ \ \ \ \ \  F \circ R_{M,g} = R_{M',g} \circ F \ \ \ \forall g\in G.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.803358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a69b91bc565f1ae8459e40bfa6198e8008b789f03a8031405ad29a92f31178","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:variational of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] Fix a degree $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and a target contraction factor $\rho \in (0, \lambda_2)$. Among all polynomials $Q$ of degree at most $m$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
Q(1) = 1, \qquad |Q(\lambda_2)| \le \rho^m,
\end{equation}
the polynomial that \emph{minimizes} the total integrated dissipation
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}(Q) := E_0 - E_1^Q = \sum_{i=2}^n |c_i|^2 \bigl(1 - |Q(\lambda_i)|^2\bigr)
\end{equation}
is (up to sign) the rescaled Chebyshev polynomial
\begin{equation}
Q_m^\ast(\lambda) := \frac{T_m(\lambda / \lambda_2)}{T_m(1 / \lambda_2)},
\end{equation}
where $T_m$ is the Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind, defined by $T_m(\cos \theta) = \cos(m\theta)$.

Moreover, this optimal polynomial simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item Maximizes the cumulative spectral entropy production $\Sigma(m) := S_{\mathrm{spec}}(1) - S_{\mathrm{spec}}(0)$ after one accelerated step;
    \item Achieves the smallest possible rigidity time $T_{\mathrm{rigid}}(\delta)$ among all degree-$m$ polynomial acceleration schemes.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.078181+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a6cd61b2db664b33399f3632df13c2c3db74c2b5eea6ca4bc05b39cd3966cf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:coeff_s of arXiv:2509.15446, 'On the pair correlation function of the $\Sineb$ process'] Consider the functions $ \mathbf{s}_{j,\nu}(u), j\ge 0, u\ge \nu$ defined in (equation eq:s_nu_def1 of the paper). For every $\nu\in {\mathbb R}$, these are well-defined, and they satisfy the  ODEs given in (equation eq:coeff_s_ode1 of the paper) and   (equation eq:coeff_s_ode2 of the paper). Moreover, we have the following norm bounds for $\nu\le u$:
\begin{align}
        \|\mathbf{s}_{k,\nu}(u)\|\le e^{\frac{\beta}{4} k u} \,  \kappa^k \prod_{j=1}^k\frac{n}{j+\frac{4}{\beta}n},
\end{align}
with some $\kappa>0$ (which only depends on $n$). 
Moreover, these functions satisfy 
\begin{align}
    \lim\limits_{\nu\to -\infty} \mathbf{s}_{k,\nu}(u)=e^{k \frac{\beta}{4}u} \mathbf{s}_{k}.
\end{align}
With $\mathbf{q}_{\nu}(u)$ given by (equation eq:q_nu_series of the paper) we also have (equation eq:q_nu_lim of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.040120+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a823ade8a28c59db2d133402cde3b91f98a0bb6a93b69242077133547a40d9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2606.26619, 'Ergodicity of stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on unbounded domains driven by space'] Assume that there exists a constant $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $\sigma(\beta) = 0$. 
\begin{itemize}
  \item [(i)] Suppose that (H4) holds. If $\alpha > 0$, then for any $\lambda>0$ satisfying $\frac{\lambda^2}{2}<\alpha$, there exists a unique invariant measure in $L^1_{\lambda}$. Moreover, the solution is exponentially mixing with respect to the Wasserstein metric $W_1$.
  \item [(ii)] Suppose that (H2) and (H4) hold. If $\alpha > \frac{L_{\sigma}^4}{8}$, then for any $\lambda>0$ satisfying $\frac{\lambda^2}{8}<\alpha - \frac{L_{\sigma}^4}{8}$ and $\frac{\lambda^2}{4}<\alpha$, there exists a unique invariant measure in $L^2_{\lambda}$. Moreover, the solution is exponentially mixing with respect to the Wasserstein metric  $W_2$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.722740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a90f8a6b5d53c2ad2069dfb973caf450bbbf00380ee0a2f39722d49d322df0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 65 (Dyadic assembly for the centered profile) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Let $0<s<1-\beta_{b,c}$, $\beta_{b,c}<\sigma<1-\beta_{b,c}$, and suppose a family of continuous Volterra
kernels has nonnegative block majorants satisfying
\begin{equation}
 b_{N,Q,M}\le C_b
 N^{-3/2+\beta_{b,c}+\eta}\bigl(Q^{3/2+\eta}+M^{3/2+\eta}\bigr),
 \qquad Q\le cN,\quad M\le CN,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
 \beta_{b,c}+2\eta<\min\{\sigma,1-\sigma,1-s\}.
\end{equation}
Then the Volterra block sum maps
\begin{equation}
 C_TL^2\cap L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma}
 \longrightarrow C_TH^{s-1}\cap C_TB_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}.
\end{equation}
For $0<T\le1$ it satisfies the quantitative estimates
\begin{align}
 \|\mathcal Bw\|_{C_TH^{s-1}}
 +\|\mathcal Bw\|_{C_TB_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}}
 &\lesssim T C_b
 \|w\|_{C_TL^2\cap L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma}},
 \\
 \|\mathcal Bw\|_{L_T^1B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}}
 &\lesssim T^2 C_b
 \|w\|_{C_TL^2\cap L_T^\infty B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma}}.

\end{align}
The same assertions hold after taking $L^p(\Omega)$ norms of random kernel
majorants.

For dyadic $L\ge2$, set
\begin{equation}
 \mathfrak F_L:=\{(N,Q,M):Q\le L,\ M\le L,\ N\le L^2\}.
\end{equation}
For every
\begin{equation}
 0<\gamma<\min\{\sigma-\beta_{b,c}-2\eta,\,1-\sigma-\beta_{b,c}-2\eta,\,
 1-s-\beta_{b,c}-2\eta,\,3/2-2\beta_{b,c}-3\eta\},
\end{equation}
the sum over the complement of $\mathfrak F_L$ has operator norm
$O(TC_bL^{-\gamma})$ in the two $C_T$ targets in
\eqref{eq:centered-assembly-map}, and $O(T^2C_bL^{-\gamma})$ in
$L_T^1B_{2,\infty}^{\sigma-1}$.  Thus the finite block sums converge in
operator norm with a deterministic tail modulus.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:06:42.724369+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a9ae8891ccfc950cb273764e24f3e363a3207de20e076dc6920d59228584b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: affine of arXiv:2411.04238, 'Holomorphic jump-diffusions'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ be an affine process on $S\subseteq \mathbb R$.  Suppose that for each $x\in S$, $K(x,d\xi)=\nu_0(d\xi)+x\nu_1(d\xi)$ for some signed measure $\nu_0,\nu_1$ such that both $|\nu_0|$ and $|\nu_1|$ have bounded support.       Set
      \begin{align*}
    \mathcal V&:=\{h\in H(\mathbb C)\colon h,h',h''\text{ are bounded on }\mathbb R\},\\
    \mathcal V^*&:=\{\mathbf{u}\in \mathcal{S}^* \colon h_\mathbf{u}=h,  \ h\in \mathcal{V}\}.
\end{align*}
The following conditions hold true.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is an $S$-valued $\mathcal V$-holomorphic process.
    \item The process $N^h$ given in equation (equation eq:localmart of the paper) is a true martingale for each $h\in \mathcal V$.
    \item 
    Fix $\mathbf{u}\in \mathcal V^*$. Assume that $h_\mathbf{u}(x)=\int_{-\tau}^\tau \exp((\varepsilon+iu)x)g(u) du$ for some $g:[-\tau,\tau]\to \mathbb R$ with $\int_{-\tau}^\tau |g(u)|du<\infty$, $\varepsilon,\tau\in[0,\infty)$ and 
 $$\mathbb E[\exp((\varepsilon+iu)X_t)|X_0=x]=\exp(\phi(t,\varepsilon+iu)+\psi(t,\varepsilon+iu)x),$$
 for all $u\in [-\tau,\tau]$ and $t\in [0,T]$. Then there exists a $\mathcal{V}^*$-valued solution $(\mathbf{c}(t))_{t\in[0,T]}$ of (equation eq: ODE pointwise of the paper) with initial condition $\mathbf{u}$ which satisfies condition~(moment formula ii of the paper) and~(moment formula iii of the paper) of Theorem~(th: moment formula holomorphic of the paper). 

 In particular, the holomorphic formula holds true:
    \begin{align*}
    \mathbb E[h_\mathbf{u}(X_T)]=h_{\mathbf{c}(T)}(x_0).
\end{align*}

\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.931601+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10aa586c2d7944bbd4054412b7d4a8269f682d7cb7d964052c630864329a0fcd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 19 (Rough regularity estimates) of arXiv:2506.19123, 'The longest increasing subsequence of Brownian separable permutons'] We have 
