{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.317890+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10aa66fa8189a8d58ac9e8103ec53cf72e12d7973c4d7bf28229a91e68b54301","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mixture-of-vrjps of arXiv:2411.06195, 'Restrictions of some reinforced processes to subgraphs'] \mbox{}\\
  Assume that the graph $G$ is finite without self-loops and partition its vertex set $\Lambda=I\cup J$,
  $I\cap J=\emptyset$, with $|J|\ge 2$. 
  Consider the vrjp $(X,T)$ in exchangeable time scale on $\Lambda$
  starting at $\rho\in J$ with weights $W$. 
  The restrictions $(X^J,T^J)$ and $(X^{J\neq},T^{J\neq})$ to $J$ without or with
  self-loops removed are
  mixtures of vrjps in exchangeable time scale on $J$ with random weights
    \begin{align}

    W^J(\beta_I)=&(W^J_{ij}(\beta_I))_{i,j\in J}:=W_{JJ}+W_{JI}([H_\beta]_{II})^{-1}W_{IJ}
                    \text{ and }\\

    W^{J\neq}(\beta_I):=&\left(W^J_{ij}(\beta_I)1_{\{ i\neq j\}}\right)_{i,j\in J},
  \end{align}
  respectively. They depend on a random
  vector $\beta_I\in{\mathbb R}^I$, where $\beta_{I\cup\{\rho\}}\in{\mathbb R}^{I\cup\{\rho\}}$ is distributed
  according to $\nu_{I\cup\{\rho\}}^{\widehat W}$ with $\widehat W\in{\mathbb R}^{(I\cup\{\rho\})\times(I\cup\{\rho\})}$
  obtained by restricting the parameters $W$ to $I$ and wiring all points
  in $J$ at $\rho$:
  \begin{align}

    \widehat W_{ij}=\widehat W_{ji}=
    \begin{cases}
      W_{ij} &\text{ for }i,j\in I,\\
      \sum_{k\in J}W_{ik} &\text{ for }i\in I, j=\rho,\\
      0 &\text{ for }i=j=\rho.
    \end{cases}
  \end{align}
  In the case of $(X^{J\neq},T^{J\neq})$ this means the
  following for any event $A\subseteq J^{{\mathbb N}_0}\times{\mathbb R}_+^{{\mathbb N}_0}$. 
  \begin{align}
    P_\rho^{W,\Lambda}((X^{J\neq},T^{J\neq})\in A)
    =&\int_{{\mathbb R}^{I\cup\{\rho\}}}
       P_\rho^{W^{J\neq}(\beta_I),J}((X,T)\in A)\,\nu_{I\cup\{\rho\}}^{\widehat W}(d\beta_{I\cup\{\rho\}})\nonumber\\
    =&\int_{{\mathbb R}^\Lambda}
       P_\rho^{W^{J\neq}(\beta_I),J}((X,T)\in A)\,\nu_\Lambda^W(d\beta_\Lambda).

  \end{align}
  The analogous statement holds for $(X^J,T^J)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.950832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10ab4452bf8979307e9ba975c20f1d4866be45f37e22980830c04ef574b4bab5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:asy-Feller of arXiv:2412.03521, 'On ergodic properties of stochastic PDEs'] A Markov transition semigroup $P_t$ on a Polish space $\mathcal{X}$ is called
  asymptotically strong Feller at $x$ if there exists a totally separating
  system of pseudo-metrics $\left\{d_n; n\ge1\right\}$ for $\mathcal{X}$ and a
  sequence $\left\{t_n; n\ge 1\right\}$ with $t_n>0$ such that
  \begin{align*}
    \liminf_{U\in \mathcal{U}_x}\: \limsup_{n\to\infty}\: \sup_{y\in U}\Norm{P_{t_n}(x,\cdot)-P_{t_n}(y,\cdot)}_{d_n}=0,
  \end{align*}
  where $\mathcal{U}_x$ is the collection of all open sets containing $x$. It is
  called asymptotically strong Feller if this property holds at every $x \in
  \mathcal{X}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.862490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10abb9247fcdace59319a8ee12b56c27e857a043d6aa280ffe41e47ec42b8bc7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 (Winning probability for \(n_m\to\infty\)) of arXiv:2605.21424, 'A dice game, a multinomial walk, and the inverted Dirichlet distribution'] With \(X_2,\ldots,X_m\) and \(\pi_1\) as in Theorem \ref{thm:rep-pi1}(i),  
\begin{align*}
&\lim_{n_m\to \infty}\pi_1(n_1,n_2,\ldots,n_m)\\
&=\int_{\frac{n_2}{n_1}}^\infty\cdots\int_{\frac{n_{m-1}}{n_1}}^\infty\int_{0}^{n_1(1+\sum_{k=2}^{m-1}s_k)}f_\Gamma(s_m)ds_{m}\ \varphi_{<m}(s_2,\ldots,s_{m-1})ds_{m-1}\dotsc ds_2,
\end{align*}
where 
\(\varphi_{<m}\) is the marginal density of \(X_2,\ldots,X_{m-1}\) and \(f_\Gamma\) is a \(\operatorname{Gamma}\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{m-1}n_k,1\Big)\) density.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.279112+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10ace581c85c20f445cfba3e800ed40c827255d62c4e2e1d02e26a1f9b91e953","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 of arXiv:2604.22461, 'Large deviation principles for stationary solutions and invariant measures of a class of S'] Assume that the conditions {\rm [A1-A5]} hold. For any $\gamma_0 > \tilde{\gamma}_0$ and $0 \leq \epsilon< \tilde{\epsilon}$, there is a pathwise unique stationary solution $\cY^{\epsilon} \in  L^{\infty}(\R; L^{2}(\Omega; H))$ for cocycle $(U^{\epsilon},\theta)$. The stationary solution $\cY^{\epsilon}(t)$ satisfies
\begin{small}
  \begin{gather}
     \E \exp \Big\{ \delta \big\{ \| \cY^{\epsilon}(t) \|_{H}^2 \big\} \Big\}  \leq 2 \, e^{2 \delta \frac{C_{A,\rho,\epsilon}}{\lambda_1 \gamma_0}};  \\
     \E \Big[ \exp \Big\{  \frac{\delta}{2} \sup_{t \geq t_1} \big( \frac{\gamma_0}{2} \int_{t_1}^t \| \cY^{\epsilon}(s) \|_{V}^{2} \, \d s-C_{A,\rho,\epsilon}   \big)(t-t_1) \big) \Big\} \Big]  \leq    2 e^{\delta  \frac{ C_{A,\rho,\epsilon} }{\lambda_1 \gamma_0}};  \\
      \E \Big[ \exp \Big\{  \frac{\delta}{2} \sup_{t \geq t_1} \big( \frac{\gamma_0}{2} \int_{t_1}^t \| \cY^{\epsilon}(s) \|_{H}^{\beta} \| \cY^{\epsilon}(s) \|_{V}^{2} \d s-\big( C_{A, \rho}+ \beta C_B \epsilon \big)(t-t_1) \big) \Big\} \Big]  \leq   2  e^{\delta  \frac{2+\beta}{2} \big( \frac{ C_{A,\rho,\epsilon} + \beta C_{B} \epsilon  }{\lambda_1 \gamma_0} \big)}, 
 \end{gather}
\end{small}
for any $t_0 \in \R$, where $\frac{ 4 C_{\rho_1}}{\gamma_0} \leq \delta<  \frac{ (1+\beta)\lambda_1 \gamma_{0}}{2(2+\beta)^2 C_{B} \epsilon} \leq \frac{ \lambda_1 \gamma_{0}}{8 C_{B} \epsilon}$. For any $-\infty<t_0<t<+\infty$, the stationary solution $\cY^{\epsilon} \in L^{2}(\Omega;\sC_{-\infty})$ $\P$-a.s. satisfies
\begin{equation} 
    \scalV{\cY^{\epsilon}(t),v} = \scalV{\cY^{\epsilon}(t_0),v} + \int_{t_0}^{t} \scalV{A^{\epsilon}(\cY^{\epsilon}(s)),v} \d s + \sqrt{\epsilon} \int_{t_0}^{t} \scalH{B (\cY^{\epsilon}(s)) \, \d W_s,v},
\end{equation}
for any $v \in V$. It is called the ``very weak solution'' in \cite[Definition 3.4]{Brzezniak_Large_2017}.

Conversely, if a $\cF_{t}$-adapted process $\cY^{\epsilon}$ satisfies Eq. \eqref{eq: stationary solution} and $\sup_{t \in \R} \E \|\cY^{\epsilon}(t) \|_{H}^2< +\infty$, then it is indeed the stationary solution for cocycle $(U^{\epsilon},\theta)$. There exists a Borel measurable map $\mathcal{G}^{\epsilon}_{-\infty}: \W \rightarrow \sC_{-\infty}$ such that $\mathcal{G}^{\epsilon}_{-\infty}(W)(\cdot)$ is an $\mathcal{F}_{t}$-adapted solution to Eq. \eqref{eq: stationary solution} and $\sup_{t \in \R} \E \|\mathcal{G}^{\epsilon}_{-\infty}(W)(t) \|_{H}^2< +\infty$, called the strong stationary solution for cocycle $(U^{\epsilon},\theta)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.363554+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10b1006478265b4a8f67232978fa07ecadbac2c613c89d0aabc4110837a5b735","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2506.19273, 'A large deviation view of \emph{stationarized} fully lifted blirp interpolation'] Assume the setup of Theorem \ref{thm:thm3}. Let also
\begin{eqnarray}
 \phi_{k_1}^{(r)} & = &
-s(\m_{k_1-1}-\m_{k_1}) \omega(k_1;p) \nonumber \\
&  & \times
\mE_{G,{\mathcal U}_{r+1}} \langle (\p_{k_1-1}\|\x^{(i_1)}\|_2\|\x^{(p_1)}\|_2 -(\x^{(p_1)})^T\x^{(i_1)})(\q_{k_1-1}\|\y^{(i_2)}\|_2\|\y^{(p_2)}\|_2 -(\y^{(p_2)})^T\y^{(i_2)})\rangle_{\gamma_{k_1}^{(r)}} \nonumber \\
 \phi_{22}^{(r)} & = &
-s\m_1 (1-p) \nonumber \\
&  & \times
\mE_{G,{\mathcal U}_{r+1}} \langle (\p_{1}\|\x^{(i_1)}\|_2\|\x^{(p_1)}\|_2 -(\x^{(p_1)})^T\x^{(i_1)})(\q_{1}\|\y^{(i_2)}\|_2\|\y^{(p_2)}\|_2 -(\y^{(p_2)})^T\y^{(i_2)})\rangle_{\gamma_{22}^{(r)}} \nonumber \\
 \phi_{01}^{(r)} & = & (1-\p_0)(1-\q_0)\mE_{G,{\mathcal U}_{r+1}}\langle \|\x^{(i_1)}\|_2^2\|\y^{(i_2)}\|_2^2\rangle_{\gamma_{01}^{(r)}} \nonumber\\
\phi_{02}^{(r)} & = & (s-1)(1-\p_0)\mE_{G,{\mathcal U}_{r+1}}\left\langle \|\x^{(i_1)}\|_2^2 \lp\q_0\|\y^{(i_2)}\|_2\|\y^{(p_2)}\|_2-(\y^{(p_2)})^T\y^{(i_2)}\rp\right\rangle_{\gamma_{02}^{(r)}}, \end{eqnarray}
where
\begin{eqnarray}
\omega(x;p) \triangleq \begin{cases}
                         1, & \mbox{if } x=1 \\
