{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:48:00.296839+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1111d38cb8f190d740889c4aad382c9375d039f8fcb281ff2f4a465479b28caf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thrm:random_apprx_noisy_robustified of arXiv:2609.03129, 'A Closed-Form Formula for Consistent Lipschitz Regression on Metric Spaces with Sparse Neu'] Suppose that Assumption~(assumption:main_1Lip of the paper) holds.  Let
$N_0,N_1\in\mathbb{N}_+$ and set $N\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}} N_0+N_1$.  Fix
$\varepsilon\in(0,1)$ and
$\delta_{\operatorname{net}},
 \delta_{\operatorname{noise}}\in(0,1)$
such that
$
    \varepsilon/2\le\operatorname{diam}(X)
$.
Set
\[
    \eta\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}\frac{\varepsilon}{4},
    \qquad
    \beta\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}\frac{\varepsilon}{2},
    \qquad
    q_\varepsilon
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \left\lceil\varepsilon^{-2}\right\rceil.
\]
Use the first $N_0$ observations as the reference block and the remaining
$N_1$ observations as the averaging block.  Assume that
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
    N_0
    &\ge
    \frac{1}{a(\varepsilon/8)^s}
    \log\left(
        \frac{
            2\mathcal N(X,\rho,\varepsilon/8)
        }{
            \delta_{\operatorname{net}}
        }
    \right),
    \\
    N_1
    &\ge
    \frac{1}{a(\varepsilon/2)^s}
    \max\left\{
        2q_\varepsilon,
        8\log\left(
            \frac{
                2N_0
            }{
                \delta_{\operatorname{net}}
            }
        \right)
    \right\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

For each $k\in[N_0]_+$, set $\xi_k\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}} X_k$.  If at least
$q_\varepsilon$ indices
$j\in\{N_0+1,\ldots,N_0+N_1\}$ satisfy
$
    \rho(X_j,\xi_k)\le\beta,
$
let
$
    j_{k,1}<\cdots<j_{k,q_\varepsilon}
$
be the first $q_\varepsilon$ such indices and define
\[
    \overline Y_k
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \frac{1}{q_\varepsilon}
    \sum_{\ell=1}^{q_\varepsilon}
        Y_{j_{k,\ell}}.
\]
If fewer than $q_\varepsilon$ such indices exist, set
$
    \overline Y_k\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}0
$.

Define, for every $x\in X$,
\begin{equation}

    \hat{f}(x)
    \ensuremath{\stackrel{\mbox{\upshape\tiny def.}}{=}}
    \frac{1}{2}
    \left[
        \min_{k\in[N_0]_+}
        \left\{
            \overline Y_k+\rho(x,\xi_k)
        \right\}
        +
        \max_{k\in[N_0]_+}
        \left\{
            \overline Y_k-\rho(x,\xi_k)
        \right\}
    \right].
\end{equation}
Then, for every fixed
$f\in\operatorname{Lip}((X,\rho);1)$:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] $\hat{f}:X\to\mathbb{R}$ is $1$-Lipschitz for every
    realization of the sample;

    \item[(ii)] the random variable
    $
        \|\hat{f}-f\|_\infty
    $
    is measurable and satisfies
    \begin{equation}

        \mathbb{P}\left(
            \|\hat{f}-f\|_\infty
            \le
            \varepsilon
            \left[
                1
                +
                \sqrt{
                    C
                    \log\left(
                        \frac{
                            2N_0
                        }{
                            \delta_{\operatorname{noise}}
                        }
                    \right)
                }
            \right]
        \right)
        \ge
        1
        -
        \delta_{\operatorname{net}}
        -
        \delta_{\operatorname{noise}}.
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.341047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1111e90efe0264d25b58b00ba5dc5b4d59aac10073097c645ea11cb0687f82ce","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Them Kellerer_consistency of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Let $T$ be an interval, and $ (\mu_t)_{t \in {T}} $ a family of probability measures on some Polish space \( E \). For every \( s < t \in T^2\), we consider a subset $ \mathcal{N}_{s,t} $ of $ \mathcal{P}(E^2)$.  Assume that, for every $s<t \in T^2$:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[(1)] $  \mathcal{N}_{s,t} $ is non-empty;
			\item[(2)] \( \mathcal{N}_{s,t} \subset \textnormal{Marg}(\mu_s, \mu_t) \);
			\item[(3)] \( \mathcal{N}_{s,t} \) is closed for the weak topology;
			\item[(4)] For every \( r < s < t \in T^3\) and  \( (P, P') \in \mathcal{N}_{r,s} \times \mathcal{N}_{s,t} \), \( P \cdot P' \in \mathcal{N}_{r,t} \);
			\item[(5)] For every \( d \geq 1 \) and \( t_1 < \ldots < t_d \in T^d \), if for every $i \in \ent{1}{d-1}$ the sequences \( (Q^{n}_{t_i, t_{i+1}})_{n \geq 1} \in \left(\mathcal{N}_{t_i, t_{i+1}}\right)^{\mathbb{N}^*} \) converge weakly to \( Q_{t_i, t_{i+1}} \), then the sequence \( \left(Q^{n}_{t_1, t_2} \circ \ldots \circ Q^{n}_{t_{d-1}, t_d}\right)_{n \geq 1} \)  tends weakly to \( Q_{t_1, t_2} \circ \ldots \circ Q_{t_{d-1}, t_d} \).
		\end{enumerate}
		Then, there exists a Markov measure \( P \in \textnormal{Marg}((\mu_t)_{t \in T}) \) satisfying \( \textnormal{proj}^{s,t}_{\#}P \in \mathcal{N}_{s,t} \) for every \( s < t \in T^2\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

