{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.094554+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_11216b3539633fdf0b154cd2a6354a80c7a3c18e50ecac868dc5939aec8c3f93","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2604.26026, 'Stochastic Ordering of Dependent Systems under Transformation Models and Archimedean Copul'] Assume the same conditions as in Proposition \ref{heterogeneous-derivative}. Fix $\ell\in\{1,\dots,n\}$ and write $N_\ell:=\{1,\dots,n\}\setminus\{\ell\}$.	Then
\begin{align}
&\sum_{j=n-k}^{n} (-1)^{j-(n-k)} \binom{j-1}{n-k-1}	\sum_{\substack{I\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}\\ |I|=j,\ \ell\in I}}\psi^\prime\left(\sum_{i\in I} t_i(x)\right) \notag\\[1.5ex]
&\qquad\quad =	(-1)^k \sum_{\substack{J\subseteq N_\ell\\ |J|=n-k-1}} \Delta_{(t_i(x))_{i\in N_\ell\setminus J}}\psi^\prime\left(t_\ell(x)+\sum_{j\in J}t_j(x)\right),
\end{align}
where $\Delta_{(t_i(x))_{i\in N_\ell\setminus J}}$ denotes the iterated forward difference with increments $(t_i(x))_{i\in N_\ell\setminus J}$. Consequently,
\begin{align}

\frac{\partial}{\partial \alpha_\ell}F^{(\alpha)}_{n-k:n}(x) =
