{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.219517+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_115107a5988cb9952a39fcbe65a84532d7a6a309e01d1c22d817f1c006b69c97","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:1.9 of arXiv:2412.06086, 'Zeros of conditional Gaussian analytic functions, random sub-unitary matrices and q-series'] For every complex $a$ it holds in the limit $q\to 1^{-}$ that
 \\[-1ex]
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{{Pr}} \Big\{ \Big| N_q\!-\!\frac{1}{1\!-\!q} \Big| \ge \frac{\xi}{(1\!-\!q)^{\alpha}} \Big| \varphi(0)\!=\!a \Big\}
\!=\!
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle{
\frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}\int_{|u|\ge \xi} e^{-u^2} du + o(1)
}\, , & \text{if $\alpha\!=\!\frac12$},
\\[4ex]
\displaystyle{
\exp\Big[\!-\frac{\xi^2\, (1\!+\!o(1))}{(1\!-\!q)^{2\alpha-1}}\Big]
} \, , & \text{if $\frac 12\!<\!\alpha\!<\! 1 $},
\\[4ex]
\displaystyle{
 \exp\Big[
\!-\frac{(\xi\!+\!1) \Psi (1\!+\!\xi) (1\!+\!o(1))}{(1\!-\!q)}
\Big]
} \, , & \text{if $\alpha\!=\!1$},
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
\\[0ex]
where in the last line
$\Psi(s)\!=\!s\!+\!W_0(-se^{-s}) \!+\!\frac{1}{s}\operatorname{Li}_2\! \big(1\!-\!e^{s+W_0(-se^{-s})}\big)$, $s\!>\!1$,
with $W_0$ and $\operatorname{Li}_2$  being, correspondingly, the principal branch of the Lambert-$W$ function and the dilogarithm function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.035407+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1151ec2eb1db79b88cbddeddbc8cf8a8097409b919ec73eedf1fec73ba386b91","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6tdivi of arXiv:2609.03926, 'Overcolored Partition $k$-tuples Restricted by Parity of the Parts'] {wom}}}]
Let $k$ and $m$ be positive integers. If $f(z)\in M_k(\Gamma_0(N),\chi(\bullet))$ has the Fourier expansion $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a(n)q^n\in\mathbb{Z}[[q]]$, then there is a constant $\alpha>0$ such that
