{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.348525+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_115a71864237a8a6d1c19614d67fd26720c39c0f1125c49f63b50530278d7cde","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 (Dimension reduction) of arXiv:2607.15872, 'Existence of $q$-Bass martingales in the semidiscrete setting'] Let $\mu,\nu\in\Prob_1(\RR)$ and $q \in \Prob(\RR)$ be such that $q \ll \lambda$, and assume that $\mu$ is $n$-atomic with representation \eqref{n_atoms_repr}. If \eqref{eq:fixed_point_atoms1} admits a solution, then $\mean(\mu)=\mean(\nu)$. Moreover, $y\in\RR^n$ solves \eqref{eq:fixed_point_atoms1} if and only if $h\in\RR^{n-1}_{>0}$, defined by $h_i:=y_{i+1}-y_i$ for $i=1,\dots,n-1$, solves
