{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.980004+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_116d7b442ee662aa4a7626157af0d3f92cb425c9fb8bf30afea89b665fa1019e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop ppn:dmultiplegraphs of arXiv:2412.00610, 'Joint Poisson Convergence of Monochromatic Hyperedges in Multiplex Hypergraphs'] Fix $d \geq 1$ and integers $2 \leq r_1 \leq r_2 \cdots \leq r_d$. Let $\bm H_n = (H_n^{(1)}, \ldots, H_n^{(d)})$ be a sequence of $d$-layered multiplex hypergraphs, where $H_n^{(i)}$ is $r_i$-uniform, for $1 \leq i \leq d$. Suppose the vertices of $\bm{H}_n$ are colored uniformly at random with $c_n$ colors as in (equation eq:cn of the paper) such that the following hold:  

\begin{itemize}
\item For $1 \leq i \leq d$, 
    \begin{align}
    \lim_{n \to \infty}\bE T_n(H_n^{(i)}) = \lim_{n \to \infty}\Var T_n(H_n^{(i)}) = \lambda_i , 
    \end{align}
    for some constant $\lambda_i \geq 0$.

\item For $1 \leq i \ne j \leq d$, 
\begin{align}
E(H_n^{(i)}) \cap E(H_n^{(j)}) = \emptyset . 
\end{align} 
    \end{itemize}
    Then, as $n \rightarrow \infty$, 
    $$ \bm T(\bm H_n) := \left(\begin{array}{c}
        T(H_n^{(1)}) \\ 
