    The claim remains true if we replace $(f^1,...,f^m)$ on the left side by some sequence $(f^1_N,...,f^m_N)$ equicontinuous and converging uniformly on compact sets as $N\to\infty$ to some $(f^1,...,f^m)$, as long as $\sup_{N} |f^j_N|$ decays exponentially fast at infinity for $j=1,...,m$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.305970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_11a25cb4e4b1b09d6e009881a809c97a06acd4dbe0b3c03377eea4701753b8a9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 70 (Martingale tightness bound) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Fix $q\ge 1$ and 
