{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:55.000597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12c3c86ec6b89e24f787db9e8853539a54ff0fddbfe9bbe1af78c8979d2c730c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:separation-cdia of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] (1) $\textsf{C}_\Diamond \in \mathcal{V}^e$. (2) $\textsf{C}_\Diamond \notin \mathcal{V}^i$.
  \begin{proof}
    (1) Take any f-model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$.
    Here, $\mathfrak{M}$ does not need to satisfy the $\Box$-p and $\Diamond$-p conditions.
    Then take any $\varphi, \psi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ and $x, x'$ such that $x \leq x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond(\varphi \lor \psi)$.
    Then there is $y'$ such that $x' \sqsubset y' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi \lor \psi$.
    Here, either $y' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \varphi$ or $y' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \psi$,
    then either $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi$ or $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \psi$, respectively,
    so $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi \lor \Diamond \psi$.
    Therefore, $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \textsf{C}_\Diamond$.

    (2) Consider the model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, where $W = \set{ x, x', y, y' }$,
    $\mathord{\leq} = \set{ (x, x), (x, x'), (x', x'), (y, y), (y', y') }$,
    $\mathord{\sqsubset} = \set{ (x, y), (x', y'), (y, y), (y', y') }$,
    and $V(p) = \set{ y }$, $V(q) = \set{ y' }$.
    The model $\mathfrak{M}$ is visualized in Figure (fig:kdia-countermodel of the paper).

    \begin{figure}[ht]
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}
      \fill (0, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 2) node[left]{$x$};

      \draw (2, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 2) node[right]{$y \Vdash^i p$};

      \draw [thick, ->, squig, rel] (0,2) to (2, 2);
      \draw (1, 2) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \fill (0, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 4) node[left]{$x'$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0, 2) to (0,4);
      \draw (0,3) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \draw (2, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 4) node[right]{$y' \Vdash^i q$};

      \draw [thick, ->, squig, rel] (0,4) to (2, 4);
      \draw (1, 4) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \end{tikzpicture}
      \caption{The model $\mathfrak{M}$. $\circ$ denotes a $\sqsubset$-reflexive world.}

    \end{figure}

    It is easy to see that $w \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond (p \lor q)$ for every $w \in W$,
    but $x \nVdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond p \lor \Diamond q$.
    Therefore, $x \nVdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \textsf{C}_\Diamond$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.634031+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12c6427d8e94de6d327e4475672dc44a8ad7f1fc8d1b18fa3ac59b40431cb921","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:secondhalfsum of arXiv:2412.09145, 'Asymptotic expansions for normal deviations of random walks conditioned to stay positive'] Fix some $a>1$ and $b>0$. Let the sequence $v_k$ be such that
\begin{align}

    |v_k| \le \frac{C\log^d k}{k^a}.
\end{align}
Then there exist polynomials $K_\nu$ of degree $\nu$ such that the following equality holds uniformly in $z$:
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=2}^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor} 
        \frac{z^m v_k}{(n-k)^{b}}e^{-\frac{z^2}{2 (n-k)}}
=
    \frac{z^m}{n^b} 
    e^{-\frac{z^2}{2 n}}
    \sum_{\nu=0}^{\lfloor a \rfloor-1}
            \frac{K_{\nu}(z^2/n)}{n^{\nu}}
+
    O\left(\frac{\log^{d+\delta_a} n}{n^{a+b-m/2-1}}\right),
\end{align*}
where $\delta_a:={\rm 1}\{a\in\mathbb N\}$.","status":"unverified"}

