{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.068114+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1363cf2e27b868493112c7b43a5a028c85abcee20293e9bab88fb60085a18c9f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Variational formula for left edge) of arXiv:2606.27774, 'Variational Formulas for the Spectrum of\\ Block Wishart Matrices'] Fix $k \ge 1$ integer, $\alpha \in (0,\infty)$, and $\nu\in\cuP_0(\sa{k}{\C})$.
Let $\mu_{\alpha,\nu}$ 
be as in Proposition~\ref{prop:asymptotic_FP}.
Then we have
\begin{equation}
 \inf \supp(\mu_\up{\alpha,\nu})= \sup_{\bS \in \cP_{-}} \lambda_{\min}\left(\bK_{(\alpha,\nu)}(\bS)\right). 
\end{equation}
where $\cP_-$ is given by
   \begin{equation}

  \cP_- \equiv \cP_-(\alpha,\nu) := \{\bS\succ\bzero : \bS^{-1}+ \bT\succ\bzero \;\; \forall\,\bT\in\supp(\nu),\;\; 
  \cuD_\bS \bK(\bS)[\,\cdot\,]\succ 0\; 
  \}.
   \end{equation}
   Furthermore, the set $\cP_-$ is equivalent to
   \begin{equation}

  \cP_- = \{\bS\succ\bzero : \bS^{-1}+ \bT\succ\bzero \;\; \forall\,\bT\in\supp(\nu),\;\;\textrm{there exists  }\bB\succ\bzero \textrm{ s.t.}  \;
  \cuD_\bS \bK(\bS)[\bB]\succ \bzero\; 
  \}.
   \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.931473+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1364b94ae14398b1fe43d0b14e08b5472cc1683219df22f6c88aa2f626a2d146","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 17 ({\cite[Theorem 1) of arXiv:2506.19123, 'The longest increasing subsequence of Brownian separable permutons'] {DG88}}]
	Let $(Y_n)_{n \geq 1}$ be a sequence of random variables with values in the set of positive integers. We assume that for any $m,n \geq 1$, we have the stochastic domination
	\begin{equation}
        Y_{m+n} \succeq \sum_{i=1}^{Y_m}Y_n^{(i)},
    \end{equation}
    where the $Y_n^{(i)}$ are i.i.d.\ copies of $Y_n$ and are independent of $Y_m$. Let $\lambda=\sup_{n \geq 1} \bbE[Y_n]^{1/n}$. If $\lambda<+\infty$, then we have the convergence in probability
    \[ Y_n^{1/n} \xrightarrow[n \to +\infty]{\mathbb{P}} \lambda. \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.019125+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_13662af4a378f20aa4b97769329fa2d206ce38028fa852921173ff1c89a7eb65","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 8 ($L^1$-Contraction) of arXiv:2605.05986, '\bf Convergence rate of the occupation measure of { classes of ergodic processes toward th'] Let $(P_t)_{t\ge0}$ denote a  {{Markov}} transition   semi-group  {with unique invariant distribution  $\nu$}. If this semi-group satisfies  the following contraction inequality:
