{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:20:15.588585+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1452a1ad438b541fed41bb267302f9028fc6838200c68335f1b9f5efc357d119","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2609.00884, 'The weakness of typicality'] Let $k \in \omega$ and $G = (V, E)$ be a graph.
\begin{itemize}
    \item A \emph{$k$-coloring} is a function $f : V \to k$ such that for every $x, y \in V$, if $(x, y) \in E$ then $f(x) \neq f(y)$.
    \item $G$ is \emph{$k$-colorable} if it has a $k$-coloring, and $G$ is \emph{locally $k$-colorable} if every finite subgraph is $k$-colorable.
    \item A set $H \subseteq V$ is \emph{$k$-homogeneous} for $G$ if every finite $V_0 \subseteq V$ induces a subgraph that is $k$-colorable by a coloring that colors every vertex in~$V_0 \cap H$ color~0.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.066576+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1452ea8687513ae49cdb212fca67c0cd17f9714eda36d646849c86912d919fb3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2606.27520, 'Critical curve of loop percolation on the $d$-regular tree'] Let $\alpha>0$ and $\kappa\geq\kappa_c$. For all distinct vertices $u$ and $v$,  
\begin{equation}
 \mu_\kappa(\ell\,:\, u \xleftrightarrow{\ell} v) = - \log( 1 - \tau_\kappa^{2d(u,v)}).
\end{equation}
In particular, 
\begin{equation}
        \P\big(\exists \ell\in\mathcal L_{\alpha,\kappa}\,:\,u \xleftrightarrow{\ell} v\big) = 1 - (1 - \tau_\kappa^{2d(u,v)})^\alpha \leq 2\alpha\, \tau_\kappa^{2d(u,v)}. 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.006213+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1453595220de6840f04e9577023a38ac062b53c7846c059641ae5c790a47b8dd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem mdd-HIHS of arXiv:2606.27573, 'Conditional Path Decomposition at the Infimum and Maximum Drawdowns for Spectrally Negativ'] Fix $a<0<b$ and let $r=b-a$.  Under $\mathbb{P}_{a,b}^{<}$, the following identities hold.
