{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.324728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1478809df36e4762802d1cc801684d6f4e9ed44266f948b837e976cf4df34d54","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p:AndiPolar of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Assume~(equation eq:QPP of the paper) holds.
Then,~$\mbfM_\circ^\mathrm{c}$ is $\boldsymbol\mssW^\mbfs$-exceptional for all~$\mbfs\in\mbfT$.

\begin{proof}
Up to resorting to the compactification~$\mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}$ of~$\mbfM$ and to the quasi-homeo\-morphism~${\boldsymbol\upalpha}$ constructed~(equation eq:Compactification of the paper) in the proof of Theorem~(t:BendikovSaloffCoste of the paper), we may and will assume with no loss of generality that~$(M,\tau)$ (hence~$(\mbfM,\tau_\mrmp)$) is compact.

For~$\mbfB\subset\mbfM$, define the \emph{first touching time}~[MaRoe92] of~$\mbfB$ for~$\boldsymbol\mssW^\mbfs$ as
\begin{equation}
\tau^\mbfs_\mbfB\eqdef \inf\set{t\geq 0 : \overline{\mbfX^\mbfs_{[0,t]}}\cap \mbfB\neq \emp}\,\mathrm{,}\;\,
\end{equation}
where, for simplicity of notation,~$\overline{\mbfX^\mbfs_{[0,t]}}$ is the $\tau_\mrmp$-closure of~$\left\{\mbfX^\mbfs_r(\omega): r\in [0,t]\right\}$.

In the rest of the proof, we choose~$\mbfB=\mbfM_\circ^\mathrm{c}$ and omit it from the notation.

Let~$P^\mbfs_{\boldsymbol\nu}$ be defined according to~(equation eq:MarkovProbab of the paper) with~$P^\mbfs_{\,\cdot\,}$ as in~(equation eq:BSCRescalingProcess of the paper).
Since~$\mbfM_\circ^\mathrm{c}$ is measurable and~$\boldsymbol\mssW^\mbfs$ has infinite lifetime by Proposition~(p:BendikovSaloffCosteRescaling of the paper) (cf.\ Thm.~(t:BendikovSaloffCoste of the paper)(i:t:BendikovSaloffCoste:5 of the paper)), it suffices to show that
\begin{equation}
P^\mbfs_{\boldsymbol\nu}\left\{\tau^\mbfs<\infty\right\}=0\,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}

To this end, note that~$\mbfM_\circ^\mathrm{c}=\cap_n \pr_n^{-1}\tparen{(M^{\scriptscriptstyle\times n}_\circ)^\mathrm{c}}$ and ${\pr_n}_* \boldsymbol\mssW^\mbfs = \mssW^{n,\mbfs}$.
Since $(\mbfM,\tau_\mrmp)$ is a compact space,~$\pr_n$ is a closed map by e.g.~[Eng89], and therefore~$\pr_n(\overline{\mbfX^\mbfs_{[0,t]}})=\overline{X^{n,\mbfs}_{[0,t]}}$.
Thus, letting~$\tau^{n,\mbfs}$ be the first touching time of~$(M^{\scriptscriptstyle\times n}_\circ)^\mathrm{c}$ for~$\mssW^{n,\mbfs}$,
\begin{equation}
\set{\tau^{n,\mbfs}<\infty} \subset \set{\tau^{n+1,\mbfs}<\infty}\subset \left\{\tau^\mbfs<\infty\right\}= \lim_n \set{\tau^{n,\mbfs}<\infty} \,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, since~$(M^{\scriptscriptstyle\times n}_\circ)^\mathrm{c}$ is $\mssW^{n,\mbfs}$-exceptional by Lemma~(l:ExceptionalFiniteDim of the paper),
\begin{equation}
P_{\boldsymbol\nu} \set{\tau^{n,\mbfs}<\infty}= P_{\nu^{\scriptscriptstyle\otimes n}}\set{\tau^{n,\mbfs}<\infty}=0 \,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
Thus, finally, combining~(equation eq:p:AndiPolar:1.5 of the paper) and~(equation eq:p:AndiPolar:2 of the paper) yields~(equation eq:p:AndiPolar:0 of the paper) by the Borel--Cantelli Lem\-ma.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.784411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_147a3c2ccabefff7034faf4993a4d20a885a624a840b5c4d9064da3be3978d01","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemat 13 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Given a contraction 
  \begin{equation}\begin{bmatrix} A_1 & A_2\cr A_2 & A_1\end{bmatrix},\end{equation}
  there exists an orthogonal projection
