{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.199905+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14802cc0cfdff9ffaf0e98462f3b40e20a8517b7bf394a34aa4d181d6093ae5b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: scaling limit of discrete polynomial chaos of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
Assume that (equation eq: definition of a general heavy-tailed random variable of the paper) holds with \(\gamma \in (1,2)\) and let \(V_{\delta}\) be defined by (equation eq: general scale of the noise of the paper).
Assume that there exist $q\in (\gamma,2]$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label={(\roman*)}]
	\item  For every $k \in {\ensuremath{\mathbb N}} $, there exists some $\boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}\in L_s^q(\Omega^k)$ such that $\lim\limits_{\delta\downarrow 0} \| \overline{\psi}_{\delta}^{(k)}- \boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}\|_q=0$;

	\item  
	The following summability conditions are satisfied: for every $C \, \geqslant\, 0$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\limsup_{\delta \downarrow 0} \sum_{k \, \geqslant\, 0} C^k \| \overline{\psi}_{\delta}^{(k)} \|_{q} < +\infty
		\quad \text{ and } \quad
		\lim\limits_{M \to +\infty} \displaystyle\displaystyle\limsup_{\delta \downarrow 0} \sum\limits_{k > M} C^k \| \overline{\psi}_{\delta}^{(k)} \|_{q} = 0.
	\end{equation*}	
\end{enumerate}
Then we have the following joint convergence in distribution in $H_{\rm loc}^{-s}(\Omega) \times \mathbb{R}$, $s > \frac{D}{2}$
\begin{equation*}
	\left(\zeta_{\delta}, \Phi_{\delta}\right) \xrightarrow{(d)} (\boldsymbol\zeta,\boldsymbol\Phi) \quad \text{ as } \delta\downarrow 0 \,.
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, for every $1 < p < \gamma$ and $\delta \in (0,1)$, for the same constant $C_0 = C_0(p,q,\gamma,\Omega)$ appearing in Theorem (thm: continuous polynomial chaos of the paper)-(equation eq: moments constant C0 of the paper), we have
\(
	\| \Phi_{\delta}\|_p \, \leqslant\, \sum\limits_{k \in {\ensuremath{\mathbb N}} } (C_0)^k \|\overline{\psi}_{\delta}^{(k)}\|_q
\)
and $\|\Phi_{\delta}\|_p \to \|\boldsymbol\Phi\|_p$ as $\delta \downarrow 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

