{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.819531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1482f70f0c1f353a284d7f7074c3d61ca80ac5e1cfecca3bc4341d795d7ff39f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: unifying-theorem of arXiv:2504.02207, 'Finite-Time behaviors of M/M/$n$ Queue: Mixing Time, Mean Queue Length and Tail Bounds'] Let $\pi_{n,t}$ be the queue length distribution at time $t \geq 0$ of the continuous-time $M/M/n$ system defined above and let the stationary distribution be $\nu_n$. \\
    For $\alpha \geq 1, n \geq 2$, we have that:
    \begin{align}

        \chi(\pi_{n,t},\nu_n) \leq e^{-(\sqrt{n}-\sqrt{\lambda_n})^2 t} \chi(\pi_{n,0},\nu_n) \, \forall t \geq 0.
    \end{align}
    For $\alpha \in (1/2,1), n \geq 2$, we have that:
    \begin{align}

        \chi(\pi_{n,t},\nu_n) \leq e^{-C_n (\sqrt{n}-\sqrt{\lambda_n})^2 t} \chi(\pi_{n,0},\nu_n) \, \forall t \geq 0,
    \end{align}    
    for some $C_n > 0$ such that $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} C_n = 1$. \\
    For $\alpha \in (0,1/2), n \geq 2$, we have that:
    \begin{align}

        \chi(\pi_{n,t},\nu_n) \leq e^{-D_n t} \chi(\pi_{n,0},\nu_n) \, \forall t \geq 0,
    \end{align}
    for some $D_n > 0$ such that $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} D_n = 1/25$. \\










    Finally, for $\alpha = 1/2$ and $n \geq 110$, we have 
    \begin{align}

        \chi(\pi_{n,t},\nu_n) \leq e^{-H_n t} \chi(\pi_{n,0},\nu_n) \, \forall t \geq 0
    \end{align}
    for some $H_n > \frac{1}{10861}$ and $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} H_n = \frac{1}{1781}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.286458+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14841cefb7ee6c2149162541ec9fdf46f852254d26f9b883971c00fdd7cab750","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Let $p\in(2,3)$ and $X:[0,T]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^d$ be a semimartingale defined on the probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{F},\mathbb{P})$. Let us define
