{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.522024+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15dfc6d551f15f99ac20f3932a544bab4dbbc2a8c134e011ed4c3d228bc2d67d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma condU of arXiv:2412.03325, 'Functional limit theorem for branching processes \\ in nearly degenerate varying environme'] For $0 < u < t \leq 1$ and $x_0 \in \mathbb{N}$
the generating function of the conditional transition 
probabilities is given by
\begin{equation} 
\mathbf{E} \left[ s^{U(t)} | U(u) = x_0, U(1) > 0 \right]
= \frac{(h_{u/t}(s))^{x_0} - 
(h_{u/t}(h_{t} (0) s))^{x_0} }
{1 - (h_{u}(0))^{x_0}} =: k_{u,t;x_0}(s).
\end{equation}
The family of laws with generating functions
$(g_t)_{t \in (0,1]}$ is an entrance law for 
the transition generating functions $k_{u,t;x}$, that is, 
for $\varepsilon \in (0,1]$ and for any $t \in [\varepsilon, 1]$,
\[
\mathbf{E}_\varepsilon \left[ s^{U(t)} | U(1) > 0 \right] = g_t(s).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.499651+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15e0fcce695d04bd39ee8f9bec90c16d63e2b90fb4633bb05ba8f5c982e0bd3b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 13 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\mathbf{X} = (X,\bbx)$ and $\bar{\mathbf{X}} = (X,\bar{\bbx})$ be two pairs of paths with the same first order process $X : [0,T] \to V$ and paths $\bbx,\bar{\bbx}: [0,T]^2 \to V \otimes V$ such that $\mathbf{X} \in \scrc^{\alpha}([0,T],V)$ for some $\alpha \in (\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{2}]$. Then the following statements are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] We have $\bar{\mathbf{X}} \in \scrc^{\alpha}([0,T],V)$.

\item[(ii)] There exists a function $F \in \calc^{2 \alpha}([0,T], V \otimes V)$ such that \eqref{F-second-order} is fulfilled.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:33:11.957423+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15e308b89e6de8ff4e1a49f0b730a80fd8b4adc9586402ee7e090f5e8783f7b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:expected-max of arXiv:2607.14087, 'Stochastic Domination of Gaussian Maxima: A Resolution of the Weak Simplex Conjecture'] \label[corollary]{cor:mean-width}
Let $S$ be an $m\times m$ positive-semidefinite matrix with $S_{ii}\leq1$,
and let $Y\sim\mathcal N(0,S)$.  Then
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb E\max_iY_i
  \leq \mathbb E\max_i\xi_i^\triangle
  =\sqrt{\frac{m}{m-1}}\,\mathbb E\max_i Z_i,
\end{equation}
where $\xi^\triangle\sim\mathcal N(0,G_\triangle)$.  Equality holds if and only if
$S=G_\triangle$.

Consequently, among convex hulls of $m$ points contained in the Euclidean unit
ball of $\mathbb R^d$, for every $d\geq m-1$, the regular $(m-1)$-simplex uniquely
maximizes mean width, up to rotation and relabeling. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.891915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15e364a0a338acc15c06bced430687c43d4e2f6c080732d6a5cd9dbfd3386d69","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 40 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] Suppose that $[\ttX]\vdash\Gamma$ is a sequent of $\sfu\RS$, and let $\epsilon_u$ be a countable enumeration of elements of $u$. Then we can construct an $\epsilon_u$-recursive $\rmbeta$-preproof $\pi$ of $[\ttX]\vdash\Gamma$ such that either
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\pi$ is a $\rmbeta$-proof, or
        \item If $B$ is an infinite branch of $\pi$ such that the union of its associated template orders is well-founded, then we can find a $B$-recursive well-founded model of $\KP + \lnot\bigvee\Gamma$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.644996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15e506b6894730acfa8fe627d90264fe0e53f56941fa86c5580b590530646ede","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2609.03556, 'An Effective SPDE Model for a Schr\"odinger Equation with a Fluctuating Magnetic Potential'] \label[definition]{def:weak_sol_effective}
    Given $\psi_0\in L^2(\R^d)$, we say that a progressively measurable process $\Psi^{\tau}$ with paths in $C_{w}([0,T];L^2)\ \mathbb{P}-a.s.$ is a weak solution of \eqref{eq:simplified_model} with $\Psi^{\tau}_0=\psi_0$ if there exists $\bar{R}>0$ such that
    \begin{align}
        \sup_{t\in [0,T]}\|\Psi^{\tau}_t\|\leq \bar{R}\quad \mathbb{P}-a.s.
    \end{align} 
    and the following identity holds for each
    $\phi\in \mathscr{S}(\R^d)$ and $t\in [0,T]$:
   \begin{align}
       i\langle \Psi^{\tau}_t,\phi\rangle&=i\langle \psi_0,\phi\rangle+\int_0^t \langle\Psi^{\tau}_s, (-\Delta+V)\phi\rangle ds\notag\\
       &\quad+4i\tau \int_0^t\langle \Psi^{\tau}_s, \operatorname{div}\left(Q\nabla\phi\right)\rangle ds-i\tau \int_0^t\langle R\Psi^{\tau}_s,\phi\rangle ds\notag\\
       &\quad-i\sqrt{8\tau}\sum_{k\in I }\int_0^t
       \langle \Psi^{\tau}_s,\sigma_k\cdot\nabla\phi\rangle dW^k_s\notag\\
       &\quad+\sqrt{2\tau}\sum_{k,j\in I }\int_0^t
       \langle \Psi^{\tau}_s\sigma_k\cdot\sigma_j,\phi\rangle dB^{k,j}_s
       \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s.
   \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.425474+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15e63e2c0d10453b5277b1e44fc8d8f41770114ed23c6d840a16c48a0d990cdd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Existence of arXiv:2605.18744, 'Lattice random-field Widom--Rowlinson models'] Assume that $\Lambda_n\uparrow \mathbb{Z}^d$, i.e., $(\Lambda_n)_n$ is an increasing sequence of finite sets with $\bigcup_{n\in \mathbb{N}}\Lambda_n=\mathbb{Z}^d$. Then, for $\zeta \in\mathcal{H}$ and $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$,
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item  there are $\mu_{\pm}^{\zeta,\lambda}\in \mathcal{G}(\zeta, \lambda)$ such that the sequence $\mu_{\Lambda_n,\pm }^{\zeta,\lambda}$ converges locally to $\mu_{\pm}^{\zeta,\lambda}$,
        \item for every $f$ local, $\zeta\mapsto \mu_{\pm}^{\zeta,\lambda}(f) $ is measurable when endowing $\mathcal{H}$ with the product-$\sigma$-algebra, and
        \item for every infinite-volume Gibbs measure $\nu^{\zeta,\lambda}$ of the WRM with external field $\zeta$, it holds that
        \begin{align*}
            \mu_{-}^{\zeta,\lambda}\preceq \nu ^{\zeta,\lambda}\preceq \mu_{+}^{\zeta,\lambda}.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.862151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15e8430efb009fb69a1397fc5d6c78a9b4e92f5572b99f83cf390e43eabb0185","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 14 of arXiv:2506.10817, 'On the Weak Error for Local Stochastic Volatility Models'] Let $X,\xi$ be two random variables, such that $\xi$ is square integrable. Let $\eps,\delta\in(0,1/2)$ and $K_\eps$ some Kernel satisfying Assumption \ref{Ass:kernel} below. 
    If $\mathbb{V}(X)>0$ define the estimator $\hE_\delta^\eps$ of the conditional expectation as
    \begin{align}
        \hE_\delta^\eps\bigl[\xi^2\big|X\bigr]=\frac{
        \E\bigl[ \xi^2 K_\eps\bigl(x-X\bigr)\bigr]\big|_{x=X}+\delta
        }{{\E\bigl[ K_\eps\bigl(x-X\bigr)\bigr]\big|_{x=X}}+\delta }.
    \end{align}
    as well as $ \hE^\eps\bigl[\xi^2\big|X\bigr]= \hE^\eps_0\bigl[\xi^2\big|X\bigr]$.
    In case $\mathbb{V}(X)=0$ set $\hE_\delta^\eps\bigl[\xi^2\big|X\bigr]=\hE^\eps\bigl[\xi^2\big|X\bigr]=\E[\xi^2]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.793875+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15ea4aa1bf5d6a464037f840dc60de98827ed0a9649e9aef8f6c3f77e9ff8c57","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 30 of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] One has the lower and upper bounds

$$-\ln (N+1) - \ln |\delta_{j+\sigma_j}| + \ln|\lambda_{j+1}^{\vsg} - \lambda_j^{\vsg}|

\leq \big( M_{\bs{\sg}_{N-1}}^{\, \vsg} \big)_{ j+\sigma_j, j }

\leq \ln 3 - \ln |\delta_{j+\sigma_j}| + \sul{s=0}{1} \ln(|\lambda_{j+s}^{\vsg}| + 1)  \, .$$

Moreover, $\big( M_{\bs{\sg}_{N-1}}^{\, \vsg} \big)_{ j+\sigma_j, j } \geq 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.635748+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15ec6e53415327b9cea7c24f502f8a109b31f8284dde909c9b73228c7c89a515","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2607.03639, '\vspace{-0.6in}An AI-Assisted Solution to the Signed BAR Conjecture: Uniqueness in the Har'] Under the standing assumptions (equation eq:Mmatrix of the paper)--(equation eq:stability of the paper), let $\pi_0$ and $(\nu_i^0)_{i=1}^d$ be the stationary distribution of the process and the corresponding boundary measure constructed in (prop:stationary-BAR of the paper). Every finite signed BAR tuple is a scalar multiple of the stationary BAR tuple. More precisely, if (equation eq:signed-BAR of the paper) holds, then there exists $c\in\mathbb R$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \bar\pi=c\pi_0,
