{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.749788+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_160c7449d0db56e9558f73a50a093937afbc4b684fe61f86998a235311f9464f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2608.18834, 'The Parabolic Anderson Model's Total Mass at Small Times: Geometry, Fluctuations, and Reno'] Define the renormalization function $\msf c:(0,\infty)\to\mbb R$ as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\rho>0$, then no renormalization
is necessary; hence $\msf c(\eps)=0$ for all $\eps>0$.
\item If $\rho=0$, then $\xi$ needs a logarithmic
renormalization: If $\mf b$ is the constant defined in \eqref{Equation: b Constant}, then
\begin{align}

\msf c(\eps)=\mf b\,\log(1/\eps),\quad\eps>0.
\end{align}
\item If $\rho<0$, then $\xi$ requires a power-law renormalization:
\begin{align}

\msf c(\eps)=\mf d\,\eps^{\rho},\qquad\eps>0,
\end{align}
where we define the constant
\begin{align}

\mf d=
\frac{\Ga(-\rho)}{2^H\Ga(d-H)\Ga(1-H_0)}\prod_{j=0}^d\frac{\Ga(2H_j+1)}{\Ga(H_j)}
\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{(\cos\theta)^{-(2H_0-1)}(\sin\theta)^{d-H}}{(\cos\theta+\sin\theta)^{-\rho}}\d\theta.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.988584+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16100041894f283918b4211f559296d6c291601b70a925e4471596edd2e5055f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:recurrent-nonstab of arXiv:2604.13968, 'Divisible sandpiles via random walks in random scenery'] For every $p\in(0,3)$ and every $d_f\in(\max(p,2),3)$, there exists a recurrent bounded-degree graph~$G$ with
		\begin{equation}
			|B(o,R)|\leq C\,R^{d_f}
			\qquad\text{for all } R\geq 1\,,
		\end{equation}
		such that for every $\mu<1$, there is an i.i.d.\ law bounded from below with $\mathbb{E}[\sigma]=\mu$ and $\mathbb{E}[|\sigma-\mu|^p]<\infty$ for which $u_\infty(o)=\infty$ almost surely. Hence the divisible sandpile does not stabilize.
		Moreover, $|B(o,r_k)|\geq c\,r_k^{d_f}$ along a subsequence $r_k\to\infty$, so $\limsup_{r\to\infty}\frac{\log|B(o,r)|}{\log r}=d_f$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

