{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.474356+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16250c464c7b7cd7988a038ba350b2e13ada7983c495ab9da81e8b566f521a05","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition cecdon1 of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $\mathcal{N} \subset \mathcal{M}$ be finite-dimensional von Neumann algebras, and let $\sigma$ be a full-rank state on $\mathcal{M}$. A linear mapping $E_{\mathcal{N}}: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{N}$ is a \emph{conditional expectation} if it is completely positive and unital, and there holds 
    \begin{align} 
        E_{\mathcal{N}}(AXB) = A E_{\mathcal{N}}(X) B, \quad \forall X \in \mathcal{M},\ A, B \in \mathcal{N}\,.
    \end{align}
Further, such a mapping $E_{\mathcal{N}}$ is called a \emph{conditional expectation with respect to $\sigma$} if it is trace-preserving with respect to $\sigma$:
    \begin{align} 
        \tr(\sigma E_{\mathcal{N}}(X)) = \tr(\sigma X), \quad \forall X \in \mathcal{M}\,.
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

