{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.325160+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1632e9f583303066b9c4633a08f3a4d8396b54c2691938e7c71a5dc1ef9c761d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:TransferQuasiHomeo of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Fix~$\pi\in\msP(\mbfT_\circ)$ and assume~(equation eq:QPP of the paper) holds.
Then,
\begin{enumerate}[$(i)$, leftmargin=2em]
\item $\mathrm{em}$ is a quasi-homeomorphism intertwining~$\big({\widehat\mcE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ and~$\big({\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi)}\big)$;

\item $\big({\widehat\mcE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ is a $\tau_\mrma$-quasi-regular Dirichlet form properly associated with the $\mcQ_\pi$-symmetric Hunt process with state space~$\msP^\pa_\iso$
\begin{equation}
\widehat\mcW_\pi\eqdef \mathrm{em}_*\widehat\boldsymbol\mssW_\pi=\seq{\widehat\Omega_\circ,\widehat\msF^\pi,\widehat\msF^\pi_\bullet,\widehat\mcX^\pi_\bullet, {\big(\widehat\mcP^\pi_\eta\big)}_{\eta\in\msP^\pa_\iso}} \,\mathrm{;}\;\,
\end{equation}
with
\[
\widehat\mcX^\pi_\bullet= \mathrm{em}\circ \widehat\mbfX^\pi_\bullet \qquad \text{and} \qquad \widehat\mcP^\pi_\eta= \widehat P^\pi_{\mathrm{em}^{-1}(\eta)}\,\mathrm{,}\;\, \quad \eta\in\msP^\pa_\iso \,\mathrm{;}\;\,
\]

\item 
$\widehat\mcW_\pi$ is the ---unique up to~$\mcQ_\pi$-equivalence--- $\mcQ_\pi$-sub-stationary $\mcQ_\pi$-special standard process process solving to the martingale problem for~$\tparen{\widehat\mcL,\hCylP{}{0}}$.
In particular, for every~$u\in\hCylP{}{0}$ ($\subset \mcC_b(\tau_\mrma)$), the process
\begin{equation}
M^{\class{u}}_t\eqdef u(\widehat\mcX^\pi_t)- u(\widehat\mcX^\pi_0)-\int_0^t (\widehat\mcL u)(\widehat\mcX^\pi_s)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} s
\end{equation}
is an adapted square-integrable martingale with predictable quadratic variation
\begin{equation}
\tsharpb{M^{\class{u}}}_t = \int_0^t \widehat\mcG(u)(\widehat\mcX^\pi_s)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} s \,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{proof}
(i:t:TransferP:4 of the paper) By Proposition~(p:AndiHatPolar of the paper)(i:p:AndiHatPolar:2 of the paper) the set~$\widehat\mbfM_\circ$ is $\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi$-polar.
Since~$\mathrm{em}\colon \widehat\mbfM_\circ\to\msP^\pa_\iso$ is a $\widehat\tau_\mrmp/\tau_\mrma$-homeomorphism intertwining~$\big({\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi)}\big)$ and~$\big({\widehat\mcE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\mcE_\pi)}\big)$, it is a quasi-homeomorphism.

(i:t:TransferP:5 of the paper) The $\tau_\mrma$-quasi-regularity of~$\big({\widehat\mcE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ follows from~(i:t:TransferP:4 of the paper) and the $\widehat\tau_\mrmp$-quasi-regularity of $\big({\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi,\msD(\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi)}\big)$ shown in Theorem~(t:DirInt of the paper)(i:t:DirInt:3 of the paper).

(i:t:TransferP:6 of the paper) A proof follows similarly to the proof of Theorem~(t:DirInt of the paper)(i:t:DirInt:7 of the paper).
Again~$\mcQ_\pi$ does not need to have full $\tau_\mrma$-support on~$\msP^\pa_\iso$, but one can restrict the arguments to~$\supp\mcQ_\pi$ since~$(\supp\mcQ_\pi)^\mathrm{c}$ is $\widehat\boldsymbol\mssE_\pi$-polar.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

