{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.501183+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_166d050e6ef29dc42cdc6969c74c03859217973bdf55bda068c3b3e4a33b6666","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2605.30030, 'Critical exponents for planar random-cluster model with cluster-weight~$q=4$'] Let~$\upsilon:[0,\infty)^2\rightarrow (0,\infty)$ be a function that is increasing in the first variable, decreasing in the second and that 
 satisfies the quasi-multiplicativity relation
\begin{equation*}
    \upsilon(r,\rho)\upsilon(\rho,R) \asymp \upsilon(r,R),\quad\text{for every }0< r\leq \rho\leq R.
\end{equation*}
If there is some constant~$\varsigma>0$ such that
\begin{align}
    \lim_{\eps\to0}\liminf_{N\to\infty} \frac{\log\upsilon(\eps N,N)}{\log \eps} = \lim_{\eps\to0}\limsup_{N\to\infty} \frac{\log\upsilon(\eps N,N)}{\log \eps} = \varsigma,
\end{align}   
then for any positive sequences~$(r_n)_{n\in\N}$ and~$(R_n)_{n\in\N}$ with~$\frac{R_n}{r_n} \to \infty$ as~$n \to \infty$, 
\begin{align}
    \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log\upsilon(r_n,R_n)}{\log(R_n/r_n)} =-\varsigma.
\end{align}
In particular,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{\log\upsilon(1,N)}{\log N}=-\varsigma.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.650072+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_166f9c7eea5dc6040ebc4713ed1879bc210d28b3f8efdf7ee3d1fdec431c6915","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.29940, 'Counterexamples to the site-percolation analogues\\ of the Easo--Severo--Tassion cutset th'] There exists an infinite, connected, locally finite graph $G$ with a distinguished vertex $\rho$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(i)] $\displaystyle\inf_{z\in V(G)}\mathbb{P}_{p}(z\leftrightarrow\infty)>0$ for every $p>2^{-1/2}$, and in particular $p_c^*(G)<1$;
  \item[(ii)] $\displaystyle\mathcal{C}_G(z\leftrightarrow\infty)\ge \tfrac14$ for every $z\in V(G)$, so $G$ is uniformly transient;
  \item[(iii)] $\displaystyle\sup_{n\ge1}\bigl|\mathcal{Q}^{\mathrm{site}}_n(\rho)\bigr|^{1/n}=\infty$, and hence $\kappa^{\mathrm{site}}(G)=\infty$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently Claims~\ref{conj:A} and~\ref{conj:B} are both false. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:29:14.939574+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16737192e4c6ed8714ee8e7c5c47ac2e3349d0d440c0b77ca259d55b239a9ce1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm2ndResult of arXiv:2606.13615, 'Data-Driven Subsampling Rates for Diffusion Parameter Estimation of SDEs'] Suppose that Assumption~(Assumptions of the paper) holds and that (equation eqGenSDEIntro of the paper) admits an invariant measure $\mu$. Let $\mathbf{X}_0 \sim \mu$ be independent of $\mathbf{W}$, let $\mathbf{X} = (\mathbf{X}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be the corresponding solution process to (equation eqGenSDEIntro of the paper) with $d$-th component $X = (X_t)_{t \geq 0}$, and assume that the ergodicity condition (equation eqErgoCond of the paper) is fulfilled. Then it holds that
\begin{equation}

\lim_{\delta \rightarrow 0} \lim_{J \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{J} \sum_{j=1}^J T_{j,\delta}(X) = 2 \quad P\text{-almost surely}.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, for $\mu$-almost all $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^d$, (equation eqStatementMainResult2 of the paper) also holds for the $d$-th component $X^{\mathbf{x}} = (X_t^{\mathbf{x}})_{t \geq 0}$ of the solution process $\mathbf{X}^{\mathbf{x}} = (\mathbf{X}_t^{\mathbf{x}})_{t \geq 0}$ with initial condition $\mathbf{X}_0^{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{x}$ $P$-a.s., in place of $X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.959514+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1675e997874549e9cc3c16a80fabc630caf7182098d14958eaccaddc1181cc19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2605.11264, 'Uniform sampling of multitype continuous-time Bienaym\'e-Galton-Watson trees'] Conditional on $\{\chi_\emptyset>t\}$, the $\Q^{(k),\bm \theta }_{T,r}$-conditional probability that during the time interval $[t,t+\epsilon)$ there are no births-off the spine, the particle  carrying all marks dies and gives birth to $\bm \ell\in \z^d_+\setminus\{\bo 0\} $ offspring, and  the marks are partitioned according to $\bo P$ with block sizes $(a_{m,q})_{m\in[d], q\in [g_m]}$, is given by
	\[
	\begin{split}
		\Q^{(k),\bm \theta }_{T,r}&\left(\left.\tau_1<t+\epsilon,\mathcal{P}_{\tau_1}=\bo{P},\bo{L}_{\tau_1}=\bm{\ell},  B_{[t,t+\epsilon)} \right| \chi_\emptyset>t\right)\\
		&\hspace{.5cm} =\E_r\left[\bo L^{\floor{\bo g}}\prod_{m=1}^d\E_m\left[e^{-\bm \theta \cdot \bo Z_{T-t-\epsilon}}\right]^{L^{(m)}-g_m}\right]\frac{\bm \ell^{\floor{\bo g}} p_r(\bm{\ell})
		\prod_{m=1}^d\E_m\left[e^{-\bm \theta \cdot \bo Z_{T-t-\epsilon}}\right]^{\ell_m-g_m}}{\E_r\left[\bo L^{\floor{\bo g}}\prod_{m=1}^d\E_m\left[e^{-\bm \theta \cdot \bo Z_{T-t-\epsilon}}\right]^{L^{(m)}-g_m}\right]}\\
		& \hspace{5cm} \times
		\frac{\prod_{\substack{m\in [d]\\ g_m\neq 0}}\prod_{q=1}^{g_m}\E_m\left[N^{\floor{a_{m,q}}}_{T-t-\epsilon}e^{-\bm \theta \cdot \bo Z_{T-t-\epsilon}}\right]}{\E_r\left[N^{\floor{k}}_{T-t}e^{-\bm \theta\cdot \bo Z_{T-t}}\right]}\alpha_r \epsilon+o(\epsilon).
	\end{split}
	\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.958266+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1678732fdcb173c1ff95db8390bf7080dd4688c93e0c92d80721d8e94d72f7cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Uniform-in-time total variation approximation) of arXiv:2608.08177, 'Uniform-in-Time Smoluchowski--Kramers Approximation\\ in Total Variation for Fractional SD'] Suppose \Cref{ass:smooth,ass:dissipative} hold.  For every $t_0>0$ and every
