{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.375766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_169159e8d3084beeacfb80188cf19edd7a9c30331b38255e64c412b994219fb3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def no slip of arXiv:2504.06238, 'Infinite Boundary Friction Limit for Weak Solutions of the Stochastic Navier-Stokes Equati'] Let  $u_0: \Omega \rightarrow L^2_{\sigma}$ be $\mathcal{F}_0-$measurable. A process $u$ which is progressively measurable in $W^{1,2}_{\sigma}$ such that for $\mathbbm{P}-a.e.$ $\omega$, $u_{\cdot}(\omega) \in C\left([0,T];L^2_{\sigma}\right) \cap L^2\left([0,T];W^{1,2}_{\sigma}\right)$, is said to be a weak solution of the equation ((NSinfty of the paper)) if it satisfies the identity
\begin{align} \nonumber
     \inner{u_t}{\phi} = \inner{u_0}{\phi} - \int_0^{t}\inner{\mathcal{L}_{u_s}u_s}{\phi}ds &- \nu\int_0^{t} \inner{u_s}{\phi}_1 ds\\ &+ \frac{1}{2}\int_0^{t}\sum_{i=1}^\infty \inner{B_iu_s}{B_i^*\phi} ds - \int_0^{t} \inner{Bu_s}{\phi} d\mathcal{W}_s
\end{align}
for every $\phi \in W^{1,2}_{\sigma}$, $\mathbbm{P}-a.s.$ in $\mathbb{R}$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Moreover $u$ is said to be unique if for any other such process $v$, $u$ and $v$ are indistinguishable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.766039+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_169459114259c789149e6b4339240ae8d2c0a57ca93ce5531f37b7d0932a73b5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Let $F_1,F_2$ be two sets with $F_1 \cap F_2 = \{z\}$. For $i=1,2$, let $(\CE_i,\CF_i)$ be a resistance form on $F_i$ with associated resistance metric $R_i$. Define $R\colon (F_1 \cup F_2) \times (F_1 \cup F_2) \to [0,\infty)$ by
 \[
 R(x,y) = \begin{cases}
 R_1(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_1,\\
 R_2(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_2,\\
 R_1(x,z)+R_2(z,y) ,& x \in F_1,\, y \in F_2 .
 \end{cases}
 \]
 Then $R$ is a resistance metric on $F_1 \cup F_2$ and the associated resistance form $(\CE,\CF)$ is given by
 \begin{align}\begin{split}
  \CF &= \{ f : f|_{F_1} \in \CF_1,\, f|_{F_2} \in \CF_2 \} ,\\
  \CE(f,f) &= \CE_1(f|_{F_1},f|_{F_1})+\CE_2(f|_{F_2},f|_{F_2}) .
 \end{split}\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

