{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.744886+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16be4718ef15e1e7a4da7cdedd5d9164907a4a75744971f59bcd623503fdc0e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Factorial moment duality) of arXiv:2605.02499, '2605.02499'] Let $X^{(N)}$ be the counting process in the MoMo of size $N$ with interactive neutral reproduction, selection, and mutation, and let $Z^{(N)}$ be as in Def. \ref{def.factorial_dual}.
Then $X^{(N)}$ and $Z^{(N)}$ are dual, that is, for all $t \geq 0$, $n \in [N]_{0}^\Delta$, and $i \in [N]_0$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    \Eb\left[ \frac{ \big( X^{(N)}_t \big)^{\! \vphantom{(N)} \underline{n}}}{N^{\underline{n}}}\, \Big\vert \, X_0^{(N)}=i \right] = \Eb\left [ \frac{i^{\underline{Z^{(N)}_t}}}{N^{\underline{Z^{(N)}_t}}}\, \Big\vert\,  Z_0^{(N)}=n  \right],
\end{equation*}
where $i^{\underline{\Delta}}/N^{\underline{\Delta}}\coloneqq 0$ for all $i$.
We refer to this relation as a \emph{factorial moment duality}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.985369+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16c75c4ad06b505e150a48b2cabee12c5c3962701c182a160f8a14f6cf1c4695","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition propostion estimation L^2 of arXiv:2412.00865, 'On the spectral problem and fractional diffusion limit for Fokker-Planck with(-out) drift '] Assume (a1 of the paper)-(a2 of the paper).  Let $ \eta_0, \lambda_0>0$ small enough.  Then, for all $\eta \in [0,\eta_0]$ and for all $\lambda \in \mathbb{C}$ such that $|\lambda|\leqslant \lambda_0$,  the solution $M_{\lambda,\eta}$ of the penalised equation (equation eq de M_eta + cont. of the paper) satisfies the following estimates \smallskip

\noindent 1.  For all $\gamma > \frac{d}{2}$, one has
\begin{equation}
\|N_{\lambda,\eta}\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)}^2 := \|M_{\lambda,\eta}-M\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)}^2 \lesssim |\lambda| + |b(\lambda,\eta)| + \eta^{\frac{\delta}{3}} ,
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\|M_{\lambda,\eta}\|^2_{L^2(\{ |v_1| \geqslant s_0\eta^{-\frac{1}{3}}\})} \leqslant \frac{1}{s_0^2} \left\| \eta^{\frac{1}{3}} v_1 M_{\lambda,\eta} \right\|_{L^2(\{ |v_1| \geqslant s_0\eta^{-\frac{1}{3}}\})} \lesssim \eta^{\frac{\delta}{3}} ,
\end{equation}
where $\delta := \frac{1}{2}(\gamma-\frac{d}{2})$ and $s_0$ is a positive constant.  \smallskip

\noindent 2.  For all $\gamma > \frac{d+1}{2}$, one has 
\begin{equation}
\left\|  |v_1|^{\frac{1}{2}} N_{\lambda,\eta} \right\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)}^2 := \left\| |v_1|^{\frac{1}{2}}(M_{\lambda,\eta}-M) \right\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)}^2 \lesssim 1 .
\end{equation}
In particular, the functions $M_{\lambda,\eta}$ and $|v_1|^{\frac{1}{2}} M_{\lambda,\eta}$ are uniformly bounded, with respect to $\lambda$ and $\eta$, in $L^2(\mathbb{R}^d,\mathbb{C})$ for $\gamma > \frac{d}{2}$ and $\gamma > \frac{d+1}{2}$ respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:26.914883+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16c78eed32793cf4bb42d7eb27595a69a42cb1ab83d47dd6a0b0673dbdecad1f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:point_measure_restricted_to_hit_B_but_not_the_closure_of_A of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Given a point measure $\mathcal{X} = \sum_{i \in I} \delta_{(x,H)_i} \in \mathbf{Z}$, some $R \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$, a finite connected set $A \subseteq V(G)$ and a subset of its exterior boundary $B \subseteq \partial_{\mathrm{ext}} A$ let us introduce the following restriction of the point measure:
	\begin{equation}

		\check{ \mathcal{X} }_{A,B}^R :=
		\mathcal{X} \, \mathds{1} \left[
			Z^R(B) \setminus Z^R \left( \overline{A} \setminus B \right)
		\right].
	\end{equation}
	That is, we consider those animals only that have volume at most $R$, hit $B$, but do not intersect $\overline{A}$ otherwise. Let us also introduce the notation 
	\begin{equation}

		  \check{\mu}_{A,B}^R (x) := \mu^{ \check{ \mathcal{X} }_{A,B}^R }(x)
		   \qquad\text{and}\qquad 
		   \check{ \Sigma }_{A,B}^R := \Sigma^{ \check{ \mathcal{X} }_{A,B}^R } = \sum_{x \in V(G)} \check{\mu}_{A,B}^R (x)
	\end{equation}

	for the (total) occupation measure of the restricted point measure.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.236608+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16ca83af6cd5e5263710e87d4127ac5b3085853dfe2f45fdd7ed008ac489a1ce","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem prop:uniform-T-W2 of arXiv:2607.00149, '\bf Uniform-in-time Propagation-of-Chaos for\\ Stein Variational Gradient Descent'] Let Assumption~(ass:Wass of the paper) hold. Recall the exponent $\mathfrak r=\mathfrak r(d,\nu)$ in (equation eq:rdef of the paper), and let
