{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.036823+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16f3619259c9b3ee7893defeb2e8d083e455fe4e97a78858dd010aa710f7e3f6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:score-gPAM of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Let $u$ be the renormalised solution of any subcritical singular SPDE in the framework of [brunedchandrachevyrevhairer2021], with regularised solutions $u^\varepsilon$, along an approximating family as in the discussion above, converging to $u$ in probability on $\{T < \tau\}$. Fix $t \in (0, T)$, let $\varphi \in C^\infty(\mathbb{T}^d)$, and set $F^\varepsilon := \langle u^\varepsilon(t), \varphi\rangle_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^d)}$.

Assume throughout that $F^\varepsilon$ converges in probability to a real-valued random variable $F$ as $\varepsilon \downarrow 0$, as it does in the standard cases of the framework (Remark~(rem:fixed-time-slice of the paper)). Part~\textup{(c)} uses nothing beyond this; parts~\textup{(a)} and~\textup{(b)} carry the further Malliavin hypotheses stated with them.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2.4em, label=\textup{(\alph*)}, ref=\textup{(\alph*)}]
