{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.357535+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_17276f4e7418a3832f99c317c2b732dfe0703d5165d3e23c5736a5c681613421","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2509.23069, 'Every cutoff profile is possible'] Let $(t_n)$ be a sequence of positive integers and $(w_n)$ be a sequence of positive real numbers such that $w_n = o(t_n)$ as $n\to \infty$. Let $p{\ensuremath{\;:\;}} {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}}  \to [0,1]$ be a weakly decreasing function (but not necessarily continuous) such that $p(x) \xrightarrow[x\to -\infty]{} 1$ and  $p(x) \xrightarrow[x\to +\infty]{} 0$. There exists a sequence of discrete time Markov chains $((X_t^{(n)})_{t\geq 0})_{n\geq 1}$ such that
