{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.902706+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_17dada677024a7e90892dcfbd839075306f0cec5b75fa7455677db2da9989739","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:BSSK_crit of arXiv:2510.26960, 'Free energy fluctuations in SK and related spin glass models: \\ A literature survey'] Consider a BSSK model where $N_1/N_2=\lambda+O(N^{-1})$ for some $\lambda\in(0,1)$ and let $\beta=\beta_c+bN_1^{-1/3}\sqrt{\log N_1}$ for some fixed $b\in\mathbb{R}$.  Then the free energy exhibits the following convergence in distribution as $N_1,N_2\to\infty$.
 \begin{equation} 
\frac{N}{\sqrt{\frac16\log N_1}}\left(F_{N_1,N_2}(\beta)-F(\beta)+\frac{\log N_1}{12N}\right)\to\mathcal{N}(0,1)+C_\lambda\max\{0,b\}\TW_1
 \end{equation} 
where $F(\beta)$ and $C_\lambda$ are given in Theorem 1.1 of [CWL_BSSK]. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.009613+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_17dc553c237b296c9beab2804ef0bafddb691fbcbf62ac3ec05f2ccff7899e91","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition LUE fac of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Let $L_{a,N}$ be an LUE with parameter $a\in\mathbb{N}$ and kernel $K_{L_{a,N}}(x,y)$. Then for $a\ge 2$, the following decomposition holds for the corresponding integral operator $\mathbf{K}_{L_{a,N}}$:
 \begin{equation}
\mathbf{K}_{L_{a,N}}=\mathbf{G}_{a,N}\mathbf{H}_{a,N}+\mathbf{H}_{a,N}\mathbf{G}_{a,N}.
\end{equation}
Thereinto, the operators $\mathbf{G}_{a,N}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{a,N}$ have kernels $\xi_{a,N}(x+y)$ and $\eta_{a,N}(x+y)$ for
\begin{equation}
    \begin{array}{ll}
   \xi_{a,N}(x):=\frac{(-1)^N}{\sqrt2}\left(N(N+a)\right)^\frac{1}{4}\sqrt{\frac{\Gamma(N+1)}{\Gamma(N+a)}}x^{\frac{a}{2}-1}e^{-\frac{x}{2}}L_{N}^{a-1}(x)\chi_{(0,\infty)}(x),\\
   \\
    \eta_{a,N}(x):=\frac{(-1)^{N-1}}{\sqrt2}\left(N(N+a)\right)^\frac{1}{4}\sqrt{\frac{\Gamma(N)}{\Gamma(N+a+1)}}x^{\frac{a}{2}}e^{-\frac{x}{2}}L_{N-1}^{a+1}(x)\chi_{(0,\infty)}(x)
    \end{array}
\end{equation}
respectively.

Let $\mathbf{K}_{Ai}$ be the integral operator with the Airy kernel defined in (equation airy kernel of the paper), then
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{K}_{Ai}=\mathbf{Ai}\mathbf{Ai}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{Ai}$ has the kernel $Ai(x+y)$.

Moreover, all operators above are Hilbert-Schmidt on $L^2(0,+\infty)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.321783+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_17dcfff1af97817b8bc4050ab51ed281dc7d9bb9e888f16c0dbc980ab9aa0652","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:existence of arXiv:2608.31071, 'Global smooth solutions to the inhomogeneous Landau--Fermi--Dirac equation'] Fix $m > 2$ and let  $f_{\rm in}\in L^\infty_m(\mathbb T^3\times \mathbb R^3)$ satisfy $0 \le f_{\rm in} \le 1$ and the non-vacuum condition from (d.nonvacuum of the paper). Then, there exists a global weak solution $f\colon \mathbb R^+\times \mathbb T^3\times \mathbb R^3 \to [0,1]$ of (equation e.LFD of the paper) in the sense of (def:weak-solutions of the paper). Moreover:
    \begin{enumerate}
