{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.039932+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_17ed498b19cfbfcf73b6e998ad2e3cd6f205bb79343b150722fbc0bee270299e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2606.26619, 'Ergodicity of stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on unbounded domains driven by space'] Let $u^i(t,\cdot)$ denotes the solution to euqation (\ref{3.1}) with initial value $u_0^i$, $i=1,2$. 
\begin{itemize}
  \item [(i)] Suppose that (H4) holds. 

Then for any $\lambda, t\geq 0$,
\begin{align}
    \mathbb{E} \Vert u^2(t) - u^1(t)\Vert_{L^1_{\lambda}} \leq 2 e^{-(\alpha -\frac{\lambda^2}{2}) t} \mathbb{E}\Vert u_0^2 - u_0^1 \Vert_{L^1_{\lambda}} .
\end{align}
  \item [(ii)] Suppose that (H2) and (H4) hold. Assume that for some $\lambda\geq 0$,  $\mathbb{E}\int_{\mathbb{R}}|u^2(t,x) - u^1(t,x)|^2 e^{-\lambda|x|} dx <\infty$ for any $t\geq 0$. 
