{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:58:09.889577+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_193f003e34d956197473d497c73e30f984da39b56d1b4ff22a4757560b5ce95f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:convergence of arXiv:2607.18824, 'Non-Abelian multiplicative chaos on the circle'] Fix $\varrho$ as above and $\kappa<\min(-2\check h,-\check h-2\lambda_\rho^+)$, with $\lambda_\rho^+$ defined in (equation eq:lambda_rho of the paper).

    For each $\epsilon>0$, let $M_{\kappa,\rho}^\epsilon$ be the random $\mathcal{H}_V$-valued Borel measure on $\mathbb S^1$ defined by 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm d M^\epsilon_{\kappa,\rho}(e^{i\theta}):=\,(1-e^{-2\epsilon})^\frac{-\Omega_\rho}{\kappa+\check h}\varrho\left(H(e^{-\epsilon+i\theta})\right)\mathrm d\theta,\qquad\qquad\theta\in\mathbb R,
\end{equation}
with $H$ sampled from $\nu_\kappa$. Then, the family $(M^\epsilon_{\kappa,\rho})_{\epsilon>0}$ converges weakly in probability as $\epsilon\to0$ to some non-trivial $\mathcal{H}_V$-valued measure $M_{\kappa,\rho}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:58:09.906371+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1940abde9128c0397caa9949abc99e0d13afe0cce339ddd56b7eea3aed42fa26","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def: nu^T_t of arXiv:2607.19658, 'Hitting time mixing for random $k$-cycles'] Let $\nu^T_t$ denote the distribution on $\mathfrak{S}_{[n]\setminus T}$  defined by the following sampling process. First, sample $M_t\in\{0,1,\ldots,n-|T|\}$ according to the distribution $\mathbf{P}[M_t=x]=\mathbf{P}(\Pois(\gamma_{n-|T|,t_T})=x)/\mathbf{P}(\Pois(\gamma_{n-|T|,t_T})\le n-|T|)$, then sample a uniformly random subset $S_t$ of size $M_t$ in $[n]\setminus T$. Finally,
\begin{enumerate}
\item When $k$ is odd, sample a uniformly random element of $\mathfrak{A}_{[n]\backslash S_t \cup T}$ and view it as an element of $\mathfrak{A}_n$ by fixing all of the elements in $S_t$ and $T$.
\item When $k$ is even, and $t$ is odd, sample a uniformly random element of $\mathfrak{A}_{[n]\backslash S_t\cup T}^c$ and view it as an element of $\mathfrak{A}_n^c$ by fixing all of the elements in $S_t$ and $T$.
\item When $k$ is even, and $t$ is even, sample a uniformly random element of $\mathfrak{A}_{[n]\backslash S_t\cup T}$ and view it as an element of $\mathfrak{A}_n$ by fixing all of the elements in $S_t$ and $T$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.565123+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1940dcd721baafcb4f7bc4c59acddf453dddd659286fae824b920ad2816ad31f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition T:Upper-t of arXiv:2602.05317, 'Strong local nondeterminism for stochastic time-fractional\\ slow and fast diffusion equat'] Under the setting of Theorem~(T:main-holder of the paper), for all positive constants
  $S$ and $T$ with $S<T$, there exists a positive constant $C = C_{S,T}$, such
  that the following statements hold with $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$ defined as
  in~(equation E:rho1-rho2 of the paper).

  \begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]

    \item If either a) $\beta \in (0,2)$; or b) $\beta = 2$ and $\gamma > 2$; or
          c) $\beta = 2$, $\gamma \in (0,2]$ and $\ell < \alpha (\gamma + 1/2)$\footnote{This condition is required to apply part (ii) of Lemma~(L:intml of the paper) on $E_{2,2 + \gamma - 1}$.}, then for all $(t,s,x)\in [S,T]^2 \times \mathbb{R}^d$ it
          holds that
          \begin{align*} 
            \mathbb{E}\left[\left(u(t, x) - u(s, x)\right)^2\right]
            \le C
            \begin{dcases}
              |t-s|^{2 (\rho_1 \wedge 1)},                    & \ell \neq \alpha(\gamma + 1) - 2, \\
              |t-s|^2 \big(1 + \big| \log(|t-s|) \big| \big), & \ell = \alpha(\gamma + 1) - 2.
            \end{dcases}
          \end{align*}

    \item If $\beta = 2$ and $\gamma = 0$, then for all $(t,s,x)\in [S,T]^2
            \times \mathbb{R}^d$ it holds that
          \begin{align*} 
