  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.104282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1944ef487d967bae3008bf9d138bfa399ed27d2e802fb8b61002c05960db1652","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 33 of arXiv:2608.28200, '\textbf On the Formality of Configuration Spaces of $\aka$'] On $\C$: we need to find $\bp$ inverse of $fd\bar z$, $f$ smooth.

and we can find $f(x,y)dx=\frac{1}{2}\partial_x F(x,y)dx$ in the Pfaffian closure. So 
\ali{
fd\bar z
=fdx+ ifdy
=\frac{1}{2}\partial_x F(x,y)dx + ifdy
=\bp F(x,y) + \LR{-\frac{1}{2}i \partial_y F(x,y) +if} dy.
}
Since $\bp(fd\bar z)=0$, we have 
\ali{
\bp \LR{-\frac{1}{2}i \partial_y F(x,y) +if} dy
=\frac{1}{2}\partial_x \LR{-\frac{1}{2}i \partial_y F(x,y) +if} dy
=0
}
i.e., $\LR{-\frac{1}{2}i \partial_y F(x,y) +if}$ only depends on $y$. So we can use Poincar\'e Lemma on $\R$.

similar, we can prove cohomology of $\R^p\times \C^q$, or its polydisk subsets, sits at degree 0. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.908504+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_194511320bf8f4fe64cd5f06e981b7f932205ee2f023e5de1a2ec76cf40de21e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:counterexample:leftsided:weight:operator of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] For any\\ $r,d \in \mathbb{N}$ with $d \ge 220 r$, there exists a measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d \times d} \rightarrow \R^m$ satisfying the NSP of order $r$ with constant $\eta_r = 1/10$ and of order $1$ with $\eta_1 = 1/(11r -1)$, a rank-$r$ matrix $\mathbf{X}_\star \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ and a matrix $\mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ such that $\mathbf{X} - \mathbf{X}_\star \in \ker(\mathcal{A})$ and $\nucnorm{\mathbf{X} - \mathbf{X}_\star} \le \frac{ \sigma_r \left( \mathbf{X}_\star \right) }{ \max \left( \eta_1 \sqrt{ d}, 2 \right)  }$, but also
