{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.453289+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_196d57b120fa6274c05ebf1700492ba0b01e4d59bdc9189bffa73481b7d811ec","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 35 of arXiv:2604.03213, 'Asymptotic expansion for transport maps \\ between laws of multimatrix models'] Let $\cX$ be a Banach space, $\cU \subseteq \cX$ be an open set, and $a \in \R$.
If $f \colon [a,\infty) \times \cU \to \cX$ is locally Lipschitz in space, locally uniformly in time and $c \in C([a,\infty);\cX)$ is such that $c(a) \in \cU$, then there exists a time $T_{\infty} \in (a,\infty]$ and a continuous function $y_{\infty} \colon [a,T_{\infty}) \to \cU$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),font=\normalfont]
    \item $y_{\infty} = c + \int_a^{\boldsymbol{\cdot}} f(t,y_{\infty}(t))\,\d t$ on $[a,T_{\infty})$;
    \item If $T \in (a,\infty]$ and $y \colon [a,T) \to \cU$ is a continuous function such that $y = c + \int_a^{\boldsymbol{\cdot}} f(t,y(t))\,\d t$ on $[a,T)$, then $T \leq T_{\infty}$, and $y_{\infty}|_{[a,T)} = y$.
\end{enumerate}
The function $y_{\infty}$ is the \textbf{maximal solution} to $y = c + \int_a^{\boldsymbol{\cdot}} f(t,y(t))\,\d t$, and $T_{\infty}$ is its \textbf{lifetime}.","status":"unverified"}

