 Then the family $\{\mathbb{P}^1_n: n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is tight on $\bZ_{T,1}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.051973+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1a0cd3e9a50c18b8af2b67a6e590b3edd0bdbd40de117e77199d3b5d88dcf891","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 2 of arXiv:2607.00853, '\sf Energy integrals and asymmetric co-potentials for closed forms'] \rm 
Take $\beta>\beta_0$ and fix it. Then the following properties hold for the class $\mathsf{S}_0$ and the metric $\rho_\beta$:  
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \textbf{(Vague convergence)} 

If $\rho_\beta(\mu_n, \mu) \to 0$ for $\mu_n, \mu \in \mathsf{S}_0$, then $\mu_n$ converges to $\mu$ vaguely.
\item[(ii)] \textbf{(Vague convergence with bounded energy implies weak convergence)} 

If a sequence $\{\mu_n\} \subset \mathsf{S}_0$ is bounded in \(\rho_\beta\), namely $\sup_{n} \form_\beta(U_\beta \mu_n, U_\beta \mu_n) < \infty$, and converges vaguely to a Radon measure $\mu$, then $\mu \in \mathsf{S}_0$ and the potential $U_\beta \mu_n$ (resp.\ the co-potential $\widehat{U}_\beta \mu_n$) converges to $U_\beta \mu$ (resp.\ $\widehat{U}_\beta \mu$) weakly in the Hilbert space $(\dom, \form^{(s)}_\beta)$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.991752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1a1002567a287b380cbf1977f95a847f52f804ef0571feb3197e7271734f2b6f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2605.11801, 'A non-local singular non-linear Fokker-Planck PDE'] Let $(P_t)$ denote the semigroup generated by $\frac12\Delta$ on $\shs$, in particular for all $\Phi\in\shs$ we define
  $P_t \Phi = \int_{\R^d} p_t(x-y) \Phi(y) dy, t \ge 0$,
 where $p$ is the heat kernel
\begin{equation}
p_t(z):=\frac1{(2\pi t)^{d/2}} \exp^{-\frac{|z|^2}t}.
\end{equation}
It is easy to see that $P_t:\shs\to\shs$. 
Moreover we can extend it to $\shs'$ by dual pairing (and we denote it with the same notation
for simplicity), namely one has $\langle P_t\Psi, \Phi\rangle:=\langle \Psi,P_t\Phi \rangle$
for all $\Psi\in\shs'$ and $\Phi\in\shs$, the equality holds without changing the sign, because $p$ is symmetric.","status":"unverified"}

