{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.709880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1a3ebe53a33f3682a53a3dba37f25fdc351c17dfd4b8f3367b74dde95460fd84","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition fig:wk-em-dual-countermodel of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] $\textbf{WK} + \varphi \lor \neg \varphi \subsetneq \textbf{K}$. Consequently, $\textbf{CK} + \varphi \lor \neg \varphi \subsetneq \textbf{K}$.
  \begin{proof}
    It suffices to prove it for $\textbf{WK}$.
    ($\subseteq$) Trivial from the axiomatizations.
    ($\neq$) It suffices to construct an infallible CK-model in which $\varphi \lor \neg \varphi$ is valid for every $\varphi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ but $\neg \Box \neg p \to \Diamond p$ is not.
    We let $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, where:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item $W \coloneq \set{ x_i, y_i; i \in \mathbb{N} }$;
      \item $\leq$ is the reflexive and transitive closure of $\set*{ (x_i, x_{i+1}), (y_i, y_{i+1}); i \in \mathbb{N} }$;
      \item $\mathord{\sqsubset} \coloneq \set*{ (x_{2i}, y_{2i}), (y_{2i}, x_{2i}), (x_{2i+1}, x_{2i+1}), (y_{2i+1}, y_{2i+1}); i \in \mathbb{N} }$;
      \item $V(p) \coloneq \set{ y_i; i \in \mathbb{N} }$.
    \end{itemize}
    The model is due to Simpson [simpson_proof_1994] and is visualized in Figure (fig:wk-em-dual-countermodel of the paper).
    \begin{figure}[ht]
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}
      \fill (0,2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0,2) node[left]{$x_0$};

      \fill (2,2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2,2) node[right]{$y_0 \Vdash p$};

      \draw [thick, <->, squig, rel] (0,2) to (2,2);
      \draw (1,2) node[above]{\tiny $\sqsubset$};

      \draw (0,3) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0,3) node[left]{$x_1$};
      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,2) to (0,3);
      \draw (0,2.5) node[left]{\tiny $\leq$};

      \draw (2,3) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2,3) node[right]{$y_1 \Vdash p$};
      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (2,2) to (2,3);
      \draw (2,2.5) node[right]{\tiny $\leq$};

      \fill (0,4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0,4) node[left]{$x_2$};
      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,3) to (0,4);
      \draw (0,3.5) node[left]{\tiny $\leq$};

      \fill (2,4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2,4) node[right]{$y_2 \Vdash p$};
      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (2,3) to (2,4);
      \draw (2,3.5) node[right]{\tiny $\leq$};

      \draw [thick, <->, squig, rel] (0,4) to (2,4);
      \draw (1,4) node[above]{\tiny $\sqsubset$};

      \draw (0,5) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0,5) node[left]{$x_3$};
      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,4) to (0,5);
      \draw (0,4.5) node[left]{\tiny $\leq$};

      \draw [thick, dashed, rel] (0,5) to (0,6);

      \draw (2,5) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2,5) node[right]{$y_3 \Vdash p$};
      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (2,4) to (2,5);
      \draw (2,4.5) node[right]{\tiny $\leq$};

      \draw [thick, dashed, rel] (2,5) to (2,6);

      \end{tikzpicture}
      \caption{The model $\mathfrak{M}$. $\circ$ denotes a $\sqsubset$-reflexive world.}

    \end{figure}

    Here, $\mathfrak{M}$ is clearly an infallible CK-model.
    Also, it is easy to see that $x_0 \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \neg \Box \neg p$ but $x_0 \not\Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond p$ since there is no $y$ such that $x_0 \leq x_1 \sqsubset y \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} p$.
    Now it suffices to show that $w \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi \lor \neg \varphi$ for any $w \in W$ and $\varphi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$,
    which is proven by an easy induction on the construction of $\varphi$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

