{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.348891+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1a528d688cc261d98f7e4a98cfcf2d5b662ce901eaa3e896fdc1d3a7ed8a378f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2607.15872, 'Existence of $q$-Bass martingales in the semidiscrete setting'] Let $\nu, q \in \Prob(\RR)$ satisfy Assumption~\ref{ass:technical-assumptions}, let $D := [0,\diam(\supp(q)))^{n-1}$, let $f$ be the corresponding $n$-atomic $q$-Bass map, and let $V : D \to \RR$ and $v : D \times \RR \to \RR$ be the maps defined in Definition~\ref{def:potential-f}.

Then $V$ is strictly concave on $D$, its restriction to $\interior(D)$ belongs to $C^2(\interior(D))$, and its first- and second-order partial derivatives extend continuously to $D$. Moreover, $\nabla V=f$ and the map $f|_{\interior(D)}:\interior(D)\rightarrow f(\interior(D))$ is a $C^1$-diffeomorphism. More specifically, for every $1\leq r\leq s\leq n-1$,
\begin{equation}

\frac{\partial^2 V(h)}{\partial h_s\,\partial h_r} = -\sum_{j=1}^r\sum_{\ell=s+1}^{n}\psi_{j\ell}(h),
\end{equation}
where $\psi_{j\ell}:D \rightarrow \RR$ are continuous non-negative functions, and $\psi_{j(j+1)}$ is strictly positive on $D$ for every $j=1,\dots,n-1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.963049+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1a52a57f64ae42226c50ef3449b8efcfa2fa2a41966b8fe475c765ea8471973c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 17 of arXiv:2605.28776, 'Limiting distributions of triangle counts in linear preferential attachment models'] For $m\ge 2$ and $\delta\in(-m,0]$, and for all $\ell\le n$,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{j<k\le n,\, j\le \ell} \mu_{i,j,k} \cong
