{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:29:27.942538+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ab8338a31ed4545ae77d9e15036ee261a0b78eec1690b205e1fcd45e909b946","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 (Illustrative convergence rate) of arXiv:2608.24426, 'Sequential operator learning under dependent data'] Under the assumptions of \autoref{thr:linear-regression}, suppose that the observation operators satisfy $\operatorname{Ran}(\obsop_\iteridx)\subseteq\mathcal{S}$ for a fixed $d$-dimensional subspace $\mathcal{S}\subset\fspace$, $\norm{\obsop_\iteridx}_\opnorm\leq L$, and $\sum_{i=1}^\iteridx\obsop_i\obsop_i^\adj\succeq\kappa\iteridx P_{\mathcal{S}}$ for some $\kappa,L>0$ and all sufficiently large $\iteridx$. Then, for every fixed $\testop\in\mathcal{S}$ and $\delta\in(0,1]$, with probability at least $1-\delta$,
\begin{equation*}
    \abs{\pairing{\targetop-\learntop_\iteridx,\testop}}
    =
    O\paren*{
        \sqrt{
            \frac{
                d\log(1+\iteridx)+\log(1/\delta)
            }{
                \iteridx
            }
        }
    },
\end{equation*}
uniformly over sufficiently large $\iteridx$. In particular, for fixed $d$ and $\delta$, the error is $O(\sqrt{\log\iteridx/\iteridx})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.523907+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1aba83a3158455a006d12791b6f327eee682a648a74275f729b4b70726fdd617","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:paraproduct of arXiv:2411.03482, 'Ergodicity of 2D singular stochastic Navier--Stokes equations'] For $\varphi, \psi \in \mathcal{S}'(\mathbf{T}^2; \mathbf{R}^d)\,$, we define the high-low paraproduct
	\begin{equ}
		\varphi \olessthan \psi := \sum_{j = -1}^{\infty} \sum_{i=-1}^{j-2}\Delta_i \varphi \otimes_s \Delta_j \psi\;, \qquad \varphi \ogreaterthan \psi := \psi \olessthan \varphi\;.
	\end{equ}
	We also define the high-high paraproduct (also called resonant term) by 
	\begin{equ}
		\varphi \odot \psi = \sum_{|i-j|\leq 1} \Delta_i \varphi \otimes_s \Delta_j \psi\;.
	\end{equ}
	Sometimes we use $\varphi \mathrel{\rlap{\kern0.07em\tikz[x=0.1em,y=0.1em,baseline=0.04em] \draw[line width=0.3pt] (0,1.93) -- (4.5,0);}\olessthan} \psi := \varphi \olessthan \psi + \varphi \odot \psi$ and $\varphi \mathrel{\rlap{\kern0.23em\tikz[x=0.1em,y=0.1em,baseline=0.04em] \draw[line width=0.3pt] (0,0) -- (4.5,1.93);}\ogreaterthan} \psi := \varphi \ogreaterthan \psi + \varphi \odot \psi$\,.","status":"unverified"}

