{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.226436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1aca5e22bba9b02475e497e71c06d4805805c04688afe06e1d6e0f3269d31319","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.00537, 'Explicit Exponential Sum Estimates and Approximate Functional Equations for the Zeta Funct'] For $x,y\in \R$ with $x\notin \Z$ and $y>0$, we have the bounds:
    \begin{align}
        |S_0(x,y)| & = \bigg| \frac{\sin(\pi x \lfloor y \rfloor )}{\sin \pi x} \bigg| \leq \begin{cases}
        \frac{1}{|\sin \pi x|} & \text{ if } y \geq 1,\\
        0 & \text{ if } 0<y<1,\\\end{cases}
        \\
        |S_1(x,y)|&\leq \tilde{S_1}(x,y)
       = \begin{cases}
        \frac{1}{|\sin(\pi x)|}\left(
        \frac{1}{y} +1 
        \right) & \text{ if } y \geq 1,\\
        0 & \text{ if } 0<y<1.\\\end{cases}


    \end{align}
    Moreover, in the special case where $x=n+1/2\  (n\in\Z)$ and $y\ge1$, we can replace the definition of $\tilde{S_1}$ with the slightly stronger bound:
   \begin{equation}
        \tilde{S_1}(n+1/2,y)=\log 2 + \mathcal{O}^*\left(
        \frac{1}{y}
        \right).
   \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.354421+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1acb1e19b916e13c5a97406b1bf50aa329bc4b04f3001d4d15bc7ca14e4c75d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.glivenkocantelli of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Suppose that $\bm{x}^n = (x^n_1,\dots,x^n_n) \in {\mathbb R}^n$, and $h^n \in {\mathbb R}_+$ are such that
    \begin{align*}
        m_{\bm{x}^n}^n \xlongrightarrow{\mathbf{d}_p} m, \qquad h^n \longrightarrow h,
    \end{align*}
    for some $m \in \mathcal{P}_p({\mathbb R})$ and $h \in (0,\infty)$. Suppose moreover that for each $n$,
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{G}^n = \big(G^n_1,\dots,G^n_n \big) \sim \gamma_{h^n}^{\otimes n}.
    \end{align*}
   Then,
    \begin{align*}
       \mathbf{d}_p\big( m_{\bm{x}^n + \bm{G}^n}^n, m * \gamma_h \big) \longrightarrow 0\quad \text{  in probability.}
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.097309+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1acbc55f72965a9e11a7f5c515ba0e16d251327e78f303ab823c7327301a80a5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm boundZ of arXiv:2412.08497, 'Time discretization of Quadratic Forward-Backward SDEs with singular drift'] Let assumptions of Lemma (lemm4.1 of the paper) hold. Then for any $p >p_0'> 1$ there is a constant $C$ only depending on $p,d,T$ and $\|b\|_{L^{\infty}([0,T];C_b(\mathbb{R}^d))}$ such that 
				\begin{equation}
					\mathbb{E}\big[\sup_{ 0\leq t\leq T}|Z_t|^p\big] \leq C(1 + S^p({\varphi})),
				\end{equation}
				where $p_0'$ is the conjugate of $p_0$ and $p_0$ is such that $\mathcal{E}\left(\nabla_z g*W\right) \in L^{p_0}$(see Remark (rem3.1 of the paper)).
				In particular, if $g$ is Lipschitz continuous in $y$ then the control process $Z$ is uniformly bounded i.e. there is a constant $C>0$ such that
				\begin{align*}
					|Z_t|\leq Ce^{\|\nabla_y g\|_{\infty}}(1+S(\varphi)).
				\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.045222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1acf1973d5c7f1fc2406d995352cd7d2f22d75e869d357aa3c018d4a9d166f2d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_3_SBO of arXiv:2411.13972, 'The Stochastic Bessel operator at high temperatures'] ~\\ \emph{
Let $B$ be a standard Brownian motion on $\mathbb{R}_+$. For $\beta>0$ and $a>-1$, the stochastic Bessel operator (known as SBO) is the random differential operator
\begin{gather*}
\mathfrak{G}^{\beta,a} = -\frac{1}{m(x)}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} x}\Big(\frac{1}{s(x)}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} x}\Big), \\ 
m(x)=\exp\big(-(a+1)x-\frac{2}{\sqrt{\beta}}B(x)\big), \quad s(x)=\exp\big(ax+\frac{2}{\sqrt{\beta}}B(x)\big),
\end{gather*}
defined on a subset of $L^2(\mathbb{R}_+,m)$ with Dirichlet and Neumann boundary conditions at $0$ and at infinity, respectively.
}","status":"unverified"}

