{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:04:07.025982+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ae8e82b0412d42f0f86aebc352467fc7311e339f2ea369180ea72960bb79bfb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:boundary_blowup of arXiv:2607.27810, 'Perturbed Beta Corners Process'] Let
\begin{equation}

	\rho(\mathbf{Y})\coloneqq\min_{1\le k\le N-1}\ \min_{1\le p\le k}\
	\min_{1\le q\le k+1}\bigl|y_p^k-y_q^{k+1}\bigr|,
	\qquad \mathbf{y}^N=\mathbf{z},
\end{equation}
denote the smallest cross-level gap of the array. There is a constant
$C=C(N,\mathbf{z},\mathsf{a})$ such that
\begin{equation}

	H_{\mathsf{a}}(\mathbf{Y})\ \ge\ \frac12\hspace{1pt}\log\frac{1}{\rho(\mathbf{Y})}-C
\end{equation}
at every point $\mathbf{Y}$ of the open Gelfand--Tsetlin polytope. In particular
$H_{\mathsf{a}}(\mathbf{Y})\to+\infty$ as $\mathbf{Y}$ approaches any
boundary point of the polytope, and every sublevel set of $H_{\mathsf{a}}$ is
a compact subset of its interior.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.286230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1aea0a7bf6c5c7ce24fca170ea69b21a6276434f33de8f7077011e25ba8cd69c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma det asymp of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Let $\zeta$ be a probability measure on $[0,1]$ with prefix $\{0,q\}$ with $q\in (0,1)$. Let $f\in \mathbb{R}$. There exists $C_\varepsilon>0$, depending only on $\varepsilon$, $f$, and $\xi$, with $C_\varepsilon\to 0$ as $\varepsilon\to 0$, such that the following holds uniformly over $\mathbf{m}\in \mathrm{SUSY}_1(\zeta,\varepsilon)$: Letting $\mathcal{B}:=\{\nabla F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m})=0,\, F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m})=Nf\}$,
		\begin{equation*}
			\left| \log \mathbb{E}\bigl[ |\det\nabla^2 F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m})|\mid\mathcal{B}\bigr]
			- \log\prod_{i=1}^N\partial_{mm}h_\zeta(q,m_i)
			- \frac{N}{2}\xi''(q)\left(\int_q^1 \zeta([0,t])\,\mathrm{d} t\right)^{\!2}
			\right|
			\leq C_\varepsilon N.
		\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.893530+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1aebef3e20374df466e5d79f558813e41be25317af5f9bd131f1946d3a322a4d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 14 (Following~\cite{CLR1,CLR2}) of arXiv:2508.04938, 'Perfect t-embeddings of doubly periodic Aztec diamonds'] Let~$(\G, \chi)$ be a weighted, finite, bipartite, planar graph. Then an embedding~$\T$ of the augmented dual graph $\G^*$ is called a perfect t-embedding if the following holds
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $\T$ is a proper embedding of~$\G^*$ such that
 all edges are line segments, the edge weights of~$\G$ given by length of dual edges are gauge equivalent to initial weights~$\chi_e$, and around each inner vertex of~$\T(\G^*)$ the sum of angles adjacent to white faces is the same as the sum of angles adjacent to black ones and both of them are~$\pi$ (we call it the angle condition); 
 \item[(b)]  The boundary vertices $\cT(v_1), \ldots, \cT(v_{2n})$ form a tangental (not necessary convex) polygon~$P$;
\item[(c)] The points~$\cT(v_{\operatorname{in},k})$ lie on bisectors of corresponding angles of~$P$ and \[(\cT(v_k)\cT(v_{\operatorname{in},k}))\cap \operatorname{Int} (P) \neq 0,\]
where~$\operatorname{Int} (P)$ is the interior of the polygon~$P$.
 \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

