{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.487860+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1af8ce405a10d8bd4f751954c764b5c2988de9936458b17db1f6ccc1bb4bb70c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2609.03468, 'Almost-invariant half-spaces of operators on $\omega$'] Let $T \in L(\omega)$. 
The following statements are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $T$ does not possess a closed almost-invariant half-space;
    \item $T$ does not possess a closed invariant half-space;
    \item There exist $\lambda \in \mathbb{C}$ and an operator $R \in L(\omega)$ of finite rank such that $T$ is conjugate to $B+ \lambda \operatorname{Id}  + R$ or to $F+ \lambda \operatorname{Id}+ R$,
\end{enumerate}
where $F$ is the Forward shift and $B$ is the Backward shift. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.605508+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1af97b77cac6ffbbbd2310014ea6ea913ee2ab24592688cd7e01a003e22aa163","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ODE_with_possibly_negligible_driving_term of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] Let $a,b>0$, let $h$ be a positive, measurable, regulary varying function of index $b$, and suppose that $f$ is a positive, differentiable function such that
\[
f'(r)\sim \frac{af+h}{r}
\]
as $r\to \infty$. 
Then $f$ is regularly varying of index $\max\{a,b\}$. Moreover, if $a\geq b$ then $h(r)=o(f(r))$ and $f'(r)\sim ar^{-1}f(r)$ as $r\to \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.043135+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1afa07cf0e1462736a04bc82b177d9f0c209790710e2bc55406cb16572e22dff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2510.24244, 'Statistical properties of Markov shifts: part II-LLT'] Let Assumptions \ref{MixAss}, \ref{Assss}, \ref{Ball Ass} be in force.
 If $(f_j)$ is irreducible then  with  $\sigma_n=\|S_nf-\bbE[S_nf]\|_{L^2}$ and $S_n=S_nf$,
