{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.218425+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1b8a4ec9401e1bee0fb4c99efbb78e7bc1c96ecb8e55b951fc6f548e5427a614","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: convergence of the disordered probability of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
Assume that the conditions of Theorem~(thm: convergence of the partition function of the paper) hold.
If furthermore,
\begin{enumerate}[label={(\alph*)}]
	\item 
	$\mathrm{P}_{\Omega_{\delta}}^{\mathrm{ref}}$ converges in ${\ensuremath{\mathcal M}} _1(E)$ to a probability distribution ${\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _{\Omega}$;
	\item 
	for every function $G \in {\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} _b(E)$ and every $k \in {\ensuremath{\mathbb N}} ^*$, $\overline{\psi}_{\Omega_{\delta}}^{(k)}( \cdot,G)$ converges in $L^q_s(\Omega^k)$;
	\item 
	for any $\hat{\beta} \, \geqslant\, 0$, the continuum partition function ${\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}_{\Omega}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}$ is almost surely strictly positive.
\end{enumerate}
Then we have the following joint convergence in distribution in $H_{\rm loc}^{-s}(\Omega) \times {\ensuremath{\mathcal M}} _1(E)$, $s > \frac{D}{2}$,
\begin{equation*}
	(\zeta_{\delta}, \mathrm{P}_{\Omega_{\delta}}^{\omega,\beta_{\delta}}) \xrightarrow{(d)} (\boldsymbol\zeta, {\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _{\Omega}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}) \quad\text{ as } \delta\downarrow 0 \,,
\end{equation*} 
where \({\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _{\Omega}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}\) is the \emph{disordered} version of \({\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _{\Omega}\); part of the statement is that the measure \({\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _{\Omega}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}\) is well-defined. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.934753+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1b8a83223b651f3bcca666f84c578de5cc64f183fd2368312c4283143fc78105","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm|EasyBound of arXiv:2504.08935, 'Higher-Order Derivatives of First-Passage Percolation with Respect to the Environment'] If $\mathcal F_k\subseteq\{T:|T|=k\}$ and if the number of elements in the family $\mathcal F_k$ is larger than or equal to $C\cdot n^{kd}$, for some fixed constant $C$, then there exists a constant $D$ such that 
