{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.307797+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1bd853f99782ad77b97aa75a0ff5bb7245b86efdd764f09cbb8187ef3c2953ff","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:clt_1 of arXiv:2411.14818, 'Scaling limits of solitons in the Box-Ball system'] Let $\zeta(i)$, $i \in {\mathbb{N}}$ be i.i.d. random variables define on any probability space with ${\mathbb{E}}[\zeta(0)] = 0$ and ${\mathbb{E}}[\zeta(0)^2] = 1$, and define $S(n) := \sum_{i = 1}^{n} \zeta(i)$, $n \in {\mathbb{N}}$. Assume that $a_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right), b_{\varepsilon}\left(t\right)$ are non-deacresing function on $[0,\infty)$ such that {for any ${\mathbf{T}} > 0$,}
                \begin{align}
