{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:43.791736+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1c3e2b8fd2b6ccb6d65eb0d81a49b39f8cf4370ae7bdf21a2cb2fb38bf33c5d3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_XVH_not_too_small of arXiv:2412.04328, 'The critical Karp--Sipser core of Erdős--Rényi random graphs'] There are absolute constants $C,c>0$ such that for $n$ large enough, if $0 \leqslant k < \widetilde{\theta}^n$ satisfies $|\widetilde{A}_k|, |\widetilde{B}_k|, |\widetilde{C}_k|<1000 \log n$, then
