{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.357409+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1cb804c1b9fca288affddfdae494af7258632e5fdae9f98049045308fea85232","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:cost-adjusted-revealability-crossing of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] Assume the setting of Theorem~(thm:two-coordinate-revelation of the paper), fix a continuation price $c>0$, and suppose that for almost every $m$ in the decision-relevant region the map $s\mapsto r(m,s)$ is continuous and strictly increasing on the conditional support of $\sigma_h\mid M_h=m$. Define the generalized critical revealability
\begin{equation}
\sigma_c(m)=\inf\{s\ge0:r(m,s)>c\},

\end{equation}
with $\inf\varnothing=+\infty$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item The scalar-margin decision is sufficient if and only if, for almost every $M_h$, either
\begin{equation}
\Pr\!\left(r(M_h,\sigma_h)\le c\mid M_h\right)=1
\quad\text{or}\quad
\Pr\!\left(r(M_h,\sigma_h)\ge c\mid M_h\right)=1.

\end{equation}
Equivalently, conditional on almost every margin value, the priced continuation gain has no mass on both strict sides of zero. Under the stated continuity and monotonicity conditions, a transparent sufficient support condition is that $\sigma_h\mid M_h=m$ is contained in $[0,\sigma_c(m)]$ or in $[\sigma_c(m),\infty)$; zero-gain ties at the boundary are value-neutral and may be assigned to either optimal meta-action.
\item If there is a set of margin values with positive probability on which
\begin{equation}
\Pr\!\left(r(M_h,\sigma_h)<c\mid M_h\right)>0
\quad\text{and}\quad
\Pr\!\left(r(M_h,\sigma_h)>c\mid M_h\right)>0,

\end{equation}
then the scalar-margin controller is strictly suboptimal:
\begin{equation}
V_{\rm meta}(\mathscr F_h)>V_{\rm meta}(M_h).
\end{equation}
Under the same monotonicity conditions, Equation~(equation eq:revealability-crossing of the paper) is implied by positive conditional mass strictly below and strictly above $\sigma_c(M_h)$. Thus conditional spread in revealability is \emph{decision-nonredundant} exactly when a positive-mass scalar fiber contains both the strict \textsc{Stop} side and the strict \textsc{Continue} side of the priced continuation decision; zero-gain ties at the boundary do not affect value.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.445431+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1cb88b5eb7e13ac821832b06d7a527a499c85f5c51379a0fdd476d1c9591c157","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.16541, 'The Hausdorff measure of the boundary of the Brownian disk'] A snake trajectory $\omega$ starting at $0$ is a continuous function $s\mapsto \omega_s$ from $\mathbb R_+$ into $\mathcal W_0$, with $\zeta(\omega_0)=0$ and such that:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[\textbullet] the lifetime $\sigma(\omega):=\sup\{s\geq 0, \ \zeta(\omega_s)> 0\}$ of $\omega$ is finite,
		\item[\textbullet] for all $0\leq s\leq s'$, $\omega_s(t)=\omega_{s'}(t)$ whenever $t\leq \min_{u\in [s, s']}\zeta(\omega_u)$.
	\end{itemize}
	We will write $\mathcal S_0$ for the set of all snake trajectories starting at $0$, endowed with the distance:
	\begin{equation*}
		d_{\mathcal S_0}(\omega, \omega')=|\sigma(\omega)-\sigma(\omega')|+\sup_{s\geq 0}d_{\mathcal W_0}(\omega_s,\omega'_s).
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.865655+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1cba7bf1daa09e28b112820c8891781f898057fc48257edb8302ce69efc2bebc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 (Exact local selector) of arXiv:2608.29107, 'PathGuide: Dynamic Classifier-Free Guidance via On-Policy Transport Alignment'] Fix $y$, and $\psi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and a committed past schedule $\vec{\omega}_i$.
Then $\mathcal{L}_i(\omega_i \mid y; \vec{\omega}_i, \psi)$, defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:exact-local-objective} is minimized for
\begin{equation}
\omega_i^\star(y; \vec{\omega}_i, \psi)
=
\frac{
