{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.774054+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d0729effb6745d0f4d4da6ad541fccf1c6aeb617eb9d1dc2f2425de07ffc560","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: BernPerc_internal_2 of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $\lambda > 0$, $d \geq 2$, and $\alpha > 0$.
Let $Q \in \mathcal{H}_{\alpha, K}$ and let $Q_L$ be defined as in (equation def: Q_L of the paper). 
There exist positive constants $R = R(\lambda, K, \alpha, d)$, $C = C(d)$, and $c = c(K, \alpha, d)$, such that for 
any $r, L$ with $L \ge r \ge R$, 
any $v \in \mathbb{T}^d_m$, and 
any $D \subseteq B_v$ with $|D|/|B_v| \ge 1/4$, we have 
