{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.888331+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d0dc22fdeec6e682f0c086313f5ae6ae832b06cd068ad6de5d048f6a9af8236","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:decreasHypExp of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $m>1$ and let $t>t_{min}$ and let $S_t:=\partial\Lambda_t$. For any $a_0\in\mathbb{R}^m$ and for any admissible random fields $W\randin C^\infty(S_t,\mathbb{R}^{m-2})$ on $S_t$ and all open set $U\subset S_t$, we have:
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}\#\sol{\mathcal{E}_0|_{S_t},W; U}\leq \mathbb{E}\#\sol{\mathcal{E}|_{S_t},W; U}.
  \end{equation} 
   In particular
  \begin{equation}
    \esol{\mathcal{E}_0|_{S_t}; U}\leq \esol{\mathcal{E}|_{S_t}; U}.
  \end{equation} 
  In both cases, the inequality is an equality if and only if the right hand side is zero in which case every term is equal to zero. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.712152+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d0dd847000e93a259ffae3d6970063f70c0ae3e2d603351c36c674a5383bdbe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma D(v) lower bound of arXiv:2604.11761, 'The smallest singular value of signed random combinatorial matrices'] {tran2020smallestsingularvaluerandom}}]
Let $\delta, \rho \in (0,1)$. Then for any vector $v \in \mathbb{S}^{n-1} \setminus \operatorname{Cons}(\delta,\rho)$, there are disjoint subsets $\sigma_1 = \sigma_1(v)\subset[n]$ and $\sigma_2 = \sigma_2(v)\subset[n]$ of cardinality $|\sigma_1|, |\sigma_2| \geq \delta n / 8$ and such that
\begin{align*}
  \frac{\rho}{\sqrt{2n}} \leq |v_i - v_j| \leq \frac{6}{\sqrt{\delta n}} \quad  \quad \text{for all } i \in \sigma_1 \text{ and } j \in \sigma_2.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.190679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d13920f02c98e07bd409d3e08da0c97997866f5744fbdc998ff652a3bce34df","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 of arXiv:2604.19200, 'Stochastic $\Sigma$-convergence in Orlicz setting and Applications'] A sequence $(u_{\varepsilon})_{\varepsilon\in E}\subset 	W^{1}L^{\Phi}_{D_{x}}(Q; L^{\Phi}(\Omega))$ is said to be weakly stochastically $\Sigma$-convergent (for $A$) in $	W^{1}L^{\Phi}_{D_{x}}(Q; L^{\Phi}(\Omega))$ to some $u_{0} \in 	W^{1}L^{\Phi}_{D_{x}}(Q; L^{\Phi}(\Omega;\mathcal{X}_{A}^{\Phi}))$ if there exist two  functions $u_{1} \in L^{1}\left(Q; W^{1}_{\#}L^{\Phi}(\Omega)\right)$ and $u_{2}\in L^{1}(Q\times\Omega; W^{1}_{\#}\mathcal{X}_{A}^{\Phi})$ such that as $E \ni \varepsilon \to 0$, we have 
		\begin{itemize}
			\item[(i)] $u_{\varepsilon} \rightarrow u_{0}$ in $L^{\Phi}(Q\times\Omega)$-weak $\digamma\,\Sigma$,
			\item [(ii)] $\frac{\partial u_{\varepsilon}}{\partial x_{i}} \rightarrow \frac{\partial u_{0}}{\partial x_{i}} + \overline{D}_{i,\Phi}u_{1} + \frac{\overline{\partial} u_{2}}{\partial y_{i}}$ in $L^{\Phi}(Q\times\Omega)$-weak $\digamma\,\Sigma$, $1 \leq i \leq d$.
		\end{itemize}
		We then express by: $u_{\varepsilon} \rightarrow u_{0}$ in $W^{1}L^{\Phi}_{D_{x}}(Q; L^{\Phi}(\Omega))$-weak $\digamma\,\Sigma$, and we refer to $u_{0}$ as the weak stochastic $\Sigma$-limit in $W^{1}L^{\Phi}_{D_{x}}(Q; L^{\Phi}(\Omega))$ of $(u_{\varepsilon})_{\varepsilon\in E}$. The functions $u_{1}$ and $u_{2}$ are the stochastic corrector and deterministic corrector for $(u_{\varepsilon})_{\varepsilon\in E}$, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:21:56.751577+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d13cd842b3c3193e56f74fd709b12f002177bfc7044c63dd2183b30ccaadb48","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma adapted4 of arXiv:2507.20324, 'Non-existence of several random fractals in Brownian motion and Brownian loop soup'] For any integer $1\leq i\leq[L/2]$, on the event $\{S\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}(\delta+2^{\sqrt{n}})\}\cap(\cap_{j=1}^{6}B_{j}^{c})$, whether a box $T\prec\mathcal{A}_{i}$ is $\delta$-good can be fully determined by $\overline{U}_{(i+4)L}$.
\par
As a direct consequence, $N_{i}$ is $\mathscr{F}_{(i+4)}$-measurable under the probability measure $\overline{\mathbb{P}}_{n}$, which is defined by
\begin{equation}
\overline{\mathbb{P}}_{n}(\cdot):=\mathbb{P}(\cdot|S\in\mathfrak{S}_{n}(\delta+2^{-\sqrt{n}}),\cap_{j=1}^{6}B_{j}^{c}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.518177+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d141093ed06215ba575de702cf74a6c5ba4325c3a44afd983bbd4c2b81de400","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Loop variables and observables of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] Let $U : E_\Lambda^+ \ra \unitary(N)$ be a lattice gauge configuration. For  loops $\ell=e_1e_2\dots e_n$, let $U_{\ell}:=U_{e_1}U_{e_2}\dots U_{e_n}$ denote the corresponding loop variable. We define
\begin{equs}
W_\ell(U) := \tr(U_\ell),
\end{equs}
where $\tr = \frac{1}{N} \Tr$ is the normalized trace. For strings $s=(\ell_1,\dots,\ell_n)$, we define
\begin{equs}
W_s(U):= \prod_{i \in [n]} W_{\ell_i}(U).
\end{equs}
We refer to $W_\ell$ as a Wilson loop observable, and $W_s$ as a Wilson string observable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["(X_n) independent","c > 0 constant"],"created_at":"2026-10-07T19:28:52+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d14ec11a3377e7a5e8111361b41381d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let (X_n)_{n=1}^\infty be independent real random variables, and fix c > 0. Define the truncated variables Y_n = X_n \mathbf{1}_{\{|X_n| \le c\}}. Then the series \sum_{n=1}^\infty X_n converges almost surely if and only if all three of the following series converge: (1) \sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbb{P}(|X_n| > c) < \infty; (2) \sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbb{E}[Y_n] \text{ converges in } \mathbb{R}; (3) \sum_{n=1}^\infty \text{Var}(Y_n) < \infty.

Preserved duplicate statement and commentary:
Let X_1, X_2, ... be independent random variables and fix a constant c > 0. For each i define the truncation Y_i = X_i 1(|X_i| <= c). Then the series sum_{i=1}^infinity X_i converges almost surely if and only if all three of the following series converge: sum_i P(|X_i| > c), sum_i E[Y_i], and sum_i Var(Y_i). If one of the three series diverges, the partial sums of sum X_i do not converge almost surely. [Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples]","status":"unverified"}

