{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:20:15.702680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d5f052b34761cdd85e0046a1260101de4ab5558203f136af8418df3e25d1855","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem main_theorem_action_angle of arXiv:2609.00973, 'The Vlasov-Maxwell point charge system : study of the spherically symmetric case'] There exists $\varepsilon>0$ such that, for all $0<\varepsilon_0<\varepsilon$, if 
        \begin{equation}

            \|(a^{30}+a^{-30}+|\theta|^{10}+\ell^{20})(\gamma+|\partial_a\gamma|+|\partial_\theta\gamma|)\|_{L^1_{\theta,a,\ell}}\leq \varepsilon_0,
        \end{equation}
        then, there exists a global solution $\gamma\in C^1_tL^1_{\theta,a,\ell}\cap C^0_tW^{1,1}_{\theta,a}$ with initial data $\gamma_0$. Moreover, there exists $\mathcal{E}_\infty\in L^\infty_a$, and $\gamma_\infty\in L^1_{\theta,a,\ell}$ such that
        \begin{equation}

            \gamma\left(t,\theta-\log(t)\frac{m^2}{a^0a^2}\mathcal{E}_\infty(a),a,\ell\right)\xrightarrow[t\rightarrow +\infty]{L^1_{\theta,a,\ell}} \gamma_\infty(\theta,a,\ell).
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.351714+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d63339b6da8e313e5b85cc9ed114141a1c47efd732e366f83391fdb6b7abadb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Them Bochner of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Let $K : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ be a symmetric map. The following conditions are equivalent: 
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item There exists $\mu \in \mathcal{M}_+(\mathbb{R})$ such that $K(s,t) = \hat{\mu}(t-s)$ for every $(s,t) \in \mathbb{R}^2.$
			\item The function $K$ is a continuous positive semi-definite stationary kernel.
		\end{enumerate}
		The measure $\mu$ is then unique and is called the spectral measure associated with $K$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.030858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d6454bda973014a81a446e95b5ce9cbdd606dd287540246118269f87f3098f8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2608.15616, '\textbf{Compact Support Property of Super-Brownian Motion with Irregular Drift}'] There exists a jointly measurable map $\bar{X}\colon \Omega \times \R_+\times \R \rightarrow \R_+\cup \{\infty\}$  such that $\bbP$-a.s. we have 
		\begin{equation*}
			\lim_{n \rightarrow  \infty} \bar{X}^n_t(x) = \bar{X}_t(x), \quad \forall (t,x) \in \R_+\times \R.
		\end{equation*} 
		Furthermore, $\bbP$-a.s.
		\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{k = 1}^n g^k(t,x) \rightarrow b_1 \mathbbm{1}_{\bar{X}_t(x)>0}, \quad (t,x) \in \R_+\times\R,
		\end{equation*}
		as $n \rightarrow \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.605801+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d65ab7cf03c287ebe883c2e564d60f28b3dfe269043381b8941469ccc1af5ad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:John of arXiv:2412.05142, 'Strong convergence of the Euler scheme for singular kinetic SDEs driven by $\alpha$-stable'] [BDG-Levy]
    Let $0\leqslant S\leqslant T$ and let ${\mathcal A}:\Omega\times[S,T]\mapsto{\mathbb R}^d$, $\xi:\Omega\times [S,T]\mapsto{\mathbb R}_+$ be  processes adapted to $({\mathcal F}_t)_{t\in[S,T]}$. Further assume  $({\mathcal A}_t)_{t\in[S,T]}$ to be continuous and integrable.  Suppose that for $(s,t)\in[S,T]_\leqslant^2$ one has
    \begin{align}
    {\mathbb E}^s|{\mathcal A}_t-{\mathcal A}_s|\leqslant \xi_s\quad a.s.
    \end{align}
    Then for any $p\geqslant1$ there exists a constant $N=N(p)$ such that for any $(s,t)\in[S,T]_\leqslant^2$ one has
    \begin{align}
        \|\sup_{r\in[s,t]}|{\mathcal A}_t-{\mathcal A}_s|\|_{L_p(\Omega)|{\mathcal F}_s}\leqslant N \|\sup_{r\in[s,t]}\xi_r\|_{L_p(\Omega)|{\mathcal F}_s}.
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.971100+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d661f827f6ba931c1a02f3349d40184999e915d777cde6e58ddc6a05e55348d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:entropic-hypocoercivity of arXiv:2506.23062, 'Shifted Composition IV\@: \\ Toward Ballistic Acceleration for Log-Concave Sampling'] Suppose that $\pi$ satisfies~(equation eq:lsi of the paper) with constant $1/\alpha$, and $-\beta I \preceq \nabla^2 V \preceq \beta I$.
    Consider the Lyapunov functional over $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbb{R}^d)$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak L(\bs\nu) \coloneqq \KL(\bs\nu \mathbin{\|} \bs\pi) + \mathbb{E}_{\bs\nu} \biggl\langle\nabla \log \frac{\bs\nu}{\bs\pi}\,,\; \Bigl(\begin{bmatrix}
            b_1/\beta & b_2/\sqrt{\beta} \\
            b_2/\sqrt{\beta} & b_3
        \end{bmatrix} \otimes I \Bigr)\,\nabla \log \frac{\bs\nu}{\bs\pi} \biggr\rangle\,.
    \end{align*}
    There exist absolute constants $b_1, b_2, b_3\in\mathbb{R}$ and $c>0$ such that for any $\bs\nu_0\in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d)$, and $\boldsymbol{P}_t$ the kernel of~(eq:ULD of the paper) up to time $t$ with friction $\gamma \asymp \sqrt\beta$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \KL(\bs\nu_0\boldsymbol{P}_t \mathbin{\|} \bs\pi)
        \leqslant \mathfrak{L}(\bs\nu_0 \boldsymbol{P}_t) \leqslant \exp\bigl({-\frac{c\alpha t}{\sqrt{\beta}}}\bigr)\, \mathfrak{L}(\bs\nu_0)\,.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.352663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d670013b8bb0936c28b8fc5adfc7c40822f28f7df17e262f27122f427b13664","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_momentbound_LQ1 of arXiv:2509.18045, 'Density convergence on Markov diffusion chaos via Stein's method'] Assume the setup of Theorem (theorem_superconvergencetogamma of the paper). Then we have the moment bounds 
