{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:06:55.275316+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1df8527aa05e9791a715754b514e214912162c87fb4901f62b5f8c9196c6bdf7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2512.21026, 'Minimax Duality in Game-Theoretic Probability\thanks{Work in progress: mistakes are likely'] Let $\mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^\Omega$ be a linear space of bounded functions containing the constant functions.
  A \emph{(financial) risk measure} on $\Omega$ is a function $\rho:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item $\rho(X+c) = \rho(X) - c$ for all $X\in\mathcal{X}, c\in\mathbb{R}$ (translation'),
  \item $X\leq Y \implies \rho(X) \leq \rho(X)$ for all $X,Y\in\mathcal{X}$ (monotonicity').
  \end{enumerate}
  The \emph{acceptance set} of $\rho$ is the set $\mathcal{A}_\rho := \{ X\in\mathcal{X} \mid \rho(X) \leq 0 \}$.","status":"unverified"}

