{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.892974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e1dc95579bf26fed4b45020f6158823c06c205750b38ad1872cbd89c328f413","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2508.04938, 'Perfect t-embeddings of doubly periodic Aztec diamonds'] Let~$\cT_N$ and~$\cO_N$ denote the perfect t-embedding and its associated origami map of the Aztec diamond of size~$2\ell N$ with~$(2\times\ell)$-periodic weights described above and~$(\xi,\eta)$ and~$(u,v)$ be as above.\\ 
1) If~$(\xi,\eta)$ is a point in the liquid region~$\mathcal F_R$, then
\begin{equation*}
(\cT_N(u,v)),\cO_N(u,v)))\to \left(\mathcal Z(\Omega(\xi,\eta)),\vartheta(\Omega(\xi,\eta))\right)
\end{equation*}
as~$N\to \infty$, where~$\Omega:\mathcal F_R\to \mathcal{R}_0$ 
is a diffeomorphism (properly defined in Proposition~\ref{prop:diffeomorphism_2xell}) known as the critical point map, and the convergence is uniform on compact subsets of~$\mathcal F_R$. \\
2) If~$(\xi,\eta)$ is in a gas or frozen region, and~$(\xi',\eta')\in \mathcal F_R$ is a sequence in the liquid region converging to a point~$(\xi_0,\eta_0)$ on the boundary of the liquid region and the gas or frozen component containing~$(\xi,\eta)$, then
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to \infty}(\cT_N(\xi,\eta),\cO_N(\xi,\eta))=\lim_{(\xi',\eta')\to (\xi_0,\eta_0)} \left(\mathcal Z(\Omega(\xi',\eta')),\vartheta(\Omega(\xi',\eta'))\right).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.410045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e210da3cc4204e224bd18626be5613a0f169ab37dbdf32ad366d2d294a597cd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2608.31070, 'Sub-Laplacians on Compact Lie Groups: Heat Kernels, Distance, and Zeta Determinants'] The map
		\begin{equation}
