{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.747172+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e55e13728305a44364d6a7a11c9c8f03762209443016ed337c0bd1e09916549","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 of arXiv:2511.19308, 'Universal scaling limits at the spectral singularity of structured random matrices'] Let $H$ be given by \eqref{eq: matrix model definition}.
    Then for all $z \in \C$ with $\Im(z) > 0$ and all $N \in \N$ the expectation of the resolvent has the integral representation
    \begin{equation} 
        \E\s*{\Tr\p*{\frac{1}{H - z}}}
        = \frac{\ii N^{K+1}}{(2 \pi \ii)^K} \int_{(0, \infty)^K} \dd a \oint^K \dd b \, \ee^{-N I(a) + N I(b)} \det\s*{ S + \diag_{i = 1}^K\p*{\frac{1}{a_i b_i}} } \sum_{j = 1}^K a_j,
    \end{equation}
    where $\dd a$ is the $K$-dimensional Lebesgue-measure and the $b$-integral is $\oint^K \dd b := \oint \dd b_1 \dots \oint \dd b_K$ where each $\oint \dd b_i$ is a counter-clockwise contour integral over the circle around $0$.
    The function $I: \R^K \to \C$ in the exponent is
    \begin{equation} 
        I(a) := \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i, j = 1}^K s_{ij} a_j a_i - \ii z \sum_{i = 1}^K a_i - \sum_{i = 1}^K \log(a_i).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

