{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.196072+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef3266b86c60e364f2ec6701070cf1dd42f3b4044ebdfc54698fdfd6dc87cdf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 25 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Length of the typical relaying zone.}

Let us consider $H$ a random variable distributed following $\mathcal{L}$. Let us further consider the landscape $\tilde{\landsc} := \landsc\cup \{(0, H)\}$. Then expectation of the length of the zone of the typical eventual relaying $\zone (0, H)$ is 
\[\e[\ell(\zone(0, H))] = \frac{1}{\lambda}\e[T],\]
where $T$ is the hitting time of the cemetery state $(-\infty, -\infty, -\infty)$ for the Markov chain $(\tilde{x}_n, \mathrm{t}_n, H_n)$ started at 
$(0, 0, +\infty)$:
\[\e[T] = \sum_{n\geq 1} \displaystyle\int_{\Half^{n+1}} g_n \delta_{(0, 0)}\displaystyle\bigotimes_{i=1}^{n} (\Leb \otimes \mathcal{L}).\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:44.009277+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef32c5d5ba77b0ad87e0e9b1cc4c6fd5d1961fd852c23f72aa9a9bfab400693","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:MagicM_ij of arXiv:2412.04579, 'Solvable Families of Random Block Tridiagonal Matrices'] For $\lambda = \{ \lambda_j\}_{j \in [\![rn ]\!] }$ and an $r\times rn $ matrix $\mathbf{X}$, define the $rn \times rn$ matrix $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}({\lambda}, \mathbf{X})$ entry-wise as
\begin{align}
 M_{i,j}=\lambda_i^{\nint{j}{r}} x_{ \nfr{j}{r},i},
\end{align}
in which
\begin{align*}

 \nint{j}{r}
 :=\left\lfloor \frac{j-1}{r} \right\rfloor, \qquad \nfr{j}{r}:=j-r \nint{j}{r}.
\end{align*}
Note that the shift in $ \nint{j}{r}$ means that $\lfloor r \rfloor_r = 0$ and hence
$\nfr{j}{r}\in [\![r ]\!]$.
For an $rn \times rn$ matrix~$\mathbf{X}$, we define $\mathbf{M}({\lambda}, \mathbf{X})$ using the $r \times rn$ submatrix of the first $r$ rows of $\mathbf{X}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.706937+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef4e59af6eb71bbc314d92828f88c6a079216319fd1f0d15ffaaac9ed66398a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem l:1104.1 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] The following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item 
Uniformly on ${\rm D}$,
\begin{equation}
	\psi_n \big(\lfloor {\rm e}^{n} \cdot \rfloor \big) 
	\underset{n \to \infty}\longrightarrow\
		\psi_{\rm D} \,,
\end{equation}
where $\psi_{\rm D}$ is as in~(equation e:1.9p of the paper). As $\psi_{\rm D}$ is a continuous function on $\overline{{\rm D}}$, the functions $\{\psi_n\}$ are uniformly bounded in the supremum norm and asymptotically equicontinuous, in the sense that 
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{\delta \downarrow 0} \limsup_{n\to \infty} \sup_{|x-y| < \delta {\rm e}^n} \big|\psi_n(x) - \psi_n(y) \big| = 0 \,.
\end{equation}
\item With $\overline{h}_{\rm D}$ as in~(equation e:1.7p of the paper),
\begin{equation}
	\overline{h}_n \underset{n \to \infty} \Longrightarrow \overline{h}_{\rm D} \,.
\end{equation}
\item 
With respect to the supremum norm on $\overline{{\rm D}}$, 
\begin{equation}

h^\psi_n(\lfloor {\rm e}^n \cdot \rfloor) \underset{n \to \infty}\Longrightarrow h^\psi_{\rm D}
\quad ; \qquad 
h^\psi_{\rm D} := \overline{h}_{\rm D} \psi_{\rm D} \,.
\end{equation}
The field $h_{\rm D}^\psi$ is Gaussian with mean zero and covariance given by
\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{E} h_{\rm D}^\psi(x)h_{\rm D}^\psi(y) = \frac{\int_{{\rm D} \times {\rm D}} G_{\rm D}(z,x) G_{\rm D}(z', y) {\rm d} z {\rm d} z'}{\int_{{\rm D} \times {\rm D}} G_{\rm D}(z,z') {\rm d} z {\rm d} z'} \,.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:30:28.832889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef57b7efa53f659a33cd018906ce412e5bfa2fb3888dc0f46d35d4f5ed1ea33","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 of arXiv:2609.02147, '2609.02147'] Suppose \eqref{A1}--\eqref{A4} and \eqref{equn}. Let $P^1_n,...,P^k_n \in \R^N$ be the points in \cref{prophk}, 
