{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.274649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f150ece8f41fe5d01619345e29125ea51b0b42a6d9ca9b1374b8523a9be0851","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 (Procrustes representation) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] For $L, M \in \mathscr{L}(N, d)$, we have
\begin{equation} 
\begin{split}
\dist_{\mathrm{AW}}^2(L, M) = \min_{Q \in \mathscr{O}(N, d)} \|L - MQ\|_{\mathrm{F}}^2 = \sum_{t = 1}^N \min_{Q_t \in \mathscr{O}(d)}  \| L_{\cdot, t} - M_{\cdot, t} Q_t\|_{\mathrm{F}}^2.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
If $(L^{\intercal} M)_{t, t} = (L_{\cdot, t})^{\intercal} M_{\cdot, t}$ has singular value decomposition $U_t \Sigma_t V_t^{\intercal}$, the minimum is attained if and only if $Q_t \in \mathscr{P}( (L^{\intercal}M)_{t,t}) \cap \mathscr{O}(d)$. We let $\mathscr{Q}(L, M)$ be the set of $Q \in \mathscr{O}(N, d)$ that has this form.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:00:15.225123+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f160f8b9229c543f60e82d86ff16f88522e49660890a788439dab3e19396d11","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2607.18619, 'Chernoff's Density Is Strongly Log-Concave'] Let
