{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.618228+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f6aad5b0c1659072083b0ee4669d4420dc4279a7fd3687d888faabff8e8baec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:uniqueness_neg of arXiv:2601.04997, 'Fluctuations of the Boundary-Driven Symmetric Zero-Range Process from the NESS'] For each $\theta<0$, there exists a unique (in law) stochastic process $\mathscr{Y}$ in the space of continuous distributions $\mathcal{C}([0, T], \mathcal{S}'_\theta)$ which satisfies both items of Proposition~(prop:uniqueness of the paper), and which also satisfies the two following additional conditions:
