{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:45:05.391094+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f8712851ca07d031aa109d8f2c83793435109ac2dbd1c2f23d29f18f0b2e898","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 128 (Stable first-jet state and value--martingale response target) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] The \emph{stable backward first-jet space} is
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr W_{Q,\cP}^{1,2}
 :=\overline{\mathfrak G_{Q,\cP}^{\rm bw,0}}
 ^{\,\mathbb S_{\cP}^2\times\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}.
\end{equation}
Its truncations are
\[
 \rho_t^{\mathscr W}(Y,Z):=(r_tY,\one_{[0,t]}Z),
 \qquad 0\le t\le T,
\]
and its graph metric is
\begin{equation}
 d_{\mathscr W}((Y,Z),(\widetilde Y,\widetilde Z))
 :=\|Y-\widetilde Y\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
   +\|Z-\widetilde Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}.
\end{equation}

The raw \emph{value--martingale response image} and its completion are
\begin{align}
 \mathscr R_{\rm VI}^{\rm bw,0}
 &:=\left\{\bigl([Y]_{\mathbb S^2},I(D_Q^{\rm pw}Y)\bigr):
       Y\in\mathscr C_{\rm bw}^{1,2}\right\},
 \\
 \mathscr R_{\rm VI}^{\rm bw}
 &:=\overline{\mathscr R_{\rm VI}^{\rm bw,0}}
 ^{\,\mathbb S_{\cP}^2\times\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}.

\end{align}
By the standing separability condition and completeness of the ambient Banach
products, both $\mathscr W_{Q,\cP}^{1,2}$ and
$\mathscr R_{\rm VI}^{\rm bw}$ are Polish.  The deterministic truncations
are continuous contractions, so these completions are causal Polish spaces in
the sense of \Cref{def:stable-causal}.

The response truncation is $(Y,M)\mapsto(r_tY,r_tM)$ and the response metric is
\begin{equation}
 d_{\rm VI}((Y,M),(\widetilde Y,\widetilde M))
 :=\|Y-\widetilde Y\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
   +\|M-\widetilde M\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}.
\end{equation}
Finally define the vertical space
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal N_Q^{\rm vert}
 :=\{Z\in\mathbb H_{\cP}^2:(0,Z)\in\mathscr W_{Q,\cP}^{1,2}\}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.737318+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f8d79db14520de9fdd317393312007894d1beba9689888611e5e371490d76bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_G_large_2 of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] In the setting of (mix of the paper), let $z=E+\mathrm{i} \eta$ with $\eta\gtrsim N^{-1/3+\tau_e}$ and $\eta/\im m (z) \sim N^{-\varepsilon_g}$ for some small constants $\tau_e,{\varepsilon}_g>0$. If $\varepsilon_g<({1/8})\wedge ({\delta_A/4})$, then for any $z_1,z_2\in\ha{z,\overline{z}}$, we have that
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            \max_{a\in [\![{D}]\!]}\absa{\mathbb{E}\avga{\left({G (z) -M (z) }\right)E_a}}\prec N^{-1}\left({\im m (z) }\right)^{-1},
        \end{aligned}
