$$W_{\mathbb{G}}(P,Q) = \inf_{\substack{(\Xi,\Sigma) \sim P \\ (\mathrm{H},\mathrm{T}) \sim Q}} \E \, d_{\mathbb{G}} \bigl( (\Xi,\Sigma), (\mathrm{H},\mathrm{T}) \bigr) = \sup_{f \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathbb{G}}} \, \biggl| \int_{\mathbb{G}} f \, dP - \int_{\mathbb{G}} f \, dQ \biggr|, \quad P,Q \in \mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{G}},$$

where $\mathcal{F}_{\mathbb{G}} = \bigl\{f \colon \mathbb{G} \to \R \bigm| \abs{f(\xi,\sigma) - f(\tau,\eta)} \leq d_{\mathbb{G}}((\xi,\sigma),(\tau,\eta)) \bigr\}$ and the second equality is due to the Kantorovich--Rubinstein theorem.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.506830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1fe611c956664b5cd3fdd9e8874a1d06e62820727b6fbe3c735503721ec30c34","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Radial ground solutions with spin of arXiv:2505.14762, 'Multiple radial SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Sutherland system'] For each radial link pattern $\alpha$, we choose Pochhammer contours $\mathcal{C}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{C}_n$ along which to integrate out the $\boldsymbol{\xi}$ variables. The integration is well-defined since the conformal dimension of $\Phi_\kappa(\boldsymbol{z}, \boldsymbol{\xi},u)$ is 1 at the $\boldsymbol{\xi}$ points, i.e. since $\lambda_b(-2 a)=1$. This leads to a new function of $\boldsymbol{z}$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}^{(m, n,\eta)}_{\alpha}(\boldsymbol{z},u):=\oint_{\mathcal{C}_1} \ldots \oint_{\mathcal{C}_m} \Phi_\kappa(\boldsymbol{z}, \boldsymbol{\xi},u) d \xi_m \ldots d \xi_1 .
\end{equation}

In angular coordinates, we obtain 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{J}^{(m, n,\eta)}_{\alpha}(\boldsymbol{\theta}):=\oint_{\mathcal{C}_1} \ldots \oint_{\mathcal{C}_m} \Phi_\kappa(\boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{\zeta}) d \zeta_m \ldots d \zeta_1 .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.301946+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1fedf870bc9b3d7ebe99cf37a92c781fb4dc39797d2eea3a53d00525c0662fd9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition the3.7 of arXiv:2604.22130, 'Reflected Stochastic Differential Equations Driven by $G$-Brownian Motion with Nonlinear C'] For $Y\in S_G^p(0,T)$ with $p\geq 1$, we have, by setting $Y_s:=Y_T$ for $s>T$,
\begin{displaymath}
\limsup_{\varepsilon\rightarrow0}\hat{\mathbb{E}}\left[\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\sup_{s\in[t,t+\varepsilon]}|Y_t-Y_s|^p\right]=0.
\end{displaymath} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.359744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1fee830f1c2ba78ec85f310488c50255934e1d94357ad50db4cb6412892bbad7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] When rolling a biased die $n$ times in a row:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $f_i=\#i$, we have $E(f_i)=np_i$.

\item For $g_i=i\#i$, we have $E(g_i)=nip_i$.

\item For $h={\rm sum}$, we have $E(h)=n\sum_iip_i$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.958886+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1fef5d937536384cf2311b3e628ad1b42092b98c6f22489c35fd4948ca06455d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 19 (Conditional Pfaffian $L$-ensemble) of arXiv:2512.02286, 'Pfaffian Transition Probability and Correlation Kernel of TASEP in Half-space'] Let $\mathcal{Z}$ be a set of points with subset $\mathcal{D}\subseteq\mathcal{Z}$ and complement $\mathcal{D}^\mathrm{C} = \mathcal{Z}\setminus\mathcal{D}$, and let $L:\mathcal{Z}\times\mathcal{Z}\to \mathbb{C}^{2\times2}$ be a skew-symmetric $2\times2$-matrix valued function. A measure $\mu$ on the powerset of the subset $2^\mathcal{D}$ is a \emph{conditional Pfaffian $L$-ensemble} on $\mathcal{D}$ within $\mathcal{Z}$ if its correlation function $\rho$ is given by
	\begin{equation}

		\rho(Y) = \frac{\pf L_{Y\cup \mathcal{D}^\mathrm{C}}}{\pf\left(J_\mathcal{D}+L\right)},
	\end{equation}
	where $J_\mathcal{D}$ denotes the kernel $J$ restricted to $\mathcal{D}$, i.e.
	\[J_\mathcal{D}(x,y)=J(x,y)\mathbbm{1}_{x,y\in\mathcal{D}}.\]","status":"unverified"}

