{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.555419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_209993496aaae3991314725893b0d18cc4806a63ebc2d186f54828240132713d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm th:h-growth of arXiv:2605.21366, 'The Martin boundary of the Directed Landscape'] On a full-probability event, any eternal solution $\mathfrak h$ satisfies
\begin{align}
    \lim_{|y|\to\infty} |y|^{-1}\bigl(\sup_{t'\le t\le t''}\mathfrak h(y,t)-ay^2\bigr) = -\infty
    \qquad \text{for all $t'<t''$ and $a>0$ in $\mathbb{R}$}.
