{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:53:51.342161+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_20fa347832f268bc32255486942d9b137a81990bec429487240a2194cafdf50b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary c:natdyn of arXiv:2605.05904, 'Schr\"odinger's problem with constraints'] Assume the setting of Theorem (thm:first of the paper). Then the following dynamics hold for $t\in [0,1]$:
\begin{align}
    dX^1_t&= \chf_{X^1_t\in E}\left(\sigma(t,X^1_t)dB_t^1 + b(t,X^1_t)dt+\sigma^2(t,X^1_t)\frac{\pi^2_y(t,X^1_t)}{\pi^2(t,X^1_t)}dt\right)\\
    dX^2_t&=\chf_{X^2_t\in E}\left(\sigma(t,X^2_t)dB_t^2 + b(t,X^2_t))dt+\sigma^2(t,X^2_t)\frac{\pi^1_y(t,X^2_t)}{\pi^1(t,X^2_t)}dt\right),
\end{align}
where, for $i=1,2$, $B^i$ is a $(P^*,\cF^{X^i})$-Brownian motion, $\pi^i$ is the function defined in Corollary~(c:conddist of the paper), and $\pi^i_y$ stands for the derivative of $\pi^i$ w.r.t. its second argument.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.318167+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_20faca56d89afd9d07f5e3ddb23b948476a1908f007da7108f32b6ee0d43aa82","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 (Martingale Difference Inequality) of arXiv:2506.20789, 'Central limit theory for Peaks-over-Threshold partial sums of long memory linear time seri'] \
  Let $p\in[1,2]$, and
  let $(Y_{m,i}\,, i=1, \ldots,m\,, m\in\NN )\subset L^p(\PP)$ be an
  array of random variables satisfying
\begin{align}

  \EE
  \Bigg[
  Y_{m,\ell + 1} \, \Bigg{|} \, \sum_{i=1}^{\ell}Y_{m,i}
  \Bigg]
  \ = \
  0
  \qquad\text{for all}\ 1\le \ell \le m-1
  \ \text{and all}\  m\in\NN\,.
\end{align}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item
It holds that
\begin{align}

  \EE
  \left|
  \sum_{i=1}^{m}
  Y_{m,i}
  \right|^p
  \ \le \
  2
  \sum_{i=1}^{m}
  \EE
  \left|
  Y_{m,i}
  \right|^p
  \qquad\text{for all}\ m\in\NN\,.
\end{align}
\item
If there exists a sequence $(Y_i)_{i\in\NN}\subset L^p(\PP)$ such that
  \begin{align}

    \lim_{m\to\infty}
    \sum_{i=1}^m
    Y_{m,i}
     =
    \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}
    Y_{i}
    \quad\text{$\PP$-almost surely, and }\quad
    \liminf_{m\to\infty}
    \sum_{i=1}^m
    \EE|
    Y_{m,i}
    |^p
    \le
    \sum_{i=1}^\infty
    \EE|
    Y_{i}
    |^p
    \,,
  \end{align}
  then
\begin{align}

  \EE
  \left|
  \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}
  Y_i
  \right|^p
  \ \le \
  2 \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}
  \EE
  \left|
  Y_i
  \right|^p
  \,.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.857717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_20fd71dc2441465894458322e925f8fc9741407991acb01c20f6c1dfc45f9384","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 18 of arXiv:2607.16479, 'The Site Frequency Spectrum in an Exponentially-Growing Population with Selection'] The following hold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $j(t)\gg\exp(t\lambda_0\lambda_1/(\lambda_0+\lambda_1))$ implies $S_{j(t)}(t)\to 0$ in $L^1$.
    \item $j(t)\sim C\exp(t\lambda_0\lambda_1/(\lambda_0+\lambda_1))$ implies:
    \[\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[S_{j(t)}(t)]=\frac{\lambda_1(av+\mu)}{(a+b)^2}C^{-\frac{\lambda_1+\lambda_0}{\lambda_1}}\left(\frac{a+b}{a+b-d}\right)^{\frac{\lambda_1+\lambda_0}{\lambda_1}}\Gamma(1+\lambda_0/\lambda_1).\]
    \item $j(t)\ll \exp(t\lambda_0\lambda_1/(\lambda_0+\lambda_1))$ implies
