{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.643034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2172c9ff5e2f0eef3200e4e0a038cf91f5e256a7d34e2c72e00cefbfd38bbfc2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2605.31471, 'Convergence Rates of Continuous-Time Random Walks to Time-Fractional Diffusions with Unbou'] Let $X, Y, Z$ be Banach spaces and let $f \colon X \to Y$,
    $g \colon Y \to Z$.  
    Let $x \in X$ and set $y = f(x)$.  
    Assume that $f$ and $g$ have continuous derivatives up to order $m$ in a neighbourhood of $x$ and $y$, respectively. Then, for $v_1,\dots,v_m\in X$,
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        \DD^m (g \circ f)(x)[ v_1, \dots, v_m]
        &=
        \sum_{k=1}^{m}
        \ \sum_{P \in \mathcal{P}(m,k)}
        \DD^k g\bigl(f(x)\bigr)
        \Bigl[
            \DD^{|P_1|} f(x)\bigl[ \bigvee_{i \in P_1} v_i \bigr],
            \dots,
            \DD^{|P_k|} f(x)\bigl[ \bigvee_{i \in P_k} v_i \bigr]
        \Bigr].
    \end{aligned}
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.310507+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2176203a12315fc7e9d828b467a8b2fab7c3560b4a0fae764ba54f033ca0bf3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Kahaneineq of arXiv:2603.14302, 'On Partition Functions for Time-Inhomogeneous Branching Random Walks'] (Kahane's Concentration Inequality)
    Let $F$ be a concave function such that:
    \begin{equation*}
        \forall x\in \mathbb{R}_+, |F(x)|\leq M\left(1+|x|^m\right)
    \end{equation*} 
    for some positive constants $M,m$, and let $\sigma$ be a Radon measure on the Borel subsets of $\mathbb{R}^d$. Given a bounded Borel set $A$, let $(X_r)_{r\in A}, (Y_r)_{r\in A}$ be two continuous centered Gaussian processes with continuous covariance kernels $K_X$ and $K_Y$ such that:
    \begin{equation*}
        \forall u,v\in A, K_X(u,v)\leq K_Y(u,v)
    \end{equation*}
    Then we have:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}\left[F\left(\int_A e^{X_r-\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}[X_r^2]}\sigma(dr)\right)\right]\geq \mathbb{E}\left[F\left(\int_A e^{Y_r-\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}[Y_r^2]}\sigma(dr)\right)\right]
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.388426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2178bb2964767070ccb1d73fbe1a1779edb20102ecadef2be69a019fbd491182","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cavitymethod of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] For any $p \in U_\beta$ and $\varepsilon > 0$, there are some $\eta, \gamma > 0$ such that,
when $X \sim \mu_\mathsf{H}^\eta(n,p)$, we have
\begin{equation}
