	\Gamma_{\overline{C}} := \left\{ (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d : |x - y| > \overline{C} \right\}.
	\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.720713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21d26a5cf41bce50a1458ca66760e0fe5058fad78871ccca214824a242dbcfd9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2609.00285, 'Multicrossing complex of knot and secant classes'] Let $K$ be  an oriented knot, $-K$ the reversed knot, and $\bar K$ the mirror knot. Then
\begin{enumerate}
    \item the multicrossing complexes for $-K$ and $\bar K$ are identified with the complex $(C_*(K,A), -d)$;
    \item the maps $\tau_n=(-1)^n id\colon C_n(K,A)\to C_n(K,A)$ define an isomorphism $\tau_*$ between $H_*(K,A)$ and $H_*(-K,A)=H_*(\bar K,A)$;
    \item for $A=\Z$, $\tau_*(\sigma_n(K))=\sigma_n(-K)=\sigma_n(\bar K)$, $n=3,4$, but $\tau_*(\sigma_2(K))=\sigma_2(-K)=-\sigma_2(\bar K)$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:25:35.679750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21d3288b1f39ce962581ad59673ffa667087d05c13e9b1e686407e0aada8e46a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition discover_nsep_first of arXiv:2603.05491, '\textbf{Local limits of uniform triangulations with boundaries in high genus}'] There exist $m_1,m_2 \ge 0$ and a partial order $i_1,\cdots,i_{m_1+2m_2}$ with $m:=m_1+2m_2 \le \varepsilon p^{-1}|\mathbf{p}|$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  For any $j \in \{1,\cdots,m_1\}$, step $j$ is $\mathbf{NSEP}$.
    \item For any $j \in \{1,\cdots,m_2\}$, step $m_1+2j-1$ is $\mathbf{SEP}$ and step $m_1+2j$ is $\mathbf{NSEP}$.
    \item  For any $i_{m+1},\cdots,i_{1000 \varepsilon |\mathbf{p}|}$ completing the partial order $i_1,\cdots,i_m$ to a full order $i_1,\cdots,i_{1000 \varepsilon |\mathbf{p}|}$ and for any $j \in \{m+1,\cdots,1000 \varepsilon |\mathbf{p}|\}$, step $j$ is $\mathbf{SEP}$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:53:54.877407+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21d380c87235686b3766eb1d6348bb867261939fe6a423b9900c615098deb791","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2607.17030, 'Stationary measures for log-gamma polymer on a strip and in half-space'] If $|u|<\alpha$, $v>-\alpha$ and $\alpha-u+2t_j>0$, $j=1,\dots N-1$, and $\alpha+v+2t_N>0$, then
\begin{multline}
     \GG_{t_1,\dots,t_N}(\alpha,u,v)
     =\Gamma(\alpha+v+2t_N) \Gamma(\alpha+u)^N \\ \cdot  \Biggl(\prod_{k=1}^{N-1}\Gamma(\alpha-u+2t_k)\Biggr)\, \EE [(1+\zeta_1+\zeta_1\zeta_2+\dots+\zeta_1\dots \zeta_{N-1})^{-u-v}]
     \end{multline}
with independent $\zeta_k\simeq \mathrm{Beta}_{II}(\alpha+u,\alpha-u+2t_{N-k})$, $k=1,\dots,N-1$.

    If $|v|<\alpha$, $u>-\alpha$ and $\alpha+v+2t_j>0$, $j=1,\dots,N$,  then
   \begin{multline}
     \GG_{t_1,\dots,t_N}(\alpha,u,v)
     =\Gamma(\alpha+u) \Gamma(\alpha-v)^{N-1} \\ \cdot   \Biggl(\prod_{k=1}^N \Gamma(\alpha+v+2t_k)\Biggr)\, \EE [(1+\xi_1+\xi_1\xi_2+\dots+\xi_1\dots\xi_{N-1})^{-u-v}]
     \end{multline}
with independent $\xi_k\simeq \mathrm{Beta}_{II}(\alpha+v+2 t_k,\alpha-v)$, $k=1,\dots,N-1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.537921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21d3ad9e9e030e0ce4eadbd19089634332802fc9c110e3fbe7e6ef152e0dd2cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 26 (Potter's bound, \protect{\autocite[Theorem 1.5.6) of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] {BinghamGoldieTeugels1987RegularVariation}}]

Let \(f\) be a \(\beta\)-varying function. For all \(C>1\) and
\(\varepsilon>0\), there is \(A>0\) such that

\begin{equation*}{
 \forall x,y \geq A,\ \frac{f(x)}{f(y)} \leq C\times\left( \frac{x}{y} \right)^{\beta+\varepsilon} \vee \left( \frac{x}{y} \right)^{\beta-\varepsilon}.
}\end{equation*}

In particular, for all \(\varepsilon>0\) we have
\(x^{\beta-\varepsilon} \ll f(x) \ll x^{\beta+\varepsilon}\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.739129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21d3c0cb5617a55b593292ce8e0c364a1ead006d51122af442c080d54c338703","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 42 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] For any $\mfl\in\mfL_+$, one has the identity
