        & \quad + 2 \| \chi \|_{L^{\infty}} \int_{\bR^2} ( |\textup{div} \, S|  +|\textup{div} \, T|) + \| \chi \|_{L^{\infty}} \int_{\bR^2} (q_x(S)_- + q_y(T)_-).
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.744067+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_227a18abf8ad146c33e629e54658b84abf314f61110aaaefeade878a2228d1dc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:manfence of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] A $3$-manifold with annular boundary fences and boundary windows, or \textbf{manifold with fences and windows} for simplicity, is a manifold with boundary and (concave) corners $M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ of the form $\overline{M \setminus N}$, where $M$ is a usual $3$-manifold with boundary (and without corners), and $N$ is a disjoint union of codimension-$0$ submanifolds with boundary and corners, disjoint from $\partial M$, and diffeomorphic to $S^1 \times I \times I$. It also comes with the data of \textbf{windows} which are subsets of $\partial M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ two types: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item A \textbf{square window} is a smoothly embedded squares $I \times I$ inside a component of $\partial M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ which is not a fence,
        \item A \textbf{toroidal window} is a an entire boundary component of $\partial M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ which is not a fence.
    \end{itemize}
    Moreover, we require that the windows are pairwise disjoint.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:50:36.762950+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_227a951ad83d7d122fed188b27646d215a9a9e00e1fd4e983f42879f2e7a22a1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Computation of $q(\pi_p\otimes\tilde\chi)$) of arXiv:2609.04155, 'The subconvexity problem for symmetric square\\ $L$-functions in Level aspect'] Let $\pi\simeq\bigotimes_\nu^\prime\pi_\nu$ be as usual. Then
