{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.569531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_228946744d8e75e49dbc59344c3d385f4ef8cd8f3d99d7f9211f5054052ff20e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:cox2charac of arXiv:2412.04589, 'Inverting the Markovian projection for pure jump processes'] Let $X$, $\mathbb{G}$, $(J_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$, $(\tau_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$ and $(\lambda^k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$ be given by Theorem~(thm:cox of the paper). Furthermore, suppose that each $\lambda^k$ is predictable with respect to $\mathbb{G}$. Let $h: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a truncation function. Then, $X$ is a $\mathbb{G}$-It\^o semimartingale whose differential characteristics associated with $h$ are
	\begin{equation*}
		\beta_t = \mathbb{E}[h(J_1)] \lambda_t,\quad
		\alpha_t = 0,\quad
		\kappa_t(d\xi) = \lambda_t \nu(d\xi),
	\end{equation*}
	where $\nu$ is the law of $J_1$ and $\lambda_t \coloneqq \sum_{k=1}^\infty \bm{1}_{\{\tau_{k-1} < t \leq \tau_k\}} \lambda^k_t$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.503375+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_228db1512fbf534aa31fd47cc6dbe1aff42e91a2f33e3ad210c24ace7d4045b9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 63 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $A : \bbr^m \to \bbr^{(m \times d) \times d}$ be the linear operator given by
\begin{align*}
A_{ij,k}(v) =
\begin{cases}
v_i, & \text{if $j=k$,}
\\ 0, & \text{otherwise,}
\end{cases}
\quad \text{for all $v \in \bbr^m$,}
\end{align*}
for all $i=1,\ldots,m$ and $j,k = 1,\ldots,d$. Then we have
\begin{align*}
v \otimes w = A(v) \cdot w \quad \text{for all $v \in \bbr^m$ and $w \in \bbr^d$.}
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.973494+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_228f39eb8f0c4be536859d22dfe7a031748daa504e22367a7f31d9342aad6c9f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def: lattice distance of arXiv:2508.13827, 'Surface sums in two-dimensional large-$N$ lattice Yang--Mills: Cancellations and explicit '] For a loop $\ell$, define its \textbf{distance} (to infinity) $d_{\ell}: \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ as follows:
        \begin{equation}
            d_{\ell}(p) = \min_{\{p_i\}_{i}\in \mathfrak{P}(p)}\,\,\,\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}n_{\mathfrak{e}(p_i,p_{i+1})}(\ell), \quad\text{for all }p\in\mathcal{P}^{+}.
        \end{equation}
        For $p\in\mathcal{P}\setminus \mathcal{P}^+$, we define $d_{\ell}(p) = d_{\ell}(p^{-1})$. For a string $s=\{\ell_1,\dots,\ell_n\}$, we define $d_s(p) = \sum_{i\in [n]}d_{\ell_i}(p)$ for all $p\in \mathcal{P}$. 

        We will often refer to an infinite path of plaquettes $\{p_i\}_{i}\in \mathfrak{P}(p)$ that achieves the minimum in (equation eq: dist def of the paper) as a \textbf{distance-achieving path}.","status":"unverified"}

