{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.164276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_22a8b17391a202cd5c063126ce33f0aa476204e648b167316053f0945e7752c7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Eq_ChiralDecomposition of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] Here is the decomposition of the objects constructed in (Sec_UniversalBundlesOfSingularSpinorsAndResidues of the paper):
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item
    Decompose the tame Fréchet space bundles $\mathbf{E}$, $\mathbf{F}$, and $\mathbf{G}$ into tame Fréchet subbundles
    \begin{equation}

      \mathbf{E} = \mathbf{E}^+ \oplus     \mathbf{E}^-, \quad
      \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{F}^+ \oplus \mathbf{F}^-, \quad\text{and}\quad
      \mathbf{G} = \mathbf{G}^+ \oplus \mathbf{G}^-.
    \end{equation}
    with fibres over $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$ given by
    \begin{align*}
      \mathbf{E}_{{\mathbf{p}},{\mathfrak l}}^\pm &=  H_a^\infty\Gamma\paren{X{\backslash} \mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}),S^\pm\otimes{\mathfrak l};{\mathbf{p}}}, \\
      \mathbf{F}_{{\mathbf{p}},{\mathfrak l}}^\pm &= H_b^\infty\Gamma\paren{X{\backslash} \mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}),S^\pm\otimes{\mathfrak l};{\mathbf{p}}}, \quad\text{and} \\
      \mathbf{G}_{{\mathbf{p}},{\mathfrak l}}^\pm &= \Gamma\paren{\mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}),\check S_{\mathbf{p}}^\pm}.
    \end{align*}
  \item
    Decompose the universal Dirac operator ${\mathbf{D}} \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{E} \to \mathbf{F}$ into the \defined{universal chiral operator} ${\mathbf{D}}^\pm \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{E}^\pm \to \mathbf{F}^\mp$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
      {\mathbf{D}}
      =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        0 & {\mathbf{D}}^- \\
        {\mathbf{D}}^+ & 0
      \end{pmatrix}.
    \end{equation*}
  \item
    Decompose the universal residue map $\mathbf{res} \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{E} \to \mathbf{G}$ into the \defined{universal chiral residue map} $\mathbf{res}^\pm \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{E}^\pm \to \mathbf{G}^\pm$.
    \qedhere
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.309330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_22a998c4f3db43daa14328e57fd9ca2a83bab8b83d9b858c75696185595d7833","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 18 of arXiv:2604.07697, 'Stochastic fractional heat equation with general rough noise'] (\cite[Definition 2.2]{HHLNT2017})
An elementary process $g$ is a process given by
$$
    g(t,x)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m} X_{i,j}\textbf{1}_{(a_i,b_i]}(t)\textbf{1}_{(h_j,l_j]}(x),
$$
   where $n$ and $m$ are finite positive integers, $0\leq a_1<b_1<\cdots<a_n<b_n<\infty$, $h_j<l_j$ and $X_{i,j}$ are $\mathcal{F}_{a_i}$-measurable random variables for $i=1,\dots,n$, $j=1,\dots,m$. The stochastic integral of such  an elementary  process with respect to $W$ is defined as
   \begin{equation}
     \begin{split}
       \int_{\mathbb{R}_{+}}\int_{\mathbb{R}} g(t,x)W(dt,dx) 
     =&\, \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}  X_{i,j}W(\textbf{1}_{(a_i,b_i]}\otimes \textbf{1}_{(h_j,l_j]}) \\
      =&\, \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}  X_{i,j}\Big[W(b_i,l_j)-W(a_i,l_j)-W(b_i,h_j)+W(a_i,h_j)\Big].
     \end{split}
   \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["H a Hilbert space (complete); K : H → H compact linear","the statements concern λI − K with λ ≠ 0, normalised to λ = 1"],"created_at":"2026-09-13T01:59:57.492717+00:00","domain":"functional analysis, Riesz–Schauder theory, compact operators","id":"claim_22a9aef86baaa158148e612db4904e9458450f7a225443cc98c30dec7fad3de3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let H be a Hilbert space (real or complex; the Banach-space version is identical with K* the Banach adjoint acting on the dual) and let K : H → H be a compact linear operator. Put T = I − K; the Hilbert adjoint is T* = I − K*, also identity minus compact. Then:
(a) ker T is finite-dimensional;
(b) range T is closed, range T = (ker T*)^⊥ and range T* = (ker T)^⊥;
(c) ker T = {0} if and only if range T = H (injective ⇔ surjective ⇔ bijective with bounded inverse);
(d) dim ker T = dim ker T* = codim range T < ∞, i.e. T is a Fredholm operator of index zero.

The alternative. EITHER the homogeneous equation u − Ku = 0 has only the trivial solution, and then u − Ku = f has exactly one solution for every f ∈ H, depending continuously on f; OR u − Ku = 0 has a d-dimensional space of solutions with 1 ≤ d < ∞, and then u − Ku = f is solvable if and only if ⟨f, w⟩ = 0 for every solution w of the adjoint homogeneous equation w − K*w = 0 (whose solution space is also d-dimensional); in that case the solution set is u_0 + ker T, an affine space of dimension d.

Spectral consequences. For each λ ≠ 0 apply the above to K/λ: σ(K) \ {0} consists of eigenvalues of finite multiplicity, is finite or a sequence tending to 0, and for such λ the generalised eigenspace ∪_m ker(λI − K)^m is finite-dimensional. If dim H = ∞ then 0 ∈ σ(K).

Where it fails. Compactness is essential: for the unilateral shift S on ℓ^2 (bounded, not compact) I − S is injective but not surjective (1 ∈ σ(S), the closed unit disc), so (c) fails. The alternative concerns I − K, not K itself: nothing is claimed at λ = 0; a compact K on an infinite-dimensional H is never surjective yet may be injective (diag(1/n) on ℓ^2), so 'K injective ⇒ K invertible' is false. In the second alternative the solvability condition is orthogonality to ker(I − K*), not to ker(I − K); they coincide when K is self-adjoint (or normal) but not in general. On an incomplete inner-product space closedness of the range and (c) can fail.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.064019+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_22abce6c680b1abb2565240d33ffdc494dc63e745ff677e7a1b0b2f288f6a133","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 (Doob's optional stopping version I, supermartingale $\{f_j \equiv \overline{\sigma}_j f\}$) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] \hspace{.1in}

