{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.590463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_22d631a43f8e6639872d4208f1eb76c3c1e60d839bd60b86e244a0a4d424420a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition HNNI-MAIN of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] For any $q \in {\mathbb{Z}^ + }$ and $t \in \mathop  \cap \limits_{j = 0}^q \left[ {0,{\mathfrak{t}_j}} \right]$, there exists a real number $\alpha \in \left( {\frac{\beta }{{b}},\beta } \right)$ such that the Nash elements of the Euler–Reynolds system (equation ERNE-3A.1 of the paper) satisfy the following inductive estimates
\begin{equation}
