    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.321594+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_237bb61f89e6c87b4933acbc51b9cadabbeadec812369d11594be3e9a35abeaf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:phibound of arXiv:2411.09058, 'Time-dependent averages of a critical long-range stochastic heat equation'] For any $n\geq 1$ and $\bm{\eta}_n \in \mathbb{R}^{nd}$,
\begin{align}
\varphi(\bm{\eta}_n)\leq \frac{1}{n!}.
  \end{align}
  Thus for any $n\geq 2$ and $\bm{\eta}_n \in \mathbb{R}^{nd}$,
\begin{align}
 \lim_{R\to \infty}   \frac{1}{R^{2n-2}}\varphi(\bm{\eta}_{n}/R) =0.
    \end{align}
  Moreover, for any $n\geq 2$ and $1\leq k\leq n-1$, when $\eta_{i}\neq 0$ for $1\leq i \leq k$, we have
\begin{align}
\varphi(\bm{\eta}_{n}) \leq \left(1\wedge \frac{1}{\|\eta_1\|^2\dots \|\eta_k\|^2} \right)   \varphi(\bm{\eta}_{k,n}).
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

