{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.333925+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_23b4743b7ed4ce86b4b24d19966f20052cd9acbe279639941371d5beeda9487c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:abstractresolventbounds of arXiv:2609.03980, 'Decay Rates and Domain Dependence of a Coupled Wave-Heat System with Spatially Dependant H'] Let $(T(t))_{t\geq 0}$ be a bounded semigroup with generator $A$ on a Hilbert space $H$  such that $\sigma(A)\cap i\mathbb{R} = \varnothing.$
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textnormal{(Batty, Duyckaerts, Burq, Lebeau)}
    The semigroup $T$ possesses logarithmic decay with rate $1/d,d>0$, i.e., 
    \begin{align}
        \forall k \in \mathbb{N} ~ \exists C_k>0: ~ \|T(t)x\|_{H} \leq \frac{C_k}{\log(t+2)^{\frac{k}{d}}} \|x\|_{A^k} ~~ \forall t\geq0,  x\in \operatorname{dom}\left(A^k\right),
    \end{align}
    if and only if the generator $A$ has exponential resolvent bounds with power $d>0$, i.e., there exists $C>0$ such that
    \begin{align}

        \|(\lambda i +A)^{-1} \|_{H \to H} \leq C \exp{(C|\lambda|^d)} ~~\forall \lambda\in \mathbb{R}, |\lambda|\gg1.
    \end{align}
    \item   \textnormal{(Borichev, Tomilov)}
    The semigroup $T$ possesses polynomial decay with rate $1/d,$  $d>0$, i.e., 
    \begin{align}
        \forall k \in \mathbb{N} ~ \exists C_k>0: ~ \|T(t)x\|_{H} \leq \frac{C_k}{(1+t)^{\frac{k}{d}}} \|x\|_{A^k} ~~ \forall t\geq0,  x\in \operatorname{dom}\left(A^k\right),
    \end{align}
    if and only if the generator $A$ has polynomial resolvent bounds with power $d>0$, i.e., there exists $C>0$ such that
    \begin{align}

        \|(\lambda i +A)^{-1} \|_{H \to H} \leq C |\lambda|^d ~~\forall \lambda\in \mathbb{R}, |\lambda|\gg 1.
    \end{align}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.439406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_23b62e671a8f3200807c31ba4153eda9b5993c4de3bf2457b256a1fbe116cce7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Local existence of mild solutions) of arXiv:2606.15425, 'Well-posedness of stochastic parabolic equations with gradient nonlinearities and applicat'] Let $d \geq 2$, $q \in (d,2d)$, $\delta \in \left(\frac dq, 1\right)$ and $\sigma >\delta-\frac dq$. Assume that $F$ and $G$ satisfy Assumptions \ref{ass:DriftNonlin} and \ref{ass:nemytskiiLipschitz}. Then, for any $p \in \left[\frac{q}{\delta q-d},\infty\right)$ and $\mathcal F_0$-measurable ${\bm z}_0 \in L^0(\Omega;B^{d/q}_{q,2p}(\mathbb T^d))$, there exists a unique maximal local solution $({\bm z},\tau)$ of \eqref{eq:FullNoiseSPDE} in the sense of Definition \ref{def:localsol}, with
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Maximality) If $(\widetilde {\bm z},\widetilde\tau)$ is any other local solution, then $\widetilde\tau\leq \tau_{\max}$ a.s. and
$\widetilde {\bm z}_t={\bm z}_t$ for all $t<\widetilde\tau$ a.s.
\item (Blow-up criterion) For any stopping time $\tau \leq \tau_{\mathrm{max}}$,
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{t\nearrow \tau_{\max}}   ~\norm{{\bm z}}_{L^\infty([\tau,t];B^{d/q}_{q,2p}(\mathbb T^d))}+ \norm{{\bm z}}_{L^{2}([\tau,t];H^{1,q}(\mathbb T^d))} + \norm{{\bm z}}_{L^4([\tau,t];H^{\delta,q}(\mathbb T^d))} =\infty.
\end{equation}
$\mathbb P$-almost surely on the event $\{\tau < \tau_{\max}<T\}$,
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.636993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_23b6709f03ea9e575109bf450328d7f4d8aab5c409d2e73d83e344a20d648282","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm relation static and dynamic SBP of arXiv:2411.13765, 'Schr\"odinger Bridge Problem for Jump Diffusions'] [follmer1988random]
The Schr\"odinger problems (equation Definition:DynamicSBP of the paper) and (equation Definition:StaticSBP of the paper) admit at most one solution $\hat{\mathbf{P}}$ and $\hat{\pi}$ respectively. Assume that the reference measure $R$ in the static problem (equation Definition:StaticSBP of the paper) and the reference path measure $\mathbf{R}$ in the dynamic problem (equation Definition:DynamicSBP of the paper) are such that $R=\mathbf{R}_{0T}$. Then, we have the following relations between the solutions: 
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item If $\hat{\pi}$ is the solution to (equation Definition:StaticSBP of the paper), then
            \begin{equation}
                \hat{\mathbf{P}}(\cdot):=\int_{\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}^n}\mathbf{R}^{xy}(\cdot)\hat{\pi}(dxdy)
            \end{equation}
        is the solution to (equation Definition:DynamicSBP of the paper), which means that $\hat{\mathbf{P}}_{0T}=\hat{\pi}$ and $\hat{\mathbf{P}}$ shares its bridges with $\mathbf{R}$ in the sense that
            \[
                \hat{\mathbf{P}}^{xy}=\mathbf{R}^{xy} \text{ for $\hat{\pi}$-a.e. $(x,y)$}.
            \]
        \item Conversely, if $\hat{\mathbf{P}}$ is the solution to (equation Definition:DynamicSBP of the paper), then $\hat{\pi}:=\hat{\mathbf{P}}_{0T}$ is the solution to (equation Definition:StaticSBP of the paper).

    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.013679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_23b734aa0ac4066c9dd270f16a7dfc693f26a4bcc10552049f591bb5ac58cfa3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Y-wellposed of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Under Assumptions~(ass:LR of the paper),~(ass:diff1 of the paper), and~(ass:SC of the paper), for each $r \in [0,T]$ and $v_0 \in H$, the initial value problem
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \mathrm{d}Y_v(s) + \mathcal{A}'_u(s, X(s))\,Y_v(s)\,\mathrm{d}s &= \mathcal{B}'_u(s, X(s))(Y_v(s))\,\mathrm{d}W(s), \quad s \in (r, T],\\
  Y_v(r) &= v_0 \in H,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
admits a unique $H$-valued continuous variational solution, tested against $V$, in the regularity class determined by the mechanism of~(SC1):
\[
  Y_v(\cdot) \;\in\;
  \begin{cases}
    C([r,T]; H) \ \text{ with } \ \mathcal{E}_r^T(Y_v) < \infty, & \text{under (SC1)(a)},\\[4pt]
    C([r,T]; H) \cap L^2(r,T; V), & \text{under (SC1)(b)},
  \end{cases}
  \quad \text{$\mathbb{P}$-a.s.},
\]
Under~(a) the controlling quantity is the weighted energy intrinsic to the degenerate linearisation, which for the $p$-Laplacian is $\int_r^T\!\!\int \lvert \nabla X \rvert^{p-2}\lvert \nabla Y_v \rvert^2$; under~(b) it is the unweighted quadratic $V$-energy furnished by $\alpha_1$.
The map $v_0 \mapsto Y_v(t)$ is linear; we view it as the linear first variation operator $Y(t,r): H \to L^0(\Omega; H)$ defined by $Y(t,r) v_0 := Y_v(t)$, the variational-SPDE analogue of the Jacobian of the stochastic flow [ELWORTHY1994252, kunita1990stochastic] in the variational framework of Liu--R\"ockner and R\"ockner--Shang--Zhang [liu2015stochastic, rockner2022wellposedness]. Under the additional structural exponential-integrability~(SC2), $Y_v \in L^2(\Omega; C([r,T]; H))$ with the mechanism-matched global energy bound ($\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{E}_r^T(Y_v)] \leq C\lVert v_0 \rVert_H^2$ under~(SC1)(a), $Y_v \in L^2(\Omega; L^2(r,T;V))$ under~(SC1)(b)), and $Y(t,r): H \to L^2(\Omega; H)$ is continuous, with the individual-fibre moment bound $\mathbb{E}[\sup_{r \leq s \leq t}\lVert Y(s,r)v_0 \rVert_H^q] \leq C_q\lVert v_0 \rVert_H^q$ for every $v_0 \in H$ and every $q \in [1, q_{\max}]$ (Proposition~(prop:Y-moments of the paper), $C_q$ independent of $v_0$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

