{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.613006+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_240375ef4f6cf6cfd1f1c9b719df0498ffc9bdcf22b385caece10874313cbbe8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition EHI:d:sbmDecomposition of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t \geq 0} = (B_{S_t})_{t \geq 0}$ be a subordinated Brownian motion, and let $\mu$ be the L\'{e}vy measure of its subordinator $S$. For all $s>0$, define the \emph{SBM decomposition} of $X$ at $s$ as follows: Let $\mu^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ and $\hat{\mu}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ be the measures given by
    \begin{equation*}
        \mu^{\left\{ s \right\}}(A) := \mu(A \cap (0, s]), \quad \hat{\mu}^{\left\{ s \right\}}(A) := \mu(A \cap (s, \infty)) \qquad\mbox{for all Lebesgue-measurable $A \subseteq (0, \infty)$}.
    \end{equation*}
    Let $S=S^{\left\{ s \right\}}+\hat{S}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ be a Meyer decomposition of $S$ such that $S^{\left\{ s \right\}}_0 = \hat{S}^{\left\{ s \right\}}_0 = 0$, $S^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ has L\'{e}vy measure $\mu^{\left\{ s \right\}}$, $\hat{S}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ has L\'{e}vy measure $\hat{\mu}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$, and $S^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ and $\hat{S}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ are independent. Let $X^{\left\{ s \right\}} = (X^{\left\{ s \right\}}_t)_{t \geq 0} = B(S^{\left\{ s \right\}}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ and $\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}} = (\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}}_t)_{t \geq 0} = \left(B(\hat{S}^{\left\{ s \right\}}_t) - B_0 \right)_{t \geq 0}$.
    Then $X=X^{\left\{ s \right\}}+\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ is a Meyer decompposition of $X$, $X^{\left\{ s \right\}}_0=X_0$, $\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}}_0=0$, and $X^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ and $\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ are independent.

    Let $j^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ and $\hat{j}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ denote the jump kernels of $X^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ and $\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ respectively. Let
    \begin{equation*}
        \lambda\left\{ s \right\} := \hat{\mu}^{\left\{ s \right\}}((s, \infty)).
    \end{equation*}
    By the L\'{e}vy-Khintchine condition, $\lambda\left\{ s \right\} < \infty$. Let $T^{\left\{ s \right\}} := \inf\{ t\geq 0 : \hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}} \neq 0 \}$ be the first time that the process $\hat{X}^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ takes a jump, and note that $T^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ is Exponential($\lambda\left\{ s \right\}$) and independent of the process $X^{\left\{ s \right\}}$.

    Denote exit times for the process $X^{\left\{ s \right\}}$ by $\tau^{\left\{ s \right\}}_U := \inf\{ t \geq 0 : X^{\left\{ s \right\}}_t \notin U \}$.

    \begin{definition}[Meyer-decomposition arising exponential stopping time]
        If $X=X'+\hat{X}'$ is a Meyer decomposition of $X$, where the processes $X'$ and $\hat{X}'$ have jump kernels $j'$ and $\hat{j}'$ respectively, with $\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \hat{j}'(|x|) \; \mathrm{d} x < \infty$, and $T'$ is defined as the first time that the process $\hat{X}'$ takes a jump, then we call $T'$ a \emph{Meyer-decomposition arising exponential stopping time}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.162756+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2406db9559a1768a1a6028fcac4a3524fa107c5c6ae71a0320dd72bc3841fff7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2606.29512, '2606.29512'] Let $u$ be an $\R^d$-valued admissible function and let
