		Then there exists $C>0$ depending only on $(\alpha,\gamma,\delta)$, $c_X$ in Definition (def: alpha-mixing of the paper) and $(c_1,c_2,N_0)$ in Definition (Def: admissible of the paper)  such that for any $\phi\in C_{lip}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ with Lipschitz constant $l_{\phi}$,
		\begin{equation}
			\Big|\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\phi(S_n)]-\max_{x\in \Gamma}\phi(x) \Big|\leq C\bigl(1+\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X_0|^{2}]\bigr)l_{\phi}\bigl(n^{-1/2}+n^{-(1-\gamma)}\bigr)\text{ for any }n\in\mathbb N.
		\end{equation}
		In particular, if $\gamma\leq 1/2$, we have the convergence rate $n^{-1/2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.608810+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_24849bfdcc27596a4556cc878baab1194fbc6cf1b8eb00d8a79cb63156c778df","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:uit_num of arXiv:2412.05239, 'Conditions for uniform in time convergence: applications to averaging, numerical discretis'] Suppose (ass of the paper) holds. Let $\nu$ be a probability measure on $\mathbb{R}^d$. Then, there exists $\tilde{\mathbf{C}}<\infty$ such that for all $l\in \mathbb{N}$ and $\delta>0$ with $\delta^{-1}\in \mathbb{N}$,
