{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.013892+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_24f7ca70d94bd889245559878391f3efa678df713a9e75e4ad0009d5236dc7fe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Y-moments of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Suppose the assumptions of Theorem~(thm:Y-wellposed of the paper) are in force (in particular,~(SC2) of Assumption~(ass:SC of the paper), at the operating constant $c_*$ of~(SC2), cf.~(equation eq:SC-exp-moment of the paper)), where $\tilde{\rho} := \rho + \rho^*$ is the linearised local-monotonicity coefficient of Remark~(rem:no-D3-old of the paper) and $\hat\rho(\tau,u) := C(1 + \lVert u \rVert_V^{2\beta})$ is the polynomial-growth bound from (D2). Then for every $q \in [2, q_{\max}]$, with $q_{\max}$ as in~(equation eq:qmax-def of the paper), there exists $C_q > 0$ such that for every $v \in H$,
