{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.993450+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_267de2c8a256f96ef43d0adcf9eebc8dbc7cd53312e0b9a080e2f2158169be90","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2605.11801, 'A non-local singular non-linear Fokker-Planck PDE'] The space $C_T\shc^{\gamma}\cap S_{L^1}$ is closed in $C_T\shc^{\gamma} $ for $\gamma\in(0,1)$.
    \begin{proof}
      Let $(u_n)\subseteq C_T\shc^{\gamma}\cap S_{L^1}$
be a sequence converging to $u \in C_T\shc^{\gamma}$.
It is obviously non-negative being a pointwise limit of non-negative functions.
      We are left to prove that for all $t\in [0,T]$
        \begin{equation*}
            \|u(t)\|_{L^1}=\int_{\R^d} u(t,x) dx \leq 1.
        \end{equation*}
Using Fatou's lemma we have for all $t\in[0,T]$
\begin{equation*}
    \|u(t)\|_{L^1}=\int_{\R^d}\liminf_{n\to\infty} u_n(t,x) dx\leq \liminf_{n\to\infty}\int_{\R^d} u_n(t,x) dx=1,
\end{equation*}
which concludes the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

