{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.248401+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_26b93878277df1dd22b012c98dc31f2f3117ff72e8307ff3f2a76547d41dcc46","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:matrix-exponent of arXiv:2512.21159, '\textsc{From multitype branching Brownian motions\\ to branching Markov additive processes'] For $\theta \in [0, \bar{\theta})$ and $i,j\in \mathcal{I}$, define 
	\begin{equation}
	M_{i,j}^{(\theta)}(t) := \mathbf{E}_{0,i}\bigg[\sum_{u\in \mathcal{N}_t} e^{-\theta X_u(t)} \mathbbm{1}_{\left\{ J_u(t) = j \right\} }  \bigg], \qquad t \ge 0.
\end{equation}
	Then for the matrix $M^{(\theta)}(t) := (M_{i,j}^{(\theta)}(t))_{i,j\in \mathcal{I}}$, we have 
	\begin{equation}
		M^{(\theta)}(t) = e^{t \mathcal{M}(\theta)}, \qquad t\ge 0,  
	\end{equation}
where the matrix exponent $\mathcal{M}(\theta)$ is given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{M}(\theta) = \mathrm{diag} (\phi_1(\theta), \ldots, \phi_{\mathtt{d}}(\theta)) + Q \circ G(\theta) 
	+ \mathrm{diag} (\beta_1 m_1-\beta_1, \ldots, \beta_{\mathtt{d}} m_{\mathtt{d}}-\beta_{\mathtt{d}}), 
\end{equation} 
where $Q \circ G(\theta)$ denotes the entry-wise multiplication of $Q$ and $G(\theta)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.099068+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_26bf661bd9cff31647792a10adcec5a0abf656c15ceb93ef4da7cdd5c1f16909","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Let $(\mathbb{G}, \mathbf o, \mathbb M )$ be a unimodular random matched rooted graph, we define the performance of $(\mathbb{G},\mathbf o,\mathbb{M})$ as:
