{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.226597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_26c1361a80229b834a858cbdd69211818eb45d648a486aba2ff246eee725a3cf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma integration-type-gronwall of arXiv:2609.00627, 'Global well-posedness of radially symmetric strong solutions to two-dimensional compressib'] Let $I=[a, b]$ and $\alpha(t), \beta(t), u(t)$ be a real-valued continuous function defined on $I$. 
    Moreover, $\alpha(t)$ is  a nondecreasing function and $\beta(t)$ is a nonnegative function. 
    if 
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            v(t) \le \alpha(t)+\int_{a}^{t} \beta(s) v(s) ds, \enspace t \in I, 
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    then, 
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            v(t) \le 2\alpha(t)e^{\int_{a}^{t} \beta(s) ds}. 
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    \begin{proof}
        Define a function 
        \[
        g(s)=e^{-\int_{a}^{s} \beta(r) dr}\int_{a}^{s}\beta(r)v(r)dr, s \in I. 
        \]
        Differentiating $g(s)$ gives
        \[
        g'(s) = (v(s)-\int_{a}^{s} \beta(r)v(r) dr)\beta(s)e^{-\int_{a}^{s} \beta(r) dr}, 
        \] 
        so we have that
        \[
        g'(s) \le  \alpha(s) \beta(s)e^{-\int_{a}^{s} \beta(r) dr}, 
        \]
        Due to $g(a)=0$, integrating above inequality on $[a, t]$, we have that
        \[
        g(t) \le \int_{a}^{t} \alpha(s) \beta(s)e^{-\int_{a}^{s} \beta(r) dr} ds. 
        \]
        Using the defination of $g(t)$, we have that
        \[
        \int_{a}^{t} \beta(s)v(s) ds = e^{\int_{a}^{t} \beta(r)dr } g(t) \le  \int_{a}^{t} \alpha(s) \beta(s)e^{\int_{s}^{t} \beta(r) dr} ds. 
        \]
        Because $\alpha(t)$ is a nondecreasing function, so we have 
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{aligned}
                v(t) &\le \alpha(t) + \int_{a}^{t} \alpha(s) \beta(s)e^{\int_{s}^{t} \beta(r) dr} ds\\
                & \le \alpha(t)+ \alpha(t) \int_{a}^{t} \beta(s)e^{\int_{s}^{t} \beta(r) dr} ds \le \alpha(t)+ \alpha(t) [ -e^{\int_{s}^{t} \beta(r)dr } ]_{s=a}^{s=t}\\
                & \le \alpha(t)+ \alpha(t) e^{\int_{a}^{t} \beta(r)dr } \le 2\alpha(t) e^{\int_{a}^{t} \beta(r)dr }. 
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation}
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.745245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_26c1868d53bf91557149cdf04c1577177672da5b20a543a44dbb8d667abdd8bc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2506.10511, '2506.10511'] For all $d\geq 1$, $\beta>0$ and $L>0$, there exist constants $c_1, c_2>0$ {\rm(}depending on $d, \beta$ and $L${\rm)} such that for each $k\in\mathds{N}$ and $\varepsilon>0$,
