{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.489374+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28344759d4c6df4473e0a014a92c30fd209e22bc20b173a44ebbba71a65f55b3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 209 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] $\mathcal S-\mathcal S \circ \mathcal P^H$ is a compact integral operator in $\mathscr{L}^2(\gamma^+)$. The operator $\textnormal{Id} + \mathcal S-\mathcal S \circ \mathcal P^H$ is invertible and its inverse can be represented as $\textnormal{Id}- \mathcal R$, where $\mathcal R$ is an integral operator whose kernel is smooth and smoothly depends on the parameters $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$ and $\boldsymbol{\alpha},\boldsymbol{\beta}$, with each partial derivative uniformly bounded in terms of $H$ and constant $C$ from Theorem~\ref{Theorem_Master_equation_12}. Finally, for each $f \in \mathscr{H}^{[2],0}$, which restricts onto $\gamma^+$ as $\hat f$, we have an identification with Definition~\ref{defofop}:
\begin{equation}

(\textnormal{Id} + \mathcal S- \mathcal S \circ \mathcal P^H)[\hat f](z) = f(z) - \frac{\mathcal{P}[f](z)}{\sigma(z)} +
\sum_{h = 1}^H \mathcal{K}_{h}\circ \mathcal{U}_{h}[f](z), \qquad z\in \gamma^+.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.889338+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2834603643b452d9e8bced50558a21949fa63283e0c1e3a7f55062109d3850d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 52 (Crossing law for a curved boundary~\cite{Nikeghbali2006essay}) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] Let $X=N+A$ be of class $(\Sigma)$ with only negative jumps and $\lim_{t\to\infty}A_t=\infty$ a.s., and let $\varphi\colon\R_+\to(0,\infty]$ be Borel, with the convention $1/\infty:=0$.  Then
\begin{equation}
  \PP\bigl(\exists\,t\ge0:\ X_t>\varphi(A_t)\bigr)
  =1-\exp\Bigl(-\int_0^\infty \varphi(x)^{-1}\dd x\Bigr)
\end{equation}
and for every $u>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \PP\bigl(\exists\,t\ge0:\ A_t<u,\ X_t>\varphi(A_t)\bigr)
  =1-\exp\Bigl(-\int_0^u \varphi(x)^{-1}\dd x\Bigr)
\end{equation}
each right-hand side being read as $1$ when its integral diverges. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.819711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_283564f0f90cb90f569981e81506d832b3c9eeed11cc12e754d4c7cd1a9f6bd9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary mixing-time-bound of arXiv:2504.02207, 'Finite-Time behaviors of M/M/$n$ Queue: Mixing Time, Mean Queue Length and Tail Bounds'] (Mixing time bounds for $M/M/n$ systems) Let $\pi_{n,t}$ be the queue length distribution at time $t$ of the continuous-time $M/M/n$ system defined above and let the stationary distribution be $\nu_n$. For $\alpha \geq 1$, we have that:
    \begin{align}


        \tau_{mix}(\varepsilon) \leq 4 n^{2\alpha-1} \log\br{\frac{\chi(\pi_{n,0}, \nu_n)}{\varepsilon}}. 

    \end{align}
    For $\alpha \in (1/2,1)$, we have that:
    \begin{align}


        \tau_{mix}(\varepsilon) \leq 4 C_n^{-1} n^{2\alpha-1} \log\br{\frac{\chi(\pi_{n,0}, \nu_n)}{\varepsilon}}

    \end{align}    
    for some $C_n > 0$ such that $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} C_n = 1$.\\
    For $\alpha \in (0,1/2)$, we have that:
    \begin{align}


        \tau_{mix}(\varepsilon) \leq D_n^{-1} \log\br{\frac{\chi(\pi_{n,0}, \nu_n)}{\varepsilon}}

    \end{align}
    for some $D_n > 0$ such that $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} D_n = 1/25$.\\
    Finally, for $\alpha = 1/2$ and $n \geq 110$, we have 
    \begin{align}


        \tau_{mix}(\varepsilon) \leq H_n^{-1} \log\br{\frac{\chi(\pi_{n,0}, \nu_n)}{\varepsilon}} 

    \end{align}
    for some $H_n > \frac{1}{10861}$ and $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} H_n = \frac{1}{1781}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.662318+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_283738e8e0e42dc4e0a4cb03f3ac73feae49f8492066a2a159e962edd60101ce","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem The1 of arXiv:2604.06012, 'Large fringe trees for random trees with given vertex degrees'] Assume Condition~(Condition1 of the paper). 
For $\kappa \geq 1$, let $T_{\kappa} \in \mathbb{T}$ be such that
as \ensuremath{{\kappa\to\infty}},
with $m_{\kappa} := |T_{\kappa}|$,
\begin{align}

&m_\kappa^2\pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa})}(T_{\kappa})\sum_{i\in\mathcal D(T_\kappa)}
\frac{p_i({\mathbf{n}}_{T_\kappa})^2}{p_i({\mathbf{n}}_\kappa)}
=o(1).
\end{align}
Then:
\begin{romenumerate}[-20pt]  
\par
 \refstepcounter{thmenumerate}\textup{(\roman{thmenumerate})\enspace}  
If\/ $|{\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa}| \pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa})}(T_{\kappa}) \rightarrow \lambda$, 
for some $\lambda \in (0,\infty)$, then 
\begin{align} 
N_{T_\kappa}({\mathcal T}_{{\mathbf{n}_\kappa}}) \overset{\mathrm{d}}{\longrightarrow}\operatorname{Po}\bigl(\lambda\bigr)
\end{align} 
with convergence of all moments.
\par
 \refstepcounter{thmenumerate}\textup{(\roman{thmenumerate})\enspace}  
If\/ $|{\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa}| \pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa})}(T_{\kappa}) \rightarrow \infty$, then 
\begin{align}
\frac{N_{T_\kappa}({\mathcal T}_{{\mathbf{n}_\kappa}})- |{\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa}| \pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa})}(T_{\kappa})}{\sqrt{|{\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa}| \pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa})}(T_{\kappa})}} \overset{\mathrm{d}}{\longrightarrow}\mathrm{N}\bigl(0,1\bigr),
\end{align}
\end{romenumerate}  
with convergence of mean and variance.

Thus, in both cases,
\begin{align}
\operatorname{\mathbb E{}}\bigsqpar{N_{T_\kappa}({\mathcal T}_{{\mathbf{n}_\kappa}})}
\sim
\operatorname{Var}\bigsqpar{N_{T_\kappa}({\mathcal T}_{{\mathbf{n}_\kappa}})}
\sim
|{\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa}| \pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}}_{\kappa})}(T_{\kappa}).   
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.776511+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28383eaa54cc38d16d3f612d4a7c7441a0c9413a13bb67ed241f29e72bcb9709","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2608.17493, 'Integrals of some compound processes'] Under Condition II, let $M\sim CP(\lambda, X)$ and $\alpha\ge0$. For $t>0$, set $\Lambda_t$ a r.v. such that
