{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.026547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28ce6eadc95a099ee559bf32c0ebe20cca7f8147fcf572891574881b7260091d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2608.09465, 'Balancing fractional Brownian motion'] Let 
\begin{align*}
c(H) = \begin{cases}
1/2 & H<1/2\\
1/2+H & H\geq 1/2
\end{cases}.
\end{align*}
\begin{enumerate}
\item [(1)] For all large $n$, with probability $1-o(1)$, 
\begin{align*}
n^{1/2-H}\lesssim\disc(f_1,\ldots,f_n)\lesssim n^{1/2-H}(\log n)^{c(H)},
\end{align*}
where the implicit constants depend only on $H$. When $H = 1/2$, for every fixed $\varepsilon\in(0,a_0)$, the implicit constant in the lower bound can be taken as $a_0-\varepsilon$, i.e., $\disc(f_1,\ldots,f_n)>a_0-\varepsilon$. 
\item [(2)] For every $H\in(0,1)$, there exists a randomized polynomial-time algorithm that returns a signing $\sigma\in\{\pm1\}^n$ satisfying, 
\begin{align*}
L(\sigma)\lesssim
\begin{cases}
n^{1/2-H}\sqrt{\log n} &0<H<1/2,\\
(\log n)^{3/2} &H=1/2,\\
\sqrt{\log n} &1/2<H<1,
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
with probability $1-o(1)$ over the sample paths (and the internal randomness of the algorithm), 
where the implicit constants depend only on $H$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.899610+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28cf15549bf7d47f1729de69e13cf446015921c425e0442ec41aac96812456df","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm.uni of arXiv:2512.19897, 'On the convoy of the ASEP speed process'] The inhomogeneous random walk $Q_n$ converges in distribution to a q-independent limit after scaling. That is, for any $x\in\left(0,1\right)$, $c = x\left(1-x\right) \in \left(0, \frac{1}{4}\right)$, we have
