{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.789117+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28dbd8fcb679295098b66b0eb6ad1edd4237628a68d9ecbabdfe0e3957ee1dd5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:compareasympv of arXiv:2411.08295, 'Improving the convergence of Markov chains via permutations and projections'] Let $P \in \mathcal{L}(\pi)$ be ergodic and $Q \in \mathcal{I}(\pi) \cap \mathcal{L}$ be an isometric involution transition matrix. For $\alpha \in [0,1]$, recall that $\overline{P}_{\alpha}(Q)$ is introduced in (equation eq:PbaralphaQ of the paper). For any $f \in \ell^2_0(\pi)$, we have
	\begin{align*}
		v(f,\overline{P}_\alpha(Q)) \leq \alpha v(f,P) + (1-\alpha) v(Qf,P).
	\end{align*}
	In particular, if $f$ satisfies $Qf = \pm f$, it leads to
	\begin{align*}
		v(f,\overline{P}_\alpha(Q)) \leq v(f,P),
	\end{align*}
	and hence
	\begin{align*}
		\min_{\alpha \in [0,1]} v(f,\overline{P}_\alpha(Q)) &= v(f,\overline{P}(Q)).
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.327060+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28dd3d0527c93140565c579b02a9d5682c13ef29fa61aef295d09f4ef00590dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma gl-sp-(co)ind-homotopical of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\alpha\colon G\to G'$ be an \emph{injective} homomorphism of compact Lie groups. Then $\alpha_!\colon\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}_\textup{$G$-global}\to\cat{$\bm{G'}$-OrthSp}_\textup{$G'$-global}$ is homotopical.
    \begin{proof}
        Let $f\colon X\to Y$ be a $G$-global weak equivalence, and let $H$ be any compact Lie group. Then ${\textup{triv}}_H \alpha_!f$ agrees up to conjugating by isomorphisms with $(\alpha\times H)_!{\textup{triv}}_Hf$, and the latter is a $(G'\times H)$-equivariant weak equivalence by Proposition~(prop:i!-left-Quillen-equiv-sp of the paper). Letting $H$ vary, this implies via Remark~(rk:projections-suffice of the paper) that $\alpha_!f$ is a $G'$-global weak equivalence.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.109518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e27f0ca52178352357f6af132a924e2edcb981d86a8715c47b88bb79eed78b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defstable of arXiv:2411.15881, 'Stable Approximation for Call Function Via Stein's method'] Let $\alpha\in(1,2)$, $\sigma\geq 0$ and $\delta\in [-1,1]$ be real numbers. We say that $Y$ is distributed according to the \emph{$\alpha$-stable law with parameters $\sigma$ and $\delta$}, and we write $Y\sim S_\alpha(\sigma,\delta)$, to indicate that, for all $\lambda\in(-\infty,\infty)$,
\[
\mathbb{E}[e^{i\lambda Y}]=
\begin{array}{lll}
{\rm exp}\big\{-\sigma^\alpha|\lambda|^\alpha(1-i\,\delta\,{\rm sign}(\lambda)\,\tan\frac{\pi\alpha}2)\big\}
\end{array}
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.965047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e3308ba12dce218eb730644104d09e8b2525cfc726dae2d98d0c03055bfba2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm GlobalEntropyDecay of arXiv:2412.03560, 'Non-asymptotic entropic bounds for non-linear kinetic Langevin sampler with second-order s'] Under Assumptions~(assu:regularity of the paper), (assu:moments of the paper) and (assu:LSI of the paper), assume furthermore that $h\sqrt{M_{1,x}+M_{1,m}}\leqslant 1/10$  and set
    \begin{equation}
     a = \frac{\gamma}{7+3(\gamma+3)^2}\,,\qquad \kappa = \frac{a}{3  \max(1,\rho^{-1})  + 6a }\,,\qquad C_2 =  \frac1\kappa \left( 9 + \frac1a\right)  C_1\,,
    \end{equation}
    with $C_1$ given by~(equation eq:C1 of the paper).     Then, for all $N,n\in\mathbb N$,
    \[\mathcal H(\nu_n^N | \nu_\infty^N) \leqslant \left( 1+ \kappa h\right)^{-n} \left[ \mathcal H(\nu_0^N | \nu_\infty^N) + 2 a \mathcal I(\nu_0^N | \nu_\infty^N) \right]  +  \rho^{-1} \delta_ N  + C_2 N d^3 h^4 \,. \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.302593+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e3a959e61cffe7f1a813dfb4fb8e8c9a1f9f2ca333ea3cd2f968bb3b9130e6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:general_restrict_max of arXiv:2605.26048, 'Classification of the eternal solutions and multiple coalescing shocks in the KPZ fixed po'] The following holds on a single event of probability one. Let $s \in \mathbb{R}$, and let $f:\mathbb{R} \to [-\infty,\infty)$ be any function which satisfies $f(y^\star) > -\infty$ for some $y^\star \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\sup_{y \in \mathbb{R}}[f(y) - ay^2] < \infty$ for all $a > 0$. Then, for any compact subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{>s}$, there exists $A = A(s,K) > 0$ so that for all $(x,t) \in K$,
    \begin{equation} 
    \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f,s) = \sup_{y \in [-A,A]}[f(y) + \mathcal{L}(y,s;x,t)] > \sup_{|y| > A}[f(y) + \mathcal{L}(y,s;x,t)].
    \end{equation}
    Furthermore, the function 
    $
    (x,t) \mapsto \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f,s)$
    is continuous on $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{>s}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.532388+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e3ee29115f7ce539ecbb2e08cce1d0033712949cef6f1bf61c03bed05dee8a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 (no universal behaviour at the critical dimension) of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] Assume that \(\alpha > 2 \text{ and } \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1} \in \N\). Also assume that $\theta$, defined here on $\Z^{\frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}}$, has a finite moment of order $\delta > \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}$. Consider \(\Tree_{n}\)-walk in \(\Z^{\frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}}\) with $\theta$-jumps.

\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
  \item 
If \Cref{Assum:SufficientConditionTransience} holds, then this \(\Tree_{n}\)-walk is transient:
\begin{equation*}{
\frac{R^{\theta}(\Tree_{n})}{n} \xrightarrow[n \to +\infty]{(\P)} \P(\esc_{+}) > 0.
}\end{equation*}
  \item If \(L(n) \to +\infty\) then this \(\Tree_{n}\)-walk is
recurrent:
\begin{equation*}{
\frac{R^{\theta}(\Tree_{n})}{n} \xrightarrow[n \to +\infty]{(\P)}  0.
}\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.586640+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e55c3e124da45226c982375a05b1c910f6278b19284f6c738ab2b837c6c437","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:generalized_tree_graph of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] Consider percolation on any transitive weighted graph. For each integer $p\geq 2$ the inequality
