where the $o(1)$ goes to zero as $A\to\infty$, at a rate which is uniform in $n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.124233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28f59ddc38174a3d625d12029cb198c0bcdad6ff359f0075e4deaefef25ff3ae","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lemmadistance of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] If $\theta\in C^1([0,T])$, then for any $\epsilon\in(0,\frac{\pi}{2})$, there exists a $\lambda_0>0$ such that $\forall\lambda>\lambda_0$, it holds that
$$
\sup_{s\in[0,t]}|\alpha_s-\beta_s|\leq c_{\theta,t}\frac{1-e^{-2\lambda\sin(\epsilon t)}}{2\lambda\sin{\epsilon}},\quad t\in[0,T],
$$
where $c_{\theta,t}:=\sup_{s\in[0,t]}|\dot{\theta}_s|$ and $\alpha_s$, $\beta_s$ are obtained by the following equations
\begin{equation}\left\{
\begin{array}{lllll}
\dot{\alpha}_s=-2\lambda\sin(\alpha_s)-\dot{\theta}_s\\
\dot{\beta}_s=-2\lambda\sin(\beta_s)\\
\alpha_0=\beta_0\in[-\frac{\pi}{2}+\epsilon,\frac{\pi}{2}-\epsilon].
\end{array}
\right.\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.473705+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28f725fd47daf0e4f9e0f98329a413a2fea400efda71c81e3f869be33bf924d6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:upper of arXiv:2606.21006, 'Law of the Iterated Logarithm for Weighted Sums of Functionals of Long-Memory Gaussian Seq'] There exists \(C_{\mathrm{up}}<\infty\) such that
