{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.058023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_292e54c9303b902c08bc52f3caea826350da6b114557f36144c2df681072345c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:brownian-fixed-gaussian-block of arXiv:2608.16119, 'Fully nonlinear parabolic equations under a fixed reference diffusion: weighted \texorpdfs'] For the fixed horizon $T$ and parameters $n\geq2$ and $\alpha\geq0$, set
$
 \mathcal H_{n,\alpha}
 :=L^2((0,T),t^\alpha\,\mathrm d t;\mathcal C_n)
$
and define the operator initially on $C_c^\infty((0,T);\mathcal C_n)$ by
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}\widehat F
 :=t^{-1}D_\xi^2\operatorname{Sol}_n\widehat F.

\end{equation}
Then $\mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}$ extends uniquely to a bounded operator from
$\mathcal H_{n,\alpha}$ into
$L^2((0,T),t^\alpha\,\mathrm d t;L^2(\gamma_d;\mathsf{S}^d))$.
Under the Brownian pullback, this is the $n$th chaos block of the physical
source-to-Hessian map.  In logarithmic time it is represented by the causal
Volterra operator
\begin{equation*}
 (\operatorname{Volt}_{n,\alpha}\widehat F)(\ell)
 =\sqrt{n(n-1)}\int_0^\ell
   e^{-(n-1+\alpha)(\ell-\ell_0)/2}
   \widehat F(\ell_0)\,\mathrm d\ell_0.
\end{equation*}
Its exact operator norm is
\begin{equation}
 \norm{\mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}}
 =\norm{\operatorname{Volt}_{n,\alpha}}_{2\to2}
 =\frac{2\sqrt{n(n-1)}}{n-1+\alpha}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.540106+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_292f148a27c2adf4f81962903219b6bae49d52e085732b0ce523f618c96b0108","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: Upper bound coarse grain of arXiv:2411.10271, '$A$-localized states for clock models on trees and their extremal decomposition into glass'] For each finite subtree $\gamma \subset V$ with a given vertex $x\in \gamma$, the expression (equation eq: Markov chain prob of the paper) is upper bounded by 
    \begin{equation}
    \sum_{\tau_{\Bar{\gamma}}\in \{0,1\}^{\Bar{\gamma}}} \pi_A(\tau_x) \prod_{(u,v)\in \Vec{E}_x(\gamma)}M(\tau_u,\tau_v)
\end{equation}
where the single-site marginals $\pi_A$ of $\mu_A$ are given in (equation def: single-site marg, diagonal elements of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.727788+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_292f6c578fed1e9ab020245edd063bd2bbb106dc3dbf39fe65fd71c451d07d0c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Markov-construction of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] For each $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, let $\mu^z = (\mu^z_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a solution to the FPE (equation eq:FPE of the paper) with initial datum $\mu^z_0 = \delta_z$, satisfying (equation eq:L1-int-SP of the paper), such that
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), font=\normalfont]
        \item $(t,z) \mapsto \mu^z_t$ is injective from $\mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}^d$ to  $\mathcal P$;

        \item for each $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $s \in \mathbb{R}_+ \setminus \{0\}$, $\mu^z_{s+}$ is an extreme point in the set of solutions to ($\mu^z_{s+}$-linFPE) (in the sense of (def:lFPE-sol of the paper)) with initial condition $\mu^z_s$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, for each $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$, there exists a unique solution path law $P^z$ to the MV-SDE (equation eq:DDSDE of the paper) with one-dimensional time marginals $P^z_t = \mu^z_t$, $t \geq 0$. $\{P^z\}_{z\in \mathbb{R}^d}$ is a nonlinear Markov core for $\{\mu^z\}_{z\in \mathbb{R}^d}$, which is uniquely determined by $\{\mu^z\}_{z \in \mathbb{R}^d}$ and (equation eq:DDSDE of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:30:07.995460+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_292f7d81b31ea22734c49ccf3d7f3c218a76f9152c5e59997a8b9214183b8e93","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Full-Diamond Backtracking Intersection of arXiv:2608.12415, 'Sharp Phase Transition for Ellipsoid Fitting'] Let \(A\) and \(B\) be two \(M_\alpha\)-shapes. We say that the composition
\(A\cdot  B\) forms a \emph{full-diamond backtracking intersection} if
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \(U_A  \equiv V_B\), where \(U_A\) is the left boundary square
    vertex of \(A\) and \(V_B\) is the right boundary square vertex of \(B\);

    \item the two circle vertices of \(A\) receive the same set of labels in $[d]$ as those in $B$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.259482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293324526e4ab5d258c0c7abd24f24a8c4569b7a5dc9270f42441697a76d621d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm 15 of arXiv:2609.01105, 'Obstruction to quasi-invariance of Gaussian measures under transport flows on Riemannian M'] Let \(q_0  \in S^0\) be defined for all~\(y\in V_2\) and~\(\eta \in \Rr^d\) by 
\begin{equation}

    q_0(y,\eta) = \omega(\eta)\theta_0^2(\kappa^{-1}(y))\left( \frac{|\eta|^{2\alpha}_{y}}{|\Jac \chi(\chi^{-1}(y))^\top\eta|_{\chi^{-1}(y)}^{2\alpha}}-1\right), 
\end{equation}
where $|\eta|^2_{z}:=g^{ij}(\kappa^{-1}(z))\eta_i\eta_j$, and with $\omega$ which is a radial function such that $\omega = 0$ near $\eta =0$ and $\omega (\eta) = 1$ for $|\eta| \geq C$ for some constant $C>0$. 
There exists~\(\delta > 0\) and~\(r \in S^{-\delta}\) such that
\[
    P_{0,1}^{-\mez} (S_\kappa P_{2,2} S_\kappa^{-1}  - P_{3,3})P^{-\mez}_{1,0} = \Op(q_0) + \Op(r).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.058602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293503513e06c6cd7352ccc3e1de7c5e71a11db3695037dd157ac1345e7678e9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 44 (Balanced reduction) of arXiv:2606.16636, 'Free energy of non-convex multi-species spin glasses with centered Ising spins'] Assume that $\xi$ is balanced with respect to $\lambda$ in the sense of Definition~\ref{d.balanced_comparison_structure}. Let $f$ be the limit free energy, or equivalently the Hopf formula in Theorem~\ref{t.main}. Then, for every $t\geq0$ and every $q\in\mcl Q_2^\sS$,
\begin{equation}

    u(t,q^\lambda)\leq f(t,q)\leq \sum_{s\in\sS}\lambda_s u(t,q_s).
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

    f(t,0)=u(t,0),\qquad t\geq0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:57:07.198358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2936a77d9698a119db2a94e28afbe5705299c13ed91b3677261d0eeaeb445556","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:growth-gadget of arXiv:2509.04333, 'The limiting law of the Discrete Gaussian level lines'] The following holds uniformly over all possible $Q$ as above. Let $\mathfrak{L}_n$ be the (unique) $(H+1-n)$ level line induced by $\xi$. Then, with $\pi^\xi_Q$-probability $1 - e^{-c\log L}$, $\mathfrak{L}_n$ lies below the point $X= (0, Y + \sigma(\log L)^a + \log L)$, where
		\begin{align}
			&Y = -\frac{N_n^{1/3}(\log L)^{2a}}{8(\tau_\beta(\theta) + \tau_\beta''(\theta))\cos(\theta)^3}\,,\\
			&\sigma^2 = \frac{N_n^{2/3}(\log L)^a}{4(\tau_\beta(\theta) + \tau_\beta''(\theta))\cos(\theta)^3}\,.\nonumber
		\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.847903+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2936dc8d2241316317f4949d6eff769731814c2566eb75a3c200ae239cc52cae","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2608.29486, 'Cabled braids and their crossing matrices'] An $n \times n$ matrix $M$ is said to {\it admit a cable-block decomposition} if $M$ is in the form of $m \times m$ blocks ($1<m<n$) as 
\begin{align*}
M=\begin{bmatrix}
A_{11} & A_{12} & \dots & A_{1m} \\
A_{21} & A_{22} & \dots & A_{2m} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
A_{m1} & A_{m2} & \dots & A_{mm} 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
such that the block $A_{ii}$ is a square matrix for each $i \in \{ 1, 2, \dots , m \}$ and all the entries in $A_{i j}$ are equal for each pair $i \neq j \in \{ 1, 2, \dots , m \}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.621656+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293af82c468b354c461344b061e732283c286d58d2457de49cfe10a1ffe268a6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma mg_problem_tilde_S of arXiv:2601.04997, 'Fluctuations of the Boundary-Driven Symmetric Zero-Range Process from the NESS'] Let $\mathscr{Y}$ be an $\mathcal{S}'$-valued stochastic process satisfying (equation eq:martingale_problem of the paper) and item i) of Proposition~(prop:uniqueness_neg of the paper) for each $H\in\mathcal{S}$. Then, for any $H\in\mathcal{\tilde S}$, the process $\{\mathscr{\tilde M}(H)_t, t\in[0, T]\}$ defined via
\begin{equation}
    \mathscr{\tilde M}_t(H):=\mathscr{Y}_t(H)-\mathscr{Y}_0(H)-\int_0^t\mathscr{Y}_s(\mathfrak{A}_\theta H)\de s
\end{equation}
is a martingale with continuous trajectories and with quadratic variation given by
\begin{equation}
    \scal{\mathscr{\tilde M}(H)}_t=t\|\partial_u H\|_{L^{2, \theta}}^2.
\end{equation}
Moreover, the process $\{\mathscr{Y}_t(H), t\in[0, T]\}$ has continuous trajectories. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.500749+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293b9e1e3e816099c71995e10c5e29e4e52a884c95ac0afdf3b4b18aeb2bf5e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:gamma of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Let $X:\mathbb{Z}^2_\ge \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\cup \{\infty\}$ be a field of independent random variables distributed as follows.
Take real parameters $\alpha$ and $\{\gamma_i\}_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}$.

For any $i\in \mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,i)\sim \operatorname{Gamma}^{-1}(\alpha+\gamma_i)$ if $\alpha+\gamma_i>0$, and $X(i,i)=\infty$ otherwise.

For any $i > j \in \mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,j)\sim \operatorname{Gamma}^{-1}(\gamma_i+\gamma_j)$ if $\gamma_i+\gamma_j>0$, and $X(i,j)=\infty$ otherwise. 
We extend the weights $X$ to all of $\mathbb{Z}^2$ by the symmetry condition $X(i,j)=X(j,i)$ for all $i,j$.

For any $u,v\in \mathbb{Z}^2$ with $u \le v$, define the \emph{partition function}
\[
Z_X(u;v)=\sum_{\pi}\prod_{(i,j)\in \pi}X(i,j),
\]
where the sum is over all up-right paths $\pi$ from $u$ to $v$, subject to the following condition:
\begin{equation}

\text{for any $(i,i)\in\pi$, necessarily $(i-1,i)\notin\pi$.}
\end{equation}
In words, paths may cross the diagonal at most once, moving from below the diagonal to above it.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.498968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293c811071723ff8df91d928ce9b6dda18c0899fea722ecc69556210feb7fd45","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2605.29608, '2605.29608'] Let $(Y_k)_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^+}$ be the Wolff dynamics with invariant measure $\mu_N$. 
	For any $\hat{J}\in[0,+\infty)$, the log-Sobolev inequality
	\begin{align} 
		E_{\mu_{N}}[f^{2}\log f^{2}]-E_{\mu_{N}}[f^{2}]\log E_{\mu_{N}}[f^{2}]\leq&e^{2\hat{J}}(e^{4\hat{J}}+1)\big(\frac{1}{2}+\hat{J}e^{\frac{e^{2\hat{J}}-1}{2}}\big)N	\mathcal{E}^{\text{W}}_{\mu_{N}}(f),
	\end{align}
	holds for all functions $f:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$, where
	\begin{align*}	
		\mathcal{E}^{\text{W}}_{\mu_{N}}(f):=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\sigma,\eta\in\Omega}[f(\sigma)-f(\eta)]^{2}P_{\text{W}}(\eta,\sigma)\mu_{N}(\eta).
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.124562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293e3f27e0f9fd9de86d67e338e3b134b51a14844f0814deec2308a4193445eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma def:irreducibility2 of arXiv:2510.00748, '\bf On irreducible central limit theorems'] Let the above assumptions and conventions prevail. Then, the sequence $\{Z_n\}$ is reducible in the sense of Definition (def:irreducibility of the paper) if and only if there exist partitions $\{B_1,\ldots,B_{m_n}\} = \{B^{(n)}_1,\ldots,B^{(n)}_{m_n}\}$ of the vertex sets $V_n$, such that, as $n \to \infty$,
