{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.290804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_294cbd6656e7f4efac47b23ac2bfdc155e3917551c23988ec75db4a89312d279","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem phi of arXiv:2411.05204, '\Large\bf Sample Path Properties of the Fractional Wiener--Weierstrass Bridge'] Let $ Y $ be a fractional Wiener--Weierstrass  bridge with parameters $\alpha$, $b$, and $H$, and $K=\min\{1,({-\log_b\alpha})\}$.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item  If $H<K$, 
\begin{align}
{\mathbb P}\left(\frac{1}{L}<v_\Phi( Y )<\infty\right)=1\quad\text{ for }\quad\Phi(x)=\bigg(\frac{x}{\sqrt{2\log\log(1/x)}}\bigg)^{1/H}.
\end{align}
\item  If $H=K$, 
\begin{align}
{\mathbb P}\left(\frac{1}{L}<v_\Phi( Y )<\infty\right)=1\quad\text{ for }\quad\Phi(x)=\bigg(\frac{x}{\sqrt{2\log(1/x)\log\log(1/x)/H}}\bigg)^{1/H}.
\end{align}
 \item  If $H>K$, 
\[
{\mathbb P}(0<v_\Phi( Y )<\infty)=1\quad\text{ for }\quad\Phi(x)=x^{1/K}.
\]
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, in all three cases, if $\Theta:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ is a function such that  $\Phi(x)=o(\Theta(x))$ as $x\to 0^+$, then $v_{\Theta}( Y )=\infty$ almost surely. Conversely, if $\Theta(x)=o(\Phi(x))$ as $x\to 0^+$, then $v_\Theta(Y)=0$ almost surely. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.538103+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_294f8c9d25d8e1f7a31e88603d024d86fdbb6fca6b0c9df9e2733a27a3ed1a9a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:max-poisson of arXiv:2411.16913, 'Entropies of the Poisson distribution as functions of intensity: ``normal'' and ``anomalou'] For any $\lambda>0$,
\begin{equation}
    \mu(\lambda):=\max_{i\ge 0} p_i(\lambda) 
    = \frac{\lambda^{\lfloor\lambda\rfloor}}{\lfloor\lambda\rfloor!}   e^{-\lambda}, 
\end{equation}
and, for $\lambda\geq1$,
\begin{equation}
    \mu(\lambda)<\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \lfloor\lambda\rfloor}}.
\end{equation}
In particular, the maximal probability of the Poisson distribution with parameter $\lambda > 0$ tends to zero as $\lambda \rightarrow \infty$. Moreover, the following modification of the estimate (equation eq:mulambdale1overla of the paper) holds true: 
\begin{equation}
    \mu(\lambda)<\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \lambda}}\left(1+\frac{1}{(\lambda-1)\vee 1}\right)^{\frac12}\exp\left(-\frac1{12\lambda+1}\right), \qquad\lambda>1.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.591161+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29504360cb830f85e01da610d3304d7569c281bd51b1b18747696191e0a51418","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor dense-conv of arXiv:2509.15790, 'Covariance matrices of volume power functionals of random simplicial complexes -- an asymp'] In the supercritical regime for an admissible sequence $(k,0), (k,\alpha)$ and $4\alpha >-d+k-1$, the number of $k$-dimensional simplices, given through $\mathcal{V}_{k}^{(0)}$, asymptotically determines $\mathcal{V}_{k}^{(\alpha)}$,
