{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.024679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29af7fac4cca08b5a058ef61ab0c2e52ba88750415435cae1437c6a1ccd40767","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma D-gamma-inv-h of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Under Assumptions~(ass:LR of the paper), (ass:diff2 of the paper), (ass:SC of the paper), and~(ass:SC-raised of the paper) at target $q^* = q/(q-2)$ (where $q \in (2,\infty]$ is the exponent from Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper)(ii), with $q^* = 1$ at $q = \infty$; cf.\ Remark~(rem:SC-raised-role of the paper)), let $h$ satisfy Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper) (all three clauses, including the Cameron--Martin compatibility~(equation eq:CM-compat-axiom of the paper)--(equation eq:CM-compat-dominator of the paper)). Assume the fixed-Tikhonov substitution regularity~(hyp:iii-F-prime of the paper) of Theorem~(thm:tikhonov-trace of the paper) for $(\mathcal{T}, \gamma_F, h) = (\Phi, \gamma_t, h)$, through either of two admissible sufficient routes. The operator-level route asks that $u_h^{(\varepsilon)} = \Phi^{*}(\gamma_t + \varepsilon I)^{-1}h$ lie in $\mathbb{D}^{1,2}(H_W)$ for every $\varepsilon \in (0,1]$, as in~(equation eq:uheps-D12 of the paper). The basis-wise route, which is the one used below, asks instead that $G^{(\varepsilon)} = (\gamma_t + \varepsilon I)^{-1}h$ lie in $\mathbb{D}^{1,2}(H)$ for every $\varepsilon > 0$, together with the H\"older-pair joint integrability~(equation eq:linear-sub-Holder of the paper) of the pair $(\Phi, G^{(\varepsilon)})$, these being the hypotheses of Theorem~(thm:linear-sub-D12 of the paper). Both routes are available automatically at the symmetric edge $p^* \geq 6$, equivalently $2 < q \leq 3$ on the edge choice $p^* = 2q/(q-2)$; sufficient conditions for $q > 3$ are catalogued in Remark~(rem:covering-iiiF-SPDE of the paper).

For each $\varepsilon > 0$, the Tikhonov regularisation
\[
  G^{(\varepsilon)} := (\gamma_t + \varepsilon I)^{-1}\,h
\]
belongs to $\mathbb{D}^{1,2}(H)$ with the exact Leibniz formula
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{D}_r G^{(\varepsilon)} = -(\gamma_t + \varepsilon I)^{-1}\,(\mathcal{D}_r\gamma_t)\,(\gamma_t + \varepsilon I)^{-1}\,h,
\end{equation}
and the pointwise spectral bound $\norm{G^{(\varepsilon)}}_H \leq \norm{\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H$ together with $G^{(\varepsilon)} \to \gamma_t^{\dagger}h$ in $L^q(\Omega; H)$ as $\varepsilon \downarrow 0$. Define the associated covering fields intrinsically via the Hilbert--Schmidt kernel $\Phi_r := D_r X(t) \in L_2(U, H)$:
\[
  v_h^{(\varepsilon)}(r) := \Phi_r^{*}\,G^{(\varepsilon)}, \qquad v_h(r) := \Phi_r^{*}\,\gamma_t^{\dagger}h.
\]

Then the covering fields satisfy the explicit sharp spectral $H_W$-estimate
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\!\left[\int_0^t \norm{v_h^{(\varepsilon)}(r) - v_h(r)}_U^2\,\mathrm{d}r\right] \;\leq\; \frac{\varepsilon}{4}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\norm{\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H^2\right],
\end{equation}
so that $v_h^{(\varepsilon)} \to v_h$ in $L^2(\Omega;H_W)$ at the rate $O(\varepsilon^{1/2})$. Each $v_h^{(\varepsilon)}$ belongs to $\mathbb{D}^{1,2}(H_W) \subset \operatorname{Dom}(\delta_U)$, the limit $v_h$ belongs to $\operatorname{Dom}(\delta_U)$, and
\begin{equation}
  \delta_U\bigl(v_h^{(\varepsilon)}\bigr) \;\longrightarrow\; \delta_U(v_h) \qquad \text{in } L^2(\Omega).
\end{equation}
Consequently, the scalar correction integral, defined intrinsically with the positive sign convention so that $\delta_U(v_h^{(\varepsilon)}) = M^{(\varepsilon)} + C^{(\varepsilon)}$ (i.e., absorbing the Nualart minus sign of~(equation eq:nualart-substitution of the paper) via the identity $\mathcal{D} G^{(\varepsilon)} = -(\gamma_t+\varepsilon I)^{-1}(\mathcal{D}\gamma_t)(\gamma_t+\varepsilon I)^{-1}h$),
\begin{equation}
  C^{(\varepsilon)} \;:=\; -\int_0^t \operatorname{Tr}_U\bigl[\Phi_r^{*}\,\mathcal{D}_r G^{(\varepsilon)}\bigr]\,\mathrm{d}r \;=\; \int_0^t \operatorname{Tr}_U\bigl[\Phi_r^{*}\,(\gamma_t+\varepsilon I)^{-1}\,[\mathcal{D}_r\gamma_t]\,(\gamma_t+\varepsilon I)^{-1}\,h\bigr]\,\mathrm{d}r,
\end{equation}
converges in $L^2(\Omega)$ as $\varepsilon \downarrow 0$; the limit is independent of the Moore--Penrose freedom of $\mathcal{D}_r(\gamma_t^{\dagger}h)$ on $\ker(\gamma_t)$ and is denoted symboli (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

