{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.960102+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29d3cd518a5c932c00cbb5a12cfca46633d812aa6053ff350d76dd1ad4978442","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 (Karamata's Integration Theorem) of arXiv:2608.12070, 'Central limit theorems for a heavy-tailed subcritical Galton-Watson process with state-dep'] \cite{RV}

Let $L(x)$ be a function slowly varying at infinity. For $\rho>-1$, we have:
\begin{align*}
\int_0^x t^\rho L(t)\text{d} t\sim \frac{x^{\rho+1}L(x)}{\rho+1},\quad x\to\infty.
\end{align*}
For $\rho<-1$, we have:
\begin{align*}
\int_x^\infty t^\rho L(t)\text{d} t\sim \frac{x^{\rho+1}L(x)}{-(\rho+1)},\quad x\to\infty.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

