{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.870766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29eaaed3db910b651e8e0fd7d621ec2acdd68bff9036403d04cd3608eb6d1c1a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 (\textsf{Zero terminal condition}) of arXiv:2609.02064, 'Coalescence and fluctuations of O'Connell--Yor polymers\\ via the free-energy correlation '] For $n\geq1$, $\be>0$, and $x\neq\wt x$,
  \begin{align}
    \rmP_{\wt x,x}^{\rfree,\be}(\tau\leq n-1)
    &=
    \frac{2}{\be^2}\sgn(\wt x-x)
    \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
    \EE\bigl[
      \log Z_{\wt x}^{\rfree,\be}\log Z_x^{\rfree,\be}
    \bigr]

    \\
    &=
    \frac{2}{\be^2}\sgn(\wt x-x)
    \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
    \Cov\bigl(
      \log Z_{\wt x}^{\rfree,\be},
      \log Z_x^{\rfree,\be}
    \bigr)
    \notag\\
    &=
    -\frac{1}{\be^2}\sgn(\wt x-x)
    \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
    \EE\left[
      \bigl(
        \log Z_{\wt x}^{\rfree,\be}
        -\log Z_x^{\rfree,\be}
      \bigr)^2
    \right].
    \notag
  \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.636370+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29ec2cc55fd3fb11e381464e02758fa909f1702ccd7f0e5a605968410357844e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2605.28770, 'Cutoff profiles for conjugacy invariant random walks on symmetric groups'] Let $\delta>0$. For each $n$, let $ {\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} ^{(n)} \in{\ensuremath{\textup{Conj}}} ^*({\ensuremath{\mathfrak{S}}} _n)$. Assume that $f_1({\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} ^{(n)})\geq \delta n$ for $n$ large enough. Let $a\in {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} $.
Let $(t_n)$ be a sequence of positive real numbers such that $t_n = \frac{\ln n + a + o(1)}{\ln(n/f_1({\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} ^{(n)}))}$. Let $\theta >0$.   
As $n\to \infty$, we have
\begin{equation}
   \frac{1}{2} \sum_{\lambda \in \widehat{{\ensuremath{\mathfrak{S}}} _n}^{**}} \left( d_\lambda \left| \chi^\lambda({\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} ^{(n)}) \right|^{t_n}\right)^{\theta} \to \sum_{r\geq 1} e^{-ar\theta} \sum_{\mu \vdash r} \left(\frac{d_\mu}{r!}\right)^\theta.
\end{equation}
In particular, as $n\to \infty$
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1}{2}\sum_{\lambda \in \widehat{{\ensuremath{\mathfrak{S}}} _n}^{**}} d_\lambda^2 \left| \chi^\lambda({\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} ^{(n)}) \right|^{2t_n} \to e^{e^{-2a}}-1.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.307957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29f20bdab00a3bbca13407e2d5e0074e886fba514118976e06c11bc197530bdb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lemma_assumption_on_rho of arXiv:2608.08299, 'Non-linear optimal stopping with Bermudan strategies: the infinite horizon case'] Let $\rho$ satisfy the properties of knowledge preservation (iii), monotonicity (iv) and consistency (v). 
  \\ If $\rho$ does not depend on the second index 
   (that is, $\rho_{S,\tau}[\cdot]=\rho_{S}[\cdot]$), 
  we \textbf{assume} that: if $\eta \in L^+_0(\mathcal{F}_{\infty})$, then
  \begin{equation} 
   \limsup_{n \rightarrow +\infty} \rho_{\theta_n} [\eta] = \eta.
  \end{equation}
   If $\rho$ depends on both indices, 
  we \textbf{assume} that: if $\eta \in L^+_0(\mathcal{F}_{\infty})$, if 
  $(\tau_k)$ $\in \Theta^{\mathbb{N}}$ non-decreasing such that $\lim_{k \rightarrow +\infty} \tau_k  = +\infty$, then
  \begin{equation} 
  \limsup_{n \rightarrow +\infty} \limsup_{k \rightarrow +\infty} \rho_{\theta_n, \tau_k} [\eta] =  \eta.
  \end{equation}
When these conditions on $\rho$ are satisfied, we have:
   \begin{equation} 
      \limsup_{n\rightarrow\infty} V(\theta_n) = \limsup_{n\rightarrow\infty} \xi(\theta_n).
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.778852+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29f30000696d4576396d37bfaf89102473e2f90cf172feaf87e877894c0a1644","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thmappen of arXiv:2411.09257, 'Iterated Generalized Counting Process and its Extensions'] The pmf $\hat{p}_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}(\bar{n},t)$, $\bar{n}\ge\bar{0}$ of $\{\bar{\mathcal{M}}(t)\}_{t\ge0}$ solves the following system of differential equations:
{\small\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}\hat{p}_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}(\bar{n},t)=-\sum_{j_0=1}^{k_0}\mu_{j_0}(1-e^{-j_0\lambda})\hat{p}_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}(\bar{n},t)+\sum_{\bar{m}\succ\bar{0}}\hat{p}_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}(\bar{n}-\bar{m},t)\sum_{j_0=1}^{k_0}\mu_{j_0}e^{-j_0\Lambda}\sum_{\substack{\Omega(k_i,m_i)\\i=1,2,\dots,q}}\prod_{i=1}^{q}\prod_{j_i=1}^{k_i}\frac{(j_0\lambda_{ij_i})^{x_{ij_i}}}{x_{ij_i}!}
\end{equation*}}
with initial condition $\hat{p}_{\bar{\mathcal{M}}}(\bar{n},0)=\delta_{\bar{n}}(\bar{0})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:26.915310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29f3ebffe31dcf3924f4de7a41ac6d73073d680bebe86958d3010f1355707397","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mr of arXiv:2505.09604, 'Exceptional force, uncountably many solutions in the KPZ fixed point'] On a single event of probability one, for every $\xi\in\Xi$, there exists a bijection between the set of equivalence classes of  $\xi$-eternal solutions of the KPZ fixed-point and the set $\overline \mathcal{T}^{\xi,-}$. Similarly, there is a bijection between this set of equivalence classes of $\xi$-eternal solutions and $\overline \mathcal{T}^{\xi,+}$. These bijections satisfy the following:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), font=\normalfont]
    \item(Trivial interfaces are mapped to the $\xi \pm$ Busemann functions)  Under both bijections, $\nu_{-\infty}$ is mapped to  the equivalence class of $(x,t) \mapsto W^{\xi +}(0,0;x,t)$, and $\nu_{+\infty}$ is mapped to the equivalence class of $(x,t) \mapsto W^{\xi-}(0,0;x,t)$.
    \item(General $\xi$-eternal solutions are a stitching together of the two Busemann functions)  If $\tau \in \mathcal{T}^{\xi,-}$ (resp. $\tau \in \mathcal{T}^{\xi +}$), then the image under each of these bijections is the equivalence class of the function
        \begin{equation} 
        b(x,t)=\begin{cases}
            W^{\xi-}(\tau_0,0;x,t) \text{ if } x \leq \tau_t\\
             W^{\xi+}(\tau_0,0;x,t) \text{ if } x \geq \tau_t,
        \end{cases}
        \end{equation}
        where $\tau_s = \tau(s)$ for $s \in \mathbb{R}$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.583334+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29f6bdecff8ab2fbecd098d51b9797fa2ccf2d5c7d850b037c7f018fb23af0f1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2609.01333, '2609.01333'] Let $e_1 =(1,0)$  and  $\mathbb{S}^1= \partial B_1$ be the unit sphere of $\mathbb{R}^2$. For  $i=0,1$ and $n\geq i$  we define the function $\beta\mapsto T^i_n(\beta)$, $\beta>0$ by

\begin{align}

T_n^i(\beta)=\int_{\mathbb{S}^1}|\theta-e_1|^n K_i(\beta|\theta-e_1|)\mathrm{d}\theta.
\end{align}
Note that, for $\theta =  (\theta_1, \theta_2) \in \mathbb{S}^1$, since  $|\theta - e_1|^2 = 2(1-\theta\cdot e_1)= 2(1-\theta_1)$, by a change of variable over the sphere $\mathbb{S}^1$, see for instance \cite[Appendix D.3]{Gra14}, together with the change of variavles $r= \beta\sqrt{2(1-\theta_1)}$ so that
$ \mathrm{d} \theta_1 = -\beta^{-2}rdr$, we have
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
T^i_n(\beta) &= 2 \int_{-1}^{1}(2(1-\theta_1))^{n/2}  (1-\theta_1^2)^{-1/2} K_i(\beta \sqrt{2(1-\theta_1)}) \mathrm{d} \theta_1\\
&=  4 \beta^{{-n-1}}\int_{0}^{2\beta } \big(4-\frac{r^2}{\beta^2}\big)^{-1/2} r^n K_i(r) \mathrm{d}r.  
\end{split}
\end{align}

Furthermore, using the formulas in \eqref{eq:Bessel1} we easily find that

\begin{align}
(-T^0_n)'(\beta)=  T^1_{n+1}(\beta)
\quad\text{ and }\quad
(-T^1_n)'(\beta)=  T^0_{n+1}(\beta)+\beta^{-1}T^1_{n}(\beta).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.352210+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29f7d1f0e551bd7754e0dfd2e1cc579687b99c39152c3f8ea92954f915d4bc39","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Them contre_exemple of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] There exists a centered stationary Gaussian measure $P \in \mathcal{P}\left(\mathbb{R}^\mathbb{R}\right)$ whose set of weak local Markov transforms is the set of all the measures $P'$ satisfying:
