{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.690953+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29fc53eaf2417b7744bf026a52d37f5e3806303b3cb648b375c70f7c1a156738","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 7 (\cite{Bou}) of arXiv:2608.30967, 'B-Frames, B-Riesz basis, and Their tensor products'] For each $ i \in \{1,2\}$, let $\{ z^i_k \}_{k \in \mathcal{K}} \subset \mathcal{Z}_i$.

 $\{ z^1_j \otimes z^2_k\}_{j,k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a frame (or Riesz basis) for $\mathcal{Z}_1 \otimes \mathcal{Z}_2$ if and only if $\{ z^i_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a frame (or Riesz basis) for $\mathcal{Z}_i $ for each $ i \in \{1,2\}$.

In addition the frame bounds of a frame $\{ z^1_j \otimes z^2_k\}_{j,k \in \mathcal{K}}$ are $A_1A_2$ and $B_1B_2$ if, for each $ i \in \{1,2\}$, the frame bounds of the frame $\{ z^i_k \}_{k \in \mathcal{K}} $ are $A_i$ and $B_i$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.294675+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29fc8a4daa8c46952577267c9532300c52c8d07b5d864685d354d5fbb8cfb7ff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:omega_upbd of arXiv:2605.22787, 'Invariant measures for half-space geometric LPP: classification and the one force--one sol'] If $b$ is an eternal solution, then for $(m,n) \in \mathbb{Z}^2_{\mathrm{HS}}$,
    \[
    \omega_{(m,n)} = \begin{cases}
    \bigl(b(m,n) - b(m-1,n)\bigr)
 \wedge  \bigl(b(m,n) - b(m,n-1)\bigr), &m > n \\
 b(m,n) - b(m-1,n), &m = n,\end{cases}
    \]
    where $\wedge$ denotes minimum. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:54.792500+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2a01aa8a37931a2bb8f3b14d2f2c0a9ae3abbb0cf771b18a5723707e3b340d58","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm JCIAn of arXiv:2608.26934, 'Around the Andreadakis--Johnson filtration'] For $k \ge 2$ and $n \ge k+2$, the following sequence is exact:
