{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.324230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2a9acce31d2429dfdc4b6815d08013ff76ab2cb263860df53fcc11df7d78ef74","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop O-mapping-CW of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $G,H$ be compact Lie, let $V$ be an $H$-representation, and let $U$ be a $G$-representation. Then $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,U)$ admits the structure of a finite based $(G\times H)$-CW-complex. Moreover, if $K\subset H$ acts faithfully on $V$, then $K$ acts freely on $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,U)$ outside the basepoint.
    \begin{proof}
        The claim is clear if $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,U)=0$, so we may assume there exists an isometric embedding $V\to U$, which allows us to identify $V$ with a vector subspace of $U$, equipped with the restricted inner product. By the previous lemma, we may then identify $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,U)\cong {\textup{O}}(U)_+\smashp_{{\textup{O}}(U-V)}S^{U-V}$ as $(G\times H)$-spaces. By Illman's triangulation theorem, ${\textup{O}}(U)$ is a $\big(G\times {\textup{O}}(U-V)\times H\big)$-CW-complex and $S^{U-V}$ is an ${\textup{O}}(U-V)$-CW-complex. In any such structure, $\infty$ is a $0$-cell (being an isolated ${\textup{O}}(U-V)$-fixed point), so that ${\textup{O}}(U)_+\smashp S^{U-V}$ inherits the structure of a based $(G\times {\textup{O}}(U-V)\times H)$-CW-complex, ultimately giving $\textbf{\textup{O}}(V,U)$ the structure of a based $(G\times H)$-CW-complex; moreover, this is finite by compactness.

        To see that $K$ acts freely outside the basepoint on ${\textup{O}}(U)_+\smashp_{{\textup{O}}(U-V)} S^{U-V}$, it will suffice to show that it acts freely on ${\textup{O}}(U)/{{\textup{O}}(U-V)}$. For this let $A\in {\textup{O}}(U)$ arbitrary and let $k\in K$. As $K$ acts faithfully on $V$ by assumption, there exists a $v\in V$ such that $k^{-1}.v\not=v$, and hence $(k.A)(v)=A(k^{-1}.v)\not=A(v)$ by injectivity of $A$. Acting with any element of ${\textup{O}}(U-V)$ does not change the values of $A$ on $V$, so that $k.A$ and $A$ define distinct classes in ${\textup{O}}(U)/{{\textup{O}}(U-V)}$, as claimed.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.566585+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2aa0f23413a6cfe69dc6459125c17877d2e5168474306e5ba90e8178d425e482","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.sym-exit of arXiv:2605.26082, 'Quantitative Einstein relation for reversible diffusions in a random environment'] Fix an environment $\omega$. Let $Z_t$ be a Feller diffusion with generator $\tfrac12\nabla\cdot(\mathbf{a}\nabla)$, where $\mathbf{a}$ is symmetric, uniformly elliptic, and Lipschitz with constants $(\Lambda^{-1},\Lambda)$. There exist constants $c,C>0$ depending only on $(d,\Lambda)$ such that, for every $R>0$ and $T>0$ and every $x\in\mathbb{R}^d$,
		\begin{equation}
			P_x^{0,\omega} \left(\sup_{0\le s\le T}|Z_s-x|\ge R\right)\le C\exp \left(-c\frac{R^2}{T}\right)\, .
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:02:17.946170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2aa199e545fc95be069b380f20d0d016ffcb159888796985e966335b53d06e93","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:phi of arXiv:2602.21315, 'The Instability of all Backoff Protocols\thanks{For the purpose of Open Access, the author'] Let $A$ and $B$ be distributions on $(\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j$. $A$ \emph{dominates $B$ from above} 
(written $A \newsym{>}{Dominates from above}{\gtrsim} B$)
if there is a distribution $C$ on pairs $(\bar{a},\bar{b}) \in (\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j \times (\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j$ such that $\bar{a}\sim A$, ${\overline{b}} \sim B$, and $\bar{a}\geq {\overline{b}}$.
If $A$ dominates $B$ from above let \newsym{phi-AB}{Stochastic domination map from distribution $A$ to $B$ where $A \gtrsim B$}{\phi_{A,B}} be the (random) function from $(\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j$ to $(\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j$ which maps $\bar{a}$ to ${\overline{b}}$ with probability $C(\bar{a},{\overline{b}})/A(\bar{a})$. By construction, $\phi_{A,B}(\bar{a}) \leq \bar{a}$ pointwise. Also, when $\bar{a} \sim A$, the random variable $\phi_{A,B}(\bar{a})$  has distribution~$B$.   
We naturally use the notation 
\newsym{phi-AB-minus}{}{\phi^{-1}_{A,B}} for the (random) function from $(\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j$ to $(\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0})^j$ which maps ${\overline{b}}$ to $\bar{a}$ with probability $C(\bar{a},{\overline{b}})/B(\bar{b})$.","status":"unverified"}

