{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.957712+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2b83bbc223c41d3cb7932f99b74c949790c68c37066152078664fc834580d5b9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 4 of arXiv:2512.02286, 'Pfaffian Transition Probability and Correlation Kernel of TASEP in Half-space'] For a fixed integer $N$, let $\mathcal{C}_1,\dots,\mathcal{C}_N$ be a collection of positively oriented closed complex integration contours satisfying:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For all $1\leq i\leq N$, each contour $\mathcal{C}_i$ encloses the point 1 and does not enclose the points $0,q^{-1}$ and $(1-q-\alpha)/\alpha$.
        \item For all $1\leq i<j\leq N$, each $\mathcal{C}_i$ is completely contained within the interior of each $\mathcal{C}_j$. Additionally, $q\mathcal{C}_j$ lies completely outside the interior of $\mathcal{C}_i$, where $q\mathcal{C}_j$ denotes the image of $\mathcal{C}_j$ under multiplication by $q$. This also implies that no part of $\mathcal{C}_j$ is contained within the interior of $q^{-1}\mathcal{C}_i$.
        \item For all $1\leq k,\ell\leq N$, the contours $\mathcal{C}_k$ and $q^{-1}\mathcal{C}_\ell^{-1}$ do not intersect and have completely disjoint interiors. The contour $\mathcal{C}_\ell^{-1}$ is the contour which traverses the points which are the reciprocal of those on $\mathcal{C}_\ell$, and whose orientation is fixed so that $q^{-1}\mathcal{C}_\ell^{-1}$ encloses the point $q^{-1}$.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.335182+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2b849658cb704b18489db0ae676caad4452cce056f6044e5438ebfc29ac27909","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:good_convergence of arXiv:2609.04138, 'The conformally invariant metric on $\CLE_4$ I:\\ subsequential limits of the non-simple C'] Jointly with~(equation eq cv subsequence of the paper),~(equation eq cv Hausdorff balls of the paper) and~(equation eq cv subsequence boundary of the paper), for all $t \in \BQ_{\ge 0}$, $x \in \BD_\BQ$, $\alpha \in \SCS'$, along a subsequence again denoted by $(\kappa_n)_{n \ge 0}$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\left(
		\SCB^{\kappa_n, \partial}_{\ka_{\kappa_n} s}(\SCB_{\ka_{\kappa_n}t}^{\kappa_n} (\SCL^{\kappa_n}(x))), \SCB^{\kappa_n, \partial}_{\ka_{\kappa_n} s}(\SCB_{\ka_{\kappa_n}t}^{\kappa_n} (\alpha))
