{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.770133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bc00a6f646f36e9b80cc987641a18a27f2c87fe358e319cd6fbbc602c37fac4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor: GNZwithoutAdditionalInfluence of arXiv:2411.02917, '\hspace*{2mm}\\[-16mm] Stein's Method for Spatial Random Graphs'] Let $(\Xi, \Sigma) \sim P \otimes Q$ be a random graph with the property that the distribution of the edge process $\Sigma\vert_{\xi^{\langle2\rangle}}$ given $\Xi=\xi+\delta_x$ is the same as given $\Xi=\xi$ for $P$-a.e.\ $\xi$.
  Then we have for any measurable function $h:\mathfrak{N} \times \mathfrak{N}_2 \times \mathcal{X} \times \mathfrak{N}_2 \to \R_+$
  \begin{equation*}
  \begin{split}
      \E \biggl(\int_{\mathcal{X}} &h \bigl(\Xi-\delta_x, \Sigma \vert_{(\Xi-\delta_x)^{\langle2\rangle}}, x, \Sigma \vert_{\langle \Xi-\delta_x,x \rangle} \bigr) \; \Xi(dx) \biggr) \\
      &= \E \biggl( \int_{\mathcal{X}} \E \Bigl( h \bigl(\Xi, \Sigma, x, \Tilde{\Sigma}_{\Xi,\Sigma,x} \bigr) \Bigm| \Xi, \Sigma \Bigr) \; L(dx \,|\, \Xi) \biggr),
  \end{split}
  \end{equation*}
  where $\Tilde{\Sigma}_{\xi,\sigma,x}$ is the random element on $\mathfrak{N}_2$ with distribution $\mathcal{L} \bigl( \Sigma \vert_{\langle \xi, x \rangle} \bigm| \Xi = \xi+\delta_x, \Sigma \vert_{\xi^{\langle2\rangle}} = \sigma \bigr) = \tilde{Q}_{2 \,|\, 1}(\xi,x,\sigma ;\, \cdot)$.

  In particular, if $h$ is constant in the last argument,
  \begin{equation*}
  \begin{split}
      \E \biggl( \int_{\mathcal{X}} &h \bigl(\Xi-\delta_x, \Sigma \vert_{(\Xi-\delta_x)^{\langle2\rangle}}, x\bigr) \; \Xi(dx) \biggr) = \E \biggl( \int_{\mathcal{X}} h \bigl(\Xi, \Sigma, x\bigr) \; L(dx \,|\, \Xi) \biggr).
  \end{split}
  \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:32:58.301402+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bc6d29f79eb91171ce52328c770e91f792685352208428b0e549a1b8ff7581c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2505.10256, 'Stochastic Oscillators out of equilibrium:\\ scaling limits and correlation estimates'] Fix a pair of bounded measurable functions $v_0,e_0:\mathbb T\to \mathbb R$. 
We say that a pair of measurable functions $(v,e)=(v(t,u), e(t,u)) \in L^2([0,T]\times \mathbb T)^2$ is a weak solution of (equation eq:hdl of the paper) if for every $G\in C^{2}(\mathbb T)$, it holds that 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\mathbb T}v(t,u)G(u)du - \int_{\mathbb T}v_0(u) G(u)du 

+ \int_0^t\int_{\mathbb T}v(s,u) 
\big(\Delta G(u) - 2\alpha\nabla G(u)\big) du ds = 0
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
& \int_{\mathbb T}e(t,u)G(u)du - \int_{\mathbb T}e_0(u) G(u)du \\
&\quad
+ \int_0^t\int_{\mathbb T}e(s,u) 
\Delta G(u) du ds 
- \int_0^t\int_{\mathbb T} v(s,u)^2 \nabla G(u) du ds = 0.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

