{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:25:39.084464+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bf2cd6f3405171c7044fae2c61c2d04b82a6a73df04f17e1b8ae4433bad4aca","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:limitprocess of arXiv:2606.11677, 'Feynman--Kac formula for the heat equation with a one-center point interaction in $d=3$.'] Fix $T>0$ and $x\in {\mathbb{R}}^3\backslash \{0\}$. Then,  \begin{align*}
\{\mathcal{W}_m\}_{m\geq 1} \text{is a $C([0,\infty),{\mathbb{R}}^3)$-Cauchy sequence, $\mathbb{P}(\cdot|\widetilde{T}^x)$-a.s.}
\end{align*} 
and hence there exists a process $\left\{X^{x,T}(s)\right\}_{s\geq 0 }$ such that $\mathcal{W}_m\to X^{x,T}$ in the locally uniform convergence sense $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. 

Moreover, there exists a random process $\{X^x(s)\}_{s\geq 0}$ such that for any $T>0$ the restriction of $X^x$ to $[0,T]$ has the same distribution as $X^{x,T}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.889395+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bf3027e0c5a8ac6488b2fc5cdb8f0935114b90f245331d6deac62081a40af26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:pointwise of arXiv:2607.04047, 'Top Singular Value in Sum-Products of Random Matrices'] There exist universal constants $c, C > 0$ such that the following holds for any $\epsilon \in (0,1)$:
\begin{proplist}
    \item If $\beta = o(1)$, then with probability at least $1 - \epsilon^{-2} e^{ - c \log m } - 2 e^{-cn\epsilon^2}$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \big|  
            \log \| X \theta \|
            -
            Z
        \big| 
        \;\leq\; 
        C
        \Big(
            \mfrac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}    
            +
            \mfrac{\beta^2 \log m}{n}
        \Big)
        \;;
    \end{align*}
    \item If $\beta = \Omega(1)$, $\log m = o(N^{1/3})$ and $N=o(n^3)$, then  
    \begin{align*}
        \mfrac{1}{\beta^2 \log m}
        \big|  
            \log \| X \theta \|
            -
            Z
        \big| 
        \;\leq\; 
        C \Big( \mfrac{\epsilon}{(1-\epsilon) \beta \log m}
                    +
                    \mfrac{1}{ \beta (\log m)^{1/4}}
                \Big)
        \;
    \end{align*}
    with probability at least $1 - (1+ \epsilon^{-2}) e^{ - c  \, (\log m)^{3/4} } - 2e^{-cn\epsilon^2}$.
\end{proplist} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.533126+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bf486b36ba2e173d5b05c303b9d90831057c95dc58a3fc0eaf47152d712a864","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 (branching capacity defined by the past) of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] For all finite sets \(A \subset \Z^{d}\),

