{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.411226+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bfecc3a30d9ca0d3ff6849c25b3d0db5862cdf880eae16678d9a8b17232ce2e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2605.15931, '2605.15931'] The sequence of processes $(n^{1/2}(f(X_{\tilde{\tau}^n(X)\wedge .})-f(x)))_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ converges in the sense of finite-dimensional distributions over any finite collection $\{t_1,t_2,...,t_m\}\subset (0,\infty)$. In particular, for any $\{t_1,t_2,...,t_m\}\subset (0,\infty)$ and $\varphi:\mathbb{R}^{m\times \ell
		}\to \mathbb{R}$ continuous and bounded we have
