{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.533853+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ced2369c67ea3073e52cd8588768c1bf2fa885863efe1f45fe526ae34ea176d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def.ConstantExtension_canonical of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given a realization $\mathbf{a}$ and ${F \subseteq E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})}$, we define \emph{the canonical grain}
		\begin{align}
			[x]^F:= \left\{
			\begin{array}{ll}
				x, & x \in \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^F({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}),\\
				\arg \min_{y \in \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^\mathbf{a}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})} \dist(y, \mathscr{O}^F(x)), & x \notin \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^F({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}) .
			\end{array}\right.
		\end{align} 
		Here we fix a lexicographic order for $\arg \min$. We also keep the convention $[x] \equiv [x]^{\emptyset}$.	

		A function $u : \{0,1\}^{E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})} \times {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \to \mathbb{R}$ follows the canonical grain extension rule if 	
		\begin{align}
			u(\mathbf{a}^F, x) = u(\mathbf{a}^F, [x]^F).
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.052065+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2cef4e2833488bd938d24b6f6af90d41d2d64bf7a52d470f032aa139c2740c37","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:phi of arXiv:2607.12564, 'Power-law and log-periodic degree tails for a family of probability generating function eq'] The series $\Phi(u):=\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb{Z}}\eta\bigl(\lambda^{\,u-\ell}\bigr)$ converges locally uniformly
on $\mathbb{R}$, and uniformly on $[0,1]$, to a continuous, strictly positive function of period $1$. Moreover, with
$N=\lfloor\log_\lambda x\rfloor$ and $\{\,\cdot\,\}$ denoting the fractional part,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  x^{\rho+1}\sum_{n\ge0}\lambda^{-(\rho+1)n}w_j(x\lambda^{-n})
  &=\Phi(\log_\lambda x)-\sum_{\ell<-N}\eta(\lambda^{\{\log_\lambda x\}-\ell})\\
  &=\Phi(\log_\lambda x)+o(1)\qquad(x\to\infty),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
the error being uniform in $\{\log_\lambda x\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.316327+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2cefdd0bf07a12c674552a7f99a9896151837959d061fba4dfa525bcd539a867","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem f-bounded-distortion of arXiv:2606.01627, 'The geometry of the giant component of random geometric graphs'] Let $M$ be a connected compact $d$-dimensional Riemannian manifold of volume $1$ with $d \geq 2$.  For each fixed $\lambda_0 > \lambda_c$, $\beta \in (0,1), \varepsilon > 0$, let $X$ be a finite $(\varepsilon/5)$-net of $M$.  Then there are constants $c = c(M,\lambda_0,\beta,\varepsilon,X) > 0$ and $n_0 = n_0(M,\lambda_0,\beta,\varepsilon,X)$ so that {if $r \le n^{-\beta}$ and $\lambda \ge \lambda_0$} we have
        $$\mathbb{P}\left(\sup_{x, x' \in X}
        |\mu(\lambda)d_M(x,x') - r d^\lambda_r(f_r(x),f_r(x'))| > \varepsilon/2\right) \leq  \exp(-c \log^2 n) $$
        for $n \geq n_0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.871626+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ceff8bfcdd491b46a033bb2bbee207d1e2b14e5451c5a9cd4f4e5a74dfe7b08","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Functional one-step inequality of arXiv:2608.24731, 'Optimal Alternating Regret for Online Learning and Games\thanks{Authors are alphabetically'] \label[lemma]{lem:functional}
