{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.167657+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d34d46e0868624fa2b3c3c99c68a44cdca2ed1ad91e98f14582a87dc50f8078","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 (Mild Solution) of arXiv:2607.03318, 'Continuous Differentiability of the Value Function for Infinite-Dimensional Finite-Horizon'] For an initial condition $t \in [0, T)$ and $x \in H$, an $H$-valued, $\F$-progressively measurable process $X(\cdot) \equiv X(\cdot; t, x)$ is a \emph{mild solution} to the abstract Cauchy problem \eqref{eq:cauchy_problem} if it satisfies $\Prob$-a.s. for all $s \in [t, T]$:
