{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.436521+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d41dfd7453471d059f8a9e9d67784fc61d4877dbabe7def052d154e9da55514","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Partial order, increasing functions and stochastic domination of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] We introduce the three following definitions:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] \textit{Partial order:} Given a subset $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}$, we define a partial order on the space $\{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$ by writing, for any pair $r , r' \in \{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$, $r \preceq r'$ if and only if, for all $x \in \Lambda, \, r_x \leq r_x'.$
\item[(ii)] \textit{Increasing function:} A function $f : \{ 0 , 1 \}^\Lambda \to \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ is called increasing if $f(r) \leq f(r')$ for all pairs of configurations $r , r' \in \{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$ with $r \preceq r'$.
\item[(iii)] \textit{Stochastic domination:} Given two probability measures $\nu , \nu'$ on $\{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$, we say that $\nu$ stochastically dominates $\nu'$, and denote it by $\nu \preceq \nu'$, if $\mathbb{E}_\nu \left[  f \right] \leq \mathbb{E}_{\nu'} \left[  f \right]$ for any increasing function $f : \{ 0 , 1\}^{\Lambda} \to [0, \infty)$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.127351+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d44f1bd92df74c4204d815302cb272c643f6e4fbb8f4d535ba6bb05b6e14c3f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 26 of arXiv:2608.26662, 'Boundary–Moment Universality and Curvature Corrections in Random Geometric Graphs on Riema'] There exist constants $C<\infty$ and $r_0>0$ such that, for every
$n\ge3$ and $0<r<r_0$, the path--triangle contrast satisfies
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Var} C_n(r)
\le
C\left(
n^3r^{2d}
+n^4r^{3d}
+n^5r^{4d+4}
\right).
\end{equation}
Accordingly, its normalized estimator obeys
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Var}\widehat{\mathcal G}_{3,n}(r)
\le
C\left(
\frac1{n^3r^{2d+4}}
+
\frac1{n^2r^{d+4}}
+
\frac1n
\right).
\end{equation}

Now let $r_n\downarrow0$.  If the bandwidth sequence satisfies
\begin{equation}
n^3r_n^{2d+4}\longrightarrow\infty,
\qquad
n^2r_n^{d+4}\longrightarrow\infty,
\end{equation}
then the normalized contrast consistently estimates the intrinsic functional:
\[
\widehat{\mathcal G}_{3,n}(r_n)
\xrightarrow{\Pp}
\mathcal G_3(f;g).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:24:45.935754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d4c5361b0823f59e80d4b3513e023f31efecc1af7816fa824b0f893bf81bb2c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn of arXiv:2509.10984, 'Weak Existence and Uniqueness for Super-Brownian Motion with Irregular Drift'] A sequence $(\eta_n)_{n\geq 1} \subset \mathcal{M}_{reg}^+$ is said to converge $m$-weakly to $\eta \in \mathcal{M}_{reg}^+$ if the following is satisfied:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)]  For all open sets $U \subset \mathbb{R}$ such that $\eta(U) = \infty$ it follows that $\lim_{ n\rightarrow \infty}\eta_n(U) = \infty$.
		\item[(ii)] For all compact sets $K \subset \mathcal{R}_\eta$ there exists $M>0$ and $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\eta_n(K) <M$ for all
		$n \geq N$.
		\item[(iii)] For all $\phi \in \mathcal{C}_c(\mathcal{R}_\eta)$ it holds that 
		\begin{equation*}
			\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\int \phi d\eta_n = \int \phi d\eta.
		\end{equation*} 
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.713132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d4ecc3db1255acd0f22612fa1098794750031eb10bafc06fe663d8bec0cbe53","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro:approximation_cluster of arXiv:2411.04778, 'Coupling between Brownian motion and random walks on the infinite percolation cluster'] There exist finite positive constants $C_1$ and $s_1$ that depend on $d,\mathfrak{p}$ such that, for every $t>0$, the following holds:
		\begin{multline}
			\sup_{f\in 1\text{-}\mathrm{Lip}(\mathbb{R}^d)} \Bigg\vert \sum_{x: x + y\in\mathscr{C}_\infty}f(x)\theta(\mathfrak{p})^{-1}\bar{p}(t,x)-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}f(x)\bar{p}(t,x)\, {\mathrm{d}} x\Bigg\vert \\
			\leqslant 
			\left\{ 		
			\begin{array}{ll}
				\mathcal{O}_{s_1}\left(C_1\lfloor\frac{1}{2}\log_3t \rfloor\right),  & d=2,  \\ 
				\mathcal{O}_{s_1}(C_1), & d\geqslant 3.
			\end{array}\right. 
		\end{multline} 


		Moreover, for each $\delta >0$, there exist finite positive constants $C_2$ and $s_2$ that depend on $d,\mathfrak{p},\delta$ and a nonnegative $\omega$-measurable random variable $\mathcal{T}_{dense,\delta}(y)$ satisfying
		\begin{equation}
			\forall T\geqslant 0, \qquad \mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{p}}\left(\mathcal{T}_{dense,\delta}(y)\geqslant T\right)\leqslant C_2 \exp\left(-\frac{T^{s_2}}{C_2}\right),
		\end{equation}
		such that on the event $\{y\in\mathscr{C}_\infty\}$, for every $t>\mathcal{T}_{dense,\delta}(y)$, we have 
		\begin{equation}
			\sup_{f\in 1\text{-}\mathrm{Lip}(\mathbb{R}^d)}\Bigg\vert \sum_{x: x + y\in\mathscr{C}_\infty}f(x)\theta(\mathfrak{p})^{-1}\bar{p}(t,x)-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}f(x)\bar{p}(t,x)\, {\mathrm{d}} x\Bigg\vert\leqslant C_2t^\delta.
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.846796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d50e39257cd80c26b5c6b40f6ed2fd0fa0968cb3a9fc106b9471b29c99b1783","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:GaussianMatrix2 of arXiv:2410.23915, 'Asymptotic Bounds and Online Algorithms for Average-Case Matrix Discrepancy'] The joint probability density function of two $m \times m$ GOE matrices with correlation $\rho \in (-1,1)$ is given by
