{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:26.944933+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d6d242b51b861525d7637b6992f501847debc9cb4a1a704ea8203752a230e9e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p: interfaces do not meet of arXiv:2505.09604, 'Exceptional force, uncountably many solutions in the KPZ fixed point'] The following holds on the event $\Omega_1$: let $\xi \in \Xi$ and assume that $(x,t) \notin \mathfrak S_{\xi}^L$. Let 
       \begin{equation} 
       t_1 := \inf\{r \le t: g_{(x,t)}^{\xi-,L}(r) = g_{(x,t)}^{\xi+,L}(r)\},\quad\text{and set}\quad y_1 := g_{(x,t)}^{\xi-,L}(t_1) = g_{(x,t)}^{\xi+,L}(t_1).
       \end{equation}
       In words, $(y_1,t_1)$ is the splitting time of the two geodesics, as in Figure (fig:interfaces can split of the paper). Alternatively, we can assume that $(x,t) \notin \mathfrak S_\xi^R$ and define $(y_1,t_1)$ similarly as above with $L$ replaced by $R$. Then, $t_1 < t$, $y_1 \in \mathfrak S_{t_1,\xi}^L \cap \mathfrak S_{t_1,\xi}^R$, and there exists $(x_1,s_1) \in \mathfrak S_\xi^L$ and $(x_2,s_2) \in \mathfrak S_{\xi}^R$ with $s_1\vee s_2 < t$ such that, for  $i = 1,2$,
       \begin{equation} 
       \tau_{(x_i,s_i)}^{\xi,-}(t_1) = \tau_{(x_i,s_i)}^{\xi,+}(t_1), \quad\text{and}\quad \tau_{(x_i,s_i)}^{\xi,-}(r) < \tau_{(x_i,s_i)}^{\xi,+}(r),\quad\forall r \in (t_1,t).
       \end{equation}
       In words, the two interfaces agree at time $t_1$, but afterwards split. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

