{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.655721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2df4727843e5d2e5ad0c1594fcf5d31bf81078cf0687599215f6bf00d0b4af7d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.03726, 'Inverse Scattering for Single Photons in Quantum Optics'] Let $n \geq 2$. Fix $\delta >1/2$ and an integer $l \geq 0$. There exists $k_\rho = k_{\rho, l, \delta}>0$ and a constant $C_{ \rho,l, \delta}>0$ such that for every $k > k_\rho$ and every $f \in H^l_\delta(\mathbb{R}^n)$, the limit
\begin{equation}
