{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.290501+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e06f2040635d904fed2069d541183ec3cfd7ec7834c989e361f59d19654e70e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem angel-spinka of arXiv:2411.04988, '{\bf A relation between isoperimetry and total variation decay with applications to graphs'] Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a countable collection of probability measures, all absolutely continuous with respect to a common $\sigma$-finite measure. Then there exists a coupling $\mathbb P$ of all the measures in $\mathcal{S}$ such that, for any two measures $\mathbb P_X,\mathbb P_Y\in \mathcal{S}$, the two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with distributions $\mathbb P_X$ and $\mathbb P_Y$ in the coupling satisfy
