Moreover, there is a constant $c>0$ such that
\begin{align*}
 f_+(u)&\le-(\log R)u, \qquad0\le u\le1;\\
 f_-(u)&\le-cu, \qquad~\,\qquad0\le u\le\tfrac12;\\
 f_-(u)&\le-c(1-u), \qquad\tfrac12\le u\le1.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.283566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e0df6f1e2911a327ab192a69ffa409b5aef28e94702b60e53b746296c35de5d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:randomtime of arXiv:2604.20327, 'Persistent Homology of the Wiener Sausage II: A Central Limit Theorem for Drifted Planar B'] Let $\Theta$ be a nonnegative random time such that
  $\mathbb{E}[e^{\lambda \Theta}] < \infty$ for some $\lambda > 0$. Then
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}[H_\Theta^2] < \infty,
  \end{equation}
  and, more generally,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}\!\left[\left(\int_{r_0}^{r_1} \beta_1^\Theta(r)\, \psi(r)\, dr\right)^{\!2}\right]
    < \infty
  \end{equation}
