{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.671047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e14055f744f00cbb477e62e12fd4b8d674c5cffa8e859b77e72b0d764e8bfac","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:gaussian:measure of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] A probability measure \( \gamma \) on \( (X,\mathcal{C}(X)) \) is called a \emph{Gaussian measure} if the characteristic functional of \( \gamma \) has the form
    \begin{equation} 
        \widehat{\gamma}(f) =
        \exp\left(ia(f)-\frac{1}{2}R(f,f)\right),
        \qquad \forall f\in X^*,
    \end{equation}
    where \( a : X^* \to \mathbb{R} \) is a linear functional and \( R : X^* \times X^* \to \mathbb{R} \) is a symmetric, positive bilinear form.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.973691+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e16a7b78fba26c7ad9d7201a7e60cb0229d31453dda07293cf1c3f3192d6838","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm wound simple loops WLE of arXiv:2508.13827, 'Surface sums in two-dimensional large-$N$ lattice Yang--Mills: Cancellations and explicit '] Let $\upbeta\in \mathbb{R}$. Suppose $\ell$ is a simple loop and set $a_{\ell}=\area(\ell)$. For $n\in \mathbb{N}$, let $\ell^n$ denote the loop $\ell$ wound around itself $n$ times. Then,
    \begin{equation}
        c(\ell^n, K_{\ell^n}) = \frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\binom{na_{\ell}-2}{n-1},
    \end{equation}
    where, when $a_{\ell}=1$, the above expression should be interpreted as $c(\ell^n, K_{\ell^n}) =\mathds{1}_{n=1}$.
    In particular, since $\mathcal{K}_{\ell^n} = \{K_{\ell^n}\}$ thanks to (rem:card-set-cK of the paper), we deduce that
    \begin{align}
        \phi(\ell^n) = \frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\binom{na_{\ell}-2}{n-1}\upbeta^{na_{\ell}}.
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.076332+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e17a758ce2863bd8423a251571adb4171b2e453f89a97ed9e44f71e087201e2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 of arXiv:2606.02464, 'Inspections'] The optimal social welfare achievable by the RR policy is always smaller than the optimal social welfare achievable by the GA policy;
