{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.158650+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e213bf6ac470cdfb8174c90ad8d427dda301c2105e47be67c687e34cbc3fc05","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] Let $f\in\mathcal S(\RR^d)$ and suppose that $\int_{\RR^d}\d x f(x)=1$. We let $\hat f$ denote the Fourier transform of $f$. Then for any $n\ge1$, $\delta\in(0,1)$, we may find a constant $C$ such that for any $x_1,\dots,x_n\in\RR^d$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \left|1-\prod_{k=1}^n\hat f(x_k)\right|\le C\sum_{k=1}^n|x_k|^\delta.
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.971729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e252c7df52c5bdb3d78eaf441efe882f7c2ced90198a03c263783da87780c3d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop weak-triangle of arXiv:2506.23062, 'Shifted Composition IV\@: \\ Toward Ballistic Acceleration for Log-Concave Sampling'] For any probability measures $\mu$, $\nu$, $\pi$, any R\'enyi order $q \in [1,\infty]$, and any relaxation parameter $\lambda \in (0,1)$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathsf{R}_q(\mu \mathbin{\|} \pi) \leqslant \frac{q-\lambda}{q-1}\,\mathsf{R}_{q/\lambda}(\mu \mathbin{\|} \nu) + \mathsf{R}_{(q-\lambda)/(1-\lambda)}(\nu \mathbin{\|} \pi)\,.
    \end{align*}
    In particular (by setting $\lambda = 1-\varepsilon$, $q = 1+\varepsilon$, and letting $\varepsilon\searrow 0$),
    \begin{align*}
        \KL(\mu \mathbin{\|} \pi) \leqslant 2 \KL(\mu \mathbin{\|} \nu) + \log(1+\chi^2(\nu \mathbin{\|} \pi))\,.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.065959+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e25474102c0f93808ad0996aa14d1105c304ebb3d94b60861521a69235297c9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 7 of arXiv:2606.00648, 'Asymptotic estimates for multiple point ranges of transient random walks on graphs'] Assume that $(U_1)$ holds and $F_{\inf} < 1$. 
