{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.248188+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ecde20e32d2e32456816c40dd2c6774da6dc80ed097091e413f59094ce784e0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 ({\cite[Theorem 1.10) of arXiv:2605.22146, 'Poisson approximation of the largest gaps between zeros of a stationary Gaussian process'] {mui23}}]

Let $(X_i)_{i\le n}$ be a centred Gaussian vector, and abbreviate  $ \|\sigma^2\|_\infty := \max_i \textrm{Var}[X_i]$. Then for all $I_1,I_2 \subseteq \{1,\ldots,n\}$, $\ell \in \R$, and $\eps > 0$,
\begin{align*}
 &  \P[X_i > \ell \ \forall i \in I_1  \cap I_2 ]  \\
& \qquad \le  \P[X_i  > \ell \ \forall i \in I_1]\P[X_i > \ell -  \eps  \ \forall i \in I_2] +   \exp \Big(- \frac{ \eps^2 }{8 \|\sigma^2\|_\infty  \rho^2(I_1,I_2) } \Big) 
\end{align*}
 where 
 \begin{equation}

 \rho(I_1,I_2)  = \sup_{ \alpha \in \R^{|I_1|} , \beta \in \R^{|I_2|} }   \frac{ | \textrm{Cov}[  \langle \alpha , X_{I_1} \rangle , \langle \beta , X_{I_2} \rangle ] | }{ \sqrt{ \textrm{Var}[\langle \alpha , X_{I_1}  \rangle ] \textrm{Var}[ \langle \beta , X_{I_2}  \rangle] } } 
 \end{equation}
 is the \textit{maximum correlation coefficient}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.669509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ecea303f0c2b8a997600f04c12a779d5925f72f80b088343e9e8a8d63c85bd6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:connecting-orbits-measure of arXiv:2411.01364, 'Global Dynamics of Granular Media Equations via Stochastic Order'] For a semigroup $\str P_t$ on $\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$,
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item a measure $\nu\in\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$ is called an invariant measure if $P_t^*\nu=\nu$ for all $t\geq0$;
\item a family $\{\nu_t\}_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\subset\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$ is called a connecting orbit from an invariant measure $\nu$ to another invariant measure $\tilde{\nu}$ if
\begin{align*}
&\str P_t\nu_s=\nu_{t+s}\text{ for any $t\geq0$, $s\in\mathbb{R}$};\\
&\mathcal W_2(\nu_t,\nu)\to0\text{ as $t\to-\8$ and }\mathcal W_2(\nu_t,\tilde{\nu})\to0\text{ as $t\to\8$}.
\end{align*}
Furthermore, if 
$$\nu_s\leq_{\mathrm{st}}\nu_t\ \ (\nu_s\geq_{\mathrm{st}}\nu_t)\text{ for all }s\leq t,$$
then we call $\{\nu_t\}_{t\in\mathbb{R}}$ an increasing (decreasing) connecting orbit from $\nu$ to $\tilde{\nu}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.589788+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ecfd397fec6bd8da7651bb4b2bc1bb2180778606f70e75f10965c12c383f162","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Fourier-isometry of arXiv:2606.02003, 'Higher-Order Multifractional Stable Motion:\\ Definition and Fundamental Properties'] Let $M_\alpha$ be a real S$\alpha$S random measure on $\mathbb{R}$
with Lebesgue control measure, and $\widetilde{M}_\alpha$ a complex
isotropic S$\alpha$S random measure on $\mathbb{R}$ with Lebesgue
control measure. For $f \in L^\alpha(\mathbb{R})$ with Fourier transform
$\widehat{f} \in L^\alpha(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C})$:
\begin{equation}

\int_\mathbb{R} f(u)\,dM_\alpha(u)
\;\overset{d}{=}\;
\mathrm{Re}\int_\mathbb{R}
C_\alpha\,
\widehat{f}(\xi)\,
e^{-i\pi(1-1/\alpha)\,\mathrm{sign}(\xi)/2}
\,d\widetilde{M}_\alpha(\xi),
\end{equation}
where the constant $C_\alpha$ is chosen so that
$\|C_\alpha \widehat{f}\|_{L^\alpha} = \|f\|_{L^\alpha}$.
Its explicit value is
