{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.740001+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f491c32267cd3ebac709f1f4df27dfc159c46785285dfac37564ec17f76a887","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 101 (Finite-cutoff reconstruction) of arXiv:2607.10813, '2607.10813'] Let $Z_\Lambda=(X_{\W,\Lambda},X_{\K,\Lambda},
Y_{\W,\Lambda},Y_{\K,\Lambda})$ solve the finite-cutoff fixed-point equation for an
adapted localization pair, and set
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 u_\Lambda^{\chi,\rho}
 &=\rho(\Psi_{\W,\Lambda}+V_{\W,\Lambda})
   +X_{\W,\Lambda}+Y_{\W,\Lambda},\\
 v_\Lambda^{\chi,\rho}
 &=\rho(\Psi_{\K,\Lambda}+V_{\K,\Lambda})
   +X_{\K,\Lambda}+Y_{\K,\Lambda}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Then, in the sense of finite-cutoff distributions,
\begin{equation}
 \chi u_\Lambda^{\chi,\rho}v_\Lambda^{\chi,\rho}
 =\chi\Theta_\Lambda
  +\mathscr F_{X,\Lambda}^{\chi,\rho}(Z_\Lambda)
  +\mathscr F_{Y,\Lambda}^{\chi,\rho}(Z_\Lambda).
\end{equation}
Consequently the reconstructed fields solve the finite-cutoff auxiliary system
\eqref{eq:cutoff-system}.  Conversely, suppose that a solution of
\eqref{eq:cutoff-system} can be written as
\[
 u_\Lambda=B_{\W,\Lambda}^\rho+R_{\W,\Lambda},
 \qquad
 v_\Lambda=B_{\K,\Lambda}^\rho+R_{\K,\Lambda},
\]
with
\[
 R_{a,\Lambda}\in C_TH^r\cap C_T^1H^{r-1}
 \quad(a\in\mathfrak C)
\]
for some $r>3/2$.  Then it determines a unique solution of the finite-cutoff fixed-point equation
through the decomposition below.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.727361+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f49cffdeadce6239a1b2af86cdeeba34128903145041d062c65d1c01aba716f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:5.2 of arXiv:2604.19294, 'On the maxima of Littlewood polynomials on $[-1,1]$'] There exists an absolute constant $C>0$ such that for all sufficiently large $N$, all $\Delta\in(0,1/2]$, and all $m,n\in[N,2N]$ with $|m-n|\leqslant\Delta N$, one has
