  We will also consider the \emph{diagonal covariance function} $\mathrm{K}(x):=\mathrm{K}(x,x)$. We will often abuse notation and call the latest also the covariance function.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.272127+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f51ea607c697517bd4511ecd5bca675435e77cbc33dddf644233ee0554455f5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 (Analytic allocation factorization) of arXiv:2609.01116, 'Vector-Carleson, Calder\'on, and Poisson--Atomic Criteria for\\ Generalized Hilbert Operat'] For a sequence $Y=(Y_j)_{j\ge0}\subset H^p$, set
\[
 P_Yb=\sum_jb_j|Y_j|^2
\]
and
\[
 \mathfrak G_p(Y)
 =\inf_{Y_j=A_jF_j}
 \|A\|_{H^\infty(\ell^t)}
 \sup_j\|F_j\|_{H^p},
 \tag{4.10}
\]
where the product is coordinatewise analytic. With equality in
$[0,\infty]$,
\[
 \boxed{\mathfrak G_p(Y)
 =\|P_Y:\ell^r\to L^r\|^{1/2}.}
 \tag{4.11}
\]
If $P_Y$ is bounded, then
\[
 \boxed{
 \inf_{Y=AF}\|A\|_{H^\infty(\ell^t)}
       \limsup_{j\to\infty}\|F_j\|_{H^p}
 =\|P_Y\|_{\mathrm e}^{1/2},}
 \tag{4.12}
\]
and $P_Y$ is compact if and only if one such factorization has
$\|F_j\|_{H^p}\to0$.  In all these infima one may require the $A_j$ to
be outer and zero-free and
\[
 \sum_j|A_j^*|^t=1\quad\text{a.e. on }\T.
 \tag{4.13}
\]
