{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.377466+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f5619371f05bf1ffa9f8d7d8f5b6fb9ef9fa3c19937c8bfb08bdd4eb6c29fa3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:Dobrushin-k=2 of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] Let $f, h:\mathbb{Z}\to \mathbb{R}$ be two finite-support functions, with $f$ additionally satisfying $\sum_{x\in \mathbb{Z}}f(x)\,\pi(x) = 0$. 
   Then, for any sequences $\beta=\beta_N\to 0$ and $N^{-1/2}\mathbf x^N \in N^{-1/2}\mathbb{Z}^2\to \mathbf z\in \mathbb{R}^2$ as $N\to \infty$,  the following triple (all coordinates being expressed in terms of the two-point gap process $X=R^1-R^2$)
   \begin{equation}
\tonde{N^{-1/2}X_{tN},N^{-1/2}\textstyle{\int_0^{tN}}h(X_s)\,{\rm d} s, N^{-1/4}\int_0^{tN}f(X_s)\,{\rm d} s}_{t\ge 0}\; \text{,}\;\;\quad\text{under}\ \mathbf   P_{\mathbf x^N}^{(\beta_N,2)}\; \text{,}\;\;
   \end{equation} weakly converges in $\ensuremath{\mathcal D}([0,\infty);\mathbb{R}^3)$ as $N\to \infty$ to 
   \begin{equation}
    \tonde{W_t,\gamma^2(h) L_t^W, \sigma(f) B_{L_t^W}}_{t\ge 0}\; \text{,}\;\;
   \end{equation}
   where the first two coordinates are given as in (equation eq:W+local-time of the paper), 
   $B$ is another Brownian motion independent from $W$ and such that $B_0=0$, and \begin{equation}
    \sigma^2(f)\coloneqq \textstyle{\sum_{x\in \mathbb{Z}}}\,\Gamma(Gf,Gf)(x)\; \text{.}
   \end{equation}
   Here, $G$ and $\Gamma$ are, respectively, the Green function and the carré-du-champ (Remark (rem:green-function-untilted of the paper)) of the untilted gap process.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.573100+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f59c143a0828441c6a5e78b520f96d0e24de0044b818a6cd1537a32de5be527","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 23 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d\geq 2$ and $\beta>0$. Let $G=(V,E)$ be a subgraph of $\mathbb Z^d$ with $0\in V$. Let $S\cap B=\emptyset$ be finite subsets of $V$, with $S$ containing $0$. Assume that $\partial S$ is separating $0$ and $B$ in the sense that every path from $0$ to $B$ has to visit $\partial S$. Then,
