{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.275335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2fdb0659fa669d1a5d0c78b6dfbdc2ba8017d4e3c66d642f4e3bc40773284c74","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.06246, 'The smallest singular value for rectangular random matrices with Lévy entries'] For fixed $\theta>0,m>0$ we define subsets $\mathbb{S}_p^{n-1}(\theta)$ and $\mathbb{S}_a^{n-1}(m)$ of $\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ via
    \begin{enumerate}
\item The set $\mathbb{S}_p^{n-1}(\theta)$ of $\theta$-peaky vectors consist of unit vectors in $\mathbb{R}^n$ that have $\ell_\infty^n$ norm at least $\theta$. \item  A vector $y\in \mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ is called $m$-sparse if $|\operatorname{supp}y|\leq m$. We then call $y\in\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ almost $m$-sparse if we can find $J\subset\{1,\cdots,n\}$ with cardinality bounded by $m$, and $\|y\chi_J\|\geq\frac{1}{2}$. We use the notation $\mathbb{S}_a^{n-1}(m)$ to denote the set of almost $m$-sparse vectors of $\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.781202+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2fde34b2d852fdab83154b409d36ccfcaece52c632157468fa2cdcc4235c87ef","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 29 (Wiener chaos, $N$-dimensional version) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] For any $n\geqs 1$, we denote by $\cH_n$ the subspace of $\cH$ 
spanned by the random variables 
\begin{equation}
 \setsuch{H_n(W(h))}{h\in \H, \norm{h}_\H = 1}\;.
\end{equation} 
For $n = 0$, $\cH_0$ is the set
of constants, which is isomorphic to $\R$. Then $\cH_n$ is called the 
\emph{homogeneous Wiener chaos of order $n$}. 
The \emph{inhomogeneous Wiener chaos of order $n$} is defined as 
\begin{equation}
 \cH_{\leqs n} = \bigoplus_{k=0}^n \cH_k\;.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

