{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.653580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3100bb886a53b2e0ca6cb4beab336637730a79a2beb0c27becc91ce1cd0f9f52","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:L-property of arXiv:2603.01823, 'on a fractional stochastic heat equation arising from the disordered pinning model'] Let $F_s$ be an adapted process  and $f(s)$ be a deterministic measurable  function. Then
\begin{align}
&\quad\mathbf{E}_{(t,x)}\left[\int_0^t F_s{\mathrm d} L_s\right]=\int_0^t \mathbf{E}_{(s,0)}\left[F_s\right]g_\rho(t-s,x){\mathrm d} \mathbf{s},
\\&\quad\mathbf{E}_{(t,x)}\left[\int_0^t f(s){\mathrm d} L_s\right]= \int_0^t f(s)g_\rho(t-s,x){\mathrm d} \mathbf{s},
\end{align}
provided the right-hand sides exist.  For a deterministic measurable symmetric function $f(s_1,\cdots,s_n)$, we have 
 \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\quad\mathbf{E}_{(t,x)}\left[\int_{[0,t]^n}f(s_1,\cdots
,s_n){\mathrm d} L_{s_1}\cdots{\mathrm d} L_{s_n}\right]\\
=&n!\int_{[0,t]_<^n}f(s_1,\cdots
,s_n)g_\rho(s_2-s_1,0)\cdots g_\rho(t-s_n,x){\mathrm d} \mathbf{s},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
provided the right-hand side exists.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:44.011545+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3100faba42c6436f184582709fdeffc9b5826de57482a2b4c59a9c38743a2988","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cycle of arXiv:2412.04579, 'Solvable Families of Random Block Tridiagonal Matrices'] Suppose that $k\ge 3$, and $z_1, \dots, z_k$ are distinct numbers. Then
\begin{align}
\sum^*_{\sigma} \prod_{i=1}^k \frac{1}{z_i-z_{\sigma(i)}}=0,
\end{align}
where the sum is over all cycles $\sigma$ supported on $\{1,2,\dots,k\}$. We also have
\begin{align}
 \sum_{\sigma\in S_k} \prod_{i=1}^k \frac{1}{z_{\sigma(i)}-z_{\sigma(i+1)}}=0,
\end{align}
where $\sigma(k+1)=\sigma(1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:24:45.928280+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_31016c1e05c7e75d6c41ba241ee1463f68463896a4d0a7a024395858331ed343","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma hermitegenerating of arXiv:2509.07372, 'On the edge eigenvalues of sparse random geometric graphs'] For $\mathbf{x},\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^d$, we have that
