		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.757828+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3102b3f2af5c43d1603f5ceb10552776ff1f6d0b072b438353ff3b87fbf9aa67","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_regular_estimate_localized_eigenvector of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Under the assumptions of (theorem_localized_eigenvector_localization of the paper), the following bounds hold for any $a\in [\![{D}]\!]$ and $\mathsf M_0\in\ha{M_{\text{\rm{sc}}}(z_0),M_{\text{\rm{sc}}}^*(z_0)}$, $\mathsf M_1\in\left\{{M(z_1),M^*(z_1)}\right\}$:   \begin{align}
\Delta_{\text{\rm{ev}}}(z_1)&=\mathrm{O}\left({\left\langle \Lambda^2 \right\rangle}\right),\\
\langle \mathsf M_0{\widetilde{\Lambda}}\mathsf M_1E_a \rangle&=\im m(z_1)\cdot \mathrm{O}\left({\left\langle \Lambda^2 \right\rangle}\right) , 
\end{align}
where $z_0$ and $z_1$ are defined in (equation eq:z1 of the paper) and (equation definition_shift_eigenvector of the paper), respectively, and ${\widetilde{\Lambda}}$ is defined as ${\widetilde{\Lambda}}:=\Lambda-\Delta_{\text{\rm{ev}}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.641782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3109eb8a6c0ee698d1ef4527495a1319a4b134d0bfeaeb4b7a3ce42655e74ab8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Let $A$ be a $C^*$-algebra, coming with a trace $tr:A\to\mathbb C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The elements $a\in A$ are called noncommutative random variables.

\item The moments of such a variable are the numbers $M_k(a)=tr(a^k)$.

\item The law of such a variable is the functional $\mu_a:P\to tr(P(a))$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.955806+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310c30987665be9d3590757ffd72d811335c24caab51040712d930e5d834f864","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Knowntheorem 26 (G. Pisier 1986 \cite{Pis}) of arXiv:2604.09365, 'Cotlar martingale transforms and related singular integrals'] Let  $1<p<\infty$ and $q$ be its conjugate exponent. For a standard normal random variable $X$, i.e., $X\sim N(0, 1)$,  we set
 $\gamma(p)=\|X\|_p$. Then, 
