{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.614242+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310d98dde1d500ecaf3b1fd7e4a9f9c21839394e395ab42445e9e0f9f4e30923","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] Fix $[s,t]\subseteq \RR$. Almost surely, for every $r\in \cT_{\0}^{\n,[s,t]}$, exactly one of the following hold.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item $\Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r}=\Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r^-}$.
  \item $\overline{\Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r}\setminus \Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r^-}}$ is an excursion about $\Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r^-}$. 
  \item There exist points $p_1=(x_1,t_1), p_2=(x_2,t_2), p_3 = (x_3,t_3), p_4=(x_4,t_4) \in \Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r^-}\cap \ZZ_\RR$ such that $t_1<t_2=t_3<t_4$ and $\overline{\Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r}\setminus \Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r^-}}$ is precisely the union of two excursions about $\Gamma_{\0}^{\n,r^-}$, one being a staircase from $p_1$ to $p_2$ and one being a staircase from $p_3$ to $p_4$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.587495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310dccc45ad15af6b9fb10a2f70c0c8e77c978f1365573c946f086bf882fe2f7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem finite_energy_approx of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] For $\mathsf{P}\in  \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ with  $\mathcal F_{\beta}({P})<\infty$  there exists a sequence $\mathsf{P}^n\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ with $\mathcal{W}^{int}(\mathsf{P}^n)\leq \mathcal{W}^{int}(\mathsf{P})+1/n$, $\mathcal E(\mathsf{P}^n)\leq \mathcal E(\mathsf{P})+1/n$ and 
    \begin{align}
        \int \sum_{x\in \xi\cap \Lambda_1}\left\lvertx^+-x\right\rvert^p\mathsf{P}^n(d\xi)<\infty.
    \end{align}
    Furthermore, $\mathsf{P}^n\xrightarrow{n\to \infty}\mathsf{P}$  w.r.t. $\mathcal T_{\mathcal L}$ (in particular weakly) and for $n\in \IN$ there exists $q_n>0$  such that the measure $\mathsf{P}^n$ is concentrated on the set \begin{align}
        \Gamma_{q_n}=\{\xi\in \Gamma: \varphi(x-y)\leq q_n,1/q_n\leq \left\lvertx-y\right\rvert \text{ for any two distinct }x,y\in \xi \}.
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.192735+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310e8ca19634cf90b7391277ea05abfbcf0daaf20b6a75aa650d4d9629f58550","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Blockage.}

    For $\landsc$ a landscape and $(x, y)\in \Half$ a point, we call (first) blocking building of $(x, y)$ in $\landsc$ the following quantity when it is well defined:
    \[\tau(x, y, \landsc) = (x_b, y_b) := \underset{\substack{(a, b) \in \landsc \\a>x}}{\text{argmax}} \frac{b-y}{a-x}.\]
    When the blocking building of $(x, y)$ is well defined, we further introduce its blockage angle as:
    \[\theta(x, y, \landsc): = \arctan\left(\frac{y_b - y}{x_b-x}\right).\]
    In the following, when the involved landscape is unequivocal, we will simply write $\tau(x, y)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.345488+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310e9c812e3a2ed30299b8dd1ce6dda790f6b3ff95f5f84b8dd905179fe3974e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Let $ f, g \in L^{2}_{\mathbb{F}}(0, T; H) $ and $ a, b \in L^{2}_{\mathcal{F}_{s}} (\Omega) $, $ 0 \leq s  < t \leq T $. Then 
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $ \displaystyle \int_s^t f d W \in L_{\mathbb{F}}^2(\Omega ; C([s, t] ; H)) $;
        \item $ \displaystyle \int_s^t(a f+b g) d W=a \int_s^t f d W+b \int_s^t g d W, \text { a.s. } $;
        \item $ \displaystyle \mathbb{E}\Big(\int_s^t f d W\Big)=0 $;
        \item $ \displaystyle \mathbb{E}\Big(\Big\langle\int_s^t f d W, \int_s^t g d W\Big\rangle_H\Big)=\mathbb{E}\Big(\int_s^t\langle f(\tau, \cdot), g(\tau, \cdot)\rangle_H d \tau\Big) $.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.680583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310f2bd90df4238bf72b825b31e229f1bbc3ccb2b2d7ec8f3aca46a5d5c70941","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:conv_lip_pert of arXiv:2412.09235, 'A semiconcavity approach to stability of entropic plans and exponential convergence of Sin'] Let $\pi=e^{-U}$, $\alpha>0,L,R\geq0$ be such that
\begin{equation}
\nabla^2 U(x)\succeq 
\begin{cases} 
-L, \quad & {|x|< R}\,, \\ 
\alpha, \quad & |x|\geq R\,.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Moreover, let $f:\mathbb{R}^d\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^d$ be Lipschitz . Then we have
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{Cov}_{X\sim\pi}(f(X)) \preceq 2\mathrm{Lip}(f)^2 \big(\alpha^{-1}+\alpha^{-2}(L+\alpha)^2R^2\big)\,.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.635544+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310fc48c3a56d4eaf072bb3eda989b6188481690248a970ab48fec075178ae6b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: asymp of dimension ratio with r K f of arXiv:2605.28770, 'Cutoff profiles for conjugacy invariant random walks on symmetric groups'] Let $r,K,f$ be three integers such that $0\leq K \leq r\leq f/2$. As $n\to \infty$, uniformly over all $\lambda \vdash n$ such that $r(\lambda)= r$ and all $\mu \vdash f$ such that $\mu \subset \lambda$ and $r(\mu) = r - K$, we have
    \begin{equation}
     \frac{d_{\mu}}{d_\lambda} \asymp_r \left(\frac{f}{n} \right)^r f^{-K};
    \end{equation}
and moreover, if $K=0$ (that is, if $\mu^* = \lambda^*$), we have, denoting $k = n-f$, 
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d_{\mu}}{d_\lambda} = \left( \frac{f}{n} \right)^r \left( 1 - T(r)\frac{k}{fn} + O_r\left( \frac{1}{f^2}\right) \right).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.865147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_311796865aecca4a703837fd98f1b65fa259b2cd4217f0ed36535b919e88f7ca","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 9 of arXiv:2512.00213, 'On strong sharp phase transition in the random connection model'] Let $\mu\in\bN_{<\infty}$ and $n\in\N_0$. Then
\begin{align*}
V^\mu_n(\xi^\mu)&\le_{st} \int V_n^x(\xi_x) \, \mu(d x),\\
