{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:30:07.957790+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_31adbbc9ad65c19fd6f3cb94db3836f4f54c40a783dbd2d46bffd84716db9b24","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:shape-concate of arXiv:2608.12415, 'Sharp Phase Transition for Ellipsoid Fitting'] Given shapes $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ that are composable, we take the concatenation $\tau_1 \circ \tau_2$ of $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ to be the shape such that:
\begin{enumerate}
		\item $V(\tau_1 \circ \tau_2) = V(\tau_1) \cup V(\tau_2)$ where the vertices in $V_{\tau_1}$ are identified with the vertices in $U_{\tau_2}$.
        \item $U_{\tau_1 \circ \tau_2} = U_{\tau_1}$ and $V_{\tau_1 \circ \tau_2} = V_{\tau_2}$.
		\item $E(\tau_1 \circ \tau_2) = E(\tau_1) \cup E(\tau_2)$. 
\end{enumerate}


This definition extends naturally to a sequence of $k$ shapes $\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_k$ such that for each $j \in [k-1]$, $|V_{\tau_j}| = |U_{\tau_{j+1}}|$. We call this a $k$-way (or $k$-fold) concatenation.  We can obtain the concatenation $\tau_1 \circ \cdots \circ \tau_k$ by concatenating these shapes together one by one (it is not hard to check that the order of the concatenations does not matter).","status":"unverified"}

