{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.832271+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_31de7f6c2cfd0eb1d4833514b3470f2b0c509311dfa7156ea1576c651b57acdf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Averaged mass of the SHF on balls, upper bound) of arXiv:2608.13359, 'Fractional moments of the Stochastic Heat Flow and\\2D Directed Polymers'] There exist constants \(C,c>0\) such that, for any \(p \in (0,1)\), uniformly in \(t>0\), \(\th\in \R\) and \(r \in (0,\sqrt{t}]\), we have
  \begin{equation}

    \EE\bigl[\mathscr{Z}_{t}^{\th}(\cU_r)^{p}\bigr]
    \leq C\, \EE\bigl[\mathscr{Z}_{t}^{\th}(\cU_r)^{2}\bigr]^{c\, p (p-1)} \,.
  \end{equation}
  In fact, we  have a more explicit upper bound: there exist constants \(C',c'>0\) such that, for any \(p \in (0,1)\), uniformly in \(t>0\), \(\th\in \R\) and \(r \in (0,\sqrt{t}]\), we have
  \begin{equation}

    \EE\bigl[\mathscr{Z}_{t}^{\th}(\cU_r)^{p}\bigr]
    \leq C' \Bigl(1+ \log\Bigl(1+\frac{t}{r^2}\Bigr)\,\cV\bigl(t \,\e^{\th}\bigr) \Bigr)^{c'\, p (p-1)}\,,
  \end{equation}
  where \(\cV(T) \coloneqq \int_0^{\infty} \frac{T^s}{\Gamma(s+1)} \dd s\) is the so-called Volterra function (see~\eqref{eq:asymp-V} for its asymptotics as \(T\) goes to \(0\) or~\(\infty\)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

