{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.421190+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_320e07ac6c448ec36ca3f5704713a15d5f77a90f2077e2dfa408e2596e11019e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2607.28215, 'Almost stochastic dominance via optimal transport'] Assume that $\gamma\in [0,1]$, $X\le_{\gamma,g} Y$ for $X,Y\in \mathcal{L}_{c,g}(E)$ with $\E[g(X)]< \E[g(Y)]$, and that there exists a metric $d$ on $E$ such that $c\le d$ and
\[
|\E[g(\hat X)]-\E[g(\hat Y)]|\le \mathrm{OT}_d(\hat X,\hat Y)\quad \text{ for all } \hat X,\hat Y\in \mathcal{L}_g(E).
\]
If $\mathrm{OT}_d(Y,\tilde Y)\le \epsilon \le \E[g(Y)]-\E[g(X)]$ for some $\tilde{Y}\in \mathcal{L}_{c,g}(E)$, then we have
\begin{align*}
\gamma^\ast(X,\tilde Y;g)\le  \tilde{\gamma} :=  \gamma+\frac{(1-\gamma)\epsilon}{\E[g(Y)]-\E[g(X)]}.
\end{align*}
Analogously, if $\mathrm{OT}_d(X,\tilde X)\le \epsilon \le \E[g(Y)]-\E[g(X)]$ for some $\tilde X\in \mathcal{L}_{c,g}(E)$, then we have $\gamma^\ast(\tilde X, Y;g)\le  \tilde{\gamma}$ for the same $\tilde{\gamma}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.669588+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3210c86c943b2fe320f8796a6aa689bcea54c08e985c828cfe14eb7c4ac75ea7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] A continuous map $f: X \longrightarrow Y$ is \emph{stratified} if it satisfies the following two conditions:
   	\begin{enumerate}
   		\item For any connected component $S$ of any stratum $Y_i \setminus Y_{i-1}$, the set $f^{-1}(S)$ is a union of connected components of strata of $X$.
   		\item For each point $p \in Y_i \setminus Y_{i-1}$ there exists a neighborhood $N$ of $p$ in $Y_i$, a topologically stratified space $F = F_k \supset F_{k-1} \supset \cdots \supset F_{-1} = \emptyset$,
   		and a stratum-preserving homeomorphism $F \times N \to f^{-1}(N)$ which commutes with the projection to $N$.
   	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.501367+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32113d8d08d95c16a096c0c965e8590c684015a588aade1dc5e7edd70fd54d8a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] Let $ R>0$ fixed.
 Say that a  $ L^{ 2}_{ \text{\rm{loc}}}$  random measure $ ~\mathsf{M}$ is $ k$-rigid on $ B\subset \mathbb{R}^{ d}$ for $ k\in \mathbb{N}$ iff
\begin{align*}
    {\mathfrak m}  _{ k}(\mathsf{M}1_{ B})

\in \sigma (\mathsf{M}1_{ B}^{ c}).
\end{align*}
Say that it is furthermore {\it linearly} $ k$-rigid on $ B$ if for each   $ {  \bf k}\in \mathbb{N}^{ d}$ such that  $ \sum_{i}k_{ i}\leqslant k$,  some kernels $ h_{ n}\in\mathscr C_{ c}^{ \infty }(B ^{ c})$ give the $ L^{ 2}(\mathbf P )$-approximation  \begin{align*}
\mathsf{M}(h_{ n})\xrightarrow[ n\to \infty ]{} \displaystyle\int_{B}x^{ {  \bf k}}d\mathsf{M} .
