{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:14.675068+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_325d50e8dea092feabcee799fe4682f57766e2fcb233e390d3e6412d73efd183","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2607.11275, 'The limit law of the maximum of discrete partial-sums distribution II'] If $\mathbb{E}S_N<0$, then the distribution functions $F_{\infty}^{(1)},\,F_{\infty}^{(2)},\,\ldots,\,F_{\infty}^{(N)}$ satisfy
\begin{align*}
F_\infty^{(j)}(n)
=
\sum_{r=1}^{N}
\sum_{\ell=m_r}^{-1}
a_{n-\ell}^{I_{jr}}
\sum_{i=0}^{\ell-m_r}
\pi_i^{(r+1)}
F_{X_r}(\ell-i),
\qquad
j=1,\,2,\,\ldots,\,N,\quad n\in\mathbb{N}_0,
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
I_{jr}:=
\begin{cases}
(j,j+1,\ldots,r-1), & j<r,\\
\varnothing, & j=r,\\
(1,\,\ldots,\,r-1,\,j,\,j+1,\,\ldots,\,N), & j>r,
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
a_n^{I_{jr}}=
\begin{cases}
\sum\limits_{k=\mu_{jr}}^{n+D}
p_k^{I_{jr}}
a_{n-k}, & n\geqslant \mu_{jr}-D, \\
0, & n < \mu_{jr}-D,
\end{cases}
\qquad
\mu_{jr}=\sum_{t\in I_{jr}}m_t,
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
p_k^{I_{jr}}
=
\mathbb{P}\!\left(
\sum_{t\in I_{jr}} X_t = k
\right),
\qquad
k\in\mathbb{Z},
\end{align*}
with the convention that, if $I_{jr}=\varnothing$, then
\begin{align*}
a_n^{\varnothing}=a_n,\qquad
\mu_{jr}=0,
\qquad
p_k^{\varnothing}
=
\begin{cases}
1, & k=0,\\
0, & k\neq 0,
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
a_{-D}=\frac{1}{f_N(D)}, \quad a_{n}=\frac{1}{f_N(D)}\left({\pmb 1}_{\{n\geqslant-2D\}}a_{n+D}-\sum_{k=1}^{n+D}f_N(k+D)a_{n-k}\right),\quad n \geqslant -D+1,
\end{align*}
and $\pi^{(N+1)}=\pi^{(1)}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.830039+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_325e37543c513adb27e1ab2b550b0825b83c2155df9f0263c8ba80c1721fe2ae","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] Let $\gamma$ be a Gaussian measure on $(E,\mathscr{B}(E))$. We define the mean $a_{\gamma}$, and the covariance $B_{\gamma}$ of $\gamma$ by
\begin{align}
&a_{\gamma}(f)\coloneqq \displaystyle\int_{E}f(x)\gamma({\rm d} x), \hskip 0,1cm f\in E^{\ast},\\
& B_{\gamma}(f,g)\coloneqq \displaystyle\int_{E}\left[ f(x)-a_{\gamma}(f)\right]\left[g(x)-a_{\gamma}(g)\right]\gamma({\rm d} x), \hskip 0,1cm f,g\in E^{\ast}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.085523+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_325fba973e0b18275a3a4a4971fe93440e3eddd52e8357c968772f40ce52613f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition e-family of arXiv:2412.08400, '\vspace{-1.0cm} Characterization of Exponential Families of \\ Lumpable Stochastic Matrice'] We say that the parametric family of irreducible stochastic matrices $$\mathcal{V}_e = \left\{ P_\theta \colon \theta = (\theta^1, \dots, \theta^d) \in \mathbb{R}^d \right\} \subset \mathcal{W}( \mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}),$$ 
forms an exponential family (e-family) of stochastic matrices with natural parameter $\theta$ and dimension $d$, when
there exist a function $K \in \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ and $d$ linearly independent functions\footnote{Although $G_1, \dots, G_d$ are technically cosets, we treat them as coset representatives in $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$.} $G_1, \dots, G_d \in \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$, 
such that
    \begin{equation*}
