{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.280234+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_327ce21ef4f7d90469a05fdfc8f268b769cc2492801e7c16c9f2a2e879d8d37b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemme allomqutre of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] Under the allometric setting, under Assumptions~(hyp:gainenergy of the paper) and (hyp:probamortel of the paper), suppose that the weight function $\omega$ has an \hyperlink{alloform}{allometric form} with $0 \leq \kappa_{1} \leq \kappa_{2}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] If $ \delta < -1$, then Assumption~(hyp:poidsomega of the paper) is equivalent to 
$$\kappa_{1}=\kappa_{2}= -\delta, \quad \alpha \in [0,1]\quad \mathrm{and} \quad \beta \leq 2+ \delta;$$
\item[-] If $-1 \leq \delta \leq 0 $, then Assumption~(hyp:poidsomega of the paper) is equivalent to 
$$ - \delta \leq \kappa_{1} \leq \kappa_{2} \leq \dfrac{1-\delta}{2} \leq 1, \quad \alpha \in [0,1], \quad \mathrm{and} \quad \beta \leq 1.$$
\item[-] If $\delta > 0 $, then Assumption~(hyp:poidsomega of the paper) cannot be verified.
\end{itemize}
These constraints on the allometic coefficients are illustrated on Figure~(fig:contraitnesallomdeux of the paper) and Figure~(fig:contraitnesallomtrois of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.436785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_327d1438bf05fb8f509ca631ce618dcbfa314a9b7bd1f8c9e67764e4d39601e9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2606.15178, 'The distribution of the de Moivre experiment'] For integers $ n\geq0 $, $ k\geq0 $ and $ s\geq0 $, the extended Eulerian number is defined by
	\begin{equation}
		A(n,k,s) = \sum _{j=0}^k (-1)^{j} \binom{n+1}{j} (k+1-j-s)^n, \qquad \ 0\leq k\leq n. 
	\end{equation}	
	For $ s=0 $ the summand reduces to that of \eqref{eq19}, so we recover the classical Eulerian numbers on their support, $ A(n,k,0)=A(n,k) $ for $ 0\leq k\leq n-1 $, with the additional value $ A(n,n,0)=0 $. Note that, contrary to the classical Eulerian numbers, the numbers $A(n,k,s)$ are not positive in general for $s\geq1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.791013+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_327db47af2d1df4e7f819a9394e709f0685862e8f5a1706834d4168e670f0bef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Large deviation principle for the un-normalised mass function) of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] The un-normalised mass function of the unconstrained model $\overline{\Pi}_{N}$ on $ \mathcal{M}_1(\mathbb{R})$
obeys a large deviation principle  at speed $N$ and rate function $I$. That is, for any open set $O \subset \mathcal{M}_1(\mathbb{R})$,

\begin{align}

\liminf_{N \to +\infty} \frac{1}{N}\ln \overline{\Pi}_{N}[O] \geq - \inf_{\mu \in O}I[\mu]

\end{align}

and, for any closed set $F \subset \mathcal{M}_1(\mathbb{R})$,

\begin{align}

\limsup_{N \to +\infty} \frac{1}{N}\ln \overline{\Pi}_{N}[F] \leq - \inf_{\mu \in F}I[\mu] \, .

\end{align}

Likewise the un-normalised mass function of the constrained model $\overline{\Pi}_{N,\mathsf{c}}$ on  $\mathcal{M}_{1,\mathsf{c}}(\mathbb{R})$
also obeys a large deviation principle at speed $N$ and rate function $I$. That is, for any open set $O \subset \mathcal{M}_{1,\mathsf{c}}(\mathbb{R})$,
\begin{align}
    \liminf_{N \to +\infty} \frac{1}{N}\ln \overline{\Pi}_{N,\mathsf{c}}[O] \geq - \inf_{\mu \in O}I[\mu]
\end{align}
and, for any closed set $F \subset \mathcal{M}_{1,\mathsf{c}}(\mathbb{R})$,
\begin{align}
    \limsup_{N \to +\infty} \frac{1}{N}\ln \overline{\Pi}_{N,\mathsf{c}}[F] \leq - \inf_{\mu \in F}I[\mu] \, .
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.088066+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_327ec5fde67ff912c04cbc9b1715819b125fbe604d683c654da05e96a8ebcbed","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2604.25769, 'The conformal dimension of the Brownian tree is one'] Set $\eta \defeq \alpha^{100}$. Then there is a universal constant $C > 0$ such that for each $p \in (1, 2)$, 
