    + \sqrt{2t m\sigma^2_{\mathcal H_m}}\sqrt{\frac{m}{N}} +C\left(\frac{1}{\varepsilon}+\sqrt{\frac{Vm^2\log(m)}{N}}\right)\frac{tm}{N}
    +\frac{CV}{N}\log^{3/2}(m)
\end{multline*}
with probability at least $1-2e^{-t}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.954231+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32b2567222d4877b9cc43972644db21485534057d4192a0c92081cf42bb5713c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2606.25494, 'A functional central limit theorem for kernel gradient flow and infinitesimal gradient boo'] For fixed hyperparameters $d\geq 1$ (tree depth), $K\geq 1$ (number of proposals at each step), $\beta\geq 0$ (softmax selection parameter) and $\alpha >0$ (beta distribution parameter for proposal) and with the sample $(x_i,y_i)_{1\leq i\leq n}$ and a predictor $F\in\mathcal{F}$ as input, we define:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\xi = (\xi_v)_{v\in \mathscr{T}_{d-1}}$ the (random) splitting scheme encoding the successive splits, with distribution $Q_{n,F}$;
\item $(A_v^\xi)_{v\in \{0,1\}^d}$ the partition of $[0,1]^p$ resulting from the splitting scheme $\xi$;
\item $T_n(\,\cdot\,;F,\xi)$ the corresponding softmax gradient tree, as defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:def-gradient-tree}.
\end{itemize}
In the totally random tree model (i.e., when $K=1$ or $\beta=0$), the distribution of the splitting scheme does not depend on the sample $(x_i,y_i)_{1\le i\le n}$ nor on $F$, and is denoted by $Q_0$.","status":"unverified"}

