{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.578375+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32f643640d5825691e6dbdd15416dd71862b55486721e9b4093b82514aaec204","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition d:RegularPotential of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] We say that~$\sigma\colon {\mathbb R}^+\to {\mathbb R}$ is a \emph{regular pair potential} if~$\sigma$ is piecewise of class~$\mcC^1$ and~(equation eq:AssSmallScaleInt of the paper) simultaneously holds for~$g=\sigma$ \emph{and}~$g=-\sigma'$.

For any regular pair potential~$\sigma\colon {\mathbb R}^+\to {\mathbb R}$ we call (\emph{repulsive}) \emph{$\sigma$-interaction energy} the function
\begin{equation}
W_\sigma\colon \mbfT\times\mbfM_\circ\longrightarrow\overline{\mathbb R}\,\mathrm{,}\;\, \qquad W_\sigma\colon (\mbfs,\mbfx)\longmapsto \tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{\substack{i,j\\i\neq j}} s_i\, s_j \, \sigma\big({\mssd(x_i,x_j)}\big) \,\mathrm{,}\;\,
\end{equation}
and \emph{attractive $\sigma$-interaction energy} the function~$-W_\sigma$.","status":"unverified"}

