{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.343628+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32f7d5288e6828afc8f89029444f89c7dc2cd87deb60fe8542919365480b570a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Satz thm:LSICone of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] For any real $p \ge 2$, $\mu_{p,n}$ satisfies an $\mathrm{LS}_q$-inequality with constant of order $n^{-1/(p-1)}$. More precisely, for any smooth enough $f \colon {\mathbb S}_p^{n-1} \to {\mathbb R}$, it holds that
	\begin{align*}
		\mathrm{Ent}_{\mu_{p,n}}(|f|^q) &\le 4^q q^{q-1} \frac {\Gamma(\frac{n-q}{p})}{p^{q/p}\Gamma(\frac n p)} \int_{{\mathbb S}_p^{n-1}} \lvert \nabla_{S} f(\theta) \rvert_q^q d \mu_{p,n}\\
		&\le 3 \cdot 4^q q^{q-1} n^{-\frac{1}{p-1}} \int_{{\mathbb S}_p^{n-1}} \lvert \nabla_S f(\theta) \rvert_q^q d \mu_{p,n},
	\end{align*}
	where the second inequality is valid for any $n \ge 3$. The same inequalities remain true if we assume $f$ to be defined and smooth in a neighbourhood of ${\mathbb S}_p^{n-1}$ and replace $\nabla_S f$ by the usual (non-intrinsic) gradient $\nabla f$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:27.995020+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32f8e726e7614de9bcca3dc665b4138c29fac3d625b0aade86eee833c106251e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:strong_rec of arXiv:2608.07042, 'Limit Points of Reflow with Minibatch Optimal Transport'] We call a coupling $\gamma$ with velocity field $v_t\in\argmin_{w_t\in L^2(\mu_t)} \mathcal L(w_t|\gamma)$ rectifiable if the flow ODE (equation eq:flow_ode of the paper)
has a unique solution for $\mu_0$-almost every $x$.
For a rectifiable coupling $\gamma$, we call the coupling $\tilde \gamma$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\int \psi(x,y)\mathrm{d}\tilde \gamma(x,y)=\int \psi(x,\Phi_1(x)) \mathrm{d} \mu_0(x)\quad\text{for all}\quad \psi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbb{R}^d),
\end{equation}
the strong rectified coupling of $\gamma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.528296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32fc94edf5a286e236062ce48dc05dac3a8b81956d50bbb4d6bfaa68b25fafb8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2609.02610, 'The frame set of the first Hermite function'] Let $a,b \in (0, \infty)$. Then, 
