{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.355336+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_334f06f374ef3715ed23af2e3e58e6cb06fd2fc5c4fe2b9b7125086d324e671d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:sum-prob of arXiv:2608.23554, 'Provably adaptive sampling with uniform and remasking \\ discrete diffusion models'] Consider the uniform or remasking process. For \(i \in [d]\), \(b \in \cV\), \(c\) in the support of \(X_0^i\), and \(t \geq 0\), we have
    \begin{equation}

        \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_0^i = c) = \cF(t, b).
    \end{equation}
    Consequently, for  \(i \in [d]\), \(b \in \cV\), \(x \in \cV^d\), and \(t, u \geq 0\), we have
    \begin{equation}

        \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_{u}^{-i} = x^{-i}) = \cF(t, b),   
    \end{equation} 
    as long as \(\Pr(X_u^{-i} = x^{-i}) > 0\). Furthermore, the same holds for \(\widehat \Pr(\cdot):\)
    \begin{equation*}
        \cF(t, b) = \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \widehat \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_0^i = c) = \sum_{a\in\cV} \cQ(a, b) \widehat \Pr(X_t^i = a \mid X_{u}^{-i} = x^{-i}).
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

