{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.782453+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_33d31236e320212f51b03452235a2c93c29fc8beab6785445cb2836b2297d138","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:informal-good-block of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] [Informal version of Definition~(def:Dtilde of the paper)]
	A block $B$ at scale $k+1$ is called good (the complement of bad), if the following hold: 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \emph{Local coupling}: If $V_t^{R}$ denotes Glauber dynamics on $R$, a box surrounding a connected set of scale-$k$ bad blocks with all-$+$ boundary conditions, then $V_t^R$ mixes (couples all initializations) in the time $[T_k,T_{k+1}]$;
		\item \emph{Coupling local and global Markov chains using buffers}: There is a buffer annulus $\mathcal A_R$ separating the boundary of $R$ from the bad blocks internal to it, such that $V_t^R(\mathcal A_R) = X_t^\pi(\mathcal A_R)$ for all $t\in [T_k,T_{k+1}]$;
		\item \emph{Linear speed of information propagation}: There does not exist  $v_1,...,v_N\in B$ for $N \ge \ell_{k+1}$ such that $v_i \sim v_{i+1}$ and their clocks ring at increasing times $t_{i_1}<...<t_{i_N} \in [T_k,T_{k+1}]$.
	\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.771282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_33d327a1864f9ac645898b1fd5fb5fed11f29059e4622030b791ac59b70e01dc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: thinning application of arXiv:2411.02917, '\hspace*{2mm}\\[-16mm] Stein's Method for Spatial Random Graphs'] For $\mathcal{X} \subset \R^d$ compact, let $\Xi$ be a point process on $\mathcal{X}_{1/q} = \tau_q^{-1}(\mathcal{X})$ fulfilling Condition~(equation eq:condsigma of the paper). Choose $r_* \geq 0$ such that $\Prob(R \geq r_*) > 0$ (to rule out a trivial case) and denote  by $P_{r_*,q} \otimes Q_{r_*,q}$ the distribution of the percolation graph $(\Xi_{r_*,q}, \Sigma_{r_*,q})$ of the Boolean model based on $\hat{R}_i$ after thinning and rescaling. \\
Let $\lambda > 0$ and $t = q^{1/d} \hat{r}_*$. Furthermore, denote by $P^\lambda$ the homogeneous Poisson process distribution with intensity $\lambda$ and set $\kappa_{t}(x,y) = \1_{\{\norm{x-y} \leq 2t\}}$. \\
Then we have
\begin{align*}
    W_{\mathbb{G},i} \bigl(P_{r_*,q} \otimes Q_{r_*,q} \hspace*{1.5pt} , \, P^{\lambda} \otimes Q^{\kappa_{t}}\bigr) 
    &\leq c_V(\lambda)\, \E\bigl(\bignorm{\lambda\,\text{Leb}^{d}(\diff x) - p(r_*) \hspace*{1.5pt} \E \bigl( L(\tau_{q}^{-1}(\cdot) \,|\, \Xi\tau_q^{-1}) \bigm| \Xi_{r_*,q} \bigr) }_{\text{TV}} \bigr) \\[0.5mm]
    &\hspace{6mm}  {}+c_E(\lambda) \, \lambda\, c_d\, p(r_*) \, q \, \E\Xi(\mathcal{X}_{1/q}) \,
    d \, 2^d \E\bigl[(\hat{R}-\hat{r}_*) \hat{R}^{d-1} \bigm| R \geq r_* \bigr],
\end{align*}
where $c_d = \frac{\pi^{d/2}}{\Gamma(d/2 +1)}$ is the volume of the unit ball in $\R^d$ and $c_V(\lambda)$, $c_E(\lambda)$ are defined in Equations~(equation eq: SteinPoissonV of the paper) and~(equation eq: SteinPoissonE of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:52.239487+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_33dac9529ac4a55872d23b4867b75b462bc60e6eee9ca8e6a103629339200915","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2512.10761, 'Special times for the point set process of the Brownian Net'] For any point $z\in\R^2$, we say it is a
   \begin{itemize}
       \item $C_n$ point if there is an incoming path $\pi\in\mathcal{N}$ at $z$ but no incoming path $\pi\in\mathcal{W}^l\cup\mathcal{W}^r$ at $z$,
       \item $C_l$ point if there is an incoming path $l\in\mathcal{W}^l$ at $z$ but no incoming path $r\in\mathcal{W}^r$ at $z$,
       \item $C_r$ point if there is an incoming path $r\in\mathcal{W}^r$ at $z$ but no incoming path $l\in\mathcal{W}^l$ at $z$.
   \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.501798+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_33dcc28a37fc8bffa929d7827b4f3b25f471064e19218ee498dc55ac48f3c07b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2605.30030, 'Critical exponents for planar random-cluster model with cluster-weight~$q=4$'] Fix~$k \ge 1$. For any~$\eps \geq \delta > 0$ and~$x,y \in \bbR^2$ with~$|x|/16 \geq |y| \geq 4\eps$,
