{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.856564+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_33f4d66a8cf4303c82aeed515479e671b1e9f4df98d8477bf872fea9c9069801","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2504.04860, 'Stochastic differential equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: dependence on the '] Let $\alpha \in (0,\frac{1}{2})$ and $g\in W_2^{1-\alpha}([0,T];\mathbb{R}^m)$. Moreover, let $\mu \in C_b^{3}(\mathbb{R}^d;\mathbb{R}^d)$ and $\sigma \in C_b^{3}(\mathbb{R}^d;\mathbb{R}^{d \times m})$. Denote by $x \in W^{\alpha}_1([0,T];\mathbb{R}^d)$ the solution of 
 	\begin{align*}
 		x_t = x_0 + \int_0^t \mu(x_s) \,\mathrm{d} s + \int_0^t \sigma(x_s) \,\mathrm{d} g_s, \qquad t \in [0,T].
 	\end{align*}
 	The mapping $$\Gamma \colon W^{1-\alpha}_2([0,T];\mathbb{R}^m) \to W^\alpha_1([0,T];\mathbb{R}^d), \,\, \ g \mapsto x(g),$$ is Fréchet differentiable.
 	For $h \in W^{1-\alpha}_2([0,T];\mathbb{R}^m)$ its derivative is given by
 	\begin{align*}
