{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.851955+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_343e033ffd28606b3404fc95e2342f1a0b8c276b750648e749745104a1ded187","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Bernoulli of arXiv:2607.10545, 'On a conjecture of Lamkin and Tkocz: Log-convexity of moments of Bernoulli sample means'] Let $p \geq 1$ and $\theta \in [0,1]$, and let $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ be i.i.d.\ $\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\theta)$ random variables. Define the sample mean by $\bar X_n = n^{-1}(X_1 + \cdots + X_n)$. For $n \in \mathbb{N}$, define
\begin{equation}
b_n(p,\theta) \vcentcolon= \mathsf{E}(\bar X_n^p) = \sum_{k = 0}^n \binom{n}{k}\left(\frac{k}{n}\right)^p\theta^k(1 - \theta)^{n - k}.
\end{equation}
Then the sequence $\{b_n(p,\theta)\}_{n \geq 1}$ is log-convex. Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
b_n(p,\theta)^2 \leq b_{n - 1}(p,\theta)b_{n + 1}(p,\theta), \qquad n \geq 2.
\end{equation}
If $p > 1$ and $0 < \theta < 1$, then every inequality in (equation eq:main.log.convexity of the paper) is strict. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.934677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3441f6a72ea8ac105b6801782a763be8b1964ace1c12ff8e9e4bfd024ab753cc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 (Kirchhoff's formula for infinite graphs) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Suppose $(G, \weight)$ is an infinite connected weighted graph and let $\bP^\weight_G$ be the law of the (wired) USF, then
