{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.471415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_344d80a2f6f40d26b4daeaa183aa3c9f234f8323687a475f512fe978433742b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary reduction corank 1 of arXiv:2608.28278, 'Reduction of symmetric time-dependent Hamiltonian systems I: presymplectic principal $\R$-'] Let $(V^*\Pi, \Omega_h, V^*\phi, J^V)$ be the symmetric presymplectic Hamiltonian system given in Proposition (prop:J^V momentum map of the paper). Moreover, let $\nu\in \mathfrak{g}^*$ such that $J^{-1}(\nu) \neq \emptyset$ and suppose that $\phi$ is free and the space of orbits $(V^*\Pi)_\nu := (J^V)^{-1}(\nu)/G_\nu$ is a manifold such that the canonical projection $\pi_\nu^V: (J^V)^{-1}(\nu) \to (J^V)^{-1}(\nu)/G_\nu$ is a submersion. Then, 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(i)] There exists a unique presymplectic closed $2$-form $\Omega_h^\nu$ on $(V^*\Pi)_\nu$ such that
        \begin{equation}
        (\pi_\nu^V)^*\Omega_h^\nu = (\iota_\nu^V)^*\Omega_h,
        \end{equation}
        where $\iota_\nu^V: (J^V)^{-1}(\nu) \hookrightarrow V^*\Pi$ is the canonical inclusion.

        \item[(ii)] The vector field $R_h$, characterized by $\ker\,\Omega_h = \langle R_h\rangle$, restricts to $(J^V)^{-1}(\nu)$ and its restriction is $\pi_\nu^V$-projectable. Its projection is a vector field $R_h^\nu$ such that
        \begin{equation}
            \ker\, \Omega_h^\nu = \langle R_h^\nu \rangle.
        \end{equation}
    \end{itemize}
    Thus, $((V^*\Pi)_\nu,\Omega_h^\nu)$ is a presymplectic manifold of corank $1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.808379+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_344f71eae18dbd9a774ccc719c9e88ac975a58c031c7f3b0dc9d6cf0a413ab0f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm l:automorphism-action-resampled of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Fix $B\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ and let $\xi,\eta_B : \Xi_\mathrm{re} \to \Xi$ be the white noises defined in (equation e:def-xi-eta of the paper).
