{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.137240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3457e6708401a63e4db6d8c37ccbf2a8d1a45f55327b7dc4de1bf03b83a56823","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 19 (Balanced structural gap and a restricted matching-order witness) of arXiv:2608.26877, 'When Is the Sharp Covariance Envelope Tight? Feature-Only Geometry for Volume-Sampled Leas'] \textbf{Universal lower clause.}  For every integer $d\ge2$, every integer
$1\le r\le d-1$, every $A\in\mathcal C_d(\sigma_0)$, and every fixed
$y\in\R^{2d}$ with $L^*(A,y)>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \Delta(A,y;r)\ge\frac{3r}{38(d-1)}.

\end{equation}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Single-pair witness clause.}  For every $d=4^n$, $n\ge1$,
there exists one deterministic $A_d\in\mathcal C_d(\sigma_0)$ and one fixed
$y_d\in\R^{2d}$ with $L^*(A_d,y_d)>0$ such that the same pair works
simultaneously for every integer $1\le r<d/2$:
\begin{equation}
  \Delta(A_d,y_d;r)<\frac{r}{d-r}.

\end{equation}

Only on exactly $d=4^n$ and $1\le r<d/2$ does the class-infimum squeeze
follow:
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\frac{3r}{38(d-1)}
  \le\Delta^*_{d,r}(\sigma_0)<\frac{r}{d-r}}.

\end{equation}
Thus the universal clause gives an $\Omega(r/d)$ gap on its full stated
class, whereas the $\Theta(r/d)$ class-infimum squeeze holds only along
$d=4^n$ and $1\le r<d/2$.  Equivalently, the normalized worst-direction ratio
$1-\Delta(A_d,y_d;r)$ approaches the universal envelope only for $r=o(d)$
along $d=4^n$, not for a fixed positive budget fraction or for all budgets.
The class definition assumes isotropy, equal ordinary row norm, and pairwise
projective separation only.  The particular witness is
ordinary full spark and angle separated but has augmented coloop $h_1=1$;
