{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.862751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3486e87bf0380bb447133b10d52ea9f3b40e8210149c2e847994285105a83702","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2506.10817, 'On the Weak Error for Local Stochastic Volatility Models'] Let $t\in[0,T)$, $Y^{t,x}$ be the solution of \eqref{Eq:target} started at time $t$ with $Y_t^{t,x}=x$. Assume that $\sigma$ is four times continuously differentiable with bounded derivatives and uniformly elliptic. Then the law of $Y_T^{t,x}$ admits a density $p(y;t,x)$ with respect to the Lebesgue measure. Furthermore, if $0\le n+m \le 4$ there are constants $0<C<\infty$ such that
    \begin{align*}
       \frac{d^m}{dy^m}\frac{d^n}{dx^n} p(y+x;t,x) \le C \frac{1}{(T-t)^{(m+1)/2}} \exp\Bigl( - \frac{y^2}{C(T-t)}\Bigr).
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.770727+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_348aff76bd78ebcfcb22085e54f47e85321315847ec30fa01f9d822c5263d9a4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: coupling time of arXiv:2411.02917, '\hspace*{2mm}\\[-16mm] Stein's Method for Spatial Random Graphs'] Assume that $\lambda$ fulfils (equation eq: stability cond of the paper).
For all $(\xi,\sigma),(\eta,\tau)\in \mathbb{G}$ the coupling time $\tau_{(\xi,\sigma),(\eta,\tau)}$ is integrable. In particular, if $\norm{\xi-\eta} = 1$, we have for any $n^*\in\N\cup\{\infty\}$
\begin{align*}
    \E(\tau_{(\xi,\sigma),(\eta,\tau)}) \leq (n^*-1)! \,\bigg(\frac{\varepsilon}{c}\bigg)^{n^*-1} \bigg(\frac{1}{c} \sum_{i=n^*}^{\infty} \frac{c^i}{i!} + \int_0^c \frac{1}{s}\sum_{i=n^*}^{\infty} \frac{s^i}{i!}\diff s\bigg) + \frac{1+\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}\sum_{i=1}^{n^*-1} \frac{\varepsilon^i}{i} =: B^{*},
\end{align*}
where
$$\varepsilon = \sup_{\norm{\tilde{\xi}-\tilde{\eta}} \, = \, 1}\int_{\mathcal{X}} \abs{\lambda(x\,|\,\tilde{\xi}) - \lambda(x\,|\, \tilde{\eta})} \, \alpha(\diff x)$$
and
$$c = c(n^*) = \sup_{\abs{\tilde{\xi} \cup \tilde{\eta}} \, \geq \, n^*}\int_{\mathcal{X}}\abs{\lambda(x\,|\,\tilde{\xi}) - \lambda(x\,|\,\tilde{\eta})} \, \alpha(\diff x).$$

For $n^* = \infty$, the bound is interpreted as $\E(\tau_{(\xi,\sigma),(\eta,\tau)}) \leq \frac{1+\varepsilon}{\varepsilon} (e^{\varepsilon}-1)$. If any of $\varepsilon$ and $c$ are zero, the bound is to be understood as the corresponding limit. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:02:34.837317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_348b6858d905b6d4b51dcfe12b08fcdb65102fdb05860a7ed7feab0856606c3b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 (Admissible configurations and regular strata) of arXiv:2607.20680, 'Exact Scale--Shape Factorization of the Typical Poisson--Voronoi Cell Volume in Arbitrary '] Fix \(d\geq1\) and \(k\geq1\), and identify \((\R^d)^k\) with \(\R^{dk}\). The admissible configuration set \(\mathcal A_{d,k}\) is semialgebraic, and hence Borel. Moreover, there exists a Lebesgue-null semialgebraic set \(\mathcal N_{d,k}\subset\mathcal A_{d,k}\) such that
  \[
    \mathcal A_{d,k}^{\rm reg}
    :=
    \mathcal A_{d,k}\setminus\mathcal N_{d,k}
    =
    \bigsqcup_{\ell=1}^{L_{d,k}}\mathcal S_\ell
  \]
is a finite disjoint union of connected smooth semialgebraic strata. The sets \(\mathcal N_{d,k}\), \(\mathcal A_{d,k}^{\rm reg}\), and \(\mathcal S_\ell\) may be chosen invariant under positive scaling. On every stratum \(\mathcal S_\ell\), the face lattice of \(P(\bfx)\) is fixed, every vertex of \(P(\bfx)\) depends smoothly on \(\bfx\), and \(\bfx\mapsto G(\bfx)\) is smooth.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.814792+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_348c130e623a76a8e027fef9bf7d89e26dd9fb1c82996d73cb9fb1d32028030b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:endpoint-spectrum of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For every fixed integer $k\geq4$, there are constants
$0<\alpha_k<\beta_k$ such that, as $h\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 \left|\mathcal R(h,k)\cap
 [\alpha_kh^{k-1},\beta_kh^{k-1}]\right|
 \geq
 h^{k-1}\exp\!\left(
  -O_k\!\left(\frac{\log h}{\log\log h}\right)\right).
\end{equation}
Consequently
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{h\to\infty}\frac{\log|\mathcal R(h,k)|}{\log h}=k-1,
 \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad
 |\mathcal R(h,k)|=h^{k-1+o_k(1)}.
\end{equation}
Here $o_k(1)\to0$ as $h\to\infty$; the subscript records that $k$ is fixed.
Together with the elementary cases, the complete fixed-cardinality exponent
law is
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{h\to\infty}\frac{\log|\mathcal R(h,k)|}{\log h}=
 \begin{cases}
  0,&k=1,2,\\
  1,&k=3,\\
  k-1,&k\geq4.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.628906+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_348c4556cf3cfb4701c6f0e5b20d09e3a761bff52250918d13fe26de72686b1b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin def:stable of arXiv:2411.03609, 'Asymptotically optimal Wasserstein couplings for the small-time stable domain of attractio'] For any $\alpha\in(0,2]$, the law of 
an $\alpha$-stable L\'evy process $\bm{Z}$
 is given by a generating triplet $(\bm{\gamma_Z},\bm{\Sigma_Z}\bm{\Sigma_Z}^{\scalebox{0.6}{$\top$}},\nu_{\bm{Z}})$
(for the cutoff function $\bm{w}\mapsto\1_{B_{\bm{0}}(1)}(\bm{w})$)
as follows: the L\'evy measure is given by~(equation eq:Levy-measure-stable of the paper), i.e.,
\begin{equation*}

    \nu_{\bm{Z}}(A)
    \coloneqq c_\alpha\int_0^\infty\int_{\mathbb{S}^{d-1}}
        \1_{A}(r\bm{v})\sigma(\mathrm{d}\bm{v})r^{-\alpha-1}\mathrm{d} r,
    \quad A\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^d_{\bm{0}}),
\end{equation*}
where $\sigma$ is a probability measure  on $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$  
and  $c_\alpha\in[0,\infty)$ an ``intensity'' parameter, satisfying
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1em, nosep]
    \item $\alpha=2$ [Brownian motion with zero drift]: $\bm{\Sigma_Z}\ne \bm{0}$, $\bm{\gamma_Z}=\bm{0}$ and $c_\alpha=0$ (i.e. $\nu_{\bm{Z}}\equiv 0$);
    \item $\alpha\in(1,2)$ [infinite variation,  zero-mean process]: $c_\alpha>0$, $\bm{\gamma_Z}=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d\setminus B_{\bm{0}}(1)}\bm{x}\nu_{\bm{Z}}(\mathrm{d}\bm{x})$ and $\bm{\Sigma_Z}=\bm{0}$;
    \item $\alpha=1$ [Cauchy process]: either $c_\alpha>0$, with symmetric angular component $\int_{\mathbb{S}^{d-1}}\bm{v}\sigma(\mathrm{d}\bm{v})=\bm{0}$, or $c_\alpha=0$ and the process $\bm{Z}$ is  a deterministic nonzero linear drift, i.e. $\bm{Z}_t=\bm{\gamma_Z}t$ for all times $t$;
    \item $\alpha\in(0,1)$ [finite variation and zero natural drift]:  $c_\alpha>0$ and  
    $\bm{\gamma_Z}=\int_{B_{\bm{0}}(1)\setminus\{\bm0\}}\bm{x}\nu_{\bm{Z}}(\mathrm{d}\bm{x})$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.064861+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_348c7557d587b09bfa48324254aa1be2db8a869b58742c27b087a71af39e4603","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Pibound of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $\mathbf{n}=(n_1, n_2, n_3) \in {\mathbb N}^3$ and ${\boldsymbol \sigma},{\boldsymbol \tau} \in \mathcal S_{\mb{n}}$. 
Set $\mathbf{a} = \type({\boldsymbol \sigma})$ and $\mathbf b = \type({\boldsymbol \tau})$. 
Suppose that $\gamma_*$ and $\Gamma_*$ for $*\in \mathcal A_3$ are twelve contours, all contained in the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, and that every pair is separated by a distance of at least $d>0$. 
Then, 
\begin{equation} 
    |\Pi^{{\boldsymbol \sigma}}_{{\boldsymbol \tau}}(\boldsymbol{\xi}, \boldsymbol{\eta}) | \le \frac{2^{4|\mathbf{n}|}}{d^{|\mathbf{a}|+|\mathbf b|}}
    \sqrt{n_1! (n_2-n_1+a_1+b_1)! (n_2-n_3+a_3+b_3)! n_3!}
\end{equation} 
for every $\boldsymbol{\xi} = ( \boldsymbol{\xi}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^1, \boldsymbol{\xi}^2, \boldsymbol{\xi}^3)$ and $\boldsymbol{\eta} = ( \boldsymbol{\eta}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^1, \boldsymbol{\eta}^2, \boldsymbol{\eta}^3)$ satisfying $\boldsymbol{\xi}^*\in (\gamma_*)^{a_*}$ and $\boldsymbol{\eta}^*\in (\Gamma_*)^{b_*}$ for each $*\in \mathcal A_3$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.334310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_348e1c00a60c47e0e6dc13e12b6ea418ce780097f7766a1aef7f1389e960cd1e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition lem-k-geq-2-very-first-rounds of arXiv:2607.22324, 'The conclave process'] {}
    Suppose $\alpha^{k-1}\leq 2<\alpha^k$. Define 
    \begin{equation}
        m_0 = \left\lceil \frac{\log 101}{\log \alpha} \right\rceil \geq k \,.
    \end{equation}
    Then the following hold with probability $1-o(1)$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(1)] $\mathcal L(\mathbf X_t)$ is a singleton set for each $2 \leq t \leq k$. In addition, all $\{ \mathcal L(\mathbf X_t):\alpha^{t-1}<2 \}$ are distinct.
        \item[(2)] $\mathcal L(\mathbf X_t) = \mathcal L(\mathbf X_{t+1})$ for $k \leq t \leq m_0$.
        \item[(3)] $\mathbf X_{m_0+1} \in \overline{\Omega}_n (M,\delta)$ for some 
        \begin{align*}
            M = (\log n)^{\Theta(1)} \geq (\log n)^{101} \mbox{ and } \delta = (\log n)^{-1+o(1)} \,,
        \end{align*}
        which satisfies the conditions of Proposition~(lem-repeat-used of the paper).
    \end{enumerate}
    In particular, the $k$-th round is the exact round that determines the eventual winner by Proposition~(lem-repeat-used of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.116720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3490c94e4759e07f255cf1217467c417aac79702c067260e40fb3a459a64b3ae","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 142 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] If $\me_{z_s;q}\le (1/\bfK)\md_{z_s}$ and $z_s$ is  to the right of $p_q^{SW}$, we have
