{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:25.282780+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_34aadbce46e794b0a32d4bd2ee2e3d3701d81354d896eb7eabfe29051b8ab494","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 13 of arXiv:2606.21778, 'Pontryagin Maximum Principle in Free Probability Theory'] Let $0<T<\infty$. Consider a fCSDE as defined in \eqref{intro-freeCSDE-diffform} on the finite interval $[0,T]$. Assume conditions (a) - (d) listed in  \Cref{sec:DiffProc:Drift} and \Cref{sec:DiffProc:Diffusion} on the drift function $a$ and diffusion functions $b^i, c^i$, $i=1,\dots d$. Additionally, let
\begin{equation*}
	\int_0^T \|a(0,U_s)\|^2 + \|b^i(0,U_s)c^i(0,U_s)\|^2ds < \infty.
\end{equation*}
Then for every $X_0 \in \mathcal{A}$, there is a unique solution $X_t$ of \eqref{intro-freeCSDE-diffform} on $[0,T]$. The solution $X_t$ is uniformly bounded in $L_\infty(\varphi)$, i.e. $\|X_t^{X_0}\|\leq M(X_0)<\infty$, where the constant $M=M(X_0)$ depends on the initial value $X_0\in\mathcal{A}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.106734+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_34ac7a4d1d323923d66f17ec828bd1e1ec0ad48233f6ba8e1b3555c7290e6e0f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.08886, 'On domination for (non-symmetric) Dirichlet forms'] A Dirichlet form $({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$ on $L^2(E,m)$ is called \emph{quasi-regular} if the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] There exists an ${\mathscr E}$-nest $\{K_n:n\geq 1\}$ consisting of compact sets.
\item[(2)] There exists an ${\mathscr E}_1$-dense subset of ${\mathscr F}$ whose elements have ${\mathscr E}$-quasi-continuous $m$-versions.
\item[(3)] There exists $\{f_k:k\geq 1\}\subset {\mathscr F}$ having ${\mathscr E}$-quasi-continuous $m$-versions $\{\tilde{f}_k:k\geq 1\}$ and an ${\mathscr E}$-polar set $N$ such that $\{\tilde{f}_k:k\geq 1\}$ separates the points of $E\setminus N$.
\end{itemize}
It is called \emph{regular} if $E$ is a locally compact separable metric space,  $m$ is a fully supported Radon measure on $E$,  and ${\mathscr F}\cap C_c(E)$ is ${\mathscr E}_1$-dense in ${\mathscr F}$ as well as uniformly dense in $C_c(E)$.  It is noteworthy that a regular Dirichlet form is always quasi-regular.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.358609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_34acb40251293d3b6e590d58e1345576ddad9dc837614cfa1b8ce22a0cd9f454","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2411.13062, 'Graphon-Theoretic Approach to Central Limit Theorems for $\epsilon$-Independence'] Let $g_L$ be a graph over $[L]$, $g'_n$ be a graph over $[n]$, and $a\in \mathcal{A}$ with mean zero and variance one. Assume that $g'_n\to w$. Let $(\mathcal{A}_l)_{l\in [L]}$ be $g_L$-independent subalgebras and $(a_k^{(l)})_{k\in [n]}\subset \mathcal{A}_l$ be $g'_n$-independent and identically distributed random variables with common law $a$. Let
    \begin{align*}
        S_n^{(l)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{k\in [n]}a_k^{(l)}.
    \end{align*}
    Then, $(S_n^{(l)})_{l\in [L]}$ converges to a joint law $\nu_w$ that depends only on $g_L$ and $w$. Moreover, the law $\nu_w$ can be written as the law of $(s_l)_{l\in [L]}$, where each $s_l$ has law $\mu_{w}$ and they are $g_L$-independent.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.772666+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_34aea590f06aaef89f546956dc3a3d598b61c3f2bd852a38bdd7d41c23ecab09","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 58 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the notation from Definitions \ref{Def.ParametersEdge} and \ref{Def.ContoursEdge}. There exist $R_0 > q^{-1}$, $\theta_0 \in (\pi/4, \pi/2)$ and a function $\psi:(0,\infty) \rightarrow (0,\infty)$, depending on $q,\kappa,c$, such that for any $\varepsilon > 0 $ 
