{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.749081+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_350b8e273587c95845e82d43d0fc56e6cbe2178b013797c2d0422aa92ef90373","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 ($(x \partial_x + b)^c$ preserves positive/unitary zeros) of arXiv:2511.01456, 'Zero distribution of multiplicative Hermite and Laguerre polynomials'] Let $P_n(x)\in \C[x]$ be a polynomial of degree $n\in \N$. Let $c\in \N_0$ and $b\in \C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)]  The positive case: If  all roots of $P_n$ are in $[0,\infty)$ and $b\in \R\backslash[-n,0]$,  then all roots of the polynomial $(x \partial_x + b)^c P_n$ are also in $[0,\infty)$.
\item[(b)] The unitary case: If  all roots of $P_n$ are located  on the unit circle $\{|z|=1\}$ and $\Re b = -n/2$,  then  all roots of the polynomial $(x \partial_x + b)^c P_n$ are also located on the unit circle.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

