{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.720343+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3512c957eb0017dfc97231778b989f1a6ee762d8e56b438f00620ac98ef90408","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition weakd of arXiv:2609.02769, 'Divergence-Free Approximation in Sobolev and Lebesgue Spaces on general unbounded domains '] For $r\in[1,\infty)$,	a function  $$\boldsymbol{y}\in \mathrm{L}^{\infty}(0,T;\mathbb{H})\cap \mathrm{L}^2(0,T;\mathbb{V})\cap \mathrm{L}^{r+1}(0,T; \mathbb{L}_{\sigma}^{r+1})),$$  with $\frac{\/\mathrm{d}\/\boldsymbol{y}}{\/\mathrm{d}\/ t}\in \mathrm{L}^{p_d}(0,T;\mathbb{V}')+ \mathrm{L}^{\frac{r+1}{r}}(0,T;\mathbb{L}_{\sigma}^{\frac{r+1}{r}}),$ is called a \emph{weak solution} to the system ((abstract-CBF of the paper)), if for every $\boldsymbol{g}\in \mathrm{L}^2(0,T;\mathbb{V}')$ and  $\boldsymbol{y}_0\in\mathbb{H}$ it satisfies
	\begin{align}
		&-\int_{t_0}^{t_1}{(\boldsymbol{y}(s),\partial_t\boldsymbol{\psi}(s))}\/\mathrm{d}\/ s+ \mu\int_{t_0}^{t_1}(\nabla\boldsymbol{y}(s),\nabla\boldsymbol{\psi}(s))\/\mathrm{d}\/ s+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\langle(\boldsymbol{y}(s)\cdot\nabla)\boldsymbol{y}(s),\boldsymbol{\psi}(s)\rangle\/\mathrm{d}\/ s\nonumber\\&\quad+\alpha\int_{t_0}^{t_1}(\boldsymbol{y}(s),\boldsymbol{\psi}(s))\/\mathrm{d}\/ s+\beta\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\langle\boldsymbol{y}(s)|\boldsymbol{y}(s)|^{r-1},\boldsymbol{\psi}(s)\rangle\/\mathrm{d}\/ s\nonumber\\&= -{(\boldsymbol{y}(t_1),\boldsymbol{\psi}(t_1))}+{(\boldsymbol{y}(t_0),\boldsymbol{\psi}(t_0))}+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\langle \boldsymbol{g}(s),\boldsymbol{\psi}(s)\rangle\/\mathrm{d}\/ s,
	\end{align}
	for all test functions $\boldsymbol{\psi}\in\mathcal{V}_T$, almost all initial times $t_0\in[0,T)$, including zero, and almost every $t_1\in(t_0,T)$. A function $\boldsymbol{y}$ is called a \emph{global weak solution} if it is a weak solution for all $T>0$.","status":"unverified"}

