{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.726456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_357ef9ab8e83979d2764a4ed0b8f1e7651827fe468d9818e3dcc0151faf01be6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma decomposition_lemma of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] Fix a path $\pi(t,dx)=\rho_t(x)\;dx$ in $ {\mathcal{D}}([0,T],\mathscr{M}_{ac})$. Assume that $ {\rho}$ is continuous in $\Omega_T$, smooth in time and that there exists an $\epsilon>0$ such that $\epsilon\leq  {\rho}_t(x)\leq 1-\epsilon,$  {for all} $(t,x)\in\Omega_T$ and that $I_{[0,T]}(\rho)<\infty$. We have that $I_{[0,T]}(\rho)=I_{[0,T]}^{(1)}(\rho)+I_{[0,T]}^{(2)}(\rho)$ where
    \begin{equation*}
        I_{[0,T]}^{(1)}(\rho)=\frac{1}{4}||\mathfrak{L}_0||^2_{-1,\sigma(\rho)}\quad\quad\text{and}\quad\quad I_{[0,T]}^{(2)}(\rho)=\int_0^T\Phi_t(a_t,b_t)\;dt,
