{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.805374+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3595922854b09b675cbd45fbfe962be3ff5a23fa867fd0751850b5b3b4df09bd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm t:AJRAS-Z-existence of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] {AJRS22}}]
    There exists a measurable map
    $\mathcal{Z} : (\Xi,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P}) \to C(\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}\times\mathbb{R}_{\geqslant 0}, \mathbb{R})$, denoted $\xi\mapsto \mathcal{Z}^\xi_{{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}}}({\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}},{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}};{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}},{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}})$,
    such that
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
        \item\textup{(Chaos expansion).}        
        For each $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ 
        and $\beta\geqslant 0$, 
        the random variable $\xi\mapsto \mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y)$ belongs to $L^2(\Xi,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$,
        and its chaos expansion ((p:chaos-expansion of the paper)) is given by 
        \begin{align}
            \mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y) = \sum_{n=0}^\infty \beta^n I_n\left(
                p^{(n)}_{(s,x),(t,y)}
            \right),
        \end{align}
        where $p^{(n)}_{(s,x),(t,y)}$ is defined in (equation e:def-pn of the paper).
        The above equality is as elements of $L^2(\Xi,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$.

        \item\textup{(Strict positivity).}
        For $\mathbb{P}$-a.e. $\xi\in\Xi$, we have $\mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y) > 0$
        for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$ and all $\beta\geqslant 0$.

        We henceforth replace $\mathcal{Z}$ with a strictly positive version, i.e. we assume $\mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y)>0$ for \emph{all} $\xi\in\Xi$ and $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}, \beta\geqslant0$.

        \item\textup{(Adapted).} 
        For all $s<t$ and all $\beta\geqslant 0$, 
        the random function 
        $\xi\mapsto \mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}};t,{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}})$ is $\mathcal{F}_{[s,t]\times\mathbb{R}}$-measurable.
        \footnote{
            As mentioned earlier, $\mathcal{F}_{[s,t]\times\mathbb{R}}$ is indeed the same $\sigma$-algebra as that in [AJRS22], by (l:nested of the paper).
        }  

        \item\textup{(Coupling of SHEs).}
        For each $(s,x)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\beta\geqslant 0$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}\Bigl(
                \mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y) = \mathcal{Z}^{\mathrm{SHE}}_{\beta}(s,x;t,y)
                \quad\textup{for all $t>s$ and all $y\in \mathbb{R}$}
            \Bigr)=1,
        \end{align*}
        where $\mathcal{Z}^{\mathrm{SHE}}_\beta(s,x;{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}},{\raisebox{1.1pt}{\hspace{0.6pt}\scaleobj{0.55}{\bullet}\hspace{0.75pt}}})$ is the unique (up to indistinguishability) continuous and $\{\mathcal{F}_{[s,t]\times\mathbb{R}}\}_{t\geqslant s}$-adapted mild solution to (equation e:she of the paper) (see [AJRS22] for details).
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.806832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_35969e2745c4f2682c9b7f2524ed64e43e248045b000db0f1163b2e5f8668815","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2608.23301, 'On a Telegraph Process with Generalized Mittag-Leffler Waiting times and Velocity Driven b'] Conditional on the event that the initial velocity is $v_1$, the absolutely continuous component of probability law of $\{\mathbb{X}_{\alpha}(t)\}_{t\geq0}$  can be expressed in terms of its forward and backward densities as follows:
		{\small\begin{align}
