{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.962526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3605b490eba7088b8df76fc4dd2f3d93efe371110e59195856468a2bf5e364a0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm existence.any_l_to_m of arXiv:2411.07103, 'On a connection between total positivity and Bernoulli stopping problems'] Consider the {$\ell$th-to-$m$th  last-success} problem in an infinite sequence of independent Bernoulli trials. Suppose $\sum_k p_k < \infty$ and that zero payoff is awarded for never stopping. Then, an optimal rule exists, and the  strategy is to stop at the first success, if any, occurring on or after index 
