{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.956681+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3636b7b3342d088e78d9a099b71c3496190c0d724214a80d36e46669194a4224","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 39 of arXiv:2604.09365, 'Cotlar martingale transforms and related singular integrals'] For $m=1, \cdots n$, set 
\begin{align}
Z^m=C_m*(M+iN) 
\end{align} 
and 
\begin{align}
Z=(Z^1, Z^2, \dots Z^n)\in \bC^n. 
