{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.246562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36af15933c9c778f99e9ba1128043eee0334bcd91909f45d0b9a3be63b28d5fb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:CRP of arXiv:2607.01946, 'Second-order fluctuations for a phase transition in random partitions'] If $j_n/(\log n)^2\to\infty$ and 
 \begin{equation}
 j_n = o\pp{\frac{n^{\alpha/(1+\alpha)}}{(\log\log n)^{1/(1+\alpha)}}},
 \end{equation}
  then
\begin{equation}
\frac{j_n^{(\alpha+1)/2}}{n^{\alpha/2}}\pp{C_{j_n}(\floor{s_n(t)})-s_n(t)^\alpha p_{j_n}\topp\alpha S_\alpha}_{t\in{\mathbb R}}\stackrel{a.s.w.}\to \pp{\frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}S_\alpha}^{1/2}\pp{\zeta_t}_{t\in{\mathbb R}}
\end{equation}
in $D({\mathbb R})$ equipped with the local $J_1$ topology with respect to ${\mathcal P}$, where $(\zeta_t)_{t\in{\mathbb R}}$ is a stationary Ornstein--Uhlenbeck Gaussian process, independent of ${\mathcal P}$. Namely, $(\zeta_t)_{t\in{\mathbb R}}$  is a centered Gaussian process with covariance function
\[
{\rm{Cov}}(\zeta(s),\zeta(t)) = e^{-|s-t|}, s,t\in{\mathbb R}.
\]
If $j_n\to\infty$ and (equation eq:j_n upper of the paper) hold but $j_n/(\log n)^2\not\to\infty$, then the convergence in (equation eq:CRP limit of the paper) still holds in the sense of weak convergence. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.285441+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36b02b5352254f9f0bb9c1118ab20629b13b01cb2e9bdb01f3d690138ae9ebc5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem annealed cond of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Suppose that $\xi$ has at least two distinct nonzero coefficients $\beta_p^2$ (mixed case). Let $n\ge2$. Let $\bm{u}=(u_1,\ldots,u_{n})$ and $\bm{q}=(q_1,\ldots,q_n)$
		be strictly increasing sequences in $(0,1)$, and let
		$\zeta\in\mathrm{Prefix}_{n+1}(\bm{u};\bm{q})$.
		Using the notation of~(equation def:Par of the paper), define the \emph{prefix free en\-ergy}
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathcal{P}^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})
			\;:=\;
			\inf_{\zeta'\,\in\,\mathrm{Prefix}_{n+1}(\bm{u};\bm{q})}
			\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta'),
		\end{equation*}
		and the \emph{$n$-step complexity functional at level $f$}
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathcal{C}^{(n)}_f(\bm{u};\bm{q})
			\;:=\;
			u_n\,\bigl(\mathcal{P}^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})-f\bigr).
		\end{equation*}

		We assume:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[\emph{(i)}]
			\emph{(Tail optimality)} The infimum $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})$ is
			attained at $\zeta$.
			\item[\emph{(ii)}]
			\emph{(Stationarity)} $(\bm{u};\bm{q})$ is a critical point of
			$\mathcal{C}^{(n)}_f$.
		\end{enumerate}
		Fix $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$ and fix an ancestor skeleton
		$(\mathbf{m}^{(1)},\ldots,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})$
		satisfying the admissibility conditions stated in Section~(section:multiple of the paper)
		(Definitions~(def:susyreplicas of the paper) and~(def:matchlaw of the paper)).

		Let $\mathcal{N}_\varepsilon(f,\zeta,q_n,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})$ be the number of critical points
		$\mathbf{m}$ of $F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}$ lying in the orthogonal band of $\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)}$
		(and satisfying $\tfrac1N F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m})\in(f-\varepsilon,f+\varepsilon)$). Set
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathrm{Skel}_{n-1}=\left(\nabla F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(1)}),F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(1)}),\ldots,\nabla F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)}), F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})\right).
		\end{equation*}

		Then
		\begin{multline}
			\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\lim_{N\to\infty}
			\frac{1}{N}\log\mathbb{E}\Bigl[
			\mathcal{N}_\varepsilon(f,\zeta,q_n,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})
			\;\Big|\;
			\mathrm{Skel}_{n-1}=(0,N\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta),\ldots,0,N\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta))
			\Bigr]
			\geq
			\mathcal{C}_f^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})
			\\=
			\zeta([0,q_n))\,(\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta)-f).
		\end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.561757+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36b106933547fb90239207068fedeb4f4d8250ab3806e28d96024de058a99d07","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:uniqueness_heat_eq of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] Let $(\bm{u}_t)_{t \in [0, T]}$ be an It\^o-admissible solution to (equation eq:heat_eq_coef_sig of the paper), with terminal condition $\bm{u}_T = 0\,$. Assume moreover that there exists $g : \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+$ with $g(x) = o(e^{c x^{2}})$ as $x \to \infty$ for some $c > 0\,$, such that 
