{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.621502+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36b62c0d512a690d15ab5b6bffcddd7db5e685442dbcaafa038b9bb1108745fd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 (Principal curvatures of $\partial K_n$) of arXiv:2605.11995, 'Intrinsic volumes of \(\ell_p\)-balls and a continuum of Maxwell--Poincar\'e--Borel laws f'] Fix a point $x\in \partial K_n$ such that $x_i\neq 0$ for all $i=1,\ldots,n$.
Then $\partial K_n$ is a $C^2$-hypersurface in the neighborhood of $x$ and
the principal curvatures $\lambda_1(x),\dots,\lambda_{n-1}(x)$  are precisely the solutions (in the variable $\lambda$) of the equation
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^n \bigl(\varphi_i'(x_i)\bigr)^2
\prod_{\substack{j=1\\ j\neq i}}^n
\left(\varphi_j''(x_j)-|\nabla F(x)|\,\lambda\right) = 0.
\end{equation}
The degree of the polynomial on the right-hand side is exactly $n-1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.470940+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36bc0aa3d536a436978f8365c013d4ff0c8298db38fd5ce0bd511de45d586447","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 28 of arXiv:2609.02561, 'NON-NEF LOCI IN MIXED CHARACTERISTIC'] Let $f: X \rightarrow S$ be a projective morphism from a normal integral scheme to an integral Noetherian scheme.
Let $g:S'\rightarrow S$ be a flat morphism of integral Noetherian schemes such that $X':=X\times_{S}S'$ is normal (e.g., an open immersion or a localization at any point of $S$).
For every $\R$-Cartier $\R$-divisor $D$ on $X$,
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
{g'}^{-1}(\Bp(D/S)) = \Bp({g'}^{*}D/S'), \\
{g'}^{-1}(\Bm(D/S)) = \Bm({g'}^{*}D/S'),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where $g':X' \rightarrow X$ is the natural morphism.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.889984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36bd8a7be0cfa1a35b4ca7be8095abbf3923525b820f65f0820865a82322cd8c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] The (real) \emph{Veronese map} is the map $\nu_\alpha:\mathbb{R}^m\to S(\mathbb{R}_{>0}^A)$ given for every $x\in \mathbb{R}^m$ by:
