{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.350027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36d4eda69daa7c7e5769c6153dbca00c3cc1264b1559a533a0464b209522655d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2506.15736, 'Coming down from infinity for coordinated particle systems'] Fix a finite set of sites $V$, and let $\Lambda_v, D_v, M_{uv}$, and $R_{uv}$ be finite measures on $[0,1]$ for $u,v \in V$. Then there exists a probability space on which for each $\pi \in \mathcal{P}$ there exists a c\`adl\`ag Markov process $(X_\pi(t))_{t \geq 0}$ called the coordinated particle system with the following properties:

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(a)] $(X_\pi(t))_{t \geq 0}$ takes values in $\mathcal{P}$ and $X_\pi(0) = \pi$.

        \item[(b)] If $\pi_1, \pi_2 \in \mathcal{P}$ with $\pi_1 \leq \pi_2$, i.e. $\pi_1(v) \leq \pi_2(v)$ for all $v \in V$, then $X_{\pi_1}(t) \leq X_{\pi_2}(t)$ for all $t \geq 0$.

        \item[(c)] If $|\pi| < \infty$, i.e. $\sum_{v \in V} \pi(v) < \infty$, then the transition rates of $(X_\pi(t))_{t \geq 0}$ are given by \eqref{eq:rates}.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.676258+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36d561fb4bcccce8215fb67db051322209d521a4ac1d7c2f73d5e9bdd390caad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition a>0.25 of arXiv:2605.28194, 'Delayed Blow-up in 3D Fluids via Pseudo-Transport Noise'] Let $a\geq \frac{1}{4}$, $2b+a>\frac{5}{2}$ and $\nu>0$. Fix $R_0,T>0$ and $0<\epsilon<1$, then there exists $N_0=N_0(\nu,R_0,\epsilon,T)$, such that for all $N>N_0$ and initial data $u_0$ satisfying 
		\begin{equation*}
			u_0\in H^{2b+a} \quad \text{and}\quad \|u_0\|_{H^{2b+a}}\leq R_0, 
		\end{equation*}
		we can construct a weak solutions (in a probabilistic sense) to (equation Euler-u of the paper) up to time $T$ with a probability no less than $1-\epsilon$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:58:44.397124+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36ddd3e611dc949a6acb1002c9e896ccf4df968fe875cb4bcaefe4713d41fa98","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 (Weyl Monotonicity Principle) of arXiv:2609.02837, '2609.02837'] Let $A,B \in \h_n$. Assume that $1 \leq r,s \leq n$, with $r+s \geq n+1$. Then
