{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.316239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3714135d38a3783b0a25992e295065108e0264d9f14be2fb5a8217d763507f78","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:finite boxplus of arXiv:2605.31356, 'P\'olya--Schur problems and free probability'] Let $p_n$ and $q_n$ be degree $n$ monic polynomials with corresponding polynomials  $P_n$ and $Q_{n}$ of degree $n$ such that $p_n(z)=P_n(\partial_{z})z^{n}$ and $q_{n}(z)=Q_n(\partial_{z})z^n$, respectively.  
	Then, the finite free additive convolution  of $p_n$ and $q_n$ is the unique degree $n$ polynomial $p_n\boxplus_{n}q_n$ such that \begin{equation}
		p_n\boxplus_{n}q_{n}(z)=Q_n(\partial_{z})P_n(\partial_{z})z^n=Q_{n}(\partial_{z})p_n(z)=P_{n}(\partial_z)q_n(z).
	\end{equation}
	Equivalently, if $p_n(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{k}e_{k}(p_n)\binom{n}{k}z^{n-k}$ and $q_n(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{k}e_{k}(q_n)\binom{n}{k}z^{n-k}$, then \begin{equation*}
		p_n\boxplus_{n}q_n(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}z^{n-k}(-1)^{k}\binom{n}{k}\Bigg(\sum_{j=0}^k\binom k j  e_{j}(p_n)e_{k-j}(q_n)\Bigg) . 
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

