{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.027679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_371f0135a51e18d45ee20a8df672e0b0259323666003ec95cc9a3efdad5ffbc4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:3 of arXiv:2512.09219, 'Strong confluence of geodesics in Liouville quantum gravity'] Fix $\gamma\in (0,2)$ and consider a whole plane GFF $h$ and the associated LQG metric $D_h$. Fix $\varepsilon>0$. Then the following holds almost surely. For any points $u,v\in \mathbb{C}$, any $D_h$-geodesic $P$ from $u$ to $v$ and any sequence of $D_h$-geodesics $\{P_n\}_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ converging to $P$ in the Hausdorff sense with respect to $D_h$\footnote{Or equivalently, with respect to the Euclidean metric and with Euclidean balls on the right side of (equation eq:14 of the paper). Indeed, since $\gamma\in (0,2)$, the $D_h$-metric is a.s.\ locally bi-H\"older with respect to the Euclidean metric (see Lemma (lem:holder_regularity of the paper)).}, for all $n$ large enough, we have
