{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.975895+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_374400ba11d8ec4b00564e8e3857131116fdaaf07b8429059843de35d1eda123","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem prop_5 of arXiv:2412.03774, '{ Refining Concentration for \\Gaussian Quadratic Chaos with\\ Applications in Sonar and C'] Let $A$ be a symmetric matrix and 
\begin{eqnarray}

\alpha=\|A\|,\,\,\,\beta=\|A\|_2^2,\,\,\,\gamma=\|A\|_3^3.
\end{eqnarray}
Also, let 
\begin{eqnarray}

\eta_1(t)=\frac{1}{12\gamma^2}(\sqrt{\beta^2+4\gamma t}-\beta)(\beta^2+8\gamma t-\beta\sqrt{\beta^2+4\gamma t}\,)
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}

\eta_2(t)=\frac{t}{\alpha}-\frac{3\beta }{4\alpha}\min\bigg\{ \frac{1}{\alpha}, \frac{\sqrt{\beta^2+4\gamma t}-\beta}{2\gamma}\bigg\}.
\end{eqnarray}
Then 
\begin{eqnarray}

\Pr(\boldsymbol{\Delta}>t)\leq \exp\big(-\kappa' \min\{\eta_1(t), \eta_2(t)\}\big),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\kappa'=\frac{b^*}{3}\approx 0.187$ with $b^*$ as the unique solution for $b$ in the equation $\frac{4}{\sqrt{3}}b\sqrt{\theta_2(b)}=1$ and~$\theta_2(b)$~as defined in~((theta_func of the paper)).","status":"unverified"}

