{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.930132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3761a9ee25e498ffb3421e70ec778cf80b2022249fb8ad29872be2e5d4db4d8c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2505.21774, 'The friendship paradox for trees'] Consider a path of $n$ vertices.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] For $n \in \{1,2\}$, any vertex is neutral.
\item[(b)] For $n = 3$, two vertices (the end vertices) are positive  and one vertex (the middle vertex) is negative.
\item[(c)] For $n \geq 4$, two vertices (the end vertices) are positive, two vertices (the neighbours of the end vertices) are negative, and the rest $n-4$ vertices are neutral.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

