{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.974794+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3764f2eae3cb1013707999c284a80d455f6dae6eb722a141c9473a1349502300","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2606.11142, 'Strong invariance principles for diffusions, Markov chains and their perturbations.'] (Theorem 1.1 from \cite{Kozhina}). Fix $T > 0$ and let us define a time-grid $\mathbb{T}_N := \{t_i\}_{i=1}^N$ for $N \in \mathbb{N}$. Under assumptions A1, A2, AD3, for $q \in (4d+1, \infty)$, there exist constants $C := C(q) \ge 1$ and $c \in (0, 1]$ such that for all $0 < t_i < t_j \le T$ and all $((x, y), (x', y')) \in (\mathbb{R}^{2d})^2$:
\begin{equation*}
|(p - p^\epsilon)(t_i, t_j, (x, y), (x', y'))| \le C \bar{\Delta}_{\epsilon,\gamma,q} \, p_{c,K}(t_j - t_i, (x, y), (x', y')),
\end{equation*}
where $p_\epsilon(t, (x, y), \cdot)$ and $p(t, (x, y), \cdot)$ denote the transition densities at time $t$ of the perturbed and original SDEs respectively, starting from $(x, y)$ at time $0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:55:37.824275+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_376522e96ce1d0955b221cbe7d57eebad0a8f821525ada7ff0048318b8d09b8f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo thm2zero of arXiv:2605.06488, 'Continuous-state branching processes with L\'evy-Khintchine drift-interaction: Laplace dua'] Assume $\neg \Htwo$,\ $\Hone$ and $\mathrm{a}=0$ in (equation branchingmechanism of the paper) (no diffusive part in $\Sigma$). There exists a $[0,\infty]$-valued càdlàg Markov process  $X^{\mathrm{e}0}$, such that
\begin{equation*}
\forall x\in [0,\infty],\ \mathbb{P}_x-\text{a.s., } \underset {n\rightarrow \infty}{\lim}\! \downarrow X^{\mathrm{e}0, (n)}_t  =X^{\mathrm{e}0}_t,\ \forall t\in [0,\infty)\text{, and }
X^{\mathrm{e}0, (n)} \underset{n\rightarrow \infty}{\Longrightarrow} X^{\mathrm{e}0} \text{ in } \mathbb{D}_{[0,\infty]}.\end{equation*}
The process $X^{\mathrm{e}0}$ is a Fellerian continuous extension of $X^{\mathrm{m}}$ at $0$, absorbed at $\infty$. It satisfied under $0^+\cdot \infty, \infty^- \cdot 0$, 
\begin{equation}\mathbb{E}_x[e^{-X_t^{\mathrm{e}0}y}]=\mathbb{E}^y[e^{-xY_t^{\mathrm{m}}}], \ \forall (x,y)\in [0,\infty]^2,\ t\in [0,\infty).\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.654249+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_376899df26b0f3e09d6ef66885bc23aeedf3a766f22d547b71bcbe474d15bd80","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 14 of arXiv:2605.07184, 'Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with mini-batching and infinite variance'] A random vector $\zeta$ is regularly varying if the sequence of measures $\{\mu_n\}_{n\geq 1}$ defined
  on $\Rset^d\setminus\{0\}$ by
  \begin{align*}
    \mu_n = \frac{\pr(n^{-1}\zeta\in \cdot)}{\pr(\|\zeta\|>n)} 
  \end{align*}
  converges vaguely to a boundedly finite measure $\mu$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.014974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3768ddd19ba72739c6711fa0a0014d22cb54433d3f36bc77545dc906a286df2c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 30 of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] Let $Y=(Y_n)_{n\in\mathbb Z}$ be a stationary, irreducible, and aperiodic Markov chain with countable state space $B$, transition matrix $P$, and unique stationary distribution $\pi$. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
\item $Y$ is geometrically ergodic;
\item $Y$ satisfies Gaussian concentration;
\item $Y$ has exponential return times;
\item $Y$ is a finitary coding of an i.i.d.\ process with exponentially decaying coding radius;
\item $Y$ is a coding of an i.i.d.\ process;
\item $Y$ is finitarily isomorphic to an i.i.d.\ process.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.962652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37693e62a16b5fc15fe064d82c753a2f2b72fd6ad95721f767630e63853a43de","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 16 of arXiv:2605.28776, 'Limiting distributions of triangle counts in linear preferential attachment models'] Let $(W, W')$ be an exchangeable pair of random variables defined on the same probability space. Suppose $\E W=0$, $\E W^2=1$, $\E \abs{W}^3<\infty $, and 
	\begin{align*}
		\E (W'-W\mid W)=-\lambda(W+R_1) \text{ and } \E \left((W'-W)^2\mid W\right)=2\lambda(1+R_2)
	\end{align*}
	almost surely for some constant $\lambda\in(0, 1)$ and random variables $R_i=R_i(W)$ for $i=1, 2$. Then
	\begin{align*}
		\dwas(W, Z) \le \E|R_1|+\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\E |R_2|+\frac{1}{3\lambda}\E|W'-W|^3,
	\end{align*}
	where $Z$ is a standard normal random variable and $\dwas(W, Z)$ denotes the Wasserstein-$1$ distance. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.020051+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_376940f187c623a86cac421be196dd29ba7d6d3c068800861aeeed0b7bee1383","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lem:StepLength of arXiv:2609.03762, '\vspace*{-2cm} Projected Riemannian Gradient Descent for the Bures--Wasserstein Barycenter'] Let $S \in \mathrm{PD}(d)$ and $A \in \pi ^{-1} [S]$.
        For any $X \in \mathrm{\mathrm{Hr}}(d)$ with $M := I - X > 0$, define $A' := MA$ and $S' := \pi(A') = MSM$.
        Then
        \begin{equation}    
            \mathrm{B}(S, S') = \|A - A'\|_\mathrm{F}^2 = \mathrm{Tr}[X^2 S],
        \end{equation}
        independently of the choice of $A \in \pi ^{-1} [S]$.
        In particular, let $f : \mathrm{PD}(d) \to \mathbb{R}$ be differentiable, $g := f \circ \pi$, and let $\eta > 0$ satisfy~(Eq:StepSizeCondition of the paper) at $S$.
        Taking $X = 2 \eta \nabla f(S)$ gives $A' = A - \eta \nabla g(A)$ and $S' = \mathrm{Exp}^\mathrm{BW}_S[-\eta\, \overline{\nabla} f(S)]$, whence
        \begin{equation} 
            \mathrm{B}(S, S') = \|A - A'\|_\mathrm{F}^2 = \eta^2\, \|\nabla g(A)\|_\mathrm{F}^2.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

