{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.357764+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_377606fa993ad269ad9b324f573cd9ad6d78ff7e975c06e4d593cb68ba7e4221","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:local-alias of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] Suppose $\Phi_c$, $g$, and $Y_h$ are continuously differentiable near $x_0$, $g(x_0)=0$, and $\Phi_c$ has locally constant rank.  If there exists
\begin{equation}
v\in\ker D\Phi_c(x_0)
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
Dg(x_0)v\neq0,
\qquad
DY_h(x_0)v\neq0,
\end{equation}
then a local curve contained in the frontier level set passes through states requiring opposite terminal actions, while the deeper observation changes to first order.  The frontier information is therefore locally decision-aliased along $v$, whereas the depth-$h$ probe is locally sensitive to that direction. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:52.239177+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3776bcbd21457b161df3c4ad9b5acd0d3bf8c464fbb59803afef51fb46443772","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2512.10761, 'Special times for the point set process of the Brownian Net'] We call $(l_1,\cdots,l_n;r_1,\cdots,r_m)$ a collection of left-right coalescing Brownian motions if $(l_1,\cdots,l_n)$ is distributed as coalescing Brownian motions with drift $-1$ and $(r_1,\cdots,r_m)$ is distributed as coalescing Brownian motions with drift $+1$ and any pair $(l_i,r_j)$ is distributed as the unique weak solution of the following stochastic differential equation:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        dL_t=\mathbf1_{\{L_t\neq R_t\}}dB^l_t+\mathbf{1}_{\{L_t=R_t\}}dB_t^s-dt,\\
        dR_t=\mathbf1_{\{L_t\neq R_t\}}dB^r_t+\mathbf{1}_{\{L_t=R_t\}}dB_t^s+dt,
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
under the constraint that 
$$L_t\leq R_t,\text{ for all }t>\tau_{L,R}, $$
where $B^l$, $B^r$, $B^s$ are three independent standard Brownian motions and $\tau_{L,R}$ is the first meeting time of $L$ and $R$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.775601+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_377769b6676f5e27ffec8924904d3105670bf52632b29017b361fee021747247","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition T:attractor of arXiv:2411.01364, 'Global Dynamics of Granular Media Equations via Stochastic Order'] {\rm (Global attractor).} Let $P^*$ be an order-preserving semiflow on $\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$. Assume that there exists an eventually positively invariant, order bounded, closed set $K\subset\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$ such that $K$ attracts each bounded subset of $\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$. Then,  
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textnormal{(\roman*)}]
    \item $\omega(K)=\mathop{\bigcap}\limits_{t\geq0}P^*_tK$ is the global attractor. Furthermore, if $K$ is connected in $\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$, $\omega(K)$ is connected in $\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$;
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}[resume,label=\textnormal{(\roman*)}]
    \item $\omega(K)\subset[\underline{\nu},\overline{\nu}]$, where $\underline{\nu}, \overline{\nu}\in\omega(K)$ are invariant measures;
    \item $\underline{\nu}$ is globally attracting from below and $\overline{\nu}$ is globally attracting from above i.e., for any $\rho\leq_{\mathrm{st}}\underline{\nu}$, we have $\mathcal W_2(P^*_t\rho,\underline{\nu})\to0$, as $t\to\infty$; for any $\rho\geq_{\mathrm{st}}\overline{\nu}$, we have $\mathcal W_2(P^*_t\rho,\overline{\nu})\to0$, as $t\to\infty$;
    \item If $\underline{\nu}$ attracts a set $U\subset\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$, then $\underline{\nu}$ attracts each measure in $\bigcup\limits_{\mu\in U}\{\rho\in\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R): \rho\leq_{\mathrm{st}} \mu\}$. Correspondingly, if $\overline{\nu}$ attracts a set $V\subset\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$, then $\overline{\nu}$ attracts each measure in $\bigcup\limits_{\mu\in V}\{\rho\in\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R): \rho\geq_{\mathrm{st}}\mu\}$;
    \item If there exists $\rho_1<_{\mathrm{st}}\overline{\nu}$ (resp. $\rho_2>_{\mathrm{st}}\underline{\nu}$) such that $\mathcal W_2(P^*_t\rho_1,\overline{\nu})\to0$ (resp. $\mathcal W_2(P^*_t\rho_2,\underline{\nu})\to0$) as $t\to\infty$, then for any $\rho\geq_{\mathrm{st}} \rho_1$ (resp. $\rho\leq_{\mathrm{st}}\rho_2$), one has $\mathcal W_2(P^*_t\rho,\overline{\nu})\to0$ (resp. $\mathcal W_2(P^*_t\rho,\underline{\nu})\to0$), as $t\to\infty$. 
    \item If $P^*$ has a unique invariant measure $\nu$, then the singleton set $\{\nu\}$ is the global attractor. That is to say, $\nu$ attracts all the bounded subsets of $\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R)$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.590862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37797b5c4fed4640b3b944546707b6a225de3820befd0363d6df88e570f0be9e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop.BDG of arXiv:2411.15485, '\bf\Large Stochastic Volterra Equations for Local Times of Spectrally Positive L\'evy Proc'] For two constants $p\geq 1$ and $T>0$, assume that
   \begin{eqnarray*}
 	\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbf{E}\Big[\big|X(t)\big|^{p}\Big]<\infty
 	\quad \mbox{and}\quad 
 	\int_0^T ds \int_0^\infty \big|g(s,y)\big|^{2p}\mu(dy) <\infty.
   \end{eqnarray*}
  Then there exists a constant $C>0$ depending only on $p$ such that
   \begin{eqnarray} 
 	\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbf{E}\Big[ \big| B(f,t) \big|^{2p}  \Big] 
 	\leq C\cdot \sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbf{E} \Big[\big|X(t)\big|^p \Big] \cdot \bigg| \int_0^T   \big|f(s)\big|^{2} ds  \bigg|^{p}
   \end{eqnarray}
  and 
   \begin{eqnarray} 
 	\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbf{E}\Big[ \big| \widetilde{N}(g,t) \big|^{2p}  \Big] 

 	\!\!\!&\leq\!\!\!& C  \cdot \sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbf{E} \Big[\big|X(t)\big|^p \Big] \cdot \bigg| \int_0^T ds \int_0^\infty\big|g(s,y)\big|^{2} \mu(dy)  \bigg|^{p} \cr
 	\!\!\!&\!\!\!& + C \cdot \sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbf{E}\Big[\big|X(t)\big|\Big]\cdot \int_0^T ds \int_0^\infty\big|g(s,y)\big|^{2p} \mu(dy) .
   \end{eqnarray} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.858936+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_377ceabb28f56fd46a0c37ce85028648a82b2bd072feba471f8c1adeb2f899b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2609.02423, '2609.02423'] There exist constants $C,C_0>0$, depending only on the fixed cutoff
functions, such that, for every dyadic
$N\ge2$, every $0<T\le1$, and every $f\in L^2(\R^2)$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{U(t)P_Nf}_{L_x^2L_{y,T}^\infty}
 +\norm{U(t)P_Nf}_{L_y^2L_{x,T}^\infty}
 \le C N^{1/2}(1+\log N)^{C_0}\norm{P_Nf}_{L^2}.
\end{equation}
One may take $C_0=1$.  Consequently, for every $\eps>0$, the factor on
the right-hand side may be replaced by $C_\eps N^{1/2+\eps}$.  The
power $1/2$ is optimal: no estimate with $N^{1/2-\delta}$, $\delta>0$,
can hold uniformly in $N$.  The theorem does not assert a
logarithm-free endpoint. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.892530+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_377d308c20055278383ba82cedd3983c578c96383a510bfaaeb793e7dc2d9854","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 16 (Euclidean exit interval) of arXiv:2608.26834, 'Small-time annealed large deviations principle for one-dimensional diffusions in a random '] Set
		\begin{align*}
		r_W(x,R):=\min\Biggl\{&
		\int_{x-R}^{x}\sqrt{\frac{\exp\{\rho(z,W)-a(z,W)\}}{2}}\,\dd z,\\
		&\int_x^{x+R}\sqrt{\frac{\exp\{\rho(z,W)-a(z,W)\}}{2}}\,\dd z
		\Biggr\}.
		\end{align*}
		There exist positive constants $C,N$ such that, for every $x\in\R$, $R>0$, and $0<t\le1$,
		\begin{equation*}
			P_x^W\left(\tau_{(x-R,x+R)}\le t\right)
			\le Ct^{-N}e^{CM((x-R,x+R))}
			\exp\left\{-a_\theta\frac{r_W(x,R)^2}{t}\right\}.
		\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.084454+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_377dff00b907f60debe5a5f4b402f688321d23913d265cad54aa94f4369a544f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem septhm of arXiv:2412.08334, 'Maker-Breaker on Galton-Watson trees'] If the offspring distribution is separable, i.e.\ $\xi\stackrel{d}{=}X_1+X_2$ with i.i.d.\ $(X_1,X_2)$, and $\mathbb{E}\,\xi>2$ then the probabilities of Breaker winning on the corresponding GW-tree with the nodes carrying no further information is given by
 \begin{equation}
 	p=\rho\,\bigg(1-\frac{\sigma\cdot\rho_\mathrm{odd}}{1-\theta\cdot(1-\theta_\mathrm{odd})}\bigg)
 \end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
	\bar{p}=\rho^2\,\bigg(1-\frac{2\sigma\cdot\rho_\mathrm{odd}\,(1-\rho_\mathrm{odd})}{1-\theta\cdot(1-\theta_\mathrm{odd})}\bigg)
\end{equation}
depending on whether Breaker or Maker starts the game.

 The (conditional) probabilities $\rho$, $\sigma$ and $\theta$ as well as $\rho_\mathrm{odd}$ and $\theta_\mathrm{odd}$  appearing here refer to the ones introduced in (equation probs of the paper), corresponding to the left-continuous random walk on $\mathbb{Z}$ starting at $0$ with i.i.d.\ increments distributed as $X_1-1$.","status":"unverified"}

