{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.899249+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3797f457cfedb00be1a63f5bb56125bf88a572fba94307c5b78e29fe27056e80","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 10 of arXiv:2606.20169, 'THEORY OF UNCERTAIN PROBABILITY:\\ CAN WE DERIVE THE PROBABILITY DENSITY FUNCTION OF UNCER'] The variance of an uncertain random variable $\mathbb{X}$ is defined by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\sigma_{\mathbb{X}}^2=Var\left(\mathbb{X}\right)=E\left[\left(\mathbb{X}-E(\mathbb{X})\right)^2\right].
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Its nonnegative square root denoted by $\sigma_{\mathbb{X}}$ is the standard deviation of $\mathbb{X}$. If $z$ is a positive integer, then $E\left(\mathbb{X}^z\right)$ is called the $z$th moment of $\mathbb{X}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.299277+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_379caefb91f758cd29e013be20f5cae87dc421119ca5bfaad4e37ca476aaff48","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:IPS:entropy-change:in-terms-of-generator of arXiv:2605.16904, 'Positive-rate PCA and IPS with stationary Bernoulli measures are rapidly forgetful \footno'] Let $\Phi$ be an IPS with generator~$L$ on $\Sigma^{\mathbb{Z}^d}$, and let $\lambda$ be a full-support probability measure that is stationary under~$\Phi$.  Let $\mu$ be a probability measure and $J\Subset\mathbb{Z}^d$.
	Let $\mu^t\coloneqq\mu\Phi^t$ and $D_J(t)\coloneqq D_J(\mu^t\relto\lambda)$.
	Then, at every $t\geq 0$, $D_J(t)$ is differentiable with
	\begin{equation}
		\dot{D}_J(t) =
			\sum_{w\in\Sigma^J} (\mu^t L)([w])\log\frac{\mu^t([w])}{\lambda([w])}
	\end{equation}
	unless $\mu^t([w])=0$ for some $w\in\Sigma^J$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.828689+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_379cdbf2b26ccc9411c2560d1e9b693f091e34f7cf1f62986764708a405fdaea","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2604.10705, '#1'] {}
A functional $F$ is said to be space-differentiable at $(t,x) \in [0,T]\times D([0, T],\mathbb{R}^d)$, in the direction of the canonical basis vector $e_i$, $i \in \{1,...,d\}$, if the following limit exists:
\begin{align}
    \partial_i F(t,x_{\wedge t}) &:= \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{F(t,x_{\wedge t}^{he_i})-F(t,x_{\wedge t})}{h}.
\end{align}

If $F$ is space-differentiable at $(t,x)$ in the direction of $e_i$, for every $(t,x) \in [0,T] \times D([0,T],\mathbb{R}^d)$ and every $i \in \{1,\ldots,d\}$, we say that $F$ is \emph{space-differentiable} and denote the gradient of space derivatives by $\nabla F := (\partial_1 F,\ldots,\partial_d F)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.269083+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_379f573b2ae15edb0bdfc3388a79f911fa56fa6fbab4b8c150dd60d63049252e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2607.26944, 'Improved bounds on the inradius and inverse inradius process of the convex hull of planar '] Let $\zeta(s)$ be the Riemann zeta function from~\eqref{eq_riemann_zeta_function_def} and $\beta(s)$ the Dirichlet beta function from~\eqref{eq:defn_beta_diri}. Then, for the inradius $\sfr$ of the standard planar Brownian motion $\bm W$, it holds that
