{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.473957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37cfc5dea83468fbccc1504309d3fbe744f0d475e1417f583817015489395c16","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2608.17562, '2608.17562'] Let $h : \R \to \R$ be a $1$-Lipschitz function and $\alpha$ a positive scalar. Then, for every $x \in \Rp$ we have 
\begin{align}
  xg_{h}'(x) &= -\alpha\int_0^{x}e^{{x^{-\alpha}}-{u^{-\alpha}}}h'(u)\dif u 
  \\
&= -\alpha (h(x) - h(0)) + \alpha^2 \int_0^{x} (h(u) - h(0))
e^{{x^{-\alpha}}- {u^{-\alpha}}  }\frac{\dif u}{u^{\alpha + 1}} .
\end{align} 
Consequently, the function $x \mapsto xg_{h}'(x)$ is $2\alpha$-Lipschitz on $(0,\infty)$.
 Furthermore, if $\alpha < 1$ and $h \in \mathcal{H}_b$ for some $b \in (0,\alpha)$, then the following bounds holds:
