{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.513190+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37d65e791288958d79f781d9a2da63ae35d851d57b2b9e453e11f63b8c654c59","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Fejer of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Let $f:[0,1]\rightarrow \lbrack 0,1]$ be continuous, strictly
increasing, with a unique interior fixed point $p\in (0,1)$ and
single-crossing pattern 
\begin{equation}
f(x)>x\ \ (x<p),\qquad f(x)<x\ \ (x>p).  
\end{equation}
Then:

\begin{enumerate}
\item For every $x\in \lbrack 0,1]$ 
\begin{equation}
|f(x)-p|\leq |x-p|,  
\end{equation}

with strict inequality if $x\neq p$;

\item If $x,y$ lie on the same side of $p$ (both $\leq p$ or both $\geq p$),
then 
\begin{equation*}
|f(x)-f(y)|\leq |x-y|.
\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.030856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37da9709447f488286dfa3f15fd1a3316dd539c938f8a9a8eb9be3e8b30af741","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 6 of arXiv:2606.07194, 'Survival Probability of Markov Linear Reccurence Sequence in a Random Environment: Subcrit'] i) The process is strongly subcritical iff for some positive $\delta$ and natural $i$
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} {\bf P}(Y_n>0|Y_0=i) s^n = +\infty
\end{equation}
for some $s: s\rho^+<1$. Moreover, $1/\rho$ is the radius of convergence of series~(\ref{ProbPowerSeries}).\\
ii) Let $1/\rho^*(h)$ be the radius of convergence of the series
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} {\bf E}(Y_n^h|Y_0=i) s^{n},\  h\in (0,\widehat{h}).
\end{equation}
Let $h^*_{++}$ be the solution of the equation
$$
R(h) = \rho,\ R'(h)>0,
$$
where if there's no solution, we say that $h^*_{++}=+\infty$. Then
$$\rho^*(h)=\left\{
\begin{array}{cc}
\rho,& h<h^*_{++},\\
R(h),& h\in [h^*_{++},\widehat{h}).
\end{array}
\right.
$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.369736+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37dd76b5fe6c13cf2d70d200df33aca6027d6a2d4b302990cadd6f2d0fbe3f47","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ap1:lyapunov1 of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Suppose $f:[0, T]\times \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m$  and $\mu_0 \in \mathcal{P}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m)$ satisfy the conditions of Theorem (thm:ap1:wellPosed2 of the paper). Let $g: \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ be a $C^1$ function such that $\nabla g$ satisfies a linear growth condition. Let $\{\mu_t\}_{t \in [0, T]}$ be the weak solution to (equation eq:ap1:formalPDE of the paper), which exists by (1) of Theorem (thm:ap1:wellPosed2 of the paper). Then for almost every $0 < r < t < T$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        \int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m} \mu_t \big( \log \mu_t + g \big) dx - &\int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m} \mu_r \big( \log \mu_r + g \big) dx = \\
        & - \int_r^t \int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m} (\nabla \mu_s + f_s \mu_s) \cdot \bigg(\frac{\nabla \mu_s}{\mu_s}  + \nabla g \bigg) dx ds.
    \end{split}

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.146525+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37decb05e961f3bc9b5ac3f9a21ed81d5865558d39d68e433712b1f282259a64","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.16103, 'Non-commutative Stein's Method: Applications to Free Probability and Sums of Non-commutati'] Define the function $\tau_n:\mathcal{A}^n\rightarrow\mathbb{C}$ via
\begin{align*}
\tau_n[a_1,\dots, a_n]
  &:=\tau[a_1\cdots a_n]	.
\end{align*}
For a given $V\subset [n]$ of the form $V=\{i_1,\dots, i_r\}$, with $i_j<i_{j+1}$, we define 
\begin{align*}
\tau_V[a_1,\dots, a_n]
  &:=\tau_n[a_{i_1},\dots, a_{i_n}].
\end{align*}
For a partition $\pi\in \mathfrak{P}(n)$, we define 
