{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.673854+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3803ae7402d31819ed54e986e1aa84b5783898a23a840f09a5a44132485d6471","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 (Smooth Bochner couple) of arXiv:2607.10769, '\fontsize{14}{17}\selectfont\bfseries Sharp Poincar\'e interpolation along Wasserstein geo'] Let $T=\nabla\Phi$ transport $\mu_0$ to $\mu_1$ and set
$H=\Hess{\Phi}$. Given a smooth mean-zero datum $g$ and constants
$a_0,a_1\geq0$, a pair of smooth
vector fields $(X,Y)$ is called a \emph{Bochner couple} with datum $g$
and constants $(a_0,a_1)$ if the relations
\begin{equation}
 Y\circ T=HX,
 \qquad
 -\diver_{\mu_0}X=g,
 \qquad
 -(\diver_{\mu_1}Y)\circ T=g,
\end{equation}
hold, the two matrix symmetry conditions
\begin{equation}
 H DX\ \text{is symmetric},
 \qquad
 ((DY)\circ T)H\ \text{is symmetric}
\end{equation}
hold, and the endpoint Bochner estimates
\begin{equation}
 \norm{X}_{L^2(\mu_0)}\leq a_0\norm{g}_{L^2(\mu_0)},
 \qquad
 \norm{Y}_{L^2(\mu_1)}\leq a_1\norm{g}_{L^2(\mu_0)}
\end{equation}
are valid.  If there is a scalar function $u$ such that
\begin{equation}
 X=H^{-1}\nabla u,
 \qquad
 Y\circ T=\nabla u
\end{equation}
then we say that the couple is generated by the weighted Poisson
equation. Here $H=DT=\Hess{\Phi}$ is the differential of the Brenier map
that relates the two fields.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.110942+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3804402fb87edf4ab36ae47901e68c29dd274db733ede6048f44e28d9b7a00a5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2608.27398, 'Strict Stability and Strict Minimality of Regular Area-Minimizing Hypercones: A Quantitati'] Define the vector field $X$ by 
