{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.049087+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3805b862a005b234db1e07094cd75bd8432709afbd5a87f18e5f18715d19d1e6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition wave kernel of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] The fundamental solution of the wave equation (or wave kernel) in dimension $d$ is defined by
\begin{align}

p_w(t,x)=\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\frac{1}{2}\mathbbm{1}_{\{|x|<t\}}     & \text{when } d=1, \\\rule{0ex}{1.5em}
\frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{1}{\sqrt{t^2-|x|^2}} \mathbbm{1}_{\{|x|<t\}}    & \text{when }d=2,\\\rule{0ex}{1.5em}
\frac{1}{4\pi t}\sigma_t& \text{when }d=3.
\end{array}\right.
\end{align}
Here $\sigma_t$ is the surface measure on $\partial \mathbf{B}(0,t)$. We notice that for all $d \leq 3$,
\begin{align}

\displaystyle{\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}p_w(t,x)\,dx=t}.
\end{align}
For $d \geq 4$, $p_w$ is not a function, but a Schwartz distribution, it will not satisfy one of our assumption, hence the case will not be covered in this dissertation. The Fourier transform of the wave kernel is given by
\begin{align}

    \hat{p}_w(t,\xi)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} e^{-i\xi x}p_w(t,x)dx=\frac{\sin(t|\xi|)}{|\xi|}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.926404+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_380b050d308cd36dfc7a1a831a5302ea009bc5992bfaf29c9153242ad4497976","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition coaff of arXiv:2412.02486, 'On the curvatures of \\ random complex submanifolds'] [bettigayet] Let $f:\mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}^r$ be $r$ independent copies of the Bargmann--Fock field. 
