{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.730981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_38fa3f41be6396e44ccef13c6194f877565526a18b2703e449d5cf13a711bcc7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:lFPE-sol of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] A \textit{distributional solution} (short: \textit{solution}) to (equation eq:lFPE of the paper) with initial condition $\zeta \in \mathcal P$ is a weakly continuous curve $\nu = (\nu_t)_{t> 0} \subseteq \mathcal P$ such that $(t,x) \mapsto a_{ij}(t,x)$ and $(t,x) \mapsto b_i(t,x),$ $1\leq i,j \leq d,$ belong to $ L^1_{\textup{loc}}([0,\infty)\times \mathbb{R}^d ;\nu_t dt)$, and for all $t >0$,
   \begin{equation}
        \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\varphi \,d\nu_t - \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\varphi\, d\zeta = \int_0^t \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} L_{a(s),b(s)}\varphi\, d\nu_s ds
    \end{equation}
    holds for all $\varphi \in C^2_c(\mathbb{R}^d)$. Again, it follows that $\nu_t \to \zeta$ weakly as $t\to 0$, and we use $(\nu_t)_{t>0}$ and $(\nu_t)_{t\geq 0}$ (with $\nu_0 := \zeta$) interchangeably.","status":"unverified"}

