where the function $h$ is as defined in \eqref{h_defn_fixed_size}, and $A_{N}$ is as defined via \eqref{A_{n}_defn}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.954611+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3904bf3adfb879f9ac3c11f2793e0bad4d8bf78a1f002b64479ab9b74d459e8b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2606.25494, 'A functional central limit theorem for kernel gradient flow and infinitesimal gradient boo'] In addition to \Cref{ass:loss}, assume
  that~\eqref{eq:cond-expectation-bounded} holds. If
  $\gamma_{\max}>1$, then there exists an RKHS
  $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}$ with continuous and bounded reproducing
  kernel $\tilde k$ such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item[-] $\mathcal{H}\subset \widetilde{\mathcal{H}}$ and $\|F\|_{\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}}\leq \|F\|_{\mathcal{H}}$ for all $F\in\mathcal{H}$;
    \item[-] $\mathcal{H}$ is dense in $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}$,
\end{itemize}
and the following properties hold: 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] The fluctuations of the vector fields satisfy a  weak convergence
\[
\big(\sqrt{n}\,(\mathcal{T}_n(F) - \mathcal{T}(F))\big)_{F\in\mathcal{H}}
\;\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow}\;
(\mathscr{W}(F))_{F\in\mathcal{H}}
\]
in the space $C_b^{0}(\mathcal{H}, \widetilde{\mathcal{H}})$ with a Gaussian limit process $\mathscr{W}$.
\item[(ii)] It the initial conditions satisfy
\begin{equation}
F_0^n=\P_n[f_0(y)]+o_{\P}(n^{-1/2}), \qquad \text{as $n\to\infty$},
\end{equation}
 with $f_0$ such that $\P[f_0(y)^2]<\infty$, then the fluctuations of the infinitesimal gradient boosting processes satisfy the weak convergence
\[
\big(\sqrt{n}(F^n_t - F_t)\big)_{t\ge 0}
\;\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow}\;
(\mathscr{F}_t)_{t\ge 0}
\qquad\text{as } n\to\infty,
\]
in the space $C^1([0,\infty), \widetilde{\mathcal{H}})$ with a Gaussian limit process $\mathscr{F}$. The limit process is equal in distribution to the unique solution of the differential equation in $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}$
\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathscr{F}_t}{\mathrm{d}t}
=
\big(\mathrm{d}_{F_t}\mathcal{T}\big)(\mathscr{F}_t)
+\mathscr{W}(F_t),
\qquad t\ge 0,
\end{equation}
with initial condition $\mathscr{F}_0$ following a centered Gaussian distribution with variance $\P[f_0(y)^2]-\P[f_0(y)]^2$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.106169+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_390907b5e9941b046539d223853741a7b16b20f49bb94bcabba34cc4966713a3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.08886, 'On domination for (non-symmetric) Dirichlet forms'] Let $\kappa$ be a positive Borel measure on $\Gamma_{\kappa,\theta}(\subset \Gamma)$,  and let $\theta$ be a positive,  symmetric measure on $(\Gamma_{\kappa,\theta}\times \Gamma_{\kappa,\theta})\setminus d$,  where $d$ denotes the diagonal of $\Gamma \times \Gamma$. 
The pair $(\kappa, \theta)$ is called \emph{quasi-admissible} if the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] $B_{\kappa,\theta}:=\Gamma\setminus \Gamma_{\kappa,\theta}$ is quasi-closed.
\item[(2)] $\kappa+\bar\theta$ is smooth with respect to the part Dirichlet form of $(\mathbf{D}, \tilde{H}^1(\Omega))$ on $\overline{\Omega}\setminus B_{\kappa,\theta}$,  where $\bar{\theta}(dx):=\theta(dx,\Gamma_{\kappa, \theta})$.  
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.739958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_390acdf299480cc709a5319f3f242dbf60046a09224f038db7e1dbcd69d549e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 47 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Suppose that $f \in \cB_{p,q}^{\alpha - \beta}$ where $\alpha \in \mbR$ and $p,q \in [1,\infty]$. Then given a $\beta$-regularising kernel $K$ in the sense of \cite[Assumption 5.1]{H0}, we have that \begin{align*}
		\| 2^{n \alpha} & \| \sup_{\psi \in \mcB_{\bar{\alpha}}^r} \langle K \ast f, \psi_y^n \rangle \|_{L^p(\mfK; dz')} \|_{\ell^q(n)} + \| \sup_{\psi \in \mcB^r} \langle K \ast f, \psi_y \rangle \|_{L^p(\mfK ; dy)}
		\\ 
		\lesssim& 	\| 2^{n (\alpha - \beta)} \| \sup_{\psi \in \mcB_{\bar{\alpha} - \beta}^r} \langle f, \psi_y^n \rangle \|_{L^p(\bar{\mfK}; dy)} \|_{\ell^q(n)} + \| \sup_{\psi \in \mcB^r} \langle f, \psi_y \rangle \|_{L^p(\bar{\mfK} ; dy)}
	\end{align*}
	for each $\bar{\alpha} > \alpha$ where $\mcB_\alpha^r$ denotes the set of elements of $\mcB^r$ that annihilate polynomials of degree less than $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.590483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_390cef0a1aa0d42f7654209026cbd9c8862a82ae717a6cb8493da168ac98e496","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary COR1 of arXiv:2411.09976, 'Occupation times on the legs of a diffusion spider'] The resolvent density (the Green function) $\mathbf{g}_\lambda$ given in (equation gk1 of the paper) is continuous on $\Gamma$, and for every $i$ and~$j$,  
