{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.630996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_390f8e2760a073add37f42e3462163d5df827e757edd379d6bfd5979002c5bde","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:scaling limit of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $\mu=\mu_n$ satisfy $\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mu\sqrt{n}=\alpha\in\mathbb R$.
    Let $\mathbf{T}_n=\mathbf T^\mu$ be a $\mu$-height-biased tree of size $n$, let $d_n$ denote the graph distance on $\mathbf{T}_n$ with each edge having length 1, and let $\sigma_n$ denote the uniform measure on the  vertices of ~$\mathbf T_n$. Then
    \begin{align*}
        \left(\mathbf{T}_n,\frac{d_n}{\sqrt{2n}}, {\sigma_n}\right) \overset d\longrightarrow \big(\mathbfcal{T}_{\alpha},d_{\alpha}\,, \sigma_{\alpha}\big),
    \end{align*}
    where the convergence in distribution above is with respect to the Gromov-Hausdorff-Prokhorov (GHP) topology. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.447345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39105fa62429fa61ea3d032f1d8613087660993b11ad317e7b5e68780e597306","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2606.15843, 'Long-time behaviour of DLRA for SDEs'] Suppose Assumptions (H3), (H7) and (H8) hold.
			Moreover, suppose  Assumption~\ref{AS:relation2} holds. Furthermore, suppose the initial condition $(\zeta,\xi)\in L^p(\Omega,V_r(\bb R^d)\times\bb R^r)$. Then, there exists a constant $C(b,\sigma,p)>0$, one has
             \begin{align}
                \bb E[\|U_t^{(\mu)}\|_{\rrm {HS}}^p]=r^\frac{p}{2},
            \end{align}
             and
			\begin{align}
				\bb E[|Y_t^{(\mu)}|^p]\le \rrm e^{-\lambda \frac{p}{2}t}\bb E[|\xi|^p]+\frac{2C(b,\sigma,p)}{\lambda p},
			\end{align}   
            where $\lambda=2L_{b,7}-2(p-1)L_{\sigma,1}-\frac{2}{p}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.306510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3910f0e2729a9d8e833dff804b706f4e38dec55cb5bccfc6700462ff4b4f24d3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 74 (Parabolic H\"older spaces) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] We define $C_c^\infty(\Psi_T)$ to be the set of functions on $\Psi_T$ that are restrictions to $\Psi_T$ of some function in $C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^{d+1})$, in particular we do not impose that elements of $C_c^\infty(\Psi_T)$ vanish at the boundaries of $\Psi_T.$ 

    For $\alpha\in(0,1)$ we define the space $C^{\alpha,\tau}_\mathfrak s(\Psi_T)$ to be the completion of $C_c^\infty(\Psi_T)$ with respect to the norm $$\|f\|_{C^{\alpha,\tau}_\mathfrak s(\Psi_T)}:= \sup_{(t,x)\in \Psi_T} \frac{|f(t,x)|}{w(x)} + \sup_{|s-t|^{1/2}+|x-y|\leq 1} \frac{|f(t,x)-f(s,y)|}{w(x)(|t-s|^{1/2}+|x-y|)^{\alpha}}.$$
		For $\alpha<0$ we let $r=-\lfloor \alpha\rfloor$ and we define $C^{\alpha,\tau}_\mathfrak s(\Psi_T)$ to be the completion of $C_c^\infty(\Psi_T)$ with respect to the norm $$\|f\|_{C^{\alpha,\tau}_\mathfrak s(\Psi_T)}:= \sup_{(t,x)\in\Psi_T} \sup_{\lambda\in (0,1]} \sup_{\varphi \in B_r} \frac{(f,S^\lambda_{(t,x)}\varphi)_{L^2(\Psi_T)}}{w(x)\lambda^\alpha}$$ where the scaling operators are defined by 
		\begin{align}

			S^\lambda_{(t,x)}\varphi (s,y) = \lambda^{-d-2}\varphi(\lambda^{-2}(t-s),\lambda^{-1}(x-y)),
		\end{align}
		and where $B_r$ is the set of all smooth functions of $C^r$ norm less than 1 with support contained in the unit ball of $\mathbb R^{d+1}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.598045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_391214a4dba2d8b6043fbe890c9430ea7db1d7db65e872a0fb3ee7a3c310d38c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary C:containment-quadrangle of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Consider a weighted directed acyclic graph $G = (V, E, M)$, and we let $H = (W, E|_{W\times W}, M|_{W})$ be the subgraph of $G$ induced by a vertex set $W \subset V$. Consider vertices $x_1 \prec y_1$ and $x_2 \prec y_2 \in H$ and suppose that we have the following {\bf path-crossing} property:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $\tau$ be any path in $H$ from $x_1$ to $y_1$ and $\pi = (\pi_0, \dots, \pi_k)$ be any path in $G$ from $x_2$ to $y_2$. Then if $\pi_i \notin W$ for some $i \in \llbracket1,k-1 \rrbracket$, there are indices $i^- < i < i^+$ such that the vertices $\pi(i^-), \pi(i^+)$ are on the path $\tau$.
\end{itemize}
Then
$$
H(x_1; y_1) + G(x_2; y_2) \le G(x_1; y_1) + H(x_2 ; y_2).
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.283265+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_391556474800cbd2c8a8d919e28b11b3222bb6054ffc14607eccbbc52c3c2d74","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:pureweak-nonstrong3 of arXiv:2507.14676, 'On the critical parameters of branching random walks'] Let $(X,K)$ and $(X,K^*)$ two irreducible continuous-time BRWs such that $k_{xy}=k^*_{xy}$ for all $x \in X \setminus A$
where $A$ is a nonempty (not necessarily finite) set.
If there exists $\lambda_0$ such that $(X,K)$ survives non-strong locally in $A$, then 
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(X, K^*)$ survives globally when $\lambda=\lambda_0$;
\item if $A$ is finite and $\lambda^*_s \ge \lambda_0$, then there is pure global survival for $(X, K^*)$, if $\lambda=\lambda_0$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:52.233126+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_391682d33062835d8dbe8b9c5820237dee52e761a4814a698685493afdaf08e7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2512.10199, 'Explicit correlation functions for the six-vertex model in the free-fermion regime'] Let $x = (x_1,x_2)$ and $y = (y_1,y_2)$ be elements of $\midedge_n$. Then $K_\theta^{-1}: \midedge_n \times \midedge_n \to \mathbb{C}$ takes the explicit form
    \begin{equation} 
        K_\theta^{-1}(x,y) = \frac{1}{n^2} \sum_{\substack{w_1^n=1 \\ w_2^n=1}}w_1^{\,x_1-y_1} \, w_2^{\,x_2-y_2} \, \big[ M_{\alpha_\theta,\beta_\theta,\gamma_\theta}(w_1,w_2)^{-1}\big]_{c(x),\,c(y)}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.561415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_391eb0a75347e4c857cbaf0855b977c6cfac0a4f341365c2e3ed992be1845d16","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:sojourn:LB of arXiv:2604.08129, 'Polarity of points for Gaussian random fields in critical dimension'] There exist constants $\delta_2\in(0,1)$ and $C_3\in(0,\infty)$ such that for all $\varepsilon \in (0, \delta_2)$ and all $n$ of the form $n = 2^p$ for some $p\in {\mathbb N}^+$ with $n \le \log\log\log(1/\varepsilon)$,
\begin{align}
{\mathbb E}[T_\varepsilon^n] \ge (C_3 n\varepsilon^d\Psi(\varepsilon))^n.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.642486+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39269066fd8bb4c43d5d30f850eb6e5b23eb03fcf7dcb61e1f2f8f50ad694e42","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:orderSopen of arXiv:2603.16824, 'Age-dependent random connection models with arc reciprocity: clustering and connectivity'] Consider \(S(t_o)\) as defined in~(equation eq:defS of the paper). Then,
\begin{equation}
\int_{1/(t\log t)}^1 S(t_o)\,\mathrm{d} t_o \asymp    
	\begin{cases}
    	1 & \text{if } \gamma < \tfrac{1}{2},
		\\
		(\log t)^{1+\1{\{\Gamma=0\}}} & \text{if } \gamma = \tfrac{1}{2},
		\\
        (t\log t)^{2\gamma-1}(\log t)^{\1\{\Gamma=0\}} & \text{if } \gamma > \tfrac{1}{2}.
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In particular, if \(\gamma<1/2\), then \(\int_{1/(t\log t)}^1 S(t_o) \,\mathrm{d} t_o\) is uniformly bounded in \(t\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.876677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3928d4f78114360860dd31827a9548b41196db277f085c12f4c3414324048340","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 (Overlap criterion) of arXiv:2608.29501, 'Disorder Thresholds and Free Energy of Brownian Directed Polymers with\\ Product and Radia'] For every \(\beta>0\), consider the following three statements:
\begin{enumerate}[ leftmargin=0pt, labelsep=5pt, align=left]
  \item \(W_\infty(\beta)>0\) \quad \text{almost surely};
  \item \(\displaystyle \int_0^\infty I_s\,\dd s<\infty \quad\text{almost surely}\);
  \item \(\displaystyle W_t(\beta)\longrightarrow W_\infty(\beta) \quad\text{in }L^1(\Pp)\).
\end{enumerate}
Then \textup{(1)} and \textup{(2)} are equivalent, and both are implied by
\textup{(3)}. This is Proposition~2.8 of Rovira and
Tindel~\cite{RoviraTindel2005}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:20:55.483810+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3929414107ddb3b24a9c5b0aea0bc5c8e564337038213d0fb74da6a47896a4d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo fnerec of arXiv:2411.07158, 'Markov chains on trees: almost lower and upper directed cases'] An irreducible {\sf AUD}\xspace transition matrix ${\sf U}$ on an infinite tree $T$ with a finite number of ends is:
  \begin{itemize} \topsep0pt   \itemsep=0pt   \partopsep=0pt   \parsep=0pt \setcounter{c}{0}
  \addtocounter{c}{1}\item[($\roman{c}$)] recurrent if and only if for all end $\mathbbm{p}$ of $T$, the transition matrix ${\sf U}^{\mathbbm{p}}$ is recurrent on $T^\mathbbm{p}$,
  \addtocounter{c}{1}\item[($\roman{c}$)] positive recurrent if and only if for all end $\mathbbm{p}$ of $T$, the transition matrix ${\sf U}^{\mathbbm{p}}$ is positive recurrent on $T^\mathbbm{p}$.
  \end{itemize}\vspace{-2em}~ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.570426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_392e2e6be6dad26f5486ef0163dc9e9e6b7eb2832554b338301bfd514450c5b0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2605.30869, 'Approximate Transitivity of Young Translation on Rough Paths'] Let \( \mathbf{X} \in \mathcal{C}^{p\text{-var}}_{\mathbf{1}}\). Then for any \( r>0 \),
