{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.348862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3935fb7c7eccf06e2ae82592720ac40fd601827d88eddb7701e0739b5c6ce3ab","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:head-correction-bounds of arXiv:2608.10184, 'The sharp SAT/UNSAT phase transition in random ellipsoid fitting'] Fix $C_0<\infty$ and set $K_d=\lfloor C_0d\log d\rfloor$. Fix $Z \in \mathsf{S}^d$ and let $P\in\mathcal{K}_{\kappa_0}\cap\mathbb{S}_{\mathrm{F}}$ be a support maximizer (equation eq:P-max of the paper). Put $R_d=\max_{i\le n}|r_i|$, and suppose $R_d\le C_A\sqrt{\log d/d}$ and work on $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{data}}$.  For each nonempty $J\subseteq[n]$ with $|J|\le K_d$, let $Q_J = \mathcal{W}_J \mathcal{W}_J^*$ and define
\begin{equation}
a_J = Q_J^{-1} r_J, \qquad
E_J = -\mathcal{W}_J^* a_J , \qquad \widetilde P_J = \frac{P + E_J}{\|P + E_J\|_{\mathrm{F}}};
\end{equation}
for $J=\varnothing$, set $a_J=0$ and $E_J=0$.  For all sufficiently large $d$, simultaneously over $|J|\le K_d$,
\begin{equation}
\widetilde P_J\in \mathcal{K}_{\kappa_1}\cap\ker\mathcal{W}_J\cap\mathbb{S}_{\mathrm{F}},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\sup_{S \in \mathcal{K}_{\kappa_1} \cap \ker\mathcal{W}_J \cap \mathbb{S}_{\mathrm{F}}} \ip{Z}{S}
\;\ge\;
\sup_{R \in \mathcal{K}_{\kappa_0} \cap \mathbb{S}_{\mathrm{F}}} \ip{Z}{R}
\;-\; C\, \|Z\|_{\mathrm{F}}\, \frac{\log d}{\sqrt d}  .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.444329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39365c08e1b2ca0ddbc12fafa5595806c6a196d7f5cab9c40a16ff75fb9f4adf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm:observable-distance-eb-rate of arXiv:2608.25551, 'Beyond Optimal Rates in Stochastic Optimization: Trajectory-Adaptive Stopping Rules'] Fix $\alpha \in (0,1)$ and suppose the conditions of Theorem~(thm:observable-distance-eb of the paper) hold. Let $R_0 = G^2/\mu^2$, $\eta_t = 1/(\mu t)$, and $t_0 = 3$. Then there exist universal constants $C, C_{\mathrm r} < \infty$ such that, almost surely,
