{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:01:56.343072+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_396c0524cbe95e210e450f128e3e57a71ec166d782397da50332e9ceb0a45c7a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor non-crossing-surfaces of arXiv:2609.01499, 'On the rigidity of sloped height functions in $d\ge 3$ \\ and non-crossing surfaces'] Fix $D\ge 2$. There exists $\beta_0(D)>0$ such that for all $p\ge 1$,  $\beta>\beta_0$, and $\theta \in (0,\infty)$, the following holds. There exists an infinite-volume Gibbs measure over an infinite family $(\psi^{(i)}_v)_{v\in \mathbb{Z}^D,i\in \mathbb Z}$ of zero-slope $|\nabla\phi|^p$ height functions conditioned on non-crossing ($\psi^{(i+1)}_v \ge \psi_{v}^{(i)}$) and expected spacing $\theta$ in the sense of $\mathbb{E}[\psi_v^{(i+1)}- \psi_v^{(i)}]=\theta$ for all $v,i$, such that: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item It is unique (modulo global integer shifts) among the family of such measures with ergodic minimal gradient and expected spacing $\theta$.
        \item There exists a pair of random variables $h_0, \alpha$, where $\alpha \sim \text{Unif}[0,1]$ if $\theta \notin \mathbb Q$ and $\alpha \sim \text{Unif}\{0,...,\frac{n-1}{n}\}$ if $\theta\in \mathbb Q$ with denominator $n$, such that  in almost every extremal component, for all $k\in \mathbb{Z}$, the $k$'th surface has dominant height $h_0 + \lfloor \alpha + k \theta \rfloor$. 
        \item The family of surfaces has exponential decay of correlations in the $\mathbb Z^{D+1}$-distance within its extremal components in the sense of~(equation eq:exponential decay local functions of the paper).
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

