{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.150230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3980a9e319daa463370d6b23c842100b08994fc254583f9d0ac579cfa8ebd1da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 37 of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] Let $a,b>0$. Let $(D,\phi,-i,\infty,-1,\eta')$ (for $\gamma\in(\sqrt2,2)$) or $(\bbH,\phi,0,\infty,-1,\eta')$ (for $\gamma\in(0,\sqrt2)$) be an embedding of the surface from 
     \begin{equation}
         \int_0^\infty a\MT(\ell,b,\ell+a)\,\d\ell
    \end{equation}
    as described in the paragraph before Theorem~\ref{thm:ASYZ3.9}. Pick $r$ from the uniform probability measure on $[0,a]$, and mark the point $w$ on $(-\infty,-1)$ such that  $(-\infty,w)$ has quantum length $r$.  Let $T$ be the duration of $\eta'$ and $\tau$ be the time when $\eta'$ hits $w$. Let $\cD_1 = (\eta'([0,\tau]),\phi,\eta|_{[0,\tau]}) {/}{\sim_\gamma}$ and $\cD_2 = (\eta'([\tau,T]),\phi,\eta|_{[\tau,T]}) {/}{\sim_\gamma}$. Then for some constant $C\in(0,\infty)$, the LQG surfaces $(\cD_1,\cD_2)$ has the law
    \begin{equation}
        C\int_0^a\int_0^\infty\int_0^b \int_0^\infty\MD(a+\ell-r;b+s-t) \times \MC(r,\ell,t,s)\,\d \ell\,\d t\,\d s\,\d r.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.538667+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3982c51f1618fcbc4ad6104850f2cf03a0f796a4bdbcfed6565ad8694e75a54e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:der-psi of arXiv:2411.16913, 'Entropies of the Poisson distribution as functions of intensity: ``normal'' and ``anomalou'] For all $\alpha>0$ and $\lambda>0$, the function $\psi(\alpha, \lambda)$ introduced by (equation psifunction of the paper) is well defined and continuously differentiable in two variables, moreover, the partial derivatives can be computed as follows:
    \begin{gather}

    \frac{\partial}{\partial \alpha} \psi(\alpha, \lambda) = 
    \sum_{i=0}^\infty (p_i(\lambda))^\alpha \log p_i(\lambda),
    \\

    \frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda} \psi(\alpha, \lambda) = \alpha e^{-\alpha \lambda} \sum_{i=0}^\infty (i-\lambda) \frac{\lambda^{\alpha i - 1}}{(i!)^\alpha},
    \end{gather}
    where $p_i(\lambda) = e^{-\lambda} \frac{\lambda^i}{i!}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.165651+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_398311fac4acae9c38c398a1b00cc3cca432ffcfb2dd0b38ee42fe852868d537","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 (Lemma 4.12, \cite{HanIyerEA26}) of arXiv:2604.17526, '2604.17526'] For any $\alpha > 0$, there exist constants $C_\alpha = C_\alpha(\alpha, U)>0$ (depending on $\alpha$) and $\hat C_N = \hat C_N(U, \nu) > 1$ (independent of $\alpha$) such that for any $\delta>0$, if 
\begin{equation}
N \geq \hat C_N \frac{K^2}{\delta^2}\,,\quad
T \geq C_\alpha \paren*{K^{(1+\alpha)\hat \gamma_r} + \frac{1}{\epsilon} + \log\paren*{\frac{1}{\delta}} + \log\paren*{N}}\,,
\end{equation}
then for each $2\leq k\leq K-1$, we have
\begin{equation}
\Err_{k+1, 0}(\psi_{2, k+1}) \leq \beta_k \Err_{k,0}(\psi_{2,k}) + c_k\,.
\end{equation}

Here, the constants $\beta_k, c_k$ are such that for every $k\in \set{2,\ldots, K-1}$, we have 
\begin{align}
\prod_{j=k}^{K-1} \beta_j &\leq C_\beta\,,\\

c_k&\leq \frac{\delta}{K}\,,
\end{align}
for some dimensional constant $C_\beta > 1$ (independent of $\alpha$, $\delta$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

