{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.488123+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3994492b1d35b8c53747ee603b1cb555df78c43d16bcb1a521ef571374270be6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 (Free energy) of arXiv:2605.28473, 'The BKT transition and surface tension differentiability'] For fixed $\beta\in(0,\infty)$, the free energy is a function $f_\beta:\R\to\R$ defined by
    \begin{equation}

        f_\beta(s) := \lim_{n\to\infty}-\frac{1}{|\Lambda_n^*|}\log Z_{\Lambda_n^*,\beta}^s.
    \end{equation}
    With this normalisation, $f_\beta$ is symmetric and strictly
    convex~\cite{Sheffield_2005_RandomSurfaces}.
    We write $f'_\beta$ for its \emph{right} derivative at points where the distinction
    matters, and $f''_\beta$ for its second derivative when it exists.
    If $f'_\beta(0)>0$, then the free energy is not differentiable at $0$ and we say that
    the free energy has a \emph{corner} at $0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.828264+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39951ea752f7629c4b175aec725f7f0c8171dab285319a8d6f6d6e6fc3b5b7f2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2604.10705, '#1'] Let $s \in [0,T]$, let $a \in D([0,s],\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let $b \in D([0,T-s],\mathbb{R}^d)$. The function $\extension \in \cadlag$ is defined via
        \begin{align*}
        (a\otimes_s b)(t) := \begin{cases}
        a(t), \text{ $t < s$,}\\
        b(t-s), \text{ $t \geq s$.}
        \end{cases}
        \end{align*}
        If $a, b \in \cadlag$, $a \otimes_s b := a_{[0,s]}\otimes_s b_{[0,T-s]}$, where $a_{[0,s]}$ and $b_{[0,T-s]}$ denote the respective restriction of $a$ and of $b$ to $[0,s]$ and $[0,T-s]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:30:08.197640+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3996e02320b4694219499e134c34cf6f08d124ceae21fc2fe33587653adcd9b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:mainBound of arXiv:2608.13452, 'Wasserstein stability of the zero cell of a Poisson hyperplane tessellation under directio'] Fix \(\gamma>0\), let \(\phi_1,\phi_2\) be nondegenerate even probability measures on $S^{d-1}$, and define stationary Poisson hyperplane processes \(\Psi_1,\Psi_2\) with common intensity \(\gamma\) and directional distributions \(\phi_1,\phi_2\), respectively.
    Let $Z_0(\Psi_1)$ and $Z_0(\Psi_2)$ be the zero cells of \(\Psi_1\) and \(\Psi_2\).
    Then, for $p \in [1, \infty)$,
    \begin{align*}
        W_{\rho_p}\bigl(Z_0(\Psi_1),Z_0(\Psi_2) \bigr) 
        \leq \frac{K_p(\phi_1,\phi_2)}{\gamma} W_{\mathrm{geo}}(\phi_1,\phi_2)^{\frac{1}{p}}, 
    \end{align*} 
    and    
    \begin{align*}
        W_{\Delta}(Z_0(\Psi_1),Z_0(\Psi_2)) 
        \leq \frac{K_\Delta(\phi_1,\phi_2)}{\gamma^d} W_{\mathrm{geo}}(\phi_1, \phi_2)^{\frac{1}{d}},
    \end{align*}
    where

\begin{align} 

        K_p(\phi_1,\phi_2)
        &:= \left(\Gamma(p+1)
        \int_{S^{d-1}} \frac{h(\Pi_1,u)^p + h(\Pi_2,u)^p}{(h(\Pi_1,u) + h(\Pi_2,u)) h(\Pi_1,u)^p h(\Pi_2,u)^p} \sigma(\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} u) \right)^{\frac{1}{p}}
    \end{align}
    and
    \begin{align}

        K_{\Delta} (\phi_1,\phi_2)
        &:= \frac{K_d (\phi_1,\phi_2)}{2^{d-1}}\left(d!\int_{S^{d-1}}
             h(\Pi_1,u)^{-d} + h(\Pi_2,u)^{-d} \sigma(\mathrm{d}u) \right)^{\frac{d-1}{d}},
    \end{align}
    where $K_d (\phi_1,\phi_2)$ is the constant (equation def:Kp of the paper) for $p = d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.303097+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39986a5944f002e3cb42b61273ec3a1f28a8a2fced6de79cb82fbc1ce3c1b7c8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem berry of arXiv:2411.06006, 'Mixing time of the torus shuffle'] Let $Y_1, \dots, Y_N$ be independent random vectors in ${\mathbf R}^2$ with mean zero,
  and let $S = Y_1 + \cdots + Y_N$. Suppose that the covariance matrix $\Sigma$, of $S$, is
  invertible. Let $Z$ be a centered normal random vector with variance matrix  $\Sigma$.
Then for any convex set $U \subset {\mathbf R}^2$, we have
\begin{align}
    \left \vert { \mathbb P}(S \in U) - { \mathbb P}(Z \in U) \right \vert \leq C(2)^{\frac{1}{4}}\gamma,

