{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:58:24.524258+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39c42924f2ee38fccd58265c7b3cdfa170b9abee1517dd71dd4b4c4dfb202a12","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:domination2supply nodestrimmed of arXiv:2601.13426, 'A uniformity principle for spatial matching'] Given any constant $\gamma \geq \max\{1,\xi^{-1} \}$,  let  $\mathsf{R}\in [0, \gamma]^{n-2} $ be a vector of service ranges. For any two additional ranges $r_1, r_2$ with $ 0 \leq r_1 \leq r_2 \leq \gamma$, any $\tau\in[0,(r_2 - r_1)/2]$, any $\varepsilon \leq w_{k, \gamma}$ and all $n \geN_{k,\xi,\gamma}$, 
    \begin{align}
        \mu_m^\varepsilon\left(\mathsf{R} \oplus \left(r_1+\tau\right) \oplus \left(r_2-\tau\right)\right)-\mu_m^\varepsilon \left(\mathsf{R} \oplus r_1 \oplus r_2 \right) \geq  \, \alpha_{k,\xi,\gamma}' \, \tau^{4} - {\beta_{k,\gamma} \, \varepsilon^{-k}}/{n}, 
    \end{align}  
    where each constant $\alpha_{k,\xi,\gamma}'$ and $\beta_{k,\gamma}$ depends only on the variables in the subscript. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.159233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39c730f82a4faf41c1468d337d7fa6883cce4dc304862889115ddbcdae7545b3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] If $d/\beta<2$, then for all $p\ge1$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{h(n)^2b(n)^d}{n^2}\mathcal I_{n,q,m}^{j\rightarrow k}\distribution\alpha^{j,k}\left(\left[\frac{2m-2}{2^q},\frac{2m-1}{2^q}\right]\times\left[\frac{2m-1}{2^q},\frac{2m}{2^q}\right]\right)
    \end{equation*}
    jointly in $j,k\in[\![N]\!]$, $j\ne k$ and $q\in[\![p]\!]$, $m\in[\![2^{q-1}]\!]$. Furthermore, we also have the convergence of the first moments.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.369489+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39caba0c67676eb4469e24e4750e52b8b0fea2108a0f8525e4a713bac4471b01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 of arXiv:2606.03534, 'Construction and simulation of a path-valued model of dendrite development'] The \textit{graph representation} of a measure $\mu \in \mathcal{N}_\mathcal{S}^{\#}$ is a graph $G=(V,E)$ describing the spatial embedding of $\mu$, defined as follows. Let 
    \begin{align*}
        V &= \Big\{ x \in X \, \Big| \, \exists \, (\omega,a) \in [\mu] \text{ s.t. } x = \omega(0) \text{ or } x=\hat{\omega}  \Big\} \,, \\
        E &= \Big\{ (x,y) \in V \times V \, \Big| \, \exists \, (\omega,a) \in [\mu] \text{ s.t. } \omega(0)=x \text{ and } \hat{\omega}= y  \Big\} \,.
    \end{align*}
    To an edge $(x,y) \in E$ we assign the corresponding path $\omega_{xy} \in \mathcal{W}$ to be the element $(\omega_{xy},a) \in [\mu]$ with $\omega_{xy}(0)=x$ and $\hat{\omega}_{xy} = y$. 
    This network is fully determined by $\mu$ and so defines a mapping $\mu \mapsto (V,E)$ from $\mathcal{N}_\mathcal{S}^\#$ into a space of networks. Denote this mapping by $G(\mu)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:19.042179+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39cc3f2829e2e8f5dafa9b06d6b14200be68e212522cd3705e7ad2d92a5972e1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm-N1 of arXiv:2609.02552, 'Resolvent Estimates for the Stokes Operator \\ in a Three-Dimensional Lipschitz Domain'] Let $\Omega$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain in $\mathbb{R}^d$, $d\ge 2$.
Let $\phi \in C^1(\overline{\Omega})$  be a solution of (equation NP0 of the paper).
Then, 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{B(x, 2r)\subset \Omega}
\left(\fint_{B(x, r)} |\phi - \fint_{B(x, r)} \phi |^q \right)^{1/q}
\le C M_2 (g) (r)
\end{equation}
for any $2< q< \infty$. Moreover, 
for $x_0\in \partial\Omega$ and $0<r< r_0$,
\begin{equation}
\left(\fint_{B(x_0, r) \cap \partial\Omega}
|\phi -\fint_{B(x_0, r) \cap \partial\Omega} \phi |^p \right)^{1/p}
+
\left(\fint_{B(x_0, r) \cap \Omega}
|\phi -\fint_{B(x_0, r) \cap\partial \Omega} \phi  |^q \right)^{1/q}
\le C  M_p (g) (r), 
\end{equation}
where $2\le p< \infty$ and  $q=\frac{pd}{d-1}$. The constant $C$ 
depends only on $d$, $p$  and the Lipschitz character of $\Omega$.","status":"unverified"}

