for any test function $\varphi\in H^1(\IR^d)\cap L_\mu^2(\IR^d)$ and almost all $t\in[0,T]$ where $v(t)=e^{-X}u(t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.045518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ad34d1b62ad673f899c4176d2e3ae638957e70d8f5f01d98731d0762c2c3fca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 99 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] Let $(\sfd_{0},q,p)$ be properly tight.
Let $ R\leq  \rho_{b} $ and
 let $g$ be a Borel bounded
function on $\bar C_{ R}$ and $f\in L_{(q,p)}(C_{ R})$.
For $(t ,x )\in C_{ R}$ define
\begin{equation}  

u(t ,x )=E_{t ,x }
\int_{0}^{\tau_{ R}}f(t +s,x_{s})\,ds+
E_{t  ,x }g(t +\tau_{ R},x_{\tau_{ R}})
\end{equation}
\($\tau_{ R} $ is the first exit time of 
$(\sft_{s},x_{s})$ from $C_{ R}$\).
  Then there exists a constant $N$, which depends
only on $\delta $  and $d$, such that   
\begin{equation}

\big|u(z_{1})-u(z_{2})\big|\le N\big(R^{-\alpha}
\rho^{\alpha}(z_{1},z_{2})\sup_{\bar C_{ R}}|g|
+R^{2}\dashnorm f\|_{L_{(q,p)}(C_{ R})}\big)
 \end{equation}
for $z_{1}$, $z_{2}\in  C_{ R/2}$,
$\alpha\in(0,\alpha_{0}]$, where $\alpha_{0}$
is taken from Lemma \ref{lem:4.2.2}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.749316+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ad467f77012e5a3dd5a163a41e3b6240e5cd7d09e5eaa267fd2b4ba9683132e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 25 of arXiv:2511.20322, 'Dissertation'] Let $A : \N_0 \to \R^{d\times d}$ and $x,B : \N_0 \to \R^d$ be sequences. Then the linear recurrence
	\[x_{n+1} = A_n x_n + B_n\]
	has the solution 
	\[x_n = x_0 \prod_{k=0}^{n-1}A_k + \sum_{l=0}^{n-1}\left(\prod_{k=l+1}^{n-1}A_k\right) B_l.\]
	\begin{proof}
		The statement holds for $n = 0$. Suppose it holds for $n\in \N$. Then,
		\begin{align*}
			x_{n+1} = & A_n x_n + B_n\\
			= & x_0 \prod_{k=0}^n A_k + \sum_{l=0}^{n-1}\left(\prod_{k=l+1}^n A_k\right) B_l + B_n \\
			= & x_0 \prod_{k=0}^nA_k + \sum_{l=0}^n \left(\prod_{k=l+1}^n A_k\right) B_l.
		\end{align*}
	\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.255920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ad7b18b8ff615b0a4161d340fa7d29ba1fe68adfecc2727b426a145ee10776b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prp 43 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] If $(F,R,\mu)\in \bbF_c^{\circ}$, then the associated heat kernel $p(t,x,\cdot)$ is $\kappa$-H\"older continuous for any $t>0,\;\kappa\in [1/2,1]$, and it holds that
\begin{align*}
  \sup_{x\in F}\|p(t,x,\cdot)\|_{\Lip^\kappa}\lesssim \g{c}{unihk,1} (t^{-(1-\kappa)}+t^{-2\kappa}) ,\qquad t\in (0,\infty),\;\kappa\in [1/2,1],
\end{align*}
where $\g{c}{unihk,1}=(\g{c}{continuity,1})^{2\kappa-1}(1+\mu(F)\diam(F))^{2\kappa-1}(\mu(F)^{-1/2}+\diam(F)^{1/2})^{2(1-\kappa)}$.\\
In particular, if
$(F_n,R_n,\mu_n,\rho_n)\in \bbF_c$ converges to $(F,R,\mu,\rho)\in \bbF_c$ in the Gromov-Hausdorff-vague topology,
then there exists constant $\g{c}{unihk,2}\in (0,\infty)$ such that
\begin{align}
  \sup_{\substack{n\in\N\\x\in F_n}}\|p_n(t,x,\cdot)\|_{\Lip^{\kappa}}\vee\sup_{x\in F}\|p(t,x,\cdot)\|_{\Lip^\kappa} \leq \g{c}{unihk,2}\left(t^{-(1-\kappa)}+t^{-2\kappa}\right),\quad t\in (0,\infty),\;\kappa\in [1/2,1],
\end{align}
where $p_n$ is the heat kernel associated with $(F_n,R_n,\mu_n)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.160548+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ad9ea4aca675f3f2f63ab2448a86b7c9a3f54d04aaf1b43bf5f3e950d9634c7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma slice-over-fibrant of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\textbf{\textup{C}}$ be a model category with collection of weak equivalences ${\mathcal{W}}$, and let $Y\in\textbf{\textup{C}}$ fibrant. Then the ($\infty$-categorical) localization $\textbf{\textup{C}}\to\textbf{\textup{C}}[{\mathcal{W}}^{-1}]$ induces a localization $\textbf{\textup{C}}_{/Y}\to\textbf{\textup{C}}[{\mathcal{W}}^{-1}]_{/Y}$. If $\textbf{\textup{C}}$ is right proper, the claim holds without the fibrancy assumption on $Y$.
    \begin{proof}
        The first claim is a special case of [cisinski-book]*{Corollary 7.6.13}. For the second claim, it will then suffice that for right proper $\textbf{\textup{C}}$ and any weak equivalence $Y\to Y'$ to a fibrant $Y'$, the induced map $\textbf{\textup{C}}_{/Y}\to\textbf{\textup{C}}_{/Y'}$ becomes an equivalence after localization, which is in turn a direct consequence of [rezk-proper]*{Proposition~2.5}.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.771671+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ada64d5eed6787fb9990d35b4c75edae5f2a80787844b649eca14074ccc81e3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 48 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same parameters as in Definition \ref{Def.ParametersEdge}. We further fix $m \in \mathbb{N}$, $\kappa_1, \dots, \kappa_m$, such that 
\begin{equation}
\bar{\kappa} > \kappa_m \geq \kappa_{m-1} \geq \cdots \geq \kappa_1 \geq 0,
\end{equation}
and set $\mathcal{T} = \{\kappa_1, \dots, \kappa_m\}$. For $\kappa \in \mathcal{T}$, we define the lattice $\Lambda_{\kappa}(N) = a_{\kappa}(N) \cdot \mathbb{Z} + b_{\kappa}(N)$, where 
\begin{equation}
a_{\kappa}(N) = \sigmap^{-1} N^{-1/2}, \mbox{ and } b_{\kappa}(N) =  - \sigmap^{-1} N^{1/2} \cdot \hp(\kappa).
\end{equation}
We let $\mathbb{P}_N$ be the Pfaffian Schur process from Definition \ref{Def.SchurProcess}. Here, we assume that $M = M(N)$ is sufficiently large so that $M \geq \lfloor \kappa_m N \rfloor$. If $(\lambda^0, \dots, \lambda^M)$ have law $\mathbb{P}_N$, we define the random variables
\begin{equation}
Y_i^{j,N} = \sigmap^{-1} N^{-1/2} \cdot \left( \lambda_i^{\lfloor \kappa_j N \rfloor} - h_2(\kappa_j)  N  - i\right) \mbox{ for } i \in \mathbb{N} \mbox{ and } j \in \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

