{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.831543+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3bcbff8cb660127b824327376a2f49237c3e34a712674637ec6518ff1fc33d93","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:bounded-state-rate of arXiv:2606.25135, 'Convergence Rates for Semistochastic Processes'] Let $\{X_t\}$ be a semistochastic process with generator (equation eq:gen of the paper) 
on the state space $\mathcal{X} = [0,k]$, satisfying 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] 
$0 <  \lambda_*  \leq  \Lambda(x)  \leq  \lambda^{*}  <  \infty$ for all $ x  \in \mathcal{X} $, 
for some constants $ \lambda_*$ and~$\lambda^{*}$, 
\item[(ii)] 
$\rho(x,\alpha)  \geq  \rho_{*} $ for all $x \in \mathcal{X}$ and $ \alpha  \in  [0, 1]$, 
for some constant $\rho_* > 0$, 
\item[(iii)]
the function $v$ is non-negative,  
Lipschitz, and $v(0) \neq 0$ with $v(x)=0$ for at most finitely many $x$.
\end{itemize}

Then $\{X_t\}$ converges exponentially fast to its unique stationary distribution $\pi$. 
Namely, for any time increment $\Delta t > 0$, and any initial distribution $\mu_0$, 

\begin{equation}  
d_{\mathrm{TV}}\left( \mu_t , \pi \right)  
\leq  
\left(1-\epsilon_{\Delta t}\right)^{\lfloor t /\Delta t \rfloor} \ , 
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}  
\epsilon_{\Delta t} 
:= 
\frac{  \rho_{*}  \Phi  \lambda_*   \exp (-\lambda^{*}  \Delta t) } { k }   \ ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\Phi 
:= \int_ 0^{\phi^ {\Delta t} (0)} [\Delta t - \psi(0, z)] \, {\mathrm{d}} z  \ ,
\end{equation}
and $\phi$ and $\psi$ defined in~(equation eq:phi-psi of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.240199+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3bd028eade70996ee95e1916db44227b339fb1699d71abe285d3b33859712824","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 58 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Let $(\eta_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an ASEP on $\Z$ and $(\bar{\eta}^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an unwrapped periodic ASEP evolving  according to the $(\rho,\theta,1,N)$-reference frame basic coupling for some $\rho \in (0,1)$, $\theta \in \R$ and $N\in \N$. We set
\begin{equation}
\xi_t(x) := \begin{cases}
\one & \text{ if } \eta_t(x)=1 \text{ and } \bar{\eta}^{\per}_t(x)=1, \\
\zero & \text{ if } \eta_t(x)=0 \text{ and }\bar{\eta}^{\per}_t(x)=0, \\
\Aup & \text{ if } \eta_t(x)=1 \text{ and } \bar{\eta}^{\per}_t(x)=0, \\
\Bup & \text{ if } \eta_t(x)=0 \text{ and } \bar{\eta}^{\per}_t(x)=1, \\
\end{cases}
\end{equation} for all $x\in \Z$ and $t \geq 0$.
 We refer to $(\xi_t)_{t \geq 0}$ on the state space $\{\one,\zero,\Aup,\Bup\}^{\Z}$ as the \textbf{extended colored ASEP with reference frame $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{I}_{N}^{\rho}(\theta,\theta+1)}$}. 

 Note that in the same way, replacing the process $(\bar{\eta}^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ by a full-space ASEP $(\eta'_t)_{t \geq 0}$, we can couple two full space ASEPs under the $(\rho,\theta,1,N)$-reference frame basic coupling, i.e., we use the same Poisson clocks for all $(x,t) \in \mathcal{I}^{\rho}_N(\theta,\theta+1)$ in the two ASEPs, and independent Poisson clocks for all for all $(x,t) \notin \mathcal{I}^{\rho}_N(\theta,\theta+1)$. We refer to the resulting process as \textbf{full space extended colored ASEP under the $(\rho,\theta,1,N)$-reference frame basic coupling}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.192990+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3bd22b521c33b4ccbbe16d4cca45dba6bf124550646e4f2dcc38720bc49f49dd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Evolution of blockage.}

