{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.851138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3c74372f6475fd0a4f1d8ce1b7327c091cd7cf7f47b1f263ca940c7b99cb55f1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def.hepp_3 of arXiv:2607.10105, 'The four-dimensional Anderson model: a case study for critical SPDEs'] Let $\mathcal T$ be a Hepp tree with marking $(N_\mathfrak n)$. A mapping $(z_\mathfrak l):\mathcal L\to\mathbb Z_M^4$ is called \emph{admissible}, if it is injective (i.e. $z_\mathfrak l$ are different for different $\mathfrak l$), and satisfies that
\begin{enumerate}[{(a)}]
\item $|z_\mathfrak l-z_{\mathfrak l'}|\geq \frac{1}{2}N_{\mathfrak l\vee\mathfrak l'}$ for $\mathfrak l,\mathfrak l'\in\mathcal L$ with $\mathfrak l\neq\mathfrak l'$;
\item For any $\mathfrak n\in\mathcal B$, all its children $\mathfrak n_1,\cdots,\mathfrak n_s$ can be connected by some links between them, such that for each link $(j,k)$, there exists $\mathfrak l\in \mathcal L_{\mathfrak n_j}$ and $\mathfrak l'\in \mathcal L_{\mathfrak n_k}$ such that $|z_\mathfrak l-z_{\mathfrak l'}|\leq N_{\mathfrak n}$.
\end{enumerate} 
Let $Z$ be a (non-repeating) subset of $\mathbb Z_M^4$ with $|Z|=r$ (the number of leaves of $\mathcal T$), we define $Z\leftrightarrow (\mathcal T,N_\mathfrak n)$, if there exists a bijective mapping $(z_\mathfrak l):\mathcal L\to Z$ that is admissible.

Now, suppose we are also given the multiplicities $m_\mathfrak l$, and let $m=\sum_{\mathfrak l\in\mathcal L} m_\mathfrak l$. For any $(y_1,\cdots,y_{m})\in(\mathbb Z_M^4)^{m}$, we define $(y_1,\cdots,y_{m})\leftrightarrow (\mathcal T,N_\mathfrak n,m_\mathfrak l)$, if there exists an admissible $(z_\mathfrak l)$ such that the \emph{multiset} $\{y_1,\cdots,y_{m}\}=\cup_{\mathfrak l\in\mathcal L}\{\textrm{$m_\mathfrak l$ copies of $z_\mathfrak l$}\}$. We also write $(y_1,\cdots,y_{m})\leftrightarrow(\mathcal T,N_\mathfrak n)$ (resp. $(y_1,\cdots,y_{m})\leftrightarrow\mathcal T$), if there exists some choice of $(m_\mathfrak l)$ (resp. some choice of $(N_\mathfrak n,m_\mathfrak l)$) such that $(y_1,\cdots,y_{m})\leftrightarrow (\mathcal T,N_\mathfrak n,m_\mathfrak l)$.","status":"unverified"}

