\item For all cycle $x_{0},x_{1},\dots,x_{n}=x_{0}$ such that $\prod_{i=1}^{n} q_{i,i-1}>0$, then 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{i=1}^{n} \frac{q_{i-1,i}}{q_{i,i-1}}=1.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:27.993143+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3c9feb0a2e8e672d7ee545d294ece3132efb28d16ce463f991774b3e8509abea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem mkthm of arXiv:2608.04730, 'A new look at some aspects of one-dimensional random sequential adsorption and its continu'] Let $M_k^{(n)}$ denote the expected number of bonded molecules under $k$-bonding on a row of $n$ molecules. Then
