{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.752198+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3cc065a707e6f96afcb10125d669ba44450b1aff2e5a999fe267e5f1a31450e5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2604.11682, 'Semilocalization for inhomogeneous random graphs'] For any $\nu > 0$ there exists $C_{\nu} > 0$ such that with $\nu$-high
  probability
  \begin{equation*}
    \#\W_{\lambda, \eta} \leq 2 \Ec \left[ \#\W_{\lambda, \eta+1} \right] \vee \frac{2\nu \log N}{\log \log N}
  \end{equation*}
  whenever
 \begin{equation} 
  C_{\nu}\sqrt{\frac{\log N}{\log\log N}} < \eta < \frac\lambda2.
\end{equation}
Moreover, the expectation on the right-hand side can be estimated as follows.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item
In general, a second moment method yields
  \begin{equation*}
    \Ec \left[ \#\W_{\lambda, \eta+1} \right] \leq \frac{m_{2}}{(\lambda - \eta)^{4}}N\,.
  \end{equation*}
  \item
     When the weights are taken to be the quantiles of the a power law distribution, as in Example \ref{ex:heavy-tail}, we have
       \begin{equation*}
    \Ec \left[ \#\W_{\lambda, \eta+1} \right] = \order\pbb{\frac{N\eta}{\lambda^{2\alpha+3-\iota}} + (\log N)^{2\delta}}\,,
  \end{equation*}
for any constant $\iota > 0$ if $\lim_{t\to \infty}L(t) = \infty$ and for $\iota = 0$ if $L$ is bounded.  
  \item
   When the weights are taken to be the quantiles of the exponential distribution, as in Example \ref{ex:exponential}, we have
  \begin{equation*}
    \Ec \left[ \#\W_{\lambda, \eta+1}  \right] = \order\pbb{\frac{N}{(\alpha+1)^{(\lambda-\eta)^{2}}} + (\log N)^{2\delta}}\,.
  \end{equation*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:11:35.822886+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3cc3856139eefb9ff9623bca1b7dd10e0b4e7deac65843f0c2eb1eea0df8c838","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:agreement of arXiv:2607.22908, 'Epidemic Phase Transitions in the Zero-Range Process'] Let $f_1, f_2:\mathbb{N}_0^{\mathbb{Z}^d\times\mathbb{R}_+}\to\mathbb{R}$ be functions with support in $B^1$ and $B^2$ respectively. Define
