{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.612034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d200ab65c89f03e275deee48d24df1020c0ee9ea3928a7cac0b46546916c82d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2510.27451, 'Bi-martingale optimal transport and its applications'] The equality $\inf \eqref{eq:conv_dom} = \inf \eqref{eq:conv_dom_gammaq}$ holds true, and
an admissible pair $(\gamma,q) = (\gamma,\zeta \gamma)  \in \Gamma Q(\mu,\nu)$ is a solution to \eqref{eq:conv_dom_gammaq} if and only if
\begin{equation*}
	\rho = \zeta \# \gamma
\end{equation*}
solves the optimal convex dominance problem \eqref{eq:conv_dom}.

If, in addition, $f$ is strictly convex, then to every  $\rho$ solving \eqref{eq:conv_dom} one can assign at least one pair $(\gamma,q) = (\gamma,\zeta \gamma)  \in \Gamma Q(\mu,\nu)$  such that $\rho = \zeta \# \gamma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

