{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.454329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d30478a047e555ea506cfc75406a391b501ef75f16be163d207ab5dbbdd9416","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2607.29020, 'Entropic Symmetrization Resistance'] \label[lemma]{lem:support_size_one_or_two}
All $f \in \Sym$ satisfy $|\supp(f)| \ge 2$, 
and if $|\supp(f)| = 2$ then $f = f_{-X}$.  

\begin{proof}
Taking $Y \sim f$, the first assertion is clear: $Y$ a constant would force $Y = 0$ (as the support of $X+Y$ must be symmetric), 

contradicting asymmetry of $X$. 

Now consider $\supp(f) = \{y_1, y_2\}$ with $y_1 < y_2$.
By symmetry of $X+Y$, $(1-p)f(y_1) = pf(y_2)
= p(1-f(y_1))$; hence $f(y_1)=p$. 

\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.051858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d304e0a692298475c321deabd00ef76f9d3bdd9b53621689891578a05729edb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 1 of arXiv:2607.00853, '\sf Energy integrals and asymmetric co-potentials for closed forms'] For any $\mu, \nu \in \mathsf{S}_0$ and $\beta > \beta_0$, the total accumulated energies coincide:
\begin{equation}
\form_\beta(U_\beta \nu, U_\beta \nu) = \form_\beta(\widehat{U}_\beta \nu, \widehat{U}_\beta \nu) = \form_\beta(U_\beta \nu, \widehat{U}_\beta \nu),
\end{equation}
and the energy of the potential differences satisfies
\begin{equation}
\form_\beta(U_\beta \mu - U_\beta \nu, U_\beta \mu - U_\beta \nu) = \form_\beta(\widehat{U}_\beta \mu - \widehat{U}_\beta \nu, \widehat{U}_\beta \mu - \widehat{U}_\beta \nu).
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the function $\rho_\beta \colon \mathsf{S}_0 \times \mathsf{S}_0 \to [0, \infty)$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\rho_\beta(\mu, \nu) := \sqrt{\form_\beta (U_\beta \mu - U_\beta \nu, U_\beta \mu - U_\beta \nu)} = \sqrt{\form_\beta (\widehat{U}_\beta \mu - \widehat{U}_\beta \nu, \widehat{U}_\beta \mu - \widehat{U}_\beta \nu)}
\end{equation}
constitutes a metric on $\mathsf{S}_0$. This metric satisfies the following equivalence relation for any $\alpha, \beta > \beta_0:$
  \begin{equation}
\bigg(K_\beta \sqrt{1 \vee \frac{\beta-\beta_0}{\alpha-\beta_0}} \bigg)^{-1} \rho_\alpha(\mu, \nu) \le \rho_\beta(\mu, \nu)
\le  \bigg(K_\alpha\sqrt{1 \vee \frac{\alpha-\beta_0}{\beta-\beta_0}} \bigg) \rho_\alpha(\mu, \nu). 
\end{equation}
In particular, the topology induced by $\rho_\beta$ is independent of the choice of $\beta > \beta_0$, and $(\mathsf{S}_0, \rho_\beta)$ forms a Polish space.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.559915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d31bb54bc6e39a9a0004287df18690846a154634beb10cc8bd5ad8646fc0021","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:collection_RG_facts of arXiv:2512.21977, 'Repeat times and a two-weight UST model'] Consider the critical Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi  random graph $\mathbb{G}(n,p)$ with $p=p(n)=1/n$. The following facts hold true.
