{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.834167+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d76da90327cfb5a5f49d65e3afb6793a3840274e0f664b9b21bc0f804f29f33","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop eq:SL0_OP of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] For ${\boldsymbol{\kappa}} \in {\mathbb R}^{d_2}$, ${\kappa}_i  >  -1$ for $1 \le i \le d_2$, let ${\alpha} = d_1-1$ and 
${\boldsymbol{\epsilon}} = (0,\ldots,0,1) \in {\mathbb R}^{d_2}$. Define 

\begin{equation} 
 {\mathsf Q}_{\bm j, \ell, m}^n(\bm x,\bm y) = \hat {\bm L}_{\bm j, n-m}^{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}+ ({\alpha}+2m){\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}}(\bm y) |\bm y|^m 
      {\mathsf Y}_{\ell}^m \left(\frac{\bm x}{|\bm y|}\right). 
\end{equation}
Then $\{{\mathsf Q}_{\bm j, \ell, m}^n: |\bm j| = n-m, \, 1 \le \ell \le \dim {\mathcal H}_m^{d_1}, \, 0 \le m  \le n, \bm j \in {\mathbb N}_0^{d_2}\}$ 
is an orthogonal basis of ${\mathcal V}_n({\mathbb S}^{d_1-1}\!\! \rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, {\mathsf W}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^L)$. Moreover, 
the norm square of ${\mathsf Q}_{\bm j, \ell, m}^n$ is given by 
\begin{equation} 
   {\mathsf h}_{m,n}^{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}  
       =   \frac{{\mathsf b}^L_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}+ \alpha{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}}}{{\mathsf b}^L_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}+(\alpha + 2m) {\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}}} \bm h_{\bm j,m}^{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}+({\alpha}+ 2m){\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}, L}
       =  ({\kappa}_{d_2} +d_1)_{2m}  \bm h_{\bm j,m}^{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}+({\alpha}+ 2m){\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}, L}.  
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.521613+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d774158926a174c6f55d57054b77edefec4f0932f5cc6ae3438b6e3df077dbb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem : Cool/GoodCase of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] Let $(\tau'_e)_{e \in \mathbb{E}^d}$ be a family of identically distributed random variables. Assume that there exists $\xi>0$ such that $\E{(\tau_e')^{d+\xi}} < \infty$, and for all $e\in \mathbb{E}^d$, $\tau_e'$ is independent of the family passage times along edges having no common endpoint with $e$. Then there exists a constant $\kappa<\infty$ such that 
    \begin{equation}

        \liminf_{n\to\infty} \min_{x\in \intint0n^d} \Pb{\begin{array}{c} \text{For all $y \in \intint0n^d$, there exists a discrete path $x\Path\pi y$ included} \\ \text{in $\intint0n^d$, such that } \tau'(\pi) \le \kappa \norme[1]{x-y} \text{ and } \norme{\pi} \le \norme[1]{x-y} +2\end{array}} >0.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.465568+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d774df6045a1a14d95509cddf48adc823d684a20bc2c1ebb6877a25d98ef001","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2606.29131, '2606.29131'] Suppose that \eqref{t1-2-1} holds for some $\beta>0$. Let $\Upsilon_\e^2 f(x,y)$ be defined in \eqref{e3-3a}. Then there exists a positive constant $C_4:=C_4(\beta)$ such that for every $f\in \M_\beta$, $x\in \R^d$ and $y\in \T^d$,
\begin{equation}
|\Upsilon_\e^2 f(x,y)|\le C_4\left(\sum_{i=0}^3\|f\|_{\M_{\beta},i}\right)(1+|x|)^{-d-\beta}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.812807+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d7884644b5d347b12edae3b654396b4a1df9b3b07ed50a2d1b9a32a855c686f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:integral of arXiv:2411.09482, 'Anomalous Regularization in Kazantsev-Kraichnan Model'] The Mellin transform of the function
    \begin{align*}
        f_{a,b,s}(t)=t^a \int_0^\pi \frac{(\sin\theta)^b }{(1-2t \cos \theta+t^2)^s} \,\mathrm{d} \theta,
        \quad a\in\mathbb{R},\,b,s>0,
    \end{align*}
    is absolutely convergent for $-a<\re z<2s-a$ and it is given by
    \begin{align}
        M[f_{a,b,s},z] =\frac{\sqrt\pi \Gamma\left(\frac{b-2s+2}{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\frac{b+1}{2}\right)}{2\Gamma(s)}
        \cdot \frac{\Gamma\left(\frac{2s-z-a}{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\frac{z+a}{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\frac{b-a-z+2}{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\frac{b+a-2s+2+z}{2}\right)}.
    \end{align}
    The analytic continuation of $M[f_{a,b,s},z]$ is a meromorphic function with only simple poles, which are contained in the set
    \begin{align*}
        (2s-a+2\mathbb{N}) \cup (-a-2\mathbb{N})
    \end{align*}
    of zeros of the denominator of (equation eq:mellinf of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.724188+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d7ad4fc15e061cf24e0e9145a40fa72ca4c373dd1dc6041caa4de41b82402cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop.localtimesBRW of arXiv:2411.19145, 'On the intersection of critical percolation clusters and other tree-like random graphs'] There exists $C>0$, such that for any $n\ge 1$, and any $x_1,\dots,x_n,z \in \mathbb Z^d$ (possibly with repetition), with $d\ge 5$, 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_z\Big[\prod_{i=1}^n \ell_c(x_i)\Big] \le C^n \sum_{\mathfrak t\in \mathbb T_n} G_{d-2}(\mathfrak t,\underline x)\cdot g_{d-2}(x_{\mathfrak t},z),
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_z\Big[\prod_{i=1}^n \ell_\infty(x_i)\Big] \le C^n \Big(\sum_{\mathfrak t\in \mathbb T_n^\circ} \widetilde G(\mathfrak t,\underline x,z) + \sum_{\mathfrak t\in \mathbb T_n} G_{d-2}(\mathfrak t,\underline x)\cdot g_{d-4}(x_{\mathfrak t},z)\Big). 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.846572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d7d450c39a25d9164a6f07cfb864072439616e31a14554ed72c03a16a32aa55","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:GaussianMatrix1 of arXiv:2410.23915, 'Asymptotic Bounds and Online Algorithms for Average-Case Matrix Discrepancy'] The probability density function of the measure $\mathrm{GOE}(m)$ is given by
        \begin{equation*}
            X \mapsto K_m \exp{-\frac{1}{4}\tr(X^2)}
        \end{equation*}
        on the space of $m \times m$ symmetric matrices, where the normalization constant $K_m$ is defined as
        \begin{equation}

            K_m \colonequals 2^{-m/2}(2\pi)^{-m(m+1)/4}.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.953160+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d84a2b537b568478a3ca40de123c2daea167fe8f4c1c3df72293e097e54c507","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2512.23957, 'Concentration and fluctuations of sine-Gordon measure around topological multi-soliton man'] Let $Q \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $Q \neq 0$. 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] Under the ensemble $\rho^Q_\varepsilon$ with $Q>0$, the field $\phi$ exhibits the typical behavior
\begin{align*}
\phi(x) \approx \sum_{j=1}^{Q} m(x-\xi_j) +\varepsilon^{\frac 12} \cdot \textup{Ornstein–Uhlenbeck}
\end{align*}

\noindent
with 
\begin{align*}
\min_{ i \neq j} |\xi_j-\xi_i| \ge \big|\log (\varepsilon \log \tfrac 1\varepsilon) \big| \to \infty
\end{align*}

\noindent 
as $\varepsilon \to 0$ and $L_\varepsilon \to \infty$. When $Q<0$ the soliton $m$ is replaced by $m^{-}$.

\medskip 

\item[(2)] 
As $\varepsilon \to 0$, the joint distribution of the centers $(\xi_1,\dots, \xi_{|Q|} )$ is the ordered statistics of $|Q|$ independent uniform random variables. In particular, each marginal $\xi_{(j)}$ has a Beta distribution. Consequently, the expected position and the expected gap are 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[\xi_{(j)}]\approx-L_\varepsilon+\frac{2L_\varepsilon j}{|Q|+1}, \qquad  \mathbb{E}[\xi_{(j)}-\xi_{(j-1)}]\approx \frac{2L_\varepsilon}{|Q|+1},
\end{align*}

\noindent 
where $\xi_{(j)}$ denotes the $j$-th ordered center in increasing rearrangement $\xi_{(1)}\le \cdots \le \xi_{(|Q|)}$. 

\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.749016+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d85da36de2ced90299a1a0ed77734559758fba2e61dfc929a25ee7ed7dc9b83","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 21 of arXiv:2511.20322, 'Dissertation'] Let $\cR : \R^d \to \R \in \dC 2$ be $\la$-strongly convex and $L$-smooth with $\nabla \cR(0) = 0$ and $\nabla^2 \cR$ Hölder continuous.
Let $X$ be locally Hölder continuous and assume that $X$ does not vanish on any closed interval of positive measure.
Let $Y_0 = Z_0 \in \R^d$, and $Y, Z$ be the solutions to the Young differential equations
\begin{align*}
dY_t = &-u_t \nabla \cR(Y_t)\,dt + u_t\,dX_t,\\
dZ_t = &- u_t \nabla^2 \cR(0) Z_t\,dt + u_t\,dX_t, \quad t\geq 0.
\end{align*}
Let $f$ be regularly varying with negative index and assume $|Z_t| \leq f(t), t\to \infty$.
Then also
\[|Y_t| \leq f(t) + o(f(t)), \quad t\to \infty.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.670418+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d890c49e07e8d577ac5b9233be8617856583401b264ecffd86f94312f81ff32","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 30 of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] If $1\leq d<n/2$, exactly one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
 \item there is a nonzero $g\in\cE_n^-$ such that $g_0=0$ and
 $g_i\geq0$ for $d\leq i\leq n$;
 \item there is an $h\in\cE_n^+$ such that
 \begin{equation}
  h_0=1,\qquad h_i=0\quad(1\leq i<d),\qquad
  h_i>0\quad(d\leq i\leq n).

 \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
In case \textup{(ii)}, the vector $A_i=w_ih_i$ is feasible for
$\operatorname{LP}_n(d)$ and has objective $2^{n/2}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.871769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d8a9113c840993081e513ae9e433fa1959da972b8ae3456dc16ddb8b4992861","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:fully_quadratic_val_sec2 of arXiv:2608.24963, 'Why and When Neural Networks Improve Local Approximation in Optimization'] Assume that $f$ is twice continuously differentiable on $\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$. A model $m_k$ is said to be \emph{fully quadratic} on $\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$ if there exist constants $\kappa_f,\kappa_g,\kappa_H>0$, independent of $k$, such that for all $x\in\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$,
\begin{equation}
    |f(x)-m_k(x)| \le \kappa_f\Delta_k^3,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \|\nabla f(x)-\nabla m_k(x)\| \le \kappa_g\Delta_k^2,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \|\nabla^2 f(x)-\nabla^2 m_k(x)\| \le \kappa_H\Delta_k.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.776321+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d8c140dab4321b6e702125c1b2e91c0826c462cf8ddde8fc3ad731a98891308","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 10 (Hermite polynomials and differential operators) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The Hermite polynomials are eigenfunctions of $\cL$. More precisely,
