    where $\Phi(w)$ denotes the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal distribution. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.681755+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3de325ec5629a943bfce58bf61c0170c5f13d8d961b66d0d26196c16e8fb0878","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-lf-conditioned of arXiv:2605.03385, 'Quantum Loewner evolution in quantum natural time: phases and Markov properties'] Let $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$, $\beta < Q$, and $s \in \partial \mathbb{D}$. Sample $h$ from $P_\mathbb{D}$ and set
\begin{equation*}
    \tilde h(z) = h(z) + \alpha G_\mathbb{D}(z,0)+\frac{\beta}{2}G_\mathbb{D}(z,s)
\end{equation*}
Fix $\ell > 0$ and let $L = \mathcal{L}^\gamma_{\tilde h}(\partial \mathbb{D})$, and define the law $\mathrm{LF}_{\mathbb{D}, \ell}^{(\alpha, 0), (\beta, s)}$ to be that of $\tilde h + \frac{2}{\gamma}\log \frac{\ell}{L}$ under the reweighted law $\frac{2}{\gamma} \ell^{\frac1\gamma(2\alpha + \beta - 2Q) - 1} L^{- \frac1\gamma(2\alpha + \beta - 2Q)} \mathbb{P}_{\mathbb{D}}(dh)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:55:57.005564+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3de6af301151c361353695a52a67f220925fd66bc0d725e836bdb2ebcc2fb8a1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 20 (Deterministic error bound \cite{halko2011finding}) of arXiv:2609.02790, 'Full-Model Optimality for Tunable Linear Generative Priors in Compressed Sensing'] Let $A$ be an $m \times n$ matrix with SVD $A=U\Sigma V$, and fix $k \geq 0$. Choose a test matrix $\Omega$, and construct the sample matrix $Y=A \Omega$. Partition $\Sigma$ as 

\[
A = U
\begin{bmatrix}
\Sigma_1 & \\
& \Sigma_2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_1^{*} \\
V_2^{*}
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\Sigma_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times k},\;
\Sigma_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{(n-k)\times(n-k)}.
\]
and define $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$ via $\Omega_1=V_1^\star  \Omega$ and $ \Omega_2=V_2^\star  \Omega$. Assuming that $\Omega_1$ has full row rank, the approximation error satisfies
