{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.631747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e15e92f4d6d174547242e79181489d468873107f973795a1e82125db8976c5e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:discrete clt of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $\mu=\mu_n$ satisfy $\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mu/n^{1/4}=\gamma>0$. Assume that $t_{\mu/n}-\floor{t_{\mu/n}}$ admits a limit $\delta\in[0,1]$.
    Then the asymptotic of the height $h(\mathbf{T}_n)$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        h(\mathbf{T}_n)-\floor{t_{\mu/n}}+2\overset d\longrightarrow X_{\gamma,\delta},
    \end{align*}
    where $X_{\gamma,\delta}$ is an integer-valued random variable distributed as
    \begin{align*}
        \Prob{X_{\gamma,\delta}=k\big.}=\frac{1}{C_{\gamma,\delta}}\exp\left(-3\left(\frac{\gamma^2}{4\pi}\right)^{2/3}\big(k-\delta\big)^2\right)\,,\;\;k\in \mathbb Z
    \end{align*}
    with $C_{\gamma,\delta}$ being a normalizing constant corresponding to the sum over $k\in\mathbb Z$ of the exponential term. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.483717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e1619289c2af25603b66ee7c01bc64992cf9fb78b0134a08378f4a8d5e3d782","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 122 (Optimal chaos-order scale up to exponential factors) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $C_0\ge1$ be an absolute constant for which
Theorem~\ref{thm:m-finite} holds with $C_m\le C_0^m$.  Then, for every
$m\ge1$,
\begin{equation}

  e^{-m/2}\le\mathsf D_m^{\mathbb R}\le C_0^m,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

  (2e)^{-m/2}\le\mathsf D_m^{\mathbb C}\le C_0^m.
\end{equation}
In the same-field Wick model,
\begin{equation}

  \left(\frac me\right)^{m/2}
  \le \mathsf W_m
  \le \mathsf R_m\mathsf D_m^{\mathbb R}
  =m^{m/2}\mathsf D_m^{\mathbb R}
  \le C_0^m m^{m/2}.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}

  1\le
  \frac{\mathsf W_m}{(m/e)^{m/2}}
  \le(C_0\sqrt e)^m.
\end{equation}
There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $C<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}

  c\,m^{-1/4}\sqrt{m!}
  \le\mathsf W_m
  \le C^m m^{-1/4}\sqrt{m!}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}

  \log\mathsf W_m
  =\frac12m\log m+O(m)
  \qquad(m\to\infty).
\end{equation}
Thus the regression contribution is exact; the remaining uncertainty in the
upper bound is only the exponential factor in the decoupled tensor
constant.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.860287+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e181a9e6bb9be23c4a1d5a3749abe739f5b3cd7af84074e15406da29d7c82c4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2609.03084, 'Convergence rates to traveling waves for an FKPP model with nonlinear advection'] Fix $\chi \in[0,1]$ and $A$ satisfying~\eqref{A_assumptions}.  Let $u$ be a solution to~\eqref{main_eq} with ``steep initial data'' $u_0$ 
