{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.609507+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e2771739792cdb58b9b8b3bb1808f3fbbb1777d421dfaa5ac192bb258ce07e5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-6.1 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Let~$b\ge2$. There exists~$\epsilon>0$ and, for each~$\beta>0$ satisfying $1<b\theta<1+\epsilon$, there exists a unique $\boldsymbol\lambda_\star\in\Sigma'$ such that
\begin{equation}

F(\boldsymbol\lambda_\star) = \boldsymbol\lambda_\star.
\end{equation}
Moreover, under Assumption~(ass-1 of the paper), for each~$\beta$ as above there exist~$\eta>0$ and~$C>0$ and, for each $\boldsymbol\lambda_{0}\in\Sigma'$, there exists $k_0\ge0$ such that, for all~$n>2k_0$,
the sequence $\{\boldsymbol\lambda_k\}_{k=0}^n$ defined from~$\boldsymbol\lambda_{0}$ via (equation E:1.5 of the paper) obeys 
\begin{equation}

