where $p^{(i)}_{j,k}$ (depends on $j$, $k$, and $H_{i-1}$ via $d_i(v_i)$) is equal to the minimum of $1$ and \eqref{eq:jk}, \eqref{eq:k}, or \eqref{eq:j} if their respective assumptions are satisfied.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.615181+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ea6dd42fe6d54df2611a767600965d0ddeb76c894e06638db54e92f9b2064b2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma EHI:l:FactorOf2ForExits of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an isotropic unimodal L\'{e}vy jump process on $\mathbb{R}^d$ starting at $X_0=0$, let $R>0$, and let $T$ be a stopping time such that the conditional distribution of $X_T$ given $\tau_{B(0, R)} \leq T$ is isotropic unimodal centered at $\tau_{B(0, R)}$. Then
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{P}_0 \left( |X_T| \geq R \right) \leq \mathbb{P}_0 \left( \tau_{B(0, R)} \leq T \right) \leq 2 \mathbb{P}_0 \left( |X_T| \geq R \right).
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.733168+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ea7accfcadc6657d4200210650d087e38e3f5fb657da1bea43f48623f61688f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:PDdeterministicDiagonalBound of arXiv:2609.02743, 'Sharp mean-field estimates for diffusive log/Riesz gases in the Hilbert--Schmidt regime'] Let $q$ be a bounded positive-definite kernel, and let $\tilde{q}$ denote its $\mu$-centering with respect to a probability measure $\mu$.  Then
\begin{align}
\|\tilde q\|_{L^\infty}\le 4\sup_x q(x,x),
\end{align}
and, for every $N\ge1$ and every configuration $X_N=(x_1,\ldots,x_N)$,
\begin{align}
\sum_{1\le i,j\le N}\tilde{q}(x_i,x_j)\ge0.
\end{align}
Consequently,
\begin{align}
-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{1\le i\ne j\le N}\tilde{q}(x_i,x_j)
\le \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \tilde{q}(x_i,x_i)
\le \sup_x \tilde{q}(x,x)
\le 4\sup_x q(x,x).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.285616+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ea9fe9e3f7cba269590cbae64dc6ea7d16bd15eb8dadec5c0e73fe28649e4a3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Homogeneous $p$-variation norm) of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Let $p\geq 1$. For any path $\mathbf{x}:[0,T]\rightarrow G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k)$,
    \begin{equation*}
        [0,T]\ni t\mapsto \mathbf{x}_t := \left(1,\mathbf{x}^{(1)}_t,\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(\lfloor p\rfloor)}_t\right) \in G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k),
    \end{equation*}
    the homogeneous $p$-variation norm of $\mathbf{x}$ is defined as
    \begin{equation*}
        \lVert \mathbf{x}\rVert_{\textup{hom $p$-var};[0,T]} := \sup_{D} \left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} d_{cc}(\mathbf{x}_{t_i},\mathbf{x}_{t_{i+1}})^p\right)^{1/p},
    \end{equation*}
    where $D=D_{[0,T]}$ is a partition of the time interval $[0,T]$. Then, for any $\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}:[0,T]\rightarrow G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k)$, the homogenous $p$-variation distance between $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ is defined as
    \begin{equation*}
        d_{\textup{hom $p$-var};[0,T]}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}) := \sup_{D} \left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} d_{cc}(\mathbf{x}_{t_i,t_{i+1}},\mathbf{y}_{t_i,t_{i+1}})^p\right)^{1/p},
    \end{equation*}
    where $\mathbf{x}_{t_i,t_{i+1}}:=\mathbf{x}_{t_i}^{-1}\bullet \mathbf{x}_{t_{i+1}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.299196+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3eab44eabfe80cbab777f09016ac771ab953272a984e471c0ee5074abf9d93a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo main result of arXiv:2604.21663, 'Large deviations for non-irreducible Markov chains on Euclidean spaces'] Assume~(hyp: pseudo lsc density of the paper),~(hyp: pseudo uniformity of the paper),~(hyp: null border of the paper) and~(hyp: getting fast to classes of the paper). Then, 
	$(L_n)$ satisfies the weak LDP with rate function $I$ satisfying 
