{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.446402+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ec1b6e56904079bdd7bacc8db0dc386e0813d8622cfa4c515fd8da71ec4098a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] suppose that $f,g\in L^1(\R^d, \C)$ are Fourier-smooth with exponents $p,q\in[-d,\infty)$. Then $f\ast g$ is Fourier-smooth with exponent $d+p+q$.
    \begin{proof}
        Let $c$ be the joint constant such that~(equation e: fourier smooth 12 of the paper) holds for $f,g$ with their respective constants.
        Then the first part of the Fourier-smoothness of $f\ast g$ is obtained by
        \begin{equation*}
            |\widehat{(f\ast g)}(k)| = |\hat{f}(k)\hat{g}(k)| \leq c^2\frac{1}{(1+\|k\|)^\frac{d+(d+p+q)}{2}},\quad k\in\R^d. 
        \end{equation*}
        The second part follows from
        \begin{align*}
            \int_{B_2^c} \sup_{s\in B_1} |\widehat{(f\ast g)}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk &\leq \int_{B_2^c} \sup_{s\in B_1} |\hat{f}(r(k+s))|^2|\hat{g}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk\nonumber\\
            &\leq c\int_{B_2^c} \frac{\sup_{s\in B_1} |\hat{f}(r(k+s))|^2}{(1+r(\|k\|-1))^{d+q}}\, dk\nonumber\\
            &\leq cr^{-(d+q)}\int_{B_2^c} \sup_{s\in B_1} |\hat{f}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk\nonumber\\
            &= O(r^{-(d+d+p+q)})
        \end{align*}
        as $r\to\infty$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.331891+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ec567828f2698a8bedbbd000aee6422821244d2fb131d0988d0a4ddb1a444ad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Upper on-diagonal heat kernel bound) of arXiv:2607.14718, 'Anchored Nash inequalities and heat kernel bounds for a class of random conductance models'] Let $d \geq 3$ and suppose that Assumption~\ref{a2-2} holds.

  \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
