{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.331968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fbd6a0452a31a2cda87c54f88693c09fa6a67709ab4c718604f137f498aee4e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop S-T-slice-univ-prop of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $S\subset T$ be a cleft, and let $X\in\Spc_T$. The Yoneda embedding $y\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{X}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}\to\ul\Spc_{T/X}$ factors uniquely through $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}\coloneqq\smash{\ul\Spc_{T/X}\times_{\ul\Spc_T}\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T}}$, exhibiting the latter as $S$-cocompletion. Moreover, given any commutative monoid structure $X^\otimes$ on $X$, $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}$ is a symmetric monoidal subcategory with respect to the slice symmetric monoidal structure on $\smash{\ul\Spc_{T/X}}$, and $\smash{y^\otimes\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{X}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}^\otimes\to\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}^\otimes}$ is a symmetric monoidal $S$-cocompletion.
    \begin{proof}
        Proposition~(prop:spc-T-cart-pres-sym-mon of the paper) shows that $\smash{\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}^\otimes}$ is a symmetric monoidal subcategory for any commutative monoid $X^\otimes$. In light of the previous proposition, it will therefore once more suffice to prove the non-monoidal universal property. By [martiniwolf2021limits]*{Theorem 7.1.13} this reduces to showing that $\smash{\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}}\subset\ul\Spc_{T/X}$ is the smallest subcategory containing the Yoneda image and closed under $S$-colimits.

        By design, $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T}\subset\ul\Spc_T$ contains the terminal object, and so the description of the Yoneda image from Remark~(rk:slice-vs-shift of the paper) shows that the Yoneda embedding factors through $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}$. Moreover, as $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T}\subset\ul\Spc_T$ is closed under $S$-colimits, so is $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}\subset\ul\Spc_T$. Conversely, let $A\in T$ be arbitrary, let $a\colon A\to 1$ be the unique map in $\PSh(T)$, and let $Y\to a^*X$ define an element of $\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T/X}(A)\subset(\PSh(T)_{/A})_{/X\times A}$; we want to show that $Y$ is an $S$-colimit of objects in the Yoneda image, which translates to saying that every $(f,g)\colon Z\to X\times A$ with $Z\in\Spc_S\subset\Spc_T$ is an $S$-colimit in $\ul\Spc_{T}(X\times{-})$ of objects in the Yoneda image. But it follows straight from the definitions that $(f,g)$ is the image under $f_!$ of $(\textup{id},g)\colon X\to X\times A$, and the latter belongs to the Yoneda image by Remark~(rk:Yoneda-image of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.688415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fbdb3ab3281b4849a779d8007d5ed729390c1e13675b86ffcdf4ecf70cce136","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor thmLogAsymptotics of arXiv:2411.18563, 'Lower tails for triangles inside the critical window'] Fix $0 < c < e^{-1/2}$. If $p =(1+o(1)) c n^{-1/2}$, then
    \begin{equation}
     \lim_{n \to \infty}   \frac{1}{n^{3/2}} \log \mathbb{P}_p(X=0) 
     = \frac{1}{2} \left[  \frac{W(2 c^2)^{3/2} +3W(2 c^2)^{1/2}}{3\sqrt{2}} - c\right]\, .
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.491648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fbe6ed3d0aa7baab467810d633b8b342a1e8bbe57b568ba78c7ec39cc76567d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 231 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] The \emph{spectral curve} associated to the above data and a non-zero $\boldsymbol{v} \in \amsmathbb{R}^H$ is the Riemann surface
\begin{equation}

\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{v}} := \left(\bigsqcup_{\boldsymbol{w} \in \mathfrak{G}.\boldsymbol{v}} \widehat{\amsmathbb{H}}^{+} \sqcup \widehat{\amsmathbb{H}}^-\right) \Big/ \sim.
\end{equation}
Here, $\widehat{\amsmathbb{H}}^{\pm} \subset \widehat{\amsmathbb{C}}$ is the upper (respectively, lower) hemisphere of the Riemann sphere in which we include $\widehat{\amsmathbb{R}}^{\pm} := \amsmathbb{R} \cup \{\infty\} = \partial \widehat{\amsmathbb{H}}^{\pm}$. We call $\amsmathbb{B}^\pm$ the segment $\amsmathbb{B}$ inside $\widehat{\amsmathbb{R}}^{\pm}$, and likewise for $\overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{h}^{\pm}$. The equivalence relation consists in identifying
\begin{itemize}
\item $\widehat{\amsmathbb{R}}^+ \setminus \amsmathbb{B}^+$ with $\widehat{\amsmathbb{R}}^- \setminus \amsmathbb{B}^-$ in the same copies;
\item $\overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{h}^{+}$ in the $\boldsymbol{w}$-th copy with $\overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{h}^{-}$ in the $T^{(h)}(\boldsymbol{w})$-th copy, for each $\boldsymbol{w} \in \mathfrak{G}.\boldsymbol{v}$ and $h \in [H]$.
\end{itemize}
For $\boldsymbol{w} \in \mathfrak{G}.\boldsymbol{v}$ we denote
\begin{equation}

\overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\pm} = \bigcup_{g \in \textnormal{supp}(\boldsymbol{w})} \overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{g}^{\pm} .
\end{equation}
We use the same notation for the image of \eqref{Bamhbw} in $\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{v}}$, and call
\begin{equation}

\overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{\boldsymbol{w}} := \overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{\boldsymbol{w}}^+ \cup \overline{\amsmathbb{B}}_{\boldsymbol{w}}^- \subset \Sigma_{\boldsymbol{v}}.
\end{equation}
the $\boldsymbol{w}$-th \emph{gluing locus}. The $\boldsymbol{w}$-th \emph{sheet}, denoted $\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{v},\boldsymbol{w}}$ is the image in $\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{v}}$ of the $\boldsymbol{w}$-th copy of $\widehat{\amsmathbb{H}}^+ \sqcup \widehat{\amsmathbb{H}}^-$ in \eqref{wcopydoubleplae}, from which we remove the $\boldsymbol{w}$-th gluing locus \eqref{wgluloc}. The $\boldsymbol{v}$-th sheet is called the \emph{principal sheet}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.120567+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fc22dc563ae91b5d5fc942cd6bda304b3900eef6abefbd27163c9f555a912c3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 5 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Assume that $\P_0[X_1^2]<\infty$. If a function $f\in C(\R)$ satisfies the Sturm--Liouville equation (\ref{main-SLeq}), then the following growth conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[i.] (\ref{main-SLgrowth}) holds.
  \item[ii.] For every $z\in \R$,
  \begin{align}

    \lim_{x\to \pm \infty}\Big(f(x+z)-f(x)\Big)=\pm \frac{z}{\P_0[X_1^2]}.
  \end{align}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.474452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fc3d918681326190ca790b0dca6daa3b39a636b96b20aae2cc96d3c0e2534ae","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 62 (Reconstruction lattice) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Fix $m\ge1$, $1\le r<\infty$, and a two-sided block support system indexed
by $I=\Lambda\times\cM$.  A solid Banach sequence lattice $E(I)$ is called
an $r$-reconstruction lattice for this system if
there is $C_{E,r}<\infty$ such that every finite block kernel family obeys
\begin{equation}

 \Prof_{m,r}(\sum_{i\in I}K_i)
 \le C_{E,r}
 \norm{(\Prof_{m,r}(K_i))_{i\in I}}_{E(I)}.
\end{equation}
It is called order continuous if, for every $x\in E(I)$,
\begin{equation}

 \norm{x\mathbf1_{I\setminus F}}_{E(I)}\longrightarrow0
\end{equation}
along the directed set of finite subsets $F\Subset I$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.898444+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fc41ceaff7dfc840b023f47cf2093ee46d5b9bd4c4a6ec6c14c98186825223d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:sublinearCorrector_martingaleFCLT of arXiv:2411.06861, 'Quenched invariance principle for random walks in random environments admitting a cycle de'] Suppose $d\geq 2$, Assumption ~(ass:P of the paper)-(i) and Assumption ~(ass:p-q-moment of the paper) hold. Let $\chi, \Phi$ be as constructed in Lemma (lem:corrector_construction of the paper). Then 
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
    \item $\chi$ is sublinear on a large scale, i.e. for $\mathbb{P}$-a.e. $\omega$,  and all $i=1, \dots, d,$
    \begin{align}

    \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\norm{\chi^i(\omega, \cdot)}_{\infty, B(n)}=0; 
    \end{align}
    \item  $M:= (\Phi(\cdot, X_t))_{t\geq 0}$ satisfies the quenched invariance principle, i.e. QFCLT holds for $M$ with a deterministic nondegenerate covariance matrix $\Sigma^2$ given by 
\begin{align}

    \Sigma^2_{i,j}=\mathbb{E}\Big[\sum_{z\in \mathcal{N}} c_{\mathrm{s}}^{\omega}(0, z)\Phi^i(\omega, z) \Phi^j(\omega, z) \Big],
\end{align}
for $1\leq i, j \leq d;$
\item $\chi$ vanishes along random walks under the diffusive scaling, i.e., 
for $\mathbb{P}$-a.e. $\omega$, and all $i=1, \dots, d$, $T>0$, 
\begin{align}

    \lim_{n\to \infty}\sup_{t\in [0, T]} \frac{1}{n}\abs{\chi^i(\omega, X_{n^2t})}=0 \qquad \text{in } \Prob_0^\omega.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:14.934853+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fc85099ed57b7bbb86b3e26321744713f0306749a3d8aaf03a5846668237dd7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2607.11756, 'Full replica symmetry breaking in the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model'] Let $\mu=\mu_\beta$.  For every $q\in\supp\mu$,
