{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.524643+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fc90d183b0ed78173111f84c7d570e72fabc5a52163d19da1cdb901ea48a2d8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Master loop equation operator of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] For $(s,K,Q) \in \Omega^\circ$, if $s$ has at least one good edge, let $\mbf{e}$ be its designated good edge and define
\begin{equs}
(Mf)(s,K,Q):=\mp \sum_{s' \in \mbb{S}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e})} f(s', K, Q) \mp \frac{1}{N^2} \sum_{s' \in \mbb{M}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e})} f(s', K, Q) \mp \frac{\beta}{N} \sum_{(s', K') \in \mbb{D}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e}, K)} f(s', K', Q).
\end{equs}
If $(s,K,Q) \in \Omega^\circ$ is stuck in the minimal union of connected components $Q'$, set 
\begin{equs}
(Mf)(s, K, Q) := \sum_{\substack{ J: P_{\Lambda} \to \N \\ \varnothing \neq \mathrm{supp}(J) \sse \partial Q' \\ J \leq K}} \frac{(N\beta)^J}{J!} f((s,K) \Delta_{\partial Q} J,Q').
\end{equs}
Lastly if $(s,K,Q) \in \partial\Omega$, set $(Mf)(s,K,Q):=\phi(s,K)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:25:39.085800+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fc9fa5d4fefd89347f7f0d909d2cf2c37b8a50b0f689f5e500ca0d2f7277554","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:es_conA of arXiv:2606.12143, 'Continuous stochastic flows driven by white noise and their duals'] Under Assumptions~(assu:H of the paper) and~(assu:A of the paper), the following properties hold.
\vspace{-0.5em}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item For any $x,y,t\in {\mathbb R}$ and $\alpha,\beta\in \{1,2\}$,
    $$
    \partial_x^\alpha \partial_y^\beta a(x,y,t) = \int_{\mathbb R} \partial_x^\alpha \sigma(x,t,s) \partial_y^\beta \sigma(y,t,s)\ensuremath{\mathrm{d}} s.
    $$
    In particular, $\partial_x \bar{\sigma}(x,t) = 2 \int_{\mathbb R} \sigma(x,t,s)\partial_x\sigma(x,t,s)\ensuremath{\mathrm{d}} s $.
    \vspace{-0.5em}

    \item The functions $a$ and $b$ belong to the classes $\widetilde{C}^{2,1}_{ub}$ and $C^{1,1}_{ub}$, respectively; see [Ku90] for the definitions of these spaces.
    \vspace{-0.5em}

    \item The coefficients $(\sigma,b)$ satisfy conditions~(item:1a of the paper)--(item:1d of the paper).
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.360754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fcbb87fee865c17f90756312686107b904868e25411aa3be786c6a77672c893","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 (Real $R$-transform) of arXiv:2506.19064, 'Variational formula for the logarithmic potential of free additive convolutions'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\mathbb{R}$ with compact support. There exists 
    \begin{equation}
        \text{a unique open interval }D_\mu\subseteq\mathbb{R} \text{ s.t. }\hat{D}_\mu \subseteq D_\mu\text{, and}
    \end{equation}

    \begin{equation}
        \text{a unique real analytic function } R_\mu:D_\mu\to\mathbb{R},
    \end{equation}
    such that for any open interval $D\subset\mathbb{R}$ containing $\hat{D}_\mu$, and any real analytic extension $f:D\to\mathbb{R}$ of $\hat{R}_\mu$ from $\hat{D}_\mu \setminus \{0\}$ to $D$, in fact $D \subset D_\mu$, and $f=R_\mu|_D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.659954+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3fcd5d4324bb81d3925dcd402973d61c3c2f2dc619231174cb845191aec354e7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 10 of arXiv:2609.03733, ''] Suppose that $\mathscr{G}$ is a $\mathscr{NBCCS}$ of a Banach space $\mathscr{B}$ and, the function $\Psi:\mathscr{G} \rightarrow \mathscr{G}$ is a continuous  with

	\begin{equation}
