{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.454294+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_407c807167095d4a7de90ef5cdefb850c8ecfc65385a37421d458cfc3018c2b5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition HN of arXiv:2505.11721, 'Variational principles for Hausdorff and packing dimensions of fractal percolation on self'] For $N\ge 1$, writing $\Pi$ for $\Pi^{D(N)}$ and $s$ for $s(N)$, set 
\begin{align*}
H_{N,k}=\sum_{n=1}^k H({W^{(n)}})+\sum_{n=k+1}^{g_s(N)} h(\Pi_{r_n}p^{(n)})\quad (0\le k\le g_s(N)),
\end{align*}
where $r_n$ is the index $r$ such that $g_{r-1}(N)+1\le n\le g_{r}(N)$ and we recall that the entropy of a finite dimentional probability vector $q=(q_j)_{j\in\mathcal J}$ is defined as $h(q)=-\sum_{j\in\mathcal J}q_j\log(q_j)$. 

Also, set 
\begin{align}
d_N&=\frac{1}{N}\min \Big (\min_{g_1(N)\le k\le  g_s(N)-1} H_{N,k},\min_{N'\ge g_s(N)} \sum_{n=1}^{N'} H(W^{(n)})\Big )\\
 \text{and}\quad
\widetilde d_N&=\frac{1}{N}\min_{g_1(N)\le k\le  g_s(N)} H_{N,k}.
\end{align}

Note that in the deterministic case, $d_N=N^{-1} H_{N,g_1(N)}=\widetilde d_N$, and that if $H(W^{(n)})\ge 0$ for all $n\ge 1$, which is the case for non-degenerate Mandelbrot measures, then $d_N= \widetilde d_N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.606707+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_407df97f0432e8f9783bb2292feb59eb6fcad347f399f120112cbaa4f70bc1e1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2506.08775, 'Markovian multivariate Hawkes population processes: Efficient evaluation of moments'] A \textrm{Markovian multivariate Hawkes process} is a point process $\bm{N}(\cdot)$, with $\bm{N}(0)=\bm{0}$, whose components $N_i(\cdot)$ satisfy
\begin{align}

\begin{split}
    \pp(N_i(t+\Delta) - N_i(t) = 0 \, | \, \cF_t ) &= 1 - \lambda_i(t)\Delta + o(\Delta),\\
    \pp(N_i(t+\Delta) - N_i(t) = 1 \, | \, \cF_t ) &= \lambda_i(t)\Delta + o(\Delta), \\
    \pp(N_i(t+\Delta) - N_i(t) > 1 \, | \, \cF_t ) &= o(\Delta),
\end{split}
\end{align}
as $\Delta \downarrow 0$, with $\cF_t = \sigma(\bm{N}(s) \, : \, s \leqslant t)$ denoting the natural filtration generated by $\bm{N}(\cdot)$.
Here, each component $\lambda_i(t)$ of the intensity function satisfies the mean-reverting dynamics
\begin{align}

    \ddiff\lambda_i(t) = \alpha_{i}(\overline{\lambda}_i -\lambda_i(t))\,\ddiff t + \sum_{j=1}^d B_{ij}(t) \,\ddiff N_j(t),
\end{align}
where $\lambda_i(0) = \overline{\lambda}_i \geqslant 0$, $\alpha_i \geqslant 0$, and for each $i,j\in[d]$, $(B_{ij}(t))_t$ is a sequence of independent random variables, distributed as the generic non-negative random variable $B_{ij}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.697888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_407f41e76fc8951c2ae91532371c4b1dbf2aa2b754db450566b312ea48792567","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 18 of arXiv:2608.30990, 'Convergence of symmetries: nilpotency, dimension, and perfectness'] \cite{breuillard-green-tao}.     Let $\phi_i : G_i \to G$ be a sequence of good approximations. Assume $G$ is a Lie group and equip its Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ with an inner product.  Set  $B   : = \exp (B_r^{\mathfrak{g}}(0)) \subset G$ and let $B_i \subset G_i$ be the open symmetric sets given by Proposition  \ref{pro:zoom-i}. Then there is $C_0 \geq 1 $ such that if $r > 0 $ is small enough, for sufficiently large $i$ one has:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $ \Vert g_1 \cdots g_m \Vert _{B_i} \leq C_0 \sum_{j =1}^m \Vert g_j \Vert _{B_i}   $ for all $ g_1, \ldots , g_m \in G_i.$
        \item $\Vert ghg^{-1} \Vert_{B_i} \leq 1000 \, \Vert h \Vert _{B_i} $ for all $g,h \in B_i^{10}$.
