{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.798187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_40d252f38fdd4c5b96fc6a15ca6d8e2dfa6d1d57da137953a3ec184c5ec53af7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem prop: duality of arXiv:2504.04792, 'Uniqueness and Longtime Behavior of the Completely Positively Correlated Symbiotic Branchi'] Let $\mu \in \mathscr{P}(C_{\text{tem}}^+ \times C_{\text{tem}}^+)$ with compact support. Let $(U^1_t, U^2_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be a solution of the martingale problem $(MP)_U$ with initial distribution $\mu$ and sample paths in $C_{C_{\text{tem}}^+ \times C_{\text{tem}}^+}[0,\infty)$. Let $\phi \in C_{\text{rap}}^+$ and let $(V_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be the unique $C_{\text{rap}}^+$-valued solution of $((eq: dual of the paper))$ with $V_0 = \phi$. Then, for each $T > 0$, the following duality formula holds:
\begin{equation}

    \mathbb{E}\left[e^{-\langle X_T, \phi \rangle}\right] = \mathbb{E}\left[e^{-\langle X_0, V_T \rangle -\frac{1}{2}\int_0^T \langle V_r^2, \left(Y_{T-r} \right)^2 \rangle \mathrm{d}r}\right],
\end{equation}
where $X_t = U^1_t + U^2_t, Y_t = U^1_t - U^2_t = S_t(U^1_0 - U^2_0).$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

