{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.694693+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4145a3f53837b485954514858188fda9faf52920c5b69c0ae1d52f14af1e6306","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop DDS of arXiv:2605.29073, 'Volterra clocks and their pure-jump limits: \\hitting times of curved boundaries'] Let $\left( \Omega\,, \mathcal F\,, (\mathcal F_t)_{t \leq T}\,, \mathbb P\right)$ be a filtered probability space supporting two adapted processes $(X_t\,, M_t)_{t \leq T}$ such that $M$ is a continuous martingale with quadratic variation $\langle M\rangle_t =: A_t\,$. Then, there exists an extended probability space $\left(\widetilde \Omega\,, \mathcal G\,, (\mathcal G_t)_{t \geq 0}\,, \widetilde{\mathbb P}\right)$ supporting processes $\widetilde X\,$, $\widetilde M\,$, $\widetilde A\,$, and a $\left(\mathcal G_t\right)_{t \geq 0}$-Brownian motion $W\,$, such that 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\left(\widetilde X\,, \widetilde M\,, \widetilde A\right) \sim (X\,, M\,, A)\,$.
        \item For any $t \leq T\,$, $\widetilde{M}_t = W_{\widetilde{A}_t}\,$.
        \item For any $t \leq T\,$, $\widetilde{A}_t$ is a $(\mathcal G_s)_{s \geq 0}$-stopping time.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.787146+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4147160412d2fc62bea527438cdb9691caa9307b80dd2afe80d96db43f18117a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:theta_pricing_system of arXiv:2507.22577, 'A Mean-Field Theory of $\Theta$-Expectations'] Let Assumption (ass:vol_existence of the paper) hold. Let the general pricing driver be $F(t,y,z',a,\mu)$, satisfying the global well-posedness conditions of Theorem (thm:global_wellposedness of the paper). Formally, if the price of a European claim $\Phi(B_T)$ is given by $V_t = v(t,B_t)$ for a function $v \in C^{1,2}([0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^d)$ and an associated predictable volatility process $Z$ for the underlying $\Theta$-Brownian motion $B_t = \int_0^t Z_s dW_s$, then the pair $(v, Z)$ must solve the following coupled system:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
    -\partial_t v(t,x) - \frac{1}{2}\mathrm{Tr}\big(Z_t Z_t^T \nabla_x^2 v(t,x)\big) \\
    \hspace{1cm} - \sup_{a \in \mathcal{U}_{g(\mu_t[v])}} F\left(t, v(t,x), \nabla_x v(t,x)^T Z_t, a, \mu_t[v]\right) = 0, \\
    \\
    G_{BM}(t, Z_t) = 0, \\
    v(T,x) = \Phi(x).
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The first equation holds for $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times \mathbb{R}^d$. The law $\mu_t[v]$ is given by $\mu_t[v] = \Law(v(t,B_t))$, where $B_t = \int_0^t Z_s dW_s$. The function $G_{BM}$ is the simplified driver defining the canonical $\Theta$-Brownian motion.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.866288+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41484673969a773c56cb45c882e2996b2395555f10a3d187f1d8dd2f9cdfa133","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 38 of arXiv:2607.16502, 'Asymptotic Behavior of Path Functionals for Vector-Valued Gaussian Processes at High Level'] For every \(\varepsilon>0\), \(\gamma\in(0,2]\), and
  \(\bm\Lambda\in\R_{\ge0}^k\), there exist constants
  \(\delta,c,C>0\) such that the following holds.  Let
  \[
    \left\{
      (\bm\chi_{\bm x}(\bm t))_{\bm t\in[\bm0,\bm\Lambda]}:
      \bm x\in\R^d
    \right\}
  \]
  be a family of continuous \(\R^d\)-valued Gaussian random fields for which
  the map
  \[
    \bm x\longmapsto
    \pk{\exists\,\bm t\in[\bm0,\bm\Lambda]:
      \bm\chi_{\bm x}(\bm t)>\bm x}
  \]
  is measurable.  Suppose that there exist \(G,\sigma\ge0\) and
  \(\bm w=(w_1,\ldots,w_d)^\top\), with
  \(\varepsilon\le w_i\le\varepsilon^{-1}\) for every \(i\), such that for
  every \(\bm x\in\R^d\) and every
  \(\bm t,\bm s\in[\bm0,\bm\Lambda]\),
  \begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0.3cm, topsep=0.3cm, label={\alph*)}]
    \item
    \[
      \sup_{F\subset\{1,\ldots,d\}}
      \sup_{\bm t\in[\bm0,\bm\Lambda]}
      \bm w_F^\top\E{\bm\chi_{\bm x,F}(\bm t)}
      \le G+\delta\sum_{j=1}^d|x_j|;
    \]
    \item
    \[
      \sup_{F\subset\{1,\ldots,d\}}
      \sup_{\bm t\in[\bm0,\bm\Lambda]}
      \Var{\bm w_F^\top\bm\chi_{\bm x,F}(\bm t)}
      \le\sigma^2;
    \]
    \item
    \[
      \sup_{F\subset\{1,\ldots,d\}}
      \Var{
        \bm w_F^\top\bm\chi_{\bm x,F}(\bm t)
        -\bm w_F^\top\bm\chi_{\bm x,F}(\bm s)
      }
      \le\varepsilon^{-1}|\bm t-\bm s|^\gamma.
    \]
  \end{enumerate}
  Then
  \[
    \int_{\R^d}e^{\bm w^\top\bm x}
    \pk{\exists\,\bm t\in[\bm0,\bm\Lambda]:
      \bm\chi_{\bm x}(\bm t)>\bm x}
    \,d\bm x
    \le C e^{c(G+\sigma^2)}.
  \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.816292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_414906e63f493451bd2f90641190f7f6b584d4ddb9b2bd138104ccfed9fce3f9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:three-point-suspension-skeleton of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For every $h\geq2$,
