Assume both kernels satisfy the Decreusefond--\"Ust\"unel conditions with respect to $\mu$. Then
\begin{equation}
\langle X\rangle^{(D^{K_1},\Pi^{K_1})}_t = \langle X\rangle^{(D^{K_2},\Pi^{K_2})}_t \quad \text{for all } t\in[0,T].
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.648315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_415604a236566fc3ee86dadbf9920caabdd2d38244d13a69ee2a0550582e5b03","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition uniformly-Skorohod of arXiv:2603.01823, 'on a fractional stochastic heat equation arising from the disordered pinning model'] Let $U=(u_1, u_2,\dots)$ be an $\mathbb R^{\mathbb N}$-valued random vector such that $u_k\in L^1$ for all $k\in\mathbb N$. Denote $H_n:=\mathbb E[\mathcal{P}_n U|\mathcal{F}_n]$. Then the following two conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For every $n$, $S_n:=\int{\raisebox{-.33em}{\hspace{-.87em}\scriptsize \bf o}}\hspace{.5em} H_n\xi$ exists and  $\{S_n\}_{n\in\mathbb N}$ is  uniformly integrable.

\item $U$ is $L^1$-Skorohod integrable.

\end{enumerate}
If any of these conditions holds, then 
$S_n$ is a martingale that converges almost surely and in $L^1$ to $\int{\raisebox{-.33em}{\hspace{-.87em}\scriptsize \bf o}}\hspace{.5em} U{\mathrm d} \xi$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.735411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41564fa68d338e22531e2a675f6ea6932380eeb8eeabb5c004c7aef271b33e13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm 12 of arXiv:2506.10213, 'Coupling of forward-backward stochastic differential equations on the Wiener space, and ap'] Suppose that $\mu$ in \eqref{fbsde:equ:general}
    satisfies Assumption \ref{coefficient:assume:general}, Assumption \ref{fbsde:basic:assume:p:general},
    and is decomposed as $\mu = \sz + A$ according to Assumption \ref{ass:linear:diffusion}. 
    For all $\az\in [0,1]$ and 
    $$(s,\oz,x,y,(z_1,z_2))\in[0,T]\times \Oz\times \R^n\times\R^m \times(\R^{m\times d})^2,$$
    set
    \begin{equation}
        \Sigma(s,\az,\oz,x,y,(z_1,z_2)) 
        := \left(\sz_{\sqrt{1 - \az^2}}(s,\oz,x,y,z_1), \sz_{\az}(s,\oz,x,y,z_2)\right),
    \end{equation}
    where
    $\sz_{r}(s,\oz,x,y,z) := r\,\sz(s,\oz,x,y) + A(s,\oz,z)$ for all $r\in [0,1]$.
    Then the following properties hold:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(1)] Lipschitz Continuity:
        For all $\az\in [0,1]$, $s\in [0,T]$, $\oz\in\Oz$ and 
        $$\tz = (x,y,(z_1,z_2)),\quad \tz' = (x',y',(z'_1,z'_2)),$$
        we have
        \begin{align*}
            |\Sigma(s,\az,\tz) - \Sigma(s,\az,\tz')| 
            &\leq L_{\mu,1} |x - x'| + L_{\mu,2}|y - y'|\\
            &\quad\quad + L_{\mu,3} |(z_1,z_2) - (z'_1,z'_2)|,
        \end{align*}
        where $\{L_{\mu,i}\}^3_{i = 1}$ are the Lipschitz constants for $\mu$ (Assumption \ref{coefficient:assume:general});
        \item[(2)] Integrability Condition:
        The function $\Sigma(\cdot,0,0,(0,0))$ belongs to the space $\cL^{2,p}([t,T]\times\Oz,\F,\P;\R^{n\times 2d})$;
        \item[(3)] Representation Property:
        For all $z\in \R^{m\times d}$,
        \begin{align*}
            &\Sigma(s,\az,x,y,(\sqrt{1 - \az^2}z,\az z))\\
            &\quad = \left(\sqrt{1 - \az^2} \mu(s,x,y,z), \az\mu(s,x,y,z)\right).
        \end{align*}
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.308774+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4157a825a412d01c7f71eaa84a8f29817a4ef9ebd0f8827043086b9fcf96930d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 5 (Matrix Freedman) of arXiv:2606.24323, 'Conditioning of incoherent sub-dictionaries sampled from a coherent dictionary'] Let $(Y_\ell)_{\ell}$ with $Y_0=0$ be a matrix martingale adapted to the filtration $(\mathcal F_\ell)_\ell$, whose values are symmetric (self-adjoint) $d\times d$ matrices.
Assume that the difference sequence $X_\ell = Y_\ell-Y_{\ell-1}$ is almost surely bounded by $R$ and that the quadratic variation process is almost surely bounded by $\sigma^2$, meaning 
\begin{align*}
    \|X_\ell\| \leq R \quad  \mbox{and} \quad & \| \sum_{\ell=1}^L \E[X_\ell^2\mid \mathcal F_{\ell-1}]\| \leq \sigma^2 \quad \mbox{(a.s.), then}\\
\P(\| Y_L \|\geq t) = \P\Big(\| \sum_{\ell=1}^L X_\ell \| &\geq t \Big)\leq d \cdot \exp\left(\frac{-t^2/2}{\sigma^2+ Rt/3}\right)\leq d \cdot \exp\left(\frac{-t^2}{2\sigma^2+ Rt}\right).
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.069104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_415899b9ec45199e91763e3dcabfca3c0bbf053c5541e83abb24709e4207bd01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 (Mild solution) of arXiv:2608.19753, 'Uniform large deviation principles for the stochastic heat equation over unbounded sets of'] Let $\eps,N\in[0,\infty)$, $u\in \A_N$, $x\in L^1(D)$ and let $X_x^{\eps,u}\col \Om\times [0,T]\times D\to \R$ be a progressively measurable  process. 
If a.s., \eqref{eq:split X} and \eqref{eq:split Y Z} are  satisfied for Lebesgue-a.e.\ $t\in[0,T]$ and $\xi\in D$ (in particular, the integrals appearing therein are well-defined), then we call $X_x^{\eps,u}$ a \emph{mild solution to \eqref{eq:split X}}. 

If $u=0$, and $X_x^{\eps,0}$ is a mild solution to \eqref{eq:split X} in the sense above, then we call $X_x^\eps\ceqq X_x^{\eps,0}$ a mild solution to \eqref{eq:SHE}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.084681+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4159e8664243c45b847d21d1f5f01d942f88b7b9f26e688802ce4a1884c7166d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defn:strong of arXiv:2510.12520, '\Large Strong Convergence: A Short Survey\thanks{Contribution to the Proceedings of the In'] Let $\boldsymbol{X}^N=(X^N_1,\ldots,X^N_r)$ be a family of random matrices
for every $N\ge 1$, and let $\boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_r)$ be a family 
of bounded operators on a Hilbert space. If
$$
	\lim_{N\to\infty}
