{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.833068+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4168c6722e1ed350b9225e6790b988a81494b653591b22945239f8b081179f7f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop eq:ST0_OP of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] For ${\gamma} > - d_1$, let ${\alpha} = {\gamma} + d_1 -1$. Define 

\begin{equation} 
 {\mathsf Q}_{\bm j, \ell, m}^n(\bm x,\bm y) = \bm T_{\bm j, n-m}^{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}}, \alpha+2m)}(\bm y) (1-|\bm y|)^m 
      {\mathsf Y}_{\ell}^m \left(\frac{\bm x}{1-|\bm y|}\right). 
\end{equation}
Then $\{{\mathsf Q}_{\bm j, \ell, m}^n: |\bm j| = n-m, \, 1 \le \ell \le \dim {\mathcal H}_m^{d_1}, \, 0 \le m  \le n, \bm j \in {\mathbb N}_0^{d_2}\}$ 
is an orthogonal basis of ${\mathcal V}_n({\mathbb S}^{d_1-1}\!\! \rtimes \triangle^{d_2}, {\mathsf W}_{{\gamma}, {\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^\triangle)$. Moreover, 
the norm square of ${\mathsf Q}_{\bm j, \ell, m}^n$ 
is given by 
\begin{equation} 
   {\mathsf h}_{m,n}^{{\gamma},{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}  
       =   \frac{\bm b^\triangle_{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}},\alpha)}}{\bm b^\triangle_{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}}, \alpha + 2m)}} \bm h_{\bm j,m}^{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}}, {\alpha}+ 2m), \triangle}
       = \frac{({\gamma} +d_1)_{2m}} {(|{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}|+ {\gamma} + d_1 + d_2)_{2m}} \bm h_{\bm j,m}^{({\boldsymbol{\kappa}}, {\alpha}+ 2m), \triangle}.  
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.500796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4169d6fc30710ac2676e4e36a4939061e4a43011bfcb0080c7e75e2c8acfd3ea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (Mixing property) of arXiv:2605.30030, 'Critical exponents for planar random-cluster model with cluster-weight~$q=4$'] There exist~$c_{\rm mix},C_{\rm mix}\in(0,\infty)$ such that for every~$r\le R/2$ and every two events~$A$ and~$B$ depending on edges in~$\Lambda_r$ and outside~$\Lambda_R$, respectively, 
