{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.590311+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_417ad0bce20447e5d365d703b4fed82ca9c2e61b5e6be76ce641f8fe7926d536","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem CTSE-O-C-1B of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] Let ${u }\left( t ,x \right)$ be the H\"{o}lder-continuous martingale solution to Eq.(equation 2DEEUGE1 of the paper) obtained from Theorem (CTSE-O-C of the paper). Assume that the exponents $p_1$ and $p_2 $ satisfy $\frac{1}{{{p_2}}} - \frac{1}{{{p_1}}} > \frac{3}{{16}}$. Then, there exist two increasing functions ${\mathfrak{\tilde h}_1}\left( {r} \right) $ and ${\mathfrak{\tilde h}_2}\left( {r} \right) $ such that ${u }\left( t ,x \right)$ exhibits intermittency
\begin{equation}
\frac{{{{\left\| u \right\|}_{{L^{{p_1}}}}}}}{{{{\left\| u \right\|}_{{L^{p_2}}}}}} \sim {\mathfrak{\tilde h}_1}\left( {r} \right)  \to  \infty , \ \ \mbox{as}\ r \to  \infty , \ \forall \, t \in {\mathrm{supp}_\mathrm{t}} \ u , \  \mathbb{P}\mbox{-a.s.} ,
\end{equation}
and inertial-range intermittency
\begin{equation}
\frac{{{{\left\| {u\left( {x + l\mathbf{n}} \right) - u\left( x \right)} \right\|}_{{L^{{p_1}}}}}}}{{{{\left\| {u\left( {x + l\mathbf{n}} \right) - u\left( x \right)} \right\|}_{{L^{{p_2}}}}}}} \sim {\mathfrak{\tilde h}_2}\left( {r} \right) \to  \infty , \ \ \mbox{as}\ r \to  \infty , \ \forall \, l \in \left[ {{l_D},{l_I}} \right] , \  \forall \, t \in {\mathrm{supp}_\mathrm{t}} \ u , \  \mathbb{P}\mbox{-a.s.}
\end{equation}
Here $r$ is the cutoff wavenumber associated with the Dirichlet kernel (see (equation XINHUI-SJI-A of the paper) in Appendix D), and $\mathbf{n}$ is a unit spatial direction vector on the two-dimensional torus $\mathbb{T}^2$. Moreover, the interval $\left[ {{l_D},{l_I}} \right]  \subset \mathbb{R}^+$ denotes the inertial range with dissipation scale $l_D > 0$ and integral scale $l_I > l_D$, and the notation ``$a \sim b$" means ${C_1}b \leqslant a \leqslant {C_2}b$ for some positive constants ${C_1} \leqslant {C_2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

