{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.849586+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41b819292fb192081eb54f4b99b831c58152558ddea8b67bf20acce6244bc4c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem recur:upperBoundVector of arXiv:2607.09841, 'Mixing and cutoff for the systematic scan dynamics of the mean-field ferromagnetic Potts m'] Let $\{x_t = (x_t^{(1)},\dots,x_t^{(q)})\}_{t \geq 0}$ be a sequence of vectors in $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^q$ such that for all $j \in Q$ and $t \geq n$, we have the recursion
\begin{align*}
x_t^{(j)} &=  \frac{\beta}{nq}\sum_{i = t-n}^{t-1} x_i^{(j)} +\frac{C}{n} \sum_{i = t-n}^{t-1}  \lVert x_i \rVert_2^2 + \frac{C}{n},
\end{align*}
where $C$ is a positive constant. Then, 
if for all $i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and all $j \in Q$, we have
$\lvert x_i^{(j)}\rvert \leq \rho$ for a small enough constant $\rho > 0$, then there exists constant $\hat C > 0$ such that
\begin{align*}
     \lVert x_t\rVert_2^2  \leq \hat C\rho^2 \gamma_n^{2t} + \frac{\hat C}{n},
\end{align*}
where $\gamma_n$ is the unique positive real root of the equation $ \sum_{i= 1}^n \gamma_n^{-i} = nq/\beta;$ 
see Section~(sec:recur of the paper) for additional details. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.069513+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41b83b94669fac3f5d78ab07d1b4ef7af41b4d5bf9fceb146101b1a33376f6f9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 (Operator-norm bound via Cauchy--Schwarz) of arXiv:2606.27774, 'Variational Formulas for the Spectrum of\\ Block Wishart Matrices'] Fix $\bZ\in\bbH_+^k$ and $\bS,\tilde\bS\in\bbH_+^k$ such that $\bF_\bZ(\bS),\bF_\bZ(\tilde\bS)$ 
are well-defined, and let $\bB,\tilde\bB\succ\bzero$.
For all integers $q\ge 1$,
\begin{equation}

\|\cuL_{\bS,\tilde\bS}^q\|_{\op\to\op}^2 
\le \|\bB\|_\op\|\bB^{-1}\|_\op\|\tilde\bB\|_\op\|\tilde\bB^{-1}\|_\op\cdot \Gamma_\bS(\bB)^q\,\Gamma_{\tilde\bS}(\tilde\bB)^q, 
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}

\Gamma_\bS(\bB) := \Big\|\bB^{-1/2}\bF_\bZ(\bS)\int \bfeta(\bS,\bT)\,\bB\,\bfeta(\bS,\bT)^*\nu(\de\bT)\, \bF_\bZ(\bS)^* \bB^{-1/2}\Big\|_\op.
\end{equation}
Here $\|\cdot\|_{\op\to\op}$ is the operator norm on linear maps 
$(\C^{k\times k},\|\cdot\|_\op) \to (\C^{k\times k},\|\cdot\|_\op)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.299105+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41b8c765f5d9adc42feb993e531d984a6fd8031093c50698e235bb675f84486b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Th:Gallo of arXiv:2605.24671, 'Immigration Processes with Binomial Catastrophes and Random Survival Parameters'] Consider the Firework process on $\mathbb{N}$ as defined above, with $\alpha_k:=\mathbb P(R\le k),k\ge0$ being the radius distribution. Then
