{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.733027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41d65f75e9a162469cbbc7f990452ef64f38edccfb501bdd6c18a08c5c994396","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Given a rooted tree $\tau$, we say that $C \subset E_+(\tau)$ is a cut of $\tau$ if any path in $E(\tau)$ with the root of $\tau$ as an endpoint contains at most one edge in $C$. We denote by $\mathbb{C}[\tau]$ the set of all cuts of $\tau$.
	We recall that for a rooted tree $\tau$, there is a natural partial ordering on $E(\tau)$ in which $e \ge e^\prime$ iff $e^\prime$ lies on the unique path from $e$ to the root.

	 We write $\tau_{\ng C}$ for the subgraph consisting of those edges $e \in E(\tau)$ such that there does not exist $\tilde{e} \in C$ with $e \ge \tilde{e}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:11:35.820974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41dc021761a4b8fc8b63b373b230c7fb780005278f2dbbcecc485939411c0624","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Slot configuration of arXiv:2607.22908, 'Epidemic Phase Transitions in the Zero-Range Process'] A \emph{slot} is a pair of coordinates $(x,i) \in \mathbb{Z}^d \times \mathbb{N}$. The sequence of slots of the form $\{(x,i)\}_{i \geq 1}$ represents a stack of positions built over site $x$.
  A \emph{slot configuration} is an element
  \begin{equation}
    \sigma = (\sigma(x, i))_{x \in \mathbb{Z}^d, i \in \mathbb{N}} \in \{0, 1\}^{\mathbb{Z}^d \times \mathbb{N}},
  \end{equation}
  assigning to each slot either $0$ (vacant) or $1$ (occupied).
  Denote by $n(\sigma, x) = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \sigma(x, i)$ the number of particles placed in slots over $x$.

  A configuration $\sigma$ is called \emph{packed} if, for every $x \in \mathbb{Z}^d$, the occupied slots are exactly those at the bottom of the stack, that is,
  \begin{equation}
    \sigma(x, i) = \textbf{1}_{ \{ i \le n(\sigma, x) \} } = 
    \begin{cases}
      1, & i \le n(\sigma, x),\\
      0, & i > n(\sigma, x).
    \end{cases}
  \end{equation}
  Therefore, when $\sigma$ is packed, $n(\sigma, x)$ represents the \emph{height} of the stack of particles at $x$.
  Denote by $\Sigma_{\text{packed}} \subset \{0, 1\}^{\mathbb{Z}^d \times \mathbb{N}}$ the state space of all
  packed slot configurations. Given a configuration $\eta \in \mathbb{N}_0^{\mathbb{Z}^d}$, its \emph{canonical slot embedding} is the unique packed slot configuration $\sigma$ with $n(\sigma, x)=\eta(x)$, for every $x\in \mathbb{Z}^d$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.347806+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41dc99283caab4f3616ee14f66f7b0f33479365b1e0d90142e8adb9b62ede07d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:gap-asymptotics1 of arXiv:2412.01489, 'One- and two-particle spectral gap identities for the symmetric inclusion process and rela'] For all graphs $G=(V,(c_{xy})_{x,y\in V})$ and   site weights $\hat\alpha = (\hat\alpha_x)_{x\in V}$,  we have
