\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.504163+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41dda3e47c3c57d30cee01fa313e3d4e9807d1d01fd17a1b7872ff31424adb82","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 75 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\xi \in \bbr^m$ be arbitrary. An $\bbr^m$-valued continuous, adapted process $Y = (Y_t)_{t \in [0,T_0]}$ for some $T_0 \in (0,T]$ is called a \emph{local solution} to the Stratonovich SDE \eqref{SDE-Strat} with $Y_0 = \xi$ if
\begin{align*}
Y_t = \xi + \int_0^t f_0(s,Y_s) ds + \int_0^t f(s,Y_s) \circ dB_s, \quad t \in [0,T_0].
\end{align*}
If we can choose $T_0 = T$, then we also call $Y$ a \emph{(global) solution} to the Stratonovich SDE \eqref{SDE-Strat} with $Y_0 = \xi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:32:58.330550+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41dde67afefdd0eb10d7374349cd22b4a01036129b012b6395391eea8e1af8e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop of arXiv:2505.12127, 'Generalised principal eigenvalues and global survival of branching Markov processes'] For any $\sigma>0$ we have
