{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.635435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_422c7a6473209a550a33e68e942c276206fef72089991e11b9c1744ce655fda4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition deg_7_expansion of arXiv:2411.13707, 'High-degree cubature on Wiener space \\ through unshuffle expansions'] Let $B$ be a Brownian motion in $\mathbb{R}^d$. Then it has expected signature given by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbb{E}[S^{(7)}_{0,1}(\circ B)] \\
={}&1 + \sum_{1\leq i\leq d}\frac{1}{2}(\xi_i, \xi_i ) +\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big [\frac{1}{2}(\xi_i, \xi_{ijj} ) +\frac{1}{4}(\xi_{ij}, \xi_{ij} ) + \frac{1}{8}(\xi_{i},\xi_{i},\xi_{j},\xi_{j})\Big ]\\
& + \sum_{1\leq i,j,k\leq d}\Big [\frac{1}{12}(\xi_{i},\xi_{ijjkk}) + \frac{1}{24}(\xi_{j},\xi_{iijkk}) + \frac{1}{6}(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ijkk}) + \frac{1}{12}(\xi_{ik},\xi_{ijjk})\\
& + \frac{1}{8}(\xi_{ijj},\xi_{ikk}) + \frac{1}{12}(\xi_{ijk},\xi_{ijk}) +\frac{1}{4}(\xi_{i},\xi_{i},\xi_{j},\xi_{jkk}) + \frac{1}{8}(\xi_{i},\xi_{i},\xi_{jk},\xi_{jk})\\
&+ \frac{1}{6}(\xi_{i},\xi_{j},\xi_{ik},\xi_{jk})+ \frac{1}{48}(\xi_{i},\xi_{i},\xi_{j},\xi_{j},\xi_{k},\xi_{k})\Big ].
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Moreover, the time-augmented case can be reduced to the above by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}[S_{0,1}^{(7)}&(\circ\widehat{B})] = \mathbb{E}[S_{0,1}^{(7)}(\circ B)] + \xi_0 + \frac 12 (\xi_{0},\xi_0) + \frac 16(\xi_{0},\xi_0, \xi_0) + \sum_{1\leq i\leq d}\Big (\frac 12 \xi_{0ii} + \frac 12 (\xi_{0},\xi_{i}, \xi_{i})\\
& +\frac 12 (\xi_0, \xi_{0ii}) + \frac 16(\xi_{0i}, \xi_{0i}) + \frac 14 (\xi_0,\xi_0,\xi_i,\xi_i)\Big ) + \sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d} \Big (\frac{1}{12}\xi_{0iijj} + \frac{1}{24}\xi_{ii0jj}\\
&+ \frac 12(\xi_0,\xi_i,\xi_{ijj}) + \frac 14(\xi_j,\xi_j,\xi_{0ii}) + \frac 14(\xi_0,\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij}) + \frac 13 (\xi_i,\xi_{0j},\xi_{ij}) + \frac 18(\xi_0,\xi_i,\xi_i,\xi_j,\xi_j)\Big ).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{proof}
The expected signature truncated at degree 7 is,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}[S_{[0,1]}^{(7)}(\circ \widehat{B})]=&\,\,\epsilon_0 + \frac{1}{2}\epsilon_0^2+\frac{1}{6}\epsilon_0^3+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}\epsilon_i^2+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2)+\frac{1}{4}\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2)\\
&+\frac{1}{8}\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)+\frac{1}{8}\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)+\frac{1}{48}\sum_{1\leq i,j,k\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2,\epsilon_k^2) .
\end{aligned}
\]
By recalling (not:xi of the paper) and the fact that for each \(i\), $\xi_i=\mathrm{e}(\epsilon_i)=\epsilon_i$,
we can obtain the following fairly trivial expansions.
\[
\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}\epsilon_i^2=\frac 12\sum_{1\leq i\leq d} (\xi_i,\xi_i ),\qquad \epsilon_0^2=(\xi_0,\xi_0),\qquad \text{and},\quad\epsilon_0^3=(\xi_0,\xi_0,\xi_0).
\]
(lemma:terms_in_signature_first of the paper) - (lemma:terms_in_signature_last of the paper) will subsequently expand each remaining term in the symmetrised Eulerian representation (equation eq:expansion of the paper), which will conclude the proof.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.224260+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_422c8efb74c443d72373652861a38c70043de8f2a0185cc55c1155b7b1abd5eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm conv-DL-zero of arXiv:2411.16376, 'Higher order approximations in arcsine laws for subordinators'] Under the assumption of Theorem $(Thm:potential-zero of the paper)$ with $\beta_0=\beta\in(0,1]$,  let us define a function $\varepsilon_2:(0,+\infty)\to\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ by 
\begin{align}
    \Pi((x,+\infty))=
    \frac{c_0 x^{-\beta}}{\Gamma(1-\beta)}+\varepsilon_2(x),
    \quad
   0<x<+\infty.	
\end{align}
Set 
\begin{align}
 \varepsilon_3(s,x)
   =
   s\Pi((s-sx,+\infty))\varepsilon_1(sx),
   \quad 0<s, x<+\infty.	
\end{align}
Then, for $s\in(0,+\infty)$, the finite measure 
\begin{align}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}\bigg(\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}\in dx,\; 0<\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}<1\bigg),
\quad 0<x<1,
\end{align}
 admits a density expressed in the form
\begin{align}
   s\Pi((s-sx,&+\infty))u(sx)
   \\
   =\,&
   \frac{\sin(\pi\beta)}{\pi}x^{-1+\beta}(1-x)^{-\beta}
   +
   \sum_{k=1}^n \frac{c_0 s^{\beta_k-\beta}}{c_k\Gamma(\beta_k)\Gamma(1-\beta)}
   x^{-1+\beta_k}(1-x)^{-\beta}
   \\
   &+
   \sum_{k=0}^n \frac{s^{\beta_k}}{c_k\Gamma(\beta_k)}
   x^{-1+\beta_k}\varepsilon_2(s-sx)
   +
   O(\varepsilon_3(s,x)),
   \quad
   \text{as $s\to0+$,}	
\end{align}
uniformly in $x\in I$ for any closed interval $I\subset (0,1)$. 
Therefore, for any closed interval $I\subset (0,1)$,
\begin{align}
	&\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}\bigg(\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}\in I\bigg)
	\\
	=\,&
	 \frac{\sin(\pi\beta)}{\pi}
	 \int_I x^{-1+\beta}(1-x)^{-\beta}\,dx
	 +
	 \sum_{k=1}^n \frac{c_0 s^{\beta_k-\beta}}{c_k\Gamma(\beta_k)\Gamma(1-\beta)}
   \int_I x^{-1+\beta_k}(1-x)^{-\beta}\,dx 
   \\	
   &+
   \sum_{k=0}^n \frac{s^{\beta_k}}{c_k\Gamma(\beta_k)}
   \int_I
   x^{-1+\beta_k}\varepsilon_2(s-sx)\,dx
   + 
   O\bigg(\sup_{x\in I}\varepsilon_3(s,x)\bigg),
   \quad
   \text{as $s\to0+$}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.652250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_422e73af89d8983e3ca07f43f68a09ac9d1d2bf5ab97ebfcde5251b01921d333","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] The resolvent set $\rho(L)$ of an operator $L$ is the set of all complex numbers $\lambda$ such that the operator $\lambda I - L$ has a bounded inverse, and the domain of this inverse is dense in $\mathcal{B}$. This inverse will be denoted by $R(\lambda, L)$. The complement of $\rho(L)$ in the complex plane is called the spectrum of $L$ and is denoted by $\sigma(L)$. The spectrum of an operator may consist of three different types of points:
			\begin{enumerate}
				\item \textbf{Residual Spectrum} $\sigma_r(L)$: These are the values $\lambda \in \sigma(L)$ for which the resolvent operator $R(\lambda, L)$ exists, but its domain $\mathcal{D}(R(\lambda, L))$ is not dense in $\mathcal{B}$.

				\item \textbf{Continuous Spectrum} $\sigma_c(L)$: These are the values $\lambda \in \sigma(L)$ for which $\lambda I - L$ has an unbounded inverse with a dense domain.

				\item \textbf{Point Spectrum} $\sigma_p(L)$: These are the values $\lambda \in \sigma(L)$ for which $\lambda I - L$ does not have an inverse.
			\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.319268+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_422fa1fa7f7b490f7b97a7449577b038a52815516ec713cb8522af70d5bc0cb9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm unstable-equiv-main of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The global $\infty$-category $\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}$ is equivariantly presentable, and the unique equivariantly cocontinuous functor $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}\to\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}$ preserving the terminal object is an equivalence.
    \begin{proof}
        The topological functor $\ensuremath{c}$ induces a global functor $\smash{{N_\textup{top}}(\textbf{\textup{Top}}^\flat)}\to\smash{{N_\textup{top}}(\cat{$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}^\flat)}$, which by Proposition~(prop:triv-ff of the paper) descends to a fully faithful and levelwise left adjoint functor $\ensuremath{c}\colon\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}\to\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{gl}$. To complete the proof, it will now suffice to show that the essential image of the composite
        \[
            \Psi\colon\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}\xrightarrow{\;\ensuremath{c}\;}\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{gl}\xrightarrow[\smash{\raise3.5pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}]{\;\,\Phi\,\;}\ul\Spc_{\textup{Glo}}
        \]
        agrees with $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}\subset\ul\Spc_{\textup{Glo}}$, where $\Phi$ denotes the unique equivalence.

        To see that $\Psi$ factors through $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}$, it suffices by Elmendorf's Theorem to show that the left adjoint $\Psi_G\colon{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_G\to\ul\Spc_{\textup{Glo}}(G)$ maps $G/H$ into $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}}(G)$ for any compact Lie group $G$ and any closed subgroup $H\subset G$. To this end, let us write $i\colon H\hookrightarrow G$ for the inclusion, so that $\ensuremath{c}(G/H)\simeq\textbf{\textup{L}}i_!\ensuremath{c}(1_H)$ by Proposition~(prop:quotient-we-global of the paper) and hence $\Psi_G(G/H)=\Phi_G\textbf{\textup{L}}i_!\ensuremath{c}(1_H)=i_!\Phi_H\ensuremath{c}(1)=i_!1\in\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}(G)$.

        For the converse, note that $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}}(\mathbb B{G})\simeq\PSh({\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}_{/\mathbb B{G}})$ is generated under ordinary colimits by the objects $(i\colon H\hookrightarrow G)=i_!1_H$ for closed subgroups $H\subset G$, so that also $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}(\mathbb B{G})$ is generated by the objects $i_!1_H$. We have just seen that $\Psi_G(G/H)\simeq i_!1_H$; thus, the essential image of the fully faithful left adjoint $\Psi_G$ contains a set of generators of $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}(\mathbb B{G})$, so it already has to be all of $\ul\Spc_{{\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}\triangleright\textup{Glo}}(\mathbb B{G})$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:28:33.706186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4237854d10efa58c327b44cf3b5fc02728540fb609d36565e0bf52bf1f66f3f4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lA10 of arXiv:2504.07255, 'A Tail-Respecting Splitting Numerical Scheme\\ for L\'evy-Driven SDEs With Superlinear Dri'] We have
