{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.748331+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42399c44d8e802a91b40c6344b875f25ba514f90527b9434e08f637c08399479","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 12 of arXiv:2511.20322, 'Dissertation'] Suppose we are given the following.
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $\al, \be \in (0,1]$ with $\al + \be > 1$,
		\item $X : [0,\infty) \to \R^m \in \CHoelLoc\al$,
		\item $\si : [0,\infty) \to \R^{d\times m} \in \CHoelLoc\be$,
		\item $A : [0,\infty) \to \R^{d\times d}\in \LpLoc 1 \cap L^\infty$,
		\item $b : [0,\infty) \to \R^d \in \LpLoc 1$.
	\end{itemize}
Let $\ph$ be the unique solution to the linear matrix integral equation
\begin{equation}

	\ph_t = 1_{d\times d} + \int_0^t A_s \ph_s\,ds.
\end{equation}
	Then the unique solution $Y : [0,\infty) \to \R^d$ to the Young differential equation
\begin{equation}

	dY_t = A_t Y_t + b_t\,dt + \si_t\,dX_t, Y_0 \in \R^d.
\end{equation}
is given by
\[Y_t = \ph_t\left(Y_0 + \int_0^t \ph_s^{-1} b_s\,ds + \int_0^t \ph_s^{-1} \si_s\,dX_s\right),\quad t\geq 0.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.310363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_423dcc24549d4b130128321e3f191f643a7a62d07f66e901be39768d50cd987c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:second-moment of arXiv:2605.30707, 'Sharp behavior of the free energy for the two-dimensional directed polymer model'] We have the following expansion of the correlation length \(1/\mathtt{F}_2(\beta)\) in terms of the parameter \(\alpha(\beta) := \pi \frac{1+\sigma^2(\beta)}{\sigma^2(\beta)}\):
       \begin{equation*}

              {\mathtt{F}_2(\beta)} = {16}\, \mathrm{e}^{-\alpha(\beta)} \Big(1 - 4 \alpha(\beta) \mathrm{e}^{-\alpha(\beta)} + O\big( \alpha(\beta)^2 \mathrm{e}^{-2\alpha(\beta)} \big) \Big)^{-1} \qquad \text{ as } \beta \downarrow 0\,.
       \end{equation*}
       As a consequence, we have \(\mathtt{F}_2(\beta)  \sim 16\, \mathrm{e}^{-\pi}\, \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{\pi}{\sigma^{2}(\beta)}}\) as \(\beta \downarrow 0\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.352518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42420af346898d716f434cfebfbeaa34eea009188495547a768bd0fd213b2a87","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi global_markov_transform of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] [Global Markov transform]
		Let $E$ be a Polish space and $P$ a probability measure on $E^\mathbb{R}.$ \begin{enumerate}
			\item We say that $P$ admits a weak global Markov transform if there exists a Markov measure $P'$ on $E^\mathbb{R}$ and $(R_n)_{n \geq 1} \in \mathcal{A}$ such that $ P_{[R_n]} \xrightarrow[n \to + \infty]{\textnormal{f.d.}} P'.$ We say that $P'$ is a weak global Markov transform of $P.$
			\item We say that $P$ admits a strong global Markov transform if there exists a Markov measure $P'$ on $E^\mathbb{R}$ such that for every  $(R_n)_{n \geq 1} \in \mathcal{A} $, we have $P_{[R_n]} \xrightarrow[n \to + \infty]{\textnormal{f.d.}} P'.$ We say that $P'$ is the strong global Markov transform of $P.$
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.891501+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42438379fe26d59e7011e594f2c6068608a9a866f373794c5e33c76a5a769f13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma thm9.7 of arXiv:2411.11393, '\fontsize{16}{19} \selectfont On differentiability of reward functionals corresponding to '] Let $Y=(Y_t)_{t\in [0,\infty)}$ be a standard process with continuous paths and values in an interval $J\subset \mathbb{R}$. Let $A$ be the weak infinitesimal operator of $Y$ and $\psi:J \to [0,\infty)$ a bounded measurable function with the property $\mathbb{E}_{\mathpalette\mathpalette\mathpalette\bigcdot@{.6}@{.6}@{.6}}[\psi(Y_t)]\to \psi$ weakly for $t\searrow 0$. We set $\varphi:=(\varphi_t)_{t\in[0,\infty)}$,
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi_t:= \int_0^t \psi(Y_s)ds, \quad t\ge0.
    \end{align*}
    Let $\tilde A$ denote the weak infinitesimal operator of the transition function $\tilde \mathbb{P}$ given by
    \begin{align*}
        \tilde \mathbb{P}(t,x,\Gamma) := \mathbb{E}_x[\mathds{ 1 }_{{\Gamma}}(Y_t) e^{-\varphi_t}], \quad t\ge 0, x\in J, \Gamma \in \mathcal{B}(J).
    \end{align*} Now 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{D}_{\tilde A}\cap \ccc^0(J)\cap \B(J) = \mathcal{D}_{A}\cap \ccc^0(J) \cap \B(J)
    \end{align*} and 
    \begin{align*}
        \tilde A f = A f - \psi f
    \end{align*} for all $f\in  \mathcal{D}_{\tilde A}\cap \ccc^0(J)\cap \B(J)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.481643+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_424537929370f6fc2a431d44c1a7ad4b3519b4ea5a6b886c5680952791c5c6b0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 48 of arXiv:2604.04267, 'On Sharpest Tail Bounds for Functions \\ of Tail Bounded Random Variables'] Let $(f_1^-,f_1^+) \in \mathcal{B}_2$ and $c_1 \in (0,1)$. Define\footnote{$h_k$ is at definition~\ref{hkdefinition}.}
\begin{align*} 
H^1 &:= \big\{ t \in [0,\infty)\  \big| \ \forall t' \in [0,t), h_1(t') < h_1(t)\big \} \\ 
    & \ \ \cup \big\{ t \in (-\infty,0] \ \big| \ \forall t' \in (t,0], h_1(t')<h_1(t) \big\} \\
G^1 &:= \overline{H^1} \cup \{0\}.
\end{align*}
Write $\widetilde{X}_1^+(s) = \sup {f_1^+}^{-1}[1-s,1], \ \forall s \in(0,1)$. Then
