{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.115960+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4281815edb1f59f2ed68bbf71c39b81403884497a8578f9f798b5f9a51e9a0fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:set_hitting_animals of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] For any $K \subset \subset V(G)$, let us introduce the notation
	\begin{equation}

		Z(K) := 
		\left\{
		(x, H) \in Z \, : \, H \cap K \neq \emptyset
		\right\},
	\end{equation}
	the subset of animals that hit the set $K$.
	Analogously, let us also define, for any $k \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$,
	\begin{equation}

		Z_k(K) := 
		\left\{
		(x, H) \in Z \, : \, H \cap K \neq \emptyset, \, \mathrm{Vol}((x,H)) = k
		\right\},
	\end{equation}
	and denote the finite union of such sets, for $R \in \mathbb{N}$, as
	\begin{equation}

		Z^R(K) :=
		\bigcup_{k = 0}^{R} Z_k(K) =
		\left\{
		(x, H) \in Z \, : \, H \cap K \neq \emptyset, \, \mathrm{Vol}((x,H)) \leq R
		\right\}.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

