{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.877296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42829b3d44d86247c96d728db4f68cdf5fbf8895313a914d29f45934c92aaf62","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm|Straightforward of arXiv:2504.08935, 'Higher-Order Derivatives of First-Passage Percolation with Respect to the Environment'] For every $i\neq j$, every $\alpha,\beta\in\{a,b\}$, and every random variable $\varphi$, 
\begin{align} 
\sigma_i^{\alpha}\circ \sigma_i^{\beta}&=\sigma_i^{\alpha};   \\
\sigma_i^{\alpha}\circ \sigma_j^{\beta}&=\sigma_j^{\beta}\circ\sigma_i^{\alpha};   \\
(\partial_i \varphi)\circ \sigma_i^{\alpha} &= \partial_i \varphi;  
\end{align} 
\begin{align}
\partial_i\partial_i \varphi&=0;   \\
\partial_i\partial_j \varphi&=\partial_j\partial_i\varphi;   \\
\varphi\cdot 1_{\omega_i=\alpha}&=\varphi\circ \sigma_i^{\alpha}\cdot 1_{\omega_i=\alpha}.  
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

