{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.839502+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42ba715e3284f956fa84f6fa8bf59453a632616468ab7df536b18351dada3036","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop radial-incidence of arXiv:2606.26049, '\textsc{Face volume densities of positive-intensity and ideal Poisson--Voronoi tessellatio'] Let \(d\geq2\), let \(k\in\{0,1,\ldots,d-1\}\), and put \(q= d-k\).
For \(\mathbf x=(x_0,\ldots,x_q)\in(\mathbb H^d)^{q+1}\), let
$$
L(\mathbf x)=\{y\in\mathbb H^d:\operatorname{dist}(y,x_0)=\operatorname{dist}(y,x_1)=\ldots=\operatorname{dist}(y,x_q)\}.
$$
For \(y\in L(\mathbf x)\), write
\(\rho(y,\mathbf x)=\operatorname{dist}(y,x_0)=\operatorname{dist}(y,x_1)=\ldots=\operatorname{dist}(y,x_q)\)
for the common distance.  Then, for every non-negative measurable function
\(h:\mathbb H^d\times[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty]\),
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\int_{(\mathbb H^d)^{q+1}}
\int_{L(\mathbf x)}
