{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.913855+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42c15b076be0d7e6be63bb9359f513ca71fcc42c37194827480daa1cab86c914","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop first derivative formula of arXiv:2609.02409, 'Membranes and Maps'] For the unpointed series, we have
    \begin{equation}

        \big[\epsilon_4^0\epsilon_5^{-1}\big]_{\mathrm{CY}} \,
        \mathsf{H}^{\mathrm{st}}\big(~;\epsvec{[5]};u\big)
        =
        u^2 \,
        \Psi\left(
            \frac{1}{2u^2}
            \sum_{i=1}^3
            \left(
                \frac{\kappa(u\epsilon_i)}{u}
                -
                \frac{1}{u^2\epsilon_i}
            \right)
        \right) \,.
    \end{equation}
    For $n\in\{1,2,3\}$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        \big[\epsilon_4^0\epsilon_5^{-1}\big]_{\mathrm{CY}} \,
        \mathsf{H}^{\mathrm{st}}\big(\epsvec[-1]{[n]};\epsvec{[5]};u\big)
        =
        \Psi \big(\mathsf{U}\big(\epsvec{[n]};u\big)\big)
        \frac{(-1)^n}{2}
        \prod_{j=1}^n \prod_{i\neq j}
            \frac{1}{\epsilon_i}\,
        +
        \delta_{n,3}\,
        \frac{2}{u^2\epsilon_1^2\epsilon_2^2\epsilon_3^3} \,,
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}

        \mathsf{U}(\epsvec{[n]};u)
        \coloneqq
        \begin{cases}
            \dfrac{1}{u}
            \dfrac{
                \varsigma(u\epsilon_2)\varsigma(u\epsilon_3)
            }{
                \varsigma(u\epsilon_1)
            }
            & n=1 \,,\\[1em]
            \dfrac{1}{u}
            \dfrac{
                \varsigma(u\epsilon_3)^3
            }{
                \varsigma(u\epsilon_1)\varsigma(u\epsilon_2)
            }
            & n=2 \,,\\[1em]
            \dfrac{1}{u}
            \left(\prod_{i=1}^3\varsigma(u\epsilon_i)\right)
            \left(
                \left(\sum_{i=1}^3\kappa(u\epsilon_i)\right)^2
                -\frac14
            \right)
            & n=3 \,.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    In particular, (thm: lamg lamg-1 limit of the paper) follows. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.565372+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_42c2334b15ed59dac18643c12438b47d3fbd9725c03313dcb9f19c0d365e46b5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Under Assumption~\ref{ass:basic}, the following statements hold.
        \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
                \item For every $z\in\C^+$, the MDE \eqref{eq:intro_mde} has a unique solution $\Pi$ satisfying $\Im\Pi(z)>0$ and $\Im(z^{-1}\Pi_{11}(z))>0$. The map $z\mapsto\Pi(z)$ is holomorphic on $\C^+$.
                      There exists a unique probability measure $\bnu$ such that
