        \end{aligned}$

    \vspace{0.5em}
    \noindent Here, $o(1)$ denotes a function that tends to zero as $n \rightarrow \infty$. Recall further that $m \ge n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.343286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4314299480d4da6ec19858b3826f2306d5996bf93a964b99bf067479930bcaf8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm-boltzmann-ldp of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] For $r\in\mathbbm N$, let $(M(r) ,z(r))$ be sampled from the Boltzmann distribution on bipolar-oriented triangulations with right boundary length $r$ and a marked left boundary vertex (Definition~(def-boltzmann-right of the paper)). 
