{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.621730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_43e5d6d6f3e65f1e5d5097af233f0805e5338f69164f4a88a4b32626c9b18ec6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 (Principal curvatures for weighted $\ell_p$-balls) of arXiv:2605.11995, 'Intrinsic volumes of \(\ell_p\)-balls and a continuum of Maxwell--Poincar\'e--Borel laws f'] Let $x\in \partial \mathbb B_p^n(a)$ be such that $x_i\neq 0$ for all $i=1,\dots, n$.  Then the principal curvatures of $\mathbb B_p^n(a)$ at $x$, denoted by $\lambda_1(x),\dots,\lambda_{n-1}(x)$, are precisely the solutions of the degree $n-1$ polynomial equation
\[
\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^{2p}|x_i|^{2p-2}
\prod_{\substack{j=1\\ j\ne i}}^n
\left((p-1)a_j^p|x_j|^{p-2}
-
\left(\sum_{r=1}^n a_r^{2p}|x_r|^{2p-2}\right)^{1/2}\lambda
\right)=0.
\]
For $m=1,\ldots, n$ the $(m-1)$-st elementary symmetric polynomial of the principal curvatures is given by
\[
