\begin{proof}
Let~$\mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}$ and~${\boldsymbol\upalpha}\colon \mbfM\to \mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}$ be defined as in the proof of Theorem~(t:BendikovSaloffCoste of the paper).
As in the proof of that theorem,~${\boldsymbol\upalpha}$ is a homeomorphism onto its image and a quasi-homeomorphism of Dirichlet forms intertwining~$\big({\boldsymbol\mssE^\mbfs,\msD(\boldsymbol\mssE^\mbfs)}\big)$ and~$\tparen{\boldsymbol\mssE^{\mbfs,{\boldsymbol\upalpha}},\dom{\boldsymbol\mssE^{\mbfs,{\boldsymbol\upalpha}}}}$.
As a consequence, letting~$\mathrm{cap}_\mbfs$ be the first-order capacity associated to~$\big({\boldsymbol\mssE^\mbfs,\msD(\boldsymbol\mssE^\mbfs)}\big)$, and analogously for~$\mathrm{cap}_\mbfs^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}$, we have~$\mathrm{cap}_\mbfs(\mbfA)=\mathrm{cap}_\mbfs^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}({\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfA))$ for every capacitable~$\mbfA\subset\mbfM$.

Now, note that~$\mbfU_{i,j,\delta}$ is $\tau_\mrmp$-open, hence so is~$\mbfU_n$, so that~$\mbfF_n^\mathrm{c}$ is $\tau_\mrmp$-closed.
Furthermore, it is readily seen that~$\mbfU_n\downarrow_n \mbfM_\circ^\mathrm{c}$, hence that~$\mbfF_n \uparrow_n \mbfM_\circ$.
Thus, it remains to show that~$\limsup_n \mathrm{cap}_\mbfs(\mbfF_n^\mathrm{c})=0$.

To this end, note that~$\overline{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfU_n)}$ is $\tau^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mrmp$-compact in~$\mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}$.
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\limsup_n \mathrm{cap}_\mbfs(\mbfF_n^\mathrm{c})=&\ \limsup_n \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfF_n^\mathrm{c})} \leq \limsup_n \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\overline{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfF_n^\mathrm{c})}}
\\
=&\ \inf_n \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\overline{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfU_n)}}= \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\cap_n \overline{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfU_n)}}
\end{align*}
where the last equality holds by properties of Choquet capacities and since $\overline{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfU_n)}$ is $\tau^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mrmp$-compact.
Then,
\begin{align*}
\limsup_n \mathrm{cap}_\mbfs(\mbfF_n^\mathrm{c})\leq&\ \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\cap_n \overline{{\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfU_n)}}
\leq \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\cap_n {\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\overline{\mbfU_n}) \cup \mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_*}
\\
\leq&\ \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\cap_n {\boldsymbol\upalpha}(\mbfU_n)} + \mathrm{cap}^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_\mbfs\tparen{\mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_*}
\\
=&\ \mathrm{cap}_\mbfs\big({\cap_n \mbfU_n}\big) + 0
= \mathrm{cap}_\mbfs\tparen{\mbfM_\circ^\mathrm{c}}=0\,\mathrm{,}\;\,
\end{align*}
where~$\mbfM^{\boldsymbol\upalpha}_*$ is defined as in~(equation eq:PolarMAlpha of the paper) and $\boldsymbol\mssE^{\mbfs,{\boldsymbol\upalpha}}$-polar by the arguments there, and the last equality holds by Lemma~(p:AndiPolar of the paper).
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.351817+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4475170defdb70cf4b065d3aaac7fcdcc5f9704ee3826141c65eaffc1f084749","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma diag:laxify of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $T$ be a small $\infty$-category with a terminal object $1$, and let ${\mathcal{C}}$ be a small $T$-$\infty$-category such that for every $p\colon A\to1$ the functor $p^*\colon{\mathcal{C}}(1)\to{\mathcal{C}}(A)$ is fully faithful and admits a right adjoint $p_*$. Moreover, let ${\mathcal{E}}$ be any presentable (non-parametrized) $\infty$-category and denote by $\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T$ the category of $T$-objects in ${\mathcal{E}}$ (Example~(ex:T-objects of the paper)). Then a $T$-functor $F\colon{\mathcal{C}}\to\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T$ is left Kan extended from $\textup{const}\,{\mathcal{C}}(1)$ if and only if for every $A\in T$ and $X\in{\mathcal{C}}(A)$ the map $F(\varepsilon\colon p^*p_*X\to X)$ in $\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T(A)={\textup{Fun}}(T_{/A}^{\textup{op}},{\mathcal{E}})$ induced by the counit becomes an equivalence after evaluation at $\textup{id}_A$.
    \begin{proof}
        Write $\pi\colon\textup{const}\,{\mathcal{E}}\to\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T$ for the unique $T$-functor extending $\textup{const}\colon{\mathcal{E}}\to\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T(1)={\textup{Fun}}(T^{\textup{op}},{\mathcal{E}})$. By [LLP]*{Theorem~6.9} (for $M=\core T$), the individual right adjoints $\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T(A)\to{\mathcal{E}}$ (evaluating at $\textup{id}_A\in T_{/A}^{\textup{op}}$) assemble into a right adjoint $\rho\colon\textup{Un}^\text{cc}(\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T)\to\textup{Un}^\text{cc}(\textup{const}\,{\mathcal{E}})={\mathcal{E}}\times T^{\textup{op}}$ over $T^{\textup{op}}$ such that the composite
        \[
            {\textup{Fun}}_T({\mathcal{D}},\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T)\xrightarrow{\;\textup{Un}^\text{cc}\;}
            {\textup{Fun}}_{/T^{\textup{op}}}(\textup{Un}^\text{cc}({\mathcal{D}}),\textup{Un}^\text{cc}(\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T))\xrightarrow{\;\rho\circ{-}\;}
            {\textup{Fun}}_{/T^{\textup{op}}}(\textup{Un}^\text{cc}({\mathcal{D}}),{\mathcal{E}}\times T^{\textup{op}})
        \]        
        is an equivalence for every $T$-$\infty$-category ${\mathcal{D}}$. Applying this for ${\mathcal{D}}={\mathcal{C}}$ and ${\mathcal{D}}=\textup{const}\,{\mathcal{C}}(1)$ yields a commutative square
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{tikzcd}[cramped]
                {\textup{Fun}}_T({\mathcal{C}},\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T)\arrow[r,"\sim"]\arrow[d,"\textup{res}"'] & {\textup{Fun}}_{/T^{\textup{op}}}(\textup{Un}^\text{cc}({\mathcal{C}}),{\mathcal{E}}\times T^{\textup{op}})\arrow[d,"\textup{res}"]\\
                {\textup{Fun}}_T(\textup{const}\,{\mathcal{C}}(1),\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T)\arrow[r,"\sim"'] & {\textup{Fun}}_{/T^{\textup{op}}}({\mathcal{C}}(1)\times T^{\textup{op}},{\mathcal{E}}\times T^{\textup{op}})\rlap.
            \end{tikzcd}
        \end{equation}
        By [HA]*{Proposition 7.3.2.6${}^{\textup{op}}$}, the individual right adjoints $p_*\colon{\mathcal{C}}(A)\to{\mathcal{C}}(1)$ assemble into a right adjoint $\gamma\colon\textup{Un}^\text{cc}({\mathcal{C}})\to{\mathcal{C}}(1)\times T^{\textup{op}}$ over $T^{\textup{op}}$, so that the right-hand vertical arrow in $((diag:laxify of the paper))$ admits a \emph{left} adjoint given by precomposing with $\gamma$. On the other hand, the left-hand vertical arrow admits a left adjoint given by parametrized left Kan extension, and it follows formally that also the square where we pass to left adjoints vertically commutes.

        As we assumed each $p^*\colon{\mathcal{C}}(1)\to{\mathcal{C}}(A)$ to be fully faithful, the right adjoint $\gamma$ is a localization at the images of the counits $\varepsilon\colon p^*p_*X\to X$ for all $A\in T$ and $X\in{\mathcal{C}}(A)$. Thus, $F\colon{\mathcal{C}}\to\ul{\mathcal{E}}_T$ is in the image of the left adjoint of the left-hand vertical map (i.e., $F$ is left Kan extended) if and only if $\rho\circ\textup{Un}^\text{cc}(F)$ inverts the above counit maps. By the explicit fiberwise description of $\rho$, this is precisely demanding that each $F(\varepsilon)$ become an equivalence after evaluation at $\textup{id}_A\in T_{/A}^{\textup{op}}$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

