{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.476548+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_44baa102f4399f526fda6cf876d36cbd196f6b11461b31354d3f1fc092b890d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Z3 of arXiv:2607.29020, 'Entropic Symmetrization Resistance'] The symmetrization resistant distributions in $G = \mathbb{Z}_3$ (with addition modulo three) are exactly the extreme two-thirds of every side of $\Delta_3$, except the corners, which are already symmetric ((fig:Z3 of the paper)). 

\begin{figure}
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=5]

\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (1,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0.5,{sqrt(3)/2});

\coordinate (U) at ($ (A)!.333!(B) + (A)!.333!(C) $);

\draw[very thin] (A) -- (B);
\draw[very thin] (B) -- (C);
\draw[very thin] (C) -- (A);

\foreach \P/\Q in {A/B,B/C,C/A} {
  \coordinate (M1) at ($ (\P)!1/3!(\Q) $);
  \coordinate (M2) at ($ (\P)!2/3!(\Q) $);
  \draw[very thick,red] (\P) -- (M1);
  \draw[very thick,red] (M2) -- (\Q);
}

\coordinate (L1) at (1,0.5);
\coordinate (L2) at ($ (L1)!2!(U) $);
\coordinate (fminus) at ($ (A)!0.115!(C) $);
\coordinate (f) at ($ (B)!0.115!(C) $);

\draw[dashed] (L1) -- (L2);

\node[below left] at (A) {$(1,0,0)$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$(0,0,1)$};
\node[above right] at (f) {$f$};
\fill (f) circle (0.013);
\node[above left] at (fminus) {$f_-$};
\fill (fminus) circle (0.013);
\node[right] at (L1) {$\Sym(f)$};
\node[above] at (C) {$(0,1,0)$};
\draw[fill=white] (A) circle[radius=0.013];
\draw[fill=white] (B) circle[radius=0.013];
\draw[fill=white] (C) circle[radius=0.013];
\fill (U) circle (0.01);
\node[below right] at (U) {$u$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The probability simplex for $\mathbb{Z}_3$. 
The red parts of the simplex are symmetrization resistant. 
A representative symmetrization-resistant PMF $f$ with symmetrizer space $\Sym(f)$ is shown. }
\end{figure}

\begin{proof}
Indeed, by (thm:no_symmetrizers_in_interior of the paper) it is sufficient to consider $f \in \partial\Delta_3$. 
By (lem:Zp_symmetrizer_space of the paper), $\dim(\Sym(f)) \ge 1$, and clearly $\dim(\Sym(f)) = 2$ iff $\Sym(f) = \Delta_3$, i.e. $f$ is symmetric. 
Since only asymmetric $f$ may be symmetrization resistant, we therefore conclude that $\dim(\Sym(f)) = 1$. 
Note as in the proof of (thm:no_symmetrizers_in_interior of the paper) that $u = (\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3}) \in \Sym(f)$ and $f_- \in \Sym(f)$. 
Since $f_-$ is on the boundary of the simplex, $u \ne f_-$ and we conclude that $\Sym(f) = {\rm aff}(\{u,f_-\}) \cap \Delta_3$. 
Thus $\Sym(f)$ intersects $\partial \Delta_3$ at exactly two points. 
One of these points is $f_-$; denote the other by $p$. 
Thus for $g \in \Sym(f) = \conv(f_-,p)$ there exists $\lambda \in [0,1]$ such that $g = \lambda p + (1-\lambda)f_-$ and 
\[ H(g) \ge \lambda H(p) + (1-\lambda)H(f_-) ,\]
In turn, $H(f_-) = H(f)$ and $f$ is not symmetrization resistant if and only if $H(f) \ge \lambda H(p) + (1-\lambda)H(f_-)$, i.e. if and only if $H(f) \ge H(p)$. 
Note that since $f$ and $p$ are in $\partial\Delta$, each must have an entry equal to zero, i.e., both $f$ and $p$ are two-point distributions, albeit with different support. 
Explicitly, writing $a \le \frac{1}{2}$ and $1-a$ for the nonzero elements of $f$, a routine computation shows that the nonzero elements of $p$ are $\frac{1-2a}{2-3a}$ and $\frac{1-a}{2-3a}$. 
Thus $H(f) \le H(p)$ if and only if $1-a \ge \frac{1-a}{2-3a}$, 

i.e. $a \le \frac{1}{3}$. 
Thus the set of symmetrization-resistant distributions in $\mathbb{Z}_3$ is exactly the extreme two-thirds of each side of the boundary of $\Delta_3$, except for the distributions which are already symmetric (in this case, just $\mathbb{1}_0$). 
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

