{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.449502+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_44f73933552e962f7dc329b8cf6dea10e22567bb75ca701f5154bd857a3bb981","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma convsubs of arXiv:2609.02213, 'Invariant subspaces for free linearizations of Lipschitz maps'] Let $(M,e)$ be a pointed metric space. Let $p\in\mathbb{N}$ and let $a_1,\ldots,a_p\in\mathbb{K}$ be such that $\sum_{i=1}^p a_i = 0$ and
		\begin{equation} 
			\sum_{i\in J}a_i\neq 0,
			\qquad \forall J\subset\{1,\ldots,p\},\; \emptyset \neq J \subsetneq \{1,\ldots, p\}.
		\end{equation}
		For $1\leq k\leq p$, let $(x_k(n))_n$ be sequences in $M$, and set $\gamma_n:=\sum_{k=1}^p a_k\delta(x_k(n))$.
		If $(\gamma_n)_n$ has a weakly convergent subsequence with limit $\gamma=\sum_{k=1}^q b_k\delta(y_k) \neq 0$,
		where $1\leq q\leq p$ and $b_k\neq 0$, then, after passing to a further subsequence,
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Each $y_k$ is the limit of at least one of the sequences $(x_m(n))_n$;
			\item If $(x_m(n))_n$ does not converge to some $y_k$, then it converges to $e$.
		\end{enumerate}
		In particular, the subsequence converge to $\gamma$ in norm.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.985147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_44f81392214d907b9473430facc90fc2c445e5f295da96ba1ba67e731b22d5ae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:multil of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Choose $m\in \mathbb N$, $\vec \mathbf k \in \mathbb R^m_+ $, and $\vec \boldsymbol{\tau}\in \mathbb R_{\ge 0}^m$ such that $\boldsymbol{\tau}_1\le \cdots \le \boldsymbol{\tau}_m$, any real $C_1>0$, and a large enough $N\in \mathbb N$. We take the rank $N$ G$\beta$E corners process $\{y^n_i\}_{1\leq i\leq n\leq N}$, with variance $\tau=2N/\beta$; and $\vec k \in \mathbb Z^m$, and integers $N\ge N_1\ge \cdots \ge N_m$,  such that
\begin{equation} 
|k_\ell-\mathbf k_\ell N^{2/3}|<C_1, \qquad  |N - \boldsymbol{\tau}_\ell N^{2/3}-N_\ell|<C_1, \qquad \text{for each }\ell\in\llbracket 1,m\rrbracket.
\end{equation}
Then as $N\to\infty$, uniformly over the ($N$--dependent) choices of $\vec k$ and $\vec N$ satisfying (equation eq_x1 of the paper):
\[
 \mathbb E \left[ \prod_{\ell=1}^m \left(\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{N_\ell} \left(\frac{y^{N_\ell}_i}{2\sqrt{N_\ell N}}\right)^{k_\ell}+\left(\frac{y^{N_\ell}_i}{2\sqrt{N_\ell N}}\right)^{k_\ell+1}\right) \right]\to \mathbf L_\beta(\vec \mathbf k, \vec\boldsymbol{\tau}).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

