{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:45:05.472898+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_450a81b1a35f1fb0f042f6ab4f4a2d2de1a90c42b5c19742362e1f8e4ae78e67","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 163 (Two-sided uniform BDG extension) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] For every $1\le p<\infty$, there are constants
$0<c_p\le C_p<\infty$, independent of
$\cP$, such that
\begin{equation}
  c_p\norm{H}_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^p}
  \le \norm{I(H)}_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^p}
  \le C_p\norm{H}_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^p}
\end{equation}
for every finite-upper-energy elementary predictable $H$.  Hence $I$
extends uniquely to a
bounded injective linear map with closed range,
\[
  I:\mathbb H_{\cP}^p
  \longrightarrow
  \mathbb S_{\cP}^p.
\]
For every $P\in\cP$ and $H\in\mathbb H_{\cP}^p$, one has the commuting
identity
\begin{equation}
 \pi_P(I(H))
 =\int_0^{\cdot}j_P(H)_s\,\dd X_s
 \qquad\text{in }\mathbb S^p(P).
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every deterministic $t$,
\begin{equation}
 I(\one_{[0,t]}H)=r_tI(H).
\end{equation}
Thus, with the natural truncations on integrands and continuous paths, the
common stochastic integral is a stable-causal closed embedding.","status":"unverified"}

