{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.114223+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45300b53bcf3ce27d36919e4d5cd3af4717d813791878c1c885f88c34226fab9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:free_product of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Suppose that we are given two non-trivial connected transitive graphs $G_i = (V(G_i), E(G_i))$, $i = 1,2$, with disjoint vertex sets and some distinguished vertices $o_i$, $i = 1,2$. The \textbf{free product}
	\begin{equation}

		G := (V(G), E(G)) :=
		G_1 \star G_2
	\end{equation}
	is constructed in the following way.
	The vertices of $G$ are all the finite {words}:
	\begin{equation}

		V(G) :=  \left\{
		x_1 \ldots x_n \, \left| \, 
		\parbox{19.5em}{
			$n \in \mathbb{N}; \, x_j \in V(G_1) \cup V(G_2) \setminus \{ o_1, o_2 \}; \\
			x_k \in V(G_i) \implies x_{k+1} \notin V(G_i), \, i=1,2$
		}		
		\right. \right\} 
		\cup
		\left\{ o \right\}
	\end{equation}
	where $o$ denotes the {empty word}, the distinguished vertex of $G$, obtained by identifying each $o_i$, $i = 1,2$ with $o$.
	If we are given words $x = x_1 \ldots x_k \in V(G)$ and $y = y_1 \ldots y_m \in V(G)$ with $x_k \in V(G_i)$ and $y_1 \notin V(G_i)$, then $xy$ stands for the concatenation as words. In addition, for all $x \in V(G)$ we define $x o = o x = x$.
	Furthermore, adjacency in $G$ is given as follows: for $i = 1,2$, if $\{ x,y \} \in E(G_i)$, then $\{ zx, zy \} \in E(G)$ for all $z = z_1 \ldots z_{\ell} \in V(G)$, with $z_{\ell} \notin V(G_i)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.163467+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45364e444c3604c1503d5bfeccde41cdb48dd7fccb807ae17d5f56ab6fb91f89","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2604.17526, '2604.17526'] Suppose the configuration space~$\mathcal X = \T^d$, and~$U$ is a regular double-well potential with non-degenerate wells of equal depth.
  Given~$\alpha, \nu > 0$, and~$\epsilon_1 > 0$, there exists constants~$ C_N$ and~$\hat C_T$ such that the following holds.

  For every~$\delta > 0$ and~$\epsilon \in (0, \epsilon_1)$, choose
  \begin{align}

    K &= \ceil[\bigg]{ \frac{1}{\epsilon \nu} }
    \,,
    \\

    T &> \hat C_T\paren[\bigg]{K^{(1+\alpha)} + \frac{1}{\epsilon} + \log\paren[\Big]{\frac{1}{\delta}} + \log N}
    \,,
    \\

    N &= \ceil[\bigg]{\frac{ C_N}{\delta^2}}K^2
    \,.
  \end{align}
  Let~$\epsilon_2$, \dots, $\epsilon_{K+1} = \epsilon$ be such that~$\set{1/\epsilon_k}_{1 \leq k \leq K+1}$ are linearly spaced.
  Run Algorithm~\ref{a:aisAutoNormalized} with these parameters, and let~$\set{X^i_K, w^i_K}_{1 \leq i \leq N}$ be the~$N$ points and normalized weights returned.
 Define the empirical measure~$\mu_N$ by
  \begin{equation}
    \mu_{N} \defeq \sum_{i=1}^N w^i_K \delta_{X^i_K}\,.
  \end{equation}
  Then, for every bounded measurable test function~$f$, we have
  \begin{equation}
    \E\abs[\big]{
      \ip{f, \mu_{N}} - \ip{f, \pi_{\epsilon}}
    }^2
    \leq \norm{f}_\osc^2 \delta^2
    \,.
  \end{equation}
  Consequently, for every~$s > d/2$ there exists an explicit dimensional constant~$\hat C_s$ (independent of~$\epsilon_1, \epsilon, \alpha, \nu, \delta$) such that
  \begin{equation}
    \E\norm[\big]{\mu_{N} - \pi_{\epsilon}}_{H^{-s}}^2
      \leq C_s \delta^2
      \,.
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

