{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.569173+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_457e8ab1796fdbe8a1b3659686645959e07c758cfbd8abce3aac7adad26e9294","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 13 of arXiv:2605.30779, '2605.30779'] Let \(U\subset\mathbb C\) be an open set, let \(x_0\in U\cap\mathbb R\), and assume that \(\{f_N\}_{N=1}^\infty\xrightarrow{\mathrm{mer}} f\) on \(U\). Suppose that \(x_0\) is not a pole of \(f\), and
  \begin{align*}
    f(x_0)= {}& 0, & f'(x_0) \neq {}& 0 .
  \end{align*}
  Then there exists \(\delta>0\) such that, for all sufficiently large \(N\), \(f_N\) has exactly one zero \(z_N\) in the disc \(\{z:|z-x_0|<\delta\}\), and \(z_N\to x_0\). Moreover,
  \[
    f_N'(z_N)\to f'(x_0).
  \]
  If, in addition, \(f_N(\overline z)=\overline{f_N(z)}\) on this disc, then \(z_N\in\mathbb R\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.276403+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_458395b5fda6adb26b50fa5c88bddab379fe4f5a201f9c8ceeba3f458118f2a8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 35 (Adapted Brenier couplings between filtered Gaussian processes) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] Let $\bX = \bX^{a, L}, \bY \in \bY^{b, M}$ be elements of $\cFG$. A Gaussian bicausal coupling $\pi^P$ of the form \eqref{eqn:joint.Gaussian}, where $P = \diag(P_1, \ldots, P_N)$ with $\| P_t \|_{2 \rightarrow 2} \leq 1$, is an adapted Brenier coupling of $(\bX, \bY)$ if and only if
\begin{equation} 
P_t \in \mathscr{P}( L_{t,t}^{\intercal} M_{t,t}), \quad t \in [N].
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.861445+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4588efe23f08ae04a09208b2aa14f29d2f3fbbc21cfadd98cf38ebf767db81ca","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prp 38 of arXiv:2511.21496, 'Optimal local central limit theorems on Wiener chaos'] Let $\mathbf{F} = \{F_n\}_{n \in \N} \subset \cW_m$ with $m \geq 2$ be such that $\E[F_n^2] = 1$ and $F_n \xrightarrow[n \to \infty]{d} \cN(0,1)$.
Then, for any $j \in \N_0$, there exists $N_{\mathbf{F},j} \in \N$ such that $\rho_{F_n} \in C_{\mathrm{b}}^{j}(\R)$ and
\begin{equation}
    \left\lVert \rho_{F_n}^{(j)} - \rho_{\cN}^{(j)}\right\rVert_{L^r(\R)} \leq \wt{C}_{\mathbf{F},j,m,r} \mathbf{M}(F_n)
\end{equation}
hold for every $n \geq N_{\mathbf{F},j}$ and $r \in [1,\infty)$, where the constant $\wt{C}_{\mathbf{F},j, m, r}$ depends on $\mathbf{F}$, $j$, $m$, and $r$ but not on $n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.453837+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_458980698caa45f0bf32cea28e94240c563613cc05c9f699035d24e041a597f0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 41 of arXiv:2604.03213, 'Asymptotic expansion for transport maps \\ between laws of multimatrix models'] Given $V_0:\R^n\to\R$ and $V_1:\R^n\to\R$, we define $V_t := tV_1+(1-t)V_0$. We also assume that $V_0,V_1\in\cC^1(\R^n)$ and that there exists a function $\psi:\R^n\times [0,1]\to\R$ such that 
        \begin{equation}

            \Delta \psi_t(x) - \langle\nabla V_t(x),\nabla \psi_t(x)\rangle_{\R^n} = \dot{V_t}(x) - \mu_{V_t}\big[\dot{V_t}\big],
        \end{equation}
        where $\Delta$ is the Laplacian, $\nabla$ the gradient, $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\R^n}$ the usual scalar product in $\R^n$, $\dot{V_t} = V_1-V_0$, and $\mu_{V_t}\big[f\big]$ is the expectation of $f$ with respect to $\mu_{V_t}$. If we set $T_t^N$ the solution to the following differential equation
        \begin{equation}

             \dot{T_t}(x) = \nabla \psi_t\left( T_t(x)\right),\quad T_0 = \id_{\R^n},
        \end{equation}
        then $T_t$ is a transport map between $\mu_{V_0}$ and $\mu_{V_t}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.564108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_458a14fee354b4f7659035f8812a293c9306542a22fd2a73550ab40fb068e292","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:tv-comparison of arXiv:2511.01058, 'Limit profiles and cutoff for the Burnside process on Sylow double cosets'] Fix \(p \ge 11\),  \(t \ge 1\) and \(\sigma \in S_{pk}\). If \(T(\sigma)=a\), then
