    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.690310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45c8e24bd6cd6cb6204deab38276f0f9cd7f6031cd10a65dfc4e33d3a4ab5141","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 (\emph{Link between $Y$ and optimal stopping in randomized strategies}) of arXiv:2607.21062, 'A new probabilistic approach for\\ mean field games of optimal stopping'] Suppose that Assumptions \ref{assumption:1A} and \ref{assumption:1B} hold. Then, for every $L \in \mathcal V$ and the associated solution $(X,Y,Z,A) \in \mathbb S^2 \times \mathbb S^2 \times \mathbb H^2 \times \mathbb A^2$ to system \eqref{MFG SDE}-\eqref{MFRBSDE}, it holds that
\begin{align}
Y_t=\underset{\hat L \in \mathcal{V}_t}{\textup{ess\,sup}}\,\mathbb{E}\bigg[\int_t^T f(s,X_s, \mathbb{E}[\bar{f}(s,X_s)L_s])\hat L_sds-\int_{t^-}^Th\bigg(s, X_s,\mathbb{E}\bigg[\int_{0^-}^T\phi(s-r)dL_r\bigg]\bigg)d\hat L_s\bigg|\mathcal{F}_t\bigg],
\end{align}
for all $0\leq t\leq T$, where $\mathcal{V}_t=\{ \hat L \in \mathcal{V}\colon\hat L_{t^-}=1\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.600104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45cbe44be92980cbad5018789c5fdcd0b942d4d6f5870186240829fbb9691c29","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:solution-wise-mass-criterion of arXiv:2608.11931, '\texorpdfstring{Nonlinear parabolic characterizations of stochastic completeness at infini'] Assume that $G$ satisfies \hyperref[def:SC-infinity]{\textup{{(SC\textsubscript{$\infty$})}}}{}.  Let $u$ be a bounded positive pointwise solution of
the (eq:GPME of the paper)
on $[0,T]\times X$ such that $u(t,\cdot)\in\ell^1(X,\mu)$ for every $t\in[0,T]$ and assume that
\begin{equation}
    F_t:=\int_0^t\Phi u(s,\cdot)\,\mathrm d s\in\ell^1(X,\mu)
    \qquad \text{for }0\le t\le T.
\end{equation}
Then $u$ satisfies the generalized mass balance~\hyperref[eq:nonlinear-generalized-mass]{\textup{(MB)}}{} and $\mathsf M_u$
satisfies the differential form of the generalized mass balance: $\mathsf M_u\in AC([0,T])$ and
