{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.231347+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45f6096b4523c40fded4c8ca39df9bc1cbb199f1affb0845e4014cb28b823ce1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 49 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Potential functions $C^0(M)$]
Given $M \subset M_0$. $C^0(M)$ is the collection of all $\mathbf{Z}$-valued linear functions defined on $C_0(M)$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
C^0(M):=Hom(C_0(M), \mathbf{Z}),
\end{equation*}
where $C_0(M)$ is the collection of all formal finite sums of the vertices of $M$ i.e.,
\begin{multline*}
C_0(M):=\{ n_1v_1 + n_2v_2 + \ldots + n_kv_k  \ |\   k \in \mathbf{N},\\ 
n_i  \in \mathbf{Z},\  v_i \in Ver(M) \ (i=1, 2, \ldots, k))\}.
\end{multline*}
Elements of $C^0(M)$ are called \textit{potential functions} (or \textit{$0$-forms}) on $M$.","status":"unverified"}

