{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.675633+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46c17d5be73a42edd37aa1a7cd1ec5e8050b35037f30c9dce82fd03ac7a04f46","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (Sparre Andersen's formula: Probabilistic version) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $n\geq1$. Let $\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n$ be real-valued,
possibly dependent random variables with partial sums $S_k:= \xi_1+\dots+\xi_k$, $1\leq k\leq n$, and suppose that the following conditions are satisfied:
\begin{itemize}
\item [(i)]  \emph{Symmetric exchangeability:} For every permutation $\sigma$ of the set $\{1,\dots,n\}$ and every choice of signs $\eps_1,\dots,\eps_n\in \{-1,+1\}$, the following equality in distribution holds:
    $$
    (\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n) \stackrel{d}{=}(\eps_1 \xi_{\sigma(1)}, \dots, \eps_n \xi_{\sigma(n)}).
    $$
\item[(ii)] \emph{No partial-sum atoms at $0$:}
For every $1\leq i \leq n$, we have $\pr[S_i = 0] = 0$.
\end{itemize}
Then
    \begin{align*}
        \pr(S_1 > 0, S_2 > 0, \dots, S_n > 0) = \frac{1}{2^{2n}}
        \binom{2n}{n}.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:16:49.557639+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46c5b64a5639fc7d31ee00bb82196a489ceb747b028d8cd16c36d3407bb1e613","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defs 12 (Metric local structure) of arXiv:2509.06869, 'Infinite Interacting Brownian Motions and EVI Gradient Flows'] Let~$\mcX$ be a topological local structure as in Definition~\ref{d:TLS}, and~$\mssd\colon X^\tym{2}\rar[0,\infty]$ be an extended distance. We say that~$(\mcX,\mssd)$ is a \emph{metric local structure}, if
\begin{enumerate}[$(a)$]
\item $\mcX$ is a topological local structure in the sense of Definition~\ref{d:TLS};

\item the space~$(X,\mssd)$ is a \emph{complete}  metric space;

\item $\msE=\msE_\mssd\eqdef \Bdd{\mssd}\cap \A$ is the localizing ring of $\A$-measurable $\mssd$-bounded sets (in particular~$\mssm$ is finite on~$\Ed$).
\end{enumerate}

If~$\mssd$ is finite, then it metrizes~$\T$,~\iref{i:d:EMLS:1} reduces to the requirement that~$(X,\mssd)$ be a complete metric space, and we say that~$(\mcX,\mssd)$ is a \emph{metric local structure}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.958056+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46c5d90774ed0d743f6b28e814712324bbc24b8d429b0a78fc099c1fe9b10f9b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:circuit-shape of arXiv:2608.23545, 'Eulerian walkers on $\Z^2$ have range exponent $2/3$'] Let $\mu$ be the passage function from
