{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.239223+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4760d3317a676513dd4f8ee9fbe00498747d30494b20a2955a64160df059f451","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 49 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Suppose that  $(\eta_t)_{t \geq 0}$ and $(\eta^{\prime}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ are defined on the same underlying graph $G=(V,E)$ (either the integer lattice or the torus of length $N$), but are allowed to have different initial data. 
We define the \textbf{basic coupling} $\mathbf{P}$ between the ASEPs $(\eta_t)_{t \geq 0}$ and $(\eta^{\prime}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ as follows.
For all edges in $E$, we place independent rate $(1+q)$ Poisson clocks. Whenever the clock of an edge $e=\{ x,x+1\}$ rings, and the exclusion processes are in states $\eta$ and $\eta^{\prime}$, respectively, we sample an independent Bernoulli-$(1+q)^{-1}$-distributed random variable $B$. If
\begin{itemize}
\item $B=1$: Then, if $\eta(x)=1-\eta(x+1)=1$ holds, we move the particle at site $x$ to site $x+1$ in configuration $\eta$. Similarly, if $\eta^{\prime}(x)=1-\eta^{\prime}(x+1)=1$ holds, we move the particle at site $x$ to site $x+1$ in configuration $\eta^{\prime}$. 
\item $B=0$. Then, if $\eta(x)=1-\eta(x+1)=0$ holds, we move the particle at site $x+1$ to site $x$ in configuration $\eta$. Similarly, if $\eta^{\prime}(x)=1-\eta^{\prime}(x+1)=0$ holds, we move the particle at site $x+1$ to site $x$ in configuration $\eta^{\prime}$. 

\end{itemize}
In all other cases, the configuration does not change.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.351897+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4763fc03f8ce7e92cd6957a8756014a35030ef991f42ed3b3c7159e0e450da44","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem-holo-asymp of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Let $\mathbbm D = \{z\in\mathbbm C : |z| < 1\}$. Let $\psi : \overline{\mathbbm D} \to \mathbbm C$ be holomorphic on $\mathbbm D$ and continuous on $\overline{\mathbbm D}$.
Assume that there exists $\nu \in (0,1)$ and $c \in \mathbbm C \setminus i \mathbbm R$ such that
\begin{equation} 
\psi(e^{i t}) = 1 + (c \mathbbm 1_{(t > 0)} + \overline c \mathbbm 1_{(t < 0)} ) |t|^\nu  + o(t^\nu)  ,\quad \text{as $t \to 0$}. 
\end{equation} 
Then setting $b  = \operatorname{Re}(c)\sec\Big(\tfrac{\pi\nu}{2}\Big)  \in \mathbbm R\setminus \{0\}$,
\begin{equation} 
\psi(1-r) = 1  + b r^\nu + o(r^\nu)  ,\quad \text{as $r \to 0$}. 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.550883+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4765da681cc8d33ca478e46f948ae63f0a03edabab8c203e26250b440024eb07","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: stable of arXiv:2411.17109, 'On the maximal correlation of some stochastic processes'] Let $(X,Y)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ be a stable random vector with index $\alpha\in(0,2)$. Let $C_{++}$ , $C_{+-}$, $C_{-+}$, $C_{--}$, $D^{x}_{+}$, $D^{x}_{-}$, $D^{y}_{+}$, $D^{y}_{-}$ be some integrals defined in Lemma~(lem: stable Op nu of the paper). Then we have that
