{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.638964+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_47a7e47577d998458679a4eaad8881caf86b577b8ad7a0885f3a15c3fce4732c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Theorem:SBridge=frakh-transform of arXiv:2411.13765, 'Schr\"odinger Bridge Problem for Jump Diffusions'] Consider the SBP (equation Definition:DynamicSBP of the paper) with a strong Markov reference measure $\mathbf{R}$ and target endpoint distributions $\rho_0,\rho_T$. Let $(\mathfrak{f},\mathfrak{g})$ be the solution to the Schr\"odinger system (equation Equation:fgSchrodingerSystem of the paper), $\mathfrak{h}$ be as in (equation def:frak_h of the paper) and $\mathbf{P}$ be as in (equation eq:def of bP of the paper). Then, the SBP solution $\hat{\mathbf{P}}$ is the $h$-transform of $\mathbf{P}$ by $\mathfrak{h}$, i.e., $\hat\mathbf{P}=\mathbf{P}^{\mathfrak{h}}$ where $\mathbf{P}^{\mathfrak{h}}$ is  given by (equation Definition:h-transform,general case with r0 of the paper)\footnote{In this case, by the definition (equation eq:def of bP of the paper) of $\mathbf{P}$, $\mathfrak{h}(0,X_0)>0$ a.s. under $\mathbf{P}$, and hence $r_0=1$ and $\mathbbm{1}_{\left\{ X_0\in A_0 \right\}}$ can be omitted in (equation Definition:h-transform,general case with r0 of the paper).} (or equivalently (equation Definition:h-transform,general case with tau0 of the paper)) with $h=\mathfrak{h}$.

    Now assume that $\mathfrak{h}$ is harmonic. We have the following: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If the operator $L$ in (equation ReferenceGenerator of the paper) satisfies \textup{(Assumption:MartingaleProblem-s,x of the paper)} and $\mathbf{P}$ is the solution to the martingale problem for $(L,\rho_0)$, then $\hat\mathbf{P}$ is the solution to the martingale problem for $(L^{\mathfrak{h}},\rho_0)$, where $L^{\mathfrak{h}}$ is given by (equation Definition:L^h of the paper) with $h=\mathfrak{h}$. 
    \item If the SDE (equation ReferenceLevyIto of the paper) satisfies \textup{ (Assumption:SDEsolution of the paper)} and $(\left\{ X_t \right\}_{[0,T]},\mathbf{P})$ is a solution to (equation ReferenceLevyIto of the paper) with $X_0\sim\rho_0$, then $(\left\{ X_t \right\}_{[0,T]},\hat\mathbf{P})$ is a solution to the SDE: \begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
       \hspace{0.5cm} dX_t=b^{\mathfrak{h}}(t,X_{t-})&dt+\sigma(t,X_{t-})dB_t \\
       &+ \int_{|z|\leq 1}\gamma(t,X_{t-},z)\tilde{N}^{\mathfrak{h}}(dt,dz)+\int_{|z|>1}\gamma(t,X_{t-},z)N(dt,dz)
    \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    with $X_0\sim\rho_0$, where $b^{\mathfrak{h}}$ is as in (equation definition_b^h of the paper) with $h=\mathfrak{h}$, $\tilde{N}^{\mathfrak{h}}(dt,dz)=N(dt,dz)-\nu^{\mathfrak{h}}(dz)dt$, and $\nu^{\mathfrak{h}}$ is given by (equation definition_nu^h of the paper) with $h=\mathfrak{h}$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

