{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.386471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4874b42bf4774702779cbd69e8acf702ce565ada1d87f4f3bf592922f535106d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:g of arXiv:2608.18320, 'Asymptotics for Beta-Splitting Trees via Homogeneous Fragmentations and Meromorphic Potent'] Fix an integer $n\geq0$, $\gamma\in(0,1)$, and
$0\le\ell\le r\le1$ such that $n+1-r>0$,
\[
        \alpha:=n+1-r>0.
\]
For an integer $N\geq1$, set $R_N:=N-\gamma$, and let $D_N$ be the
positively oriented boundary of the square
\[
        \left\{
        w\in\mathbb C:
        |\operatorname{Re} w|\leq R_N,\quad
        |\operatorname{Im} w|\leq R_N
        \right\}.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
        \sup_{\ell\leq\eta\leq r}
        \sup_{w\in D_N}
        |g(n+\eta,w)|
        =
        \mathrm{O}(
        R_N^{-\alpha})=
        \mathrm{O}(N^{-\alpha}),
        \qquad \text{as $N\to\infty$.}
\end{equation}
Consequently, for all sufficiently large $N$ and every
$\eta\in[\ell,r]$, the function $f_{n+\eta}$ does not have zeros nor
poles on $D_N$, and
\begin{equation}
        \sup_{\ell\leq\eta\leq r}
        \sup_{w\in D_N}
        \left|
        \frac1{f_{n+\eta}(w)}
        -
        \frac1{c(n+\eta)}
        \right|=
        \mathrm{O}(N^{-\alpha}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.467349+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4878c08d5abf75a6b66e7f78f0e67afa06f4accfd5ea99fb67b452fd110c9e91","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (Intrinsic coefficient geometry and two-sided Gaussian recovery) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] For every $m\ge1$, $1\le r<\infty$, and $1\le p<\infty$, there are
constants
\begin{equation}

 0<\alpha_{m,p,r}\le\beta_{m,p,r}<\infty,
 \qquad
 0<\lambda_{m,p,r}\le\Lambda_{m,p,r}<\infty
\end{equation}
such that, for every $K_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\CutCoeff_{m,r}$ and every
$K_{\mathrm{sym}}\in\Coeff_{m,r}$,
\begin{align}

 \alpha_{m,p,r}\norm{K_{\mathrm{dec}}}_{\CutCoeff_{m,r}}
 &\le\norm{\cT_{K_{\mathrm{dec}}}^{(m)}}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \le\beta_{m,p,r}\norm{K_{\mathrm{dec}}}_{\CutCoeff_{m,r}},\\

 \lambda_{m,p,r}\norm{K_{\mathrm{sym}}}_{\Coeff_{m,r}}
 &\le\norm{I_m(K_{\mathrm{sym}})}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \le\Lambda_{m,p,r}\norm{K_{\mathrm{sym}}}_{\Coeff_{m,r}}.
\end{align}
Both evaluators are Banach-space isomorphisms onto closed subspaces.
Consequently, at fixed $m,p,r$, coefficient convergence is equivalent to
convergence of the corresponding decoupled or same-field chaos in
$L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)$.  At the Hilbert fixed point the probability exponent
$p=2$ gives the exact
identities
\begin{equation}

 \norm{\cT_{K_{\mathrm{dec}}}^{(m)}}_{L^2(\Omega;\Sch_2)}
 =\norm{K_{\mathrm{dec}}}_{\CutCoeff_{m,2}},
 \qquad
 \norm{I_m(K_{\mathrm{sym}})}_{L^2(\Omega;\Sch_2)}
 =\sqrt{m!}\,\norm{K_{\mathrm{sym}}}_{\Coeff_{m,2}}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.501010+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_487a3d605f9d06aafc6ee453f0b463ee4454b948a152998551b4f6f441f1907d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lm 10 of arXiv:2609.02576, 'Sharp estimates for the Jacobi and trigonometric Dunkl heat kernels'] One has
