{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.822543+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48bd57cb20b65a195576d06d5a385a3017c000cb234f73ede015c5fee187e74a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def: branched rough paths of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] For $\alpha\in(0,1]$, $N:=\lfloor\frac{1}{\alpha}\rfloor$ and $T>0$, a branched $\alpha$-rough path is a map $\mathbb X:[0,T]_\leq^2\to \mathcal{H}^\ast_N$ such that 
		\begin{itemize}
			\item For any $(s,u,t)\in[0,T]_\leq^3$, $\mathbb X$ satisfies Chen's relation in $\mathcal{H}_N^\ast$
			\begin{equation}
				\mathbb X_{st}=\mathbb X_{su}\star\mathbb X_{ut}\,.
			\end{equation}
			\item For any $\tau\in{\mathcal T}$
			\begin{equation*}
				|\mathbb X_{st}(\tau)|\lesssim |t-s|^{|\tau|\alpha}\, ,
			\end{equation*}
			uniformly on $0\le s\le t\le T$.
			\item For any forest $w=\tau_1\cdots\tau_k\in{\mathcal F}^{\leq N}$,
			\[\mathbb X_{st}(w)=\prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb X_{st}(\tau_i)\,.\]
		\end{itemize}
		If in addition $\mathbb X_{st}(\bullet_i)=\delta X^i_{st}$ for a path $X:[0,T]\to \mathbb{R}^d$, we say that $\mathbb X$ is an $\alpha$-branched rough path lift of $X$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:20:30.647825+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48c12b3c5d99198ccd79ca7d6569e16a89fe6a0e8df781c82b1f62fce4cac2da","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 36 of arXiv:2609.01932, 'Essential dimension and faithful rank of finite $p$-gerbes'] A vector bundle on $\cX$ is $f$-faithful if and only if its
restriction to $\fC_f$ is faithful.  Consequently
\begin{equation}
\rdim(f)=
\min_{\mathcal B\text{ basis of }\fC_f^*}
\sum_{\chi\in\mathcal B}m_\chi(\cX).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.488337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48c2cde8b127b8f7d5114e0f8bde95abe72d556b187d9707823c4a457eee41b9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4 of arXiv:2607.03924, 'Tree Coordinates and Range Martingales for Positive Operator-Valued Measures'] Let $m\geq2$, and let 
\begin{equation}
\Omega_{m}=\left\{ 0,\ldots,m-1\right\} ^{\mathbb{N}}
\end{equation}
with its product Borel structure. If $E$ is a POVM on $\Omega_{m}$,
write 
\begin{equation}
E_{w}=E\left(\left[w\right]\right),\qquad H_{w}=\overline{ran}\,(E^{1/2}_{w})
\end{equation}
for each finite word $w$ in the alphabet $\left\{ 0,\ldots,m-1\right\} $.
Then, for every $w$, there are unique positive contractions 
\begin{equation}
A^{\left(0\right)}_{w},\ldots,A^{\left(m-1\right)}_{w}\in B\left(H_{w}\right)
\end{equation}
such that 
\begin{equation}
\sum^{m-1}_{j=0}A^{\left(j\right)}_{w}=I_{H_{w}}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
E_{wj}=E^{1/2}_{w}A^{\left(j\right)}_{w}E^{1/2}_{w}.
\end{equation}

Conversely, suppose that $E_{\varnothing}=I$, and that the cylinder
values are defined recursively by positive contractions $A^{\left(j\right)}_{w}\in B\left(H_{w}\right)$
satisfying \prettyref{eq:2-13} and \prettyref{eq:2-14}. Then there
is a unique POVM $E$ on $\Omega_{m}$ with these cylinder values.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.227068+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48c36c9402e61efd5c5af938973856cc32522e1ec3ec5c5e805d1ecd01682521","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-lacunary of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] \begin{enumerate}
    \item A point process $\theta$ is called \textbf{lacunary} on $(0,\infty)$ if there exist constants $c,d \in (0, \infty)$, $c<d$, such that  
    \begin{equation} 
        \theta([cx, d x ]) \overset{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow} 0
    \end{equation} 
    as $x \rightarrow \infty $\footnote{The intuitive meaning of this definition is that atoms of the point process $\theta$ in $(0,\infty)$ are extremely spaced out and have large gaps, hence the choice of the word \textbf{lacunary}.}. 

    \item A point process $\theta$ is called \textbf{exponentially-lacunary} on $(0,\infty)$ if there exist constants $c,d \in (0, \infty)$, $c<d$, such that 
    \begin{equation}
         \theta([c \log x, d x ]) \overset{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow} 0
    \end{equation}
    as $x \rightarrow \infty $. 
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.826731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48c3fd1036fd6de420c40886ba5071cea5c7c123dde855ee2c4d4d356c873482","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2608.13235, 'Mixing Properties of Random Laguerre Tessellations'] Let $X$ be a stationary random tessellation. It is called 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)]ergodic, if for all $A,B \in \borel(\mathcal{F}(\FdAp))$,
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{a \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\lambda^d((-a,a)^d)} \int_{(-a,a)^d} \p(X \in A, \mathcal{T}_z(X) \in B) \,\dx z = \p(X \in A) \p(X \in B),
\end{equation*}
    \item[(ii)] mixing, if for any $A, B \in \borel(\mathcal{F}(\FdAp))$,
    \begin{equation*}
    \lim_{\norm{z}\rightarrow \infty} \p(X \in A, \mathcal{T}_z(X) \in B) = \p(X \in A) \p(X \in B),
\end{equation*}
\item[(iii)] $\alpha$-mixing, if for any $a > 0$,
\begin{equation*} 
        \lim_{b \rightarrow \infty} \alpha(\sigma_X(\tailFnula{a}),\sigma_X(\tailFnula{-b})) = 0,
    \end{equation*}
where $\sigma_X(\tailFnula{a})\coloneqq \sigma(\{\{X \in A\}: A \in \tailFnula{a}\})$ and $\sigma_X(\tailFnula{-b})\coloneqq\sigma(\{\{X \in B\}: B \in \tailFnula{-b}\})$. 
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:43.769233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48c6178f7555f944c9ba32ba5c3cc2988fc3ea2c04c4a27492008c52276e042b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2412.04328, 'The critical Karp--Sipser core of Erdős--Rényi random graphs'] For $i \geqslant 2$, let $D_i(n)$ be the number of vertices of degree $i$ in the Karp--Sipser core of $ \mathrm{G}[n, \mathrm{e}/n]$. Then we have the joint convergence in distribution
	\begin{equation}
	\left(\begin{array}{c}n^{-3/5} \cdot D_{2}(n) \\  n^{-2/5} \cdot D_{3}(n) \\ n^{-1/5} \cdot D_4(n) \\ D_5(n) \end{array}\right)  \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{(d)} \left( \begin{array}{c} \frac{2^{9/5}3^{4/5}}{\mathrm{e}^{3/5}} \vartheta^{-2} \\  
	\frac{2^{16/5}3^{1/5}}{\mathrm{e}^{2/5}} \vartheta^{-3} \\ \frac{2^{13/5}3^{3/5}}{\mathrm{e}^{1/5}} \vartheta^{-4} \\ \mathrm{Poi} \left( \frac{48}{5} \vartheta^{-5} \right) \end{array}\right),
	\end{equation}
	where $\vartheta=\inf\{t \geqslant 0 : W_t=t^{-2}\}$ for a standard Brownian motion $W$ started from $0$, and $\mathrm{Poi}(\lambda)$ stands for a Poisson variable with parameter $\lambda$. Moreover, with high probability, we have $D_i(n)=0$ for all $i \geqslant 6$. Finally, conditionally on $\left( D_i(n) \right)_{i \geqslant 2}$, the Karp--Sipser core is a configuration model\footnote{We refer to Section~(sec:model of the paper) for a precise definition of the configuration model.} conditioned to be simple.
	Moreover, the same result is true if we replace $\mathrm{G}[n, \mathrm{e}/n]$ by $ \mathrm{G}(n,m_n)$ where $(m_n)_{ n \geqslant 1}$ is a sequence satisfying $m_n=\frac{\mathrm{e}}{2} n +O \left( \sqrt{n}\right)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.370136+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48c66f5543c356c740ac0171e53d4727884feb96a3beac8b3f4b6db79605ad52","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 50 of arXiv:2512.07202, 'Hitting Probabilities for Hypoelliptic Differential Equations Driven by Fractional Brownia'] For any $m \geqslant 0$ and $p > 1$, one has
