{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.757940+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_49736ca8dc2be1b7ffdb73245154a7887cfbfb9011e802c28956b7a414d4905c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:apriori of arXiv:2411.08018, 'Last passage percolation in hierarchical environments'] The following is true almost surely.
    For all $\ell\in\llbracket1,{M}\rrbracket$, every rectangle $R^{(\ell)}\in\mathscr{R}^{(\ell)}$ enjoys the following two properties:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item
        \emph{(Large area).}
        \[
            \bigl|R^{(\ell)}\bigr| \ge {\lambda}^2\frac{n^2}{2^{2\ell{\zeta}}}.
        \]
        \item
        \emph{(Controlled slope).}
        There exists $R^{(\ell-1)} \in \mathscr{R}^{(\ell-1)}$ such that $R^{(\ell)} \subset R^{(\ell-1)}$ and 
        \begin{equation*}
            \frac{1}{1+2\frac{{\rho}}{{\lambda}^2}}
            \le \frac{\mathsf{Slope}(R^{(\ell)})}{\mathsf{Slope}(R^{(\ell-1)})}
            \le 1+2\frac{{\rho}}{{\lambda}^2}.
        \end{equation*}

        Thus, iterating yields the bounds
        \begin{align}
            \left(1+2\frac{{\rho}}{{\lambda}^2}\right)^{-\ell} \le 
            \mathsf{Slope}\bigl(R^{(\ell)}\bigr)
            \le \left(1+2\frac{{\rho}}{{\lambda}^2}\right)^{\ell}.
        \end{align}
        Note that since $\ell\le {M} \le C \frac{\log n}{\log\log n}$ and $\frac{{\rho}}{{\lambda}^2}=\left(\frac{\log\log n}{\log n}\right)^{1/2}$,
        these last bounds are roughly of the form
        $e^{\pm C\left(\frac{\log n}{\log\log n}\right)^{1/2}}$
        and hence are sub-polynomial in $n$ which will be crucial in our later applications.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:25:35.588848+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_497468ec67b899722c4a22186ebdc9dfb4dae77780635ddb835896fa589e4102","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:BD-chain of arXiv:2603.00705, '$L^2$-cutoff for the averaging process \\ on random regular graphs'] Fix $d\ge 3$.
	Let $R_t$ denote the transition kernel of the  continuous-time biased random walk on $\mathbb{N}_0:=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ with transition rates $p^R(i)$ (resp.\ $q^R(i)$) of jumping from $i$ to $i+1$ (resp.\ $i-1$) given by
	\begin{equation}
		p^R(i):= \begin{dcases}
			d/2 &\text{if}\ i=0\\
