{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.873676+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_499a38c0d3933fd5ff4b64bf4f364cdfed18b393a8a1cd643ee3aab9ecfe53a1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:BKK of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $A\subset \mathbb{Z}^m$ be a finite subset and for all $i=1,\cdots, m$, let $Q^{i}(w):=\sum_{a\in A}c^i_a w^a$ with $c_a^i\in \mathbb{C}$. Then for a generic choice of $c_a^i$, we have 
  \begin{equation}
    \#\mathcal{S}\left\{Q^1,\ldots, Q^m;(\mathbb{C}^*)^m\right\}=m! \vol_m (P)
  \end{equation}
  where $P:=\conv(A)\subset \mathbb{R}^m$ is the convex hull of $A$, also called the \emph{Newton polytope}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.935650+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_499cf7e807f165f6f438d9a98e7a2a07fb72fb49b1f5dafb21fa78bc64afe29c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 30 of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Let $(G_n)_{n\in \N} = ((V_n, E_n))_{n \in \N}$, with $|V_n| = n$, be a sequence of $b$-expanders with maximal degree $\Delta$, and let $(\mu_n)_{n \in \N}$ be a sequence of environment distributions. Then there exists $p^* = p^*(\Delta, b), c_1, c_2, \gamma > 0$ such that for $a_1 = a_1(n)$ and $a_2 = a_2(n) \geq a_1$ satisfying 
        \begin{align*}
            \mu_n(-\infty, a_1) &\leq 1/(\Delta - 1) - p^*/2,\\
            \mu_n(a_2, \infty) &\leq p^*, \\
            a_2 - a_1 &\ll \log n,
        \end{align*}
        for all $n \in \N$, we have for any $\delta > n^{-\gamma}$ that
        \begin{equation}
            \widehat \P \left( \frac{\sqrt{n}}{c_1 \chi^{c_2} } \leq \diam(\cT) \leq c_1 \chi^{c_2} \sqrt{n}  \right) \geq 1-\delta,
        \end{equation}
        where
        \begin{equation*}
            \chi = \chi(a_1,a_2,\delta, n) = \Delta \exp(a_2-a_1) \delta^{-1} \log n .
        \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

