{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.612499+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_49b275c3656740f10e627cfadbaa111adb07a0d28ad69e24bc5c42ce2880795f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2510.27451, 'Bi-martingale optimal transport and its applications'] For any  $\mu,\nu \in\mathcal{P}_2^\ba(\Rd)$ we have the equalities between the three sets,
	\begin{align*}
		\Pi_{\succeq_c \mu} (\nu)   = 	\Pi_{\succeq_c \nu} (\mu)  =  \mu \curlyvee \nu.
	\end{align*}
	In one dimension the projection set always consists of exactly one element being the least upper bound \eqref{eq:lub} for convex order:  $\Pi_{\succeq_c \mu} (\nu) = \{ \mu \vee \nu\}$. 
	In dimension $d>1$, $\Pi_{\succeq_c \mu} (\nu)$ is a non-empty convex set that may contain more than one element. 

	Moreover, for any $\ov\rho \in \mu \curlyvee \nu$ we have,
	\begin{align}

		\min_{\rho \succeq_c \mu} Z_2(\nu,\rho) &= \frac{m_2(\ov\rho) - m_2(\nu)}{2} = \frac{Z_2(\mu,\nu) -\frac{1}{2}\big(m_2(\nu)-m_2(\mu)\big)}{2}, \\

		\min_{\rho \succeq_c \nu} Z_2(\mu,\rho) &= \frac{m_2(\ov\rho) - m_2(\mu)}{2}  = \frac{Z_2(\mu,\nu) -\frac{1}{2}\big(m_2(\mu)-m_2(\nu)\big)}{2}.
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.563820+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_49b56d8a54af1a96c9270008c85dfbd28d9f39f8fc8352693a4f27ebb7c418e0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:cT_exc_dia_C1 of arXiv:2512.21977, 'Repeat times and a two-weight UST model'] Let $\operatorname{Exc}_{\max}$ and $\operatorname{diam}_{\max}$ be the maximum excess and diameter of the connected components in the Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi  random graph $\mathbb{G}(n,1/n)$, respectively. Then, with $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{P}}}$-probability at least $1 - n^{-1}$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
    \operatorname{diam}({\ensuremath{\mathcal T}} ) \leq 2 \max_{u \in V} d_{\ensuremath{\mathcal T}} (u, \mathcal{L})
    + 2 \max_{2 \leq i \leq k}  d_{\ensuremath{\mathcal T}} ({\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} _i, {\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} _1) + 12( \operatorname{Exc}_{\max} + 2) \operatorname{diam}_{\max}.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.856945+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_49b59a5b9bc454eb74e289877002e02463449efd6a88fdb43a2b227239592246","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2511.20519, '\textbf{High-dimensional limits arising from \\ hyperbolic Poisson $k$-plane processes}'] Let $(d_n,k_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a sequence satisfying $k_n < d_n$, $2k_n > d_n+1$ and $d_n \to \infty$ as $n\to\infty$.
Let $(0,0,\nu_{d_n,k_n})$ denote the Lévy triplet of $Z_{d_n,k_n}$ and let $\widetilde Z_{n}$ be a random variable with Lévy triplet $(0,0,\nu_{d_n,k_n}/\sigma^2_{d_n,k_n})$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\emph{(\alph*)}]
    \item If the codimension $d_n-k_n = b \in \N$ is constant, then $\widetilde Z_n$ converges in distribution to an infinitely divisible random variable $\widetilde Z^{(b)}$ with Lévy triplet $(0,0,\widetilde\nu^{(b)})$, where
    \begin{equation*}
        \widetilde\nu^{(b)}(\dint x) = \Gamma({\textstyle\frac{b}{2}})^{-1} x^{-2} \bigl(-\log x\bigr)^{\frac{b-2}{2}}\;\mathbf 1_{(0,1)}(x)\,\dint x.
    \end{equation*}
    In particular, $\widetilde Z^{(b)}$ has finite moments of all orders and $\V(\widetilde Z^{(b)}) = 1$.
    \item If $d_n-k_n \to \infty$, then $\widetilde Z_n$ converges in distribution to a standard Gaussian law.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:55.094171+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_49b9afe144170727f74c95b4c8ff8b913eee7248c83aa0b96b1f26ef42ad6e9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:fik-is-weaker-than-eikm of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] $\textbf{FIK} \subsetneq \textbf{KI}$ i.e.\ 
  (1) $\textbf{KI} \vdash \textsf{wCD}$ and (2) $\textbf{FIK} \nvdash \textsf{CD}$.
  \begin{proof}
    (1) Follows from the derivation below:
    \begin{center}
      \AxiomC{$ $}
      \RightLabel{\scriptsize{$\textsf{CD}$}}
      \UnaryInfC{$\Box(\varphi \lor \psi) \to (\Diamond \varphi \lor \Box \psi)$}

      \AxiomC{$ $}
      \UnaryInfC{$(p \lor q) \to ((p \to q) \to q)$}
      \UnaryInfC{$(\Diamond \varphi \lor \Box \psi) \to ((\Diamond \varphi \to \Box \psi) \to \Box \psi)$}

      \RightLabel{\scriptsize{Syll.}}
      \BinaryInfC{$\Box (\varphi \lor \psi) \to ((\Diamond \varphi \to \Box \psi) \to \Box \psi)$}
      \DisplayProof
    \end{center}
    (2) By soundness, it suffices to construct a $\Diamond$-p model that falsifies $\textsf{CD}$.
    Let $\mathfrak{M}_{\textsf{CD}} = (\set{x, x', y'}, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, where
    $\mathord{\leq} = \set{ (x,x), (x,x'), (x',x'), (y',y') }$,
    $\mathord{\sqsubset} = \set{ (x',y') }$,
    $V(p) = \set{ y' }$, and $V(q) = \emptyset$,
    then $\mathfrak{M}_{\textsf{CD}}$ is clearly a $\Diamond$-p model.
    The model $\mathfrak{M}_{\textsf{CD}}$ is visualized in Figure (fig:cd-countermodel of the paper).
    \begin{figure}[htb]
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}
      \fill (0, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 2) node[left]{$x$};

      \fill (0, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 4) node[left]{$x'$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0, 2) to (0, 4);
      \draw (0, 3) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \fill (2, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 4) node[right]{$y' \Vdash^{\text{h}} p,\,\not \Vdash^{\text{h}} q$};

      \draw [thick, ->, squig, rel] (0, 4) to (2, 4);
      \draw (1, 4) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \end{tikzpicture}
      \caption{The model $\mathfrak{M}_{\textsf{CD}}$.}

    \end{figure}

    Here, we have $x \Vdash^{\text{h}} \Box (p \lor q)$ since $x \leq x' \sqsubset y' \Vdash^{\text{h}} p$ and there is no $y$ such that $x \sqsubset y$.
    Also, we have $x \not \Vdash^{\text{h}} \Box q$ by $x \leq x' \sqsubset y' \not \Vdash^{\text{h}} q$.
    However, we have $x \not \Vdash^{\text{h}} \Diamond p$ since there is no $y$ such that $x \sqsubset y$.
    Therefore, $x \not \Vdash^{\text{h}} \textsf{CD}$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

