{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.759635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_49ff2993ebdaad3627f9d8f4a592ff0c36672f8b7f281eccf645198d3c6ed9fa","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 8 of arXiv:2604.12233, 'An upper bound on the smallest singular value of dense random combinatorial matrices'] For a vector $v\in \R^n$, and parameters $\gamma\in (0,1)$, $\alpha>0$, the combinatorial Least Common Denominator (CLCD) is defined as
    \begin{equation*}
        \mbox{CLCD}_{\alpha,\gamma}(v)=\mbox{LCD}_{\alpha, \gamma}(D(v))=\inf_{\theta>0}
        \left\{\operatorname{dist}(\theta D(v), 
        \Z^k)<\min\left\{\gamma\|\theta D(v)\|_2, \alpha\right\}\right\},
    \end{equation*}
    where $k={\binom{n}{2}}$,  $D(v)$ is the vector in $\R^k$ with coordinates $v_i-v_j$ for $i<j$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.989436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4a0022dc509ddea88ef4e5017a5657d40a247f2b3ec4ec5894d189201725fe0d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (\cite{DiC}) of arXiv:2604.00882, 'On some extensions of generalized counting processes'] The solution $\tilde p_j^\nu(t)$ of the Cauchy problem \eqref{Dic1}--\eqref{Dic2}, for $j \in \mathbb{N}_0, \nu \in (0,1]$ and $t \ge 0$, is given by
