{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.064038+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4abb590473308e156dc1172289bbed9f8b1f289624349763c729b59b128ebf0f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p.reg_Phi of arXiv:2411.14105, 'Simultaneous replica-symmetry breaking \\ for vector spin glasses'] Let $\Phi = \Phi_{\mu,L,\alpha}$ be any Parisi PDE solution.
The following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  For every $\mathbf{i}\in\mathscr{I}$, the real-valued function $\partial^\mathbf{i}_x\Phi$ exists and is continuous on $[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^{D}$.
    \item  For every $\mathbf{i}\in\mathscr{I}$ (including $\mathbf{i}=\emptyset$), there is a constant $C_\mathbf{i}>0$ such that for any two Parisi PDE solutions $\Phi_{\mu,L,\alpha}$ and $\Phi_{\mu,L',\alpha'}$, it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{(t,x)\in [0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^{D}}\left|\partial_x^\mathbf{i}\Phi_{\mu,L,\alpha}(t,x) - \partial_x^\mathbf{i}\Phi_{\mu,L',\alpha'}(t,x)\right|\leqslant C_\mathbf{i}\left(3 \left\|L-L'\right\|_{L^\infty} + \left\|L\right\|_\mathrm{Lip}\left\|\alpha-\alpha'\right\|_{L^1} \right).
    \end{align*}
    \item  For every $\mathbf{i}\in\mathscr{I}$ with $|\mathbf{i}|\geqslant 1$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{(L,\alpha)}\sup_{[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^{D}}\left|\partial^\mathbf{i}_x\Phi\right|<\infty. \end{align*}
    In particular, we have $|\nabla \Phi|\leqslant \sup_{\sigma \in\supp\mu}|\sigma|$ everywhere on $[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^D$.
    \item  
    For every $\mathbf{i}\in\mathscr{I}$, $\partial_t \partial^\mathbf{i}_x\Phi$ exists and is bounded almost everywhere on $[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^{D}$ and thus $\Phi$ satisfies the Parisi PDE~(equation e.Parisi_PDE of the paper) almost everywhere. 

    In particular, letting $\mathsf{disc}_\alpha$ be the set of discontinuity points of $\alpha$, if $L$ is continuously differentiable, then $\partial_t\partial^\mathbf{i}_x\Phi$ exists and is continuous on $\left([0,T]\setminus \mathsf{disc}_\alpha\right)\times \mathbb{R}^{D}$ for every $\mathbf{i}\in\mathscr{I}$ and $\Phi$ satisfies~(equation e.Parisi_PDE of the paper) everywhere on $\left([0,T]\setminus \mathsf{disc}_\alpha\right)\times \mathbb{R}^{D}$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

