for all $N\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $N>qq_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.680805+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ac5018323cdc83ec86e59e97360f7e60254255bdcec705b997c6446685d2f61","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary esp:small:light:tails:del=0 of arXiv:2412.09235, 'A semiconcavity approach to stability of entropic plans and exponential convergence of Sin'] Assume that $c$ satisfies (equation eq:ass:hessia:bounded of the paper) and that $\rho$ satisfies (equation eq:ass:light:tails of the paper). Then Sinkhorn's algorithm converges exponentially fast. Moreover, if $H(c)=h(c)$ then, for 
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon\leq \sqrt{2\tauH(c)^2\,(1+(L+2)\,[R^2\vee C^{-\nicefrac2\delta}])}
\end{equation}
the rate is
\begin{equation*}
1-\frac{\varepsilon^2}{\varepsilon^2+2\tauH(c)^2(1+(L+2)^2\,[R^2\vee C^{-\nicefrac2\delta}])}\,.
\end{equation*}
Otherwise, if $H(c)>h(c)$ (and set $\nicefrac{1}{0}=+\infty$) then for
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon\leq 1\wedge\frac{\delta_H}{(R^{\delta} C-1)_+}\wedge\biggl(\tau\delta_H+2\tauH(c)^2\,(1+(L+2)^2\,C^{-\nicefrac2\delta}(1+2\delta_H)^{\nicefrac2\delta}\biggr)^{\nicefrac{\delta}{2+2\delta}}
\end{equation}
we have rate of convergence 
\begin{equation*}
1-\frac{\varepsilon^{2+\nicefrac2\delta}}{\varepsilon^2+\tau\varepsilon\delta_H+2\tauH(c)^2\,(1+(L+2)^2\,C^{-\nicefrac2\delta}(\varepsilon+2\delta_H)^{\nicefrac2\delta})}\,.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.190209+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ac64d6f1d2957307a3371d680fcd9de0948738c6bda1610a8977c5d05ab14ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 39 of arXiv:2510.20492, 'Heterochromatic two-arm probabilities for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] For any $d\ge 7$, there exist $\Cl,\Cl,\cl>0$ such that for any $M\ge 1$,  $n\ge \Cref{const_highd_volume_two_arm1}$, $N\ge \Cref{const_highd_volume_two_arm2}n$ and $\psi\in \Psi(n,N)$, 
