{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.829615+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4aed8c41bac23255f6437d7d608a4fe454989768c94f03bf6a94e2741faf86db","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hawkes-identifiability of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] Assume $\lambda_0=\ell=\mu$ and $\alpha>0$. Let
\begin{equation}
F(T)=\mathbb{E}[N_T].
\end{equation}
Then the Hawkes parameters are recovered from $F$ near $T=0$ by
\begin{equation}
\mu=F'(0),
\qquad
\alpha=\frac{F''(0)}{F'(0)},
\qquad
\beta=\frac{F''(0)}{F'(0)}-\frac{F'''(0)}{F''(0)}.

\end{equation}
Thus the first expected-signature coordinate $T\mapsto\inner{\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Sig}^M(X)_{0,T}]}{1}$ locally identifies $(\mu,\alpha,\beta)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:35:59.919432+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4af2bc0361f44a12c392b65bb0050da234bc53ec5782431255154b5d0f506051","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2608.26084, 'Localic Esakia Duality\\via Conic Frames'] A frame~$L$ is called:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \emph{algebraic} if~$K(L)$ forms a join-basis for~$L$;
		\item \emph{coherent} if it is algebraic and~$K(L)$ is a bounded sublattice~of~$L$;
		\item \emph{compact} if~$\top\in L$ is a compact element;
		\item \emph{zero-dimensional} if its complemented elements form a join-basis;
		\item \emph{Stone} if it is compact and zero-dimensional.
	\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.105839+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4af3fced2dd581d6d00d7cb42d2eccbf9bb50bc90e8bc9d4b9d1e2c5ced368e9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:uniform-integrability-fibered-measures of arXiv:2606.21556, 'Mean field limit of non-exchangeable interacting diffusions on co-evolutionary networks'] The space \((\widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_{p,q,\nu}(\mathcal{X}\times [0,1]),\delta_{W_p,q})\) is Polish. Moreover we have:
	\begin{enumerate} [label=(\roman*)]
		\item A subset \(\mathcal{K}\subset (\widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_{p,q,\nu}(\mathcal{X}\times [0,1]),\delta_{W_p,q})\) is relatively compact if and only if it is tight and  
		\begin{equation} 
		 	\lim_{R\to \infty} \sup_{\mu \in \mathcal{K}} \int_{0}^{1} \left( \int_{\mathcal{X}\setminus B_R(x_0)} d(x,x_0)^p d\mu^{\xi}(x)\right)^{q/p} d\xi = 0.
		\end{equation}
		\item The space \((\widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_{p,q,\nu}(\mathcal{X}\times [0,1]),\delta_{W_p,q})\) is compact if and only if \(\mathcal{X}\) is compact.
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.068235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4afa9434eab5275504c03ab80892159a4bf56b4bf7693b633ee7eda5736427b4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2607.13401, 'Total variation cutoff for Kac's walk on the sphere'] For every \(\varepsilon>0\), there are constants
\(n_0=n_0(\varepsilon)\) and \(C_\varepsilon<\infty\) such that, for all
\(n\ge n_0\) and all \(t\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
        \mb E W_t^2
        \le
        C_\varepsilon
        \exp\left\{-\left(\frac{29}{32}-\varepsilon\right)\frac{t}{n}\right\}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every \(2<p<4\) and every \(\varepsilon>0\), with
\begin{equation}
        \sigma_p
        :=
        \left(2-\frac p2\right)\frac12
        +
        \left(\frac p2-1\right)\frac{29}{32}
        =
        \frac{6+13p}{64},
\end{equation}
there are constants \(n_0=n_0(p,\varepsilon)\) and
\(C_{p,\varepsilon}<\infty\) such that, for all \(n\ge n_0\) and all
\(t\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
        \mb E W_t^{p/2}
        \le
        C_{p,\varepsilon}
        \exp\left\{-\left(\sigma_p-\varepsilon\right)\frac{t}{n}\right\}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.663403+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4afb12b2b942157e482860f197f030c2c4113f60918e37b507b598b2e112d18b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 (\bf Energy Estimate) of arXiv:2608.29984, 'On Cauchy Problems for Parabolic Equations with Rough Coefficients'] Let $u^{\epsilon}$ be the classical solution of \eqref{approxnondiv}, then we have
