{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.811158+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4b3582d3094f296b49be09e42d8cf2f7a289364370f8189ea6ede0dc35c58d6a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn d:narrow-wedge of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] The \emph{CDRP free energy profile} is the random continuous function on $\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}\times \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ given by
    \begin{align}
        \mathfrak{H}^\xi_\beta(s,x;t,y) \coloneqq  
        \frac{2^{1/3}}{\beta^{4/3}}
        \left(
            \mathcal{H}_\beta^\xi(s, x2^{1/3}\beta^{2/3}; t, y2^{1/3}\beta^{2/3}) 
            - 2\log\beta 
            + \frac{(t-s)\beta^{4}}{24}
        \right),
    \end{align}
    where $\mathcal{H}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y) \coloneqq  \log \mathcal{Z}_\beta^\xi(s,x;t,y)$ as in (equation e:KPZ-cole-hopf of the paper).

    In other works such as [W23], $\mathfrak{H}^\xi_\beta$ is referred to as the \emph{scaled narrow wedge solution to the KPZ equation}. 
    Since our focus is on the CDRP, we use the above terminology instead.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.747673+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4b36ee96fc60d2f710c11650028f0ea3f4574f2f70203e35d1fc1f7cf2ec9112","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Satz 3 of arXiv:2511.20322, 'Dissertation'] Let $\be \in (0,1)$, $c > 0$, $L, \la > 0$ and $\cR : \R^d\to \R \in \dC 2$ be $\la$-strongly convex and $L$-smooth, such that $\nabla^2 \cR$ is Hölder continuous.
Let $Y$ be the solution to the Young differential equation
\begin{equation}
dY_t = - \frac{1}{(1+ct)^\be} \nabla \cR(Y_t)\,dt + \frac{1}{(1+ct)^\be} \si\,d\hat W_t,
\end{equation}
driven by an epoched Brownian motion $\hat W$ with period $T$. Suppose further there exists a number $J\in \N$, such that $\cI := \set{(\hat W_{(j+t)T}-\hat W_{jT})_{t\in [0,1]} : j\in \N}|$ satisfies $|\cI| = J$, almost surely. Then, for all $\al \in(0,1/2)$,
\[\left|Y_t - (\nabla \cR)^{-1}(T^{-1}\si \hat W_T)\right| \leq C_\al T^{1/2-\be} |\si| \left(\frac{1}{1-2^{-\al}} \frac{L}{\la} + 1\right)\frac{1}{t^{\be}} + o\left(C_\al t^{-\be}\right),  t\to \infty, \quad a.s.\]
where $C_\al = \max_{w\in \cI} \nrm{w}{\al}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.100386+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4b37c20decdc835cbe6cd147263c170897a52f392ce94f4a5c4736760b28691e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 10 (CLT for Centrosymmetric Matrices with Exploding moments) of arXiv:2604.26499, 'Spectrum of Random Matrices with Exploding Moments'] Let $M = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}} X$ be a $2N\times 2N$ random centrosymmetric matrix satisfying 
Conditions \ref{Condtion: Centro_exploding}. For any partition $\pi\in\bigcup_{k}\mathcal{P}(k)$, let $Z_N(T_\pi)$ be defined as in \eqref{eq: Z_N T pi}.
Then, as $N \to \infty$, the collection $\big(Z_N(T_\pi)\big)_{\pi\in\bigcup_{k}\mathcal{P}(k)}$ converges in
distribution to a centered Gaussian process $\big(z(T_\pi)\big)_{\pi\in\bigcup_{k}\mathcal{P}(k)}$. Moreover, for any two partitions $\pi_1,\pi_2$, the covariance of the limiting Gaussian process is given by
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Cov}\left(z(T_{\pi_1}),z(T_{\pi_2})\right)
&=\sum_{r_1,r_2 \in \{1,2\}}\sum_{T \in\mathcal{P}^{\#}(T_{\pi_1}^{(r_1)},T_{\pi_2}^{(r_2)})} \tau^c[T ]+ o(1),
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
\tau^c[T]
=
\begin{cases}
   \displaystyle\prod_{\substack{k,l\ge0\\ k+l\ge2}} \widetilde C_{k,l}^{\,q_{k,l}}, &\text{if $T$ is a colored fat tree},\\[0.8em]
    0, & \text{otherwise.}
   \end{cases}
\end{align*}
Here $q_{k,l}$ denotes the number of vertex pairs $\{u,v\}$ such that there are $k$ directed edges from $u$ to $v$ with weights from matrix $M^{(1)}$ (blue) and $l$ directed edges from $u$ to $v$  with weights from matrix $M^{(2)}$ (red) and there is no edge from $v$ to $u.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.437235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4b38860696e1cba5e42d83457f1636af40344c128165f7d3893b2a1454d79730","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def: limitlog of arXiv:2509.26254, '$K$-Branching Random Walk with Noisy Selection: \\ \Large Large Population Limits and Phas'] A sequence of (random) point measures $(\mu_N)_{N \in \mathbb{N}}$ is said to have a {\it limiting log-profile} if there exists a deterministic function $g \in \mathcal{D}$ such that 
