{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.611242+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4b6dbe4f6feddab29058477896d506e6622562b9d8c51336213d2d85656f4a87","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 (Hoskins and Steinerberger \cite{Hoskins-Steinerberger2022}) of arXiv:2506.08910, 'Critical points of random polynomials and finite free cumulants'] Let $p_N$ be as in \eqref{eq:random polynomial definition} and assume the measure $\mu$ is supported on the real line, has mean $0$, variance $1$, and moments of all orders. If \begin{equation}
	\tilde{p}_{k,N}(x)=\mathcal{D}_{\sqrt{N}}\partial_{k|N}p_{N}\left(x+\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}X_{j}\right),
\end{equation} then, for any fixed $k\in\N$\begin{equation}
	\lim\limits_{N\rightarrow\infty}\tilde{p}_{k,N}\left(x\right)=\He_{k}(x),
\end{equation} pointwise, almost surely, where $\He_{k}$ is the degree $k$ Hermite polynomial\begin{equation}
