{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.041985+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4c3376429d062be3ad57586cc19276fc04357384166b25060de65e14f05cd22f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 (Pinching estimate) of arXiv:2608.26598, 'Pinching cones for positive isotropic curvature in dimensions seven and eight'] Let $n=7,8$ and let $C(b)$, $\tilde C(b)$, $b_{max,2}$ and $\tilde b_{max,2}$ be as in
        Definitions \ref{def:pinchingCones1} and \ref{def:pinchingCones2}. Put
        $B_n:=b_{max,2}+\tilde b_{max,2}$ and define
        \begin{equation}
            \hat C(\beta):=
            \begin{cases}
                C(\beta),& 0<\beta\le b_{max,2},\\
                \tilde C(B_n-\beta),& b_{max,2}<\beta<B_n.
            \end{cases}
        \end{equation}
        Then $\{\hat C(\beta)\}_{0<\beta<B_n}$ is a continuous family of closed convex
        $O(n)$-invariant cones, each transversally invariant under $\dt R=Q(R)$, with the two
        properties
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item \emph{(Entry.)} For every $\theta>0$ and $N>0$ there exists $\beta_0>0$ such that
            \begin{equation*}
            \begin{split}
                \{R:R-\theta\Scal(R)\,\mathrm{id}\wedge\mathrm{id}\in\CPIC\}&\cap
                \{R:\Ric(R)_{11}+\Ric(R)_{22}-\theta\Scal(R)+N\ge0\}\\
                &\subset\{R:R+N\,\mathrm{id}\wedge\mathrm{id}\in\hat C(\beta_0)\}.
            \end{split}
            \end{equation*}
            \item \emph{(Pinching.)} If $\beta_j\nearrow B_n$, there are $\varepsilon_j\searrow0$ with
            \begin{equation*}
                \hat C(\beta_j)\subset
                \{R:R+\varepsilon_j\Scal(R)\,\mathrm{id}\wedge\mathrm{id}\in\CPICone\}.
            \end{equation*}
        \end{enumerate}
        Consequently, for every compact $K\subset\operatorname{int}\CPIC$ and every $T>0$ there exist
        $\theta>0$, $N>0$, an increasing concave function $f>0$ with $\lim_{s\to\infty}f(s)/s=0$, and a
        continuous family $\{F_t\}_{t\in[0,T]}$ of closed convex $O(n)$-invariant sets, invariant under
        $\dt R=Q(R)$, with $K\subset F_0$ and
        \begin{equation}
        \begin{split}
            F_t\subset{}&\{R:R-\theta\Scal(R)\,\mathrm{id}\wedge\mathrm{id}\in\CPIC\}\cap
            \{R:\Ric(R)_{11}+\Ric(R)_{22}-\theta\Scal(R)+N\ge0\}\\
            &\cap\{R:R+f(\Scal(R))\,\mathrm{id}\wedge\mathrm{id}\in\CPICtwo\}
        \end{split}
        \end{equation}
        for all $t\in[0,T]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.385654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4c399a15254d4af18f6bb2a09d3adbe7167cf2aaf577672f6a396eaa8611db9d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_apply_conc of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] There exists~$C_{\mathrm{dev}} \ge 1$ such that the following holds. Let~$x \in \R^d$ with~$\|x\| \ge 1$ and~$t \in [1,\|x\|]$. Then, 
