{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.076653+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ca209f546c51f1315f90278b9fe40b47f4f0d622d135d4677dd7a2e359a371d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 2 of arXiv:2606.02570, 'Stochastic completeness for landmark space'] Suppose that the radial scalar function $k\colon [0,\infty)\to\R$ is smooth on $(0,\infty)$ and that its even extension to $\R$ is twice continuously differentiable at zero. In addition, assume that $k$ possesses a positive continuous Fourier transform $\widehat{k}\colon\R^d\to\R$ and that there exist $p\geq 1$ as well as a constant $b>0$ such that, for all sufficiently high frequencies $\xi\in\R^d$,
