{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:27:56.792746+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4cd057a25cab3f8ca9d24869ab65c5fcc2d2f05f56fad0eadd05bd1f63df6dea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Intersection of Moment Level Curves) of arXiv:2609.00209, 'Exit Times for Brownian Motion and Location Detection'] Let $D$ denote any of the non-rectangular domains from Table \ref{tab:domains} and suppose the level curves for the first two exit time moments $\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_D]=c_1$ and $\mathbb{E}^{(x,y)}[\tau_D^2]=c_2$ intersect for some positive constants $c_1$ and $c_2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] If $D=\mathcal E$, then the level curves intersect at either four points, two points, or one point determined by $c_1$ and $c_2$.
\item[2.] If $D=\mathcal P, \mathcal H, \mathcal W$, then the level curves intersect at either two points or a single point determined by $c_1$ and $c_2$.
\item[3.] If $D=\mathcal T$, then the level curves intersect at either six points, three points, or a single point determined by $c_1$ and $c_2$.
\end{itemize}

