{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.770833+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d18e0abfad736d3e9e0d1313bdaa53c57405038e52fe552d8540912f2908fe5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary K_est of arXiv:2411.08220, 'Nonlocal operators with Neumann conditions'] For $\alpha\in (0,2)$, $t>0$, $x>0$, and $y<0$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{K}(t,x,y) := \int_0^t {\rm d}s \int_D {\rm d}z \, p_s^D(x,z)\nu(z,y) e^{-\nu(y,D)(t-s)} \approx \Big(1\wedge \frac{|x|^{\alpha/2}}{\sqrt{t}}\Big)\Big(1\wedge \frac{|y|^{\alpha/2}}{\sqrt{t}}\Big)p_t(x,y).
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.762321+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d1a7414bde8fbed78614d22255661253561e475ef81031cc43360640f2e741e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Galerkin of arXiv:2411.02459, 'Existence and higher regularity of statistically steady states for the stochastic Coleman-'] For all $U_0=(u_0,\eta_0)\in\mathcal{H}^0_\mu$ and $t\ge 0$, there exists a unique strong solution pair $(V_N,\eta_N)\in C([0,t];H\timesM^0_\mu)$. Furthermore, there exists a positive constant $c(U_0,t)$ independent of $N$ such that the following holds
\begin{align*}
&\mathbb{E}\sup_{0\le r\le t}\|(V_N(r),\eta_N(r))\|^2_{\mathcal{H}^0_\mu}+\int_0^t\mathbb{E}\big(\|V_N(r)\|^2_{H^1}+\|\eta_N(r)\|^2_{M^0_\mu} +\|V_N(r)\|^{p+1}_{L^{p+1}}+\|\varphi(V_N(r)+\xi(r))\|^q_{L^{q}}\Big)\emph{d} r\\
&\le c(U_0,t),
\end{align*}
where $q=(p_0+1)/p_0$ and $p_0$ is as in \nameref{cond:phi:2}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.568829+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d1bd4a2097094a25c29092fbac53411e363c9fe90df7db5b633cd3e0f653b86","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 46 of arXiv:2609.01460, 'On cost-induced Santal\'o-type inequalities \\ in Polish measure spaces'] Let $A\subset \mathbb{S}^n$ be a measurable set with positive measure. Then,
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{n}(A^s) \leq \sigma_{n}(C_A^s).

\end{equation}
If $0<\sigma_n(A) < \frac{1}{2}$, there is equality in \eqref{eq:sph_BS} if and only if $A$ is a spherical cap up to removal of null sets. If $\sigma_n(A)\geq \frac{1}{2}$, there is always equality, namely, both sides of \eqref{eq:sph_BS} are zero. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.480785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d1d53a3e2e1024e7fd2f3a245f0485b115d3f06be66986d7de8400a16964c18","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Radial Loewner chain of arXiv:2505.14762, 'Multiple radial SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Sutherland system'] Let $g_t$ satisfies the radial Loewner equation

\begin{equation}
\partial_t g_t(z)=g_t(z) \frac{\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \theta_t}+g_t(z)}{\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \theta_t}-g_t(z)}, \quad g_0(z)=z,
\end{equation}

where $t \mapsto \theta_t$ is real continuous and called the driving function. 
Let $K_t$ be the set of points $z$ in $\mathbb{D}$ such that the solution $g_s(z)$ blows up before or at time $t$.  $K_t$ is called the radial SLE hull driven by $\theta_t$.

Radial Loewner chain in arbitrary simply connected domain $\Omega \subsetneq \mathbb{C}$ with a marked interior point $u \in D$, is defined via a conformal map from $\mathbb{D}$ onto $\Omega$ sending 0 to $u$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.196763+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d228cc885d60bafc855028a80f16d6525a529fb7bfa3a89082afb6e068c2755","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 27 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{General Range.}

We call \textit{range} a convex compact subset $\mathrm{R}$ of $\R^ 2$ that is symmetric ($x\in R \leftrightarrow (-x)\in \R$). For $x_1, x_2$ two points of $\Half$, we say that $x_2$ of  is in range from $x_1$ if
\[x_2 \in \mathrm{R}+x_1.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.886587+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d243e624fc04346fcf90c8240b981cecfd7299ebe296d61b8c48f08a2067d1f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 28 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] For a linear order $\alpha$ and a transitive set $u$, we define a set $\bfL(u)_\alpha$ and the support function $\supp^{\bfL(u)}_\alpha\colon \bfL(u)_\alpha\to [\alpha]^{<\omega}$ as follows:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Each $v\in u$ is a term of $\bfL(u)_\alpha$ and $\supp^{\bfL(u)}_\alpha(v) = \varnothing$.
        \item For each $\xi\in\alpha$, we have a term $\ttL(u)_\xi$ with $\supp^{\bfL(u)}_\alpha(\ttL(u)_\xi) = \{\xi\}$.
        \item Let $\phi(x,y_0,\cdots,y_{n-1})$ be a $\Delta_0$-formula with its all free variables displayed.
        Now for $\xi\in\alpha$ and $a_0,\cdots,a_{n-1}\in \bfL(u)_\alpha$ with $\supp^{\bfL(u)}_\alpha(a_i) < \xi$, we form a term $\{x\in \ttL(u)_\xi \mid\phi(x,a_0,\cdots,a_{n-1})\}$ with
        \begin{equation*} \textstyle 
            \supp^{\bfL(u)}_\alpha\bigl(\{x\in \ttL(u)_\xi \mid\phi(x,a_0,\cdots,a_{n-1})\}\bigr) = \{\xi\}\cup \bigcup_{i<n} \supp^{\bfL(u)}_\alpha(a_i).
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.591159+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d253a5e00419dd641140a0ec1d382246db482b6d2b33452098acac2c1743775","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:productSpace of arXiv:2606.03079, 'Mean Field Limits for Stochastic, Underdamped Reactive Langevin Dynamics Models'] For reaction $\mathcal{R}_{\ell}$ with $\tilde{L}+1 \leq \ell \leq L$, i.e., having at least one product particle, define the product position and velocity product space analogously to $\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V}'^{(\ell)}$,
$$
\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V'}^{(\ell)}\in (\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d)^{|\beta^{(\ell)}|},
$$
where we write
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V}'^{(\ell)} & \supseteq (\bm{y},\bm{v'}) =\bigl((y_{1}^{(1)},{v'}_{1}^{(1)}), \cdots, (y_{\beta_{\ell 1}}^{(1)},{v'}_{\beta_{\ell 1}}^{(1)}), \cdots, (y_{1}^{(J)},{v'}_{1}^{(J)}), \cdots, (y_{\beta_{\ell J}}^{(J)},{v'}_{\beta_{\ell J}}^{(J)})\bigr).
\end{align*}

 For $(\bm{y},\bm{v'}) \in \mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V'}^{(\ell)}$ a sampled product position and velocity configuration for one individual $\mathcal{R}_{\ell}$ reaction, $y_{r}^{(j)}$ then labels the sampled position for the $r$th product particle of species $j$ involved in the reaction, and ${v'}_{r}^{(j)}$ labels the sampled velocity for the same product particle. Let $d \bm{y} d\bm{v'}=\bigl(\bigwedge_{j=1}^{J}(\bigwedge_{r=1}^{\beta_{\ell_{j}}} d y_{r}^{(j)}d {v'}_{r}^{(j)})\bigr)$ be the corresponding volume form on $\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V}'^{(\ell)}$, which also naturally defines an associated Lebesgue measure.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.499258+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d25d14b8a7da6fb0dfd99eb0cf460bdf8689e9031c156595ab122d7af33497d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] We define the two linear operators $\Sym, \An \in L(V \otimes V)$ as
\begin{align*}
\Sym(x) := \frac{x + x^*}{2} \quad \text{and} \quad \An(x) := \frac{x - x^*}{2}
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:48:00.288494+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d25e649c542e952e725b13c4a55d4827f5ca3aaeff9a2e33933ae5ad343db4f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm proteus of arXiv:2609.03033, 'An Overview of Einstein $4$-Manifolds'] Let $(M,J)$ be a compact complex surface of general  type, and let $(X, \check{J})$ be its minimal model. 
 Then any Riemannian metric $g$ on $M$ satisfies the following  curvature estimates: 
\begin{eqnarray}
\int_M s^2d\mu &\geq& 32\pi^2 c_1^2 (X) \\
\int_M (s-\sqrt{6}|W_+|)^2d\mu &\geq& 72\pi^2  c_1^2 (X) 
\end{eqnarray}
where $s$ and $W_+$ respectively denote the scalar and Weyl curvatures of $g$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.887050+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d2c0e69e5cf830572a02437486b350e51249e4e48bdb3896fdea7da0bf1b652","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2607.02410, 'Polynomial mixing for polygonal side matchings'] The mixing time of the fixed genus chord swap chain satisfies 
