{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.743749+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d30cf3fb300e29a207b17df4d3093950037abc5557df0df15bd3fc5706614ac","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2506.10511, '2506.10511'] For $n\in \mathds{N}$, let $\mathcal{A}_n$ be a collection of subsets of $[1,n]_{\mathds{Z}}$. 
We say $\mathcal{A}_n$ is a \textit{Sperner family} 
if for each $A\in \mathcal{A}_n$ either of the following holds:
\begin{itemize}

\item[(1)] There exists $B_A\subset [1,n]_{\mathds{Z}} \backslash A$ with $|B_A|\geq \frac{1}{2} n$ such that for all $A' \supset A$ and $A'\cap B_A\neq \emptyset$, we have $A'\notin \mathcal{A}_n$;
\medskip

\item[(2)] There exists $B'_A\subset A$ with $|B'_A|\geq \frac{1}{2} n$ such that for all $A' \subset A$ and $ B'_A\backslash A'\neq \emptyset$, we have $A'\notin \mathcal{A}_n$.

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:06:55.278130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d312a7d9464810ef86e7ef66cc79bc775ca538f2e8b9da6e97b5e7f6d3cd4b5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary co:U of arXiv:2601.08184, 'Wasserstein-p Central Limit Theorem Rates: From Local Dependence to Markov Chains'] Given i.i.d.\ $\{Z_i\}_{i=0}^{n-1}$ as in Section (sec:U of the paper) such that $\mathbb{E}[\|h(Z_0, \cdots, Z_{r-1})\|^3]<\infty$. We have
\begin{align*}
&\mathcal{W}_1\Big(\mathcal{L}\big(\tfrac{\sqrt{n}U_n}{r}\big), \mathcal N\big(0, \operatorname{Var}\big(\mathbb{E}[h(Z_0,\ldots,Z_{r-1}) \mid Z_0]\big)\big)\Big) \in \mathcal O (n^{-1/2}).
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.256637+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d3146965e73ecfc2c829e6b6013594011e2aa7bfbe6ceecc7be4285bef49aeb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 52 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] For any $x,y\in F,\;x_n,y_n\in F_n,\;f\in \BL^{\kappa}(M)$ and $i\in\N$, we have that
\begin{align*}
  |G^{\circ i}_xf(y)-(G^n_{x_n})^{\circ i}f(y_n)|
  \leq &\g{c}{itkill}\left( 1+D_n^i +D_n^{i-1}\sum_{j=0}^{i-2}D_n^j\right)\\
  &\qquad\qquad\qquad\times \Bigl\{\|f\|_{K_n}(d(x,x_n)+d(y,y_n))+\|f\|_{\BL^{\kappa}(K_n)}d_{\BL^{\kappa}}(\mu,\mu_n)\Bigr\}, 
\end{align*}
where $\g{c}{itkill}=\diam K_n\mu_n(F_n)^2+(1+\diam K_n)^2\mu_n(F_n)+\diam F+1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:21:06.858199+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d34bcfcd85963c4c5a8a960c25011ac845d5cc383d9868ad32e1965f628149e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2608.21063, '\textbf{Non-bijective scaling limits and phase transitions of planar maps}'] Suppose that $\nu=1$ and that condition~\eqref{eq:bwregvar} holds for some $1<\alpha<2$.  Let $\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}>\alpha-1$.  Suppose that for some integer $p$ with
	\begin{align*}
		p >	\max\left(2, \frac{4\alpha}{2\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}+1-\alpha} \right)
	\end{align*}
	there is a constant $C>0$ such that
	\begin{align*}
		\sum_{k=1}^{m}\Ex{\mD_k^p} \le 	Cm^{1+p\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}}
	\end{align*}
	for all $m\ge1$.  Then, for every $\epsilon>0$,
	\begin{align*}
		\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
		\sup_{\substack{N\ge1\\\Pr{|\mT|=N}>0}}
		\Prb{\max_{v\in\mT_N} \sum_{u\in[o,v]_{\mT_N}} \mD_u \one_{\{d_{\mT_N}^+(u)\le\delta b_N\}} > \epsilon b_N^{\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}} } = 0.
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.585604+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d34cb69b3d3f1c29229e56f1beb4fb7959f3e3cd993f1ff93a6db117d97ef7e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem direc of arXiv:2602.12713, 'GIG random matrices \\ and \\ a Yang-Baxter extension of the Matsumoto-Yor property'] [LetWes2024]
		Let $X$ and $Y$ be independent $\Omega_+$-valued random matrices with distributions $\mathrm{MGIG}(\lambda,\alpha a,b)$ and $\mathrm{MGIG}(\lambda,\beta b,a)$, respectively. Let $\phi^{(\alpha,\beta)}:\Omega_+^2\to\Omega_+^2$ be defined by 
		\begin{equation}
			\phi^{(\alpha,\beta)}(x,y)=\left(y(I+\alpha xy)^{-1}(I+\beta xy),\,x(I+\beta yx)^{-1}(I+\alpha yx)\right)
		\end{equation}
		and let
		$$
		\left( U,\, V\right)=\phi^{(\alpha,\beta)}(X,Y)
.		$$

\vspace{1mm}
		Then $U$ and $ V$  are independent, $ U\sim\mathrm{MGIG}(\lambda,\alpha b,a)$ and $ V=\mathrm{MGIG}(\lambda,\beta a,b)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.255181+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d35ecfcdb1c4fed9607256d7a676f3f1860cbe1c4e5ac5ac81c35493cd882b6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn 31 (Resistance metric, {\cite[Definition 2.3.2) of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] {Kig01}}]
Let $F$ be a set. A function $R:F\times F\to[0,\infty)$ is called a resistance metric if and only if, 
for any non-empty finite set $V\subseteq F$, 
there exists an electrical network
$G$ with the vertex set $V$ such that the effective resistance on $G$ coincides with $R|_{V\times V}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:29:27.983560+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d36201f9bb76930a67f1f2686cc2430c437ea29e52c666b87012ade5b52aea1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Uniform logarithmic Sobolev inequality) of arXiv:2608.24863, 'Uniform Logarithmic Sobolev Inequalities for the 2D Coulomb Gas at the Diffusive Temperatu'] For every fixed $\beta>0$, there exists $\rho_*(\beta)>0$ such that, for all $N\ge2$ and every real-valued $f\in\mathsf D_{N,\beta}$,
\begin{equation}
 \Ent_{\mathbb P_{N,\beta}}(f^2)
 \le \frac2{\rho_*(\beta)}\cE_{N,\beta}(f).
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every compact interval $I\Subset(0,\infty)$,
\begin{equation}
 \inf_{\beta\in I}\inf_{N\ge2}\rho_{N,\beta}>0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.048627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d3c67fd63c5e33dee21b79d560963c45553cbb33c89cd4510fbdba410fc64f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2510.25900, 'Dixie cup problem in an interlacing process'] Let the sequence $\alpha = \{a_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty }$ is formed as the \textit{interlacing} of two subsequences $\beta = \{b_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty }$ and $\delta = \{d_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty }$, as given in relation (\ref{sb1}).  Then,  as $N=2M \rightarrow \infty$ we have
\begin{equation}
E\left[T_{m}\left(N;\alpha\right)\right]\sim \left(D_M +B_M\right)\int_{0}^{\infty}\left[1-\prod_{j=1}^M
 \bigg(1-S_m(d_{j}u)e^{-d_{j}u}\bigg)\right]du 
\end{equation}
where, 
\begin{align*}
B_M:=\sum_{j=1}^M b_j\,\,\,\,\,\text{and}\,\,\,\,D_M:=\sum_{j=1}^M d_j,
\end{align*}
provided that the coupons from the sequence $\delta$ are far rarer than the ones from the sequence $\beta$ (see, (\ref{deca})--(\ref{cond3})). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.497286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d464c594f8ae6789d97648fa418a28207f1f30548cf5ff3f3333c42e46afd5f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2607.04387, 'An Elementary Proof of the Dvoretzky--Kiefer--Wolfowitz--Massart Inequality'] \be
\P\lp\sup_{x\in\Reals}\lp\hat{F}_n(x)-F(x)\rp> \varepsilon \rp \leq e^{-2n \varepsilon^2}, \forall \varepsilon >0

\ee (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:44:12.630490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d474a3b3729c0fe0dfa7714c6a2893f925a21db5ada59f5fe621049559ed76b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:LDP of arXiv:2511.22162, 'Landau Hamiltonian with Gaussian white noise potential and the asymptotics of its bottom o'] Fix a bounded open box $B$. As $\beta \to 0$, the sequence of random variables $\lambda_1^B(0, \mathrm{h}_\beta, \mathrm{Z}_\beta)$ satisfies a LDP with rate $\beta^{2}$ and the good rate function \footnote{Given any bounded open box $B$ and $\varphi \in L^2(B)$, the operator $-\Delta + \varphi$ (with zero Dirichlet b.c. on $B$) is self-adjoint on $L^2(B)$ with compact resolvent. This can be seen by the construction given in Section (sec:construction of the paper). Indeed, it coincides with $\Phi^B(0,q)$ with $q = (G\ast\varphi, -F\ast\varphi - |\nabla G\ast\varphi|^2) \in \ccM$.}
	\begin{equ}
		I_B(x) = \inf\left\{\frac12 \left\| \varphi \right\|_{L^2(B)}^2: \text{$\varphi \in L^2(B)$ s.t. $x$ is the lowest e.v. of $-\Delta + \varphi$}\right\}, \quad x \in \R,
	\end{equ}
	with the convention $\inf \emptyset = \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.675819+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d47c631d7f4fa5fdea36c99cae6eac40b7cd08e601658255b0431257a2d60d2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Wendel's formula: Combinatorial version) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $n, d \ge 1$ be integers with $n \geq d+1$.
For every regular $X \in \R^{d\times n}$, the cardinality of the set $\mathcal M(X)$ is given by
\begin{align*}
    |\mathcal M(X)| = 2 \sum_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{n-1}{j}.
