{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.961530+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d8f6914c645d7b2bef4ff6a5210ca9ce8c9c08bc1755d28b9016e25aaa3bbbb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2605.11279, 'Flocking with Multiple Types: Competition, Fluid Limits and Traveling Waves'] Suppose Assumption \ref{assumption0} holds. Then for any $T > 0$, the sequence $\curlbr*{\mu_n(\cdot)\!: n\in\nat}$ is Wasserstein-equicontinuous on $[0, T]$. That is, for any $\varepsilon > 0$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{\delta\to 0} \limsup_{n\to\infty} \prob\parens*{\sup_{\substack{0\leq s\leq t\leq T \\ 0< t-s<\delta}} \WWW_1\big(\mu_n(s), \mu_n(t)\big) \geq \varepsilon} = 0.
    \end{align*}
    Here, $\WWW_1$ denotes the $1$-Wasserstein distance on the space $\real \times \{+, -\}$; that is, for two measures $\nu_1$ and $\nu_2$ on $\real \times \{+, -\}$,
    \begin{align*}
        \WWW_1(\nu_1, \nu_2) := \sup_{f\in\HHH} \curlbr*{\int f \text{d} (\nu_1 - \nu_2)}.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

