{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.612411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d9d08fad048d11b7ebb31a82c09c09524ca96e9db8dc58df0a819995f5dc3a6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 17 of arXiv:2510.27451, 'Bi-martingale optimal transport and its applications'] For a pair  $\mu, \nu \in \mathcal{P}^\ba_2(\Rd)$, the following conditions are equivalent: 
	\begin{enumerate}[label={(\roman*)}]
		\item the convex order relation $\mu \preceq_c \nu$ holds true;
		\item $\ov{u}(x) = -\frac{1}{2} \abs{x}^2 $ solves the Zolotarev problem \eqref{eq:Z2_again}  or, equivalently,
		\begin{equation}

			Z_2(\mu,\nu) = \frac{m_2(\nu) - m_2(\mu)}{2};
		\end{equation}
		\item $\ov{\rho} = \nu$ is the (unique) solution of the minimal convex dominance problem \eqref{eq:C_rho} or, equivalently,
			\begin{equation*}
				\mathcal{C}(\mu,\nu) = m_2(\nu);
			\end{equation*}
		\item the set of optimal bi-martingale plans equals $\ov{\Gamma}(\mu,\nu) = \Gamma_{\mathrm{M}}(\mu,\nu)$ or, equivalently,
		\begin{equation}

			\ov{\Gamma Q} (\mu,\nu)  = \Big\{  (\gamma,q) \, : \, \gamma \in \Gamma_{\mathrm{M}}(\mu,\nu), \ q = y \gamma  \Big\}.
		\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.335362+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d9e4b2561d5c6a3b121c39bbc67f545d3e0337db4ef1d5c36bfd347b71d1e3d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Psi_bounds of arXiv:2606.17425, 'Order statistics for edge eigenvectors of Wigner matrices'] Let $H(t)$ be the interpolation (equation interpolation_step_1 of the paper), (equation diagonal_var_interpolation_eq of the paper), or (equation gaussian_to_gaussian_interpolating_flow of the paper), and recall $\Psi$ from (equation Psi_def of the paper) and $\eta, \tilde{\eta}$ from (equation eta_defs of the paper). Then, uniformly for $t \in {\mathbb R}_{\geq 0}$,
	\begin{align}
		\Psi(t) \prec \frac{1}{N \tilde{\eta}}.

	\end{align}
		Further, for any (large) $D > 0$ it holds uniformly for $t \in {\mathbb R}^+$ that, \begin{equation}
		\sup_{\substack{E \in [E_L, -2 + N^{-2/3 + \epsilon}] \\
			u,v \in \mathbb{B}}} \max(\abs{G_{uv}(t, E + \ii \eta)}, \abs{G_{uv}(t, E + \ii \tilde{{\eta}})}) \leq 1, 

	\end{equation} with probability $\geq 1 - N^{-D}$ for $N \geq N_0(D, H)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.377904+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4da1614b3ea0b122ad8e6e4bb8511aef641f8cd3d42786c968d1b50dec73ea13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:HKUB-collisions of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] Fix $A>0$, $k\ge 3$, $\delta\in (0,\frac14)$, $0<t_0<T$, and $r\ge 1$. Then, for some $C>0$, and all $\mathbf x\in\mathbb{Z}^k$, $N\in \mathbb{N}$, $|\beta|\le AN^{-1/8}$ and $t\in[t_0,TN]$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf P_\mathbf x^{(\beta,k)}
(|R_t^i-R_t^j|\le r,\,
|R_t^\ell-R_t^m|\le r)
\le C t^{-1+\delta}\; \text{,}\;\;
\end{equation}
for all distinct unordered pairs $\{i,j\}\neq\{\ell,m\}$ in $\{1,\ldots,k\}$.
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
\int_0^{TN}
\mathbf P_\mathbf x^{(\beta,k)}(
|R_t^i-R_t^j|\le r,\,
|R_t^\ell-R_t^m|\le r
)   
\,{\rm d} t
\le C N^\delta\; \text{.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.736025+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4da369584aef852902894fbac23cd34f5d25155e483563c69d6fc382bdae8636","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-Ward of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Let $\mathcal A$ be a Hermitian matrix. Define its resolvent as $R(z):=(\mathcal A-z)^{-1}$ for any $z= E+ \mathrm{i} \eta\in \mathbb{C}_+$. Then, we have 
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
\sum_x \overline {R_{xy'}}  R_{xy} = \frac{R_{y'y}-\overline{R_{yy'}}}{2\mathrm{i} \eta},\quad
\sum_x \overline {R_{y'x}}  R_{yx} = \frac{R_{yy'}-\overline{R_{y'y}}}{2\mathrm{i} \eta}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
As a special case, if $y=y'$, we have
\begin{equation}
\sum_x |R_{xy}|^2 =\sum_x |R_{yx}|^2 = \frac{\im R_{yy}}{ \eta}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.547693+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dad07dca86f743cb89c7cf0a74a3dca892b709744e92cf8f8723b83b289e47f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:H+H- of arXiv:2412.03366, 'Regularity of Weighted Tensorized Fractional Brownian Fields and associated function space'] For $\alpha \in [0,1]$ and $H \in (0,1)$, we set
\begin{equation}
H_\alpha^+ := (1+\alpha)H \quad \text{ and } \quad H_\alpha^- := (1-\alpha)H    
\end{equation} 
and we define the Gaussian field $\{X^{\alpha,H}_{(x_1,x_2)}\}_{(x_1,x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2}$ by 
\begin{equation}
X^{\alpha,H}_{(x_1,x_2)} :=  \int_{\mathbb{R}^2}  \frac{(e^{i x_1 \xi_1}-1) (e^{i x_2 \xi_2}-1)}{\phi_{\alpha,H}(\xi_1,\xi_2)}  d\hat{\bf{W}}({\boldsymbol{\xi}}) 
\end{equation}
where the function
\[\phi_{\alpha,H}(\xi_1,\xi_2)=\min(\vert \xi_1 \vert, \vert \xi_2 \vert)^{H_\alpha^- +\frac{1}{2}}\max (\vert \xi_1 \vert, \vert \xi_2 \vert)^{H_\alpha^++\frac{1}{2} }\]
denotes the square root of the inverse of the spectral density of the field.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.049914+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dada11b8c32ca4c33695219bd5371b9a2ef6d45ef87a5a405aaa8c76dc2e27a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 1 of arXiv:2510.26216, 'A functional Breuer-Major theorem with Poisson noise'] For $\gamma>0$ and $M\in\NN$, the class $\cC_{M}^{\gamma}$ consists of functions $\phi: \RR \rightarrow \RR$ such that there exists $C>0$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \sup_{0 \leq \ell \leq [\gamma]} |\phi^{(\ell)}(x)| \leq C (1 + |x|)^{M}\;, \qquad \sup_{|h|<1} \frac{|\phi^{([\gamma])}(x+h) - \phi^{([\gamma])}(x)|}{|h|^{\gamma-[\gamma]}} \leq C(1 + |x|)^{M}
    \end{equation*}
    for all $x \in \RR$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.309918+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dafa9a39c2e6d7d539a9fa242b8e116b85eac2ac857fbb932d0ce42845e088e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 of arXiv:2604.07697, 'Stochastic fractional heat equation with general rough noise'] Let $\frac{3-\alpha}{4}<H<\frac12$ and let $\lambda(x)$ be defined by \eqref{eq lambda}.
Let $u_{\varepsilon}$ be the approximate solution defined by \eqref{eq 4.39} and assume that $u_0(x)$ belongs to $\mathcal{Z}_{\lambda,0}^p$. Then  the following properties hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i).]  If $p>\frac{2(\alpha+1)}{4H-3+\alpha}$, then
\begin{equation}
\left\|\sup_{t\in[0,T],\,x\in\mathbb{R}}\lambda^{\frac1{p}}(x)\mathcal{N}_{\frac12-H}u_{\varepsilon}(t,x)\right\|_{L^{p}(\Omega)}\le c_{\alpha,T,p,H}\left(\|u_{\varepsilon}\|_{\mathcal{Z}_{\lambda,T}^p}+1\right).
\end{equation}
\item[(ii).]  If $p>\frac{2(\alpha+1)}{2H+\alpha-2}$ and $0<\gamma<\frac{2H+\alpha-2}{2\alpha}-\frac{\alpha+1}{\alpha p},$ then
\begin{equation}
\left\|\sup_{t,t+h\in[0,T],x\in\mathbb{R}}\lambda^{\frac1{p}}(x)\left[u_{\varepsilon}(t+h,x)-u_{\varepsilon}(t,x)\right]\right\|_{L^{p}(\Omega)}\le c_{\alpha,T,p,H,\gamma}|h|^{\gamma}\left(\|u_{\varepsilon}\|_{\mathcal{Z}_{\lambda,T}^p}+1\right).
\end{equation}


\item[(iii).]  If $p>\frac{2(\alpha+1)}{2H+\alpha-2}$ and $0<\gamma<\frac{2H+\alpha-2}{2}-\frac{\alpha+1}{p},$ then
\begin{equation}
\left\|\sup_{t\in[0,T],\,x\in\mathbb{R}}\frac{u_{\varepsilon}(t,x)-u_{\varepsilon}(t,y)}{\lambda^{-\frac1p}(x)+\lambda^{-\frac1p}(y)}\right\|_{L^{p}(\Omega)}\le c_{\alpha,T,p,H,\gamma}|x-y|^{\gamma}\left(\|u_{\varepsilon}\|_{\mathcal{Z}_{\lambda,T}^p}+1\right).
\end{equation}
  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.475343+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4db42b337a8cf39a3ed283d67c29854527deefdfbaa5ac5338bd81dc8a15a264","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hypercube_variance of arXiv:2607.29020, 'Entropic Symmetrization Resistance'] Let $Y = (Y_1, \dots, Y_d)$ and $X = (X_1, \dots, X_d)$ be as in (thm:hypercube_entropy of the paper). 
Then, $\@ifstar{\oldnorm}{\oldnorm*}{\Cov(Y)}_{1,1} \ge \frac{1}{d} \@ifstar{\oldnorm}{\oldnorm*}{\Cov(X)}_{1,1}$

with equality if and only if the following conditions are all satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item[(i)] The $Y_i$ are uncorrelated.
\item[(ii)] For all $i \in [d]$, $f_{Y_i} = f_{-X_i}$. 
\item[(iii)] $\Cov(X) = \Var(X_i)J_d$, for all $i \in [d]$, where $J_d$ is a p.s.d. matrix with all entries in $\{\pm1\}$. 
\end{enumerate}
\begin{proof}
We compute
\begin{align}
\@ifstar{\oldnorm}{\oldnorm*}{\Cov(Y)}_{1,1} 
&\ge {\rm tr}(\Cov(Y)) \label[ineq]{ineq:hypercube_covariance_1}\\

&\ge 

{\rm tr}(\Cov(X)) \label[ineq]{ineq:hypercube_covariance_2}\\
&\ge \frac{1}{d} \@ifstar{\oldnorm}{\oldnorm*}{\Cov(X)}_{1,1}. \label[ineq]{ineq:hypercube_covariance_3}
\end{align}
(ineq:hypercube_covariance_1 of the paper) 
attains equality exactly when the off-diagonal entries in $\Cov(Y)$ are all zero, i.e., the $Y_i$ are uncorrelated. 
(ineq:hypercube_covariance_2 of the paper) follows from $d$ applications of (thm:VarY_exceeds_VarX of the paper) and the fact that ${\rm tr}(\Cov(Y)) = \sum_i \Var(Y_i)$ (and similar for $X$); 
thus equality is attained in (equation ineq:hypercube_covariance_2 of the paper) exactly when 

$f_{Y_i} = f_{-X_i}$ for all $i \in [d]$. 
Finally, (equation ineq:hypercube_covariance_3 of the paper) follows from (lem:d_tr_dominates_oneone_norm of the paper). 
It is possible for equality to be attained in (equation ineq:hypercube_covariance_3 of the paper); one example is $X_1 = \dots = X_d$. 
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.016415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4db6629324820619480d1fea2cf175bf6df3c626919310b88a654d1dc6585607","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2 of arXiv:2608.26288, 'Muon with Finite Newton--Schulz: The Smoothing Benefit in Nonsmooth Nonconvex Optimization'] Under~\cref{ass:general-spectral-link}, for every $C>0$ the function $\widetilde\Phi_{h,C}$ is convex and continuously differentiable on all of $\R^{m\times n}$, and its gradient is
