{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.296805+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4df1e61fbc4be749d2d129e930eef9a1b2915d2813f94ca29e93d7191e900416","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma intkolm of arXiv:2605.22787, 'Invariant measures for half-space geometric LPP: classification and the one force--one sol'] Fix $\varepsilon\in (0,1)$ and $M_0>0$. For each $m\in \mathbb{N}$ and $M \in (0,\infty)$, let $V_{m,M}$ be the set of all $(y_1,y_2)\in \mathbb{Z}^2_{\mathrm{HS}}$ satisfying
\begin{align}
    y_1-y_2 \le M \sqrt{m}.
\end{align}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), font=\normalfont]
    \item  Suppose $c\in [0,1)$. Then, there exists $\lambda,M_1>0$ depending on $\varepsilon,q,c,M_0$ such that for all $m\ge M_1$ and $(y_1,y_2)\in V_{m,M_0\lambda}$ we have
     \begin{align}
        \mathbb P_{\mathrm{Inter};q,c}^{m,(y_1,y_2)}\left(\inf_{j\in \llbracket 0,m\rrbracket} \left(Q_2(j)-y_2+\frac{(m -j)q}{1-q}\right) \ge -\lambda \sqrt{m}\right) \ge 1-\varepsilon.
    \end{align}
    \item  Suppose $c\in [1,q^{-1})$. Then, there exists $\lambda>0$ depending on $\varepsilon,q,c$ such that for all $m\in \mathbb{N}$ and $y\in \mathbb{R}$, we have
 \begin{align}
\mathbb P_{\mathrm{Geom};c^{-1}q}^{m,y}\left(\inf_{j\in \llbracket 0,m\rrbracket} \left(Q(j)-y+\frac{(m -j)q}{c-q}\right) \ge -\lambda \sqrt{m}\right) \ge 1-\varepsilon.
    \end{align}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.185771+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4df2c1e929cbc3def5225028bd5cea202389ff2193a4f67dad9b9e49b40678f8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.26996, 'A note on shifting distributions via Poisson races'] Let $P,Q$ be two distributions over the same finite set.
If $D(Q||P) < \infty$ then
there is a $P$ to $Q$ simulator $I$ such that
\begin{align*}
\E [\log I] 
\leq D(Q||P) + 1.45 \|Q-P\|_1.
\end{align*}
On the other hand, for every $P$ to $Q$ simulator $I$,
\[\E[\log I] \geq \frac{1}{2} \max \{ D(Q||P),  \|Q-P\|_1 \}.\]
In particular, if $D(Q||P)=\infty$ then there is no $P$ to $Q$ simulator. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

