{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.219030+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e69d0b07696f976db180cc6d2891cdeaee8b9de1dd8c98e082a226c75f49202","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: continuous polynomial chaos for general noise of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	Let $\boldsymbol\zeta$ be a Lévy white noise with Lévy measure $\lambda$ such that there exist $1 < p < q < 2$ such that $m_p(1,+\infty) < +\infty$ and $m_q(0,1) < +\infty$.
	Suppose the family of functions $(\boldsymbol\psi^{(k)})_{k \, \geqslant\, 0}$ verifies the following: for every constant $C \, \geqslant\, 0$, 
	\begin{equation}
		\sum\limits_{k \, \geqslant\, 0} C^k \big\|\boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}\big\|_q < +\infty\,.
	\end{equation}
	Then $\Phi^{(a)}$ is well-defined for every $a \in (0,1]$ and $(\boldsymbol\Phi^{(a)})_{a \in (0,1]}$ is a time-reversed martingale with respect to the filtration ${\ensuremath{\mathcal F}}  =({\ensuremath{\mathcal F}} _a)_{a > 0}$.

	Additionally, $(\boldsymbol\Phi^{(a)})_{a \in (0,1]}$ is bounded in $\mathds{L}^{p}$ and thus admits a limit \(\boldsymbol\Phi := \lim_{a\downarrow 0} \boldsymbol\Phi^{(a)}\) a.s.\ and in \(\mathds{L}^p\).
	Furthermore, there exist a universal constant $C_0'$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		\big\| \boldsymbol\Phi \big\|_p \, \leqslant\, \sum\limits_{k \, \geqslant\, 0} \Big(\frac{C_0'}{p-1}(m_p(1,+\infty)+m_q(0,1))\Big)^k \big\| \boldsymbol\psi^{(k)} \big\|_{q} \,.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

