{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.537313+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4edc3d3dab922b8acb63b4af05dbb29c98d0a3d3dd726326225882a3d94341ac","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 15 of arXiv:2609.02610, 'The frame set of the first Hermite function'] Let $\beta>0$, $\delta\in(0,1)$, $N\in\N$, $N\geq2$, and
\begin{equation}
  \sigma=\frac{N-1}{2}\,\delta.
\end{equation}
Let $\cW_N$ be the Wronskian defined in \eqref{eq:wronskian}.
Then, there exists $\eta=(\eta_n)_{n\in\Z}\in\ell^\infty(\Z)$ such that
\begin{equation}
