\end{equation}
Moreover, for any $t\ge 1$, let $P_{n}^{*t}$ be the $t$-fold convolution of $P_n$, viewed $P_{n}^{*t}$ as a random variable in $\mathfrak{S}_n$. Given $n=ml$ with $l\ge 2$, the random variable $\sim_l\backslash P_{n}^{*t}\in\sim_l\backslash\mathfrak{S}_n$ is induced from (defi: quotient of space of the paper) in the obvious way.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.992477+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ef11b91732ac50c5c1481f815229522ccd7da0a6692e031d3103d96740c2e17","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2605.11801, 'A non-local singular non-linear Fokker-Planck PDE'] Let $\g\in\R$ and $\theta\in (0,1)$. If $g\in\shc^{\g}$ then for all $t>0$ we have
