where $\del^k_wY (w)$ is the $k$-th derivative of $Y$, for any integer $k\ge 2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.898303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4efc127eb8faa3f1c759d44ed6545b3d7b7f02749d559876ad13f4ea00c8f162","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:mixing-spectral of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] Let $ \mathsf{P}$ a weakly stationary random measure~with spectral measure $ \mathsf{S}$. Then if $ \mathsf{P}$ is mixing, or more weakly if $ \mathsf{P}$ has uncorrelated linear statistics, i.e. for $ \varphi \in  \mathscr  C_{ c}^{ \infty }(\mathbb{R}^{ d})$, 
