    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.894488+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4f484ee6e7246bc8c29a44fba4e1bb26c1644693193171a93e8587a8eaeb5ce2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Y of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] Given a compact $n$-dimensional Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$, and any $I\subset (0,+\infty)$ bounded, we define the random variable $Y_M(I)$ and the constant $c_n$ as
    \begin{equation} 
Y_M(I):=\frac{1}{c_n\mathrm{Vol}^{n}(M)}\cdot \frac{\mathcal{L}_{\phi_{I}^M}(M)}{\lambda(\phi_I^M)}, \quad c_n:= \frac{s_{n-1}}{s_n\sqrt{n}}
    \end{equation}
where $\phi_I$ is defined as in (def:RRW of the paper) and $\lambda(\phi_I^M)$ is as in (eq:phiI of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.219324+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4f4c59cd0f1c39a797616350feb4ad5409a5aea065ff679c9b87a235280e4250","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 of arXiv:2607.17576, 'How much randomness is needed to symmetrize a random variable?'] Let $\lambda>0$. Let $X\sim \text{Exp}(\lambda)$ be an exponential random variable with the probability density function $f_X(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbb{R}_+}(x)$. Let $Y$ be an absolutely continuous independent symmetrizer of $X$. Then we have for all $\alpha>0$ that
\begin{equation}
h_\alpha (Y)\ge h_\alpha(X)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
T_\alpha (Y)\ge T_\alpha(X).
\end{equation}
Equality holds if and only if $Y$ is an independent copy of $-X$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.153920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4f505bb27f96d4bb6cd9fa9be4b9f1218092f9aa0e16b21910e1cca1bb9a1afb","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:G-Wass of arXiv:2608.13716, 'Graph Causal Optimal Transport \\ and Wasserstein Distances'] Let $G$ be a directed graph and $p \geq 1$. The $p$\textsuperscript{th} order \emph{$G$-causal Wasserstein distance} between $\mu, \nu \in \Gpprobabilities{p}{\mathcal{X}}$ is given by
    \begin{equation}
        W_{G,p}(\mu, \nu)^p \coloneq \inf_{\pi \in \Gbicouplings{\mu}{\nu}} \int d_{\mathcal{X}}(x,y)^p \, d\pi(x,y).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.483553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4f52e208feb24bd984ed8b321735d7a24e3cb7e68a5d6bc754459c65d98adbfb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 32 of arXiv:2607.28238, 'Well-posedness and large deviations for the obstacle problem of first-order stochastic con'] For any $M>0$, let $\{h^{\varepsilon}; 0<\varepsilon<1\}\subset \mathcal{A}_M$. Then
