{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.238248+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fa0a1595d3a35faff8fe28b2209972386ff09ac8346f0bfde757f33e06b2c34","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stability_downscaling of arXiv:2608.29448, '\method: Self-Scaled Adaptive Preconditioning for Physics-Informed Learning'] Let $\mathcal{H}_t, \mathcal{H}_{t+1} \in \mathbb{S}_{++}^d$ be Hessian operators at steps $t, t+1$ with spectral decompositions $\mathcal{H}_t = \mathbf{Q}_t \mathbf{\Lambda}_t \mathbf{Q}_t^\top$ and $\mathcal{H}_{t+1} = \mathbf{Q}_{t+1} \mathbf{\Lambda}_{t+1} \mathbf{Q}_{t+1}^\top$, where $\mathbf{Q} \in \mathrm{O}(d)$. Let $\mathbf{V}_t = \operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{v}_t)$ be the accumulated second moment in basis $\mathbf{Q}_t$. Let $\mathcal{U} = \mathbf{Q}_{t+1}^\top \mathbf{Q}_t$ be the unitary transition operator. Furthermore, we define the variance estimators in the new basis $\mathbf{Q}_{t+1}$ as
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{V}_{\text{proj}} &:= \operatorname{diag}(\mathcal{U} \mathbf{V}_t \mathcal{U}^\top) \quad \text{(Exact Reprojection)} \\
    \mathbf{V}_{\text{scale}} &:= \gamma \mathbf{V}_t, \ \gamma \in (0, 1) \quad \text{(Scalar Downscaling)}.
\end{align}
Let $\mathbf{V}^*_{t+1}$ be the optimal oracle variance estimator aligned with $\mathcal{H}_{t+1}$. Then, the following bounds hold:

\begin{enumerate}
    \itemsep 0em
    \item \textbf{Instability of Reprojection:} The relative error of $\mathbf{V}_{\text{proj}}$ is lower-bounded by the condition number $\kappa(\mathbf{V}_t) = {\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{V}_t)}/{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{V}_t)}$. Specifically, there exists a rotation $\mathcal{U}$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        \sup_{\mathcal{U} \in \mathrm{O}(d)} \frac{\| \mathbf{V}_{\text{proj}}^{-1/2} - (\mathbf{V}^*_{t+1})^{-1/2} \|_\infty}{\| (\mathbf{V}^*_{t+1})^{-1/2} \|_\infty} \geq \sqrt{\kappa(\mathbf{V}_t)} - 1.
    \end{equation}

    \item \textbf{Boundedness of Downscaling:} Assuming Lipschitz continuity of the Hessian spectrum with constant $L_{\mathcal{H}}$, the error of $\mathbf{V}_{\text{scale}}$ is bounded linearly by the subspace misalignment. If the gradient distribution satisfies sub-Gaussian tail bounds, then with high probability
    \begin{equation}
        \| \mathbf{V}_{\text{scale}} - \mathbf{V}^*_{t+1} \|_F \leq (1-\gamma)\| \mathbf{V}_t \|_F + L_{\mathcal{H}} \| \mathbf{I} - \mathcal{U} \|_F.
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.058932+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fa301cbb97f332c1dc5fd920eacb44d71d593d15aa8ff44a84334123863780c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 48 of arXiv:2606.16636, 'Free energy of non-convex multi-species spin glasses with centered Ising spins'] Assume the setting of~\cite{chen2025free}. For every $t > 0$ and $q \in \mcl Q_1$, there exist $p^+, p^- \in \mcl Q_{\infty}$ satisfying $\Ll|p^\pm\Rr|_{L^\infty}\leq 1$ such that
