{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.376738+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fb346e7f10bd3e02451186b8fad87aae3743e8a33c581ac794fdc8bebcdb6ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:secondorder of arXiv:2412.01995, 'Exciting games and Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations'] Fix $x^0 \in \partial \Delta$ with $x^0_1=\dotso=x^0_k=0$,  $x^0_{k+1},\dotso,x^0_d > 0$, and $\sum_{j=k+1}^d x_j^0<1$. Taking $r= (1-\sum_{i=k+1}^d x^0_i)/{(2d)}$,  there exist two positive constants $c_1(x^0)$, $c_2(x^0)$ such that for any $x_1,\dotso, x_k \in (0,r)$,
    \begin{align*}
    c_1(x^0) Diag(1/x^2_1,\dotso,1/x^2_k, 1,\dotso, 1) 
  &\leq \nabla^2 g(x_1,\dotso, x_k, x^0_{k+1},\dotso,x^0_d) \\
  &\leq  c_2(x^0)Diag(1/x^2_1,\dotso,1/x^2_k, 1,\dotso ,1),
  \end{align*}
    where $Diag(1/x^2_1,\dotso,1/x^2_k, 1,\dotso, 1)$ denotes a diagonal matrix with the $(i,i)$-th entry being $1/x^2_i$ for $1\leq i \leq k$ and $1$ for $k+1 \leq i \leq d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.625730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fb65c705c1671136a0c3d4d4f0a1a68c9ff7e9df695217285514ea47e35ce24","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lem: 1226 of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] Given $X_1,X_2\in \mathcal{H}^d$ on a sublinear expectation space $(\Omega,\mathcal{H},\hat{\mathbb{E}})$, let $\Gamma_i$ such that $X_i\sim \Gamma_i$, $i=1,2$. For any $\phi\in C_{lip}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ with Lipschitz constant $l_\phi$, define $v_i(t,x):=\sup_{y\in \Gamma_i}\phi(x+ty)$, $i=1,2$. Then for any $(t,x)\in \mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb R^d$,
		\begin{equation*}
			|v_1(t,x)-v_2(t,x)|\leq tl_{\phi}\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X_1-X_2|].
		\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.672675+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fbe645be4d81d2e28b1ad37c263d91892994a432a63cb2e555664f448d6ea6d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2607.10725, 'From the Airy line ensemble to the Gaussian free field'] Fix $t \in \mathbb{R}$, $x < 0$, $\eta > 0$. Let $S(z;t,x)$ be as in (\ref{Eq.DefS}) and assume the notation in Definition \ref{def: contour}. Then the following statements hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $z^{\pm} = \crit^\pm +\sqrt{3}u \pm \im u \in \refbeth^{\pm,0}$, then 
\begin{equation}
\Re \left[S(z^{\pm}; t, x)- S(\crit^\pm;t, x)\right] = -2 \sqrt{3} \cdot \sqrt{-x} u^2.
\end{equation}
\item[(b)] If $z^{\pm} = \crit^\pm + \sqrt{3}\eta \pm \im u \in \refbeth^{\pm,1}$ and $u \geq \eta$, then
\begin{equation}
\Re \left[S(z^{\pm}; t, x)- S(\crit^\pm;t, x)\right] \leq -\sqrt{-x} u\eta.
\end{equation}
\item[(c)] If $\eta< \sqrt{-x}$, $z^{\pm}=\crit^\pm - \sqrt{3}\eta \mp \im u \in \refbeth^{\pm,1}$ and $\eta \leq u \leq \sqrt{-x}$, then
\begin{equation}
\Re \left[S(z^{\pm}; t, x)- S(\crit^\pm;t, x)\right] \leq -\sqrt{-x}\eta^2.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, the same inequalities hold with $\refbeth^\pm$ replaced by $\contour^\pm$ and $S$ replaced by $-S$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.461944+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fc435094c69866ed9dc989d8e4ce6d05d9ec8dae743b16dbd421db790c7120e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Extended generator) of arXiv:2606.28678, '2606.28678'] For any $f\in C^2(\mathcal{S}^d)$, we have
