{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.437020+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_501a753736317933138bc16e176134e177dfd8ccb98444c9b155242de8d7483e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def: D of arXiv:2509.26254, '$K$-Branching Random Walk with Noisy Selection: \\ \Large Large Population Limits and Phas'] A function $g: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}_+ \cup \{-\infty\}$ belongs to the class $\mathcal{D}$ if and only if 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $g$ admits a left limit and a right limit for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$, and
\item[(b)] $g$ has bounded support $\mbox{Supp}(g) = \{x \in \mathbb{R} :\: g(x) \neq -\infty \}$, in which case we define $L(g) \leq U(g)$ such that $[L(g), U(g)]$ is the smallest interval containing $\mbox{Supp}(g)$. We call $L(g)$ and $U(g)$ the {\it lower edge} and {\it upper edge} of $g$ respectively.
\end{itemize}
A function $g: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}_+ \cup \{-\infty\}$ belongs to the class $\mathcal{C}$ if and only if $g \in \mathcal{D}$ and 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)] $g$ is concave on its support.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

