{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.294739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_507d04831df379c669134b19708c95c23f22c79f291ad228b2ffdb9de5e91440","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:eternal_construction of arXiv:2605.22787, 'Invariant measures for half-space geometric LPP: classification and the one force--one sol'] Let $\nu_1,\nu_2,\ldots,$ be a sequence of invariant measures for the corresponding half-space LPP problem on the space $\mathbb{R}^\mathbb{N}$. Then, on some probability space $(\widetilde \Omega,\widetilde{\mathcal{F}},\widetilde{\mathbb P})$, there exists weights $(\widetilde \omega_{\mathbf x})_{\mathbf x \in \mathbb{Z}^2_{\mathrm{HS}}}$, equal in distribution to the original weights, and a sequence of eternal solutions $b_1,b_2,\ldots,$ such that, for each $p \in \mathbb{N}$ and $n \in \mathbb{Z}$,
    \begin{equation} 
    \bigl(b_p(n+k,n) - b_p(n,n)\bigr)_{k \in \mathbb{N}} \sim \nu_p,
    \end{equation}
    and the process $\bigl(b_p(i,j)\bigr)_{i\ge j, j \le n, p\in \mathbb{N}}$ is independent of $(\widetilde \omega_{i,j})_{i \ge j \ge n+1}$ \rm{(}the weights on levels $n + 1$ and above\rm{)}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.400460+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_507f9bd3e0c321f149cc9324c45094ebb1b3998a535dad9a215c9d8dc698361e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t: partition persistence of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $p\in[-d,\infty)$. Suppose that $(\Phi,\tau)$ is an invariant random partition, and suppose that $\Psi, \Gamma$ are invariant locally square-integrable random measures. Further, assume that, for some $\vartheta>0$,
\begin{equation}
    \BE_0^\Phi\bigg[\Phi(B_\vartheta)\bigg(\int_{C(0)} \sqrt{\|x\|^{d+\max(p,0)}\rho(\|x\|)+1}\,(\Psi+\Gamma)(dx)\bigg)^2\bigg] <\infty,
\end{equation}
and that
\begin{equation}
    \int_{V_0} x^{\otimes q}\, (\Psi-\Gamma)(dx) = 0,\quad q\in\N_0,q<\max(\tfrac{d+p}{2},1).
\end{equation}
Then $\Psi$ is (beyond) $p$-uniform iff $\Gamma$ is (beyond) $p$-uniform.
\begin{proof}
    At its core, this Theorem is derived from Proposition~(p:moment setting of the paper). For this matter, we define the transport kernels $K,L$ as
    \begin{equation}
        K(x, B) := \Psi(B\cap C(x)), \quad L(x, B) := \Gamma(B\cap C(x)),\quad x\in\R^d, B\in\cB^d.
    \end{equation}
    Then we can derive from~(equation e:partition difference 0 of the paper) that~(equation e:markov difference 0 of the paper) from Proposition~(p:moment setting of the paper) holds. Further, the proof that~(equation e:partition integrability of the paper) implies Condition~(c:condition square-integrable general with p of the paper) with respect to $K,\Phi$ and $L,\Phi$ is analogous to the proof of Proposition~(p:independent square-integrable of the paper). Hence, the assertion is implied by Proposition~(p:moment setting of the paper).
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.830437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_508095608475b05a67df63c9376da03f1cd6a44b29b4c8e227904dfdd365af47","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm B-Sig of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] For $\mu > -\frac12$ and ${\boldsymbol{\kappa}} \in {\mathbb R}^{d_2-1}$ with ${\kappa}_i > -1$, $1 \le i \le d_2-1$, define
\begin{align} 
  {\mathfrak D}_{\mu,{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb B}\rtimes \Sigma} u : = \, &   |\bm y| \Delta_{\bm x} + 2 {\langle} \bm x, \nabla_{\bm x} {\rangle} \partial_{y_d} - 
   {\langle} \bm x, \nabla_{\bm x} {\rangle} 
      +  \sum_{i=1}^{d_2} \left( y_i \partial_{y_i}^2 + (\kappa_i +1 - y_i) \partial_{y_i} \right)  \\
   &  + 2 |{\bm y}| \partial_{y_d}^2 - 2 {\langle} \bm y, \nabla_{\bm y} {\rangle} \partial_{y_d} + (2\mu + d_1 - |{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}| - d_2) \partial_{y_d}. \notag
\end{align}
Then ${\mathcal V}_n\big({\mathbb B}^{d_1} \rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, \bm W_{\mu, {\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb B}\rtimes \Sigma}\big)$ is an eigenspace of 
$ {\mathfrak D}_{\mu,{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb B}\rtimes \Sigma}$ for each $n \in {\mathbb N}_0$. More precisely, 
\begin{align} 
  {\mathfrak D}_{\mu,{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb B} \rtimes \Sigma} u = - n u, \qquad \forall 
         u \in {\mathcal V}_n\big ({\mathbb B}^{d_1} \rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, \bm W_{\mu, {\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb B}\rtimes \Sigma}\big).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.842899+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_50835df746086d3c78b3536cb405ec84abcfcd2a586ccad845c145ed98243c4c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 of arXiv:2511.09930, 'Martingale dimensions for a class of metric measure spaces'] There exist positive constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ such that
