{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:15:46.790701+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_50e8dd5f5c2445660072f95c151427b26f1fcc2ffab5f0e8878151a9d57f0827","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[OPEN PROBLEM - not a proved fact. Stated in arXiv:2511.10535, 'Eigenvalues of Brownian Motions on $\mathrm{GL}(N,\mathbb{C})$' (conj environment labelled conj.b0).] Let $b_0$ be an invertible operator in a $W^\ast$-probability space $(\A,\tau)$. Define
\[ \tilde{p}_0(z) = \tau[|b_0-z|^{-2}] \qquad \& \qquad \tilde{p}_2(z) = \tau[b_0^\ast b_0|b_0-z|^{-2}] \]
where, as usual, for any operator $a\in\A$, $|a|^2:=a^\ast a$. Define $T(b_0,\cdot)\colon\C\to\R_+$ by
\begin{equation} T(b_0,z) = \frac{\log(\tilde{p}_2(z))-\log(|z|^2\tilde{p}_0(z))}{\tilde{p}_2(z)-|z|^2\tilde{p}_0(z)}.
\end{equation}
For each $t>0$, define the open precompact set $\Sigma(b_0,t)$ by
\begin{equation} \Sigma(b_0,t) = \{z\in\C\colon T(b_0,z)<t\}. \end{equation}
Let $b(t,\zeta)$ be a free multiplicative Brownian motion (see Notation (notat.ovweparam)) freely independent from $b_0$. Then the support of the Brown measure of $b_0b(t,\zeta)$ is equal to the image $\overline{\Sigma(b_0,t,\zeta)}$ of $\overline{\Sigma(b_0,t)}$ under the map $\Psi^{b_0}_{t,\zeta}$ of (eq.Psi). (Verbatim from the paper's TeX; symbols are the source paper's. Stated as open/conjectural; no resolution of it was found in the paper's own text by the rules of problem_index.py - a paper may still prove special cases of a conjecture it states.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.617751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_50ea4c7cb28595fef4a9ac3ccc3ce1808fac5894a166f720a927f177e9911d17","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 67 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] There exist positive constants $\mu_k,d_k,c_k>0$ such that for all $2\leq 2t<s$ and $\mathbf{a}$ additionally satisfying $[\mathbf{a}-s,\mathbf{a}+s]\subseteq [-n^{1/3}/4,n^{1/3}/4]$, and all $\varepsilon>0$ with $\alpha\coloneqq \log\varepsilon^{-1}$ satisfying
