{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.170027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_510ba57dd3de2ff04a51a02d397e21f4ef0e16520f15514220bb655199b72b0b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 34 (Admissible Obstacles) of arXiv:2607.03318, 'Continuous Differentiability of the Value Function for Infinite-Dimensional Finite-Horizon'] Let Assumptions \ref{ass:coefficients} and \ref{ass:problem_data} hold. Let $u_\varepsilon$ be the unique mild solution of equation \eqref{eq:hjb_penalized}. A dynamic obstacle $\Phi \in Lip_m([0, T] \times H)$ is said to be \emph{admissible} for the parabolic optimal stopping problem if the associated family of penalty terms, defined by:
