where $C > 0$ is a universal constant.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.668229+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5129ae69a4065e27e3c9eb65f16a40ca22bb6499f61bb951912f7b8570904ac2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm corner process and product process of arXiv:2411.01104, 'Gaussian universality of $p$-adic random matrix products via corners'] Set $t=1/p$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let $i,n$ be integers with $1 \le i  \le n$, $\mu^{(i)} \in \Sig_{n-i+1}$, and $A^{(i)}\in\Mat_{(n-i+1)\times n}(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ be random with $\SN(A^{(i)})=\mu^{(i)}$ fixed and distribution invariant under $\GL_{n-i+1}(\mathbb{Z}_p) \times \GL_n(\mathbb{Z}_p)$ acting on the left and right. Let $A^{(i+1)} \in\Mat_{(n-i) \times n}(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ be the last $n-i$ rows of $A$. Then $\SN(A^{(i+1)})$ is a random element of $\Sig_{n-i}$ with distribution
\begin{equation}
\Pr(\SN(A^{(i+1)}) = \mu^{(i+1)}) = P_{\mu^{(i)}/\mu^{(i+1)}}(1;t) \frac{P_{\mu^{(i+1)}}(t,\ldots,t^{n-i};t)}{P_{\mu^{(i)}}(1,\ldots,t^{n-i};t)}.
\end{equation} 

\item Let $A\in\Mat_{n\times m}(\mathbb{Z}_p)$ be the same as (ex: nxm corner of invertible of the paper). Then the distribution of $\SN(A)$ is given by the Hall-Littlewood measure
$$\mathbf{P}(\SN(A)=\lambda)=\frac{P_\lambda(1,t,\ldots,t^{n-1};t)Q_\lambda(t^{m-n-1},\ldots,t^{N-n};t)}{\Pi_t(1,t,\ldots,t^{n-1};t^{m-n-1},\ldots,t^{N-n})}$$
Here $Q_\lambda$ is the Hall-Littlewood $Q$ polynomial, and $\Pi_t$ is the Cauchy Kernel, see [Macdonald].
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.865752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_512a11dfa7ee4a9e4dd551a6da2b53d622f5f62007703cf330f1d6bf9a9260d9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th1 of arXiv:2510.10299, '\Large Sparse graphs and their Benjamini-Schramm limits: a spectral tour'] Let $(U_1,\ldots,U_d)$ be Haar distributed on $\mathrm{U}_n, \mathrm{O}_n$ or $\mathrm{S}_n$, and let $\rho_n$ be the random representation of $\mathrm{F}_d = \langle g_1, \ldots, g_d \rangle$ of dimension $k_n = n^q$ defined by $ \rho_n(g_i) = \pi_n(U_i)$, with $\pi_n$ as in (equation eq:pin of the paper). Then, as $n \to \infty$, $\rho_n$ restricted to $H_n^\perp$ converges strongly toward $\lambda$ in probability. More precisely, for any $a \in \mathbb {C}[\mathrm{F}_d]$,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{k_n} {\mathrm{Tr}} (\rho_n(a))  = \tau ( a )

\quad \hbox{ and } \quad 

\lim_{n\to \infty} \| \rho_n(a)_{|H_n^\perp} \|   = \| \lambda(a) \|. 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.280491+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_512c4f47204221bb7b28590f3343bc720606833a98b25f65d8324431ee880702","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:gaps of arXiv:2607.10801, 'Relaxation times of non-reversible\\ Markov processes'] \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item The \emph{spectral gap} of $(\mathcal{L},\dom(\mathcal{L})$ is defined as 
            \begin{equation}
                \gap(\mathcal{L})\ =\ \inf\left\{\Re(\lambda):\lambda\in\spec(-\mathcal{L}|_{\mathcal{D}_0})\right\}\,.
            \end{equation}
\item The \emph{Poincaré constant} of $(\mathcal{L},\dom(\mathcal{L}))$ is defined as 
            \begin{equation}
                \lambda(\mathcal{L})\ = \ \inf\left\{\frac{\mathcal{E}_1^\mathcal{L}(f,f)}{\norm{f}^2}:f\in \mathcal{D}_0\setminus\{0\}\right\}\,.
            \end{equation}
        \item The \emph{singular value gap} of $(\mathcal{L},\dom(\mathcal{L}))$ is defined as 
            \begin{equation}
                \sing(\mathcal{L})\ =\ \inf\left\{\frac{\mathcal{E}_2^\mathcal{L}(f,f)^{1/2}}{\norm{f}}:f\in \mathcal{D}_0\setminus\{0\}\right\}\,.

            \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:26:26.774007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_512d8424731cd98ab30f1d76f2ae9765bfe565431dcbbb7bc0da90dee440de77","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:sc-dual-Woodbury of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] In the setting of Theorem~(thm:sc-dual-Darboux of the paper), suppose
