{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.293386+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5182e62c44e37c808e4fa30046846e9804e1fa4bf337424f0a6785a14cf4c749","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition type_stochastic of arXiv:2411.05235, 'A comparative study of three mathematical approaches applied to the reversal of AMR'] \textbf{Stability of an Equilibrium Point of a Stochastic Differential Equation.}\\
Let $\xi$ be an equilibrium point of ((SDE of the paper)). We say that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\xi$ is a stable equilibrium point in probability of ((SDE of the paper)) if and only if for every $\epsilon > 0$ and $r \in (0, 1)$ there exists a $\delta > 0$ such that for all $x_0 \in I$, $|x_0 - \xi| < \delta$, it holds that $$\mathbb{P}\Big(|X(\cdot, t; x_0) - \xi| < \epsilon, \text{ for all } t \in \mathbb{R}_{0}^{+}\Big) \geq 1 - r.$$ In the contrary case, we say that $\xi$ is an unstable equilibrium point of ((SDE of the paper)).
    \item $\xi$ is an asymptotically stable equilibrium point in probability of ((SDE of the paper)) if and only if $\xi$ is a stable equilibrium point in probability of ((SDE of the paper)) and for every $r \in (0, 1)$ there exists a $\delta > 0$ such that for all $x_0 \in I$, $|x_0 - \xi| < \delta$, it holds that $$\mathbb{P}\Big(\lim_{t \to \infty} X(\cdot, t; x_0) = \xi \Big) \geq 1 - r.$$   
    \item $\xi$ is a globally asymptotically stable equilibrium point in probability of ((SDE of the paper)) if and only if $\xi$ is a stable equilibrium point in probability of ((SDE of the paper)) and for all $x_0 \in I$, it holds that $$\mathbb{P}\Big(\lim_{t \to \infty} X(\cdot, t; x_0) = \xi \Big) = 1.$$
\end{enumerate}
Where $X(\cdot, t; x_0)$ represents the global solution $X$ of ((SDE of the paper)) that starts at $x_0$ at time $t.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.947853+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5187569ca0a13352221ca893aaa1dde7bcca40bc8d7152c291ee526bd54c2b68","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main2 of arXiv:2412.03511, 'Near-optimal shattering in the Ising pure $p$-spin and \\ rarity of solutions returned by '] Let $\beta \in(\bar{\beta}_{d}, \beta_c)$, $L > 0$, $B > 0$. There exists $\varepsilon = \varepsilon(\beta,\xi)>0$ such that for all $\beta' \in [(1-\varepsilon)\beta,\beta]$ there exists a subset $E(\bm{G}) \subset S_{\beta'}(\bm{G})$ depending only on $\beta', L, \xi, \bm{G}$ with exponentially small Gibbs mass relative to $\mu_{\beta,\bm{G}}$:
  \begin{equation}
     \operatorname{\mathbb{E}} \mu_{\beta,\bm{G}}(E(\bm{G})) \le e^{-c_0N}\,,~~~ c_0 = c_0(\beta,L,\xi)>0\,, 
\end{equation}
 such that if $\mathcal{A}$ is a $L$-Lipschitz algorithm satisfying $\sup_{\bm{G}}\|\mathcal{A}(\bm{G})\| \le \sqrt{BN}$ and $\operatorname{\mathbb{P}}(\mathcal{A}(\bm{G}) \notin S_{\beta}(\bm{G})) \le \varepsilon_0$ for some $\varepsilon_0= \varepsilon_0(B,\beta,\xi)>0$, we have 
 \begin{equation}
     \operatorname{\mathbb{P}}\big(\mathcal{A}(\bm{G}) \in E(\bm{G})\big) + 4 \operatorname{\mathbb{P}}\big(\mathcal{A}(\bm{G}) \notin S_{\beta'}(\bm{G})\big) \ge 1- e^{-c_1N}\,,~~~ c_1 = c_1(\beta,L,\xi)>0\,. 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.748385+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_518c0c3e0bcbb1ee4eb0af1949e330f4d739448aed7fd2fe9ae879bc55532477","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cumulants_division of arXiv:2608.28169, 'characteristic polynomial of self-normalized random matrices'] Let $(V_{\ell, m})_{\ell, m \geqslant 1}$ be a family of random variables such that the families $\left( V_{\ell, \cdot} \right)_\ell$ 
are independent.
Let us define the family $(Z_k)_{k \geqslant 1}$ by $Z_k = \sum_{\ell | k} V_{\ell, k/\ell}$. Then one has
    \begin{equation}

