{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.221155+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51bce0638aebf7ab3081193335951a8ef108beec81ae5d78783fd1411b5fda1a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 (A trajectory of triangles) of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] Given $M \subset M_0$. A \textit{trajectory} on $M$ is an arrangement of triangles in $Tri(M)$ obtained by connecting triangles via common edges. In general, arrangements are non-linear (i.e., they contain branches). $TRAJ(M)$ denotes the collection of all trajectories on $M$, i.e,
\begin{equation*}
TRAJ(M):=\{ \psi \subset Tri(M) \ | \  \text{$\psi$ is a trajectory on $M$}\}.
\end{equation*}
Given $\psi \in TRAJ(M)$ and $abc \in Tri(M)$. \underline{We write $abc \in \psi$ if $abc$ is contained} \underline{in $\psi$}. Given $\psi_1, \psi_2 \in TRAJ(M)$. \underline{We write $\psi_1 \subset \psi_2$ if $abc \in \psi_2$ for $\forall abc \in \psi_1$}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:28:08.697261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51bd9887994052709b1303cc044edf8bd2d4426c4634977c4a1facbe4e145757","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 8 (Wasserstein characterization of substitution-MASD) of arXiv:2607.29560, 'Tractable Relaxations of Multivariate Stochastic Dominance via Optimal Transport and CVaR'] For every $0<\gamma\le1$,
\begin{equation}

P\preceq_{s,\lambda_\beta,\gamma}^{\rm CSD}Q
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
P\preceq_{\gamma,\beta}^{\rm MASD}Q,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

P\preceq_{\gamma,\beta}^{\rm MASD}Q
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\Delta_\beta(P,Q)
\ge
\frac{1-\gamma}{1+\gamma}W_{1,\beta}(P,Q).
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

Q\text{ FOSD-dominates }P
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\Delta_\beta(P,Q)=W_{1,\beta}(P,Q).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.338258+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51bdf2d19aa6ef23c7b5e0297966aca5bb3d63cd83700740d21c22d18660b7c1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 (Lemma 1.2.3, \cite{nualart2006malliavin}.) of arXiv:2506.22213, 'No arbitrage assumption implies the differentiability of derivative pricing function'] Let $(F^n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a sequence of random variables in $\bbD^{1,2}$ that converges to $F$ in $L^2(\Omega)$ and
