{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.894578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51c9dcc5377e3815b3a3b41c4d31f7d17c6d7cf4dbdc579f08e65ec7e6d37a51","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 (Polynomials and their coefficients) of arXiv:2606.19115, 'Finite free perpetuities'] Every polynomial $p\in \tilde\pols_{\leq n}(\C)$ can be written uniquely in the form
\begin{align*}
    p(z) &= \sum_{k=0}^n (-1)^k \binom{n}{k} \coef{k}{n}(p) z^{n-k}.
\end{align*}
In particular, if $p(z) = \prod_{i=1}^n (z-\lambda_i) \in \pols_n(\C)$, then
\[ \coef{k}{n}(p) = \binom{n}{k}^{-1} \sum_{1 \le i_1 < \dots < i_k \le n} \lambda_{i_1} \cdots \lambda_{i_k} \]
for $k\in\{1,\dots,n\}$, while $\coef{0}{n}(p) = 1$.

We say that a polynomial $p\in\tilde\pols_{\leq n}(\C)$ is $d$-monic, $d\in\{0,\ldots,n\}$, if $\coef{d}{n}(p)=1$. In particular, monic polynomials are $0$-monic. More precisely, this notion depends on both \(n\) and \(d\). We suppress the parameter \(n\) when it is clear from the context.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.453903+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51caa15cf49f6ab6663d7d135809635d72af6512e80e466eb5a09c8a007c9218","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem dimKd'' of arXiv:2505.11721, 'Variational principles for Hausdorff and packing dimensions of fractal percolation on self'] If the linear parts of the affine maps $f_i$ are equal, with probability~1, conditional on $\{K_\cdot\neq \emptyset\}$, one has  
\begin{equation*}
\dim_H K_\omega=\max\{\dim_H(\mu_\omega): \, \text{$\mu_\omega$ is a non degenerate MM supported on $K_\omega$}\}.
\end{equation*} 
Moreover, the maximum is attained at a unique Mandelbrot measure. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.026513+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51ce9ca79ac86c6579ae0ad8433fdf926204a1f2ae60df397b8527053a781619","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:D-gamma of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Under Assumptions~(ass:LR of the paper), (ass:diff2 of the paper), and~(ass:SC of the paper), suppose in addition that $\Phi \in \mathbb{D}^{1,p_0}(L^2([0,t]; L_2(U,H)))$ for some $p_0 \geq 2$. Then the Malliavin covariance operator $\gamma_t$ is trace class a.s.\ and, viewed as an $L_2(H,H)$-valued random variable, belongs to $\mathbb{D}^{1,p_0/2}(L_2(H,H))$; for $r \in [0, t]$, its Malliavin derivative is given, as an identity in $L_2(U, L_2(H,H))$, by
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{D}_r\gamma_t = \int_0^t \Bigl[(\mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s)\,\Phi_s^* + \Phi_s\,(\mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s)^*\Bigr]\,\mathrm{d}s,
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_s := Y(t,s)\,\mathcal{B}(s, X(s)) \in L_2(U, H)$. Since the product is quadratic in $\Phi$, the exponent drops by a factor of two, so that the membership $\gamma_t \in \mathbb{D}^{1,2}(L_2(H,H))$ follows from this route precisely when $p_0 \geq 4$, and at smaller $p_0$ it is supplied instead by~(equation eq:CM-gamma-D12 of the paper) of clause~\textup{(iii)} of Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper). The Malliavin derivative $\mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s$ takes different forms depending on the relative position of $r$ and $s$:

\begin{enumerate}[\textup{Case} 1:]
  \item \textup{($s < r$):}
  \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s = \mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)\,\mathcal{B}(s, X(s)),
  \end{equation}
  where $\mathcal{Z}(t,s;r) = \mathcal{D}_r Y(t,s)$ is the Malliavin--second variation (Lemma (lemma:DtYts of the paper)).

  \item \textup{($s > r$):} By the product rule,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s = \mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)\,\mathcal{B}(s, X(s)) + Y(t,s)\,\mathcal{B}'_u(s, X(s))\bigl(Y(s,r)\,\mathcal{B}(r, X(r))\bigr),
  \end{equation}
  where the second term arises from the chain rule:
  \[
    \mathcal{D}_r[\mathcal{B}(s, X(s))] = \mathcal{B}'_u(s, X(s))\,\mathcal{D}_r X(s) = \mathcal{B}'_u(s, X(s))\,Y(s,r)\,\mathcal{B}(r, X(r)).
  \]
\end{enumerate}
The boundary $\{s = r\}$ is a single point, hence a measure-zero subset of the integration domain in (equation eq:D-gamma-formula of the paper), and its pointwise value does not affect the resulting integral.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:29:14.968587+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51cfd4f41efebf2e2508b3aa77f0c8f9ce157586e6958a08f2ecec382baadfe4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition procha3 of arXiv:2606.15203, 'The existence of invariant sublinear expectations for $G\text{-}$SDEs'] If Assumption (Ass1 of the paper) and (Ass2 of the paper) hold, then there exist constants $\bar{\alpha} \in (0,1)$, $\beta > 0$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}$, such that for all $k\geq n$,
