{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.525550+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51d89db67bc8dd4a852d5dc18f69f354b466b8c6c1d77ab523c06a9361bf517c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:transience_r_def of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{def:transience_renorm_scheme}
    Let $\varepsilon > 0$, and let $r_0 \in \mathbf{N}$ be a large constant.

    We define $s=s(\varepsilon)$ to be the least integer satisfying simultaneously $2(1+s)^2\ge (1+\varepsilon)/\varepsilon$.

    We set $
    \bar r_0=r_0$, set $m_0=0$, and for $i \geq 1$ we iteratively define 

    \begin{equation}

        m_i 
        =
        2 
        (i+s)^2
        \quad
        \text{and}
        \quad
        r_i
        =
        r_{i-1}
        m_i.
    \end{equation}

    Let $((\Tile{i}{j})_{j \geq 0})_{i \geq 0}$ be the renormalisation scheme associated to the renormalisation scales $(r_i)_{i \geq 0}$ in \thref{def:recursive_tiles}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.648889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51d8ed77362f4c440b0fa3933698a7fe69786ae1a528b4355cd06d85ba21beb2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:kmonotone of arXiv:2608.11378, 'On the most likely geodesic in last passage percolation'] Take $\gamma \in \Gamma_n$ and let $\hat{\gamma}$ be the result of applying the bump move of Figure~(fig:pathbump of the paper) to $\gamma$. Then $C_{\hat{\gamma}}^{1, 1} > C_{\gamma}^{1, 1}$. Similarly if $\hat{\gamma}$ is the result of the unwind move of Figure~(fig:pathunwind of the paper).
\begin{proof}
    We should show using Lemma~(lem:areaformula of the paper) that $C_{\hat{\gamma}}^{1, 1} - C_{\gamma}^{1, 1} > 0$. Suppose the corner which is bumped is above the diagonal and thus initially pointed south-east. Then one sees that $D(\hat{\gamma}, \gamma)$ has a single negative component lying above the diagonal. The difference $y - x - 1$ is positive here, so the area formula evaluates to a positive quantity. When the corner is below the diagonal, the area is positive, while $y - x - 1$ is negative. This once again gives a positive difference.

    Following the same idea, suppose $\hat{\gamma}$ is the result of unwinding a portion from below to above the diagonal. Then $D(\hat{\gamma}, \gamma)$ is formed from a single negative region. The portion below the diagonal has its reflection about the diagonal also in $D(\hat{\gamma}, \gamma)$. If $(x, y)$ is the north-west corner of a box above the diagonal with contribution $y - x - 1$, then the reflection has north-west corner $(y - 1, x + 1)$  and opposite contribution $(x + 1) - (y - 1) - 1 = 1 + x - y$. The total contributions of these two areas cancel, and what is left is negative area above the diagonal. Here $y - x - 1$ is positive, and $C_{\hat{\gamma}}^{1, 1} - C_{\gamma}^{1, 1} > 0$.
\end{proof}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.767196+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51d9c15d5597278782d4dfc62564f11409cc0f048404c1869ff8d57824aaa996","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 37 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Consider the setup described in Section~\ref{subsec:tightness_setup}, and for each $\epsilon \in (0,1]$ let $\rmetapprox{\epsilon}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\Gamma} = (\rmetapproxres{\epsilon}{V}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\Gamma})_{V \in \metregions}$ be as defined in Section~\ref{se:rmet_approx_scheme}. Let $\median{\epsilon} > 0$ be as defined Section~\ref{se:approx_scheme_general_def}.
Let $V \in \metregions$ be chosen according to some probability distribution given $\Gamma$, and for each $\epsilon$ let $V_\epsilon \in \metregions$ be such that $V_\epsilon \supseteq V$.

Then each sequence $(\epsilon_n)$ with $\epsilon_n \to 0$ contains a subsequence $(\epsilon_{n_m})$ along which we have the weak convergence of the laws of $\median{\epsilon}^{-1} \rmetapproxres{\epsilon}{V_{\epsilon}}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\Gamma}\big|_{(\ol{V} \cap \Upsilon_\Gamma) \times (\ol{V} \cap \Upsilon_\Gamma)}$ in the topology described in Section~\ref{subsec:tightness_setup}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.010669+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51d9e3332e82eaa6e0a67de544e8cc2ed1e6a14cab0b78b430ee47541b21c0eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2 (Theorem 1-3, Siegel and Xu\cite{siegel2024}) of arXiv:2605.04168, 'Error analysis for learning fractional stochastic differential equations with applications'] Let $\phi$ be a bounded activation function and $D_\phi\subset {L}^p([0,T]\times[-N,N]^d)$ the corresponding uniformly bounded dictionary. Then for $f\in B_1(D_\phi),$ we have
\begin{align}
\inf_{f_n\in \Sigma_{n,1}(\phi)}\Vert f-f_n\Vert_{W^{r,p}(K)}\leq \frac{C}{\sqrt{n}},\end{align}
where the constant $C>0$ depends on $\sup_{d\in D_\phi}\Vert d\Vert_{W^{r,p}(K)}$, $r$, $p$, and $K$, but not on $n.$

Moreover, in the case of $k$-ReLU activation there exists a constant $M = M(p, k, d) > 0$ such that, for all $f \in B_1(D_k)$, we have
\begin{align}
\inf_{f_n \in \Sigma_{n, M}(D_k)} \| f - f_n \|_{{L}^p([0,T]\times[-N,N]^d)} \le C n^{-\frac{1}{2} - \frac{pk+1}{p(d+1)}}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.779989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51da2ce9e79e7356622ed4cc1a710a2459a1d1a961347e17a184eb4120824021","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:gauge-link of arXiv:2608.01253, 'The pseudo-quantum representation of \\finite reversible Markov chains'] Let $\mathcal{E}:=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(i^{\,n-k}\bigr)_{k=0}^{n}$, a fixed unitary diagonal
matrix independent of $t$, and let $\lvert \psi_\theta\rangle:=e^{-i\thetaJ_x}\lvert n\rangle$ be
the register coherent state of the imaginary slice. Then

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}\,J_x\,\mathcal{E}^{-1}=J_y,\qquad \mathcal{E}\lvert n\rangle=\lvert n\rangle,\qquad
\mathcal{E}\ket{\psi_{\theta}}=e^{-i\thetaJ_y}\lvert n\rangle,

\end{equation}

so $\ket{\sqrt{\pi_t}}=\mathcal{E}\ket{\psi_{\theta(t)}}$ and
$\sigma^{\mathrm{FR}}_t=\mathcal{E}\,\ketbra{\psi_{\theta(t)}}{\psi_{\theta(t)}}\,\mathcal{E}^{*}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.988063+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51da516e3135b51d802b4389c4c69f0fbd9a18ee10a6b4fc4db586a5c25d56f3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2604.00742, 'Delayed Logistic equation as a limit\\ of long memory Markov chains'] We have for every $T>0$ that
