{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.923787+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5244c13283cce44db68498d8c5e3f638f6d43c16aa41056a1d0fc34980a1d51f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p:k-walk_bound of arXiv:2412.02087, 'The spectral distribution of random graphs with given degree sequences'] [Bounds on an arbitrary $k$-walk]

Suppose an arbitrary $k$-walk consists of $r$ ``good" repeated edges, $p$ ``good" simple edges, and $L$ ``bad" edges. Then the following bound is achieved
    \begin{align}\begin{split}
        &\mathbb{E}\biggl[\left(H_1-\frac{1}{D-1}\right)^{\alpha_1}\left(H_2-\frac{1}{D-1}\right)^{\alpha_2}\cdots\left(H_r-\frac{1}{D-1}\right)^{\alpha_r}
        \\&\left(H_{r+1}-\frac{1}{D-1}\right)\cdots\left(H_{r+p}-\frac{1}{D-1}\right)
