{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.055079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5297ff3b09bf4fa404237f3cd3c32ef728de1ac758ce7b6e83e338794c47f951","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (Limit of martingale) of arXiv:2607.01629, 'Quadratic fluctuations of speed-change Kawasaki dynamics'] For $f\in C^\infty\left(\mathbb{T}^{2d}\right)$, the sequence of martingales $\{\mathcal M^N(f)\}_{N \in \N}$ admits a subsequential limit $\mathcal M(f)$ as $N\to\infty$ in $D([0, T], \mathbb{R})$. Every such limit point $\mathcal M(f)$ is a martingale in $C\left([0,T],\R\right)$ and satisfies
