{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.993928+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52b88d3a565dfc55c2bae441a3e5db97ad272bc8378892948df85587751150f7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2604.01685, 'Probability with Measure\footnote{Lecture notes. University of Ljubljana.}'] Let $\AA\subset 2^\Omega$; then 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_\Omega(\AA):=\cap\{\FF\in 2^{2^\Omega}:\FF\text{ a $\sigma$-field on }\Omega\text{ and }\AA\subset \FF\}
\end{equation*}
is called the $\sigma$-field generated on $\Omega$ by $\AA$ [remark that $2^\Omega$ is certainly a $\sigma$-field on $\Omega$ that contains $\AA$ so the intersection is of a non-empty family].  For two $\sigma$-fields $\BB_1$ and $\BB_2$ on $\Omega$ set $\BB_1\lor \BB_2:=\sigma_\Omega(\BB_1\cup \BB_2)$ and call it the join of $\BB_1$ and $\BB_2$. More generally, for a family $(\BB_\lambda)_{\lambda\in \Lambda}$ of $\sigma$-fields on $\Omega$ we set $\lor_{\lambda\in \Lambda}\BB_\lambda:=\sigma_\Omega(\cup_{\lambda\in\Lambda}\BB_\lambda)$ and call it the join of said family.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.291393+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52b9097af3c9bfa72a0a6bbea0a78b985fea63de2198f5c991ba1ae2121220d6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l2 of arXiv:2411.05204, '\Large\bf Sample Path Properties of the Fractional Wiener--Weierstrass Bridge'] Let $0<H<1/2$ and $h:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ be a nonnegative step function whose support is $I$, which is a finite union of bounded open intervals. Denote by
\begin{align}h_*:=\min h(I)=\min (h([0,\infty))\setminus\{0\}).
\end{align}Then for all $\delta\leq h_*$,
\begin{align}
{\mathbb E}\left[\Big|\int_I hdW_H\Big|^2\right]\geq \frac{\delta^2|I|^{2H}}{L}+{\mathbb E}\left[\Big|\int_I (h-\delta) dW_H\Big|^2\right].
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.578540+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52b987647b1df87fbfa3d163704cb17ab6dfe3803cc095eb801e24798dfd0a0f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition mart-m2 of arXiv:2512.10625, 'Bessel and Dunkl processes with drift'] For all $x,\lambda\in\mathbb R^N$ and $j,l=1,\ldots,N$, 
  $$  L_k^{\lambda,Dunkl}m_{2; j,l}(x) =\delta_{j,l} + \lambda_j m_{1,l}(x) +\lambda_l m_{1,j}(x),$$
Moreover, if $(X_t^\lambda)_{t\ge0}$ is a  Dunkl processs
  with drift $\lambda$  such that the starting distribution  $P_{X_0}^\lambda\in M^1(\mathbb R^N)$ has second moments,
  then for all $j,l=1,\ldots,N$,
  \begin{align}
  ( m_{2; j,l}(X_t^\lambda)- E( m_{2; j,l}(X_t^\lambda)) &- m_{1;l}(X_t^\lambda)E( m_{1;j}(X_t^\lambda))- m_{1;j}(X_t^\lambda)E( m_{1;l}(X_t^\lambda))
  \notag \\ &+2E( m_{1;l}(X_t^\lambda))E( m_{1;j}(X_t^\lambda)) )_{t\ge0}
  \end{align}
  is a martingale.
  In particular, for $P_{X_0}^\lambda=\delta_0$ and $l,j=1,\ldots,N$,
  \begin{equation}
    E(m_{2; l,j}(X_t^\lambda))= t\delta_{j,l}+t^2 \lambda_j\lambda_l.\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.732309+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52ba0e0ebe090400201ba4c2898a930eecf77ce39d0fd99a541453dc570d5380","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Suppose that $(\Hb, \Hp, \bar{\mathcal{W}})$ is a solution of the system
	\begin{align*}
		\Hb &= \mathcal{P}_\gamma \mathbf{1}_+ [(\partial_x \Hb)^2 + \<b0>] + \mathcal{G} \psi
		\\
		\Hp &= \mathcal{P}_\gamma \mathbf{1}_+ [(\partial_x \Hp)^2 + \<p0>] + \mathcal{G} \psi
		\\
		\bar{\mathcal{W}} &= \mathcal{P}_{\tilde{\gamma}} \mathbf{1}_+ [(\partial_x \bar{\mathcal{W}} + \partial_x \mathcal{V}_{\<0t>} )(\partial_x \Hb + \partial_x \Hp) + \<1t> ( \partial_x \Hb - \<b1> + \partial_x \Hp - \<p1>) + \<b2t> + \<p2t>].
	\end{align*}
	Then if $\mathcal{W} = \bar{\mathcal{W}} + \mathcal{P}_{\tilde{\gamma}} \mathbf{1}_+ \<0t>$, we have that $(\Hb, \Hp, \mathcal{W})$ is a solution of the system of Lemma~\ref{lem:recon_iden}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.727036+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52ba521617e4d8a1d7212516d2f70cac413a4b8156a44cf6c652f8659bdb524f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ReductionLemma of arXiv:2411.19163, '\bfseries Random polytopes in convex bodies:\\ Bridging the gap between extremal container'] For any $\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ let $\mathbf{v}=(v_1,\ldots,v_m)\in \mathbb{S}^{m-1}_+\subset W_m$ be such that $\mathbf{w} = (v_1 \mathbf{u}_1,\ldots,v_m\mathbf{u}_m)$ for some $\mathbf{u}_i\in\mathbb{S}^{d_i-1}$, $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$. Then, for all $\operatorname{bd}=(d_1,\ldots,d_m)$ with $d_i\ge 1$ and all ${\boldsymbol{\beta}}=(\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_m)$ with $\beta_i>-1$, $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ we have that
        \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{P}_{d}(Z_{\operatorname{bd}}\cap H^+(\mathbf{w},s);{\boldsymbol{\beta}},\operatorname{bd}) = \left(\prod_{i=1}^m \frac{c_{\beta_i,d_i}}{c_{\beta_i,d_i-1}c_{\beta_i}}\right) \mathbb{P}_{m}(C^+(\mathbf{v},s);(\operatorname{bd}-\mathbbm{1})/2+{\boldsymbol{\beta}}),
    \end{equation*}
    and
    \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{Vol}_{d-1}(Z_{\operatorname{bd}}\cap H(\mathbf{w},s); {\boldsymbol{\beta}},\operatorname{bd})=\left(\prod_{i=1}^m \frac{c_{\beta_i,d_i}}{c_{\beta_i,d_i-1}c_{\beta_i}}\right)\operatorname{Vol}_{m-1}(C(\mathbf{v},s);(\operatorname{bd}-\mathbbm{1})/2+{\boldsymbol{\beta}}),
    \end{equation}
    where $c_{\beta,d}$ and $c_{\beta}$ are the normalizing constants of the beta density given by (equation eq:CbetaConstant of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.496232+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52bade6c76c13a511969ff56173c3e938533d43d2fb58b041d0276f33517a307","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l-cross of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] For each $n\geq 0$, the function $L_{n}$ has two fixed
points at $0$ and $1$, and a unique fixed point in $(0,1)$. If we denote the
latter by $c_{n}$, then
\begin{equation}
L_{n}(x)<x\quad \text{for }x\in (0,c_{n}),\qquad L_{n}(x)>x\quad \text{for }
x\in (c_{n},1).  
\end{equation}

Moreover, there exists a closed interval $O_{n}\subset (0,1)$ such that $
c_{n}\in O_{n}$ and 
\begin{equation}
L_{n}^{\prime }(x)\geq 1\quad \text{for all }x\in O_{n}.  
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.145360+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52bbb483199f7dc2349cbe0e28a7608b60c4c2938180f0926291cf34f0800157","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:polyroot of arXiv:2602.00988, 'Wealth exchange under ceiling and flooring constraints: a modified Bennati–Dragulescu–Yako'] Given $0 \leq a < \mu < b$ with $a, \mu, b \in \mathbb N$, and define for $x \geq 0$ the polynomial function
\begin{equation}
f(x) =  (b-\mu)\,x^{b+2-a} + (\mu-b-1)\,x^{b+1-a} + (\mu-a+1)\,x + a - \mu.
\end{equation}
Then \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item when $\mu<\frac{a+b}{2}$, $f(x)$ has a unique root within $(0,1)$,
\item when $\mu=\frac{a+b}{2}$, $f(x)$ has a unique root at $x= 1$,
\item when $\mu>\frac{a+b}{2}$, $f(x)$ has a unique root within $(1,\infty)$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.650557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_52bbba48ae31ed27ce6413f8e359225a082f879ec222d691dfd3d9c05ed2a3da","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Determinant of affine meander matrix of arXiv:2506.01306, 'Asymptotic of Coulomb gas integral, Temperley-Lieb type algebras and pure partition functi'] Let $\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_n^d$ denote the Gram matrix for the standard module with $n$ nodes and $d = n - 2m$ through-lines. Then:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\det \widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_n^0 &= \prod_{k=1}^{n/2} \left(a^2 - 4 \cos^2\left( \frac{4k\pi}{\kappa} \right) \right) \binom{n}{n/2 - k}, \\
\det \widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_n^d &= \prod_{k=1}^{m} \left(4 - 4 \cos^2\left( \frac{4(k + d/2)\pi}{\kappa} \right) \right) \binom{n}{(n-d)/2 - k}, \quad \text{for } d > 0, \\
