{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.952141+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5307548a90f053b7639aedd6ae5d102c1326f1c12765f0e54728187f6b9b73ae","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-12proper of arXiv:2412.03067, 'Geodesic Trees and Exceptional Directions in FPP on Hyperbolic Groups'] We fix the base-point $1$.
There exists a full measure subset $\Omega_0 \subset \Omega$ such that
for all  $\omega \in \Omega_0$, 
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $i_\omega^F: F(1,\omega) \to \Gamma$ is  proper in the sense of Lemma~(lem-proper of the paper), 
	\item $i_\omega^F: F(1,\omega) \to \Gamma$ is uniformly 2-proper in the sense of Definition~(def-2proper of the paper).
\end{enumerate}
Hence for  all  $\omega \in \Omega_0$, 
$i_\omega^F: F(1,\omega) \to \Gamma$ admits a Cannon-Thurston map. 

Further, given $\xi \in {\partial G}$, and all $\omega \in \Omega_0 \cap \Omega_{\xi}$, 
\begin{enumerate}
\item $i_\omega^T: T(\xi,\omega) \to \Gamma$ is  proper in the sense of Lemma~(lem-proper of the paper), 
\item $i_\omega^T: T(\xi,\omega) \to \Gamma$ is  2-proper in the sense of Definition~(def-2proper of the paper).
\end{enumerate}
Hence for  all $\xi \in {\partial G}$, and $\omega \in \Omega_0 \cap \Omega_{\xi}$, 
$i_\omega^T: T(\xi,\omega) \to \Gamma$ admits a Cannon-Thurston map.
Finally, (after possibly passing to a further full measure subset)
 the (boundary values of the) Cannon-Thurston maps $\partial i_\omega^F: \partial F(1,\omega) \to {\partial G}$ and $\partial i_\omega^T: \partial T(\xi,\omega) \to {\partial G}$ are surjective.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.029511+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53099845e7a584de5f23c48d3b6c811ae138a7e8522a53e697ed1ff648f70381","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2606.07096, 'Non-Selection of Lagrangian Trajectories in the Zero-Noise Limit for a Class of Stochastic'] Let $a_0>0$ and $\delta>0$ be chosen sufficiently small and let $(\ell_q)_{q \in \N}$, $(\kappa_q)_{q \in \N}$, $(t_q)_{q \in \N}$ be defined as in Section~\ref{subsec:parameters}; let $u$ be the velocity field constructed in Section~\ref{subsec:construction_velocity} and $(u_q)_{q\in\N}$ be given by~\eqref{eq:defn_uq}. Let $A^{(j)}\subset \T^2$ be the set defined in~\eqref{d:A-j} for any $j \in \N$. 

Then, for any $q\in \N$ there exists $\bar{q}= \bar{q} (q) \to \infty$ as $q \to \infty$, such that the following property holds true for any $q\in\N$. If $X^{\kappa_q}(y)$ is a solution to~\eqref{eq:pathwise-sde} and $\|\xi\|_\infty \leq q$, then for all $x\in A^{(\bar{q})}$ it holds that
\begin{equation}

\sup_{t\in [0,t_q]} \left|X_t^{\kappa_q}(y) - X_t^q (x) \right| \leq 2 q^2 \kappa_q \norm{\xi}_{\infty} + \frac{\ell_q}{2q}\quad \text{for all $y$ such that}\quad |y-x| < q\kappa_q \norm{\xi}_{\infty} + \frac{\ell_q}{2q^2} \,,
\end{equation}
where $X_t^q$  denotes the Lagrangian flow  associated with the smooth vector field $u_q$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.521717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_530aa4d78cd7319263c1724d4e40a07144f67faab6466492f41f51ef2938363e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn strong inequality-1 of arXiv:2512.18353, 'A note on the planar Skorokhod embedding problem'] The Hilbert transform of a $2\pi$-periodic function $f$ is defined
by 

\[
\mathcal{H}\{f\}(x):=\frac{1}{2\pi}P.V\left\{ \int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x-t)\cot({\textstyle \frac{t}{2}})dt\right\} =\frac{1}{2\pi}\lim_{\eta\rightarrow0}\int_{\eta\leq|t|\leq\pi}f(x-t)\cot({\textstyle \frac{t}{2}})dt
\]
where $P.V$ denotes the Cauchy principal value. Note that the Hilbert
transform is an anti-involution on zero-mean functions, i.e. $\mathcal{H}^{2}=-id$.
The Hilbert transform exists a.e. for any function $f\in L^{p}(\mathbb{T})$
with $p\geq1$. In addition, if $p>1$ then 
\begin{equation}
\Vert H\{f\}\Vert_{p}\leq\lambda_{p}\Vert f\Vert_{p}
\end{equation}
for some positive constant $\lambda_{p}$. See [duren2000theory].","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.126741+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_530c0dfe87e4a195c1931ab96b53e6d85aa29436be4764603d9e13f85e0a8e6b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 (Smooth lattice approximate identity) of arXiv:2608.22342, 'Completely Positive Entropy and Fourier Central Limit Theorems for Stationary Random Measu'] For every $h\in C_c^\infty(\bR^d;\bC)$ and every $N\geq1$, there is
$C_{h,N}<\infty$ such that, for $R\geq1$ and $s\in\bT^d$,
\begin{equation}

    K_R^h(s)
    \leq C_{h,N}R^d
      \bigl(1+R\|s\|_{\bT^d}\bigr)^{-N}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

    \int_{\bT^d}K_R^h(s)\,ds
    =R^{-d}\sum_{k\in\bZ^d}|h(k/R)|^2
    \longrightarrow\|h\|_2^2.
\end{equation}
Consequently, if $q\in L^1(\bT^d)$, the chosen representative satisfies
$q(0)=0$, and $0$ is a Lebesgue point in the sense of
\eqref{eq:torus-lebesgue-point-definition}, then
\begin{equation}

    \int_{\bT^d}K_R^h(s)q(s)\,ds\longrightarrow0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.561908+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_530dcd3f506296eec729bcafd279c0d19fcd1172fb063f3fb9db6465b9052248","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem existence_riccati of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] Let $\bm p \in \mathcal B\,$. Assuming that $\mathbb{E}[\exp(\bracket{\bm p}{\sigW[t,T]})] \neq 0$ for all $t \in [0,T]\,$, and letting $\bm \psi$, $\varepsilon$, and $r$ be as in Theorem (theorem:log_analyticity_general of the paper), we have
    \begin{enumerate}
