{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.753553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_541eb751b58e31794b67bce20d44eb8358f15e0920a8444cce4d0ad6e917c161","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 12 of arXiv:2511.02763, 'The Cayley-Moser problem with Poissonian arrival of offers'] Suppose $M=+\infty$ and the function $\phi(x)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} \frac{x\phi(x)}{\int_x^{\infty}\phi(u)du}=p,\;\;\;p>0.
\end{equation}	
Then $\mu(t)$ is regularly varying with exponent $\frac{1}{p+2}$, and, in particular, we have
\begin{equation}\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \frac{\mu(t)}{t^c}=\begin{cases}
	+\infty & c< \frac{1}{p+2}\\
	0 & c>\frac{1}{p+2}\end{cases}.\end{equation}
	$Var[S_t]$, as a function of $t$ is regularly varying with exponent $\frac{2}{p+2}$. In particular, we have
	$$\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}\frac{Var[S_t]}{t^c}=\begin{cases}
+\infty & c<\frac{2}{p+2}\\
0 & c>\frac{2}{p+2}
\end{cases}.$$","status":"unverified"}

