{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.382427+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_542013584141c843c34e7a926f7abbc9848a65495d50b7440cae499948d8ab32","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:local_clt of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] As in (equation eq:sigmadef_local_intro of the paper), set
\begin{equation}

    \varsigma_n^2 = \mleft( 1 - p(1-p) \sum_{j=1}^K \beta_j p^{\mathsf{e}_j-2} \sum_{\rho \in \mathcal{V}_j} \mathsf{d}_\rho (\mathsf{d}_\rho-1) \mright)^{-1} \times p (1-p) (n-1).
\end{equation}
Then for any deterministic vertex $v \in [n]$ and any $\varepsilon > 0$, if $X$ is distributed as (equation eq:tilt of the paper)
