where $p_{t,x}$ denotes the biased multivariate Gaussian distribution with mean vector $\upe^{-t}x$ and covariance matrix $\left(1-\upe^{-2t}\right)\Ibf_n$, and $\psi_\kappa^{t,x}(y) := h_\kappa(\frac{y-\upe^{-t} x}{\sqrt{1-\upe^{-2t}}})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.177085+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_547b380d7e79080f69ae1d65b943500dcf81e5d6800b27786d2c8d747daa5c2e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Lyapunov-arguemnt-fails of arXiv:2511.10223, 'Stochastic Reaction Networks Within Interacting Compartments with Content-Dependent Fragme'] Let $N$ denote the compartment model in (equation eq:potentially-explosive of the paper), let $\mathcal N$ denote its state space, and for $n\in\mathcal N$ define
\begin{align*}
    C(n)&:=\sum_{x\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^2} n_x\\
    S(n)&:=\sum_{(e,s)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^2} sn_{(e,s)}\\
    E(n)&:=\sum_{(e,s)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^2} en_{(e,s)}.
\end{align*}
These represent, respectively, the total numbers of compartments, substrate molecules, and enzymes when the system is in state $n$. Let $\mathcal L$ and $\mathcal A$ denote the generators of $N$ and  of the CRN in (equation eq:CRN-projection of the paper), respectively. Suppose that $f$ is some function $f:\mathbb Z^3_{\ge0}\to[0,\infty)$, and suppose $V:\mathcal N\to[0,\infty)$ is defined by $V(n):=f(E(n),S(n),C(n))$.
If $V$ has finite sublevel sets and there exists $c,d$ such that $\mathcal LV(n)\le cV(n)+d$ for all states $n$, then $f$ has finite sublevel sets and $\mathcal Af(e,s,y)\le cf(e,s,y)+d$ for all states $(e,s,y)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.605226+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_547d5e928bc839efe14e0194e24f1e8094f880f9078cfa9da02e52a3c8ce2299","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem EHI:t:KM of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t \geq 0}=(B_{S_t})_{t \geq 0}$ be a subordinate Brownian motion, where $S$ is a subordinator and $B$ is a Brownian motion on $\mathbb{R}^d$, independent of $S$. Assume there exists a $C>0$ such that the jump kernel of $X$ satisfies
    \begin{equation*}
        j(r+1) \leq j(r) \leq C j(r+1) \qquad\mbox{for all $r>1$}.
    \end{equation*}
    Suppose also that the subordinator $S$ satisfies the three following conditions:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=A-\arabic*]
        \item The potential measure of $S$ has decreasing density. In other words, there exists a decreasing function $u(t)$ such that $U(dt)=u(t) \; \mathrm{d} t$.
        \item The L\'{e}vy measure of $S$ is infinite (in other words, $\mu((0, \infty))=\infty$) and has a decreasing density.
        \item There exist constants $\sigma>0$, $\lambda_0>0$, and $\delta \in (0, 1]$ such that
        \begin{equation*}
            \frac{\phi'(\lambda x)}{\phi'(\lambda)} \leq \sigma x^{-\delta} \qquad\mbox{for all $x \geq 1$ and $\lambda \geq \lambda_0$}.
        \end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}
    Then $X$ satisfies $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \leq 1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.233301+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_547ef0cc8a8e9ba9429af3472f34faeb235bba1d2c95a195055e9f5bd6653f66","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2609.00567, 'Hodge--Tate splitting and Akizuki--Nakano vanishing in positive characteristic'] Let \(f\colon\mcalY\to\mcalX\) be a morphism of stacks. By \citeSta{06NW}, the induced functor between the inherited fpqc sites gives a morphism of topoi. Moreover, the canonical morphism \(f^{-1}\mcalO_{\mcalX}\to\mcalO_{\mcalY}\) is an equivalence: on every object \(T\to\mcalY\), both sides are the ring \(\Gamma(T,\mcalO_T)\). We therefore obtain a morphism of ringed fpqc topoi. We denote the resulting derived adjunction by
  \begin{equation*}
    Lf_{\mathrm{sh}}^*\colon\mcalD(\mcalX_{\fpqc},\mcalO_{\mcalX})\rightleftarrows\mcalD(\mcalY_{\fpqc},\mcalO_{\mcalY})\colon Rf_*^{\mathrm{sh}};
  \end{equation*}
  see \citeSta{07A6}. Thus
  \begin{equation*}
    Lf_{\mathrm{sh}}^*(\mathord{-})\defeq\mcalO_{\mcalY}\otimes_{f^{-1}\mcalO_{\mcalX}}^Lf^{-1}(\mathord{-}).
  \end{equation*}
  We call \(Lf_{\mathrm{sh}}^*\) the \emph{site-theoretic derived pullback} and \(Rf_*^{\mathrm{sh}}\) the \emph{site-theoretic derived pushforward}.","status":"unverified"}

