{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.368846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_549b217203e8051ba996f6c684249b84f47149f5233bce110f968931244196e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 34 of arXiv:2512.07202, 'Hitting Probabilities for Hypoelliptic Differential Equations Driven by Fractional Brownia'] There are constants $0<r_1<r_2$ and  $\zeta\in(0,1]$ such that for any $x\in\mathbb{R}^N$ and $y\in\left\{x+F_l(u,x):~u\in \mathfrak{g}^{(l)},~\|u\|_{\mathrm{HS}}<r_1\right\}$, there is a diffeomorphism $S_{x,y}$ between $M_{x,y}\cap\left\{u\in\mathfrak{g}^{(l)}:||u||_{\mathrm{HS}}<r_1\right\}$ and $M_{x,x}\cap\left\{u\in\mathfrak{g}^{(l)}:||u||_{\mathrm{HS}}<r_2\right\}$, which satisfies
