{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.548886+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5547423d8fbe823e8a0fb8a9ff593dc234d4db1307ec937b6867fa47f8d3aca1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 42 (Excursion measure $n$ for reflecting Brownian motion) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] We define the excursion measure $n$ for reflecting Brownian motion $R$ on $[0,\infty)$) as the unique sigma-finite measure on $\mathcal{E}$ with the following properties, see e.g. \cite[Chapter XII]{revuz2013continuous}.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For all $y \in (0,\infty)$ we have $n(T_y(f)<\infty) = 1/2y$.
        \item For all $y \in (0,\infty)$, the conditional distribution $n(\cdot| T_y(f) < \infty)$ is the distribution of the following process $Z = (Z_t)_{t \geq 0}$. We have that $(Z_t)_{0 \leq t \leq T_y(Z)}$ has the law of a Bessel process of dimension $3$ started from $0$, and let us denote this by $P_0^{\text{Bes}(3)}$. Then the process $(Z_{T_y(Z) + t})_{t \geq 0}$ is independent of $(Z_t)_{0 \leq t \leq T_y(Z)}$ and has the law of a Brownian motion on $[0,\infty)$ started at $y$ and absorbed when it hits $0$. The Bessel process of dimension $3$ is a diffusion with infinitesimal drift $\mu(y) = 1/y$ and infinitesimal variance $v(y) = 1$, see e.g. Revuz-Yor \cite[Chapter XI]{revuz2013continuous}.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.723997+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55477fd6f4472d784be47fe01559b376c97c391961a4b2f0617296c0ddc55002","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 38 (Scaling law for Polyak) of arXiv:2609.02728, 'Momentum in large-batch training: Polyak enlarges the critical batch size, Nesterov improv'] \normalfont
Assume \(\eta\lesssim1\) and \(k\gtrsim\max(\eta^{-1}_\rho,(1-\rho)^{-1})\). Then
