{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.771131+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_558bf270f9c22806fa87fb135fbc441f28255105f0cd1ebfd99da9dfbb625cf6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: Poisson approx in Wasserstein of arXiv:2411.02917, '\hspace*{2mm}\\[-16mm] Stein's Method for Spatial Random Graphs'] Let $(\mathrm{H},\mathrm{T})\sim P^{\lambda}\otimes Q^{\kappa}$ be a generalised random geometric graph with $\lambda(\cdot \,|\, \xi) = \lambda(\cdot)$ integrable and therefore $\mathrm{H} \sim \mathrm{Pop}(\lambda)$. Let $(\Xi,\Sigma)$ be a random graph having law $P\otimes Q$, where $Q$ is an edge kernel and $\Xi\sim P$ fulfils Condition (equation eq:condsigma of the paper) and has Papangelou kernel $L$. Then, in the notation of Theorem~(thm: general approx result of the paper), 
\begin{align*}
    W_{\mathbb{G},i}(P\otimes Q, P^{\lambda}\otimes Q^{\kappa})
    &\leq c_V(\lambda)\,\E\biggl(\int_{\mathcal{X}}\big\vert\lambda(x) \alpha(\diff x)-L(\diff x\vert\Xi)\big\vert \biggr) \\
    &\hspace*{4mm} {} + c_E(\lambda)\, \int_{\mathcal{X}}  \E\bigl[\norm{{\hat{v}(\Tilde{\Sigma}_{\Xi,\Sigma,x})}}_1 \bigr] \,\lambda(x) \, \alpha(\diff x) \\
    &\hspace*{4mm} {} + c_V(\lambda)\, \E \biggl( \int_{\mathcal{X}}\int_{\mathfrak{N}_2} \bigabs{\tilde{Q}_1(\Xi,x;\diff \sigma) - Q(\Xi;\diff \sigma)}\,L(\diff x \,\vert \Xi) \biggr),
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align}
    c_V(\lambda) &= \min \biggl\{ C_i, 
    \frac{1}{\Lambda} \,\bigl(1 +  ({1-e^{-{\Lambda}}}) \log^+{\Lambda} \bigr)\,\tilde{C}_i \biggr\},\\
    c_E(\lambda) &= \min \biggl\{ \frac14, \frac{2-e^{-\Lambda}}{\Lambda} - \frac{1}{\Lambda^2}\Bigl(\frac{3}{2} - e^{-\Lambda}\Bigr) \biggr\} \hspace*{1.5pt} C_E,

\end{align}
using $\Lambda = \int_{\mathcal{X}} \lambda(x) \, \alpha(dx)$. Here $C_i = C_V + \frac{i}{2} C_E$ denotes the upper bound on $d_{\mathbb{G},i}$, whereas $\tilde{C}_i = C_V +  i \hspace*{1.5pt} C_E$ is the maximal penalty per additional vertex; see Subsection~(ch: metric of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.650621+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_558d71cca6408fb72a62af685ea68893ea2858775f90d7c704a7a4373dc0b6ef","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 8 of arXiv:2607.24170, '\large\bf ON NON-MONOTONICITY OF LOGARITHMIC ENERGY\\ FOR RANDOM MATRICES'] For every $n\geq1$ and $\varepsilon>0$, there is a probability measure
$\beta_{n,\varepsilon}$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \nu_{n,\varepsilon}
 =
 \gamma_{s_{n,\varepsilon}}*\beta_{n,\varepsilon},
 \qquad
 s_{n,\varepsilon}\overset{\mathrm{def}}{=}
 \frac{\varepsilon}{n\sqrt{1+\varepsilon^2}}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, $\nu_{n,\varepsilon}$ has a density bounded by
\[
 \frac{n^2(1+\varepsilon^2)}{\pi\varepsilon^2},
\]
satisfies $m_2(\nu_{n,\varepsilon})\leq1$, and has finite logarithmic
energy.","status":"unverified"}

