{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:14.917412+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55a9b79f5ce290e5a5aed5a08e2d4b694d62b672aa8f6345322e4ea1f75fa8ff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main of arXiv:2609.03751, 'Sparse bounds for maximal rough singular integrals'] Let \(d\geq1\), and suppose that
\(\Omega\in L^1(S^{d-1})\) satisfies
\[
    \int_{S^{d-1}}\Omega(\theta)\,d\sigma(\theta)=0.
\]

\begin{enumerate}
\item
If \(\Omega\in L^\infty(S^{d-1})\), then, for every
\(1<p<\infty\) and every pair of bounded, compactly supported
functions \(f_1,f_2\), there exists a \(1/2\)-sparse collection
\(\mathcal S=\mathcal S(f_1,f_2,p)\) such that
\begin{equation}
    \left|
        \left\langle T_\Omega^\ast f_1,f_2\right\rangle
    \right|
    \lesssim_d
    (p')^4\|\Omega\|_{L^\infty(S^{d-1})}
    \sum_{Q\in\mathcal S}
        |Q|\langle f_1\rangle_Q\langle f_2\rangle_{p,Q}.
\end{equation}

\item
Let \(1<q<\infty\). If
\(\Omega\in L^{q,1}\log L(S^{d-1})\), then, for every \(q'\leq p<\infty\)
and every pair of bounded, compactly supported functions
\(f_1,f_2\), there exists a \(1/2\)-sparse collection
\(\mathcal S=\mathcal S(f_1,f_2,p)\) such that
\begin{equation}
    \left|
        \left\langle T_\Omega^\ast f_1,f_2\right\rangle
    \right|
    \lesssim_{d,q}
    (p')^4\|\Omega\|_{L^{q,1}\log L(S^{d-1})}
    \sum_{Q\in\mathcal S}
        |Q|\langle f_1\rangle_Q\langle f_2\rangle_{p,Q}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.381039+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55aa06b8ff810c70f359c9422879b1b1d5566e49d85ea8d69bd184d3fcbfd328","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ergodic via independece of core of arXiv:2411.02030, 'Finite ergodic components for upper probabilities'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},V,T)$ be an UPPS. Then the following statements are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item [(i)] $V$ is of finite ergodic components;
		\medskip
		\item [(ii)]there exist  $Q_1,\ldots,Q_n\in\mathcal{ M}(T)\cap\operatorname{core}(V)$ and $A_1,\ldots,A_n\in\mathcal{I}$ satisfying that  $\Omega=\cup_{j=1}^nA_j$, $Q_j(A_j)=1$, and $A_j\cap A_k=\emptyset$ when $j\neq k$ such that  for any  $P\in\operatorname{core}(V)$, 
		\begin{equation}
			\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}P(B\cap T^{-i}C)=\sum_{j=1}^nQ_j(C)P(A_j\cap B)\text{ for any }B,C\in\mathcal{F}.
		\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.251517+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55aa1a5578fc56e8dd2fd845b66b3f8efbadb8cb00131640bf5f0dfd6c233a05","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 64 (Bulk mixing) of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] The following holds if the constant $L$ in \Cref{def:bulkcells} is large enough. Fix real numbers $q \ge \overline{q}$ and a point $z_0 \in \mathfrak{L}_{q}$ satisfying \eqref{eq:distanzabis} and  
\begin{flalign}

	0 \le q - \overline{q} < N^{5A\delta/4-1} (\mathfrak{d}_{z_0} \mathfrak{e}_{z_0;q})^{-1/2}.
\end{flalign}

\noindent Further assume that $H_0 (z) \le
        \mathcal{H}_{q} (z)+N^{\delta-1}$ for each $z \in 
        \mathfrak{Y}$. 
        Under the Glauber dynamics restricted to $\mathfrak Y$, with the ceiling constraint $\cH_q+N^{\delta-1}$ on $\mathfrak{Y}$, 
        we have w.o.p. 
        \begin{eqnarray}

H_t (z) \le \mathcal{H}_{\bar q} (z),  
        \end{eqnarray}
        for all
        \begin{equation}

          z\in \mathfrak{Y}^-,\qquad t \in[\mathtt t,N^{5}
            ],  \quad \mathtt t:= q^{-1} N^{1+6 A\delta}(\md_{z_0}\me_{z_0;q})^{-1/2}.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.164780+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55ad2b2a3cca30bec0dc109c0baf51edaa00178e3037ab43a8957aad9346f93b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:main of arXiv:2411.16103, 'Non-commutative Stein's Method: Applications to Free Probability and Sums of Non-commutati'] If $\mu$ is a probability measure with moments of arbitrary order, then the equation 
