{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.057405+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55ae6e81d1828cdcbb59cb998e3294e30b1c7ee57626734d925341f20d877292","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma compute_expectation of arXiv:2608.12056, 'Deviation inequalities for U-processes of growing order, with applications to half-space d'] Fix $1 \leq p < \infty$, $0 < \alpha, l < \infty$. Let $\gamma > 0$ and suppose $\xi$ is a positive random variable such that for some $c, u_* > 0$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb P(\xi > \gamma u) \leq c e^{-2^{l-1}u^\alpha} \,,
    \end{align*}
    for every $u \geq u_*$. Then,
    \begin{align*}
        \left(\mathbb E  \xi^p \right)^{1/p} \leq \gamma\left(C^{1/p}\left(\frac{p}{\alpha}\right)^{1/\alpha + 1/(2p)}2^{-(l-1)/\alpha - 1/(2p)} + u_*\right) \,,
    \end{align*}
    where $C$ is some absolute constant.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.344706+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55b2c67944fbf2845c120fce27e7b09ed2034785dc158231d49126b300512025","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] If $ f(\cdot) $ is a simple function in the form (eqSimplefuction of the paper). 
    We call $ f(\cdot) $ Bochner integrable if $ \mu(E_{j}) < \infty $ for any $ j = 1, \cdots, k $.
    For any $ E \in \mathcal{F} $, we define the Bochner integral of $ f(\cdot) $ over $ E $ as 
    \begin{align*}
        \int_E f(s) d \mu=\sum_{j=1}^k \mu\left(E \cap E_j\right) u_{j} .
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.632323+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55b32bad51d6b087540aeb7c0f0d9fc74b4e9624368cba518cba8d6cf05f6089","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma EHI:l:SpecialIntegralAsymptotic' of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] For all $\alpha \geq 1$ and $\beta>0$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        \int_r^\infty s^{-2-\alpha} (\log s)^{1+\beta} \; \mathrm{d} s \asymp r^{-1-\alpha} (\log r)^{1+\beta} \qquad\mbox{as $r \to \infty$}.
    \end{equation*}
    (The constants implicit in $\asymp$ depend on $\alpha$ and $\beta$.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.624814+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55b4bcdee4ab4b316fb7c7478b4b5cd8834bfe4361d9f6c49eacb68a63a8ee32","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def: admissible of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] A subsequence $\{t_k\}_{k\geq 1}$ of $\mathbb{R}_+$ is called $(\gamma,\delta)$-admissible for some $\delta\geq 0$ and $0\leq \gamma\leq 1$ if there exist $c_1,c_2>0$ and $N_0>0$ such that for any $n\geq N_0$, there exists $\Pi_n\subset \{1,\cdots,n\}$ such that
		\begin{equation}
			|\Pi_n|\leq c_1n^{\gamma}, \ \text{ and } \ \inf_{1\leq i\neq j\leq n, i,j\notin \Pi_n}|t_i-t_j|\geq c_2(\ln n)^{1+\delta}.
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.314280+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55b58627bdf7a7bdaa5fdc7bcea4bf1e20354196ae6d50dc3c97b5b729863115","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: hext pos reg of arXiv:2506.21466, 'The Markov property for $\varphi^4_3$ on the cylinder'] Let $\varphi_-,\varphi_+ \in \mathcal C^{-1/2-\kappa}_z$. Then for every $p \in [1,2)$, there exists $s>0$ sufficiently small and $C>0$ such that
