{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.598534+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55c86cd3a58669589b7ca112f5878aa410c364fc80c406b4049550b920cbdb13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem perfect_1 of arXiv:2411.10502, 'Strategies in a mis\`{e}re two-player tree search game'] Assuming that both players play optimally, and know this about each other, the probability that player $1$ wins in $MS(P_n)$ is given by
\begin{equation}

	1/2\textrm{ when n is even;}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}

	\frac{2k}{4k+1}\textrm{ when }n = 4k+1 \textrm{ for some integer k;}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}

	\frac{2k+2}{4k+3}\textrm{ when }n = 4k+3\textrm{ for some integer k.}
\end{equation}

Further, the guesses that achieve these maximum winning probabilities are described as follows.

\begin{enumerate}
\item When $n = 4k$ for some $k,$ player 1 should choose a number that divides the game into ranges of sizes $4k_1 + 1$ and $4k_2 + 2,$ for any choice of $k_1, k_2$ with $k_1 + k_2 = k - 1.$
\item When $n = 4k + 1$ for some $k,$ all of player 1's choices are of equal strength.
\item When $n = 4k + 2$ for some $k,$ player 1 should choose a number that divides the game into ranges of sizes $4k_1 + 1$ and $4k_2,$ for any choice of $k_1, k_2$ with $k_1 + k_2 = k.$
\item When $n = 4k + 3$ for some $k,$ player 1 should choose a number that divides the game into ranges of sizes $4k_1 + 1$ and $4k_2 + 1,$ for any choice of $k_1, k_2$ with $k_1 + k_2 = k.$
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.290578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55c8b6e9bc0242e4988ef40b9f63ee531f6d3091a375fef2717212b32b52b630","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:LLN of arXiv:2605.06887, 'Law of large numbers for activated random walk on villages'] Given $V$,  $P$, $\lambda$ as in Section (ss_vm_jk_sr of the paper) and initial conditions
$\sigma$ and $\nu$ (cf.\ (equation eq_sigma_nu_initial of the paper)) that also satisfy $\sigma_x \leq \frac{\lambda_x}{1+\lambda_x}$ for all $x \in V$, the system of equations
\begin{eqnarray} 

s_x  & = & - m_x + \sigma_x +\nu_x + \sum_{y} m_y P_{y,x}, \qquad  x \in V \\ 

s_x & = & \sigma_x e^{-(\nu_x + \sum_y m_y P_{y,x})} + \frac{\lambda_x}{1+\lambda_x} \left(1-e^{-(\nu_x + \sum_y m_y P_{y,x})}\right),  \qquad  x \in V, \end{eqnarray}
has a unique solution $s_* \in [0,1]^V$ and $m_* \in \mathbb{R}_+^V$ and the random vectors $s_*^{(n)}$ and $m_*^{(n)}$ converge in probability to the deterministic limits $s_*$ and $m_*$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.189870+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55c9c1cbfe69794a5d0a76fbb3e97bc0d47f52230839f16df924b96c2fafd969","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:stopped_Z of arXiv:2608.06263, 'Almost sure path localisation for the derivative martingale of branching Brownian motion'] Let $(\mathcal{L}_t,\tau_t)_{t \ge 0}$ be a non-decreasing family of stopping lines such that, for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$, the following two conditions hold $\mathbb{P}_x$-almost surely:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item any stopping line is anterior to some generation: for any $t \ge 0$, there exists $s \ge 0$ such that $(\mathcal{L}_t,\tau_t) \preceq (\mathcal{N}_s,s)$.
        \item any generation is anterior to some stopping line: for any $s \ge 0$, there exists $t \ge 0$ such that $(\mathcal{N}_s,s) \preceq (\mathcal{L}_t,\tau_t)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, the stopped martingale $Z_{\tau_t}$ converges almost surely to $Z_\infty$ as $t \to \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.495769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55cbb8c2e1bd0ed1f0c7eddaea9002567a57db4a491fc9eb9791048d5ac7ccbe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2605.28801, 'Microscopic Weak Selection Principle for the Logistic Branching Brownian Motion with selec'] Given the Log-BBM($1,c$) $(\boldsymbol{X^c}(t))_{t\geq 0}$,
	\begin{itemize}
