{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:44.027969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55e5b6a7de3700392662db2fecb3d69afca15e300254f9a3699921d19a275932","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Model_SoftEdgeConv of arXiv:2412.04579, 'Solvable Families of Random Block Tridiagonal Matrices'] Suppose that $\mathbf{T}_n$ is an $rn\times rn$ Jacobi $r$-block matrix with blocks $\mathbf{A}_{n,i}$, $\mathbf{B}_{n,i}$. Let~$\mathbf{H}_n$ and $m_n$ be defined as in (equation eq:Hn of the paper).
Expressing $\mathbf{H}_n$ as $ m_n^2 \Delta_n + m_n {\mathbf{V}}_n$,
denote the running sums of the diagonal and upper diagonal block components of the potential $\mathbf{V}_n$ by: for $0 \le x \le (rn)^{2/3}$,
\begin{align*}

 {\bf{Y}}_{n,1}(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{[m_n x]} \left( - \frac{1}{\sqrt{\gamma m_n}}
 \mathbf{A}_{n,i} \right),
 \qquad
 {\bf{Y}}_{n,2}(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{[m_n x]}
\left( m_n^2 \mathbf{I}_r -
 \frac{1}{\sqrt{\gamma m_n}} \mathbf{B}_{n,i} \right).
\end{align*}
Now assume that
\begin{itemize}\itemsep=0pt
\item[$(i)$] Both $\{ {\bf{Y}}_{n,1}(x)\}_{x \ge 0}$ and
$\{ {\bf{Y}}_{n,2}(x)\}_{x \ge 0}$ are tight and
\begin{equation*}

 {\bf{Y}}_{n,1}(x) +
 \bigl({\bf{Y}}_{n,2}(x) + {\bf{Y}}_{n,2}^{\dagger}(x) \bigr)\ \Rightarrow \
 \frac{1}{2} r x^2 \mathbf{I}_r+ \sqrt{\frac{2}{\gamma}} B(x)
\end{equation*}
in law in the uniform-on-compacts topology;

\item[$(ii)$] For $j=1,2$, defining the increments via
\smash{$ {\bf{Y}}_{n,j}(x):=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\gamma m_n}} \sum_{i=1}^{[m_n x]}
 (\Delta {\bf{Y}})_{n,j,i}$}
 there is a~decomposition
\begin{equation*}

 (\Delta {\bf{Y}})_{n, j,i}
 = \boldsymbol\eta_{n,j,i} + (\Delta {\boldsymbol\omega})_{n,j,i}
\end{equation*}
such that the following holds. The $\boldsymbol\eta_{n,j, i} $ are diagonal and
$\boldsymbol\eta_{n,j}(x) = \boldsymbol\eta_{n,j,[m_nx]}$ satisfy
\begin{align}
 \kappa^{-1} x - \kappa \le \boldsymbol\eta_{n,1}(x) + \boldsymbol\eta_{n,2}(x) \le \kappa x + \kappa, \qquad
 \boldsymbol\eta_{n,2}(x) \le 2 m_n, 
\end{align}
for some $\kappa \ge 1$. Also, with \smash{$\boldsymbol\omega_{n,j}(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{[m_nx]} (\Delta {\boldsymbol\omega})_{n,j,i}$}, there exists an $\epsilon > 0$ so that
\begin{align}
 \| \boldsymbol\omega_{n,1}(x) - \boldsymbol\omega_{n,1}(y)\|^2 + \| \boldsymbol\omega_{n,2}(x) - \boldsymbol\omega_{n,2}(y)\|^2 & \le \kappa_n x^{1-\epsilon} + \kappa_n, 
\end{align}
for all $|x-y| \le 1$. Here
$\|\cdot\|$ denotes the spectral norm, and $\kappa_n \ge 1$ a sequence of tight random constants.
\end{itemize}

Then, with $\mathcal{H}_{\beta, \gamma}$ defined in (equation eq:H_op of the paper),
we have
that any finite collection of ordered eigenvalues of~$\mathbf{H}_n$, along with their associated eigenfunctions as elements in $L^2 (\mathbb{F}^r)$, converge jointly in law to the corresponding eigenvalues/eigenfunctions of $ \mathcal{H}_{\beta, \gamma}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:50:36.765014+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55e6b96702d063a05e51118b658e5fc02252077e2c2257840e946f923214a5b1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 20 (Evaluation of $a\md{q}$-sum) of arXiv:2609.04155, 'The subconvexity problem for symmetric square\\ $L$-functions in Level aspect'] Let $q=2^kr=2^kcq_1^2q_2^2$ where, $2^k||q$, $\mu^2(c)=1$, $q_1|c^\infty$, and $(c, q_2)=1$. The evaluation of $\mathcal{G}_q(m, n)$ is given by the following,
