{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.281322+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_560f2f62ed13ab7d4c37f7f7b746dc726418a38c373e3c2c47c76fb4f357536b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2604.22567, 'Sign-balance of random Laplace eigenfunctions'] Let $(\Mcc,g)$ be a smooth Riemannian $d$-manifold, and $\eta=\eta(T)\in (0,T]$ satisfying either (a) $\eta(T)\rightarrow \infty$ or (b) $\Mcc=\Sc^{d}$. Let $u \in \R$, and define $\Bc_{T,u}(r)$ as in \eqref{eq:sign-imbal def waves}. Set 
\begin{equation}

\bar{r}_{T} =  \frac{\min\left\{ (\log{T})^{\frac{1}{d-1}} , \left(\frac{T\log{T}}{\eta}\right)^{\frac{1}{d}}  \right\}}{T} \quad \quad  \ubar{r}_{T} =  \frac{\min\left\{ (\log{T})^{\frac{1}{2(d-1)}} , \left(\frac{T\log{T}}{\eta}\right)^{\frac{1}{2d}}  \right\}}{T}  .
\end{equation}  
Then, as $T \to \infty$, the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}[i.]
\item There exists a number $\mu>0$ sufficiently large, only depending on $\Mcc$, so that
\begin{equation}

 \sup_{r \ge \mu \cdot \bar{r}_{T} }  \Bc_{T,u}(r) \xrightarrow{P} 0.
\end{equation}
\item Assume that either (a') there exists a number $\delta_{0}>0$ so that $\eta(T)>T^{\delta_{0}}$ or (b') $\Mcc=\Sc^{d}$. Then there exists a number $\mu>0$ sufficiently small, only depending on $\Mcc$, so that:
\begin{itemize}
\item If $u \neq 0$, $\left\{\Bc_{T,u}(\mu\cdot \bar{r}_{T})\right\}_{T\ge 1}$ is bounded away from zero in probability.
\item If $u = 0$, $\left\{\Bc_{T,u}(\mu\cdot \ubar{r}_{T})\right\}_{T\ge 1}$ is bounded away from zero in probability.
\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.580175+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_560f41d51b6d36c02c0a4e7afbddb4212aa4371bbd82ba9456ce64a50e7089af","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:nSD of arXiv:2411.15401, 'The reference interval in higher-order stochastic dominance'] [{\color{black}[SS07]}]
Let $n\in\mathbb{N}$. For $X,Y\in\mathcal X$, we say that $X$ dominates $Y$ in the sense of $n$th-order stochastic dominance on $\mathbb{R}$ ($n{\rm SD}_{\mathbb{R}}$), denoted by $X\ge_{n} Y$ or $F_X\ge_{n} F_Y$ if 
\begin{align*}
F_X^{[n]}(\eta)\le F_Y^{[n]}(\eta),~\forall \eta\in\mathbb{R}
\end{align*}
or equivalently,
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[(\eta-X)_+^{n-1}]\le \mathbb{E}[(\eta-Y)_+^{n-1}],~\forall \eta\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.755808+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5612573a4c1d3da5e627e3fe89fd7af259251ee7fe9b6bf6f9a2f89b3e99c25e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:full-cm-extension of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For a potential-realizable response satisfying
\begin{equation}
