{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.548003+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_561e9a03b363bc502b491912ca6b54b315fb2b994cff75eb10068cea7e6163b4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 33 (The range of ooSBM) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $\mu_{\rm d}$, $\mu_{\rm a} \in \mathcal{M}_F(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be such that $\mu_{\rm d} + \mu_{\rm a}$ is a non-zero measure and let $\gamma$, $c$, $\Tilde{c}>0$. Let $\mathcal{Y}=(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm d)}, \mathcal{Y}^{(\rm a)})$ be an $\text{ooSBM}(\gamma, c, \Tilde{c})$ with initial measure $(\mu_{\rm d}, \mu_{\rm a})$ and let $\mathcal{X}$ be an $\text{SBM}(\gamma)$ with initial measure $\mu_{\rm d} + \mu_{\rm a}$. Then there exists a coupling for $\mathcal{Y}$ and $\mathcal{X}$ such that 
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm d)}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm a)}) = \text{supp}(\mu_{\rm d}) \cup \text{supp}(\mu_{\rm a}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{X}) \hspace{0.25cm} \text{a.s.} 
    \end{equation*}
    In particular, we have that $\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm d)}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm a)})$ is a closed set a.s. and is equal in distribution to 
    \newline
    $\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm d}) \cup \text{supp}(\mu_{\rm a}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{X})$. Therefore
    \begin{equation*}
        \text{dim}(\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm d)}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm a)})) = \text{dim}(\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm d})) \vee \text{dim}(\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm a})) \vee (4 \wedge d) \text{   a.s.}
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

