{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.746833+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_565b2e5b6898cae857b4d8c2db673122aaca4e3638b919294acca5c5fb89fd58","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2606.11994, 'Sample Path Properties of the\\ Fractional Wiener--Weierstrass Bridge II'] Assume $H>1/2$, and put $p=1/H$.  For step functions $f,g$ on
$[0,1]$, the inner product on $\calH$ has the concrete
representation
\begin{equation}   \left\langle f,g\right\rangle_{\calH}   =   H(2H-1)\int_0^1\int_0^1   f(x)g(y)|x-y|^{2H-2}\,\dd x\,\dd y .   
\end{equation}
Moreover, $L^p[0,1]$ embeds continuously into $\calH$: there is a finite
constant $C_H$ such that
\begin{equation}   \norm{f}_{\calH}   \le C_H\norm{f}_{L^p[0,1]},   \qquad f\in L^p[0,1].   
\end{equation}
Under this embedding, \eqref{eq:calH-inner-product} remains valid for all
$f,g\in L^p[0,1]$.  Finally,
\begin{equation*}   \norm{f}_{\calH}=0,\quad f\in L^p[0,1],   \quad\Longrightarrow\quad   f=0\text{ Lebesgue-a.e.}

\end{equation*}
In particular, the embedding $L^p[0,1]\hookrightarrow\calH$ is injective.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.751603+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_565b3d7bb308baf478e5441045b69bd079bec9cec9e065baa01e5c193ff14712","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 32 of arXiv:2608.18834, 'The Parabolic Anderson Model's Total Mass at Small Times: Geometry, Fluctuations, and Reno'] Let $\ka>0$. Define the function
\begin{align}

\msf r_\ka(t)=\begin{cases}
0&\rho>0,\\
-\ka^2\mf b\,t\log(1/t)+\mf e_\ka\,t&\rho=0,\\
\tfrac{\ka^2\mf b}{(1+\rho)\rho}t^{1+\rho}&\rho<0,
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\mf e_\ka\in\mbb R$ is as in Corollary \ref{Corollary: Limits of E minus Renormalization}.
There exists $\th_\ka>0$ such that for every $t\in(0,\th_\ka)$,
\begin{align}

\mbf E\big[\msf M_{\ka}(t)\big]
&=\mr e^{\msf r_\ka(t)}\int_D\mbf E\left[\mbf 1_{\{\tau_D(B^x)>t\}}\mr e^{\frac{\ka^2}{2}\ga_t(B)}\right]\d x,\\

\mbf{Var}\big[\msf M_{\ka}(t)\big]
&=\mr e^{2\msf r_\ka(t)}\int_{D^2}\mbf E\Bigg[\mbf 1_{\cap_{i\leq2}\{\tau_D(B^{x_i}_i)>t\}}\mr e^{\sum_{i=1}^2\frac{\ka^2}{2}\ga_t(B_i)}
\left(\mr e^{\ka^2\al_t(x)}-1\right)\Bigg]\d x.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.729900+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_56690b214de525937ace5dfc5349e43a5c98ee644e9d721f599d98c42fae5b5a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:NL-MP-construction-old of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] {RehmeierRoeckner2025NonlinearMarkov}}]
    Let $\mathcal P_0 \subseteq \mathcal P$, and for any $\zeta \in \mathcal P_0$ let $\mu^\zeta = (\mu^\zeta_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a solution to (equation eq:FPE of the paper) satisfying (equation eq:L1-int-SP of the paper) such that
