{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.275596+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_56819df99b019a5ed0fddf0fb0d4067f2c5c2ac2bb56f25f05b4a0de48b7cac2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2604.07579, 'Topology of Percolation Clusters: Central Limit Theorems beyond the Lattice'] Let $f$ be the growth function of a quasi-transitive
      graph. The function $f$ has subexponential growth if and only if
      there exists a function $g:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ satisfying the
      following conditions:
      \begin{enumerate}
        \item  $\lim\limits_{r\to\infty}g(r)=\infty$;

        \item  $0<g(r)<r$ for all sufficiently large $r$;

        \item  $\displaystyle \lim\limits_{r\to\infty}
          \frac{f(r)-f(r-g(r))}{f(r)}=0$.

        \item  For any fixed $k \geq 0$,
          $\lim\limits_{r\to\infty}\frac{f(2g(r)+k)}{f(r)}= 0,$
      \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

