{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.212272+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_56b970df1d5a1a607891e34b1baf260e45703a901731847a75fce93535420209","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2512.06640, 'Existence and sharpness of the phase transition for the frog model on transitive graphs'] Let $(X_{1},d_{1})$ and $(X_{2},d_{2})$ be two metric spaces. A map $f: X \rightarrow Y$ is called a \textbf{quasi-isometry} if there exists $K>0$ such that 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For all $z,y \in X_{1},$ we have that 
        $$ K^{-1}d_{1}(z,y) - K \leq d_{2}(f(z),f(y)) \leq Kd_{1}(z,y) + K \, . $$
        \item For all $x_{2} \in X_{2}$ there exists some $x_{1} \in X_{1}$ such that $d_{2}(x_{2},f(x_{1})) \leq K.$
    \end{itemize}
We say that $X_{1}$ is quasi-isometric to $X_{2}$ if there exists a quasi-isometry $f:X_{1}\rightarrow X_{2}.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.365245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_56bd0b8f47080ed555cf2d45982cb4fbe9af077c2f8cb60e1126fbfe585a05ea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 18 of arXiv:2506.20146, 'Parabolic Anderson Model in the Hyperbolic Space. Part I: Annealed Asymptotics'] There exists a universal constant $C_{d}$
