{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.653123+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_56e4a7fde3b4cb2991f74a3a28e0a9b1c48fcd83ad919a2f118d2cbfd5ca14cd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LMMMNNNNS of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] Assume $A$ and $\pi$ satisfy the same conditions as in Lemma (PII of the paper), and let  $C \in {L}(\mathbb{R}^n\times \mathbb{R}^n,L(\mathbb{R}^d,\mathbb{R}^n))$. Consider the following linear equation:
				\begin{align}
					\begin{split}
						\mathrm{d}y_t &= A\left( y_t, \int_{-r}^{0} y_{\theta+t} \pi(\mathrm{d}\theta) \right) \mathrm{d}t + \epsilon C(y_t, y_{t-r}) \mathrm{d}B_t(\omega), \\
						y_{\theta} &= \xi_{\theta} \quad \text{for} \quad \theta \in [-r, 0], \quad \xi \in C([-r, 0], \mathbb{R}^n).
					\end{split}
				\end{align}
