{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.787007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_572f0574d5bb6886cb18eba26cd8ead6c0c34c193e83b945fd162ecc444bada1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:value_process_decomposition of arXiv:2507.22577, 'A Mean-Field Theory of $\Theta$-Expectations'] Let the assumptions for global well-posedness hold. Let $(X,Y,Z,A)$ be the unique solution to the Mean-Field $\Theta$-FBSDE with terminal condition $Y_T = \xi \in L^2(\mathcal{F}_T)$. Then, the value process $Y_t = \mathfrak{E}[\xi|\mathcal{F}_t]$ admits the following decomposition for all $t \in [0,T]$:
\begin{equation}
    Y_t = Y_0 + \int_0^t Z_s \mathrm{d} B_s - \int_0^t F(s, X_s, Y_s, Z_s, A_s, \mu_s) \mathrm{d} s.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the process $M_t$ defined by
\begin{equation} 
M_t \coloneqq Y_t + \int_0^t F(s, X_s, Y_s, Z_s, A_s, \mu_s) \mathrm{d} s
\end{equation}
is a square-integrable martingale with respect to $(\mathbb{F}, P_0)$.","status":"unverified"}

