{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.668755+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_57e70573d7b092d684d199c15fc49c58f6fd9ff9a271bb0e9140ca06e260f70e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop macro-order-uniform of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] Assume Assumption~(ass:weight of the paper) and
\begin{align*}
 \liminf_{n\to\infty} \frac{\ell\log n}{n}>0, 
 \qquad
 \ell\leqslant\frac n2.
\end{align*}
Then for every $\varepsilon>0$,
\begin{align}
\sup_{x\in\mathds{T}_n\setminus\{0\}}
\pr\left(
 \frac{\mathfrak{a}_\ell T_n^{(\ell)}(0,x)}{\log n}\leqslant1-\varepsilon
\right)
\to0.
\end{align}
Moreover, for every fixed $p>1$,
\begin{align}
\limsup_{n\to\infty}
\sup_{x\in\mathds{T}_n\setminus\{0\}}
\E\left[
 \left(
 \frac{\mathfrak{a}_\ell T_n^{(\ell)}(0,x)}{\log n}
 \right)^p
\right]
<\infty .
\end{align}
In particular, $\mathfrak{a}_\ell T_n/\log n$ is tight and bounded away from zero in probability, uniformly in the target, and
\begin{align}
 1
 \leqslant
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}
 \inf_{x\in\mathds{T}_n\setminus\{0\}}
 \frac{\mathfrak{a}_\ell\E T_n^{(\ell)}(0,x)}{\log n}
 \leqslant
 \limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \sup_{x\in\mathds{T}_n\setminus\{0\}}
 \frac{\mathfrak{a}_\ell\E T_n^{(\ell)}(0,x)}{\log n}
 <\infty.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.116323+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_57e7c3f9c5c31f6e261ba4a16801494637660ff5300d2ef252504efa5252749c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 of arXiv:2604.04823, '2604.04823'] Under the assumptions and choices of Theorem~\ref{t:main-theorem-intro}, let~$\nu_0$ be an initial distribution on~$\mathcal X_\pt = (\T^d)^{N+1}$ such that~$\nu_0 \ll \pi_\pt$. Then, for all~$m\in \N$,
