{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.842992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_586d50aeb34721201076f7420be73717d1e38bcbf33707523ffd644f8d773809","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 15 of arXiv:2506.13913, '2506.13913'] {}{}
 A \(d\)-dimensional stochastic process \(\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0} = (X_t^1, \dots, X_t^d)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^d\) is called an \textbf{Itô diffusion process} if it satisfies the SDE  
 \[
\mathrm{d}X_t = \mu(t, X_t) \, \mathrm{d}t + \sigma(t, X_t) \, \mathrm{d}W_t, \quad X_0 = Z,
\]
where \(\{W_t\}_{t \geq 0}\) be an \(m\)-dimensional Brownian motion and \(\mu: \mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d\), \(\sigma: \mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^{d \times m}\), $Z\in\mathbb{R}^d$ satisfy the conditions in Theorem \ref{th:existence-uniqueness}. Furthermore, \(\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0}\) is called an \textbf{ (time-homogeneous) Itô diffusion} if $\mu$ and $\sigma$ do not depend explicitly on time, i.e., $\mu(t, X_t)=\mu(X_t)$ and $\sigma(t, X_t)=\sigma(X_t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.881938+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_586d94c2800d8ac35d14e69ff9868ecb3db6078dd5e0254db6ed27e7be3f6d8a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] We define the natural sum over $\varepsilon(X)$ as follows:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $0\#\alpha = \alpha\#0:=\alpha$.

        \item For $u,v\in X$, 
        \begin{equation*}
            \varepsilon_u \# \varepsilon_v =
            \begin{cases}
                \omega^{\varepsilon_u} + \omega^{\varepsilon_v} & \text{If }u\ge v, \\
                 \omega^{\varepsilon_v} + \omega^{\varepsilon_u} & \text{If }u<v.
            \end{cases}
        \end{equation*}

        \item Let $u\in X$ and $\alpha_0\ge \cdots\ge\alpha_{m-1}$ are in $\varepsilon(X)$. Let $k< m$ be the least number such that $\varepsilon_u>\alpha_k$ if there is such a number; Otherwise, let $k=m$. We define
        \begin{equation*}
            \varepsilon_u \# (\omega^{\alpha_0}+\cdots+\omega^{\alpha_{m-1}}) 
            = (\omega^{\alpha_0}+\cdots+\omega^{\alpha_{m-1}})  \# \varepsilon_u = \omega^{\alpha_0}+\cdots+ \omega^{\varepsilon_u} + \omega^{\alpha_k}+ \cdots +  \omega^{\alpha_{m-1}}.
        \end{equation*}

        \item For $\alpha_0\ge\cdots\ge\alpha_{m-1}$ and $\beta_0\ge\cdots\ge\beta_{n-1}$ in $\varepsilon(X)$, let $\gamma_0\ge\cdots\ge\gamma_{m+n-1}$ be the unique re-enumeration of the sequence $\alpha_0,\cdots,\alpha_{m-1},\beta_0,\cdots,\beta_{n-1}$.
        We define
        \begin{equation*}
            (\omega^{\alpha_0}+\cdots+\omega^{\alpha_{m-1}}) \# (\omega^{\beta_0}+\cdots+\omega^{\beta_{n-1}}) = \omega^{\gamma_0} + \cdots + \omega^{\gamma_{m+n-1}}.
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.578533+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_586ee0b86304793a9b60f55d633d38cada4a7c69b0e8d5d1f45bca9b22de7803","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:NestPhi of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Let~$\sigma$ be a regular pair potential and~$\tau$ be satisfying the conditions in Definition~(d:OnePlusMoment of the paper).
Then, there exists~$\varepsilon\colon {\mathbb R}^+\to{\mathbb R}^+$ satisfying~(equation eq:NestPhi of the paper) and additionally such that
\[
\sigma\circ \varepsilon \leq \tau^{1/4} \quad \text{and} \quad -\sigma' \circ\varepsilon \leq \tau^{1/4} \quad \text{eventually on~${\mathbb R}^+$} \,\mathrm{.}
\]
\begin{proof}
Let~$\bar\sigma\eqdef \sigma\vee -\sigma'$ and note that it too satisfies~(equation eq:AssSmallScaleInt of the paper).
In particular, it is invertible on~$(0,\delta]$, and we denote by~$\bar\sigma^{-1}\colon (\bar\sigma(\delta),\infty)\to{\mathbb R}$ its inverse.
Define
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon\colon t \longmapsto \begin{cases} \delta & \text{if } 0<t<\bar\sigma(\delta) \\ (\bar\sigma^{-1}\circ \tau^{1/4})(t) &\text{if } t\geq \bar\sigma(\delta)\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and note that~$\varepsilon$ satisfies~(equation eq:NestPhi of the paper) since~$\tau$ is non-decreasing and~$\bar\sigma$ satisfies~(equation eq:AssSmallScaleInt of the paper).
Furthermore, on~$[\bar\sigma(\delta),\infty)$,
\[
\sigma\circ \varepsilon\,\mathrm{,}\;\, -\sigma'\circ\varepsilon \leq \bar\sigma\circ\varepsilon = \tau^{1/4}\,\mathrm{,}\;\,
\]
which concludes the assertion.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.068965+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5870087806c3cbef2da55a3e6755bc26131970ffa07cd2cfc79a8aef50ceb7cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 ({ULDP on $C([0,T) of arXiv:2608.19753, 'Uniform large deviation principles for the stochastic heat equation over unbounded sets of'] ;L^p(D))$}]  
