{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.736462+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_588dcce42582dce4510b3a5395398d21547a1e3eb8d54791e552df874b460374","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn type of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a foliation on $\mathbb{T}^2$. We define the \textbf{type} of $\mathcal{G}$ as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If $\mathcal{G}$ has no Reeb annuli,
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item If $\mathcal{G}$ has no $\sharp$ nor $\flat$ annuli, we say that it is of type $\natural$. Otherwise,
            \item If $\mathcal{G}$ has only $\sharp$ but no $\flat$ annuli, we say that it is of type $\sharp$,
            \item If $\mathcal{G}$ has only $\flat$ annuli but no $\sharp$ annuli, we say that it is of type $\flat$,
            \item Otherwise, $\mathcal{G}$ has both $\sharp$ and $\flat$ annuli, and we say that it is of type $\dagger$.
        \end{enumerate}
    \item If $\mathcal{G}$ has Reeb annuli,
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item If all leaves which spiral toward the right have the same slope, and all leaves which spiral toward the left have the same slope, then we say that it is of type $\aleph$.
            \item If the previous condition fails, we say that $\mathcal{G}$ is not of an admissible type.
        \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
Besides the last case, we call these types \textbf{admissible}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.348175+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_588f729aa2339a4a97507ffcac5dbe4efa4f2eec09e6c390343f7e929a961306","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:feature-sat of arXiv:2608.10184, 'The sharp SAT/UNSAT phase transition in random ellipsoid fitting'] If $n/d^2 \to \rho_* \in (0, 1/2)$, there are constants $0 < c \le C < \infty$ (depending only on $\rho_*$) such that, with probability tending to one,
