{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:48:02.413560+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_599e028cfe485ce881e04a55e35656560d21ef776050a6d0f5db8b0af43610c8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lma:commutatorasymptotics of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] Let $p, b \in \mathbb R^\times$, let $Z = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\\zeta&\delta\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ be an arbitrary real $2\times 2$ matrix, and let $Y = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}\upsilon&\eta\\0&-\upsilon\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ be traceless with $Y_{21} = 0$. Put $P = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&p\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ and $P_b = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&b\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, and let
\begin{equation*}
	M(t) = \bigl(I + tY + t^2Y^{(2)} + O(t^3)\bigr)P \ \ \ \ \
	K(t) = \bigl(I + tZ + t^2Z^{(2)} + O(t^3)\bigr)P_b
\end{equation*}
be real analytic, with $Y^{(2)}, Z^{(2)}$ arbitrary. Then
\begin{equation*}
	M^{-1}K^{-1}MK = I + t\begin{pmatrix}p\zeta & * \\ 0 & -p\zeta\end{pmatrix} + O(t^2) \ \ \ \ \
	MK^{-1}M^{-1}K = I + t\begin{pmatrix}-p\zeta & * \\ 0 & p\zeta\end{pmatrix} + O(t^2)
\end{equation*}
Moreover,
\begin{equation*}
	\bigl(M^{-1}K^{-1}MK\bigr)_{21} = -\zeta\bigl(p\zeta + 2\upsilon\bigr)t^2 + O(t^3)
\end{equation*}
and this coefficient is independent of $\eta$, of $\alpha, \beta, \delta$, of $b$, and of $Y^{(2)}, Z^{(2)}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.539965+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_599ec51a71275d50bf476a79cfe3c2e3472f41d96c7743829c4208b1e0b8e9f5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: Key Lemma of arXiv:2411.10271, '$A$-localized states for clock models on trees and their extremal decomposition into glass'] Let $A\subset\mathbb{Z}_q$ with $|A|\geq 2$ and $A\neq \mathbb{Z}_q$. Furthermore, let $\beta$ be large enough and $\mu_A$ be the $A$-localized state for the ferromagnetic nearest-neighbor clock model with potential (equation eq: Potential ferr n.n. model of the paper) fulfilling the $u,U,d$-bounds in (equation eq: u,U-bounds of the paper). Then, there exist functions $\epsilon_1(\beta)$, $\epsilon_2(\beta)$ and $\lambda(\beta)$ satisfying $\lim_{\beta\rightarrow \infty}\epsilon_1(\beta)=\lim_{\beta\rightarrow \infty}\epsilon_2(\beta)=0$ and $\lim_{\beta\rightarrow \infty}\lambda(\beta)=\infty$ such that the following statements hold.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*)]
        \item For $\mu_A$-almost every $\omega$ and every $v\in V$, we have
        \begin{equation}
        \pi(\sigma_v\neq \omega_v|\omega) \leq \mathds{1}_{B_{A,v}}(\omega)+\epsilon_1(\beta).
    \end{equation}
    Here, $B_{A,v}$ is the bad event defined in (equation eq: Bad events of the paper).
    \item Moreover, the following inequality holds
    \begin{equation}
        \mu_A(B_A(\gamma)) \leq C_4e^{-\lambda(\beta) |\gamma|}
    \end{equation}
     for each finite subtree $\gamma \subset V$ where $B_A(\gamma)$ was given in (equation eq: Bad events of the paper). Hence, we have $\mu_A(B_{A,v})\leq \epsilon_2(\beta)$ for any $v\in V$.
    \end{enumerate}
    The function $\lambda$ in $b)$ can be chosen as $\lambda(\beta)=\Tilde{c}+c\delta_0 \beta$ where $c:= \min\{\frac{d^2+1}{d}u-U,\frac{1}{d}\}$ and 
    \begin{equation*}
        \Tilde{c}:=d\log\left(\delta_0 \right)-d\log(d+1)-\delta_0\log(C_1+2C_3).
    \end{equation*}
    Here, $\delta_0$ is given in (equation eq: Definition delta_0 of the paper) and $C_1$ is the positive constant defined in Proposition (prop: Bounds A-loc. states of the paper). Furthermore,
    \begin{equation*}
        \epsilon_1(\beta)=C_3e^{-\frac{(d-1)}{2}\beta u}~~\text{and}~~\epsilon_2(\beta)=C_5e^{-\lambda(\beta)}
    \end{equation*}
    and
    \begin{align*}
        C_3:=2(q-1),~~C_4:=2C_1(d+1)/\delta_0~~\text{and}~~C_5:=2(q-1)C_4.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.358614+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_599f1e5c8e7b2ea7828a98809c01d2dd4514e788245fa51c6843fee1e8c2185d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:cross-moment-decomposition of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] Assume square integrability, a shared conditional mean across banks, and $c_{\mathscr X}\ge0$ almost surely.  Then
\begin{align}
M_\times(f)
&=
\mathbb E[(\mu-f)^2]+\mathbb E[c_{\mathscr X}] \\
&=
\mathbb E[(m_{\mathscr H}-f)^2]
+
\mathbb E[\operatorname{Var}(\mu\mid\mathscr H)]
+
\mathbb E[c_{\mathscr X}],

\end{align}
and therefore
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
V_B(\mathscr H)-\mathcal V(\pi_f)
\le
\sqrt{M_\times(f)}.
}

\end{equation}
Consequently, for any policy $\pi_G$ measurable with respect to a refinement $\mathscr G\supseteq\mathscr H$,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal V(\pi_G)-V_B(\mathscr H)
\ge
\bigl\{\mathcal V(\pi_G)-\mathcal V(\pi_f)\bigr\}
-
\sqrt{M_\times(f)}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

