{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:45:05.413921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_59b012ac0f3c1fb68efadd1d9e101d46cfcc81e5e4f857dad56fd3edc087422d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 129 (Exact martingale calibration at deterministic and stopping horizons) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] Let $\tau:\Omega\to[0,T]$ be a bounded raw stopping time.  There is a
canonical contractive truncation
\[
 \mathsf T_\tau:\mathbb H_{\cP}^2\longrightarrow\mathbb H_{\cP}^2,
 \qquad
 \mathsf T_\tau Z=:\one_{[0,\tau]}Z,
\]
obtained as the upper-energy limit of dyadic predictable stopping
truncations.  It satisfies
\begin{equation}
 I(\one_{[0,\tau]}Z)=r_\tau I(Z)
 \qquad\text{in }\mathbb S_{\cP}^2,
\end{equation}
and, for bounded raw stopping times $\sigma,\tau$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf T_\sigma\mathsf T_\tau
 =\mathsf T_{\sigma\wedge\tau}.
\end{equation}
The truncation is continuous in the stopping horizon: if
$\|\tau_n-\tau\|_\infty\to0$, where
$\|\sigma-\tau\|_\infty:=\sup_{\omega\in\Omega}|\sigma(\omega)-\tau(\omega)|$
is the raw uniform norm, then, for every
$Z\in\mathbb H_{\cP}^2$,
\begin{equation}
 \|\mathsf T_{\tau_n}Z-\mathsf T_\tau Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}
 +\|r_{\tau_n}I(Z)-r_\tau I(Z)\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
 \longrightarrow0.
\end{equation}
Moreover it obeys the exact stopping-horizon It\^o isometry
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\one_{[0,\tau]}Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}
 =\|I_\tau(Z)\|_{\mathcal L_{\cP}^2}.}
\end{equation}
For deterministic $\tau=t$ this is
\begin{equation}
 \|\one_{[0,t]}Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}
 =\|I_t(Z)\|_{\mathcal L_{\cP}^2}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 \|Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}
 =\|I_T(Z)\|_{\mathcal L_{\cP}^2}
 \le\|I(Z)\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
 \le2\|Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}.
\end{equation}
In particular, on the stable first-jet graph,
\begin{equation}
 d_{\mathscr W}((Y,Z),(\widetilde Y,\widetilde Z))
 =\|Y-\widetilde Y\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
  +\|I_T(Z-\widetilde Z)\|_{\mathcal L_{\cP}^2}.
\end{equation}
Thus terminal and stopped martingale responses calibrate the jet geometry
isometrically; the whole-path martingale response is their Doob propagation.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.343957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_59b183b2188d6aa54105eb844c29e4d18c6af901a39a29e5f7d57a7b81c6c398","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mixing of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume Hypotheses (hyp:hemicont of the paper),  (hyp:coerc of the paper), (hyp:coerc-g of the paper), (hyp:monotonicity of the paper), (hyp:monotonicity-g of the paper), (hyp:growth of the paper) and (hyp:regularity of the paper), (hyp:reg2 of the paper), (hyp:quant of the paper), and (hyp:mixing of the paper) hold true. Suppose that $\eta\equiv 0$ or $\rho\equiv 0$. Let $\gamma\in (0,\delta_2]$ be as in Hypothesis~(hyp:mixing of the paper).
