{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.691074+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5a75695800e70a855d3dcb08bbbabc699e61c4989d76cf17fd69b8d8f9022003","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 8 of arXiv:2608.30967, 'B-Frames, B-Riesz basis, and Their tensor products'] For each $ i \in \{1,2\}$, let $\{ x^i_k \}_{k \in \mathcal{K}} \subset \mathcal{B}_i$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\{ x^1_j \otimes x^2_k\}_{j,k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a b-frame (or b-Riesz basis) for $\mathcal{Z}_1 \otimes \mathcal{Z}_2$ if and only if $\{ x^i_k\}_{k \in \mathcal{K}}$ is a b-frame (or b-Riesz basis) for $\mathcal{Z}_i $ for each $ i \in \{1,2\}$.

\item if, for each $ i \in \{1,2\}$, the b-frame bounds of the frame $\{ x^i_k \}_{k \in \mathcal{K}} $ are $A_i$ and $B_i$, then  the b-frame bounds of the b-frame $\{ x^1_j \otimes x^2_k\}_{j,k \in \mathcal{K}}$ are $A_1A_2$ and $B_1B_2$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:25:40.221838+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5a792abc2edb739db7ac721dc9187a5c86ecbeb7f72d2d9aa64dc0c727afe40f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:master-generating of arXiv:2609.02544, 'A finiteness theorem for geodesic Leech wheels'] A geodesic Leech labeling satisfies the following identity.
