{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.081143+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5aa858a0853ed92ce5f7da6958b74f28aecb92c79afdb76ea39380ae7a901878","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defini 5 of arXiv:2608.27934, 'An example of biquandle and an invariant of long virtual knots defined through it'] (Biquandle)\\
A {\it biquandle} $\left( B; \underline{\triangleright}, \overline{\triangleright}\right)$ is a triad of a set $B$ and operations $\underline{\triangleright},\ \overline{\triangleright}: B\times B \rightarrow B$ which satisfies: 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] for all $x \in B$, $x\ \underline{\triangleright}\ x = x\ \overline{\triangleright}\ x$, 
\item[(ii)] for all $y \in B$, the maps $(\cdot \ \underline{\triangleright} \ y),\ (\cdot \  \overline{\triangleright} \ y) : B\rightarrow B$ are invertible, 
\item[(iii)] the map $S:B\times B\rightarrow B\times B$ defined by $S\left(x,y \right)=\left( y\ \overline{\triangleright}\ x, x\ \underline{\triangleright}\ y\right)$ is invertible, and
\item[(iv)] for all $x,y,z\in B$, the following exchange lows hold. 
  \begin{align}
(x\ \underline{\triangleright}\ y)\ \underline{\triangleright} \ (z\ \underline{\triangleright}\ y)  &= (x\ \underline{\triangleright}\ z)\ \underline{\triangleright}\ (y\ \overline{\triangleright}\ z) \notag \\
(x\ \underline{\triangleright}\ y)\ \overline{\triangleright} \ (z\ \underline{\triangleright}\ y)  &= (x\ \overline{\triangleright}\ z)\ \underline{\triangleright}\ (y\ \overline{\triangleright}\ z) \notag  \\
(x\ \overline{\triangleright}\ y)\ \overline{\triangleright} \ (z\ \overline{\triangleright}\ y)  &= (x\ \overline{\triangleright}\ z)\ \overline{\triangleright}\ (y\ \underline{\triangleright}\ z) \notag 
     \end{align}
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:28:33.896411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5aabb0b7d3aa05dba16f7f7a99755a877f09554d9efdb36097f3b474de42a865","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Hproperty of arXiv:2505.01739, 'Stochastic dominance for linear combinations of infinite-mean risks'] Let $X\sim F$ where $F\in \mathcal{H}$. Then 
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (i)}]
\item 
$F^\beta\in \mathcal{H}$ where $\beta\ge 1$;
\item
$\phi(X)\in\mathcal{H}$ where $\phi$ is strictly increasing, $\phi(0)=0$, and $1/\phi^{-1}(1/x)$ is concave in $x\in\mathbb{R}_+$;
\item 
if $Y\sim G$ is independent of $X$ where $G\in\mathcal{H}$, then $\max\{X,Y\}\in\mathcal{H}$;
\item 
if in addition $X$ and another  random variable $Y$ have densities and $X\le_{\rm lr}Y$, then $Y\in\mathcal{H}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.330893+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5aadc81bdf5b2fdcf03b8c60dda790842bc1c70aec376285e739417f56ffd7e0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma omega-oo-both-adjoints of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The evaluation functor $\Omega^\infty\coloneqq\ev_0\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{lvl}\to\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_{\textup{gl},*}$ admits both a fully faithful left adjoint $\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty$ as well as a fully faithful right adjoint.
    \begin{proof}
        The existence of the right adjoint is a special case of Lemma~(lemma:ev-V-param-la of the paper). For the existence of the left adjoint, observe that $\ev_0\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Sp}\to\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_*$ is right Quillen by Lemma~(lemma:ev-RA of the paper), with left adjoint given by the usual suspension spectrum functor. It then only remains to verify the Beck--Chevalley condition. Unravelling definitions, this amounts to showing that for any $\alpha\colon G\to G'$, any cofibrant $X\in\cat{$\bm{G'}$-$\textbf{\textup{ L}}$-Top}_*$ and any cofibrant replacement $f\colon Y\to \alpha^*X$, the induced map $\Sigma^\infty f\colon \Sigma^\infty Y\to \Sigma^\infty \alpha^*X$ is a $G$-global level weak equivalence, i.e.\ for every $V$ the map $S^V\smashp f$ is an $(O(V)\times G)$-global weak equivalence. This is however simply an instance of Corollary~(cor:global-smash-well-based of the paper).

        Finally, it is clear that the ordinary unit $X\to\Omega^\infty \Sigma^\infty X$ is an isomorphism for every (cofibrant) $X$; thus, the derived unit $\textup{id}\to\Omega^\infty\,\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty$ is invertible, i.e.\ $\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty$ is fully faithful. 
        It follows formally (see e.g.~[haine2025fullyfaithfulfunctorspushouts]*{Lemma 1.2}) that the right adjoint of $\Omega^\infty$ is also fully faithful.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.144830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5aadd0b3cef18ead423be391098b1d4824ad53bb3ca933e92dda74116622a9fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm lorenz96 of arXiv:2607.05097, 'Asymptotics of Lyapunov Exponents and Phase Transitions for Fluids with Degenerate Forcing'] For each $z \in \mathbb{R}^K$, let $A(z): \mathbb{R}^{2K} \to \mathbb{R}^{2K}$ be given by
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        (A(z)x)_{2k} &= (x_{2k+1} - x_{2k-1})z_k \\
        (A(z)x)_{2k+1} &= (z_{k+1}-z_k)x_{2k}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
(the indices for $x$ are taken modulo $2K$ and those for $z$ are taken modulo $K$) 
and define $\lambda(z)$ to be the largest real part of the eigenvalues of $A(z)$. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{\epsilon \downarrow 0} \lambda_\epsilon^\perp = \mathbb{E}[\lambda(Z)] > 0 \,,
    \end{equation}
    where $Z$ is an $\mathbb{R}^K$-valued Gaussian random variable with mean $0$ and covariance matrix $\frac{1}{2}I$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.534649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5aafc009421e2de1f436f343743c8deb3678acd60c73de21e92b6676a8969943","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.weight_estimate of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given $k \in \mathbb{N}_+, N\in \mathbb{N}$ and $0 \leqslant h \leqslant k-1$, we define a weight function
		\begin{align}
			A_{\alpha, \beta}(e') := \sum_{\substack{{\scaleobj{1}{\square}},{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'\in \mathcal{P}^{(N,k)}_{h}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \\ e \in E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) }} |{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}|^\alpha|{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'|^\beta\boldsymbol{1}_{\{e\subseteq \mathsf{r}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \cap \mathsf{r}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}', e' \subseteq \mathsf{r}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'\}},
		\end{align}
		and there exists a finite positive constant $C(k,h,\mathsf{r},d)$, such that every cube ${{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m \in \mathcal{G}^{(N)}_k}$ is $N$-$\mathsf{stable}$ for the following estimate: for every $\alpha, \beta >0$ and every positive function ${w : E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \to \mathbb{R}_+}$, we have
		\begin{align}
