{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.399966+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ace9f6fe342e76c850b0c2881dc169bd17fade6d15562b7d7d5e9197dc9646d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary C: critical distance of arXiv:2511.11036, 'Analysis of a class of Recursive Distributional Equations Including the Resistance of the '] Assume that $p = \frac{1}{2}$ and $\mathbb{P} \{ 0 \leq D^{(0)} \leq +\infty \} = 1$ and let $q = \mathbb{P} \{ 0 < D^{(0)} < +\infty\}$.  Then $\mathbb{P} \{ 0 < D^{(n)} < +\infty \} = q$ for any $n$ and the law of $D^{(n)}$ conditional on this event has the following limit:
		\begin{align*}
			\lim_{N \to \infty} \mathbb{P} \{ N^{-\frac{1}{2}} \log D^{(N)} \leq 2 x \sqrt{ |\sigma| }  \, \mid \, 0 < D^{(N)} < + \infty \} = \left\{ \begin{array}{r l}
