{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:25:40.387630+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5af7a58693cd24b14a220ad60fe48fcd7b51deb45b85592f44178a7d4da69b3f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2503.21737, 'High-intensity Voronoi percolation on manifolds'] Let \(r, \rho > 0\).
    We say that a locally finite point set \(\xi\subset\mathbb R^d\) is \emph{\((r,\rho)\)-robust}, when the following holds for all \(x,y \in \xi \cap B_{\mathbb R^d}(0,r)\):
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If the (Euclidean) Voronoi cells of \(x\) and \(y\) intersect in \(B_{\mathbb R^d}(0,2r)\), then there exists a \(w_{xy} \in B_{\mathbb R^d}(0,2r)\) such that
        \begin{equation*}
            \mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(w_{xy},x) = \mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(w_{xy}, y) < \mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(w_{xy}, \xi\setminus\{x,y\}) - \rho \cdot r.
        \end{equation*}
        \item If the (Euclidean) Voronoi cells of \(x\) and \(y\) do not intersect then
        \begin{equation*}
            \max\{ \mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(w,x), \mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(w,y) \} > \mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(w, \xi) + \rho \cdot r
        \end{equation*}
        holds for all \(w \in B_{\mathbb R^d}(0,2r)\).
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.277453+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5af91eadb85384041b9f69e279e78ae99688b1376d704a37029daa9c6257b395","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:strongconditioned of arXiv:2507.14676, 'On the critical parameters of branching random walks'] {cf:BBHZ}}]
	For any BRW 

	and $A,B \subseteq X$, the following statements are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate} 
		\item there exists $x \in {X}$ such that ${\mathbf{q}}(x,B) < {\mathbf{q}}({x},A)$;
		\item there exists $x \in {X}$ such that ${\mathbf{q}}({x},B\setminus A) < {\mathbf{q}}({x},A)$;
		\item there exists $x \in {X}$ such that ${\mathbf{q}}({x},B) < {\mathbf{q}}_0({x},A)$;
		\item there exists $x \in {X}\setminus A$ such that,
		starting from $x$
		there is a positive chance of survival in $B$ without ever visiting $A$;
		\item there exists $x \in {X}$ such that, starting from $x$ there is a positive chance of survival in $B$ and extinction in $A$;
		\item 
		\[
		\inf_{x \in {X}\colon {\mathbf{q}}(x,B)<1} \frac{1-{\mathbf{q}}(x,A)}{1-{\mathbf{q}}(x,B)}=0.
		\]
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

