Hence for a process whose time reversal exchanges the excitation roles, the expected cross-area changes sign, as in the reversal experiment of (sec:numerics of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.236296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bccfc602b013796080e65dc3606dc21af53aa6d6bf29576de5b9c0aae0e8948","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: convergence of discrete multiple integrals of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	Assume that \(\omega\) is centered and satisfies~(equation eq: definition of a general heavy-tailed random variable of the paper) with \(\gamma\in (1,2)\).
	Let $\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}} : \Omega^k \to {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} $ be a symmetric function and let for every $\delta > 0$, $f_{\delta} : \Omega_{\delta}^k \to {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} $ be a symmetric function that vanishes on the diagonals, and assume that there is some $q \in (\gamma,2]$ such that
	\begin{equation*}
	\lim\limits_{\delta \downarrow 0} \| \ensuremath{\mathbf{f}} - \overline{f}_{\delta} \|_q = 0.
	\end{equation*}
	Then for every $s > \frac{D}{2}$, we have the following joint convergence in distribution in $H^{-s}_{\rm loc}(\Omega) \times \mathbb{R}$: as~\(\delta\downarrow 0\),
	\begin{equation*}
		(\zeta_{\delta}, X_{\delta}(f_{\delta})) \xrightarrow{(d)} (\boldsymbol\zeta, \mathbf{X}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}})),
	\end{equation*}
	where \(\boldsymbol\zeta\) is a \(\gamma\)-stable Lévy noise and $\mathbf{X}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}) = \int_{\Omega^k} \ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}(x_1,\ldots, x_k) \prod_{j=1}^k \boldsymbol\zeta(\mathrm{d} x_j)$ is defined in Proposition~(prop: martingale property of multiple integrals of the paper) (and is \(\boldsymbol\zeta\)-measurable).
	The conclusion extends to the joint convergence of a finite collection of chaos $(X_{\delta}(f_{j,\delta}))_{1 \, \leqslant\, j \, \leqslant\, M}$, provided for each $j \in \intg{1}{M}$, $f_{j,\delta}:\Omega^{k_j} \to {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} $ ($k_j \, \geqslant\, 1$) is symmetric, vanishes on the diagonals, and converges in $L^q_s(\Omega^{k_j})$ to some function $\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}_{j}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.534060+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bce72ef95a8874d659b39d2ebfb2673ad01dd408690c576d5ab0fbb348f3b14","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2603.28093, 'Random Stability of Random Variables'] Under the condition~\eqref{eq:ln-N}, any symmetric $N$-stable $X$ has the following characteristic function 
\begin{equation}

f(u) = \mathbb E\left[e^{\mathrm{i}uX}\right] = L(\beta |u|^{\alpha})
\end{equation}
for some $\beta > 0$ and $\alpha \in (0, 2]$, where $L$ is the Laplace transform of the standard $N$-stable random variable $Y$. This $X$ can be represented as 
\begin{equation}

X \stackrel{d}{=} Y^{1/\alpha}Z,\quad \mathbb E\left[e^{\mathrm{i}uZ}\right] = \exp\left(-\beta |u|^{\alpha}\right).
\end{equation}
Here, $Z$ is independent of $Y$, and is symmetric $\alpha$-stable. Conversely, any $X$ having representation~\eqref{eq:repr-N-stable-symm}, or characteristic function~\eqref{eq:repr-N-stable-symm}, is symmetric $N$-stable. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:15:34.384218+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bcfae1b8155b222c7b1fb5d425aa2afc4772807796b6742dccb85ee94acdf50","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2609.03248, 'Connections between colored restricted $b$-ary and ordinary partitions'] For integers $b\geq 2$ and $k\geq 0$ and for complex $q$ with $|q|<1$, we have
\begin{equation}
\prod_{j=0}^k\left(1+q^{b^j}+q^{2b^j}+\cdots+q^{(b-1)b^j}\right)
=\frac{1-q^{b^{k+1}}}{1-q},
\end{equation}
and consequently
\begin{equation}
\prod_{j=0}^\infty\left(1+q^{b^j}+q^{2b^j}+\cdots+q^{(b-1)b^j}\right)
=\frac{1}{1-q}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.593067+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bd22ba57720ac14bc6fd7749ad48c11f9481eae3d6f380f07da141cf3a5a779","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:estimation of v2 of arXiv:2512.12902, 'Non-equilibrium fluctuations for the \\stirring process with births and deaths'] Let $t\in(0,T]$. Then, for any $\zeta>0$ there is a constant $c>0$ such that
