{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.142618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bf249eff0a847a5731966a9cae14904ccf097184cc3e4819a92cc3200df5280","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] In the setting above, suppose $(h, A)$ is a random variable which is the coupling of an instance $h$ of a  GFF with a random element $A\subseteq D$ of $\Gamma(D)$. We say that $A$ is a \emph{local set} for $h$ if the following is true. Conditioned on $\fA$, (a regular version of) the conditional law of $h$ is that of $h_0+\fh$ where $h_0$ is the GFF with zero boundary data on $D\setminus A$ and the original boundary data elsewhere, and $\fh$ is an $\fA$-measurable random distribution 
    \begin{comment}
    (i.e., as a distribution-valued function on the space of distribution-set pairs $(h, A)$, $\fh$ is $\fA$-measurable)
    \end{comment}
    which is a.s.~harmonic on $D\setminus A$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.977754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bf4363977110ae95653f9213e48915cad64a562377af11e141d7ab6f2b4bfb1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem THM:3 of arXiv:2512.23957, 'Concentration and fluctuations of sine-Gordon measure around topological multi-soliton man'] Let $Q \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $Q\neq 0$, $\overline L_\varepsilon\sim L_\varepsilon=\varepsilon^{-\frac 12+\eta}$.

\begin{itemize}

\item[(i)] The joint distribution of the centers $(\xi_1,\dots, \xi_{|Q|})$ is given by the ordered statistics of $|Q|$ independent uniform random variables on $[-\overline L_\varepsilon, \overline L_\varepsilon]$
\begin{align*}
\rho_\varepsilon^Q \{ \pi_\varepsilon^{T}(\phi) \in A \}
= \frac{\big|A \cap \{-\overline L_\varepsilon \le \xi_1 \le \dots \le \xi_{|Q|} \le \overline L_\varepsilon    \} \big|}{ \big|\{-\overline L_\varepsilon \le \xi_1 \le \dots \le \xi_{|Q|} \le \overline L_\varepsilon    \} \big|  }(1+O(\varepsilon^{0+}) )
\end{align*}

\noindent 
as $\varepsilon \to 0$, where $A\subset  \{ -\overline L_\varepsilon \le \xi_1\le \cdots \le \xi_Q \le \overline L_\varepsilon   \}  $ is a measurable subset.

\medskip 

\item[(ii)] The marginal distribution, that is, each center $\xi_j$, has a Beta-shaped fluctuation
\begin{align*}
\rho_\varepsilon^Q\big\{ \pi^T_{j}(\phi) \in B  \big\}= \int_{B} f_j(x) dx \cdot (1+O(\varepsilon^{0+}))
\end{align*}

\noindent
as $\varepsilon \to 0$, where $B\subset [-\overline L_\varepsilon, \overline L_\varepsilon] $ is a measurable subset  and
\begin{align*}
f_j(x)= \frac{|Q|!}{ (2L_\varepsilon)^{|Q|} }\frac{(x+\overline L_\varepsilon)^{j-1}}{(j-1)!} \frac{ (\overline L_\varepsilon-x)^{|Q|-j}  }{(|Q|-j)! }, \; \; -\overline L_\varepsilon<x<\overline L_\varepsilon.
\end{align*}

\medskip 

\item[(iii)] The expected location of each soliton is given by
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{\rho_\varepsilon^Q } \big[  \pi^T_{j}(\phi)  \big]=\Big(-\overline L_\varepsilon+\frac{2\overline L_\varepsilon j}{|Q|+1} \Big) \cdot (1+O(\varepsilon^{0+}) ).
\end{align*}

\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.018415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bf66d8d641bbc1d819593d3338cfa32e0e89c34cc7a8e7a5e45c12f6546d43d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 15 (Shifted-Laguerre terminal-to-earlier block) of arXiv:2608.15137, '2608.15137'] Use the notation of Theorems~\ref{thm:fixed-orientation-kernel} and~\ref{thm:orientation-reversing-kernel}. In the fixed-orientation case, if $B\geq1$, then
\begin{equation}
 K_{10}(x,y)
 =
 (-1)^Ay^{r_0}
 \sum_{j=0}^{p-1}
 \frac{(j+A)!}{(j+r_0)!}
 L_{j+A}^{(r)}(x)L_j^{(r_0)}(y).
\end{equation}
If $B=0$, then
\begin{equation}
 K_{10}(x,y)
 =
 (-1)^A
 \sum_{j=0}^{p-1}
 \frac{(j+A)!}{(j+r_0)!}
 L_{j+A}^{(r)}(x)L_j^{(r_0)}(y).
\end{equation}
In the orientation-reversing case, $K_{10}$ is also given by \eqref{eq:shifted-laguerre-without-power}. All Laguerre parameters in these formulas are nonnegative integers.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.660238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bf6d7a0fe9ae0a6562283a21d04c0c30fd32ee1bd0610af78ad87630a5f9450","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2604.23462, 'Invariant measures for the open KPZ equation: the Gaussian case'] Let $R_{\ep} : \R \to \R$ denote the 2-periodic function $x \mapsto \sum_{n \in \Z} p(\ep,x-2n)$ for $p(t,x) = (2\pi t)^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/2t}$ given by the standard heat kernel. There is a finite constant $C = C(t,\kappa) > 0$ such that
        \begin{align*}
            \mbf{E}\left|\bb{E}^{\mrm{poly},\theta}_{t,x_1,x_2} \int_{0}^{t} R_{\ep}(X^1_s - X^2_s) d(X^1_s - X^2_s)\right| \leq C \ep^{1/4} |\log \ep|^2.
        \end{align*}
        In particular, as $\ep \to 0$ (and $t$ and $\kappa$ are kept fixed) this quantity vanishes in $L^1(\Omega^{\mrm{noise}})$ uniformly in $x_1$ and $x_2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.678155+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bf7f214b790dc4c01be6ec81ac43ea47537ee597d179b5b58f735899bd9816c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 28 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] Let $f : X \rightarrow Y$ be a topologically semi-small map such that $X$ is a closed topological manifolds, the fibered set $R$ is a closed stratified space, and $Y$ is a closed topological $n$-pseudomanifold. Set $B:=f^{-1}(R)$, let $R=\bigsqcup_{\beta}T_{\beta}$ be a suitable decomposition of the fibered set, and let $R_{\operatorname{rel}}$ be its relevant part. Furthermore, let the filtration $Y \supset Y_{n_{R}}(=R) \supset Y_{n_{R}-1} \supset \cdots$ be the refined stratification on $Y$ obtained by Proposition~\ref{RefinedStra}, where $n_{R}:= \dim(R)$. Set $S_{k}:= Y_{k} \setminus Y_{k-1}$, and assume the followings:
    	\begin{enumerate}
    		\item for each $r \in R$ the fiber $f^{-1}(r)$ is a closed orientable topological manifold;
    		\item the normal bundle of the manifold $f^{-1}(S_{k})$ in $X$ exists, for each $0 \leq k \leq n_{R}$, and is orientable, and for each $S_{k}\in R_{\operatorname{rel}}$ and each $r\in S_{k}$ there is a small open neighborhood $U$ of $r$ such that the connecting homomorphism in the Gysin sequence of the normal bundle of $S_{k}$ restricted to $f^{-1}(U)$ vanishes in degree $\operatorname{codim}_{X}(B)-1$.
    			\end{enumerate}
    		Let $R(f|_{X \setminus B})_{*}\,\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X \setminus B}\simeq \underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{Y \setminus R}\oplus \mathcal{L}$, where $\mathcal{L}$ is the local system on $Y \setminus R$ associated to the covering map $f|_{X \setminus B}$. If each $\operatorname{codim}_{Y}(S_{k})$ is even and $\operatorname{codim}_{X}(B)\geq 2$, then
    	\begin{align}
    		Rf_{\ast}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X}[n]\simeq \bigoplus_{S_{k} \in R_{\operatorname{rel}}}\iota_{k\ast}(IC_{\overline{S_{k}}} (R^{0}f\vert_{f^{-1}(S_{k})}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{S_{k}})) \oplus IC_{Y}(\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{Y \setminus R} \oplus \mathcal{L}),
    	\end{align}
    	where $\iota_{k}:\overline{S_{k}} \hookrightarrow Y$ be the canonical closed inclusion, and $IC^{\overline{p}}_{Y}$ is Deligne's intersection complex with respect to the above refined stratification and the perversity $\overline{p}\in\{\overline{n},\overline{m}\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.616173+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bfa7f420b749dfcf02630ab3bd4a34d3ab8a1c86831e243b5ddc9e4a6ea3eb9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:DARCMasLL of arXiv:2603.16824, 'Age-dependent random connection models with arc reciprocity: clustering and connectivity'] Let \(H\) be a non-negative functional that acts on rooted digraphs and their root and that only depends on a bounded graph neighbourhood of the root in the undirected sense. Assume further that the family
