		\item[(ii)] There exists $\theta_{\rm crit} \in (0,\infty)$ such that, if $\theta<\theta_{\rm crit}$, $(1/c, \ldots, 1/c)$ is the unique optimizer of (equation eq:potts_unconstrained_opt of the paper) and if $\theta>\theta_{\rm crit}$, there exist exactly $c$ distinct optimizers, none of which are uniform. With $(x_{\theta,1},\ldots ,x_{\theta,c})$ denoting the unique optimizer satisfying $x_{\theta,1}>\max_{2\le r\le c} x_{\theta,r}$, the map $\theta\mapsto (x_{\theta,1},\ldots ,x_{\theta,c})$ is continuous on $(\theta_{\rm crit},\infty)$.

