{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.723939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bfda67b846bafdff2f29c112af2db4d0fab8a7254349e52af4d16fc6a01f9b6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] Let $\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{ST}}$ be a first-order language (see, e.g., [shoenfield_mathematical_2018]) with a binary predicate symbol $\sqsubset$ and unary predicate symbols $V_p$ for every $p \in \mathord{\mathrm{PropVar}}$.
  For $\varphi \in \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$, and a free variable $x$, the \emph{standard translation} $\mathrm{ST}_x(\varphi)$ is defined inductively:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\bot) = \bot$, $\mathrm{ST}_x(p) = V_p(x)$;
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\psi_1 \circledcirc \psi_2) = \mathrm{ST}_x(\psi_1) \circledcirc \mathrm{ST}_x(\psi_2)$, for $\circledcirc \in \set{ \land, \lor, \to }$;
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\Box \psi) = \forall y\,(x \sqsubset y \to \mathrm{ST}_y(\psi))$, where $y$ is fresh;
    \item $\mathrm{ST}_x(\Diamond \psi) = \exists y\,(x \sqsubset y \land \mathrm{ST}_y(\psi))$, where $y$ is fresh.
  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.322737+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5bfed6a053cd76d00faf95bb7f2deb8af8dbaca7819566970b3da536606098d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:linear_compactness of arXiv:2608.31071, 'Global smooth solutions to the inhomogeneous Landau--Fermi--Dirac equation'] Suppose that $\mathcal A_n$ and $\mathcal B_n$ are families of measurable coefficients such that: $\mathcal A_n$ are uniformly bounded in $L^\infty([0,T]\times \mathbb T^3\times\mathbb R^3)$; $\mathcal B_n$ are uniformly bounded in $L^2([0,T]\times \mathbb T^3\times \mathbb R^3)$; and
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal A_n(t,x,v) \ge \lambda(t,x,v)\,\mathrm{Id} , \quad \text{where } \quad \lambda(t,x,v)\text{ is uniformly positive on  compact subsets of }(0,T)\times \mathbb T^3\times\mathbb R^3.
    \end{equation*}
    Further suppose that $f_{\rm in}^n$ is a family of initial data that is uniformly bounded in $L^2_{\rm loc}(\mathbb T^3\times\mathbb R^3)$. Then, any corresponding family $f_n\colon [0,T]\times \mathbb T^3\times \mathbb R^3$ of weak solutions to (equation eq:linear_kolmogorov of the paper) is locally compact in $L^2_{t,x,v}$. 

    If additionally $\left\langle v \right\rangle^\sigma f_{\rm in}^n$ is uniformly bounded in $L^2_{x,v}$ for some $\sigma > 0$, then $\left\{ f_n \right\}$ is compact in $L^2(0,T;L^2_{x,v})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:21:06.876606+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c01428561348b9b932a2bdfa0eb6a348effe2a770c60a803edfa1c1651992c0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.21255, 'Laws of the iterated logarithm for random Dirichlet series with general weights'] Let $N$ be a positive integer-valued function satisfying $\lim_{s\to 0+}N(s)=\infty$. For all $\rho>0$,
