{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.503241+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c413068b9a5bc0977dee05d9d5271a7bf033616a23a0546cba7f9f767f0ac3f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 61 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] \cite[Thm. 3.1]{Friz-Hairer}
Let $V$ be a Banach space, and let $\bfx = (X,\bbx)$ be a stochastic process $\bfx = (\bfx_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ such that for each $\omega \in \Omega$ we have paths $X(\omega) : [0,T] \to V$ and $\bbx(\omega) : [0,T]^2 \to V \otimes V$ satisfying Chen's relation \eqref{Chen-relation}. Let $q \geq 2$ and $\beta > 0$ be constants such that $\beta - \frac{1}{q} > \frac{1}{3}$. We assume there is a constant $C > 0$ such that
\begin{align*}
| X_{s,t} |_{L^q} &\leq C |t-s|^{\beta} \quad \text{for all $s,t \in [0,T]$,}
\\ | \bbx_{s,t} |_{L^{q/2}} &\leq C |t-s|^{2 \beta} \quad \text{for all $s,t \in [0,T]$.}
\end{align*}
Then there exists a version of $\bfx$ which belongs to $\scrc^{\alpha}([0,T],V)$ for every $\alpha \in (\frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{2}]$ with $\alpha < \beta - \frac{1}{q}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.023648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c4162dd4fcdde4459eaba45faf64fefebaa929a3fb2b4cda35826fb2da3961e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theo:general of arXiv:2609.03858, 'High-Dimensional Learning Dynamics of \\ Attention-Indexed Models'] Under Assumption (assum:attention-index of the paper), the joint distribution of the quadratic forms converges weakly to a multivariate Gaussian, and expectations converge as follows:
\begin{equation}
