{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:15:34.405760+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c6cef8af000ba3c93106530acf0136308e76aaaefd1b3151f239594fca5e6bd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Beauville's conjecture for smooth toric varieties) of arXiv:2609.03282, 'Characteristic-free characterizations of projective space for smooth toric varieties'] Let $X=X_\Sigma$ be an $n$-dimensional smooth projective toric variety over $\Bbbk$, and let $\cL$ be an ample line bundle on $X$. Suppose that
\[
H^0\left(X,\bigwedge^pT_X\otimes\cL^{-p}\right)\neq0
\]
for some integer $1\leq p\leq n$. Then one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
\item $(X,\cL)\simeq(\PP^n_\Bbbk,\cO_{\PP^n_\Bbbk}(1))$;
\item $p=n=1$ and $(X,\cL)\simeq(\PP^1_\Bbbk,\cO_{\PP^1_\Bbbk}(2))$;
\item $p=n=2$ and $(X,\cL)\simeq(\PP^1_\Bbbk\times\PP^1_\Bbbk,\cO(1,1))$.
\end{enumerate}
Conversely, each of these polarized varieties, with the indicated values of $p$, satisfies the nonvanishing condition. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.547790+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c6e9777fb21bd7b654f1fce050c4c7f3b9132555e8a38eba7cc15beaef85dd2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 31 (The Hausdorff dimension) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be a measurable set. For $\delta>0$ we call a $\delta$-cover any collection of open sets $U_j, j \in J$, such that 
    \begin{equation*}
        A \subseteq \bigcup_{j \in J}U_j; \hspace{0.5cm} \text{sup}_{j \in J}\text{Leb}(U_j) < \delta.
    \end{equation*}
    We define $\mathcal{H}^s_\delta(A) = \inf \sum_{j \in J}|U_j|^s$ where the infimum is taken over all $\delta$-covers, and $\mathcal{H}^s(A) = \lim_{\delta \rightarrow 0} \mathcal{H}^s_\delta(A)$. The \emph{Hausdorff dimension} of $A$, which we denote $\text{dim}(A)$, is defined to be the unique number such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{H}^s(A) = \infty \text{   for all   } 0 \leq s < \text{dim}(A); \hspace{0.25cm} \text{   and   } \hspace{0.25cm} \mathcal{H}^s(A) = 0 \text{   for all   } \text{dim}(A) < s < \infty.
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.209694+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c704fa863e03212fb123f22c037d8e6b08e66134b9a6a08ad18f53e57585afe","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2608.03771, '\vspace{-5ex}\bfseries\scshape{Local law and delocalization for the Sachdev--Ye--Kitaev mo'] For any $\delta>0$ we have
\begin{equation}

\frac{1}{D}\sum_{j=1}^D\E[\mathrm{IPR}(\psi_j)]\le \delta\int_0^\infty \e^{-\delta t} R_N(t)\,\d t, \qquad\quad R_N(t):=\frac{1}{D}\sum_{x\in \{0,1\}^{n}}\E[|\langle e_x, \e^{-\i t H_q} e_x\rangle|^2],
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.600529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7958a152dd23d5fa3933b264c7575585a531023c17c14a513125cebeb1fe92","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:area of arXiv:2606.21049, '\textsc{Locality of rough path lifts}'] Let $\gamma\in (0,1]$ and $(X,Y) \in C^\gamma \times C^\gamma$.
An \emph{area process} on $(X,Y)$ is an element
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{A}(X,Y) = (\mathbb{A}_{s,t}(X,Y))_{(s,t) \in \triangle} \in B
\end{equation*}
such that, for all $0 \leq s \leq u \leq t \leq 1$, one has the \emph{Chen identity}
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{A}_{s,t}(X,Y) = \mathbb{A}_{s,u}(X,Y) + \mathbb{A}_{u,t}(X,Y) + \frac12(X_{s,u}Y_{u,t} - Y_{s,u}X_{u,t})\;.
\end{equation}
For a subset $P \subset C^\gamma \times C^\gamma$, an area process on $P$ is a map $\mathbb{A} \colon P \to B$ such that $\mathbb{A}(X,Y)$ is an area process on $(X,Y)$ for all $(X,Y) \in P$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.997987+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7aaf01e1aee63b5f26483611e6eb8e5406096ada0506616dd53db60996dea4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop conv of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Take $m\in \mathbb N$, $\vec \mathbf k \in \mathbb R^m_+ $, and $\vec \boldsymbol{\tau}\in \mathbb R_{\ge 0}^m$ such that $\boldsymbol{\tau}_1\le \cdots \le \boldsymbol{\tau}_m$, and $C_1>0$.
For any large enough $N\in \mathbb N$, we take $\vec k$ and $N\ge N_1\ge \cdots \ge N_m$ satisfying (equation eq_x1 of the paper).
Then as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}   
2^{-m}\left(\frac{\hat{\mathcal P}_{k_m}^{N_m,2N/\beta}}{(2\sqrt{N_mN})^{k_m}}+\frac{\hat{\mathcal P}_{k_m+1}^{N_m,2N/\beta}}{(2\sqrt{N_mN})^{k_m+1}}\right) \cdots \left(\frac{\hat{\mathcal P}_{k_1}^{N_1,2N/\beta}}{(2\sqrt{N_1N})^{k_1}}+\frac{\hat{\mathcal P}_{k_1+1}^{N_1,2N/\beta}}{(2\sqrt{N_1N})^{k_1+1}}\right) [1]_{x_1=\dots=x_N=0}    
\end{equation}
converges to $\mathbf L_\beta(\vec\mathbf k,\vec\boldsymbol{\tau})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.634774+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7cce1812de95a1e383da15ff482a75d550533f932aff9800514da42a761a02","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: character bound finite level general with M sigma of arXiv:2605.28770, 'Cutoff profiles for conjugacy invariant random walks on symmetric groups'] Let $r\geq 0$. As $n\to \infty$, uniformly over all $\lambda \vdash n$ such that $\lambda_1 = n-r$ and every $\sigma \in {\ensuremath{\mathfrak{S}}} _n$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        \left| \ensuremath{\textup{ch}}^\lambda(\sigma) \right| \lesssim_r M(\sigma)^r;
    \end{equation}
and equivalently,
\begin{equation}
     \left| \ensuremath{\textup{ch}}^\lambda(\sigma) \right| \lesssim_r d_\lambda^{J(\sigma)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.096477+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7f0844997233c38a40f7ede77115283fad401dc148f192597bc132be28719b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 (Stationary target mass) of arXiv:2604.26298, 'The Expiring Coupon Collector\\ Sliding-Window Surjection Flux and Rare-Entry Laws'] For $M\ge n$,
\[
        \pi_{n,M}:=\Pbb(W_t\in A_{n,M})
        =\frac{n!S(M,n)}{n^M}
        =\sum_{j=0}^n(-1)^j\binom{n}{j}\left(1-\frac jn\right)^M.
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.796498+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7f6d1e2d861a44a4bb8153f249b28bacddd058134e11336e875edfb118638b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:tailrl-binary-recovery of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] Suppose $r_i\in\{0,1\}$ and let
$M:=\sum_{i=1}^N\mathbf 1\{r_i=1\}$. If $M>0$, then every successful
rollout receives weight $1/M$ and every unsuccessful rollout receives weight
zero. Hence the uncentered TailRL{} estimator reduces to
\begin{equation}
g_{\mathrm{TailRL}}^{(N)}(x)
=
\frac{1}{M}
\sum_{i:r_i=1}S_i,

\end{equation}
which is the MaxRL estimator. After mean-centering, successful rollouts have
weight $1/M-1/N$ and unsuccessful rollouts have weight $-1/N$. If $M=0$,
all uncentered and centered weights are zero.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.850267+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7f9d0ca2946c8ccf8af26290bcd9809c28c16c7997814f06888d70b7e16708","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 14 of arXiv:2609.03869, '\bf Sharp asymptotics for a transport model with a nonlocal condition of the Fisher-KPP ty'] Assume that $u_0(i,x)$ is a nontrivial, absolutely continuous and compactly supported function  in $[0,i_\dagger)\times\R$, which is  nonnegative for $x\ge 0$ and nonpositive  for $x\le 0$, uniformly in $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$, such that
	\begin{equation}

	\int_0^{+\infty}\Big(  \K_* *  u_0(i,x)+ \K_* *  u_0(i,-x)\Big)\md x=0~~~~~ \hbox{for all $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$.}
\end{equation}
	 Let $\mathbf\Phi$ be the solution   to the linear renewal equation \eqref{renewal eqn=3} for $t>0$ and $x\in\R$ starting with initial condition $u_0$. Choose $T>i_\dagger$ sufficiently large so that Theorem \ref{thm_3.1} holds true for all $t\ge T$. Then, up to increasing $T$ if necessary, the function $\mathbf\Phi$ has the following asymptotics  
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathbf\Phi(t,x)\sim \frac{x-c_*t}{t^{\frac{3}{2}}}e^{-\frac{(x-c_*t)^2}{4bt}},~~~~~~~~t\ge T,~~|x-c_*t|\le O(t^{\frac{1}{2}+\varsigma}),
	\end{equation*}
	for $\varsigma\in(0,\frac{1}{2})$ small enough. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.977932+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c80dba5b0e525ad9cba898ae6c241670d66acbcc82448650d39462a8ed8824a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:critical_dim_moments_main of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] If $d=3\alpha<6$ then 
\begin{equation}

\mathbb P_{\beta_c}(|K|\geq n) \sim \text{\emph{const.}}\; \frac{(\log n)^{1/4}}{\sqrt{n}}
\end{equation}
as $n\to \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.778943+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c82de37e2f27477f9b10e02cd7e0668294400c69b379d83b13fce1a16b49c1f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2603.25615, 'Fourier dimension of Mandelbrot cascades on planar curves'] For some $p >4$, let $\Phi(t)\lesssim t^{-p}$ when $t \geq 1$. Let $X_1,\ldots, X_N$ be independent random variables with zero expectation such that
\begin{equation}
\PP(X_k>t)\le\Phi(t)    
\end{equation}
for all $1\le k\le N$. Then, for all $a_1,a_2,\ldots a_N\ge 0$, $M>1$, and $t>0$,
\[\PP\left(\sum_{k=1}^N a_k X_k>t\right)=N\Phi(M)+\exp\left(-\lambda t+O(\lambda^2)\sum_{k=1}^N a_k^2\right)\,,\]
where 
\begin{equation}
    \lambda=\frac{q\log M}{M\max_{1\le k\le N}a_k}\,.
\end{equation}
and $q\leq p/2-1$. The $O$-constant only depends on $p$ and $\Phi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.330951+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c8996ff7e212c2f34a4f13ab6297d3d0f6f23e658a9a1c870bafdb53261a56d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 13 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] Let $(\xi^R,\Xi^R)$ be the underlying MAP of $R$ with respect to the norm $\|\cdot\|_{\text{L}^1}$ in dimension $d=2$. For every $f\in C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^+)$ satisfying the boundary conditions




	\begin{enumerate}[i]
		\item $(1,1,-1)\cdot \grad{f}(w,0,1)=0\enspace\enspace  \forall w\in\mathbb{R};$
		\item $(1,-1,1)\cdot \grad{f}(w,1,0)=0\enspace\enspace \forall w\in\mathbb{R},$
	\end{enumerate}
	the process
	\begin{equation*}
		f(\xi_t^R,\Xi^R_t)-f(\xi_0^R,\Xi^R_0)-\int_0^t Af(\xi^R_s,\Xi_s^R) ds,
	\end{equation*}
	for $t<\varsigma\coloneqq\inf\{s>0:\Xi^R_s=\dagger\}=\int_0^\upsilon \|R_s\|_{\text{L}^1}^{-\alpha} ds$, is a martingale, where the operator $A:C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^+)\to C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^+)$ is defined by
	\begin{equation*}
		Af(y,\boldsymbol{\theta})={\rm e}^{\alpha y} \mathcal{A}f(y,\boldsymbol{\theta}),
	\end{equation*}
	where $\mathcal{A}:C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^+)\to C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^+)$ is the operator









	\begin{align*}
		\mathcal{A}f(y,\boldsymbol{\theta})=& c_1\int_y^\infty (f(y,\begin{pmatrix}
			\theta^{(1)}+(1-{\rm e}^{y-x})\theta^{(2)} \\ {\rm e}^{y-x}\theta^{(2)}
		\end{pmatrix})-f(y,\boldsymbol{\theta}) \\
		&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad+(1-{\rm e}^{x-y})\mathbbm{1}_{(y,\log(1+{\rm e}^y))}(x)\begin{pmatrix}
			1 \\ \theta^{(2)} \\ -\theta^{(2)}
		\end{pmatrix}\cdot \grad{f(y,\boldsymbol{\theta})}){\rm e}^x({\rm e}^x-{\rm e}^y)^{-(1+\alpha)} dx \\
		&+c_1\int_y^\infty (f(y,\begin{pmatrix}
			{\rm e}^{y-x}\theta^{(1)} \\ (1-{\rm e}^{y-x})\theta^{(1)}+\theta^{(2)}
		\end{pmatrix})-f(y,\boldsymbol{\theta}) \\
		&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad+(1-{\rm e}^{x-y})\mathbbm{1}_{(y,\log(1+{\rm e}^y))}(x)\begin{pmatrix}
			1 \\ -\theta^{(1)} \\ \theta^{(1)}
		\end{pmatrix}\cdot \grad{f(y,\boldsymbol{\theta})}){\rm e}^x({\rm e}^x-{\rm e}^y)^{-(1+\alpha)} dx,
	\end{align*}
	where $c_1\coloneqq\frac{-\Gamma(1+\alpha)\sin(\pi\alpha)}{\pi}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.856448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c8cfe2d48036f25afaa0c88de485380d978db0722fee8ba412ca39e58948640","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2605.20115, 'Moment bounds on correctors for the degenerate random conductance model'] There exist $C_0,C_1,C_2\ge1$ depending only on $\Lambda$, such that for $C_2\le p\le C_1^{-1}\gamma$, the (stationary) corrector gradient satisfies
