{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.888747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d3fa210eed17995755b20584e8794d152f2fce455182be8afff9c85c1cbb94e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $A,B\subset\mathbb{R}^m$ be finite sets and let $\alpha\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^A$ and $\beta\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^B$. Consider the diagonal embedding $\Delta:\mathbb{R}^m\to \mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^m$, that is $\Delta(x):=(x,x)$. Then, in law, we have the equality
