{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.119108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dbc7cd2a4b1809c632afb7cf4f6d9ad1df8fa7bf8afa0dd704f776963b1949f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 39 (Holley--Stroock) of arXiv:2604.04823, '2604.04823'] Let~$(\mathcal X, \mathcal B, m)$ be a measure space, and let~$\tilde p_1, \tilde p_2$ be non-negative measurable functions such that $\tilde p_1, \tilde p_2 \in L^1(m)$ and do not vanish simultaneously. Define probability measures~$\pi_i$ by $d\pi_i/dm \propto \tilde p_i$, and let~$P_i(x,dy)$ be the transition kernels of Metropolis chains with stationary measures~$\pi_i$ and a common proposal kernel~$Q(x,dy)$.

Assume that the proposal kernel is symmetric in the following sense: for each~$x \in \mathcal X$, $Q(x,dy)$ admits a density~$q(x,y)$ on~$\mathcal X \setminus \{x\}$ with respect to~$m(dy)$, and $q(x,y) = q(y,x)$ for all~$x,y \in \mathcal X$.

If there exist constants~$a,b>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
    a \leq \frac{\tilde p_1(x)}{\tilde p_2(x)} \leq b\,, \quad \forall x \in \mathcal X,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
   (b^{-1}a)^2\,\gap(P_2) \leq \gap(P_1) \leq (a^{-1}b)^2\,\gap(P_2)\,.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.824147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dbcbe452a6b82ef2cbccc3218bd82135ae66d24f25a26b90e2c14949569ece6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:hilbert-strata of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $h\geq1$, $k\geq2$, and a field $K$.  Recall the open normalized
exponent-shape simplex
\[
 \mathcal S_k=
 \{x\in\mathbb R^k:0=x_1<x_2<\cdots<x_k=1\}
\]
and put
\[
 N_h(x)=\left|\{u\cdot x:u\in\mathcal U_{h,k}\}\right|.
\]
For every $n\in\mathcal R(h,k)$, the locus
\[
 \mathcal H_{h,k}(n)=\{x\in\mathcal S_k:N_h(x)=n\}
\]
is a finite disjoint union of relatively open rational polytopes, and
\begin{equation}
 \max\{\dim P:P\text{ is a cell of }\mathcal H_{h,k}(n)\}
 =k-2-r_{h,k}(n).
\end{equation}
For rational $x\in\mathcal S_k$, choose $D\geq1$ with
$Dx\in\mathbb Z^k$ and let $C_x\subset\mathbb P^{k-1}$ be the projective
monomial curve defined by the kernel of
\[
 K[X_1,\ldots,X_k]\longrightarrow K[s,t],
 \qquad X_i\longmapsto s^{D(1-x_i)}t^{Dx_i}.
\]
The kernel is independent of the choice of $D$, and
\[
 H_{C_x}(h)=N_h(x).
\]
Consequently, $r_{h,k}(n)$ is exactly the codimension in exponent-shape
space of the largest stratum with degree-$h$ Hilbert value $n$.
If $h\geq2$, $k\geq3$, and $A_q$ is uniform in $\binom{[q]}k$, then
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(|hA_q|=n)
 =k!c_{h,k,n}q^{-r_{h,k}(n)}
  +O_{h,k}(q^{-r_{h,k}(n)-1}),
\end{equation}
and hence the same integer has the four equivalent descriptions
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 r_{h,k}(n)
 =k-\deg F^{(k)}_{h,n}
 =\operatorname{codim}_{\mathcal S_k}\mathcal H_{h,k}(n)
 =-\lim_{q\to\infty}
   \frac{\log\mathbb P(|hA_q|=n)}{\log q}.}
\end{equation}
Here the codimension of a finite polyhedral union means the codimension of
its largest-dimensional cell.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.922790+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dbe2429855825dab1b1e9fb9879f89676826afad6978cbd2c4f5962ab1319c7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem semicircle of arXiv:2412.02087, 'The spectral distribution of random graphs with given degree sequences'] Consider the degree sequence $\{ d_1^{(n)}, d_2^{(n)}, \cdots, d_n^{(n)} \}$ with total degree $D^{(n)}=d_1^{(n)}+d_2^{(n)}+\cdots+d_n^{(n)}$ for each $n\geq 1$. Suppose that $\min\{d_i^{(n)}\}_{i\in [n]} \gg \sqrt{D^{(n)}/n}$ as $n \rightarrow \infty$. Consider the normalized Laplacian matrix $M^{(n)}$ as defined in (equation e:defM of the paper). Then, the moments of the empirical eigenvalue distribution of $M^{(n)}$ converge to the moments of the semicircle distribution:
    \begin{align}

    \lim_{n\rightarrow \infty}\frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}[{\rm Tr}[({M^{(n)}})^k]] =  
        \begin{cases} 
            C_{k/2} & \text{for even $k$}, \\
            0 & \text{for odd $k$},
        \end{cases}
   \end{align}
    where $C_{k/2}$ is the $k/2$-th Catalan number. Moreover, the empirical eigenvalue distribution $\mu_n$ of $M^{(n)}$ weakly converges to the semicircle distribution $\rho_{\rm sc}$ in probability:
    \begin{align}
        \mu_n \xrightarrow{D} \rho_{\rm sc},\quad  \text{in probability}.
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:09.038514+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dbe7b1e1e390ccd8d7bff47ad5beff4d21547ffc420be5028e8d7367b7fdbd7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop mom-cor of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] For any $C_1>0$, there exists $C=C(C_1)>0$ such that the following holds.
For any $N\in\mathbb N$, $N>C_1^3$, $0\le \boldsymbol{\tau}_1<\boldsymbol{\tau}_2\le C_1$, $\boldsymbol{\tau}_2-\boldsymbol{\tau}_1\ge N^{-2/3}$, $C_1^{-1}N^{2/3}<k<C_1N^{2/3}$, we have
