{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.563552+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ddb9bf18cfcd90c6274dbbc56f01b26daf5d2eb5b7d4c29ffed758a2e89acf6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.tail-conversion of arXiv:2605.26082, 'Quantitative Einstein relation for reversible diffusions in a random environment'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and $\sigma\in(0,1]$. Let $T\colon\Omega\to[0,\infty)$ and $\lambda_0\colon\Omega\to(0,1]$ be random variables. Suppose there exist deterministic $C_T<\infty$ and $c_T>0$ such that
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb{Q}(T\ge M)\le C_T\exp(-c_T M^\sigma)\qquad\text{for every }M\ge 0\, ,
		\end{equation}
		and that for every $p>0$ there exists a deterministic $C_p<\infty$ such that
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb{Q}(\lambda_0\le 2^{-k})\le C_p 2^{-pk}\qquad\text{for every }k\in\mathbb{N}\, .
