{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.235586+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ddd3e4bea405715fb427330ee0fabb706767595edcbb4b3e66ecca6093c9a7f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 72 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Exactness and closedness]
Given $M \subset H_0$ and $\beta \in C^1(M)$. $\beta$ is called \textit{exact} if $\exists \alpha \in C^0(M)$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\beta=d^0\alpha. 
\end{equation*}
$\beta$ is called \textit{closed} if 
\begin{equation*}
d^1\beta=0.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.373842+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5de1bf771244163de8c61d90e0d02163706a0bd235765be5fe74597fffcb35e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conjecture conj: Discriminant of arXiv:2603.26932, 'On the satisfaction frequency of spectral characterization conditions'] Suppose that $\bM$ is chosen uniformly at random from all symmetric $n\times n$ matrices with $\{0,1 \}$-valued entries. 
    Then, for every prime $p$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to \infty}\bbP\bigl(p \nmid \Delta_{\bM}\bigr) = \Bigl( 1 - \frac{1}{p}\Bigr) \prod_{k=1}^\infty\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^{2k}}\Bigr). 
    \end{equation}
    Further, for every odd prime $p$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to \infty}\bbP\bigl(p^2 \nmid \Delta_{\bM}\bigr) = \Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^2} \frac{3p-1}{p+1}\Bigr)\prod_{k=1}^\infty\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^{2k}}\Bigr) 
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, the limiting probability for being odd and square-free factorizes over the constituent primes:
    \begin{align}
        \lim_{n\to \infty} \bbP\bigl(\Delta_{\bM} \textnormal{ is odd and square-free}\bigr) &= \frac{6}{7} \prod_{p \textnormal{ prime}}\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^2} \frac{3p-1}{p+1}\Bigr)\prod_{k=1}^\infty\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^{2k}}\Bigr) \\ 
        &= 0.1686\ldots \nonumber 
    \end{align} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:16.145993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5de3ebe5017cd51f2127df6326e41a80bb2e0b9414ff2b9736f364ccab558ab5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2604.13149, 'The Distributional Tail of Worst--Case Quickselect'] For all \(a>0\) and integers \(j\ge 1\), \(\ell\in\{0,\dots,j-1\}\) with \(r=j-\ell\ge 1\),
