{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.906717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5de57ab784801a831e551c89a531480f39937896560815087758b714b7f0668f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:log-normality-SHF of arXiv:2511.08479, '\Large From disordered systems to the Critical 2D Stochastic Heat Flow'] For a Euclidean ball $B(x,\delta) := \big\{y \in \mathbb{R}^2 \colon \ |y-x| < \delta \big\}$, denote the uniform distribution over it by
${\ensuremath{\mathcal U}} _{B(x,\delta)}(\cdot) := \frac{1}{\pi \delta^2} \, \mathrm{1}_{B(x,\delta)}(\cdot)$.
Then for any $t > 0$ and $x\in\mathbb{R}^2$, the following convergence in distribution holds:
\begin{equation}
	\forall \varrho \in (0,\infty) \colon \qquad
	\mathscr{Z}_{t}^{\vartheta} \big({\ensuremath{\mathcal U}} _{B(x,\sqrt{e^{\varrho \vartheta}})}\big)
	\ \xrightarrow[\ \vartheta \to -\infty \ ]{d} \
	e^{{\ensuremath{\mathcal N}} (0,\sigma^2) - \frac{1}{2}\sigma^2}
	\qquad \text{with } \sigma^2 = \log(1+\varrho) \,.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.312157+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5de6fee76373103a32b27458e58f7c97444a13947e1933696a8be3d34b77de95","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2604.08127, 'Asymptotics of Brownian occupation measures \\ with unusually large intersections'] A sequence $\widetilde{\mu}^{\otimes p}$ in $\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_1^{\otimes p}$ converges to $\{ \widetilde{\alpha}_i^{\otimes p} \}$ in $(\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\otimes p}, \mathbf{D}^{\otimes p})$ if there exists a decomposition
    \[
    \mu_n^{\otimes p} = \sum_{i \in I} \alpha_{i, n}^{\otimes p} + \beta_n^{\otimes p}
    \]
    and points $x_{i, n} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\alpha_{i, n}^j \ast \delta_{x_i^n} \to \alpha_i^j$ weakly for all $i \in I$ and  $j = 1 , \dots , p$,
        \item $\beta_n^j$ \emph{totally disintegrates}, i.e., $\lim_{n \to \infty} \sup_{x \in \mathbb{R}^d} \beta_n^j (B(x, R)) = 0$ for any finite $R > 0$.
        \item Distinct sequences are \emph{widely separated}, i.e., $\lim_{n \to \infty} |x_{i_1}^n - x_{i_2}^n| = \infty$ for any $i_1 \ne i_2$.
    \end{enumerate}
    As a consequence, the inclusion $\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_1 \hookrightarrow \widetilde{\mathcal{X}}_{\le 1}^{\otimes p}$ is a continuous injection and $\widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_1$ is a dense subset of $\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}_{\le 1}^{\otimes p}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.595007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5de7322382d2e477af769c2dd6e409320ea8b4f1d26cf48190c668fad670a374","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma-5.5b of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Let~$\beta\in(0,\beta_\text{\rm c}]$, $\alpha\in(-\ffrac12,\ffrac12)$ and let $w_1$ be as in (equation Eq:2.8 of the paper). Then
