{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.011602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dfefd91c8d04f0c87613810011002e06ff69cd6a476d50d5f0f7d98e4d35871","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2608.14802, 'Dynamical Gibbs-non-Gibbs transitions for finite-alphabet models on trees'] Let $t> 0$, $\theta>4$ and $k\in \{2,3\}$. Further, define the sequence $(z_{n}^k(t))_{n\in \N_0}$ recursively by
    \begin{equation*}
        z_0^k(t):=\begin{cases}
            (x_-,1),~&\text{if}~~l=l_-^{(1)},\\
            (1,1),~&\text{if}~~l=l_{\mathrm{free}},
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    where $x_-$ is defined in Lemma \ref{lem: Fixed points normalized simplex Potts model}, and by $z_{n+1}^k(t):=\widetilde{g}_t^{[k]}(z_{n}^k(t))$ for all $n\in \N_0$. Then, the limit $z^*_k(t):=\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty}z_{n}^k(t)$ exists and is a fixed point of $\widetilde{g}_t^{[k]}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.582013+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dffaf46880842124e9e26022150b6268678dda60ee55e8c96e3c7158231387e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Moment of arXiv:2411.15485, '\bf\Large Stochastic Volterra Equations for Local Times of Spectrally Positive L\'evy Proc'] For each $p\geq 1$, there exists a constant $C>0$  such that for any $\zeta,t\geq 0$,
  \begin{eqnarray}
   \sup_{n\geq 1}\mathbf{E}\Big[\big| X_\zeta^{(n)}(t) \big|^p  \Big] 
   \leq C\cdot (\zeta \vee \zeta^p) \cdot (1+t)^{2p-1}. 
  \end{eqnarray} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.593223+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e03754b9f7138e391ff2b1a61ca312635cf8b347181acf03296bf3f17afe7d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-4 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Suppose that Assumptions~(ass-1 of the paper)--(ass-2 of the paper) hold and assume $\beta=\beta_\text{\rm c}$. For each~$b\ge2$ there exist~$\eta>0$ and $C>0$ such that for all~$n\ge k\ge0$,
\begin{equation}

0< \frac{a_k(q)}{a_k(0)}\le \frac{C\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{-\eta q^2}}{(1+\sqrt k)^{|q|}}, \quad  q\in\mathbb Z.
\end{equation}
Moreover,  each $v_k$ is a $C^\infty$ function with
\begin{equation}

\sup_{z,z'\in\mathbb R}\bigl|v_{k+1}(z)-b v_k(z')\bigr|\le \frac C{\sqrt{1+k}}
\end{equation}
 for all $n>k\ge0$,  and
\begin{equation}

\sup_{z\in\mathbb R}\max\bigl\{ |v_k'(z)|,|v_k''(z)|\bigr\}\le \frac C{\sqrt{1+k}}
\end{equation}
for all~$n\ge k\ge0$. Furthermore, we have
\begin{equation}

\frac{a_k(1)}{a_k(0)}=\frac{1}{\sqrt k}\biggl[\frac{b^3-1}{(b-1)^2(b+1)^3}\biggr]^{1/2}+O(k^{-1})+O\biggl(\,\,k^{-1/2}\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\sum_{j\ge\min\{\sqrt k,n-k\}}\fraktura d_j\biggr),
\end{equation}
 where~$\{\fraktura d_j\}_{j\ge0}$ is the sequence from Assumption~(ass-1 of the paper).  Consequently,
\begin{equation}

v_k'(z) = \frac{4\pi}{\sqrt k}\biggl[\frac{b^3-1}{(b-1)^2(b+1)^3}\biggr]^{1/2}\sin(2\pi z) +O(k^{-1})+O\biggl(\,\,k^{-1/2}\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\sum_{j\ge\min\{\sqrt k,n-k\}}\fraktura d_j\biggr),\end{equation}
where~$o(1)\to0$ as~$\min\{k,n-k\}\to\infty$, uniformly in~$z\in\mathbb R$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.664113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e04ef178a83b6123697375e7c65ffb459575bfbd044ee6b71054c0698e7f4d6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem hazard-residual of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] Let $\mathcal{I}$ be a finite set of edges and let $L_e\geqslant0$, $e\in\mathcal{I}$, be deterministic lower bounds. Conditionally on the event
$
 \{\go_e>L_e\text{ for all }e\in\mathcal{I}\},
$
the random variables
\begin{align*}
 H(\go_e)-H(L_e)=\go_e^\theta-L_e^\theta,
 \quad e\in\mathcal{I},
\end{align*}
are independent rate-one exponentials. The same holds conditionally on any exploration history that reveals only the lower bounds $L_e$ for the edges in $\mathcal{I}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.520988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e0622d8a57795c89daf9935d73f58a891bfb8083e8ecd2d335c3b56c4c98097","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:u_pm of arXiv:2512.17808, 'Zeros of polynomial powers under the heat flow'] We define the two solutions to $\frac u {\sqrt t} +\frac{\sqrt t} u =\frac z{\sqrt t}$ by
	\begin{align}

		u_{t}^{\pm}(z):=\frac{1}2(z\pm\sqrt{z^2-4t}), \quad z\in\mathbb{C}\setminus \big( (-\infty,-2\sqrt t]\cup [2\sqrt t,\infty) \big),
	\end{align}
	where we choose the branch of the root such that $u_t^+(z)\sim z$ as $|z|\to\infty$ with $z\in \mathbb{C}_+$, and $u_t^-(z)\sim z$ as $|z|\to\infty$ with $z\in \mathbb{C}_-$, respectively.
	If $t=1$ we will denote the solutions by $u^{\pm}(z):=u_1^\pm(z)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.829512+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e078ba4bfd90857fee1415f8034e9f7c48bb20c4e77df23906af35c04df4856","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem eq:diffHa of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] Let $\rho: {\mathbb R}^{d_2} \mapsto {\mathbb R}$ and $h: {\mathbb R}^{d_1} \mapsto {\mathbb R}$ be differentiable. Then the function
$$
   H({\bm x}, {\bm y}) := [\rho({\bm y})]^m h\left(\frac{{\bm x}}{\rho({\bm y})} \right)
$$
satisfies the following relations
\begin{equation} 
  \rho({\bm y})  \partial_{y_i} H = \partial_{y_i} \rho({\bm y}) \left( m  - {\langle} {\bm x},\nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle} \right) H, \quad 1 \le i\le d_2,
\end{equation}
and, in particular, 
 \begin{equation} 
  \rho({\bm y})  {\langle} {\bm y}, \nabla_{{\bm y}}{\rangle}  H = {\langle} {\bm y}, \nabla_{{\bm y}}{\rangle} \rho ({\bm y}) \big( m  - {\langle} {\bm x},\nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle} H \big). 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.674674+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e0984a401938d2c766371fcde171b89d062a51b72b60536e81198423eeff998","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem informal-1 of arXiv:2605.28194, 'Delayed Blow-up in 3D Fluids via Pseudo-Transport Noise'] Assume $T>0, 2b+a>\frac{5}{2}$, and fix a bounded ball $\mathcal{B}\subset H^{2b+a}$. The following holds:
    	\begin{itemize}
    		\item [(1)] \emph{Navier-Stokes case} ($\kappa =1$). Let $a\in [\frac{1}{4},\frac{1}{2})$. Then, for every $\epsilon\in (0,1)$ and $\nu>0$, there exists a finite family divergence-free vector fields $\{\sigma_k\}\in \mathcal{S}^\nu$, such that for all divergence-free vector field $u_0\in \mathcal{B}$, the stochastic NS equations (equation fluid eq of the paper)  admits a unique strong solution up to time $T$ with a probability no less than $1-\epsilon$.
    		\item [(2)] \emph{Euler case} ($\kappa =0$). Let $a\in [-1,0]$. Then, for every $\epsilon\in (0,1)$, there exists $\nu\gg 0$ and a finite family divergence-free vector fields $\{\sigma_k\}\in \mathcal{S}^\nu$, such that for all divergence-free vector field $u_0\in \mathcal{B}$, the stochastic Euler equations (equation fluid eq of the paper)  admits a unique strong solution up to time $T$ with a probability no less than $1-\epsilon$.
    	\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.801079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e0a596b6e02a38f5bfa254d8956ecc4d4ab9795486f860e6af46782e7fe07de","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 51 of arXiv:2607.10486, 'Weak martingale solutions to stochastic Navier-Stokes-Cahn-Hilliard system with transport '] Suppose the assumptions stated in Section \ref{Ass-Abstract formulation} hold and that $\bX_n\coloneqq(\bu_n,\phi_n) \to (\bu,\phi)\coloneqq \bX$ in $\bZ_T$. Then, the sequence $M_\cdot^{n,z}(\bX_n)$ is equicontinuous with respect to $t$. Moreover, for all $t \in[0,T]$ and $z \in \mathscr{V}$, it holds that
 \begin{equation}
     \begin{aligned}
