{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:48:30.907222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e16077c36d5ee55376791fe3bc86e09273e747a543c1e085fd87c680ee2a0aa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 ({\cite[Theorem~1.1) of arXiv:2609.00936, 'Extended Abstract of Counting Divisorial Contractions with Centre a $cA_n$-Singularity'] {Kaw02}, \cite[Theorem~1.13]{Kaw03} and \cite[Theorem~6.1]{Pae24Counting}}] 
Let $k$ be a positive integer and $P$ a $cA_k$ singularity of a terminal variety~$X$. Let $\varphi\colon Y \to X$ be a proper birational morphism with centre~$P$. Then $\varphi$ is a divisorial contraction if and only if one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item  $\varphi$ is locally analytically equivalent to the $(r_1, r_2, a, 1)$-blowup of $\mathbb V(f)$ at $\bm 0$ where $f \in \mathbb C\{x, y, z, t\}$ is such that
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item  $r_1, r_2$ and $a$ are positive integers such that $r_1 \leq r_2$, $a(k+1) = r_1 + r_2$ and $a$ is coprime to both $r_1$ and $r_2$, and
  \item  $f = xy + g(z, t)$ where $\operatorname{wt} g = r_1 + r_2$,
  \end{enumerate}
\item  $k = 1$ and $\varphi$ is locally analytically equivalent to the $(1, 5, 3, 2)$-blowup of $\mathbb V(f)$ at $\bm 0$ where $f \in \mathbb C\{x, y, z, t\}$ is such that
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item  $f = xy + z^2 + t^3$,
  \end{enumerate}
\item  $k = 2$ and $\varphi$ is locally analytically equivalent to the $(4, 3, 2, 1)$-blowup of $\mathbb V(f)$ at $\bm 0$ where $f \in \mathbb C\{x, y, z, t\}$ is such that
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item  $f = x^2 + y^2 + z^3 + x t^2$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.831214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e16c24fb92ec4017d06ef205904e6eafad9007b133658b62f701654713f418f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1231219 of arXiv:2606.29143, 'Comonotonic and moment matching approximations for sums of lognormal random variables'] Let $A = (a_1, ..., a_m)^{'}$ and $Q = (q_1, ..., q_m)^{'}$ be two numerical vectors with $\min(a_1, ..., a_m) \geq 0, \max(a_1, ..., a_m) > 0$ and $0 = q_0 < q_1 < q_2 < ... < q_m = 1$. Let $X$ be a continuous random variable and $X_{A, Q}$ the step-weighted version of $X$ associated with the numerical vectors $A$ and $Q$. Then it holds that 
\begin{itemize} 
\item $F_{X_{A, Q}}(x)  = G_{A, Q}(F_X(x)) \,\, \text{for any} \,\, x \in \mathbb{R}$, 
\item $F_{X_{A, Q}}^{-1}(p) = F_X^{-1}(G^{-1}_{A, Q}(p))$ for any $p \in (0, 1)$, 
 \item $F_{X_{A, Q}}^{-1+}(p) = F_X^{-1+}(G^{-1+}_{A, Q}(p))$ for any $p \in (0, 1)$, 
\end{itemize} 
where $G_{A, Q}(t)$ is the continuous distortion function as given in Lemma (1231168 of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

